{"record_id":"D56-B28-R01","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Storage I/O","concept":"Synchronous durability cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can stronger storage durability increase write latency?","answer":"Durability mechanisms may require additional synchronization with storage before an operation can be acknowledged, introducing waiting that is not present when acknowledgment occurs before equivalent persistence guarantees.","principle":"Choose durability semantics explicitly and measure their impact rather than treating persistence cost as incidental.","tradeoff":"Stronger durability can reduce data-loss exposure while increasing write latency or limiting write throughput.","constraints":["Durability requirements must be explicit","Correctness must not depend on undocumented persistence behavior","Performance measurements must use representative storage conditions"],"security_implication":"Durability and integrity requirements should not be weakened solely to improve performance without an explicit risk decision.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no storage latency measurement is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B28-R02","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Storage I/O","concept":"Log I/O amplification","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can excessive logging affect application performance?","answer":"High-volume logging can consume CPU for message construction and formatting, memory for buffering, and storage or network bandwidth for output and downstream processing.","principle":"Treat logging as workload-producing I/O and CPU activity rather than free observability.","tradeoff":"Reducing logs can improve performance but may remove information needed for diagnosis and audit.","constraints":["Required security and audit events must remain available","Logging policies should distinguish diagnostic detail from mandatory records","High-volume paths should avoid unnecessary message construction"],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured logging overhead is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B28-R03","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Storage I/O","concept":"Buffered versus immediate output","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What performance trade-off exists between buffered and immediate output?","answer":"Buffering can reduce the frequency of small output operations by grouping them, while immediate output can provide lower observation delay or stronger durability semantics depending on the system.","comparison":{"buffered":{"potential_advantages":["Fewer small I/O operations","Potentially lower per-record overhead"],"potential_costs":["Data remains buffered longer","Buffer memory is required","Flush behavior must be controlled"]},"immediate":{"potential_advantages":["Lower output delay","Explicit persistence or visibility points"],"potential_costs":["More frequent I/O operations","Potentially higher per-event overhead"]}},"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured comparison is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B28-R04","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Storage I/O","concept":"Temporary-file contention","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can temporary-file usage create an unexpected performance bottleneck?","answer":"Concurrent temporary-file activity can increase filesystem operations, storage bandwidth consumption, metadata work, cleanup work, and contention with other storage users.","failure_mode":"A workload that appears CPU-bound becomes limited by temporary-file creation, reads, writes, or cleanup under larger concurrency.","cause":"Intermediate data is repeatedly materialized on shared storage.","solution":"Measure temporary-file volume and I/O, reduce unnecessary materialization where safe, and isolate or bound temporary storage usage.","tradeoff":"Avoiding temporary storage can increase memory consumption or implementation complexity.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no filesystem measurement is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B28-R05","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Storage I/O","concept":"Storage performance verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should storage-related performance changes be tested?","answer":"Test with representative data sizes, concurrency, filesystem or storage characteristics, and durability settings while measuring latency, throughput, I/O volume, and resource utilization.","test_case":{"test_input":"Representative read/write workload across controlled concurrency and data-size conditions.","expected_test_result":"Measured storage behavior satisfies the declared latency and throughput requirements without correctness or durability regression.","measurements":["Read latency","Write latency","Throughput","I/O volume","CPU utilization","Memory utilization","Error rate"],"failure_conditions":["Data corruption","Durability requirement violation","Unexpected I/O amplification","Unacceptable latency regression"]},"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"TEST_DESIGN_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}

{"record_id":"D56-B29-R01","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Contention","concept":"Critical-section duration","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why does critical-section duration matter under concurrency?","answer":"Longer critical sections keep shared resources unavailable to competing workers for longer, increasing waiting and reducing the amount of work that can proceed concurrently.","principle":"Keep synchronization scopes no larger than required for correctness and validate changes against contention measurements.","tradeoff":"Shortening a critical section may require restructuring data ownership or performing work outside the protected region.","security_implication":"Moving operations outside synchronization must preserve state invariants and security checks.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no contention reduction is claimed.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B29-R02","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Contention","concept":"Shared-state contention","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can heavily shared mutable state limit scalability?","answer":"As concurrency increases, workers increasingly compete for synchronization, ownership, cache coherence, or serialized access to the same state, limiting useful parallelism.","failure_mode":"Adding workers produces diminishing or negative returns because shared state becomes the dominant bottleneck.","cause":"Many concurrent operations depend on a common mutable resource.","solution":"Measure contention and consider ownership partitioning, immutable representations, local accumulation, or controlled aggregation where correctness permits.","tradeoff":"Partitioning state can increase merge or coordination costs and complicate consistency semantics.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no scalability curve is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B29-R03","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"CPU Cache Locality","concept":"Data locality","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can memory-access locality matter to CPU performance?","answer":"Access patterns that reuse nearby data can make more effective use of processor cache hierarchies, while irregular access patterns can increase memory-access latency and reduce useful CPU work.","principle":"Consider data-access locality when profiling identifies memory-access behavior as a significant contributor to performance.","constraints":["Correctness must remain unchanged","Data layout changes require representative measurement","Hardware-specific behavior should not be generalized without evidence"],"tradeoff":"A representation optimized for locality may be less flexible or may increase complexity elsewhere.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no hardware-specific improvement is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B29-R04","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Contention","concept":"False sharing investigation","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What is false sharing and why can it matter in concurrent workloads?","answer":"False sharing can occur when independently updated data resides within the same hardware cache-coherence unit, causing unnecessary coherence traffic between processors even though the logical data is unrelated.","explanation":"This is a hardware- and implementation-dependent optimization area. It should only be investigated after measurement indicates a relevant contention or memory-access problem.","constraint":"Do not assume false sharing is the cause of a performance problem without hardware-aware evidence.","tradeoff":"Padding or restructuring data can reduce one form of interference but may increase memory consumption and reduce locality elsewhere.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no hardware measurement is supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B29-R05","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Contention","concept":"Contention regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should a concurrency optimization be validated against contention regression?","answer":"Repeat the representative workload at multiple concurrency levels and verify that the change preserves correctness while measuring useful throughput, latency distribution, CPU usage, and contention indicators.","test_case":{"test_input":"Equivalent workload at low, moderate, and high controlled concurrency.","expected_test_result":"No correctness regression occurs; measured performance behavior is characterized across concurrency levels; contention does not unexpectedly dominate the workload.","measurements":["Throughput","Latency distribution","CPU utilization","Wait time","Lock or synchronization indicators where available"],"failure_conditions":["Correctness failure","Unexpected serialization","Unbounded wait time","Performance regression under representative concurrency"]},"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"TEST_DESIGN_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}

{"record_id":"D56-B30-R01","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Performance Regression","concept":"Baseline preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is a trustworthy baseline necessary for performance regression analysis?","answer":"A baseline provides a controlled reference against which later measurements can be compared, making it possible to distinguish meaningful changes from workload, environment, or measurement noise.","principle":"Preserve the workload definition, measurement method, environment assumptions, and relevant baseline measurements when establishing performance evidence.","requirements":["Stable workload definition","Known environment","Consistent measurement procedure","Recorded variability or uncertainty","Versioned baseline"],"tradeoff":"Maintaining baselines requires storage and maintenance effort, but without them regression interpretation becomes less reliable.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — this record defines verification practice rather than reporting a measured result.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
