{"record_id":"D56-B30-R04","dataset_id":"D56","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Performance Regression","concept":"Canary performance validation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can staged rollout support performance regression detection?","answer":"A staged rollout can expose a changed implementation to a controlled portion of representative traffic while performance, resource, error, and latency signals are compared against an appropriate reference.","principle":"Use progressive exposure when performance risk is material and the deployment system can provide representative measurements and safe rollback.","requirements":["Comparable reference population","Representative traffic","Defined observation window","Performance and correctness signals","Predefined rollback conditions"],"tradeoff":"Staged rollout increases deployment complexity and can prolong exposure to multiple versions, but it can limit the blast radius of an undetected regression.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no rollout outcome is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
