{"record_id":"D56-B31-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Workload Modeling","concept":"Workload characterization","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why should a performance investigation begin by characterizing the workload?","answer":"Performance behavior depends on workload shape, including request mix, input size, concurrency, temporal locality, dependency behavior, and resource distribution. Without workload characterization, measurements may not represent the condition being optimized.","principle":"Define the workload before interpreting performance measurements.","requirements":["Representative request or task mix","Representative input sizes","Relevant concurrency levels","Known dependency behavior","Defined measurement interval"],"procedure":["Identify dominant workload classes","Measure their relative frequency where evidence exists","Identify input-size distributions","Identify concurrency and burst behavior","Define representative test scenarios","Record assumptions that cannot be measured"],"tradeoff":"Detailed workload modeling requires additional measurement and maintenance but improves the relevance of performance conclusions.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no workload-specific production measurement is supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}
{"record_id":"D56-B31-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Workload Modeling","concept":"Workload mix distortion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an inaccurate workload mix lead to an incorrect performance conclusion?","answer":"If a benchmark overrepresents one request class, optimization may improve that class while leaving the dominant real workload unchanged or even degrading other classes.","failure_mode":"Benchmark performance improves while actual workload performance does not materially improve.","cause":"Test workload distribution differs from the workload being optimized.","root_cause":"Performance evaluation treated a narrow workload as representative without validating its composition.","solution":"Compare benchmark composition with available workload evidence and test multiple representative workload classes.","tradeoff":"More representative workload suites require additional test maintenance and execution time.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no benchmark-versus-production discrepancy is claimed.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B31-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Workload Modeling","concept":"Warm-up effects","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can warm-up state affect performance measurements?","answer":"Caches, connection pools, compiled code, adaptive runtimes, lazy initialization, and other stateful mechanisms can make early measurements differ from steady-state measurements.","principle":"Separate initialization or warm-up behavior from steady-state behavior when the performance question concerns sustained operation.","constraints":["Warm-up duration must reflect the system being tested","Cold-start performance may itself be a required metric","State must be documented between repetitions"],"tradeoff":"Discarding warm-up measurements improves steady-state characterization but can hide startup performance problems.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no warm-up effect is measured in this record.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B31-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Workload Modeling","concept":"Cold-start versus steady-state performance","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should cold-start and steady-state performance sometimes be measured separately?","answer":"Cold-start execution may include initialization, loading, connection establishment, cache population, or compilation work that is not representative of later requests. Conversely, systems with frequent restarts may make startup cost operationally significant.","comparison":{"cold_start":{"measures":["Initialization cost","First-use latency","Initial resource acquisition"],"risk":"May overstate steady-state request cost"},"steady_state":{"measures":["Sustained request behavior","Stable resource usage","Long-lived cache or connection state"],"risk":"May hide startup-related user or deployment costs"}},"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured comparison is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
