{"record_id":"D56-B32-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Startup Performance","concept":"Lazy initialization trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How can lazy initialization change performance characteristics?","answer":"Lazy initialization can reduce startup work by postponing initialization until a resource is needed, but the first request using that resource may pay the initialization cost.","principle":"Choose eager or lazy initialization according to startup requirements, first-use latency requirements, resource cost, and failure semantics.","tradeoff":"Eager initialization increases startup cost but can make first-use latency more predictable; lazy initialization can reduce unnecessary startup work but shifts cost to first use.","edge_case":"If many requests trigger the same lazy initialization concurrently, initialization must be coordinated to avoid duplicate work.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured startup or first-use change is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B32-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Startup Performance","concept":"Initialization storm","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What performance problem can occur when many workers initialize simultaneously?","answer":"Simultaneous initialization can multiply CPU, memory, network, disk, or dependency load and create contention during startup or recovery.","failure_mode":"A deployment or restart event produces a burst of initialization work that delays readiness or overloads dependencies.","cause":"Many workers independently perform expensive initialization at the same time.","solution":"Coordinate initialization where appropriate, bound concurrency, stagger startup, or precompute shared resources when correctness and architecture permit.","tradeoff":"Coordination can increase startup complexity or delay individual workers while reducing aggregate initialization pressure.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no startup incident or measured amplification is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B32-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Startup Performance","concept":"Precomputation versus runtime computation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When can precomputation improve runtime performance?","answer":"Precomputation can move expensive deterministic work from request time to an earlier stage when the required inputs are known and the precomputed representation remains valid.","comparison":{"precompute":{"potential_advantages":["Lower repeated request-time computation","More predictable runtime cost"],"potential_costs":["Additional build or startup work","Memory or storage requirements","Invalidation complexity"]},"runtime_compute":{"potential_advantages":["Fresh computation","Lower precomputed-state maintenance"],"potential_costs":["Repeated request-time CPU cost","Potential latency variability"]}},"constraint":"Precomputation is appropriate only when the result can remain correct for the required validity period.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no runtime improvement is claimed.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B32-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Startup Performance","concept":"Startup readiness versus initialization completeness","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should readiness not automatically mean that every expensive initialization task is complete?","answer":"Requiring all optional initialization work before readiness can increase startup time even when the service could safely handle core traffic without that work.","principle":"Define readiness according to the minimum state required to provide safe and correct service.","constraints":["Required dependencies must be initialized before serving dependent requests","Deferred initialization must have explicit failure handling","Optional components must not create hidden latency spikes"],"tradeoff":"Earlier readiness can improve deployment responsiveness but may move initialization costs into request paths.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no startup-time result is asserted.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
