{"record_id":"D56-B33-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Performance Lessons","subtopic":"Garbage Collection","concept":"Garbage-collection pressure","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can garbage-collection activity affect application performance?","answer":"High allocation rates or large temporary object populations can increase garbage-collection work in runtimes that use managed memory, potentially consuming CPU and introducing latency variability.","principle":"Treat garbage collection as a measurable consequence of allocation behavior rather than assuming it is the root cause of latency.","constraints":["Runtime-specific behavior must be considered","GC metrics must be interpreted with allocation and workload data","No universal GC threshold should be assumed"],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no GC measurement is supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}
