{"record_id":"D56-B57-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Capacity Planning","subtopic":"Headroom","concept":"Capacity headroom","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should performance planning include capacity headroom?","answer":"A system operating continuously at its measured limit has limited tolerance for workload variation, failures, maintenance, or resource interference. Capacity planning should therefore distinguish measured sustainable capacity from desired operating conditions and document the assumptions behind the headroom policy.","principle":"Capacity decisions should account for variability and failure conditions rather than treating peak measured throughput as automatically sustainable capacity.","requirements":["Define the workload represented by capacity measurements.","Record resource and environment assumptions.","Include expected variability and relevant failure scenarios."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no capacity measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B57-R02","D56-B57-R03","D56-B57-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B57-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Capacity Planning","subtopic":"Saturation Thresholds","concept":"Empirical saturation boundary","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How should a saturation threshold be established?","answer":"A saturation threshold should be derived from controlled measurements that define acceptable latency, throughput, error behavior, resource utilization, and workload conditions. A single CPU or memory percentage should not automatically be treated as the system's universal saturation point.","principle":"Define saturation relative to service objectives and observed workload behavior.","requirements":["Specify the target SLO or performance acceptance criteria.","Measure multiple relevant signals.","Record workload composition and environment."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no empirical threshold was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B57-R01","D56-B57-R04","D56-B57-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B57-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Capacity Planning","subtopic":"Workload Variability","concept":"Capacity under changing workload composition","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can capacity estimates fail when workload composition changes?","answer":"Different request types can consume substantially different CPU, memory, I/O, storage, or downstream resources. A capacity estimate based on one workload mix may therefore be invalid for another mix even when request volume is similar.","principle":"Model capacity using resource-relevant workload composition, not request count alone.","tradeoff":"More detailed workload modeling improves capacity fidelity but increases measurement and modeling effort.","edge_case":"A low-volume operation with unusually high resource cost can become the limiting factor despite a small share of total requests.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no workload-composition measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B57-R01","D56-B57-R02","D56-B57-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B57-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Capacity Planning","subtopic":"Overload Response","concept":"Capacity exhaustion behavior","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should a performance lesson capture when capacity is exhausted?","answer":"It should identify which resource became constrained, how demand exceeded available capacity, how latency and errors changed, what overload controls were activated, and whether downstream dependencies amplified the condition. The lesson should distinguish observed evidence from inferred causality.","failure_mode":"Resource saturation causes queue growth, latency degradation, errors, or cascading dependency pressure.","cause":"Demand exceeded the sustainable capacity of a constrained resource or service dependency.","solution":"Establish evidence-based thresholds, overload controls, capacity signals, and recovery procedures.","performance_implication":"Saturation behavior should be recorded as a workload- and environment-specific lesson rather than a universal threshold.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no actual incident evidence was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B57-R02","D56-B57-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B57-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Capacity Planning","subtopic":"Capacity Verification","concept":"Capacity curve measurement","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should sustainable capacity be measured?","answer":"Increase workload in controlled steps while measuring throughput, latency distribution, errors, queueing, and relevant resource signals. Define acceptance criteria before the test and stop according to safety limits rather than forcing the system into uncontrolled failure.","test_case":"Controlled capacity characterization","test_input":["baseline load","incremental load levels","mixed workload composition","dependency-constrained workload"],"expected_test_result":"A workload-specific capacity curve and observed degradation boundary are recorded with environment, configuration, and acceptance criteria.","testing_guidance":"Do not report the maximum observed throughput as universal capacity.","verification_method":"Controlled load characterization with predefined safety and acceptance criteria.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — test has not been executed.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B57-R01","D56-B57-R02","D56-B57-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B58-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Multi-Tenant Performance","subtopic":"Noisy Neighbors","concept":"Tenant-induced resource contention","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can one tenant degrade another tenant's performance?","answer":"Shared CPU, memory, I/O, connection pools, caches, queues, or downstream quotas can allow a high-demand tenant to consume resources needed by others. Multi-tenant performance lessons should therefore identify the shared resource and the isolation mechanism protecting tenant objectives.","principle":"Performance isolation should be designed and measured at shared resource boundaries.","requirements":["Identify shared resources.","Define tenant-level resource or fairness requirements.","Measure cross-tenant impact under controlled contention."],"security_implication":"Insufficient isolation can also create data or availability risks across tenants.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no multi-tenant measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B58-R02","D56-B58-R03","D56-B58-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B58-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Multi-Tenant Performance","subtopic":"Fairness","concept":"Tenant fairness versus aggregate throughput","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can maximizing aggregate throughput conflict with tenant fairness?","answer":"A scheduler or shared resource policy may maximize total utilization by allowing some workloads to consume disproportionate capacity. Tenant fairness may instead require quotas, weighted allocation, reservations, or other controls that constrain some aggregate optimization opportunities.","principle":"Define whether the performance objective is aggregate throughput, per-tenant latency, fairness, or a combination before optimizing shared resources.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation or fairness can reduce peak aggregate utilization while improving predictability across tenants.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured fairness-versus-throughput result was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B58-R01","D56-B58-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B58-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Multi-Tenant Performance","subtopic":"Resource Quotas","concept":"Per-tenant resource controls","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can tenant-level resource controls reduce noisy-neighbor effects?","answer":"Per-tenant quotas, concurrency limits, rate limits, reservations, or weighted scheduling can constrain resource consumption. The appropriate mechanism depends on which resource is shared and whether the objective is isolation, fairness, or admission control.","principle":"Apply controls at the resource boundary where cross-tenant contention is actually occurring.","requirements":["Identify the constrained resource.","Define enforcement semantics.","Define behavior when a tenant reaches its limit.","Measure tenant-level outcomes."],"edge_case":"A tenant can remain within its individual quota while creating contention in a downstream dependency shared by many tenants.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no implementation result was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B58-R01","D56-B58-R02","D56-B58-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B58-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Multi-Tenant Performance","subtopic":"Isolation Anti-Pattern","concept":"Aggregate-only performance monitoring","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is aggregate performance monitoring insufficient for multi-tenant systems?","answer":"An aggregate metric can remain healthy while a subset of tenants experiences severe latency or resource starvation. Tenant-scoped measurements are required when tenant-level performance or fairness is part of the service contract.","failure_mode":"Tenant-specific degradation is hidden by healthy aggregate metrics.","cause":"Performance telemetry is aggregated without sufficient tenant attribution or segmentation.","solution":"Collect appropriately scoped latency, throughput, errors, queueing, and resource metrics while controlling metric cardinality and privacy exposure.","security_implication":"Tenant attribution must not expose sensitive tenant data through observability systems.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no production observation was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B58-R01","D56-B58-R03","D56-B58-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B58-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Multi-Tenant Performance","subtopic":"Isolation Testing","concept":"Noisy-neighbor test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should multi-tenant performance isolation be tested?","answer":"Run representative workloads for multiple tenants while intentionally varying one tenant's demand within safe test limits. Measure tenant-scoped latency, throughput, errors, resource consumption, and aggregate behavior.","test_case":"Multi-tenant noisy-neighbor isolation test","test_input":["balanced tenants","one high-demand tenant","multiple high-demand tenants","tenant-specific quota enforcement"],"expected_test_result":"Tenant-level behavior matches the defined isolation and fairness requirements, and cross-tenant degradation is measurable rather than hidden by aggregate metrics.","testing_guidance":"Keep tenant identities synthetic or appropriately protected and record resource configuration with the test.","verification_method":"Controlled multi-tenant load test with tenant-scoped telemetry and correctness checks.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — test has not been executed.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B58-R01","D56-B58-R02","D56-B58-R03","D56-B58-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B59-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Performance Observability","subtopic":"Sampling Bias","concept":"Representative performance telemetry","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can sampling distort performance conclusions?","answer":"Sampling can underrepresent rare slow requests, tail latency, short-lived spikes, or specific workload classes. Performance lessons should therefore document the sampling method and assess whether the captured population represents the question being answered.","principle":"Treat observability data as a sampled measurement of a population, not automatically as the complete population.","requirements":["Record sampling behavior.","Understand which requests are sampled or excluded.","Use appropriate aggregation and tail analysis for the decision."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no telemetry study was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_OBSERVABILITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B59-R02","D56-B59-R03","D56-B59-R05"]}
