{"record_id":"D56-B59-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Performance Observability","subtopic":"Metric Cardinality","concept":"High-cardinality telemetry cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can detailed performance labels create a new performance problem?","answer":"Adding dimensions to telemetry can increase storage, processing, indexing, network, and query costs. High-cardinality labels should therefore be justified by diagnostic value and governed according to the observability system's capacity and privacy constraints.","principle":"Increase telemetry detail only when its diagnostic value justifies the resource and operational cost.","tradeoff":"More diagnostic dimensions can improve attribution while increasing telemetry cost and cardinality risk.","security_implication":"Identifiers or sensitive attributes should not be exposed merely because they improve performance debugging.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no telemetry-cost measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_OBSERVABILITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B59-R01","D56-B59-R04","D56-B59-R05"]}
