{"record_id":"D56-B60-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Lesson Synthesis","concept":"Evidence-backed performance lesson","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"What structure makes a performance lesson reusable for future engineering decisions?","answer":"A reusable lesson should connect the observed bottleneck to the measurement that established it, the intervention, the measured or unknown result, trade-offs, regressions, applicable conditions, and the generalized lesson. This prevents an isolated optimization story from being mistaken for a universal rule.","principle":"Preserve the evidence chain from observation through intervention and outcome before generalizing a performance lesson.","requirements":["Record the original measurement context.","Distinguish measured, estimated, inferred, and unknown claims.","Record regressions and trade-offs.","Preserve applicability boundaries."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — this record defines the lesson structure rather than reporting an operational outcome.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B60-R02","D56-B60-R03","D56-B60-R04","D56-B60-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B60-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Regression Knowledge","concept":"Recording failed performance interventions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should unsuccessful performance interventions be retained as knowledge?","answer":"A failed intervention can prevent repeated investigation of an already tested hypothesis and can reveal constraints or trade-offs that were not obvious initially. The record should preserve what was actually measured and avoid turning an unsuccessful experiment into a general prohibition.","principle":"Failed performance experiments are valuable evidence when their conditions, intervention, and result are preserved accurately.","failure_mode":"Future engineers repeat an ineffective optimization because prior negative evidence was not retained or discoverable.","cause":"Performance experiments are recorded only when successful or are documented without sufficient context.","solution":"Record unsuccessful interventions with baseline, hypothesis, workload, measurement, observed result, limitations, and applicability boundaries.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no specific failed experiment was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_LEARNING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B60-R01","D56-B60-R03","D56-B60-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B60-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Generalization Boundaries","concept":"Conditional performance knowledge","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a measured performance lesson be generalized?","answer":"Generalization should preserve the conditions under which the evidence was collected, including workload, implementation, runtime or framework version, environment, configuration, measurement method, and relevant resource constraints. A result should not be generalized beyond its evidence without additional support.","principle":"Generalize only to the boundary supported by evidence.","requirements":["Preserve workload assumptions.","Preserve software and environment versions where material.","Identify known limitations.","Use explicit uncertainty when evidence is incomplete."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no empirical lesson was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B60-R01","D56-B60-R02","D56-B60-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B60-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Contradictory Evidence","concept":"Contradiction-aware performance knowledge","knowledge_type":"CONFLICTED_KNOWLEDGE_RULE","question":"How should conflicting performance results be represented?","answer":"Conflicting results should remain distinguishable when they arise from different workloads, versions, environments, implementations, measurement methods, or other material conditions. The system should preserve both evidence records and their applicability boundaries instead of forcing a single universal conclusion without sufficient evidence.","principle":"Explain performance contradictions through conditions and provenance before attempting synthesis.","requirements":["Preserve each result's provenance.","Compare measurement populations and methodology.","Record unresolved contradictions explicitly.","Do not convert conflicting evidence into a fabricated consensus."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no conflicting empirical records were supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B60-R01","D56-B60-R03","D56-B60-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B60-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Knowledge Maintenance","concept":"Performance lesson regression and revalidation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When should a stored performance lesson be revalidated?","answer":"A performance lesson should be reconsidered when material implementation, runtime, framework, dependency, hardware, workload, configuration, architecture, or measurement changes invalidate its original assumptions. Revalidation should compare the new environment with the provenance of the original evidence.","principle":"Performance knowledge is conditional and should be revalidated when its supporting conditions materially change.","requirements":["Track evidence provenance and applicable versions.","Detect material environment or workload changes.","Mark stale lessons rather than silently treating them as current.","Retain historical evidence for comparison."],"verification_method":"Compare lesson applicability metadata with the current environment and rerun the relevant benchmark or measurement when material assumptions have changed.","verification_guidance":"Do not delete historical results merely because newer evidence differs; preserve supersession and contradiction relationships.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no revalidation execution was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B60-R01","D56-B60-R02","D56-B60-R03","D56-B60-R04"]}

{"record_id":"D56-B61-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Serialization Performance","subtopic":"Serialization Cost","concept":"Serialization as a measurable application bottleneck","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can serialization become a performance bottleneck?","answer":"Serialization converts application state into a transport or storage representation and can consume CPU, allocate memory, and increase data volume. In high-frequency or large-payload paths, its cost should be measured separately from downstream I/O and application logic.","principle":"Measure serialization and deserialization cost independently before optimizing the representation.","requirements":["Measure serialization and deserialization separately where practical.","Record payload characteristics.","Preserve representation compatibility requirements."],"tradeoff":"A faster serialization format may increase implementation complexity, reduce interoperability, or change payload size.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no workload-specific measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B61-R02","D56-B61-R03","D56-B61-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B61-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Serialization Performance","subtopic":"Payload Size","concept":"Representation size versus processing cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should serialization format selection consider both payload size and processing cost?","answer":"A representation that produces smaller payloads may require more CPU work to encode or decode, while a representation that is easy to process may produce larger transfers or storage requirements. The appropriate choice depends on the complete path and its constraints.","principle":"Evaluate representation size, encode/decode cost, compatibility, and end-to-end behavior together.","tradeoff":"Smaller representations can reduce transfer or storage volume while potentially increasing computational work.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no comparative measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B61-R01","D56-B61-R03","D56-B61-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B61-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Serialization Performance","subtopic":"Schema Complexity","concept":"Serialization of oversized object graphs","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can oversized or unnecessarily deep object graphs affect performance?","answer":"Serializing large object graphs can increase traversal work, temporary memory use, payload size, and downstream processing. The correct optimization may be to reduce the data required for the operation rather than merely changing the serializer.","failure_mode":"Latency and memory usage increase as payloads contain unnecessary or recursively expanded data.","cause":"The application serializes more state than the consumer actually requires.","solution":"Define a minimal response or storage representation, avoid unnecessary graph expansion, and measure the resulting end-to-end behavior.","performance_implication":"Reducing unnecessary data can affect CPU, memory, and transfer costs simultaneously.","edge_case":"Lazy or computed fields can trigger additional database or application work during serialization.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B61-R01","D56-B61-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B61-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Serialization Performance","subtopic":"Optimization Safety","concept":"Serialization optimization without compatibility validation","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can changing serialization format solely for performance cause regressions?","answer":"Consumers may depend on schema, field representation, ordering assumptions, encoding behavior, compatibility guarantees, or error semantics. A performance optimization is incomplete if it improves encoding speed while breaking valid consumers or data interchange requirements.","failure_mode":"Performance change introduces compatibility or correctness regressions in consumers.","cause":"Serialization performance was optimized without validating the complete producer-consumer contract.","solution":"Define compatibility requirements, test representative consumers, validate encoded and decoded semantics, and measure the actual workload.","security_implication":"Serialization changes must preserve validation and trust-boundary assumptions; performance optimization must not bypass required validation.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no compatibility failure was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B61-R01","D56-B61-R02","D56-B61-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B61-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Serialization Performance","subtopic":"Benchmark Verification","concept":"Serialization differential benchmark","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should serialization implementations be compared?","answer":"Use equivalent logical data and measure encoding time, decoding time, output size, memory behavior where measurable, and end-to-end impact. Verify semantic equivalence before interpreting performance differences.","test_case":"Serialization format comparison","test_input":["small payload","large payload","deep object structure","repeated fields","representative production-shaped payload"],"expected_test_result":"Decoded data preserves the required semantics and performance differences are reported only for the measured implementation, environment, and workload.","testing_guidance":"Separate microbenchmark results from end-to-end service results.","verification_method":"Differential correctness testing plus controlled serialization benchmark.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — test has not been executed.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B61-R01","D56-B61-R02","D56-B61-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B62-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Compression Performance","subtopic":"Compression Cost","concept":"Compression CPU versus transfer reduction","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What performance tradeoff does payload compression introduce?","answer":"Compression can reduce the amount of data transferred or stored, but compression and decompression consume computational resources and can affect latency. The useful result depends on payload characteristics, hardware, compression settings, and the constrained resource.","principle":"Evaluate compression as an end-to-end resource tradeoff rather than assuming smaller payloads automatically produce lower latency.","requirements":["Measure compression and decompression cost.","Measure resulting payload size.","Consider CPU and memory availability.","Preserve correctness and compatibility."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no compression benchmark was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B62-R02","D56-B62-R03","D56-B62-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B62-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Compression Performance","subtopic":"Workload Characteristics","concept":"Compressibility-dependent performance","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can compression performance differ substantially between payload classes?","answer":"Compression effectiveness depends on the structure and redundancy of the input. Highly compressible and poorly compressible payloads can have different size reduction and processing characteristics, so a result measured on one payload class should not automatically be generalized.","principle":"Benchmark compression using representative payload distributions rather than a single synthetic input.","requirements":["Include representative payload classes.","Record compression configuration.","Measure both processing cost and output size."],"edge_case":"Already compressed or high-entropy data may receive little size reduction while still incurring compression work.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no payload-specific benchmark was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B62-R01","D56-B62-R04","D56-B62-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B62-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Compression Performance","subtopic":"CPU Saturation","concept":"Compression-induced CPU contention","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can compression create a performance regression in a CPU-constrained service?","answer":"Compression adds processing work to the request path. If CPU capacity is already constrained, compression may increase queueing or reduce capacity available for application logic, even if the payload becomes smaller.","failure_mode":"CPU utilization and request latency increase after enabling or strengthening compression.","cause":"Compression work competes with latency-sensitive application processing on a constrained CPU resource.","solution":"Measure CPU cost and end-to-end latency, tune compression only where beneficial, and consider where compression should occur in the architecture.","tradeoff":"Lower transfer volume can come at the cost of additional CPU and potentially increased latency.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no production regression was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B62-R01","D56-B62-R04","D56-B62-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B62-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Compression Performance","subtopic":"Blind Compression","concept":"Compressing unsuitable data","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should applications avoid blindly compressing every payload?","answer":"Some payloads may already be compressed or have characteristics that provide little reduction. Applying compression indiscriminately can add CPU, memory, and latency costs without a corresponding benefit.","failure_mode":"Compression consumes resources while providing negligible reduction in data volume.","cause":"Compression policy does not account for payload type, size, or measured benefit.","solution":"Define evidence-based eligibility rules and validate them against representative payload distributions.","performance_implication":"Compression overhead can become a bottleneck when the avoided transfer or storage cost is small.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured result was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B62-R01","D56-B62-R02","D56-B62-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B62-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Compression Performance","subtopic":"End-to-End Verification","concept":"Compression benefit validation","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should compression be validated as a performance optimization?","answer":"Compare equivalent workloads with compression enabled and disabled while measuring payload size, CPU, memory, latency, throughput, and correctness. Use multiple payload classes and record the exact compression configuration.","test_case":"Compression end-to-end comparison","test_input":["highly compressible payload","poorly compressible payload","small payload","large payload","CPU-constrained environment"],"expected_test_result":"Correctness remains equivalent and the measured resource tradeoff is documented for each payload class.","testing_guidance":"Do not report a single compression ratio as an end-to-end performance result.","verification_method":"Controlled differential workload with resource and correctness measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — test has not been executed.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B62-R01","D56-B62-R02","D56-B62-R03","D56-B62-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B63-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Batch Processing Performance","subtopic":"Batch Size","concept":"Batch-size optimization","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can batching improve performance?","answer":"Batching can amortize fixed per-operation costs such as scheduling, function calls, transaction setup, or communication overhead. Larger batches can also increase waiting time or memory use, so the appropriate size must be determined from the workload and latency requirements.","principle":"Choose batch size by balancing per-item overhead against waiting time, memory use, and downstream capacity.","tradeoff":"Larger batches may improve throughput efficiency while increasing latency, memory consumption, or burstiness.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no workload-specific measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B63-R02","D56-B63-R03","D56-B63-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B63-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Batch Processing Performance","subtopic":"Latency Tradeoff","concept":"Batching delay","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How can batching improve throughput while worsening latency?","answer":"A batch may wait for additional items before processing begins. This can amortize fixed costs but adds queueing delay for items that arrived early, particularly when demand is low or bursty.","principle":"Optimize batch size against the service's latency objective rather than maximizing batch efficiency alone.","edge_case":"Under low traffic, a large batching window can dominate total request latency.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured batching result was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B63-R01","D56-B63-R03","D56-B63-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B63-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Batch Processing Performance","subtopic":"Adaptive Batching","concept":"Size-or-time bounded batching","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a batch be bounded by both size and time?","answer":"A size limit controls memory and processing burst size, while a time limit prevents low-volume traffic from waiting indefinitely for a full batch. Combining both constraints can provide predictable bounds while allowing batching benefits when demand is sufficient.","principle":"Use explicit size and time bounds when batching has both throughput and latency objectives.","requirements":["Define maximum batch size.","Define maximum waiting interval.","Define behavior under partial batches.","Measure latency and throughput across workload levels."],"tradeoff":"Adaptive or bounded batching adds policy complexity but can avoid extremes of tiny and excessively delayed batches.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no implementation result was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B63-R01","D56-B63-R02","D56-B63-R04","D56-B63-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B63-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Batch Processing Performance","subtopic":"Burst Amplification","concept":"Batch-induced downstream bursts","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can batching create downstream performance problems?","answer":"A batch can convert a smooth incoming stream into larger bursts toward a downstream service, database, or resource. If downstream capacity or transaction behavior cannot absorb those bursts, batching can shift rather than remove the bottleneck.","failure_mode":"Downstream latency or resource contention increases after batching groups previously distributed work.","cause":"Batch size or dispatch policy creates bursts that exceed downstream sustainable behavior.","solution":"Measure downstream resource behavior, bound batch size, control dispatch rate, and preserve backpressure.","performance_implication":"Local throughput improvement can coexist with downstream latency regression.","security_implication":"Externally influenced batch growth should be bounded to prevent resource-exhaustion behavior.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no downstream incident evidence was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B63-R01","D56-B63-R03","D56-B63-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B63-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Batch Processing Performance","subtopic":"Batch-Size Testing","concept":"Batch-size sweep","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should batch size be evaluated?","answer":"Run equivalent workloads across multiple batch sizes and measure end-to-end latency, throughput, memory use, downstream behavior, and correctness. Include both low-volume and high-volume conditions.","test_case":"Batch-size sensitivity experiment","test_input":["low arrival rate","steady high arrival rate","bursty arrival rate","small batch","medium batch","large batch"],"expected_test_result":"The workload-specific relationship between batch size, latency, throughput, and resource behavior is recorded without assuming a universal optimal batch size.","testing_guidance":"Include timeouts and partial-batch behavior in correctness and latency measurements.","verification_method":"Controlled batch-size sweep with downstream and functional verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — test has not been executed.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B63-R01","D56-B63-R02","D56-B63-R03","D56-B63-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B64-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Database Access Performance","subtopic":"Query Shape","concept":"Data-access amplification","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can application access patterns amplify database work?","answer":"An application can issue multiple database operations for one logical task, repeatedly retrieve overlapping data, or request data it does not use. The resulting database work can become a bottleneck even when individual queries appear inexpensive.","principle":"Measure database work at the logical-operation level, not only per-query latency.","requirements":["Trace database operations to logical application requests.","Identify repeated or redundant access.","Preserve correctness and transaction semantics while optimizing."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no workload-specific database measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B64-R02","D56-B64-R03","D56-B64-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B64-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Database Access Performance","subtopic":"Query Consolidation","concept":"Reducing redundant database round trips","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can consolidating database access improve performance?","answer":"If multiple operations retrieve overlapping information or perform unnecessary round trips, consolidating compatible access can reduce coordination and transport overhead. The change must preserve transaction boundaries, consistency, authorization, and result semantics.","principle":"Reduce redundant data-access coordination only when the resulting operation preserves required semantics.","tradeoff":"Fewer database interactions can reduce overhead while producing more complex queries or larger result sets.","security_implication":"Query consolidation must preserve authorization boundaries and must not cause unauthorized data to be fetched merely for convenience.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured improvement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B64-R01","D56-B64-R04","D56-B64-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B64-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Database Access Performance","subtopic":"Over-Fetching","concept":"Fetching unused data","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can fetching more database data than required hurt performance?","answer":"Unnecessary columns or rows can increase database work, transfer volume, application memory use, and downstream processing. Data access should match the actual information required by the operation when practical.","failure_mode":"Latency and resource usage increase because operations retrieve substantially more data than they consume.","cause":"Data-access contracts are broader than the actual application requirement.","solution":"Define minimal data requirements, measure query behavior, and validate that narrower access preserves functionality.","performance_implication":"Over-fetching can amplify cost across database, network, serialization, and application layers.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no measured result was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B64-R01","D56-B64-R02","D56-B64-R05"]}
