{"record_id":"D56-B67-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Startup Performance","subtopic":"Initialization Duplication","concept":"Repeated initialization across workers","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can repeated per-worker initialization increase system startup cost?","answer":"When each worker independently performs expensive initialization, total startup resource consumption can increase substantially and shared dependencies may experience a burst. The correct design depends on process isolation, resource ownership, and consistency requirements.","failure_mode":"Deployment or restart events cause synchronized initialization load and delayed readiness.","cause":"Expensive initialization is repeated independently across workers without considering shared or reusable state.","solution":"Measure initialization cost and concurrency, identify safe reusable artifacts, and control startup parallelism where appropriate.","security_implication":"Shared initialization artifacts must preserve tenant and security isolation.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no deployment measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SOFTWARE_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B67-R01","D56-B67-R02","D56-B67-R05"]}
