{"record_id":"D56-B68-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Resource Pool Performance","subtopic":"Pool Sizing","concept":"Resource pool sizing","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can resource-pool sizing affect performance?","answer":"Pools for connections, workers, buffers, or similar resources can become bottlenecks when too small or create resource contention and waste when too large. Pool behavior should be evaluated against demand, service time, resource capacity, and failure conditions.","principle":"Size resource pools from measured demand and resource constraints rather than arbitrary large values.","tradeoff":"Larger pools can increase concurrency while increasing resource consumption and downstream pressure.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no pool-sizing measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B68-R02","D56-B68-R03","D56-B68-R05"]}
