{"record_id":"D56-B68-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Resource Pool Performance","subtopic":"Oversized Pools","concept":"Excessive pool concurrency","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can making a resource pool much larger fail to solve latency problems?","answer":"A larger pool can increase concurrent work against a constrained downstream resource, causing contention, queueing, memory pressure, or saturation elsewhere. Pool size should therefore be treated as part of a complete capacity system.","failure_mode":"Increasing pool size shifts or amplifies contention without improving end-to-end performance.","cause":"The pool was treated as an isolated bottleneck even though another dependency constrained throughput.","solution":"Trace resource usage through downstream dependencies and characterize the complete bottleneck chain.","tradeoff":"More local concurrency can increase utilization while reducing stability at a downstream bottleneck.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no pool-sizing experiment was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B68-R01","D56-B68-R02","D56-B68-R05"]}
