{"record_id":"D56-B69-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Performance Reliability","subtopic":"Long-Tail Causes","concept":"Rare slow-path analysis","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can rare slow requests affect a performance lesson?","answer":"Rare slow requests can dominate tail latency or cause SLO violations even when most requests remain fast. Analysis should determine whether slow paths correspond to specific inputs, dependencies, resource states, retries, or other identifiable conditions.","failure_mode":"Tail latency degrades because a small subset of requests follows an expensive or delayed path.","cause":"A rare condition triggers disproportionately expensive work or waiting.","solution":"Segment slow requests by relevant attributes, trace representative examples, and preserve uncertainty when the cause cannot be established.","security_implication":"Diagnostic segmentation must avoid exposing sensitive request attributes through telemetry.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no slow-path population was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_PERFORMANCE_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B69-R01","D56-B69-R04","D56-B69-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B69-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Performance Reliability","subtopic":"Retry Amplification","concept":"Retries as performance workload","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should retries be included in performance analysis?","answer":"Retries create additional work and can amplify load on an already degraded dependency. A performance lesson that measures only successful first attempts can understate the actual resource demand during failure or latency conditions.","principle":"Treat retry traffic as part of system workload when analyzing degraded performance.","requirements":["Measure retry volume separately.","Record retry policy and triggering conditions.","Analyze downstream amplification."],"tradeoff":"Retries can improve recovery from transient failures while increasing work and congestion during sustained degradation.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no retry amplification measurement was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_RELIABILITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B69-R02","D56-B69-R04","D56-B69-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B69-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Performance Reliability","subtopic":"Retry Storms","concept":"Unbounded or synchronized retries","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can aggressive retry behavior worsen a performance incident?","answer":"Retries can add demand precisely when a dependency has reduced capacity. If retries are excessive, synchronized, or insufficiently bounded, they can increase queueing and delay recovery.","failure_mode":"Dependency degradation causes retry traffic to increase load and further degrade latency or availability.","cause":"Retry policy does not adequately account for dependency saturation or repeated failure.","solution":"Define bounded retry behavior, appropriate delay or backoff, attempt limits, and failure handling based on dependency semantics.","security_implication":"Externally triggered repeated requests should not create uncontrolled retry amplification that enables resource exhaustion.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no retry incident evidence was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_RELIABILITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B69-R03","D56-B69-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B69-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Performance Reliability","subtopic":"Degraded-Mode Testing","concept":"Retry and tail-latency experiment","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should retry behavior be evaluated under degraded dependency performance?","answer":"Use a controlled test dependency that introduces bounded latency and failures, then measure original demand, retry volume, downstream demand, latency distribution, and recovery behavior while enforcing safe test limits.","test_case":"Retry amplification and tail-latency test","test_input":["normal dependency","elevated latency","transient failure","sustained failure","bounded retry policy"],"expected_test_result":"Retry behavior remains within defined limits and the effect on latency, workload amplification, and recovery is measurable.","testing_guidance":"Use isolated test infrastructure and explicit workload ceilings to prevent uncontrolled amplification.","verification_method":"Controlled dependency-degradation test with retry telemetry and correctness verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — test has not been executed.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B69-R02","D56-B69-R03","D56-B69-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B70-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Intervention Attribution","concept":"Separating intervention effects from workload changes","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can a performance lesson distinguish an intervention effect from a workload change?","answer":"Compare measurements under controlled or sufficiently comparable conditions and record workload composition, environment, configuration, and software version. Without such controls, a performance change may be associated with the intervention without being attributable to it.","principle":"Performance attribution requires comparable evidence before and after the intervention.","requirements":["Preserve baseline conditions.","Record workload changes.","Record software and environment changes.","Identify confounding changes explicitly."],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no intervention experiment was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"EXPERIMENTAL_PERFORMANCE_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B70-R02","D56-B70-R03","D56-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B70-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Confounding Variables","concept":"Environment-induced performance differences","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should infrastructure or environment changes be separated from application performance changes?","answer":"Hardware, runtime, operating-system configuration, dependency versions, network conditions, and workload changes can alter performance independently of application code. A lesson that ignores these factors may incorrectly attribute the observed difference to the code intervention.","failure_mode":"Performance change is attributed to an application modification even though the environment changed materially at the same time.","cause":"Baseline and post-change measurements were not sufficiently controlled or documented.","solution":"Record environment provenance, isolate variables where practical, and explicitly classify attribution confidence.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no specific confounded experiment was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"EXPERIMENTAL_PERFORMANCE_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B70-R03","D56-B70-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B70-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Experiment Design","concept":"One-variable-at-a-time performance experiments","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"When is changing one material performance variable at a time useful?","answer":"Changing one material variable at a time can make causal interpretation simpler when interactions are limited and the experiment is sufficiently controlled. It is not universally optimal because real systems can contain meaningful interactions that require factorial or other experimental designs.","principle":"Choose experimental design according to the causal question and expected variable interactions.","requirements":["Define the hypothesis before measurement.","Preserve baseline evidence.","Control material environmental variables.","Record interaction limitations."],"tradeoff":"Simple experiments can improve attribution clarity while requiring more iterations when multiple variables interact.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no experiment was executed for this record.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","evidence_level":"EXPERIMENTAL_PERFORMANCE_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B70-R02","D56-B70-R04","D56-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B70-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Evidence Quality","concept":"False performance attribution","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should be recorded when a performance improvement cannot be confidently attributed to one intervention?","answer":"The lesson should retain the observed change, list concurrent changes or confounders, state the attribution uncertainty, and avoid presenting the intervention as the proven cause. Additional controlled measurement can be linked if later performed.","principle":"Uncertain attribution is a valid knowledge state and should not be converted into a verified causal claim.","failure_mode":"An observational performance change becomes a falsely causal optimization rule in future knowledge retrieval.","cause":"Evidence limitations were omitted when the lesson was generalized.","solution":"Store attribution confidence, confounding factors, provenance, and follow-up validation requirements.","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no empirical attribution case was supplied.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B70-R02","D56-B70-R03","D56-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B70-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Attribution Verification","concept":"Controlled before-and-after performance experiment","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should a performance intervention be experimentally validated for attribution?","answer":"Define the intervention and primary performance hypothesis, capture a baseline, control or document material workload and environment variables, apply the intervention, repeat the measurement, and analyze both performance and correctness outcomes.","test_case":"Performance intervention attribution test","test_input":["baseline implementation","optimized implementation","representative workload","stress workload","controlled environment"],"expected_test_result":"The observed difference is reproducible under documented conditions, attribution confidence is explicitly classified, and correctness remains valid.","testing_guidance":"If multiple variables changed simultaneously, record that limitation rather than claiming single-variable causality.","verification_method":"Controlled differential experiment with repeated measurements, provenance capture, and functional verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — test has not been executed.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B70-R02","D56-B70-R03","D56-B70-R04"]}

{"record_id":"D56-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Storage Write Performance","subtopic":"Write Amplification","concept":"Storage writes can become a performance bottleneck when one logical update causes substantially more physical or durable work than expected.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can write amplification become a performance bottleneck?","answer":"Measure the relationship between logical application writes and underlying storage work, then determine whether batching, data layout, write coalescing, or reduced redundant updates can lower the amplification without violating durability or consistency requirements.","explanation":"A workload may issue relatively few logical updates while the storage layer performs additional metadata updates, journal writes, copy-on-write work, synchronization, or other physical operations. The useful lesson is to measure the relevant layers rather than infer storage cost from application write counts alone.","principle":"Optimize measured write amplification while preserving required durability and correctness semantics.","rule":"Do not reduce storage synchronization or durability work solely for speed unless the resulting durability semantics are explicitly acceptable.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary storage work without weakening required correctness guarantees.","requirements":["Measure logical write activity and relevant storage behavior.","Identify redundant or unavoidable write work.","Preserve required durability and consistency semantics."],"constraints":["Storage behavior depends on the filesystem, database, runtime, storage device, and configuration.","Application-level measurements may not expose physical write behavior directly."],"preconditions":["A reproducible workload exists.","The required durability semantics are known."],"input":"A workload that performs persistent updates.","output":"A measured characterization of logical-to-storage write amplification and candidate interventions.","expected_behavior":"Performance changes are evaluated using workload evidence and durability requirements rather than write-count assumptions.","failure_mode":"Reducing synchronization or persistence work causes unacceptable data-loss exposure.","cause":"An optimization changed durability behavior instead of only removing redundant work.","root_cause":"Performance was optimized without treating durability semantics as a hard constraint.","solution":"Separate optional redundant work from required durability operations and validate both performance and durability behavior.","alternative":"Retain stronger durability semantics and optimize batching, data layout, or redundant-update elimination instead.","tradeoff":"Lower write amplification may require additional memory, batching latency, or more complex data handling.","edge_case":"A workload dominated by small durable writes may respond differently from a workload dominated by large sequential writes.","security_implication":"Durability changes can affect the reliability of security-relevant audit or state records.","performance_implication":"Write amplification can increase latency, I/O utilization, and queueing under sustained load.","test_case":"Compare representative workloads while measuring logical writes, persistence latency, and relevant storage activity.","test_input":"Equivalent workloads using baseline and optimized persistence paths.","expected_test_result":"The optimized path reduces unnecessary work without violating required durability behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test both normal and failure-interruption scenarios.","verification_method":"Review measured storage behavior and independently verify durability semantics.","verification_guidance":"Treat performance improvement and durability preservation as separate acceptance criteria.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B71-R02","D56-B71-R05"],"observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no benchmark or operational measurement is attached.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Storage Write Performance","subtopic":"Durability Synchronization","concept":"Synchronous durability operations can impose latency because completion may depend on persistence guarantees.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What performance tradeoff exists when synchronous durability is required?","answer":"Synchronous durability can increase write latency and reduce throughput relative to less synchronous persistence strategies, but removing or weakening it changes failure semantics. The correct lesson is to optimize around the required durability contract rather than silently weakening it.","explanation":"Persistence synchronization is not merely an implementation detail when the application promises durable completion. Performance analysis must distinguish required durability from optional synchronization or redundant persistence work.","principle":"A performance optimization must not silently change the durability contract.","rule":"Treat durability guarantees as an explicit requirement during persistence optimization.","objective":"Improve persistence performance while preserving the intended failure semantics.","requirements":["Document required durability behavior.","Measure persistence latency under representative workloads.","Validate behavior under interruption or failure."],"constraints":["Storage devices and persistence layers differ in synchronization behavior.","Durability requirements may prevent otherwise attractive latency optimizations."],"failure_mode":"A faster write path reports completion before the required persistence guarantee is satisfied.","cause":"Durability semantics were treated as an implementation detail.","root_cause":"Performance goals were optimized independently from correctness requirements.","solution":"Model durability as an explicit acceptance criterion and optimize batching, layout, or redundant work around it.","alternative":"Use asynchronous persistence only where the application contract explicitly permits it.","tradeoff":"Stronger durability can increase latency and reduce peak throughput; weaker guarantees may reduce latency while increasing recovery exposure.","edge_case":"A system may have different durability requirements for transactional state, cache data, and telemetry.","security_implication":"Security audit records may require stronger persistence guarantees than disposable telemetry.","performance_implication":"Synchronous persistence can become a critical path under high write concurrency.","test_case":"Interrupt or fail the persistence path at controlled points and verify the documented durability contract.","test_input":"Writes issued under the required durability mode followed by controlled interruption.","expected_test_result":"Observed recovery behavior remains consistent with the documented durability contract.","testing_guidance":"Do not use a latency-only benchmark as evidence that a durability optimization is safe.","verification_method":"Failure-injection testing plus inspection of persistence semantics.","verification_guidance":"Verify both latency characteristics and post-failure state.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B71-R01","D56-B71-R03"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Storage Write Performance","subtopic":"Write Coalescing","concept":"Combining compatible small writes can reduce repeated overhead when batching is compatible with latency and durability requirements.","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can write coalescing improve storage performance?","answer":"Write coalescing can help when many small compatible updates incur repeated fixed overhead and the application can tolerate bounded batching delay. The optimization should be validated against latency, memory, ordering, failure, and durability requirements.","explanation":"Coalescing changes several small operations into fewer larger operations. This may reduce per-operation overhead, but it can also increase waiting time, memory consumption, or failure scope.","principle":"Use bounded write coalescing when its reduced fixed overhead outweighs its added latency and complexity.","rule":"Bound coalescing by time, size, or both, and preserve required ordering and durability semantics.","objective":"Reduce redundant per-write overhead without creating unbounded latency.","requirements":["Define acceptable batching delay.","Preserve required update ordering.","Validate failure behavior for grouped writes."],"constraints":["Not all writes can be safely combined.","Batching can increase latency for the first item in a group."],"failure_mode":"An oversized or unbounded write batch creates latency spikes or excessive memory usage.","cause":"The optimization prioritized throughput without bounding accumulation.","root_cause":"No explicit latency or memory constraint was included in the batching design.","solution":"Use bounded coalescing with explicit size and time limits.","alternative":"Keep individual writes when low latency or independent failure handling is more important.","tradeoff":"Fewer storage operations may improve throughput while increasing individual-item waiting time and batch complexity.","edge_case":"A sparse workload may rarely fill the batch, causing little benefit while still adding timer-based delay.","security_implication":"Combining security-sensitive updates must not reorder or merge records in a way that weakens audit integrity.","performance_implication":"The benefit depends on workload composition and the fixed overhead per storage operation.","test_case":"Sweep bounded batch size and maximum wait time using representative workloads.","test_input":"Small-write workload with multiple batch configurations.","expected_test_result":"A selected configuration satisfies latency and correctness constraints while demonstrating evidence of reduced redundant work.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate both bursty and sparse traffic.","verification_method":"Measure end-to-end latency and persistence behavior while checking ordering and failure semantics.","verification_guidance":"Do not assume larger batches are faster across all workloads.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B63-R01","D56-B71-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B71-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Storage Write Performance","subtopic":"Write Optimization Failure","concept":"Removing persistence operations without classifying their semantic purpose can convert a performance optimization into a correctness defect.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is a dangerous storage performance optimization?","answer":"A dangerous optimization is deleting, weakening, or reordering persistence operations merely because they appear expensive without determining why they exist and what guarantees they provide.","explanation":"Storage operations may implement durability, ordering, transactional integrity, crash recovery, or audit guarantees. Their cost alone is insufficient evidence that they are unnecessary.","principle":"Understand the semantic purpose of expensive persistence work before attempting to remove it.","rule":"Every removed or weakened persistence operation requires an explicit correctness and durability justification.","objective":"Prevent performance work from introducing silent correctness or recovery failures.","requirements":["Identify the semantic purpose of the operation.","Document the changed guarantee.","Test normal and failure paths."],"constraints":["Some guarantees are difficult to validate through ordinary success-path tests.","Storage behavior can vary across platforms and configurations."],"failure_mode":"The system appears faster in normal execution but loses or corrupts state after interruption.","cause":"Persistence semantics were changed without failure-path validation.","root_cause":"The optimization treated measured cost as evidence of unnecessary work.","solution":"Classify persistence operations by semantic role before optimizing them.","alternative":"Optimize surrounding computation, batching, layout, or redundant updates instead.","tradeoff":"Preserving strong guarantees may leave some unavoidable performance cost.","edge_case":"A persistence operation may be redundant for one data class but mandatory for another.","security_implication":"Removing durable security records can weaken forensic or audit evidence.","performance_implication":"Unnecessary persistence work should be optimized, but required work remains a constrained cost.","test_case":"Compare normal completion and interrupted execution before and after the proposed optimization.","test_input":"Equivalent persistent workload with controlled interruption.","expected_test_result":"Required state and guarantees remain intact after interruption.","testing_guidance":"Include crash-recovery and partial-write scenarios where applicable.","verification_method":"Failure-path testing and semantic review.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence for every weakened persistence guarantee.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B71-R02","D56-B71-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B71-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Storage Write Performance","subtopic":"Validation","concept":"Storage-write optimizations require controlled comparison because workload composition can otherwise be mistaken for an intervention effect.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should a storage-write performance intervention be validated?","answer":"Run controlled baseline and intervention workloads with equivalent data, workload composition, persistence semantics, and environmental conditions, then compare relevant latency, throughput, resource, and correctness measurements.","explanation":"Storage performance is sensitive to workload shape and environment. A controlled comparison reduces the risk of attributing natural variation or workload changes to the optimization.","principle":"Performance evidence must isolate the intervention from major confounding variables.","rule":"Do not classify a storage optimization as effective from an uncontrolled before-and-after comparison.","objective":"Determine whether the intervention produces a reproducible improvement under defined conditions.","requirements":["Stable workload definition.","Baseline and intervention configurations.","Measurements covering performance and correctness."],"constraints":["Storage measurements may include external device or filesystem effects.","Reproducibility can be difficult on shared systems."],"preconditions":["The baseline behavior is measurable.","The intervention can be enabled or disabled deterministically."],"input":"Baseline and optimized storage configurations.","output":"A comparative evidence record with measurements and validation results.","expected_behavior":"Differences are attributable to the intervention only to the extent supported by the experimental design.","failure_mode":"An apparent improvement disappears when workload composition or environment is controlled.","cause":"The original comparison contained uncontrolled variables.","root_cause":"The experiment did not isolate the intervention.","solution":"Use controlled workloads and repeatable measurement conditions.","alternative":"Use a randomized or repeated experimental design when environmental variation cannot be eliminated.","tradeoff":"Stronger experimental controls increase test effort and infrastructure requirements.","edge_case":"Storage caches may make repeated runs behave differently from cold-start runs.","security_implication":"Test data must not expose sensitive persistent records.","performance_implication":"Warm-cache and cold-cache behavior may require separate measurements.","test_case":"Execute repeated baseline and intervention runs under equivalent conditions.","test_input":"Representative storage workload with controlled persistence settings.","expected_test_result":"The evidence clearly distinguishes observed measurements from assumptions or estimates.","testing_guidance":"Record environment, workload version, configuration, and measurement method.","verification_method":"Review experiment metadata and reproduce selected runs where feasible.","verification_guidance":"Store raw measurements or references to them when available.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R05","D56-B71-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Filesystem Performance","subtopic":"Metadata Operations","concept":"Filesystem metadata operations can become a bottleneck when workloads perform very large numbers of file creation, lookup, rename, or deletion operations.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can filesystem metadata become a performance bottleneck?","answer":"A workload that performs many metadata operations can spend significant time on directory traversal, lookup, allocation, journaling, synchronization, or other filesystem work even when the payload data volume is modest.","explanation":"File-oriented workloads can be limited by operation count rather than transferred bytes. Performance analysis should therefore measure operation rates and latency, not only aggregate data throughput.","principle":"Measure metadata-operation cost separately from payload I/O.","rule":"Do not infer filesystem performance solely from bytes per second when metadata operations dominate the workload.","objective":"Identify and reduce excessive metadata work where the data model permits it.","requirements":["Measure metadata-heavy operations.","Characterize directory and file-count behavior.","Preserve required filesystem semantics."],"constraints":["Filesystem behavior varies by operating system, filesystem, storage medium, and workload.","Changing file layout can affect operational tooling and compatibility."],"input":"A file-intensive workload.","output":"A characterization of metadata-related performance behavior.","expected_behavior":"Performance analysis identifies whether metadata operations materially contribute to observed latency.","failure_mode":"Increasing storage bandwidth does not resolve latency caused by metadata operations.","cause":"The workload is operation-count limited rather than payload-throughput limited.","root_cause":"Measurement focused only on transferred bytes.","solution":"Measure operation counts and metadata latency, then consider layout or operation-count reductions.","alternative":"Use fewer larger files or an indexed storage system when compatible with requirements.","tradeoff":"Reducing file count can improve metadata efficiency but may complicate partitioning, access, or lifecycle management.","edge_case":"Large directories may exhibit different behavior from hierarchical layouts depending on filesystem implementation.","security_implication":"Changing file organization must preserve access-control boundaries and audit expectations.","performance_implication":"Metadata-heavy workloads can remain slow even when sequential data throughput is high.","test_case":"Compare equivalent workloads with different file-layout strategies.","test_input":"Same logical dataset represented using multiple file organization schemes.","expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies whether metadata operations materially affect the workload.","testing_guidance":"Include both small-file and larger-file cases where relevant.","verification_method":"Measure metadata operation latency and aggregate workload latency.","verification_guidance":"Record filesystem and operating-system versions as part of provenance.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B72-R02","D56-B72-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Filesystem Performance","subtopic":"File Count Scaling","concept":"A large number of small files can increase filesystem metadata and lifecycle overhead.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What performance lesson applies to workloads containing many small files?","answer":"When a workload creates or accesses very large numbers of small files, measure file-count-dependent metadata costs and consider storage layouts that reduce operation count without sacrificing required access, isolation, or lifecycle semantics.","explanation":"Many-small-file workloads can generate substantial metadata operations relative to payload size. Consolidation can reduce operation count, but it may introduce larger reads, more complicated indexing, or coarser update granularity.","principle":"Choose a storage representation based on workload access patterns rather than payload size alone.","rule":"Evaluate file-count scaling when small-file workloads grow substantially.","objective":"Prevent metadata operation count from becoming an unrecognized scalability constraint.","requirements":["Characterize file creation, lookup, update, and deletion patterns.","Measure behavior at representative file counts.","Preserve required access and lifecycle semantics."],"constraints":["Consolidation may increase update or retrieval granularity.","External tools may depend on individual files."],"failure_mode":"Performance degrades as file count grows despite stable average file size.","cause":"Metadata operation volume scales with file count.","root_cause":"Storage layout was selected without evaluating file-count scaling.","solution":"Benchmark representative file counts and consider consolidation or alternative layouts when justified.","alternative":"Retain individual files and optimize indexing, directory structure, or access patterns where supported.","tradeoff":"Fewer files may reduce metadata work while increasing data movement or indexing complexity.","edge_case":"Workloads requiring independent retention or access-control decisions may resist consolidation.","security_implication":"Consolidation must not accidentally merge data with different authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Metadata overhead can become dominant before payload throughput reaches device limits.","test_case":"Measure representative workloads across increasing file counts.","test_input":"Equivalent datasets at multiple file-count scales.","expected_test_result":"The relationship between file count and performance is characterized rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":"Separate cold and warm filesystem-cache conditions when relevant.","verification_method":"Compare operation latency and end-to-end workload latency across scales.","verification_guidance":"Keep workload composition constant while varying file count.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B72-R01","D56-B76-R02"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Filesystem Performance","subtopic":"Directory Layout","concept":"Filesystem layout can influence metadata lookup behavior and operational scalability, so directory organization should be evaluated using the target filesystem and workload.","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should directory layouts be evaluated for performance?","answer":"Compare candidate layouts using the actual filesystem, access pattern, file-count range, and lifecycle operations rather than assuming one directory organization is universally faster.","explanation":"Filesystem implementations differ, and application behavior may involve creation, enumeration, lookup, rename, and deletion. A layout that helps one workload can be neutral or harmful for another.","principle":"Filesystem-layout performance is workload- and implementation-dependent.","rule":"Treat layout changes as hypotheses requiring measurement on the target environment.","objective":"Select a layout that meets operational and performance requirements.","requirements":["Define representative access operations.","Test realistic file counts.","Capture relevant filesystem and platform versions."],"constraints":["Results may not generalize across filesystems or operating systems.","Changing layout may affect operational procedures."],"failure_mode":"A layout optimization based on another platform provides no benefit or worsens performance.","cause":"Evidence was transferred across incompatible environments.","root_cause":"Environment-specific behavior was treated as universal.","solution":"Benchmark candidate layouts on the target environment.","alternative":"Choose the layout primarily for correctness and operational simplicity when performance impact is immaterial.","tradeoff":"Optimizing layout may increase implementation complexity and maintenance cost.","edge_case":"Directory enumeration workloads may behave differently from point lookups.","security_implication":"Directory structure can interact with permissions and information-disclosure controls.","performance_implication":"Different access patterns can produce different metadata bottlenecks.","test_case":"Evaluate point lookup, enumeration, creation, rename, and deletion operations under representative scale.","test_input":"Multiple candidate directory layouts with equivalent logical data.","expected_test_result":"The selected layout is supported by environment-specific measurements.","testing_guidance":"Avoid mixing filesystem changes with unrelated application changes during the experiment.","verification_method":"Controlled benchmark with documented environment metadata.","verification_guidance":"Record workload and platform assumptions with the result.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B72-R01","D56-B72-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B72-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Filesystem Performance","subtopic":"Enumeration Cost","concept":"Operations that enumerate large file sets can create latency and resource spikes even when individual lookups are inexpensive.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can unrestricted filesystem enumeration cause performance problems?","answer":"Unrestricted enumeration can process large numbers of entries, consume CPU and memory, increase metadata I/O, and delay unrelated work. Use bounded, incremental, indexed, or filtered enumeration where requirements permit.","explanation":"A point lookup and a full directory scan have different scaling behavior. Systems often encounter performance problems when a maintenance or request path unexpectedly enumerates an entire large collection.","principle":"Avoid placing unbounded collection-wide filesystem enumeration on latency-sensitive paths.","rule":"Bound or isolate large enumeration operations unless full traversal is an explicit requirement.","objective":"Prevent large collection scans from causing latency or resource contention.","requirements":["Define enumeration scope.","Bound result size or work where possible.","Schedule heavy scans outside latency-sensitive paths when feasible."],"constraints":["Some operations genuinely require complete enumeration.","Incremental approaches require state management."],"failure_mode":"A single request or maintenance task scans an unexpectedly large collection.","cause":"Enumeration scope was not bounded.","root_cause":"Collection size growth was not treated as a performance constraint.","solution":"Use pagination, incremental scanning, filtering, indexing, or asynchronous processing as appropriate.","alternative":"Retain full enumeration but isolate it from critical request paths and monitor its resource use.","tradeoff":"Bounded enumeration reduces individual work but may require multiple passes or persistent scan state.","edge_case":"A newly deployed system may appear fast because its collection is small, masking future scaling behavior.","security_implication":"Enumeration should respect authorization and avoid exposing unauthorized metadata.","performance_implication":"Enumeration cost generally grows with the amount of metadata that must be inspected.","test_case":"Increase collection size while keeping the requested subset constant and measure response behavior.","test_input":"Collections with progressively larger entry counts.","expected_test_result":"The system remains within defined latency and resource constraints or explicitly reports bounded behavior.","testing_guidance":"Include worst-case and empty-result scans.","verification_method":"Measure work performed, latency, CPU, and memory during enumeration.","verification_guidance":"Check that bounds are enforced independently of caller behavior.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B72-R01","D56-B72-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B72-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Filesystem Performance","subtopic":"Scaling Validation","concept":"Filesystem performance lessons should be validated at representative data and metadata scales.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a filesystem performance validation include?","answer":"Validation should vary the dimensions that drive filesystem work, including file count, file size, access pattern, cache state, and relevant lifecycle operations, while keeping other variables controlled.","explanation":"A benchmark using only one small dataset can hide metadata and scaling effects. Representative validation should exercise the dimensions that determine actual filesystem work.","principle":"Validate the scaling dimensions that drive the suspected bottleneck.","rule":"Do not generalize a filesystem performance result beyond the tested workload and environment without supporting evidence.","objective":"Establish the scope and limits of a filesystem performance lesson.","requirements":["Document workload dimensions.","Record filesystem and operating-system versions.","Repeat representative measurements."],"constraints":["Cross-platform generalization requires additional evidence.","Storage-device variability may affect results."],"input":"A suspected filesystem bottleneck and candidate intervention.","output":"A scoped validation result describing tested conditions and limitations.","expected_behavior":"Performance conclusions remain tied to observed evidence.","failure_mode":"A result from a narrow benchmark is applied universally.","cause":"Test scope was not documented.","root_cause":"Validation lacked explicit applicability boundaries.","solution":"Record environment, workload, scale, cache conditions, and measurement methodology.","alternative":"Mark unsupported dimensions as UNKNOWN rather than extrapolating.","tradeoff":"Broader validation costs more test time but reduces unsupported generalization.","edge_case":"A filesystem optimization may reverse behavior at a larger scale.","security_implication":"Synthetic test data should preserve relevant authorization and isolation properties without exposing real sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Metadata and cache behavior can change materially with scale.","test_case":"Run controlled tests across representative file counts and access patterns.","test_input":"Multiple filesystem workload scales.","expected_test_result":"The validated performance lesson has explicit scope and limitations.","testing_guidance":"Retain raw measurements when possible.","verification_method":"Independent review of test scope and evidence.","verification_guidance":"Reject claims that exceed the tested environment without additional evidence.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B72-R01","D56-B72-R03"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Logging Performance","subtopic":"Synchronous Logging","concept":"Synchronous logging on latency-sensitive paths can add measurable work to request or transaction processing.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can synchronous logging affect performance?","answer":"When logging performs formatting, serialization, I/O, synchronization, or flushing directly on a latency-sensitive path, the logging operation becomes part of that path and can contribute to latency and resource contention.","explanation":"Logging is often treated as ancillary work, but synchronous logging executes as part of the caller's critical path. The actual impact depends on log volume, formatting cost, destinations, buffering, and flush behavior.","principle":"Measure logging as part of the execution path when it is synchronous.","rule":"Do not assume logging is performance-neutral merely because the logged message is small.","objective":"Understand and control logging overhead without losing required observability.","requirements":["Measure logging overhead under representative volume.","Preserve required security and audit records.","Define acceptable logging latency and loss behavior."],"constraints":["Some logs must remain synchronous for correctness or audit reasons.","Logging behavior varies by runtime and logging backend."],"preconditions":["The application emits logs during representative workload execution."],"input":"Application workload with configured logging.","output":"A measured characterization of logging contribution to workload performance.","expected_behavior":"Logging cost is visible in performance analysis and separated from unrelated work.","failure_mode":"Increasing log volume causes unexpected latency or resource saturation.","cause":"Logging overhead scales with message volume or processing complexity.","root_cause":"Logging was excluded from the performance model.","solution":"Measure log formatting, queuing, I/O, and synchronization costs and optimize non-critical logging paths.","alternative":"Use asynchronous or buffered logging where the loss and ordering semantics are acceptable.","tradeoff":"Asynchronous logging can reduce caller latency but introduces buffering, ordering, delivery, and loss considerations.","edge_case":"A logging backend outage can cause blocking if the application waits synchronously for delivery.","security_implication":"Security and audit logs may require stronger delivery guarantees than diagnostic logs.","performance_implication":"High-volume logging can consume CPU, memory, I/O bandwidth, and synchronization capacity.","test_case":"Measure representative workloads with logging enabled at baseline and controlled reduced-volume configurations.","test_input":"Equivalent workload with different logging configurations.","expected_test_result":"The contribution of logging overhead is characterized without treating estimates as measurements.","testing_guidance":"Include normal operation and logging-destination failure scenarios.","verification_method":"Compare critical-path latency and resource utilization with controlled logging changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that required audit events remain preserved.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B73-R02","D56-B73-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B73-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Logging Performance","subtopic":"Log Volume","concept":"Excessive log volume can consume shared compute and I/O resources and may become a system-level performance constraint.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can excessive logging become a performance bottleneck?","answer":"High log volume can consume CPU for formatting and encoding, memory for buffering, network or storage bandwidth, and downstream processing capacity. Log volume should therefore be treated as a workload dimension.","explanation":"Logging overhead can propagate beyond the application process. A high-volume producer can overload collectors, storage systems, indexing pipelines, or network links, creating secondary latency.","principle":"Treat observability volume as workload rather than free metadata.","rule":"Set and monitor logging-volume expectations for high-throughput paths.","objective":"Prevent diagnostic output from becoming an uncontrolled resource consumer.","requirements":["Define expected log volume.","Monitor logging resource consumption.","Provide appropriate filtering or level controls."],"constraints":["Reducing logs may reduce diagnostic detail.","Audit requirements can constrain filtering."],"failure_mode":"A logging increase causes resource contention in application or observability infrastructure.","cause":"Log volume exceeded capacity assumptions.","root_cause":"Observability traffic was not included in capacity planning.","solution":"Measure log volume, filter non-essential messages, aggregate repeated events, or buffer appropriately.","alternative":"Increase observability capacity when the information is genuinely required.","tradeoff":"Lower log volume reduces overhead but can reduce troubleshooting detail.","edge_case":"An incident can cause a feedback loop where failures generate more logs, which further increases resource pressure.","security_implication":"Log reduction must not remove mandatory security or audit evidence.","performance_implication":"Logging can consume multiple resource classes simultaneously.","test_case":"Generate representative high-volume events and observe application and logging-pipeline resource behavior.","test_input":"Controlled workload with increasing diagnostic event rates.","expected_test_result":"Resource behavior remains within defined limits or controlled degradation is observed and documented.","testing_guidance":"Test failure storms because log volume may increase during incidents.","verification_method":"Correlate log volume with CPU, memory, I/O, network, and latency measurements.","verification_guidance":"Verify that filtering rules preserve mandatory event classes.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B73-R01","D56-B73-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B73-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Logging Performance","subtopic":"Log Aggregation","concept":"Aggregating repeated diagnostic events can reduce per-event overhead when individual event granularity is not required.","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can log aggregation reduce performance overhead?","answer":"Aggregation can reduce repeated formatting, transmission, and storage work when many equivalent events can be summarized without losing required diagnostic, security, or audit information.","explanation":"Repeated identical or highly similar events may not need one complete downstream record per occurrence. Aggregation can reduce volume, but the design must preserve counts, time ranges, identifiers, and other information required for investigation.","principle":"Reduce redundant observability work while preserving information required for diagnosis and accountability.","rule":"Aggregate only event classes for which loss of individual records is explicitly acceptable.","objective":"Reduce observability overhead without destroying useful evidence.","requirements":["Define which fields must survive aggregation.","Preserve counts and relevant temporal context.","Keep mandatory audit events individually represented where required."],"constraints":["Aggregation can make individual-event investigation harder.","Security or compliance requirements may prohibit aggregation."],"failure_mode":"Aggregation removes information needed to reconstruct an incident.","cause":"Information-retention requirements were not defined before optimization.","root_cause":"Observability volume was optimized without considering investigative requirements.","solution":"Define retention semantics and preserve required identifiers and counts.","alternative":"Retain raw events while reducing formatting or transport overhead elsewhere.","tradeoff":"Aggregation reduces volume but can reduce event-level visibility.","edge_case":"High-cardinality identifiers may prevent effective aggregation.","security_implication":"Security events may require individual evidence for accountability or forensic analysis.","performance_implication":"Aggregation may reduce CPU, network, and storage pressure when events are highly repetitive.","test_case":"Compare raw and aggregated logging for an equivalent repeated-event workload.","test_input":"Controlled repeated diagnostic events.","expected_test_result":"Resource use is reduced only where required diagnostic information remains available.","testing_guidance":"Attempt incident reconstruction from the aggregated representation.","verification_method":"Compare retained information against defined observability requirements.","verification_guidance":"Validate both performance characteristics and investigative completeness.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B73-R02","D56-B74-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B73-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Logging Performance","subtopic":"Failure Amplification","concept":"Logging can amplify an incident when failures generate large numbers of additional diagnostic events.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can logging amplify a performance incident?","answer":"A failure that produces repeated exceptions, retries, or diagnostic messages can increase logging work precisely when application resources are already constrained, creating additional CPU, I/O, network, or collector pressure.","explanation":"Observability is intended to help during failures, but unbounded failure logging can become part of the failure workload. Systems should distinguish useful incident evidence from uncontrolled repetition.","principle":"Failure-path observability must remain bounded enough to avoid materially worsening resource exhaustion.","rule":"Design failure logging with rate, volume, or aggregation controls where appropriate.","objective":"Maintain useful diagnostics during incidents without creating an observability-driven resource spiral.","requirements":["Define failure logging limits.","Preserve high-value security and audit events.","Test behavior under repeated failures."],"constraints":["Aggressive suppression can hide important evidence.","Different event classes require different retention guarantees."],"failure_mode":"A failure storm causes logging resources to saturate and further degrades service.","cause":"Failure logging volume scales with failure frequency without effective bounds.","root_cause":"Failure-path observability was not capacity-tested.","solution":"Use controlled aggregation, sampling, suppression, or bounded queues while preserving mandatory evidence.","alternative":"Allocate dedicated observability capacity and isolate application resources.","tradeoff":"Suppression lowers overhead but may reduce diagnostic detail.","edge_case":"A novel security incident may produce previously unseen event patterns that bypass assumed aggregation rules.","security_implication":"Security events should have explicit retention and priority semantics so optimization does not erase critical evidence.","performance_implication":"Failure-induced log volume can consume resources needed for recovery.","test_case":"Generate controlled failure bursts and observe application and observability behavior.","test_input":"Increasing rates of equivalent failures.","expected_test_result":"Diagnostic controls prevent uncontrolled resource amplification while retaining required evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test both application recovery and observability recovery.","verification_method":"Measure resource consumption and retained event coverage during failure injection.","verification_guidance":"Verify high-priority events remain available under saturation.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B73-R02","D56-B48-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B73-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Logging Performance","subtopic":"Performance Attribution","concept":"Logging overhead should be isolated experimentally rather than inferred from total request latency.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can logging overhead be isolated during performance analysis?","answer":"Compare controlled configurations that differ primarily in logging behavior while keeping workload, application logic, environment, and other observability features stable.","explanation":"Total latency includes many components. Removing or changing several observability mechanisms simultaneously prevents reliable attribution to logging itself.","principle":"Change the smallest relevant variable needed to isolate logging contribution.","rule":"Do not attribute an observed latency change to logging when multiple major variables changed simultaneously.","objective":"Produce defensible evidence about logging overhead.","requirements":["Stable workload.","Controlled logging configuration.","Consistent environment and application version."],"constraints":["Removing logging can itself change timing and buffering behavior.","Some logging is mandatory and cannot be disabled."],"input":"Baseline logging configuration and controlled intervention configuration.","output":"An attribution result scoped to the tested logging change.","expected_behavior":"The measured difference is attributable to the logging intervention to the extent supported by the experiment.","failure_mode":"A logging optimization appears effective because unrelated workload or environment variables changed.","cause":"Uncontrolled experimental design.","root_cause":"No isolated baseline was established.","solution":"Use controlled comparisons and repeat runs.","alternative":"Use instrumentation or profiling that directly measures logging stages.","tradeoff":"More controlled experiments require additional test time and infrastructure.","test_case":"Run equivalent workload under baseline and controlled logging configurations.","test_input":"Representative request mix with stable application version.","expected_test_result":"Logging-related performance differences are explicitly separated from unrelated changes.","testing_guidance":"Record log volume, level, destination, buffering, and environment.","verification_method":"Review experiment metadata and measurement outputs.","verification_guidance":"Require reproducible evidence before storing a claim as measured.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R05","D56-B73-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B74-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B74","topic":"Telemetry Performance","subtopic":"Instrumentation Overhead","concept":"Instrumentation itself consumes resources and can affect the behavior being measured.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why must instrumentation overhead be considered in performance analysis?","answer":"Instrumentation can add CPU work, memory usage, synchronization, allocations, I/O, and network traffic. Its overhead should therefore be characterized when measurements are sensitive to those costs.","explanation":"Observability changes the execution environment. Heavy tracing or profiling can alter latency and resource usage, so measurements should distinguish application behavior from measurement overhead.","principle":"Measurement mechanisms are part of the experimental environment and may influence results.","rule":"Consider instrumentation overhead when interpreting performance measurements.","objective":"Obtain performance evidence that accounts for measurement-induced effects.","requirements":["Document enabled instrumentation.","Estimate or measure its overhead where material.","Use consistent instrumentation between compared runs."],"constraints":["Some instrumentation is required for diagnosing a bottleneck.","Overhead varies by workload and instrumentation configuration."],"input":"A workload executed with specified instrumentation.","output":"Performance evidence scoped to the instrumentation configuration.","expected_behavior":"Performance conclusions identify relevant measurement overhead.","failure_mode":"An optimization appears necessary because instrumentation itself created the bottleneck.","cause":"Instrumentation cost was mistaken for application cost.","root_cause":"The measurement environment was not characterized.","solution":"Compare instrumented and minimally instrumented configurations when feasible and document the difference.","alternative":"Use lower-overhead instrumentation for broad measurements and detailed instrumentation for targeted diagnosis.","tradeoff":"More detailed observability can improve diagnosis while increasing measurement perturbation.","edge_case":"Rare code paths may experience disproportionately high tracing overhead.","security_implication":"Instrumentation can capture sensitive values; performance measurement must respect data-minimization and access controls.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation overhead can affect both throughput and latency measurements.","test_case":"Run representative workloads with controlled instrumentation levels.","test_input":"Equivalent workload under baseline and diagnostic instrumentation configurations.","expected_test_result":"The effect of instrumentation is characterized sufficiently to interpret results.","testing_guidance":"Avoid comparing measurements with materially different instrumentation unless explicitly accounted for.","verification_method":"Compare resource and latency measurements across instrumentation configurations.","verification_guidance":"Record instrumentation settings as part of benchmark provenance.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B74-R02","D56-B74-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B74-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B74","topic":"Telemetry Performance","subtopic":"Trace Sampling","concept":"Sampling tracing data can reduce observability overhead when complete event capture is unnecessary, but it changes the evidence available for analysis.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the performance tradeoff of trace sampling?","answer":"Sampling can reduce tracing CPU, memory, storage, and transport overhead, but it may omit rare events or reduce statistical coverage. Sampling policy must therefore match the diagnostic objective.","explanation":"A trace sample is not equivalent to complete tracing. Sampling may be appropriate for high-volume routine traffic while targeted capture is needed for rare failures or security-sensitive events.","principle":"Choose sampling based on the evidence required, not only the overhead that can be saved.","rule":"Do not treat sampled telemetry as complete population evidence.","objective":"Control telemetry overhead while retaining sufficient evidence for the intended analysis.","requirements":["Define the sampling policy.","Document what populations can be omitted.","Preserve required security and audit events through appropriate mechanisms."],"constraints":["Rare events may be missed.","Different sampling policies produce different observability coverage."],"failure_mode":"A sampled trace set fails to contain the rare behavior responsible for a performance incident.","cause":"Sampling was optimized for volume without considering event rarity.","root_cause":"Evidence requirements were not linked to the sampling strategy.","solution":"Use targeted or adaptive capture for relevant populations and explicitly label sampled evidence.","alternative":"Increase sampling coverage for diagnostic windows or retain separate high-value event streams.","tradeoff":"Higher sampling improves coverage while increasing resource and storage cost.","edge_case":"Uniform sampling can underrepresent rare but high-impact events.","security_implication":"Security events may require dedicated non-sampled audit mechanisms.","performance_implication":"Sampling generally reduces telemetry processing and transport work relative to capturing every event, but the exact reduction is workload-dependent.","test_case":"Compare observability coverage and resource cost across defined sampling policies.","test_input":"Equivalent workload containing common and rare event classes.","expected_test_result":"The chosen policy meets defined evidence requirements within resource constraints.","testing_guidance":"Include rare-event detection scenarios.","verification_method":"Measure resource overhead and evaluate event coverage against known injected events.","verification_guidance":"Label results as sampled and retain the sampling policy with the evidence.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B74-R01","D56-B74-R03"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B74-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B74","topic":"Telemetry Performance","subtopic":"High-Cardinality Metadata","concept":"High-cardinality telemetry dimensions can increase processing, indexing, memory, and storage costs.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can high-cardinality telemetry affect performance?","answer":"Telemetry systems may incur additional processing and storage costs when each event contains dimensions with many distinct values, especially when those dimensions are indexed, aggregated, or propagated across systems.","explanation":"High-cardinality identifiers can be operationally useful but expensive for downstream telemetry systems. Performance analysis should consider both producer overhead and consumer/indexing behavior.","principle":"Telemetry dimensions should be selected with both diagnostic value and resource cost in mind.","rule":"Treat high-cardinality telemetry as an explicit capacity dimension rather than free metadata.","objective":"Preserve useful diagnostic dimensions without uncontrolled telemetry-resource growth.","requirements":["Identify high-cardinality dimensions.","Measure producer and downstream costs where relevant.","Define acceptable cardinality or indexing behavior."],"constraints":["Removing dimensions can reduce diagnostic usefulness.","Telemetry backends differ substantially in their handling of cardinality."],"failure_mode":"Adding a unique identifier to every event causes telemetry processing or storage pressure.","cause":"Cardinality increased beyond backend capacity assumptions.","root_cause":"Dimension cost was not included in telemetry design.","solution":"Measure cardinality, limit unnecessary dimensions, and use appropriate indexing or aggregation strategies.","alternative":"Retain high-cardinality fields only in raw event storage while excluding them from expensive aggregations where supported.","tradeoff":"Lower indexed cardinality can reduce resource cost but may limit query flexibility.","edge_case":"A dimension may be low-cardinality during normal operation but become high-cardinality during an incident.","security_implication":"Identifiers in telemetry may reveal sensitive information and require access controls or redaction.","performance_implication":"Cardinality can affect memory, indexing, query, storage, and transport workloads.","test_case":"Increase the number of distinct telemetry dimension values while holding event rate approximately constant.","test_input":"Telemetry workloads with controlled cardinality changes.","expected_test_result":"The relationship between cardinality and resource behavior is characterized.","testing_guidance":"Test both producer and backend behavior where feasible.","verification_method":"Measure event-processing latency, memory, storage growth, and query behavior.","verification_guidance":"Do not extrapolate backend behavior across different telemetry systems without evidence.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B74-R01","D56-B74-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B74-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B74","topic":"Telemetry Performance","subtopic":"Observability Overhead Failure","concept":"Adding observability without capacity controls can shift a performance bottleneck from the application into the telemetry pipeline.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is an observability-related performance anti-pattern?","answer":"A common anti-pattern is enabling increasingly detailed instrumentation, tracing, and telemetry without accounting for producer overhead, transport volume, collector capacity, storage growth, or query costs.","explanation":"Observability is a distributed workload. An application-level optimization can be masked by telemetry overhead, and a diagnostic configuration can create new resource constraints.","principle":"Treat observability as an engineered subsystem with explicit resource budgets.","rule":"Do not enable high-cost telemetry globally without understanding its resource and evidence implications.","objective":"Prevent observability from becoming an uncontrolled source of resource consumption.","requirements":["Define telemetry budgets or limits where appropriate.","Measure producer and backend overhead.","Provide controlled diagnostic modes."],"constraints":["Incident diagnosis may require temporarily higher observability.","Some compliance or security telemetry is mandatory."],"failure_mode":"Telemetry resources saturate and begin affecting the system being observed.","cause":"Observability volume or processing cost exceeded available capacity.","root_cause":"Telemetry was not included in performance and capacity planning.","solution":"Use controlled sampling, buffering, filtering, isolation, or dedicated capacity as appropriate.","alternative":"Reduce diagnostic detail for routine traffic and increase it selectively for targeted investigations.","tradeoff":"Lower observability overhead can reduce diagnostic detail.","edge_case":"A severe incident may simultaneously increase workload and telemetry volume.","security_implication":"Performance controls must not disable mandatory security monitoring without explicit authorization.","performance_implication":"Telemetry can consume CPU, memory, network, storage, and backend query capacity.","test_case":"Stress both application workload and telemetry volume to identify resource interactions.","test_input":"Controlled application workload with escalating observability intensity.","expected_test_result":"Resource limits and degradation behavior remain within documented bounds.","testing_guidance":"Test failure and incident modes, not only steady state.","verification_method":"Observe application and telemetry resource metrics under controlled load.","verification_guidance":"Verify mandatory telemetry remains available under pressure.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B74-R01","D56-B74-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B74-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B74","topic":"Telemetry Performance","subtopic":"Measurement Validation","concept":"Telemetry performance lessons should include both measurement overhead and evidence-coverage validation.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should telemetry performance changes be validated?","answer":"Validate both resource effects and whether the resulting telemetry still contains the information required for the intended diagnostic or audit task.","explanation":"Reducing telemetry overhead is not a complete success if the resulting data can no longer answer the questions it was intended to support. Performance and evidence coverage are separate acceptance dimensions.","principle":"Optimize telemetry jointly for resource cost and information sufficiency.","rule":"A telemetry optimization should not be accepted solely because it lowers resource usage.","objective":"Ensure lower telemetry overhead does not silently destroy required observability.","requirements":["Define required evidence coverage.","Measure telemetry resource cost.","Test representative diagnostic tasks."],"constraints":["Evidence requirements vary by use case.","Some diagnostic coverage cannot be evaluated solely through aggregate metrics."],"input":"Baseline and optimized telemetry configurations.","output":"A scoped assessment of resource cost and evidence coverage.","expected_behavior":"The optimized configuration satisfies explicit observability requirements while controlling resource use.","failure_mode":"Resource usage falls but required rare-event or audit evidence is lost.","cause":"Only telemetry volume was measured.","root_cause":"Evidence coverage was not included in acceptance criteria.","solution":"Add diagnostic-task and event-coverage tests to performance validation.","alternative":"Keep higher-cost telemetry for populations where evidence coverage is critical.","tradeoff":"Greater coverage can require more compute, storage, or network resources.","edge_case":"Rare events require targeted tests because ordinary workloads may not exercise them.","security_implication":"Mandatory security telemetry must remain available and protected.","performance_implication":"The optimal configuration balances observability value against resource overhead.","test_case":"Inject known diagnostic events and compare their detection and traceability under baseline and optimized telemetry.","test_input":"Representative workload containing common and rare events.","expected_test_result":"Required events remain observable while measured overhead satisfies defined constraints.","testing_guidance":"Record sampling and filtering policies with the result.","verification_method":"Review resource measurements and evidence-coverage results.","verification_guidance":"Do not classify unsupported coverage as verified.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B74-R02","D56-B74-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B75-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B75","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Lookup Structure Choice","concept":"Data structure choice can dominate performance when the workload performs many repeated lookups, insertions, deletions, or membership checks.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can data structure choice become a performance lesson?","answer":"Select data structures based on the operations and workload distribution that matter to the application, then measure representative behavior. Asymptotic complexity provides useful reasoning but does not replace workload-specific validation.","explanation":"Arrays, hash-based structures, trees, heaps, and other structures have different operation characteristics and memory behavior. The best choice depends on access patterns, mutation patterns, key distribution, ordering requirements, and actual implementation costs.","principle":"Choose data structures according to required operations and measured workload behavior.","rule":"Do not select a data structure from a single complexity label without considering workload and implementation constraints.","objective":"Align data representation with dominant workload operations.","requirements":["Identify dominant operations.","Define ordering and mutation requirements.","Measure representative workloads where performance is material."],"constraints":["Changing representation can affect memory use, ordering, serialization, and compatibility.","Language runtime implementations differ."],"input":"A workload and candidate data structures supporting its required operations.","output":"A workload-scoped data-structure selection supported by complexity reasoning and evidence.","expected_behavior":"The selected structure satisfies functional requirements and is appropriate for measured workload characteristics.","failure_mode":"A theoretically favorable structure performs poorly for the actual workload.","cause":"Constant factors, locality, memory behavior, or workload distribution dominate the expected benefit.","root_cause":"Theoretical complexity was treated as a complete performance model.","solution":"Combine complexity analysis with representative measurement and memory considerations.","alternative":"Retain the simpler structure when measured performance is adequate.","tradeoff":"A specialized structure may improve a dominant operation while increasing memory usage or implementation complexity.","edge_case":"Very small collections may not benefit from a more complex structure despite better asymptotic complexity.","security_implication":"Data-structure changes must preserve authorization, isolation, and sensitive-data handling semantics.","performance_implication":"Lookup complexity, memory locality, allocation behavior, and constant factors can all affect observed performance.","test_case":"Compare candidate structures using realistic operation mixes and collection sizes.","test_input":"Equivalent logical workload represented using candidate structures.","expected_test_result":"The result identifies the workload and scale where each candidate is appropriate.","testing_guidance":"Include small, medium, and large representative sizes.","verification_method":"Combine complexity review with controlled benchmarks and memory measurements.","verification_guidance":"Store the tested language/runtime version with benchmark evidence.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B75-R02","D56-B76-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B75-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B75","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Memory Locality","concept":"A data structure with favorable theoretical complexity can still perform differently because of memory locality, allocation behavior, and representation overhead.","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should memory behavior be considered when choosing a data structure?","answer":"Observed performance depends on more than operation complexity. Memory layout, locality, allocation frequency, object indirection, cache behavior, and representation size can influence the cost of repeated operations.","explanation":"Two structures with similar asymptotic operation complexity can have different runtime behavior because their representations interact differently with the processor and memory subsystem.","principle":"Complexity analysis describes scaling behavior but not every runtime cost.","rule":"Consider memory representation and access locality when performance is sensitive to data-structure choice.","objective":"Avoid incomplete performance reasoning based solely on asymptotic complexity.","requirements":["Understand the dominant access pattern.","Consider representation and allocation behavior.","Measure when the distinction is performance-critical."],"constraints":["Low-level memory behavior varies by language and runtime.","Detailed hardware measurements may not be available in every environment."],"input":"Candidate data structures with equivalent logical semantics.","output":"A performance analysis incorporating both complexity and representation effects.","expected_behavior":"The selection rationale distinguishes theoretical and measured evidence.","failure_mode":"An expected complexity advantage does not appear in practice.","cause":"Constant factors or memory behavior dominate at the tested scale.","root_cause":"The model omitted relevant implementation costs.","solution":"Profile and benchmark representative operations and collection sizes.","alternative":"Use the simpler structure if evidence does not justify added complexity.","tradeoff":"Improving locality or reducing indirection can increase implementation complexity or restrict flexibility.","edge_case":"A structure may become advantageous only beyond a workload-specific crossover size.","security_implication":"Representation changes must not expose or duplicate sensitive data unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Memory access patterns can materially affect runtime behavior.","test_case":"Measure equivalent workloads across collection sizes and operation mixes.","test_input":"Candidate structures with identical logical content.","expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies whether representation differences materially affect the target workload.","testing_guidance":"Record runtime and hardware context when making low-level observations.","verification_method":"Controlled benchmark with profiling where available.","verification_guidance":"Classify hardware-specific observations as scoped evidence rather than universal facts.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B75-R01","D56-B76-R02"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B75-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B75","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Ordering Requirements","concept":"Choosing an unordered structure for speed can be invalid when deterministic ordering is part of the required behavior.","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can ordering requirements constrain data-structure optimization?","answer":"A data structure that provides efficient membership or lookup operations may not provide the ordering, iteration semantics, or deterministic behavior required by the application. Performance optimization must preserve those functional requirements.","explanation":"Data-structure selection is not purely a speed problem. Ordering may be required for reproducibility, user-visible output, serialization, testing, or downstream processing.","principle":"Functional and determinism requirements constrain permissible performance optimizations.","rule":"Do not replace an ordered representation with an unordered one unless the required ordering semantics are explicitly preserved elsewhere.","objective":"Avoid performance optimizations that change observable behavior.","requirements":["Identify whether ordering is part of the contract.","Define deterministic output requirements.","Validate downstream assumptions."],"constraints":["Maintaining ordering may add memory or processing cost.","Different languages and runtimes have different ordering guarantees."],"failure_mode":"A data-structure replacement changes iteration or serialization order and causes downstream failures.","cause":"Ordering was assumed to be incidental.","root_cause":"Functional requirements were not captured before optimization.","solution":"Document ordering requirements and choose a structure that satisfies them or add an explicit ordering mechanism.","alternative":"Use unordered storage internally and perform explicit sorting only at required boundaries.","tradeoff":"Deferred sorting may reduce internal constraints but adds boundary processing cost.","edge_case":"Tests may pass accidentally when an unordered structure happens to produce a stable order in one environment.","security_implication":"Ordering changes must not affect security policy evaluation or audit interpretation.","performance_implication":"Sorting can add work, while ordered data structures may have different update costs.","test_case":"Run equivalent workloads across supported environments and verify output ordering.","test_input":"Inputs that intentionally produce multiple possible iteration orders.","expected_test_result":"Required deterministic ordering remains stable and documented.","testing_guidance":"Do not infer guarantees from accidental runtime behavior.","verification_method":"Contract tests plus implementation documentation review.","verification_guidance":"Verify ordering against the actual supported language/runtime contract.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B75-R04","D56-B75-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B75-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B75","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Optimization Failure","concept":"Replacing a simple data structure with a more complex one without measured need can increase memory, maintenance, and correctness costs without meaningful performance benefit.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is a common data-structure performance anti-pattern?","answer":"Introducing a more complex or specialized data structure solely because it has a theoretically better complexity class can create unnecessary complexity when the actual workload is small or dominated by other costs.","explanation":"Complexity advantages matter only when the relevant operation count and scale make them material. More complex representations can increase memory usage, implementation burden, and bug surface.","principle":"Complexity improvements should be justified by workload requirements and evidence.","rule":"Do not replace a simpler structure solely from asymptotic reasoning when performance impact is unverified and operational cost is material.","objective":"Prevent complexity-driven optimization without demonstrated benefit.","requirements":["Identify the dominant bottleneck.","Estimate whether scale makes the difference material.","Measure when uncertainty remains significant."],"constraints":["Some workloads are too dynamic for stable assumptions.","A future scale increase may change the decision."],"failure_mode":"The new structure increases complexity without improving the target workload.","cause":"The presumed bottleneck was not dominant.","root_cause":"Optimization was selected without evidence about actual workload behavior.","solution":"Measure the current bottleneck and establish a decision threshold before introducing complexity.","alternative":"Retain the simpler structure and add monitoring for future scale changes.","tradeoff":"Simplicity can sacrifice theoretical scalability, while specialization can increase maintenance cost.","edge_case":"A structure may be unnecessary today but become justified after a known workload-scale transition.","security_implication":"Complexity can increase the chance of security defects if invariants become harder to maintain.","performance_implication":"Added indirection, memory use, or maintenance overhead can offset theoretical gains.","test_case":"Compare baseline and specialized structures under the actual workload range.","test_input":"Representative operation mix and collection sizes.","expected_test_result":"The optimization is adopted only if the measured or otherwise supported benefit justifies its added cost.","testing_guidance":"Include maintenance and correctness tests, not only speed measurements.","verification_method":"Benchmark plus code-review assessment of complexity and invariants.","verification_guidance":"Record the workload assumptions that justify the choice.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B75-R01","D56-B75-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B75-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B75","topic":"Data Structure Performance","subtopic":"Selection Validation","concept":"Data-structure performance claims should be tied to a defined workload, collection size, operation mix, and runtime environment.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is required to validate a data-structure performance lesson?","answer":"Validation should document the workload, operation mix, collection sizes, language/runtime, candidate structures, measurement method, and relevant correctness constraints.","explanation":"A data structure can perform differently across languages, runtimes, scales, and operation distributions. Without these dimensions, a measured result is difficult to interpret or reproduce.","principle":"Performance evidence must include enough context to establish its applicability.","rule":"Do not store an environment-specific data-structure benchmark as a universal performance fact.","objective":"Preserve reproducible and appropriately scoped data-structure knowledge.","requirements":["Record workload and operation mix.","Record collection-size range.","Record language/runtime and relevant environment details."],"constraints":["Cross-runtime comparisons require equivalent semantics and careful experimental design.","Hardware-specific behavior may limit generalization."],"input":"Data-structure benchmark or analysis.","output":"A scoped performance lesson with explicit applicability boundaries.","expected_behavior":"Consumers can determine whether the evidence applies to their workload.","failure_mode":"A benchmark result is reused for a different workload without justification.","cause":"Context was omitted from the knowledge record.","root_cause":"Provenance and workload metadata were incomplete.","solution":"Store benchmark context and label unsupported generalizations as UNKNOWN.","alternative":"Use qualitative complexity reasoning when no measured evidence is available.","tradeoff":"More metadata increases record size but improves retrieval and reasoning quality.","edge_case":"A result may be stable across several scales but still fail outside the tested operation mix.","security_implication":"Benchmark datasets should avoid sensitive production data.","performance_implication":"Operation mix and scale can dominate observed data-structure performance.","test_case":"Reproduce the benchmark under the documented workload and environment.","test_input":"Equivalent candidate structures and operation distribution.","expected_test_result":"The recorded observation can be independently checked within its stated scope.","testing_guidance":"Preserve raw benchmark outputs when possible.","verification_method":"Reproduction or independent review of benchmark methodology.","verification_guidance":"Mark unexecuted examples as illustrative rather than measured.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B75-R01","D56-B75-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B76-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B76","topic":"Algorithmic Performance","subtopic":"Complexity Versus Constants","concept":"Asymptotic complexity and constant-factor costs answer different performance questions and should be considered together.","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is asymptotic complexity not sufficient for every performance decision?","answer":"Asymptotic complexity describes how resource requirements scale with input size, while constant factors, fixed overhead, memory behavior, and implementation details influence actual cost at finite sizes. Both should inform performance reasoning.","explanation":"An algorithm with a better asymptotic bound may be slower for the input sizes actually encountered if it has substantially larger fixed or constant costs. Conversely, a simple algorithm may stop scaling acceptably beyond a workload-specific crossover point.","principle":"Use complexity analysis to reason about scaling and measurement to characterize finite-workload behavior.","rule":"Do not convert asymptotic superiority directly into an observed-performance claim.","objective":"Make algorithm-selection decisions using both scaling behavior and workload-specific evidence.","requirements":["Define realistic input sizes.","Identify dominant operations.","Measure when competing algorithms have uncertain crossover behavior."],"constraints":["Measurements depend on implementation and environment.","Complexity models may omit important resource dimensions."],"input":"Candidate algorithms and workload size distribution.","output":"A scoped algorithm-selection rationale.","expected_behavior":"The decision distinguishes theoretical scaling from observed runtime behavior.","failure_mode":"A theoretically superior algorithm is slower for the target workload.","cause":"Constant factors or implementation overhead dominate at the tested sizes.","root_cause":"Theoretical complexity was treated as a runtime benchmark.","solution":"Measure representative sizes and identify crossover regions.","alternative":"Use the simpler algorithm where tested sizes remain below a justified crossover threshold.","tradeoff":"Choosing for present workload may require reassessment as input scale changes.","edge_case":"An algorithm can have multiple crossover regions under different hardware or data distributions.","security_implication":"Algorithm replacement must preserve security properties and input-validation behavior.","performance_implication":"Complexity and constants jointly shape practical performance.","test_case":"Benchmark candidate algorithms across representative input sizes.","test_input":"Equivalent workloads at multiple scales.","expected_test_result":"The performance relationship is characterized over the relevant workload range.","testing_guidance":"Avoid reporting a single size as representative without workload evidence.","verification_method":"Complexity review plus controlled benchmark.","verification_guidance":"Record implementation and environment details for measured claims.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B75-R01","D56-B76-R02"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B76-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B76","topic":"Algorithmic Performance","subtopic":"Crossover Point","concept":"Competing algorithms may have workload-specific crossover points where one becomes preferable to another.","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is an algorithmic crossover point?","answer":"A crossover point is a workload range at which the relative cost of two candidate algorithms changes. It should be established through analysis or measurement rather than assumed from complexity notation alone.","explanation":"An algorithm with higher fixed cost may be preferable for large inputs while a simpler algorithm may perform better for small inputs. The exact transition depends on implementation, environment, and workload characteristics.","principle":"Algorithm choice can be conditional on workload size and composition.","rule":"Represent algorithm recommendations with their tested or reasoned applicability range.","objective":"Avoid applying one algorithm indiscriminately across incompatible workload scales.","requirements":["Define candidate algorithms.","Define input-size and workload dimensions.","Measure or reason about relevant transition points."],"constraints":["Crossover behavior may change across runtimes and hardware.","Multiple workload dimensions can prevent a single threshold from being sufficient."],"input":"Two or more candidate algorithms.","output":"A workload-scoped comparison and applicability range.","expected_behavior":"Algorithm selection changes only when supported by evidence or explicit requirements.","failure_mode":"A fixed threshold becomes invalid after an implementation or workload change.","cause":"The threshold was stored without provenance or validity conditions.","root_cause":"The knowledge model omitted version and workload dependencies.","solution":"Store threshold evidence with environment, implementation, workload, and version metadata.","alternative":"Use adaptive measurement or conservative selection when the workload is highly variable.","tradeoff":"Adaptive selection adds runtime or implementation complexity.","edge_case":"Input distribution rather than maximum input size may determine the practical crossover.","security_implication":"Adaptive algorithm selection must not bypass security checks for certain input classes.","performance_implication":"Workload-sensitive selection can avoid unnecessary overhead when validated.","test_case":"Measure candidate algorithms across a range of representative inputs.","test_input":"Equivalent inputs spanning the target workload-size distribution.","expected_test_result":"The applicable range and uncertainty are explicitly recorded.","testing_guidance":"Repeat after significant runtime, compiler, or implementation changes.","verification_method":"Benchmark plus review of applicability conditions.","verification_guidance":"Mark unsupported crossover thresholds as UNKNOWN rather than fabricated estimates.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B76-R01","D56-B76-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B76-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B76","topic":"Algorithmic Performance","subtopic":"Input Distribution","concept":"Average input size alone can hide expensive tails or pathological distributions that determine real workload cost.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should input distribution be included in algorithm performance analysis?","answer":"Two workloads with the same average input size can have different performance if one contains a larger tail, skewed values, repeated worst-case patterns, or different operation mixes. Performance lessons should therefore characterize relevant distributions rather than only averages.","explanation":"Algorithmic cost often depends on input structure as well as size. Tail behavior can dominate latency-sensitive systems even when average inputs look benign.","principle":"Represent workload shape, not only aggregate averages, when evaluating algorithm performance.","rule":"Do not validate an algorithm using only average input size when tail or input structure materially affects cost.","objective":"Capture workload distributions that influence algorithmic behavior.","requirements":["Define relevant input dimensions.","Include representative tail cases.","Measure latency distribution where appropriate."],"constraints":["Production distributions may change over time.","Synthetic workloads can fail to reproduce pathological structures."],"input":"Candidate algorithms and representative input distributions.","output":"A workload-scoped performance characterization.","expected_behavior":"The evaluation identifies whether performance depends materially on input distribution.","failure_mode":"Average benchmark results look acceptable while rare input classes cause unacceptable latency.","cause":"Tail inputs were excluded from validation.","root_cause":"The workload model used averages without distributional coverage.","solution":"Include representative tail and pathological cases in performance testing.","alternative":"Use defensive bounds or specialized handling when pathological inputs are expected.","tradeoff":"Broader test coverage increases test effort but improves confidence about tail behavior.","edge_case":"Adversarially chosen inputs may intentionally target algorithmic worst cases.","security_implication":"Untrusted inputs can exploit expensive algorithmic paths for resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Tail behavior can dominate user-visible latency or resource consumption.","test_case":"Evaluate common, high-percentile, and pathological input shapes separately.","test_input":"Controlled input distributions including representative tails.","expected_test_result":"The performance envelope and limitations are documented.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarial cases where input is externally controlled.","verification_method":"Distribution-aware benchmark and worst-case analysis.","verification_guidance":"Separate measured tail behavior from theoretical worst-case claims.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B76-R04","D56-B69-R02"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B76-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B76","topic":"Algorithmic Performance","subtopic":"Algorithmic Complexity Abuse","concept":"An algorithm that is acceptable for trusted bounded inputs may become a resource-exhaustion risk when exposed to untrusted or adversarial inputs.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can algorithmic performance become a security concern?","answer":"If attackers can influence input size or structure, algorithms with expensive worst-case behavior can consume disproportionate CPU or memory and contribute to denial-of-service conditions. Security analysis should therefore consider adversarial input shapes.","explanation":"Performance and security overlap when resource consumption can be externally influenced. A workload that is harmless under normal inputs may become expensive under intentionally constructed inputs.","principle":"Treat externally controllable algorithmic resource consumption as part of the security boundary.","rule":"Evaluate algorithmic worst-case behavior and input bounds when untrusted inputs can control expensive operations.","objective":"Prevent algorithmic resource exhaustion from becoming an externally triggerable failure mode.","requirements":["Identify attacker-controlled input dimensions.","Define resource or size bounds where appropriate.","Test pathological input classes."],"constraints":["Strict bounds can reduce legitimate workload flexibility.","Not every theoretical worst case is practically reachable."],"input":"Externally influenced algorithm and input model.","output":"A scoped resource-exhaustion risk assessment and validation plan.","expected_behavior":"The system bounds or safely handles expensive input classes according to requirements.","failure_mode":"Adversarial input causes excessive CPU, memory, or latency consumption.","cause":"Expensive algorithmic behavior was reachable without effective controls.","root_cause":"Performance worst cases were excluded from threat modeling.","solution":"Use appropriate bounds, algorithms, quotas, timeouts, or workload isolation.","alternative":"Redesign the algorithm to provide more predictable resource behavior where feasible.","tradeoff":"Defensive controls can add validation overhead or reject some large inputs.","edge_case":"An input that is individually acceptable may become expensive when repeated concurrently.","security_implication":"Algorithmic complexity can directly affect availability.","performance_implication":"Protective bounds can improve resource predictability at the cost of some flexibility.","test_case":"Submit controlled pathological inputs within and beyond defined limits.","test_input":"Normal, large, and adversarial input structures.","expected_test_result":"Resource usage remains within documented safety bounds or the request is rejected predictably.","testing_guidance":"Include concurrency to test amplification.","verification_method":"Resource monitoring combined with boundary and negative tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify limits are enforced before excessive resource consumption occurs where practical.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B76-R03","D56-B76-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B76-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B76","topic":"Algorithmic Performance","subtopic":"Complexity Validation","concept":"Algorithm performance lessons should preserve the distinction between theoretical complexity, measured performance, and workload-specific inference.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should algorithmic performance claims be classified?","answer":"Theoretical complexity should be recorded as analytical knowledge, measured runtime as measured evidence, and workload-specific conclusions as scoped inference unless directly supported by measurement.","explanation":"These evidence types answer different questions. Mixing them can cause the knowledge fabric to present an analysis as though it were an observed benchmark.","principle":"Preserve evidence provenance and claim classification throughout performance learning.","rule":"Never convert a complexity argument into a measured performance claim without execution evidence.","objective":"Maintain evidence integrity in algorithm-performance knowledge.","requirements":["Classify the claim type.","Record benchmark context for measured results.","Preserve uncertainty where no execution evidence exists."],"constraints":["A theoretical result may be strong without being a benchmark.","Measured results remain environment-specific."],"input":"Algorithm analysis and/or benchmark evidence.","output":"A correctly classified performance knowledge record.","expected_behavior":"Consumers can distinguish fact, inference, estimate, and measurement.","failure_mode":"A generated or theoretical statement is stored as a measured result.","cause":"Evidence classification was omitted.","root_cause":"Knowledge ingestion collapsed distinct evidence states.","solution":"Require explicit performance claim classification and provenance fields.","alternative":"Store unsupported performance conclusions as UNKNOWN or INFERRED.","tradeoff":"Strict classification can reduce apparent certainty while improving reliability.","edge_case":"A benchmark may be executed successfully but still fail to represent the target production workload.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive performance conclusions should preserve their test scope and threat assumptions.","performance_implication":"Evidence quality directly affects the reliability of performance-driven automation.","test_case":"Inspect algorithm records for consistency between claim classification, execution status, and provenance.","test_input":"Records containing theoretical, measured, and illustrative claims.","expected_test_result":"No theoretical or illustrative record is incorrectly marked as measured.","testing_guidance":"Validate evidence fields automatically where possible.","verification_method":"Schema and semantic consistency checks.","verification_guidance":"Flag contradictions between execution status and performance claim classification.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B76-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B77-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Concurrency Model Performance","subtopic":"Execution Model Selection","concept":"Threads, asynchronous execution, and processes introduce different coordination and resource costs, so model selection should follow workload characteristics and runtime semantics.","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should concurrency models be compared for performance?","answer":"Compare concurrency models using the actual workload characteristics, including I/O waiting, CPU work, shared-state requirements, isolation needs, scheduling behavior, and runtime constraints. No concurrency model is universally optimal.","explanation":"Different concurrency models trade coordination overhead, isolation, memory use, scheduling behavior, and programming complexity differently. The relevant question is which costs dominate the target workload.","principle":"Select a concurrency model according to workload and correctness requirements rather than convention.","rule":"Do not claim one concurrency model is universally faster without workload-specific evidence.","objective":"Match execution-model characteristics to the dominant workload bottlenecks.","requirements":["Characterize CPU-bound and I/O-bound portions.","Define isolation and shared-state requirements.","Measure representative workloads when performance is material."],"constraints":["Language runtime semantics differ.","Changing the concurrency model can change correctness and failure behavior."],"input":"Workload and candidate concurrency models.","output":"A scoped comparison and selection rationale.","expected_behavior":"The selected model satisfies both functional and performance requirements.","failure_mode":"A concurrency-model change increases overhead or complexity without improving the target bottleneck.","cause":"The model was selected without measuring the dominant workload characteristic.","root_cause":"Concurrency choice was based on generic assumptions.","solution":"Profile the workload and compare candidate models under representative conditions.","alternative":"Retain the existing model if its performance satisfies requirements.","tradeoff":"Higher concurrency can improve utilization for some workloads while increasing coordination and resource overhead.","edge_case":"A mixed workload may require different execution strategies for different stages.","security_implication":"Concurrency-model changes must preserve isolation and authorization invariants.","performance_implication":"Coordination, scheduling, context switching, synchronization, and runtime behavior all contribute to total cost.","test_case":"Compare candidate models using equivalent workload and resource limits.","test_input":"Representative mixed workload.","expected_test_result":"The comparison identifies workload-specific performance characteristics without universal claims.","testing_guidance":"Include saturation and failure behavior.","verification_method":"Controlled benchmark plus concurrency-correctness testing.","verification_guidance":"Record runtime and environment versions.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B41-R01","D56-B77-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B77-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Concurrency Model Performance","subtopic":"Asynchronous I/O","concept":"Asynchronous I/O can reduce idle waiting in workloads with substantial I/O wait, but it does not automatically reduce CPU work or make every workload faster.","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What performance lesson applies to asynchronous I/O?","answer":"Asynchronous I/O can allow a runtime to make progress on other work while an I/O operation is waiting, but the total workload can remain CPU-bound or be limited by downstream capacity. Its benefit must be validated against the actual workload.","explanation":"Asynchronous execution changes how waiting is overlapped; it does not eliminate the underlying I/O or CPU work. Poorly matched workloads can gain little or incur additional coordination complexity.","principle":"Asynchronous execution primarily changes waiting and overlap behavior, not the underlying amount of required work.","rule":"Use asynchronous I/O when workload waiting and runtime semantics make overlap valuable, and validate the result.","objective":"Improve utilization or latency through appropriate overlap of independent I/O operations.","requirements":["Identify independent I/O operations.","Ensure the runtime and libraries support the intended asynchronous model.","Preserve cancellation and error semantics."],"constraints":["CPU-heavy work may still block the execution mechanism.","Some libraries may not provide truly asynchronous operations."],"input":"I/O-bound workload and asynchronous implementation.","output":"A workload-scoped assessment of asynchronous execution.","expected_behavior":"Independent waits can overlap when supported by the runtime and underlying I/O system.","failure_mode":"Async code adds complexity without meaningful performance improvement.","cause":"The workload is CPU-bound, serialized by dependencies, or limited by another resource.","root_cause":"The expected source of waiting was not measured.","solution":"Profile the workload before and after introducing asynchronous execution.","alternative":"Use synchronous execution when the workload and simplicity requirements make it sufficient.","tradeoff":"Async execution can improve overlap while increasing lifecycle, cancellation, debugging, and state-management complexity.","edge_case":"Blocking calls inside an asynchronous execution path can negate expected benefits.","security_implication":"Cancellation and timeout handling must not leave security-sensitive operations partially completed.","performance_implication":"The benefit depends on the amount of independent waiting that can actually overlap.","test_case":"Compare equivalent I/O workloads with controlled synchronous and asynchronous implementations.","test_input":"Representative workload with known I/O waits.","expected_test_result":"The experiment characterizes whether async execution improves the target performance metric.","testing_guidance":"Check for hidden blocking calls.","verification_method":"Profiling and controlled workload comparison.","verification_guidance":"Validate cancellation and error handling separately from performance.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B77-R01","D56-B77-R03"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B77-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Concurrency Model Performance","subtopic":"Process Isolation Cost","concept":"Process-based isolation can increase memory and lifecycle overhead compared with less isolated execution models.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What performance tradeoff can process isolation introduce?","answer":"Separate processes can provide stronger fault or resource isolation but may increase memory consumption, startup cost, inter-process communication, and lifecycle-management overhead. The tradeoff should be evaluated against the isolation requirement.","explanation":"Process isolation changes the resource and communication model. It can be valuable for correctness or security even when it adds performance cost.","principle":"Performance cost is one dimension of concurrency-model selection; isolation requirements remain independent constraints.","rule":"Do not remove required process isolation solely because it introduces measurable overhead.","objective":"Balance execution efficiency with required isolation guarantees.","requirements":["Define required isolation boundaries.","Measure process lifecycle and communication overhead where material.","Validate failure containment."],"constraints":["Process overhead varies by operating system and runtime.","Isolation requirements may be non-negotiable."],"input":"Workload requiring candidate isolation models.","output":"A scoped isolation/performance tradeoff assessment.","expected_behavior":"The selected model satisfies required isolation while remaining within performance constraints.","failure_mode":"An optimization removes isolation and creates cross-component failure or security exposure.","cause":"Performance was treated as the sole selection criterion.","root_cause":"Isolation requirements were not represented as hard constraints.","solution":"Keep required isolation and optimize communication, process lifetime, or workload placement instead.","alternative":"Use a less isolated model only when requirements explicitly permit it.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation can increase resource and communication overhead.","edge_case":"Short-lived processes can amplify lifecycle overhead if created at high frequency.","security_implication":"Process isolation may be a security boundary and cannot be weakened without explicit authorization.","performance_implication":"Inter-process communication and lifecycle management can become measurable costs.","test_case":"Measure candidate models while validating failure containment and isolation behavior.","test_input":"Equivalent workload deployed under different isolation models.","expected_test_result":"The comparison documents both performance and isolation outcomes.","testing_guidance":"Include abnormal termination of one component.","verification_method":"Performance benchmark plus isolation/failure-containment tests.","verification_guidance":"Keep security and performance results separate in the evidence record.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B77-R01","D56-B78-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B77-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Concurrency Model Performance","subtopic":"Mixed Workloads","concept":"A single concurrency model may be poorly matched to heterogeneous workload stages with different resource characteristics.","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should mixed workloads influence concurrency-model design?","answer":"Identify distinct workload stages and choose execution strategies according to their dominant constraints while preserving clear boundaries and controlled coordination.","explanation":"A workload may contain I/O-heavy ingestion, CPU-heavy transformation, and serialized persistence. Treating all stages identically can create avoidable contention or blocking.","principle":"Concurrency strategy can be stage-specific when workload characteristics differ materially.","rule":"Do not force heterogeneous stages into one execution model solely for architectural uniformity.","objective":"Align concurrency mechanisms with stage-specific bottlenecks.","requirements":["Identify workload stages.","Define communication and backpressure boundaries.","Measure stage-level behavior."],"constraints":["Multiple execution models increase operational complexity.","Cross-stage coordination can become a new bottleneck."],"input":"A heterogeneous processing pipeline.","output":"A scoped concurrency architecture based on stage characteristics.","expected_behavior":"Each stage uses a model compatible with its dominant workload and correctness constraints.","failure_mode":"A CPU-heavy stage blocks an I/O-oriented execution mechanism or vice versa.","cause":"Heterogeneous work was executed under one mismatched model.","root_cause":"Stage-level workload analysis was omitted.","solution":"Separate stages where justified and define explicit coordination and resource limits.","alternative":"Use one model when measurements show it satisfies all stages adequately.","tradeoff":"Stage specialization can improve resource matching but increases architectural complexity.","edge_case":"Small workloads may not justify multiple execution mechanisms.","security_implication":"Stage separation must preserve data isolation and authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Poor model matching can create hidden blocking or resource contention.","test_case":"Measure stage-level utilization and latency under representative mixed workloads.","test_input":"Pipeline containing I/O-heavy and CPU-heavy stages.","expected_test_result":"The architecture avoids unintended blocking while remaining within resource limits.","testing_guidance":"Include backpressure and overload behavior.","verification_method":"Stage-level profiling and end-to-end benchmark.","verification_guidance":"Validate correctness independently of performance measurements.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B77-R01","D56-B77-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B77-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Concurrency Model Performance","subtopic":"Model Selection Validation","concept":"Concurrency-model changes require simultaneous validation of performance, correctness, cancellation, failure handling, and resource behavior.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should a concurrency-model performance change be validated?","answer":"Use equivalent workloads and compare target performance metrics while also testing synchronization, cancellation, error propagation, resource limits, and failure behavior.","explanation":"A concurrency model changes execution and lifecycle semantics. A performance improvement is insufficient if it introduces races, stuck work, resource leaks, or incorrect cancellation behavior.","principle":"Concurrency performance validation must include behavioral correctness and lifecycle safety.","rule":"Never accept a concurrency optimization based solely on throughput or latency.","objective":"Verify that performance gains do not compromise concurrency correctness or lifecycle behavior.","requirements":["Equivalent workload.","Defined performance metrics.","Concurrency correctness tests.","Failure and cancellation tests."],"constraints":["Race conditions may be timing-dependent.","Performance tests may not expose rare concurrency defects."],"input":"Baseline and candidate concurrency implementations.","output":"A combined performance and correctness validation result.","expected_behavior":"The candidate meets performance requirements without introducing known concurrency defects.","failure_mode":"Performance improves while rare races or resource leaks are introduced.","cause":"Testing focused only on aggregate performance.","root_cause":"Concurrency correctness was treated as separate from optimization acceptance.","solution":"Combine performance tests with stress, cancellation, failure, and correctness validation.","alternative":"Use formal or deterministic concurrency reasoning for critical components in addition to runtime tests.","tradeoff":"Broader validation increases engineering effort but reduces regression risk.","edge_case":"A defect may appear only under high concurrency or specific timing.","security_implication":"Concurrency defects can cause authorization or isolation violations.","performance_implication":"Concurrency itself introduces coordination and lifecycle costs that must be measured.","test_case":"Run repeated concurrent workloads with injected cancellation and component failures.","test_input":"Representative workload at multiple concurrency levels.","expected_test_result":"Performance and correctness remain within defined acceptance criteria.","testing_guidance":"Repeat tests to increase exposure to timing-dependent failures.","verification_method":"Stress testing, race analysis where available, and performance comparison.","verification_guidance":"Keep performance claim classification separate from correctness verification.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B77-R01","D56-B77-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B78-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Process Lifecycle Performance","subtopic":"Process Creation","concept":"Frequent process creation can introduce lifecycle overhead that becomes material when processes are short-lived or created at high frequency.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How can frequent process creation affect performance?","answer":"Creating and initializing processes can require operating-system and runtime work, memory setup, resource allocation, and communication setup. If performed frequently, this lifecycle cost can become a significant part of total workload time.","explanation":"Process creation is not free. A design that repeatedly creates short-lived workers may spend substantial effort on lifecycle management rather than useful computation.","principle":"Reuse long-lived execution resources when lifecycle overhead is material and reuse is safe.","rule":"Measure process lifecycle cost before optimizing by increasing or decreasing process reuse.","objective":"Identify whether process creation or teardown contributes materially to workload cost.","requirements":["Measure process creation and teardown frequency.","Characterize useful work per process lifetime.","Preserve isolation and cleanup requirements."],"constraints":["Long-lived processes can retain memory or stale state.","Isolation requirements may require process replacement."],"input":"A workload using repeated process creation.","output":"A characterization of lifecycle overhead and reuse tradeoffs.","expected_behavior":"Process lifecycle cost is separated from useful application work.","failure_mode":"Short-lived workers spend disproportionate effort in initialization and teardown.","cause":"Worker lifetime is too short relative to lifecycle cost.","root_cause":"Process lifecycle overhead was omitted from workload modeling.","solution":"Reuse workers when safe, increase useful work per lifetime, or redesign boundaries.","alternative":"Retain short-lived processes when isolation or fault containment requires them.","tradeoff":"Reuse can reduce lifecycle overhead while increasing state-management and fault-isolation complexity.","edge_case":"Long-lived processes may accumulate memory fragmentation, caches, or stale state.","security_implication":"Process reuse must not leak credentials, sensitive data, or authorization state between tasks.","performance_implication":"Lifecycle overhead becomes more significant as useful work per process decreases.","test_case":"Compare equivalent workloads using different worker lifetimes.","test_input":"Repeated tasks with controlled worker reuse policies.","expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies lifecycle contribution and relevant reuse constraints.","testing_guidance":"Monitor memory and state isolation during reuse.","verification_method":"Profile lifecycle events and compare end-to-end workload latency.","verification_guidance":"Validate cleanup and isolation separately from performance.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B78-R02","D56-B78-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B78-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Process Lifecycle Performance","subtopic":"Worker Reuse","concept":"Worker reuse can amortize lifecycle costs across multiple tasks but requires careful state isolation and resource management.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the tradeoff of reusing worker processes?","answer":"Reusing workers can reduce repeated lifecycle overhead, but persistent workers can accumulate state, memory, connections, caches, or failures. Reuse should therefore be bounded and validated against isolation and resource requirements.","explanation":"Worker reuse trades repeated startup/teardown work for longer-lived state. The performance benefit depends on task frequency and lifecycle cost, while correctness and resource behavior depend on cleanup quality.","principle":"Amortize lifecycle cost only when persistent worker state can be controlled safely.","rule":"Define explicit worker lifecycle and reset policies when reusing processes.","objective":"Capture lifecycle-efficiency benefits without allowing stale or contaminated state.","requirements":["Define worker reset semantics.","Bound worker lifetime where appropriate.","Monitor memory and resource retention."],"constraints":["Some workloads require strong process isolation.","Resetting state can itself add overhead."],"input":"Reusable worker architecture and task stream.","output":"A lifecycle policy balancing reuse and isolation.","expected_behavior":"Workers handle multiple tasks without unauthorized state leakage or uncontrolled resource growth.","failure_mode":"Persistent worker state affects later tasks or resource use grows over time.","cause":"State cleanup and lifecycle policy were insufficient.","root_cause":"Worker reuse was optimized without modeling persistent state.","solution":"Define cleanup, recycling, and maximum-lifetime policies.","alternative":"Use short-lived workers when isolation or state freshness dominates.","tradeoff":"More frequent recycling improves freshness and containment but increases lifecycle cost.","edge_case":"A worker may hold external resources that are difficult to reset reliably.","security_implication":"Sensitive state must not cross task or tenant boundaries through reused workers.","performance_implication":"Reuse can amortize lifecycle work but may increase memory retention and cleanup costs.","test_case":"Run diverse tasks through reused workers and inspect state isolation and resource growth.","test_input":"Sequential and mixed-task workloads using the same worker pool.","expected_test_result":"No prohibited state persists between tasks and resource behavior remains bounded.","testing_guidance":"Include tenant or privilege boundary transitions where relevant.","verification_method":"State-isolation tests plus long-duration resource monitoring.","verification_guidance":"Verify both reuse efficiency and cleanup behavior.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B78-R01","D56-B78-R03"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B78-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Process Lifecycle Performance","subtopic":"Container Lifecycle","concept":"Container creation and teardown can add lifecycle overhead, especially for workloads composed of many short-lived tasks.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What performance lesson applies to short-lived container workloads?","answer":"Containerized execution can introduce image, namespace, process, networking, storage, and runtime lifecycle work. For short-lived tasks, these fixed costs may become material relative to useful computation and should be measured.","explanation":"Container boundaries provide isolation and deployment benefits, but lifecycle cost depends on the runtime, image state, storage configuration, and task duration.","principle":"Evaluate container lifecycle cost relative to useful task duration and isolation requirements.","rule":"Do not assume container startup and teardown are negligible for very short tasks.","objective":"Identify lifecycle overhead in containerized task execution.","requirements":["Measure actual lifecycle stages relevant to the runtime.","Define required isolation properties.","Compare against representative task durations."],"constraints":["Container implementations and runtimes differ.","Isolation requirements may justify lifecycle cost."],"input":"Containerized short-lived workload.","output":"A scoped lifecycle-performance characterization.","expected_behavior":"Lifecycle overhead is identified as a separate workload component where material.","failure_mode":"Most task time is consumed by lifecycle work rather than useful computation.","cause":"Task duration is too short relative to container lifecycle cost.","root_cause":"Execution-unit granularity was selected without lifecycle measurement.","solution":"Increase task granularity, reuse appropriate workers, or choose a different execution boundary when requirements allow.","alternative":"Retain short-lived containers when isolation is the primary requirement.","tradeoff":"Longer-lived execution can improve efficiency but may increase state and isolation-management concerns.","edge_case":"Warm image or runtime state can materially change lifecycle behavior.","security_implication":"Reducing isolation boundaries can create security consequences and requires explicit authorization.","performance_implication":"Lifecycle overhead is workload-duration dependent and environment-specific.","test_case":"Compare container lifecycle overhead across representative task durations and runtime states.","test_input":"Short, medium, and longer tasks under controlled container conditions.","expected_test_result":"The contribution of lifecycle work is characterized without unsupported generalization.","testing_guidance":"Test both cold and warm lifecycle conditions when relevant.","verification_method":"Measure lifecycle stages and end-to-end task latency.","verification_guidance":"Record runtime, image, and environment metadata.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B78-R01","D56-B78-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B78-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Process Lifecycle Performance","subtopic":"Lifecycle Granularity","concept":"Execution-unit granularity affects how much useful work is available to amortize process or container lifecycle overhead.","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How does task granularity influence process or container performance?","answer":"If execution units are extremely small, fixed lifecycle costs can represent a larger share of total work. Larger units can amortize fixed costs but may increase latency, failure scope, memory retention, or scheduling imbalance.","explanation":"Lifecycle cost is partly fixed per execution unit. Granularity therefore creates a tradeoff between efficiency and responsiveness or isolation.","principle":"Choose execution-unit granularity according to lifecycle cost and latency/failure constraints.","rule":"Do not optimize solely for maximum reuse; preserve acceptable latency and failure isolation.","objective":"Find a bounded execution granularity appropriate to the workload.","requirements":["Define acceptable task latency.","Define failure and isolation boundaries.","Measure lifecycle and useful-work proportions."],"constraints":["Task granularity may be constrained by external contracts.","Large tasks can increase retry or failure scope."],"input":"Task stream and candidate execution granularities.","output":"A scoped granularity policy.","expected_behavior":"The chosen granularity balances fixed lifecycle cost with latency and isolation requirements.","failure_mode":"Tasks are too small to amortize lifecycle cost or too large to meet latency requirements.","cause":"Granularity was selected without considering competing constraints.","root_cause":"Fixed and variable costs were not modeled together.","solution":"Measure lifecycle contribution and evaluate bounded granularity options.","alternative":"Use a dynamic batching policy when workload variability justifies it.","tradeoff":"Larger units improve amortization but can increase tail latency and failure scope.","edge_case":"Highly variable task duration can make a single fixed granularity inappropriate.","security_implication":"Combining tasks must not cross authorization or tenant boundaries.","performance_implication":"Granularity influences lifecycle overhead, batching, queueing, and resource utilization.","test_case":"Sweep task granularity using representative workload distributions.","test_input":"Equivalent logical work partitioned into different execution-unit sizes.","expected_test_result":"A configuration is selected based on explicit latency, resource, and isolation criteria.","testing_guidance":"Include failure injection at task boundaries.","verification_method":"Controlled workload comparison plus failure-scope validation.","verification_guidance":"Record applicability boundaries and workload assumptions.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B78-R03","D56-B63-R01"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B78-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Process Lifecycle Performance","subtopic":"Lifecycle Validation","concept":"Lifecycle optimizations require validation that reduced creation or teardown work does not weaken isolation, cleanup, or failure containment.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should process or container lifecycle optimizations be validated?","answer":"Measure lifecycle and end-to-end performance while testing state cleanup, resource release, isolation, abnormal termination, and repeated execution over time.","explanation":"Lifecycle reuse can improve performance but introduces persistent state and resource-retention risks. Short benchmark runs may miss these effects.","principle":"Validate lifecycle efficiency together with long-lived correctness and isolation behavior.","rule":"Do not accept lifecycle reuse solely from short-run latency measurements.","objective":"Ensure lifecycle optimization remains safe and bounded over repeated execution.","requirements":["Repeated execution.","Resource monitoring.","Isolation tests.","Abnormal termination tests."],"constraints":["Long-duration validation costs more resources.","Some failures are timing-dependent."],"input":"Baseline and lifecycle-optimized execution configurations.","output":"A combined lifecycle-performance and correctness assessment.","expected_behavior":"The candidate maintains bounded resources and required isolation while meeting performance objectives.","failure_mode":"Short-run performance improves but long-run memory or state retention grows.","cause":"Persistent worker or container state was not observed over sufficient duration.","root_cause":"Validation focused on immediate execution cost.","solution":"Add long-duration, recycling, cleanup, and failure tests.","alternative":"Use bounded worker lifetimes and explicit reset policies.","tradeoff":"More frequent recycling increases lifecycle cost but can control persistent-state risks.","edge_case":"Rare failures may leave resources retained only after specific task sequences.","security_implication":"Repeated reuse must be tested for cross-task or cross-tenant state leakage.","performance_implication":"Lifecycle cost and persistent-state cost must both be included in the model.","test_case":"Run repeated task sequences with controlled worker reuse and injected failures.","test_input":"Long-running mixed workload with recycling policies.","expected_test_result":"Resource usage, isolation, and lifecycle behavior remain within defined bounds.","testing_guidance":"Inspect memory, file descriptors, connections, temporary data, and task state where applicable.","verification_method":"Long-duration stress testing and failure-path validation.","verification_guidance":"Separate observed performance results from unexecuted expectations.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B78-R01","D56-B78-R02"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B79-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Runtime Performance Stability","subtopic":"Warm-Up Effects","concept":"Some runtimes and execution environments exhibit warm-up behavior, so early measurements may not represent steady-state performance.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can warm-up affect performance measurements?","answer":"Runtime compilation, cache population, lazy initialization, memory allocation behavior, and other state transitions can make early executions differ from later executions. Measurement plans should explicitly distinguish cold, warm-up, and steady-state behavior when relevant.","explanation":"A benchmark that mixes initialization and steady-state execution can obscure the behavior of either phase. The relevant measurement regime depends on the application workload and runtime.","principle":"Define the execution phase being measured before interpreting performance results.","rule":"Do not combine cold-start and steady-state measurements as though they represent the same performance regime.","objective":"Produce phase-specific performance evidence.","requirements":["Define cold and warm measurement phases where applicable.","Document warm-up policy.","Keep comparison configurations consistent."],"constraints":["Some workloads are inherently short-lived and never reach steady state.","Warm-up behavior varies by runtime and workload."],"input":"Runtime workload with repeated execution.","output":"Phase-specific performance characterization.","expected_behavior":"Cold-start, warm-up, and steady-state behavior are distinguished where material.","failure_mode":"A warm-up improvement is incorrectly interpreted as a steady-state optimization.","cause":"Measurement phases were not separated.","root_cause":"Benchmark methodology did not define runtime state.","solution":"Measure and report distinct execution phases.","alternative":"Use only the phase relevant to the production workload and explicitly state the scope.","tradeoff":"Phase-specific benchmarks require more measurement design and data.","edge_case":"A serverless or short-lived workload may spend most of its lifetime in cold or warm-up phases.","security_implication":"Warm-up data should not cause sensitive information to persist unexpectedly in caches or compiled state.","performance_implication":"Phase composition can materially affect observed latency and throughput.","test_case":"Measure repeated executions and separate early and later observations.","test_input":"Equivalent workload executed from a controlled initial state.","expected_test_result":"Performance phases can be distinguished from each other.","testing_guidance":"Record runtime version, configuration, and initialization state.","verification_method":"Repeated controlled runs with phase labeling.","verification_guidance":"Do not mark steady-state claims as measured if only cold runs were observed.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B67-R01","D56-B79-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B79-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Runtime Performance Stability","subtopic":"Just-In-Time Compilation","concept":"Just-in-time compilation can change runtime behavior during execution, making benchmark results dependent on runtime state and workload history.","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"How should JIT-related performance effects be treated in performance lessons?","answer":"JIT-sensitive measurements should document runtime configuration, warm-up strategy, workload history, and measurement phase. Claims should remain scoped to the tested runtime and conditions.","explanation":"JIT systems may optimize code based on observed execution patterns. The resulting behavior can change during a run or after code/configuration changes.","principle":"Runtime optimization state is part of the measurement context.","rule":"Do not transfer JIT-sensitive measurements across materially different runtime configurations without supporting evidence.","objective":"Preserve accurate interpretation of runtime-dependent performance observations.","requirements":["Record runtime version and relevant optimization configuration.","Define warm-up methodology.","Repeat measurements sufficiently for the target workload."],"constraints":["JIT behavior can depend on workload history and runtime implementation.","Different environments may optimize differently."],"input":"JIT-enabled workload and measurement configuration.","output":"A runtime-scoped performance observation.","expected_behavior":"Measurements are interpreted according to documented runtime state and workload history.","failure_mode":"A benchmark result changes after a runtime or workload change even though source code is unchanged.","cause":"Runtime optimization behavior changed.","root_cause":"The knowledge record omitted runtime-state dependencies.","solution":"Record runtime version, configuration, workload, and measurement phase.","alternative":"Use execution modes with more stable behavior when reproducibility requirements dominate.","tradeoff":"More stable execution can sacrifice some runtime optimization opportunities.","edge_case":"Rare code paths may not receive the same runtime optimization as hot paths.","security_implication":"Runtime optimizations must not bypass security checks or alter required semantics.","performance_implication":"JIT state can affect both steady-state and transition performance.","test_case":"Repeat benchmark after controlled warm-up and across documented runtime configurations.","test_input":"Equivalent workload under specified runtime versions or modes.","expected_test_result":"Differences are documented as environment/version-specific rather than universal.","testing_guidance":"Keep warm-up and measurement windows explicit.","verification_method":"Controlled repeated benchmark and runtime metadata inspection.","verification_guidance":"Flag cross-version comparisons that lack equivalent runtime conditions.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B79-R01","D56-B79-R03"],"performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED"}
{"record_id":"D56-B79-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Runtime Performance Stability","subtopic":"Optimization Stability","concept":"A runtime optimization may depend on workload history, so changing request order or data distribution can change observed performance.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can workload history affect runtime performance?","answer":"Runtime caches, compiled paths, adaptive optimization, memory state, and other learned execution state can depend on previous operations. Performance experiments should therefore control or document workload history when it can materially affect results.","explanation":"Two runs with identical current inputs can behave differently if their prior execution history differs. This matters particularly when measuring adaptive runtimes or stateful caches.","principle":"Control relevant execution history when it influences the performance regime being measured.","rule":"Do not assume identical current inputs imply identical runtime state.","objective":"Reduce hidden state as a confounding variable in performance experiments.","requirements":["Define initial runtime state.","Control warm-up and preceding workload where material.","Repeat experiments with documented history."],"constraints":["Some real workloads naturally contain changing histories.","Fully resetting runtime state may be expensive."],"input":"Performance workload with potentially adaptive runtime behavior.","output":"A performance characterization with explicit history conditions.","expected_behavior":"Results are interpreted according to controlled or documented runtime history.","failure_mode":"Performance differs across runs because prior workload changed runtime state.","cause":"Execution history was uncontrolled.","root_cause":"The benchmark treated current inputs as the only workload variable.","solution":"Control, reset, or record relevant execution history.","alternative":"Use randomized run ordering and repeated trials to reduce systematic bias when reset is impractical.","tradeoff":"More controls increase benchmark complexity but improve attribution quality.","edge_case":"A production workload may have a continuously evolving state that cannot be reproduced exactly.","security_implication":"Stateful caches must not retain sensitive information across authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"History-dependent state can affect cache hits, compiled paths, memory behavior, and latency.","test_case":"Repeat equivalent workloads after different controlled warm-up histories.","test_input":"Same target workload with multiple documented preceding histories.","expected_test_result":"The influence of history is characterized rather than hidden.","testing_guidance":"Record state-reset methodology.","verification_method":"Controlled history comparison and repeated measurements.","verification_guidance":"Classify history-sensitive results as scoped observations.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B79-R01","D56-B79-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B79-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Runtime Performance Stability","subtopic":"Benchmark Instability","concept":"Uncontrolled runtime state can produce noisy or misleading performance comparisons.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is a runtime-state performance benchmarking anti-pattern?","answer":"A common anti-pattern is comparing a cold baseline with a warm optimized run, or otherwise allowing runtime state to differ materially between configurations while attributing the difference to the code change.","explanation":"Warm-up, caches, compilation state, memory state, and workload history can all affect performance. If they differ between compared configurations, attribution is weakened.","principle":"Comparable performance experiments require comparable execution state.","rule":"Keep runtime state and measurement phase consistent across baseline and intervention runs unless the state difference is itself the subject of the experiment.","objective":"Prevent runtime-state confounding in performance learning.","requirements":["Define state initialization.","Use equivalent warm-up policy.","Record runtime configuration."],"constraints":["Some state cannot be perfectly controlled in shared environments.","Cold-start and warm-state effects may both be operationally relevant."],"failure_mode":"An apparent optimization disappears when both configurations are measured under equivalent state.","cause":"Baseline and intervention states were not comparable.","root_cause":"Experiment design omitted runtime-state control.","solution":"Standardize state or explicitly test state transitions as separate dimensions.","alternative":"Report separate cold and warm performance instead of mixing them.","tradeoff":"More experiment dimensions increase test cost but improve evidence quality.","edge_case":"A production service may legitimately have a mixture of cold and warm requests.","security_implication":"State reset and warm-up procedures must not expose or retain sensitive data.","performance_implication":"State differences can dominate observed performance changes.","test_case":"Repeat baseline and intervention measurements using matched cold and warm conditions.","test_input":"Equivalent workloads under controlled state regimes.","expected_test_result":"Performance differences remain interpretable within each regime.","testing_guidance":"Do not pool incompatible regimes into one aggregate result.","verification_method":"Experimental-design review and repeated controlled measurements.","verification_guidance":"Reject unsupported causal attribution when state differs materially.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B79-R03","D56-B70-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B79-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Runtime Performance Stability","subtopic":"Phase-Aware Validation","concept":"Runtime performance lessons should state whether they describe cold, warm-up, steady-state, or transition behavior.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a runtime performance record state about execution phase?","answer":"A performance record should identify the execution phase represented by its evidence and avoid applying phase-specific results to other phases without supporting evidence.","explanation":"Cold-start, warm-up, steady-state, and transition costs can represent different operational realities. A single aggregate metric can obscure those distinctions.","principle":"Performance evidence must identify the runtime phase it represents.","rule":"Do not generalize a steady-state measurement to cold-start behavior or vice versa without evidence.","objective":"Preserve phase-specific performance knowledge.","requirements":["Identify measurement phase.","Document initialization and warm-up policy.","Record runtime and workload conditions."],"constraints":["Some applications have no meaningful steady-state phase.","Phase boundaries can be workload-dependent."],"input":"Runtime benchmark or operational measurement.","output":"A phase-labeled performance knowledge record.","expected_behavior":"Consumers can determine whether the evidence applies to their operational phase.","failure_mode":"A phase-specific optimization is deployed based on evidence from another phase.","cause":"Evidence phase was omitted.","root_cause":"Performance knowledge lacked phase metadata.","solution":"Require explicit phase classification and applicability boundaries.","alternative":"Store separate records for distinct operational phases.","tradeoff":"Phase-specific records increase dataset granularity but improve retrieval accuracy.","edge_case":"A short-lived workload may spend most of its lifetime transitioning rather than operating steadily.","security_implication":"Initialization and warm-up paths may handle credentials or sensitive configuration and require equivalent security validation.","performance_implication":"Phase composition can determine which optimization matters operationally.","test_case":"Validate the intervention separately across relevant runtime phases.","test_input":"Cold, warm-up, and steady-state execution conditions where applicable.","expected_test_result":"Each phase has appropriately scoped evidence.","testing_guidance":"Avoid averaging incompatible phases when the user-visible objective differs by phase.","verification_method":"Phase-aware benchmark review.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit phase metadata before storing a runtime performance claim as measured.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B79-R01","D56-B79-R04"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B80-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"Regression Gates","concept":"A performance lesson becomes more durable when its known failure condition is represented as an automated regression check.","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a performance lesson be converted into a durable engineering guardrail?","answer":"Translate a validated lesson into an explicit metric, workload, threshold or comparison rule, test procedure, and applicability scope that can be executed or reviewed during future changes.","explanation":"A lesson stored only as prose can be forgotten or interpreted inconsistently. A guardrail connects the lesson to a repeatable verification mechanism while preserving the conditions under which the lesson is valid.","principle":"Durable performance knowledge should be connected to reproducible verification where feasible.","rule":"Do not create an automated performance gate from an unverified or poorly scoped performance claim.","objective":"Convert validated performance knowledge into repeatable regression protection.","requirements":["A defined performance objective.","A reproducible workload or test method.","Evidence supporting the guardrail.","Explicit applicability conditions."],"constraints":["Performance tests can be noisy.","Thresholds may need environment-specific calibration."],"input":"A validated performance lesson and its evidence.","output":"A scoped performance guardrail definition.","expected_behavior":"Future changes can be evaluated against the documented lesson without inventing unsupported thresholds.","failure_mode":"A guardrail fails frequently because its threshold was arbitrary or its environment was unstable.","cause":"The gate was created without sufficient baseline evidence.","root_cause":"Knowledge was converted directly into a threshold without validation.","solution":"Derive guardrails from measured evidence and document their environment and tolerance assumptions.","alternative":"Use comparative regression detection without a fixed absolute threshold when appropriate.","tradeoff":"Strict gates improve regression detection but can increase false failures and maintenance cost.","edge_case":"A legitimate workload improvement may shift the baseline and require guardrail maintenance.","security_implication":"Performance gates must not encourage removal of security controls merely to satisfy latency thresholds.","performance_implication":"Automated guardrails can detect regressions earlier but are themselves part of test-system cost.","test_case":"Introduce a controlled performance regression and verify that the guardrail detects it.","test_input":"Baseline workload plus intentionally degraded implementation.","expected_test_result":"The guardrail identifies the regression within its documented sensitivity and scope.","testing_guidance":"Test both true regressions and acceptable variation.","verification_method":"Automated regression test with reviewed acceptance criteria.","verification_guidance":"Record whether the threshold is measured, estimated, or heuristic.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B70-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B80-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"Threshold Design","concept":"Performance thresholds should represent explicit requirements or evidence-supported variation rather than arbitrary numbers.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should performance regression thresholds be established?","answer":"Use documented requirements, measured baseline variation, statistical or engineering analysis, and environment scope to define thresholds. If sufficient evidence is unavailable, preserve the threshold as provisional rather than presenting it as a universal limit.","explanation":"An arbitrary threshold can create false failures or allow meaningful regressions. Threshold design is part of the evidence model and must remain traceable to its justification.","principle":"A performance gate is only as defensible as the evidence supporting its acceptance boundary.","rule":"Every nontrivial performance threshold should have a documented rationale and scope.","objective":"Prevent arbitrary performance gates from corrupting regression decisions.","requirements":["Known metric definition.","Measurement methodology.","Variation characterization or explicit requirement.","Environment scope."],"constraints":["Noise can vary across environments.","Requirements may specify a bound even when baseline data are limited."],"input":"Performance metric and candidate acceptance boundary.","output":"A scoped threshold with evidence classification and rationale.","expected_behavior":"The threshold can be traced to a requirement, measurement, or explicitly labeled heuristic.","failure_mode":"A threshold causes repeated false failures or misses meaningful regressions.","cause":"The acceptance boundary did not reflect actual variation or requirements.","root_cause":"Threshold selection lacked evidence.","solution":"Measure baseline variability and align thresholds with explicit requirements.","alternative":"Use trend or comparative analysis when an absolute threshold is not justified.","tradeoff":"Tighter thresholds improve sensitivity but may increase noise-related failures.","edge_case":"Different hardware classes may require different thresholds.","security_implication":"Thresholds must not incentivize bypassing security validation to recover performance.","performance_implication":"Poor thresholds reduce the practical value of automated performance regression detection.","test_case":"Evaluate the threshold against historical or repeated measurements including controlled regressions.","test_input":"Baseline variation plus known degraded cases.","expected_test_result":"The threshold separates acceptable variation from the intentionally introduced regression within its defined scope.","testing_guidance":"Recalibrate only with documented evidence.","verification_method":"Statistical or engineering review of threshold rationale and test behavior.","verification_guidance":"Mark unsupported thresholds as HEURISTIC or UNKNOWN rather than MEASURED.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B80-R01","D56-B80-R03"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B80-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"Regression Detection","concept":"Performance guardrails should distinguish meaningful regressions from ordinary measurement variation.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How should performance guardrails handle measurement noise?","answer":"A guardrail should account for known measurement variation and distinguish reproducible degradation from ordinary noise using an evidence-supported comparison method.","explanation":"Performance measurements vary because of workload differences, system state, scheduling, background activity, and measurement error. A gate that ignores this variation can become unstable.","principle":"Regression detection should be sensitive enough to detect meaningful changes without treating normal variance as failure.","rule":"Define the comparison method and noise assumptions explicitly.","objective":"Reduce false performance regressions while retaining meaningful detection sensitivity.","requirements":["Repeated or representative measurements.","Known comparison methodology.","Documented variation assumptions."],"constraints":["Noise characteristics can change across environments.","Reducing false positives can also reduce sensitivity."],"input":"Baseline and candidate performance observations.","output":"A regression decision scoped to the measurement method and environment.","expected_behavior":"The decision reflects documented evidence rather than a single noisy observation.","failure_mode":"A single anomalous run blocks an otherwise acceptable change.","cause":"No variation handling was included.","root_cause":"The gate treated one measurement as definitive.","solution":"Use repeated measurements, robust comparison methods, or controlled environments as appropriate.","alternative":"Use a review-required state for borderline results instead of automatic rejection.","tradeoff":"More measurements increase test time but improve decision reliability.","edge_case":"Rare tail regressions may be masked by aggregate averages.","security_implication":"Review paths must preserve security checks even when performance results are ambiguous.","performance_implication":"Tail-sensitive guardrails may need different metrics from average throughput or latency.","test_case":"Run repeated baseline and candidate measurements with known injected regressions and noise.","test_input":"Equivalent workload with controlled performance degradation.","expected_test_result":"The guardrail detects the meaningful degradation while tolerating documented normal variation.","testing_guidance":"Include both stable and noisy environments where relevant.","verification_method":"Repeated controlled benchmark and regression-classification review.","verification_guidance":"Record the statistical or engineering decision rule used.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B80-R01","D56-B80-R02"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B80-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Performance Knowledge Maintenance","subtopic":"Lesson Versioning","concept":"A performance lesson can become invalid when implementation, runtime, workload, infrastructure, or requirements change, so lessons require applicability and version metadata.","knowledge_type":"KNOWLEDGE_MAINTENANCE","question":"Why must performance lessons be version-aware?","answer":"Performance behavior is often dependent on implementation, runtime, hardware, workload, and configuration. A lesson should therefore record its applicability conditions and be revalidated when materially relevant dependencies change.","explanation":"A previously valid optimization may become neutral, harmful, or unnecessary after a runtime upgrade, architectural change, workload shift, or infrastructure change. Version-aware knowledge prevents stale evidence from being treated as current truth.","principle":"Performance knowledge is scoped knowledge, not timeless universal truth.","rule":"Trigger review when a performance lesson's relevant implementation, runtime, workload, or environment assumptions change materially.","objective":"Prevent stale performance knowledge from driving incorrect future decisions.","requirements":["Record relevant versions and environment assumptions.","Define conditions that invalidate or trigger review.","Preserve historical evidence rather than silently rewriting it."],"constraints":["Not every dependency change materially affects performance.","Revalidation can require significant benchmark effort."],"input":"Existing performance lesson with applicability metadata.","output":"A version-aware lesson with review status and evidence lineage.","expected_behavior":"Consumers can determine whether the lesson remains applicable or requires revalidation.","failure_mode":"An outdated performance lesson is reused after a material environment change.","cause":"No review trigger or version metadata existed.","root_cause":"Knowledge was stored without applicability boundaries.","solution":"Attach version and environment metadata and define revalidation triggers.","alternative":"Mark the lesson UNKNOWN or CONFLICTED until new evidence is available.","tradeoff":"Version-aware maintenance adds metadata and review work but reduces stale-knowledge risk.","edge_case":"A minor patch may have no measurable performance effect, while a runtime or architecture change may have a large effect.","security_implication":"Security-control performance lessons require review when security mechanisms or configurations change materially.","performance_implication":"Version drift can invalidate optimization assumptions.","test_case":"Change a documented dependency or workload condition and verify that the lesson is flagged for review.","test_input":"Knowledge record plus simulated material version/workload change.","expected_test_result":"The system identifies the applicability change without silently retaining unsupported certainty.","testing_guidance":"Preserve previous evidence as historical provenance.","verification_method":"Metadata validation and revalidation-trigger test.","verification_guidance":"Do not overwrite historical measurements when new evidence conflicts.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B79-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D56-B80-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Performance Knowledge Maintenance","subtopic":"Evidence-to-Automation Traceability","concept":"A durable performance knowledge record should connect its lesson to evidence, applicability conditions, validation method, and any automated guardrail without conflating those artifacts.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a performance lesson suitable for automated reuse?","answer":"A reusable performance lesson should preserve the observed or analytical claim, evidence classification, workload and environment scope, intervention, tradeoffs, validation method, and relationship to any regression guardrail.","explanation":"Automation requires more than a textual recommendation. The knowledge fabric must retain enough context to determine whether the lesson applies and how it should be verified.","principle":"Performance automation should be evidence-traceable and scope-aware.","rule":"Do not promote an unverified lesson into automated decision logic merely because it sounds plausible.","objective":"Make performance knowledge safe for retrieval, reasoning, code-generation guidance, and regression automation.","requirements":["Evidence classification.","Applicability metadata.","Validation method.","Explicit relationships to tests or guardrails where available."],"constraints":["Not every lesson can be automated safely.","Some performance evidence remains environment-specific."],"input":"Performance lesson and supporting evidence or test design.","output":"A traceable performance knowledge record suitable for controlled reuse.","expected_behavior":"Automated consumers can distinguish evidence-backed observations from inference, recommendation, or unknown status.","failure_mode":"Automation applies a scoped performance lesson to an unsupported workload or environment.","cause":"Applicability and evidence state were omitted.","root_cause":"Knowledge ingestion optimized for semantic similarity instead of evidence traceability.","solution":"Require evidence, scope, relationships, and validation metadata before automated promotion.","alternative":"Allow retrieval for human review while preventing automatic enforcement when evidence is insufficient.","tradeoff":"Strict promotion criteria reduce automation coverage but improve reliability.","edge_case":"A high-confidence general principle may still require workload-specific testing before automatic action.","security_implication":"Performance automation must not weaken security controls or authorization requirements.","performance_implication":"Traceable lessons can support regression detection while limiting unsupported optimization actions.","test_case":"Attempt to promote records with missing evidence, scope, or validation metadata and verify appropriate handling.","test_input":"Complete and incomplete performance lesson records.","expected_test_result":"Only records satisfying promotion requirements become eligible for automated guardrail or decision use.","testing_guidance":"Test contradictory and superseded lessons as well as complete records.","verification_method":"Schema, semantic, relationship, and promotion-policy validation.","verification_guidance":"Keep execution, validation, evidence, and automation eligibility as separate states.","language":"GENERAL","version_range":"UNKNOWN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B80-R01","D56-B80-R04","D56-B50-R05"],"performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN"}

{"record_id":"D56-B81-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B81","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Fan-out amplification","concept":"Distributed fan-out can multiply latency and resource consumption even when each downstream operation is individually fast.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can a request become slow when it invokes many downstream services concurrently?","answer":"Because total request cost can be dominated by fan-out coordination, network overhead, downstream queueing, response aggregation, and the slowest required dependency rather than the average latency of one call.","explanation":"Parallel fan-out can reduce serial waiting, but it also increases the number of operations, connections, responses, and failure opportunities associated with one logical request.","principle":"Optimize logical request cost, not only individual dependency latency.","rule":"Measure fan-out width, downstream latency distributions, aggregation time, and resource consumption together before changing the architecture.","objective":"Identify distributed fan-out as a source of performance amplification.","requirements":["Measure per-dependency latency","Measure fan-out width","Measure aggregation overhead","Include tail latency"],"constraints":["Downstream dependencies may have independent capacity limits","Parallelism does not eliminate downstream work"],"preconditions":["A request invokes multiple downstream operations"],"expected_behavior":"The system should maintain acceptable logical-request latency and resource consumption as fan-out changes.","failure_mode":"Distributed fan-out amplification","cause":"Many downstream operations are attached to one logical request.","root_cause":"Performance is evaluated at dependency level rather than end-to-end request level.","solution":"Measure and control fan-out width, downstream concurrency, and aggregation cost.","alternative":"Reduce dependency count or precompute frequently required information when correctness permits.","tradeoff":"Reducing fan-out may increase local storage or data-staleness complexity.","edge_case":"One slow required dependency can dominate the entire request despite fast median dependency latency.","performance_implication":"End-to-end latency and resource consumption can increase nonlinearly with fan-out.","test_case":"Compare logical-request latency and resource use across controlled fan-out widths.","test_input":{"fan_out_widths":[1,2,4,8],"workload":"controlled equivalent requests"},"expected_test_result":"Measurements identify how request latency and resource consumption change as fan-out increases.","testing_guidance":"Use equivalent workloads and record dependency-level and end-to-end measurements.","verification_method":"Controlled fan-out experiment with distributed tracing or equivalent timing instrumentation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that attribution includes downstream latency, coordination, and aggregation rather than only client-observed latency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B69-R01","D56-B70-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B81-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B81","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Tail amplification","concept":"A distributed request can inherit or amplify downstream tail latency.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can p99 latency become poor even when average downstream latency is acceptable?","answer":"A logical request depending on multiple operations has a greater chance of encountering at least one slow operation, so tail latency can dominate the completion time.","explanation":"The relevant distribution for an aggregated request is not the average of independent dependency averages. Required dependencies expose the request to their slow paths.","principle":"Tail behavior of composed operations must be evaluated at the composition level.","rule":"Do not use average dependency latency as the sole performance acceptance criterion for distributed requests.","failure_mode":"Tail-latency amplification","cause":"Multiple required operations contribute slow-path exposure.","root_cause":"Performance validation focuses on averages instead of request-level distributions.","solution":"Measure percentile latency at both dependency and logical-request levels and identify slow-path contributors.","alternative":"Use bounded parallelism, optional dependency degradation, or architectural reduction of required dependencies where semantics allow.","tradeoff":"Graceful degradation may reduce latency while reducing response completeness.","edge_case":"A dependency with low average latency but severe rare stalls can materially affect end-to-end tail latency.","performance_implication":"High-percentile latency can degrade without a large change in average latency.","test_case":"Compare request-level latency percentiles as the number of required dependencies changes.","test_input":{"dependency_count":[1,2,4,8],"percentiles":["p50","p95","p99"]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment exposes changes in tail latency associated with dependency composition.","testing_guidance":"Keep workload and dependency behavior controlled and inspect individual slow traces.","verification_method":"Distributed trace analysis plus percentile comparison.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that identified slow requests contain evidence of dependency-level tail contribution.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B81-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B81-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B81","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Optional dependency degradation","concept":"Making non-critical dependencies optional can protect critical-path latency when degraded operation is semantically acceptable.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When can optional dependency degradation improve performance reliability?","answer":"When a dependency is not required to produce a valid response, allowing it to time out, be omitted, or use a bounded fallback can prevent its latency from blocking the critical path.","explanation":"This is a semantic decision rather than a pure optimization. The fallback must preserve correctness requirements and explicitly define what information may be missing.","principle":"Do not allow non-essential work to determine essential response latency.","rule":"Only degrade an optional dependency when the resulting response remains within defined correctness and product requirements.","objective":"Reduce critical-path exposure to non-essential dependency latency.","requirements":["Classify dependencies by criticality","Define valid degraded behavior","Bound fallback work","Verify semantic correctness"],"constraints":["Some dependencies cannot safely be degraded","Fallback behavior can introduce stale or incomplete information"],"preconditions":["Dependency criticality has been explicitly defined"],"expected_behavior":"Non-critical dependency degradation prevents avoidable latency while preserving required semantics.","failure_mode":"Optional work blocking critical path","cause":"All dependencies are treated as mandatory.","root_cause":"Dependency criticality is not represented in request design.","solution":"Define critical and optional dependencies and enforce bounded degraded behavior.","alternative":"Precompute optional information or move it outside the synchronous request path.","tradeoff":"Lower latency may be accompanied by reduced freshness or completeness.","edge_case":"A dependency initially classified as optional may become semantically required after a feature change.","security_implication":"Fallbacks must not bypass authorization or security-critical checks.","performance_implication":"Critical-path latency can become less sensitive to optional dependency stalls.","test_case":"Inject controlled latency into an optional dependency and compare critical-path behavior with and without degradation.","test_input":{"optional_dependency_delay_ms":[0,100,500],"fallback_enabled":[false,true]},"expected_test_result":"The bounded fallback prevents optional dependency delay from exceeding the defined critical-path budget without bypassing required controls.","testing_guidance":"Validate both latency behavior and semantic/security invariants.","verification_method":"Fault-injection test with response correctness assertions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only explicitly optional work is bypassed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B48-R03","D56-B81-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B81-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B81","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Dependency timeout budgets","concept":"Independent dependency timeouts can sum into an end-to-end budget violation when not coordinated.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is assigning the same timeout independently to every downstream call dangerous?","answer":"Independent timeouts can allow each dependency to consume a full budget, causing the logical request to exceed its overall latency objective.","explanation":"A request-level budget must account for elapsed time, concurrency, retries, aggregation, and remaining work rather than blindly assigning an identical timeout to every operation.","principle":"Timeouts should be derived from an end-to-end budget and request semantics.","rule":"Do not treat per-dependency timeout values as independent from the parent request deadline.","invalid_example":{"timeout_ms":1000,"downstream_calls":5,"pattern":"allow each call to consume 1000 ms regardless of parent deadline"},"failure_mode":"Timeout budget accumulation","cause":"Child operations ignore the parent request deadline.","root_cause":"Timeout configuration is performed locally without end-to-end budget accounting.","solution":"Propagate an absolute deadline or remaining budget to downstream operations.","alternative":"Use stage-specific budgets derived from measured critical-path behavior.","tradeoff":"Shorter child deadlines can increase fallback or failure rates.","edge_case":"Parallel calls may not add their durations serially, but they can still consume shared resources simultaneously.","security_implication":"Deadline propagation must not weaken security-critical checks.","performance_implication":"Coordinated deadlines reduce wasted work after the parent request can no longer succeed within its budget.","test_case":"Inject controlled delays into multiple downstream operations and compare independent timeouts with deadline propagation.","test_input":{"parent_deadline_ms":500,"child_timeout_ms":500,"downstream_calls":3},"expected_test_result":"Deadline-aware calls stop according to remaining parent budget instead of independently consuming the full child timeout.","testing_guidance":"Measure elapsed time and downstream cancellation behavior.","verification_method":"Controlled timeout and cancellation experiment.","verification_guidance":"Verify that child operations observe the propagated deadline and do not continue unnecessary work after parent cancellation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B69-R03","D56-B81-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B81-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"D56-B81","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Fan-out verification","concept":"Distributed performance changes require request-level and dependency-level evidence to avoid incorrect attribution.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a fan-out optimization be verified?","answer":"Verify the end-to-end request objective, dependency-level behavior, resource consumption, error behavior, and workload equivalence before attributing improvement to the intervention.","explanation":"A change that reduces client latency can simply shift work to downstream services or increase resource consumption. Verification must therefore cover the whole composition.","principle":"An optimization is valid only when the intended system-level objective improves without violating required constraints.","rule":"Require evidence at both the optimized component and logical-request levels.","objective":"Prevent local optimization from being mistaken for system-level improvement.","requirements":["Controlled workload","Baseline measurements","Post-change measurements","Dependency measurements","Resource measurements"],"expected_behavior":"Performance claims remain attributable to the intervention under comparable workload conditions.","failure_mode":"Local optimization misattribution","cause":"Only one layer is measured.","root_cause":"Verification scope is narrower than the performance effect surface.","solution":"Use multi-layer performance measurements and controlled comparison.","tradeoff":"Broader measurement increases instrumentation and analysis effort.","edge_case":"A local latency reduction may intentionally increase downstream CPU if the system-level objective permits that tradeoff.","performance_implication":"Prevents false claims of performance improvement.","test_case":"Compare baseline and intervention across end-to-end latency, dependency latency, CPU, memory, throughput, and error rate.","expected_test_result":"Any claimed improvement is supported by controlled evidence and documented trade-offs.","testing_guidance":"Keep workload composition and environment as stable as practical.","verification_method":"Controlled before-and-after experiment with multi-layer telemetry.","verification_guidance":"Classify the result as measured only when actual measurements exist.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R05","D56-B81-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B82-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B82","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Connection reuse","concept":"Reusing established network connections can avoid repeated connection-establishment overhead.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can connection reuse improve repeated network operations?","answer":"It can avoid repeated transport or protocol setup work and reduce per-request connection-establishment overhead.","explanation":"The benefit depends on protocol behavior, connection lifetime, workload shape, server limits, and resource-management policies.","principle":"Avoid repeatedly paying setup costs when connection reuse is semantically and operationally safe.","rule":"Measure connection establishment separately from application request processing before concluding that connection reuse is beneficial.","objective":"Identify avoidable connection setup cost in repeated network workloads.","requirements":["Measure connection setup","Measure request processing","Observe connection pool behavior","Validate resource limits"],"constraints":["Connections consume resources","Idle connections may become stale","Servers may impose connection limits"],"preconditions":["Repeated communication with compatible endpoint semantics"],"expected_behavior":"Repeated operations reuse eligible connections without causing resource exhaustion or stale-connection failures.","failure_mode":"Repeated connection setup overhead","cause":"Connections are created and discarded unnecessarily.","root_cause":"Connection lifecycle is not aligned with workload reuse opportunities.","solution":"Use bounded connection reuse or pooling with health and lifecycle controls.","alternative":"Persistent protocol sessions where supported and appropriate.","tradeoff":"Reuse reduces setup work but introduces pool sizing, lifetime, and failure-recovery complexity.","edge_case":"A stale pooled connection can fail and require transparent reconnection.","security_implication":"Connection reuse must preserve endpoint identity, authentication, encryption, and isolation requirements.","performance_implication":"Repeated setup overhead can be reduced, particularly for workloads with many small operations.","test_case":"Compare repeated operations using fresh connections versus bounded connection reuse.","test_input":{"operation_count":100,"connection_modes":["fresh","reused"]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies whether connection establishment materially contributes to workload cost.","testing_guidance":"Measure both latency and connection-resource usage.","verification_method":"Controlled network workload with connection lifecycle telemetry.","verification_guidance":"Do not generalize the result beyond the tested protocol, endpoint, and workload without additional evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B44-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B82-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B82","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Connection pool saturation","concept":"A connection pool can become a hidden queue when demand exceeds available connections.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What does connection-pool wait time indicate?","answer":"It indicates that callers are waiting for an available connection rather than actively performing network work.","explanation":"Pool wait can become a substantial component of latency while endpoint processing remains unchanged.","principle":"Resource acquisition latency is part of request latency.","rule":"Measure connection acquisition wait separately from network and server processing time.","failure_mode":"Connection pool saturation","cause":"Concurrent demand exceeds available usable connections.","root_cause":"Pool capacity is treated as an implementation detail rather than a workload resource.","solution":"Measure pool wait, concurrency, connection utilization, and endpoint capacity before changing pool size.","alternative":"Reduce unnecessary concurrency or connection demand.","tradeoff":"Increasing pool size may reduce wait but can increase downstream pressure and local resource consumption.","edge_case":"A larger pool can move the bottleneck from the client to the server.","performance_implication":"Pool wait can increase tail latency without changes to endpoint processing time.","test_case":"Sweep pool capacity under equivalent concurrency and measure acquisition wait and downstream load.","test_input":{"pool_sizes":[1,2,4,8],"concurrency":16},"expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies the relationship between pool capacity, wait time, and downstream resource pressure.","testing_guidance":"Record pool wait separately from connection and application latency.","verification_method":"Controlled concurrency experiment with pool telemetry.","verification_guidance":"Select capacity based on measured workload and downstream constraints, not a universal pool-size rule.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B68-R02","D56-B82-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B82-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B82","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Connection lifetime","concept":"Connection lifetime policies balance reuse benefits against stale connections and resource retention.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why is indefinite connection reuse not automatically optimal?","answer":"Long-lived connections can retain resources, encounter network-state changes, or become invalid while remaining in a pool.","explanation":"Connection lifecycle requires policies for idle time, maximum age, health checks, and recovery appropriate to the protocol and environment.","principle":"Reuse should be bounded by validated lifecycle requirements.","rule":"Define connection lifetime behavior from observed protocol and infrastructure characteristics rather than assuming unlimited persistence.","objective":"Maintain connection reuse without uncontrolled resource retention or stale-state failures.","requirements":["Connection lifecycle policy","Failure recovery","Resource bounds","Health validation"],"constraints":["Network infrastructure may terminate idle connections","Servers may impose connection lifetime limits"],"failure_mode":"Stale or over-retained connections","cause":"Connection lifetime is unbounded or incompatible with infrastructure behavior.","solution":"Use bounded lifetime and idle policies with safe reconnection.","alternative":"Rely on protocol-native keepalive or health mechanisms where appropriate.","tradeoff":"More frequent connection renewal adds setup cost but may reduce stale-resource failures.","edge_case":"A very short lifetime can recreate the setup overhead that pooling was intended to avoid.","security_implication":"Connection lifecycle changes must not weaken encryption or authentication guarantees.","performance_implication":"Optimal connection lifetime depends on workload reuse and infrastructure behavior.","test_case":"Compare connection failure rate, setup overhead, and resource usage across lifecycle policies.","test_input":{"max_lifetime_modes":["short","medium","long"]},"expected_test_result":"Measurements identify a workload- and environment-specific lifecycle tradeoff without assuming a universal optimum.","testing_guidance":"Include idle periods and infrastructure-induced connection termination when representative.","verification_method":"Controlled lifecycle experiment with connection health and latency measurements.","verification_guidance":"Validate both steady-state performance and recovery from stale connections.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B82-R01","D56-B82-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B82-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B82","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Connection pooling anti-pattern","concept":"Unbounded connection pools can replace client-side waiting with downstream overload.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can increasing a connection pool without measuring downstream capacity worsen performance?","answer":"More connections can increase concurrent work against the endpoint, causing server queueing, contention, resource exhaustion, or throttling.","explanation":"A connection is a concurrency resource, not merely a latency optimization mechanism.","principle":"Client concurrency must respect downstream capacity.","rule":"Do not increase pool size solely to eliminate client-side wait without measuring downstream saturation and error behavior.","failure_mode":"Downstream overload caused by excessive connection concurrency","cause":"Pool capacity is increased independently of endpoint capacity.","root_cause":"Connection acquisition is optimized without considering the complete dependency path.","solution":"Tune concurrency using measured client wait, downstream saturation, latency, and error signals.","alternative":"Reduce request concurrency or improve request efficiency before increasing connection capacity.","tradeoff":"Lower concurrency may increase local wait while protecting end-to-end stability.","edge_case":"An endpoint may have different capacity limits for different request classes.","security_implication":"Resource-exhaustion conditions can become availability or denial-of-service risks.","performance_implication":"Excess concurrency can increase queueing and tail latency rather than improve throughput.","test_case":"Increase pool capacity stepwise while monitoring endpoint utilization, latency, and errors.","test_input":{"pool_sizes":[2,4,8,16,32]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies a capacity region and detects where additional concurrency stops helping or becomes harmful.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled workloads and stop if safety or service-protection limits are approached.","verification_method":"Capacity sweep with endpoint and client telemetry.","verification_guidance":"Do not label the largest pool as optimal without evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B68-R03","D56-B82-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B82-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B82","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Connection reuse verification","concept":"Connection reuse should be evaluated using both performance and failure-recovery evidence.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can connection reuse be validated without assuming it improves performance?","answer":"Compare fresh and reused connection strategies under representative workloads while measuring setup cost, latency, resource usage, stale-connection failures, and recovery behavior.","objective":"Determine whether connection reuse provides a net benefit for the actual workload.","requirements":["Representative workload","Equivalent request semantics","Connection telemetry","Failure injection where appropriate"],"preconditions":["Both strategies can execute equivalent operations"],"test_case":"Run controlled repeated network operations using fresh and reused connections.","test_input":{"workload":"repeated small requests","modes":["fresh_connections","bounded_reuse"]},"expected_test_result":"The evidence identifies measured differences in setup overhead, latency, resource consumption, and failure behavior.","testing_guidance":"Repeat measurements across representative concurrency and idle intervals.","verification_method":"Controlled A/B performance and resilience experiment.","verification_guidance":"Classify claims as MEASURED only after actual execution and recorded results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B82-R01","D56-B82-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B83-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B83","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Protocol payload efficiency","concept":"Protocol metadata and framing overhead become significant when messages are small and numerous.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"When can protocol overhead materially affect performance?","answer":"When individual payloads are small relative to headers, framing, encoding, connection management, or per-message processing overhead.","explanation":"The same protocol overhead that is negligible for large payloads can become a dominant fraction of work for high-volume small-message workloads.","principle":"Evaluate protocol overhead relative to actual message size and frequency.","rule":"Measure payload-to-overhead ratio before optimizing message size or protocol usage.","objective":"Identify workloads where communication overhead is disproportionately large.","requirements":["Measure payload size","Measure message frequency","Measure protocol overhead","Preserve protocol semantics"],"constraints":["Larger aggregation can increase latency","Protocol changes may affect compatibility"],"expected_behavior":"Communication efficiency improves only when reduced overhead outweighs added batching or processing costs.","failure_mode":"Small-message overhead amplification","cause":"Many small messages incur fixed per-message costs.","root_cause":"Communication cost is evaluated per byte rather than per operation and byte.","solution":"Measure message granularity and consider safe aggregation where semantics permit.","alternative":"Use a protocol or representation with lower fixed overhead when justified by evidence.","tradeoff":"Aggregation can improve efficiency while increasing waiting time or failure scope.","edge_case":"Real-time interactions may prohibit aggregation even when it would reduce overhead.","security_implication":"Protocol optimization must preserve framing, authentication, integrity, and input-validation requirements.","performance_implication":"Per-message overhead can dominate CPU, network, and syscall costs for small messages.","test_case":"Compare equivalent workloads using different message granularities.","test_input":{"payload_size_bytes":[64,256,1024],"messages_per_request":[1,4,16]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment reveals whether fixed communication overhead materially contributes to workload cost.","testing_guidance":"Keep total logical work equivalent across message-granularity variants.","verification_method":"Controlled protocol-level workload measurement.","verification_guidance":"Measure both end-to-end latency and total resource consumption.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B63-R02","D56-B82-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B83-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B83","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Message aggregation","concept":"Message aggregation can reduce fixed communication overhead but may increase waiting and failure scope.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the primary performance tradeoff of aggregating small network messages?","answer":"Aggregation can reduce per-message overhead and improve transfer efficiency, but waiting for more messages can increase latency and a larger unit can increase retransmission or failure impact.","explanation":"The useful aggregation boundary depends on workload latency requirements, message arrival patterns, protocol behavior, and failure semantics.","principle":"Optimize total logical communication cost rather than minimizing either message count or latency in isolation.","rule":"Evaluate aggregation using both latency and resource metrics.","objective":"Determine whether message aggregation improves the actual workload objective.","requirements":["Representative arrival pattern","Latency objective","Resource measurements","Failure semantics"],"preconditions":["Messages are safely aggregatable"],"expected_behavior":"Aggregation reduces communication overhead without violating latency or correctness requirements.","failure_mode":"Over-aggregation","cause":"Aggregation target is chosen without considering arrival latency or failure semantics.","solution":"Use bounded size-or-time aggregation based on measured workload requirements.","alternative":"Adaptive aggregation based on queue depth or arrival rate.","tradeoff":"Larger groups can reduce overhead but increase waiting and blast radius.","edge_case":"Sparse workloads may rarely fill the aggregation threshold and therefore experience mostly timeout-based waiting.","performance_implication":"There may be a workload-specific crossover where aggregation stops providing net benefit.","test_case":"Sweep aggregation thresholds and measure latency, throughput, and resource usage.","test_input":{"max_messages":[1,4,8,16],"max_wait_ms":[0,5,20]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies measured tradeoffs for the representative workload.","testing_guidance":"Preserve equivalent logical work and include sparse and bursty arrival patterns.","verification_method":"Controlled aggregation parameter sweep.","verification_guidance":"Record the tested workload rather than presenting one threshold as universal.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B63-R01","D56-B83-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B83-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B83","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Payload duplication","concept":"Repeated transmission of identical or derivable metadata can consume bandwidth and processing without increasing logical value.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can redundant network metadata become a performance problem?","answer":"Repeated metadata increases bytes transferred and may also increase parsing, serialization, memory, and downstream processing costs.","explanation":"The impact depends on message frequency, payload size, and whether the metadata can be represented safely through shared context or a more compact representation.","principle":"Avoid transmitting information that can be safely derived or represented more efficiently.","rule":"Do not remove metadata solely for performance; first establish whether it is required for correctness, security, observability, or interoperability.","objective":"Identify safe opportunities to reduce redundant communication work.","constraints":["Metadata may be required by downstream validation or auditing","Removing metadata can change semantics"],"failure_mode":"Redundant payload overhead","cause":"Repeated information is transmitted independently in many messages.","root_cause":"Payload design is optimized for local convenience without workload-level cost analysis.","solution":"Measure repeated fields and evaluate compact or shared representations while preserving required semantics.","alternative":"Reference shared context using validated identifiers where protocol semantics permit.","tradeoff":"Compact representations can add lookup or state-management complexity.","edge_case":"A field that appears redundant may be required for independently verifiable authorization or audit context.","security_implication":"Do not remove security-relevant context merely because it increases payload size.","performance_implication":"Reducing unnecessary payload bytes can lower transfer and processing cost.","test_case":"Compare equivalent payload designs while verifying semantic and security equivalence.","test_input":{"payload_modes":["repeated_metadata","compact_representation"]},"expected_test_result":"Any measured performance improvement is accompanied by preserved required semantics and controls.","testing_guidance":"Include serialization, transfer, parsing, and validation costs.","verification_method":"Controlled payload equivalence experiment.","verification_guidance":"Verify that security and audit requirements remain satisfied.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B61-R02","D56-B83-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B83-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B83","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Payload size and security","concept":"Performance optimization of payload content must preserve security-critical context and validation data.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security risk exists when reducing network payloads for performance?","answer":"Removing or weakening fields needed for authentication, authorization, integrity, auditing, or validation can create security failures while appearing to improve performance.","explanation":"Performance optimization must distinguish redundant data from security-critical data. A smaller payload is not automatically a safer or better payload.","principle":"Security invariants take precedence over non-essential communication optimization.","rule":"Classify payload fields by security and semantic necessity before removing or compressing them.","requirements":["Field classification","Security review","Semantic equivalence validation","Performance measurement"],"constraints":["Some security metadata cannot be omitted","Compact representations may still require validation"],"security_implication":"Incorrect payload minimization can bypass security decisions or weaken auditability.","performance_implication":"Security-preserving optimization may require a smaller representation rather than removing required information.","test_case":"Attempt a payload reduction and verify authentication, authorization, integrity, and audit behavior remain unchanged.","test_input":{"payload_variants":["baseline","reduced"]},"expected_test_result":"The reduced payload is accepted only if all required security and semantic invariants remain satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Test both valid and invalid authorization scenarios.","verification_method":"Security regression test combined with payload performance measurement.","verification_guidance":"A latency improvement does not validate a security-sensitive payload change by itself.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B83-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B83-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B83","topic":"Network Performance","subtopic":"Communication granularity testing","concept":"Network granularity changes should be tested against both sparse and bursty workloads.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should communication-granularity performance tests include different arrival patterns?","answer":"Because an optimization that helps dense traffic may hurt sparse traffic through waiting, while an optimization that helps sparse traffic may lose efficiency during bursts.","explanation":"Workload arrival distribution determines whether fixed per-message overhead or waiting dominates.","objective":"Prevent workload-specific communication optimizations from being generalized incorrectly.","requirements":["Sparse workload","Burst workload","Equivalent logical work","Latency measurements","Resource measurements"],"test_case":"Compare multiple communication granularities across sparse, steady, and bursty traffic.","test_input":{"arrival_patterns":["sparse","steady","bursty"],"message_group_sizes":[1,4,8,16]},"expected_test_result":"Results identify workload-dependent performance effects and do not assume one universal granularity.","testing_guidance":"Measure p50 and tail latency, throughput, CPU, memory, and bytes transferred where relevant.","verification_method":"Controlled workload matrix.","verification_guidance":"Preserve workload descriptions with every measured claim.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B83-R02","D56-B70-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B84-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B84","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Coordination overhead","concept":"Distributed coordination can add latency and resource cost that is independent of the primary business computation.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should coordination overhead be measured separately from useful work?","answer":"Coordination may include synchronization messages, state checks, serialization, consensus-related processing, retries, and scheduling that do not directly perform the business computation.","explanation":"A system can have efficient individual operations while still spending substantial time coordinating them.","principle":"Separate useful work from coordination work when analyzing distributed performance.","rule":"Measure coordination operations and their latency separately when they materially contribute to request cost.","objective":"Identify performance cost caused by distributed coordination.","requirements":["Identify coordination operations","Measure coordination latency","Measure coordination resource use","Preserve correctness semantics"],"failure_mode":"Coordination overhead dominance","cause":"Many coordination operations are introduced around a relatively small amount of useful work.","root_cause":"System performance is evaluated only at business-operation level.","solution":"Measure coordination paths and reduce unnecessary coordination only when correctness permits.","alternative":"Co-locate compatible state or aggregate coordination operations where semantics permit.","tradeoff":"Less coordination can increase local state complexity or consistency requirements.","edge_case":"Removing coordination can create stale-state or race conditions.","security_implication":"Coordination that enforces authorization or integrity cannot be removed solely for speed.","performance_implication":"Coordination overhead can become dominant at small work-unit sizes.","test_case":"Compare equivalent workloads with controlled coordination frequency.","test_input":{"coordination_frequency":["low","medium","high"]},"expected_test_result":"Measurements identify the contribution of coordination to total workload cost.","testing_guidance":"Separate coordination time from business processing time in traces or instrumentation.","verification_method":"Controlled coordination-frequency experiment.","verification_guidance":"Confirm semantic equivalence across compared variants.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B81-R01","D56-B70-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B84-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B84","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Coordination frequency","concept":"Coordination performed too frequently can dominate workloads with small useful work units.","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What happens when distributed coordination is performed for every small unit of work?","answer":"The system may spend a large fraction of its time coordinating rather than performing useful work, especially when coordination has fixed per-operation costs.","explanation":"The effect depends on work-unit size, coordination latency, concurrency, and consistency requirements.","principle":"Coordination granularity should be proportional to useful work and correctness requirements.","rule":"Measure coordination-to-useful-work ratio before increasing coordination frequency.","failure_mode":"Coordination amplification","cause":"Fixed coordination costs are repeated for small work units.","root_cause":"Coordination frequency is chosen without workload-level cost measurement.","solution":"Aggregate safe coordination operations or increase work granularity where semantics allow.","alternative":"Use local decisions with periodic coordination when consistency requirements permit.","tradeoff":"Less frequent coordination can increase staleness or recovery complexity.","edge_case":"High-frequency coordination may be mandatory for strict consistency or safety invariants.","security_implication":"Do not weaken security enforcement merely to reduce coordination cost.","performance_implication":"Coordination overhead can dominate throughput and latency.","test_case":"Measure useful-work time and coordination time as work-unit size changes.","test_input":{"work_unit_sizes":[1,10,100,1000]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment shows how the coordination-to-useful-work ratio changes with work granularity.","testing_guidance":"Use equivalent total logical work.","verification_method":"Controlled work-granularity experiment.","verification_guidance":"Preserve consistency and security semantics during comparisons.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B41-R03","D56-B84-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B84-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B84","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Coordination batching","concept":"Batching coordination operations can reduce fixed overhead when consistency semantics permit grouped decisions.","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How can coordination overhead sometimes be reduced without removing coordination entirely?","answer":"Compatible coordination operations can be grouped so that fixed communication and processing costs are amortized across multiple logical work units.","explanation":"Grouping is valid only when the grouped operations can share the same consistency, authorization, failure, and ordering semantics.","principle":"Amortize fixed coordination costs without weakening required invariants.","rule":"Batch only operations whose semantics remain valid when coordinated together.","objective":"Reduce fixed coordination cost while preserving correctness.","requirements":["Semantic compatibility","Bounded batch size","Defined failure behavior","Defined ordering semantics"],"constraints":["Larger groups can increase latency","Partial failure handling becomes more complex"],"preconditions":["Operations are safely batchable"],"expected_behavior":"Coordination overhead per logical work unit decreases without violating correctness requirements.","failure_mode":"Invalid coordination batching","cause":"Operations with incompatible semantics are grouped.","solution":"Classify operations by consistency, authorization, ordering, and failure requirements before batching.","alternative":"Batch only non-critical coordination while keeping critical decisions immediate.","tradeoff":"Lower coordination overhead versus increased batch latency and failure scope.","edge_case":"One operation requiring immediate consistency can prevent a larger group from being safely batched.","security_implication":"Authorization decisions must remain bound to the correct identity, scope, and resource set.","performance_implication":"Fixed coordination cost may be amortized across multiple work units.","test_case":"Compare per-unit coordination cost for safe batch sizes while validating semantic equivalence.","test_input":{"batch_sizes":[1,4,8,16]},"expected_test_result":"Measured coordination overhead changes with batch size without correctness violations.","testing_guidance":"Include partial-failure and ordering tests.","verification_method":"Controlled coordination-batching experiment plus invariant testing.","verification_guidance":"Reject performance improvements that alter required semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B63-R01","D56-B84-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B84-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B84","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Consistency and performance","concept":"Reducing coordination can change consistency guarantees and therefore cannot be classified as a pure performance optimization.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must consistency changes be treated separately from ordinary performance tuning?","answer":"Because reducing coordination or synchronization may alter which state a request observes, potentially changing correctness, authorization, or safety behavior.","explanation":"A faster path that observes stale or incomplete state may violate system invariants even if latency improves.","principle":"Performance changes must preserve explicitly required consistency and security invariants.","rule":"Classify any reduction in coordination or state synchronization as a semantic change requiring correctness validation.","objective":"Prevent performance optimization from silently changing consistency-sensitive behavior.","requirements":["Defined consistency requirement","State visibility model","Security invariant validation","Performance measurement"],"security_implication":"Stale authorization or policy state can create security failures.","performance_implication":"Weaker or less frequent synchronization may reduce latency and resource cost but can increase staleness.","failure_mode":"Unsafe consistency relaxation","cause":"Coordination is reduced without validating semantic consequences.","root_cause":"Consistency is treated as implementation detail rather than a system requirement.","solution":"Document consistency requirements and validate them explicitly for performance changes.","test_case":"Introduce controlled synchronization reduction and test state visibility and authorization invariants.","test_input":{"sync_modes":["baseline","reduced"]},"expected_test_result":"Any performance change is accepted only when required consistency and security invariants remain satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Include stale-state and concurrent-update scenarios.","verification_method":"Consistency regression test combined with performance measurement.","verification_guidance":"Record consistency semantics alongside performance results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B84-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B84-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B84","topic":"Distributed Performance","subtopic":"Coordination measurement","concept":"Coordination optimization requires evidence that separates reduced coordination work from shifted workload.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is required before claiming that coordination optimization improved performance?","answer":"Measure end-to-end latency, coordination operations, useful work, downstream load, errors, and consistency behavior under equivalent workloads.","explanation":"A reduction in coordination messages can simply shift work into larger operations or create hidden correctness costs.","principle":"Measure the complete effect of coordination changes.","rule":"Do not classify reduced coordination volume as performance improvement without end-to-end evidence.","test_case":"Compare baseline and optimized coordination under controlled equivalent workloads.","test_input":{"metrics":["end_to_end_latency","coordination_operations","useful_work_time","resource_usage","errors","consistency_invariants"]},"expected_test_result":"The optimization is classified according to measured system-level effects and verified invariants.","testing_guidance":"Capture both performance and semantic evidence.","verification_method":"Controlled before-and-after experiment with invariant checks.","verification_guidance":"Use MEASURED classification only when actual execution evidence exists.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R05","D56-B84-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B85-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B85","topic":"Performance Modeling","subtopic":"Amdahl-style limits","concept":"Optimizing a portion of a workload has a bounded effect when other portions remain unchanged.","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can a large optimization produce only a modest end-to-end improvement?","answer":"Because the optimized portion represents only part of total execution cost; the remaining work limits the overall improvement.","explanation":"Performance reasoning should consider the fraction of workload affected by an intervention rather than extrapolating component-level speedup directly to the whole system.","principle":"End-to-end improvement is constrained by the proportion of total work affected by the optimization.","rule":"Estimate the affected workload fraction before interpreting component-level optimization results, while treating estimates as estimates rather than measured outcomes.","objective":"Prevent unrealistic extrapolation from local performance improvements.","constraints":["Workload composition can change","Parallelism and contention can alter simple models"],"edge_case":"An optimization can indirectly change the cost of other components, invalidating a simple independent model.","performance_implication":"The same local speedup can produce different end-to-end effects under different workload compositions.","test_case":"Compare workloads with different fractions of time spent in the optimized component.","test_input":{"optimized_fraction":[0.1,0.5,0.9],"local_speedup":"controlled"},"expected_test_result":"End-to-end improvement varies with the fraction of work affected by the optimization.","testing_guidance":"Use measured baseline component proportions when available.","verification_method":"Controlled workload composition experiment.","verification_guidance":"Label analytical predictions separately from measured results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B76-R01","D56-B70-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B85-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B85","topic":"Performance Modeling","subtopic":"Workload composition","concept":"A performance lesson may stop applying when the workload composition changes.","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can a previously effective optimization lose its benefit after workload composition changes?","answer":"Because the optimized component may represent a smaller fraction of total work, or another bottleneck may become dominant.","explanation":"Performance is a property of a workload and system configuration, not merely of an isolated implementation.","principle":"Performance lessons should include their workload applicability conditions.","rule":"Record workload characteristics and bottleneck assumptions with reusable performance knowledge.","objective":"Prevent stale performance lessons from being generalized beyond their evidence conditions.","requirements":["Workload description","Bottleneck identification","Applicability conditions"],"failure_mode":"Performance lesson invalidation","cause":"Workload composition changes.","root_cause":"The lesson was stored without applicability metadata.","solution":"Attach workload, environment, and bottleneck conditions to performance lessons.","alternative":"Continuously validate high-value lessons against representative regression workloads.","tradeoff":"More metadata increases dataset maintenance but improves retrieval precision.","edge_case":"A change can both reduce one workload component and increase another.","performance_implication":"Historical performance claims may not predict current behavior.","test_case":"Replay the same optimization across materially different workload compositions.","test_input":{"workload_profiles":["compute_heavy","io_heavy","network_heavy"]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies where the lesson remains applicable and where it does not.","testing_guidance":"Preserve workload descriptors with results.","verification_method":"Cross-workload replay experiment.","verification_guidance":"Do not promote workload-specific evidence into a universal rule.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B85-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B85-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B85","topic":"Performance Modeling","subtopic":"Bottleneck migration","concept":"Optimizing one bottleneck can expose another previously hidden bottleneck.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is bottleneck migration after an optimization?","answer":"It is the condition where reducing one dominant cost causes another component to become the limiting factor.","explanation":"This is not necessarily a failure of the original optimization; it means the system's limiting factor has changed.","principle":"Evaluate the whole system after meaningful optimization changes.","rule":"Re-profile after an intervention rather than assuming the original bottleneck remains dominant.","objective":"Detect newly exposed limiting factors after performance changes.","failure_mode":"Bottleneck migration","cause":"One major cost is reduced while another remains unchanged or becomes relatively larger.","root_cause":"Post-optimization analysis is omitted or limited to the modified component.","solution":"Repeat end-to-end profiling and update the bottleneck model after each material intervention.","alternative":"Use a staged optimization process with explicit re-measurement gates.","tradeoff":"Repeated profiling adds analysis cost but prevents stale optimization assumptions.","edge_case":"The new bottleneck may appear only under high concurrency or tail workloads.","performance_implication":"Further optimization must target the newly dominant constraint rather than the previously optimized component.","test_case":"Profile before and after an intervention across multiple workload intensities.","test_input":{"load_levels":["low","medium","high"]},"expected_test_result":"The resulting bottleneck distribution is identified from measurements rather than assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Compare component contribution and end-to-end metrics.","verification_method":"Differential profiling across baseline and post-intervention workloads.","verification_guidance":"Record the changed bottleneck as a new evidence-backed lesson only after measurement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B85-R01","D56-B70-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B85-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B85","topic":"Performance Modeling","subtopic":"Model validity","concept":"Analytical performance models are useful for reasoning but do not substitute for representative measurements.","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What is the limitation of using a performance model without measurements?","answer":"A model can omit contention, runtime behavior, workload variability, infrastructure effects, and implementation details that materially change observed performance.","explanation":"Analytical models can narrow hypotheses and expose theoretical limits, but empirical validation is required before making measured performance claims.","principle":"Use models to reason and experiments to validate.","rule":"Never label an analytical prediction as a measured result without execution evidence.","objective":"Keep performance knowledge epistemically correct.","requirements":["Explicit model assumptions","Representative measurements for validation","Separate predicted and observed results"],"constraints":["Model fidelity depends on assumptions","Real systems can violate simplified independence assumptions"],"performance_implication":"Models can identify useful bounds or hypotheses but may misestimate actual behavior.","test_case":"Compare model predictions against controlled measurements across multiple workloads.","test_input":{"workloads":["representative_A","representative_B"],"model":"explicit assumptions"},"expected_test_result":"Differences between predicted and measured behavior are documented rather than hidden.","testing_guidance":"Store assumptions and measurement conditions with the result.","verification_method":"Model-versus-measurement comparison.","verification_guidance":"Classify model output as ESTIMATED or INFERRED unless actual measurement supports the claim.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B85-R01","D56-B70-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B85-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B85","topic":"Performance Modeling","subtopic":"Bottleneck revalidation","concept":"A durable performance lesson should record the condition under which a bottleneck was observed and the evidence supporting it.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should bottleneck lessons be stored for future retrieval?","answer":"Store the workload, environment, measurement method, observed bottleneck, intervention, resulting measurements, trade-offs, and applicability conditions.","explanation":"Without these dimensions, a future system may retrieve a historical bottleneck as though it were a universal rule.","principle":"Performance knowledge is conditional evidence, not context-free folklore.","rule":"Do not promote a bottleneck observation into a general lesson without preserving its evidence context.","objective":"Make performance lessons reusable without overgeneralization.","requirements":["Stable record identity","Workload context","Measurement evidence","Applicability conditions","Contradiction tracking"],"expected_behavior":"Future retrieval can distinguish measured historical observations from general engineering principles.","test_case":"Retrieve the lesson under matching and mismatching workload conditions.","test_input":{"contexts":["matching","partially_matching","mismatching"]},"expected_test_result":"The lesson is considered more applicable to matching contexts and remains explicitly conditional elsewhere.","testing_guidance":"Test retrieval metadata and applicability filters.","verification_method":"Knowledge-record validation and retrieval-context test.","verification_guidance":"Preserve provenance and measurement status independently from explanatory text.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B70-R05","D56-B85-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B86-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B86","topic":"Performance Regression","subtopic":"Threshold selection","concept":"Performance regression thresholds should reflect service objectives and measurement variability rather than arbitrary percentages.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How should a performance regression threshold be selected?","answer":"It should be based on the relevant performance objective, measurement noise, workload variability, and the cost of false positives and false negatives.","explanation":"An arbitrary fixed threshold can either miss meaningful regressions or create excessive alerts from normal measurement variation.","principle":"Regression detection thresholds must be evidence- and objective-driven.","rule":"Do not treat one percentage threshold as universally appropriate across systems or metrics.","objective":"Detect meaningful performance regressions without excessive false alarms.","requirements":["Defined performance objective","Baseline variability","Representative workload","Decision policy"],"constraints":["Measurement noise can vary by environment","Different metrics require different interpretation"],"performance_implication":"Threshold selection directly affects regression-detection sensitivity.","test_case":"Evaluate candidate thresholds against historical or controlled variation and known injected regressions.","test_input":{"threshold_candidates":["small","medium","large"],"conditions":["baseline_variation","injected_regression"]},"expected_test_result":"The selected policy distinguishes meaningful changes from expected measurement variation under the tested conditions.","testing_guidance":"Use repeated measurements and document environment and workload.","verification_method":"Threshold sensitivity analysis.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim threshold validity outside the tested measurement regime.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B70-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B86-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B86","topic":"Performance Regression","subtopic":"Noise discrimination","concept":"Normal measurement variability must be distinguished from actual performance regression.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a performance gate fail even when the implementation has not regressed?","answer":"Measurement noise, workload variation, environment differences, warm-up state, or infrastructure variability can produce apparent changes without a real implementation regression.","explanation":"A robust performance gate needs a baseline distribution or other evidence model rather than relying on one observation.","principle":"Performance regression detection must account for expected variability.","rule":"Treat isolated deviations as evidence requiring investigation unless the measurement policy establishes them as actionable.","failure_mode":"False-positive performance regression","cause":"Expected variability is mistaken for implementation change.","root_cause":"Baseline variability is not characterized.","solution":"Establish repeatable measurement procedures and characterize baseline variance.","alternative":"Use statistical or policy-based detection appropriate to the metric and workload.","tradeoff":"More measurements increase test cost but improve confidence.","performance_implication":"Poor noise handling can block valid changes or desensitize teams to genuine regressions.","test_case":"Repeat unchanged workloads and measure natural variation before injecting a known change.","test_input":{"repetitions":10,"condition":"unchanged_implementation"},"expected_test_result":"The baseline variability is quantified before regression thresholds are applied.","testing_guidance":"Keep execution environment and workload stable where possible.","verification_method":"Repeated baseline measurement.","verification_guidance":"Store variance characteristics with the regression policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R01","D56-B86-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B86-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B86","topic":"Performance Regression","subtopic":"Guardrail tradeoffs","concept":"Strict performance gates can reduce regressions but may increase delivery friction when measurement uncertainty is high.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the tradeoff of enforcing strict automated performance gates?","answer":"Strict gates can catch regressions early but can also block changes because of noisy measurements, workload shifts, or legitimate trade-offs.","explanation":"A performance gate should reflect the reliability of the measurement process and the criticality of the performance objective.","principle":"Regression controls should be proportional to evidence quality and system objectives.","rule":"Do not create strict blocking gates around metrics that are not sufficiently reproducible.","objective":"Balance regression prevention with trustworthy engineering workflow.","requirements":["Reliable measurement","Defined objective","Escalation policy","Known variability"],"constraints":["Some performance metrics are inherently noisy","Blocking decisions can have operational cost"],"performance_implication":"Gate strictness affects both regression escape risk and false-positive rate.","test_case":"Evaluate the gate against repeated baseline runs and controlled regressions.","test_input":{"gate_modes":["informational","warning","blocking"]},"expected_test_result":"The chosen mode reflects measured reliability and business/system criticality.","testing_guidance":"Track both detected regressions and false alarms.","verification_method":"Historical or controlled gate evaluation.","verification_guidance":"Document why the gate mode is appropriate for the metric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B86-R01","D56-B86-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B86-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B86","topic":"Performance Regression","subtopic":"Security-sensitive performance gates","concept":"Performance regression policies for security-critical paths must not incentivize removal of required controls.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security risk can arise from performance gates on security-sensitive code?","answer":"Teams may attempt to improve a performance metric by weakening validation, authentication, authorization, logging, or other required controls.","explanation":"A performance target is subordinate to explicit security and correctness requirements unless the security requirement itself is intentionally changed and reviewed.","principle":"Performance gates must encode security invariants alongside performance objectives.","rule":"Reject performance improvements that violate required security controls.","objective":"Prevent performance optimization from becoming a security regression mechanism.","requirements":["Security invariants","Performance objective","Validation policy","Change review"],"security_implication":"Security controls must remain mandatory even when they add measurable latency or resource cost.","performance_implication":"Security-sensitive paths require multi-objective optimization rather than single-metric optimization.","test_case":"Run performance regression tests together with authorization, validation, and audit regression tests.","test_input":{"variants":["baseline","performance_optimized"]},"expected_test_result":"A variant fails acceptance if it improves performance by violating a required security invariant.","testing_guidance":"Include negative authorization cases and malformed input cases.","verification_method":"Combined performance and security regression suite.","verification_guidance":"Record security status independently from performance status.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B47-R05","D56-B83-R04","D56-B86-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B86-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B86","topic":"Performance Regression","subtopic":"Regression policy validation","concept":"Performance regression policies themselves require validation against stable baselines and known changes.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should an automated performance regression policy be validated?","answer":"Run unchanged workloads to characterize false-positive behavior and controlled performance changes to characterize detection behavior.","objective":"Verify that the regression policy responds appropriately to both normal variability and known regressions.","requirements":["Stable baseline","Known controlled change","Repeated measurements","Defined decision policy"],"test_case":"Evaluate the policy against unchanged and intentionally modified implementations.","test_input":{"scenarios":["unchanged","controlled_slowdown","controlled_improvement"]},"expected_test_result":"The policy distinguishes normal variability from the tested controlled changes according to its documented decision criteria.","testing_guidance":"Preserve raw measurements and policy decisions.","verification_method":"Regression-policy validation suite.","verification_guidance":"Do not treat policy behavior as universally valid beyond its tested workloads and measurement regime.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B86-R01","D56-B86-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B87-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B87","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Representative workload design","concept":"A performance test can be technically repeatable yet irrelevant if its workload does not represent important production behavior.","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why is workload representativeness necessary for performance lessons?","answer":"Because performance behavior depends on input distribution, concurrency, data shape, request mix, and workload intensity; an unrepresentative test may optimize the wrong behavior.","explanation":"Repeatability establishes consistency of the test, while representativeness establishes relevance to the target workload.","principle":"Repeatability and representativeness are separate performance-test properties.","rule":"Document both why a workload is reproducible and why it represents the intended target.","objective":"Prevent high-confidence conclusions about irrelevant workloads.","requirements":["Workload characterization","Target population definition","Repeatable execution","Representative data shape"],"constraints":["Production workloads may be heterogeneous","Privacy or security restrictions can limit direct replay"],"failure_mode":"Irrelevant performance evidence","cause":"Test workload differs materially from target workload.","root_cause":"Test selection prioritizes convenience over workload relevance.","solution":"Characterize target workloads and construct representative test distributions.","alternative":"Use multiple workload profiles when no single profile is representative.","performance_implication":"Irrelevant tests can produce misleading optimization priorities.","test_case":"Compare conclusions from a synthetic simplified workload and a representative workload.","test_input":{"workloads":["simplified","representative"]},"expected_test_result":"Differences in conclusions are identified rather than hidden.","testing_guidance":"Document request mix, data sizes, concurrency, and workload intensity.","verification_method":"Workload characterization and comparative performance test.","verification_guidance":"Attach workload provenance to every performance result.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R03","D56-B85-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B87-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B87","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Synthetic workload bias","concept":"Synthetic workloads can omit correlations and rare behaviors that materially affect system performance.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can synthetic benchmarks produce misleading performance lessons?","answer":"They may simplify data distributions, request sequences, concurrency patterns, or failure conditions in ways that remove the behavior responsible for real bottlenecks.","explanation":"Synthetic tests remain useful for controlled experiments but should not automatically be treated as representative of all workloads.","principle":"Controlled does not mean representative.","rule":"State explicitly which aspects of a synthetic workload are controlled and which target behaviors it does not represent.","objective":"Prevent benchmark scope from being mistaken for production universality.","failure_mode":"Synthetic benchmark bias","cause":"Important workload characteristics are absent from the benchmark.","root_cause":"Benchmark design optimizes controllability without documenting applicability limits.","solution":"Combine controlled microbenchmarks with representative workload tests where relevant.","alternative":"Use multiple synthetic profiles targeting different workload dimensions.","performance_implication":"A synthetic benchmark can identify mechanisms while failing to predict system-level behavior.","test_case":"Run an intervention across multiple synthetic profiles and a representative profile.","test_input":{"profiles":["micro","mixed","representative"]},"expected_test_result":"Applicability differences are documented across profiles.","testing_guidance":"Preserve exact benchmark assumptions and input distributions.","verification_method":"Cross-profile benchmark comparison.","verification_guidance":"Classify results according to the tested profile rather than extrapolating universally.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B87-R01","D56-B85-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B87-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B87","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Data-shape sensitivity","concept":"Performance can change materially with data size and shape even when logical operation count remains constant.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should performance tests vary data shape rather than only operation count?","answer":"Different sizes, distributions, locality, cardinalities, and skew can alter memory behavior, algorithm paths, compression, indexing, and downstream processing.","explanation":"Two workloads with the same number of logical operations can exercise very different computational and resource paths.","principle":"Logical operation count is not sufficient to characterize workload cost.","rule":"Include representative data-shape dimensions in performance experiments where they can affect the implementation.","objective":"Capture data-dependent performance behavior.","requirements":["Data-size variation","Distribution variation where relevant","Equivalent logical semantics","Resource measurements"],"edge_case":"Pathological data distributions can expose behavior absent from average-case tests.","performance_implication":"Data shape can change both average and tail performance.","test_case":"Run equivalent logical operations across controlled data-size and distribution variants.","test_input":{"data_sizes":["small","medium","large"],"distribution":["uniform","skewed"]},"expected_test_result":"The test identifies whether performance depends materially on data shape.","testing_guidance":"Record data characteristics alongside measurements.","verification_method":"Data-shape sensitivity experiment.","verification_guidance":"Do not generalize results beyond tested data characteristics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B75-R01","D56-B87-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B87-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B87","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Sensitive workload dimensions","concept":"A performance lesson should identify which workload dimensions were held constant and which were varied.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should performance experiments explicitly record workload dimensions?","answer":"Because without knowing which variables changed, performance differences cannot be reliably attributed to the intervention.","explanation":"Workload dimensions can include concurrency, data shape, request mix, cache state, dependency behavior, and arrival rate.","principle":"Performance evidence requires experimental context.","rule":"Record manipulated, controlled, and observed workload variables for every substantive performance experiment.","objective":"Improve attribution and reproducibility of performance lessons.","requirements":["Independent variables","Controlled variables","Observed variables","Workload provenance"],"test_case":"Review a performance experiment record for complete workload-variable metadata.","test_input":{"required_metadata":["changed_variables","held_constant_variables","observed_variables"]},"expected_test_result":"The record identifies the variables needed to interpret the performance comparison.","testing_guidance":"Reject records that cannot establish what changed between baseline and intervention.","verification_method":"Dataset-level metadata validation.","verification_guidance":"Treat incomplete experimental context as reduced evidence quality rather than filling missing facts.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B87-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B87-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B87","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Workload representativeness validation","concept":"Representativeness should be evaluated against explicit target workload characteristics rather than asserted informally.","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can workload representativeness be validated?","answer":"Define target workload dimensions, compare the test workload against them, and document which dimensions match, differ, or remain unknown.","objective":"Make workload applicability explicit and auditable.","requirements":["Target workload definition","Test workload description","Dimension comparison","Known limitations"],"test_case":"Compare test and target workloads across request mix, concurrency, data shape, intensity, and dependency behavior.","test_input":{"dimensions":["request_mix","concurrency","data_shape","arrival_pattern","dependency_behavior"]},"expected_test_result":"Each dimension has an explicit match, difference, or unknown status.","testing_guidance":"Do not infer representativeness solely from similar aggregate throughput.","verification_method":"Structured workload comparison.","verification_guidance":"Store limitations with the resulting performance lesson.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B87-R01","D56-B87-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B88-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B88","topic":"Performance Variability","subtopic":"Heterogeneous hardware","concept":"Performance measurements can differ across hardware configurations even when software behavior is unchanged.","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should hardware characteristics be recorded with performance evidence?","answer":"CPU architecture, cache hierarchy, storage devices, memory bandwidth, accelerators, and virtualization can materially affect execution cost.","explanation":"A performance lesson measured on one hardware configuration may not transfer directly to another.","principle":"Performance evidence is conditional on relevant execution environment.","rule":"Record hardware characteristics when they can materially influence the measured behavior.","objective":"Prevent environment-specific measurements from being generalized incorrectly.","constraints":["Hardware details may be unavailable in managed environments","Cloud instance types can change underlying hardware characteristics"],"performance_implication":"Hardware differences can alter both bottleneck identity and absolute performance.","test_case":"Repeat a representative performance test across materially different hardware profiles.","test_input":{"hardware_profiles":["profile_A","profile_B"]},"expected_test_result":"Any performance differences are associated with documented hardware conditions rather than attributed solely to software.","testing_guidance":"Record CPU, memory, storage, virtualization, and accelerator information when relevant.","verification_method":"Cross-environment performance comparison.","verification_guidance":"Keep environment metadata with each performance result.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R02","D56-B85-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B88-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B88","topic":"Performance Variability","subtopic":"Virtualization effects","concept":"Virtualized or shared environments can introduce performance variability unrelated to application code changes.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can shared infrastructure affect performance measurements?","answer":"Resource contention, scheduling behavior, storage variability, network variability, and infrastructure-level throttling can change observed performance without source-code changes.","explanation":"This makes environment characterization important when comparing performance results across time or systems.","principle":"Separate application changes from infrastructure-induced variability.","rule":"Record relevant environment characteristics and avoid attributing unexplained performance differences to code alone.","failure_mode":"Infrastructure-induced performance variance","cause":"Underlying resource availability changes.","root_cause":"Environment is treated as constant when it is not controlled or observable.","solution":"Characterize the execution environment and repeat measurements when attribution is uncertain.","alternative":"Use isolated or controlled environments for sensitive comparisons where feasible.","tradeoff":"More controlled environments can increase test cost and reduce environmental realism.","performance_implication":"Infrastructure variance can obscure small software regressions or improvements.","test_case":"Repeat the same workload under controlled and variable infrastructure conditions.","test_input":{"environment_modes":["controlled","shared"]},"expected_test_result":"The experiment identifies the magnitude and pattern of environment-induced variability.","testing_guidance":"Avoid mixing environment changes with code changes in one comparison.","verification_method":"Repeated environment-controlled performance experiment.","verification_guidance":"Classify unexplained variation as UNKNOWN rather than attributing it without evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R02","D56-B88-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B88-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B88","topic":"Performance Variability","subtopic":"Environment normalization","concept":"Normalizing performance environments can improve attribution but can reduce ecological validity.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the tradeoff between controlled and production-like performance environments?","answer":"Highly controlled environments improve repeatability and attribution, while production-like environments better expose real infrastructure variability and interactions.","explanation":"Both environments can be valuable for different questions and should not be treated as interchangeable evidence.","principle":"Use the environment appropriate to the performance question being investigated.","rule":"State whether an experiment targets causal isolation, production representativeness, or both.","objective":"Match environment design to evidence requirements.","requirements":["Explicit experimental objective","Environment description","Known limitations"],"constraints":["Full production replication may be impractical","Controlled environments may omit real-world effects"],"performance_implication":"Environment choice affects both reproducibility and applicability of performance conclusions.","test_case":"Compare an intervention in a controlled environment and a representative environment.","test_input":{"environment_types":["controlled","representative"]},"expected_test_result":"Differences in conclusions and variability are documented by environment.","testing_guidance":"Do not merge results from different environments without preserving their context.","verification_method":"Cross-environment experiment.","verification_guidance":"Classify each result according to its execution environment.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B87-R01","D56-B88-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B88-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B88","topic":"Performance Variability","subtopic":"Security isolation overhead","concept":"Isolation mechanisms can add measurable overhead while providing security and reliability properties that must be preserved.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should performance lessons involving isolation overhead be interpreted?","answer":"The measured cost should be evaluated against the security, reliability, or tenant-isolation property the mechanism provides rather than treated as unnecessary overhead by default.","explanation":"Removing isolation to improve a benchmark can invalidate the system's security model.","principle":"Performance analysis must account for the purpose and invariant provided by isolation mechanisms.","rule":"Do not remove security or tenant-isolation controls solely to improve performance metrics.","objective":"Ensure performance optimization respects isolation requirements.","requirements":["Isolation requirement","Performance measurement","Security validation","Threat model"],"security_implication":"Reduced isolation can expose data, resources, or execution contexts across security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Isolation may increase resource consumption or latency but can be a required system property.","test_case":"Measure isolated and non-isolated configurations only for diagnostic comparison while preserving the required production configuration.","test_input":{"modes":["required_isolation","diagnostic_reduced_isolation"]},"expected_test_result":"The performance cost of required isolation is quantified without treating reduced isolation as an acceptable production optimization.","testing_guidance":"Never deploy the diagnostic reduced-isolation configuration where the security model requires isolation.","verification_method":"Controlled performance comparison plus security invariant verification.","verification_guidance":"Store security status separately from performance measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B47-R05","D56-B86-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B88-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B88","topic":"Performance Variability","subtopic":"Environment provenance","concept":"Environment metadata is part of performance provenance when environmental factors can influence the result.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What environment information should accompany a performance lesson?","answer":"Include relevant software versions, hardware characteristics, deployment mode, resource limits, dependency versions, and execution conditions sufficient to interpret the result.","explanation":"The exact metadata required depends on the workload and mechanism being measured.","principle":"Performance evidence requires enough environmental context to determine applicability.","rule":"Do not store environment-dependent performance claims without documenting the conditions that materially influence them.","objective":"Preserve interpretability and reproducibility of performance knowledge.","requirements":["Environment metadata","Workload metadata","Measurement metadata","Version metadata"],"test_case":"Validate a performance record for required environment fields based on its topic.","test_input":{"record_topic":"environment-sensitive performance lesson"},"expected_test_result":"The record contains sufficient environment context or explicitly marks unavailable information as UNKNOWN.","testing_guidance":"Never invent missing environment details.","verification_method":"Schema and provenance validation.","verification_guidance":"Missing metadata should reduce evidence quality rather than be inferred.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B88-R01","D56-B88-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B89-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B89","topic":"Performance Change Management","subtopic":"Optimization reversibility","concept":"Performance interventions should be reversible or safely removable when their assumptions become invalid.","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should performance optimizations be designed for reversibility?","answer":"Because workload, infrastructure, dependencies, and correctness requirements can change, causing a previously useful optimization to become ineffective or harmful.","explanation":"Reversibility reduces the cost of learning from performance interventions and limits the persistence of stale assumptions.","principle":"Performance changes are hypotheses that should remain manageable when evidence changes.","rule":"Prefer interventions with clear rollback or disablement paths when practical.","objective":"Reduce risk from obsolete performance assumptions.","requirements":["Change identification","Rollback path","Evidence tracking","Applicability conditions"],"constraints":["Some architectural changes are difficult to reverse","Rollback itself can have performance effects"],"performance_implication":"Reversible optimizations reduce the cost of responding to performance regressions.","test_case":"Validate that the optimization can be disabled or rolled back while preserving required functionality.","test_input":{"states":["optimized","baseline"]},"expected_test_result":"The system can safely transition between states without introducing uncontrolled behavior.","testing_guidance":"Include rollback performance and correctness checks.","verification_method":"Controlled enable/disable or rollback test.","verification_guidance":"Record the rollback mechanism with the performance lesson.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B70-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B89-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B89","topic":"Performance Change Management","subtopic":"Optimization expiry","concept":"Performance lessons can become obsolete as software, workloads, hardware, or dependencies change.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is performance knowledge drift?","answer":"It is the condition where a previously valid performance observation no longer accurately describes the current system or workload.","explanation":"Knowledge drift can occur without the original lesson being incorrect; its applicability conditions may simply no longer hold.","principle":"Performance knowledge requires lifecycle management.","rule":"Attach version, workload, environment, and applicability context to durable performance lessons.","failure_mode":"Stale performance knowledge","cause":"System conditions change while historical lessons remain active.","root_cause":"Performance knowledge is treated as permanent documentation.","solution":"Periodically revalidate high-value lessons and mark obsolete or superseded evidence explicitly.","alternative":"Use expiration or review triggers for highly environment-dependent lessons.","tradeoff":"Revalidation consumes resources but reduces stale guidance.","performance_implication":"Stale lessons can lead to unnecessary or harmful optimizations.","test_case":"Replay historical lessons after material system or workload changes.","test_input":{"states":["original_context","changed_context"]},"expected_test_result":"Lessons are classified as still applicable, conditionally applicable, contradicted, or unknown based on evidence.","testing_guidance":"Preserve historical evidence rather than silently overwriting it.","verification_method":"Knowledge revalidation workflow.","verification_guidance":"Use contradiction and supersession relationships when appropriate.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B85-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B89-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B89","topic":"Performance Change Management","subtopic":"Feature-controlled optimization","concept":"Feature-controlled performance interventions can support controlled comparison and safer rollback when the architecture permits them.","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How can a performance optimization be introduced with lower rollback risk?","answer":"When appropriate, expose the optimization through a controlled configuration or feature mechanism that allows comparable baseline and intervention behavior.","explanation":"This is useful only when the control itself does not materially distort the performance being measured.","principle":"Controlled exposure can improve causal comparison and reversibility.","rule":"Ensure the control mechanism is itself included in performance analysis if it adds meaningful overhead.","objective":"Enable safer performance experimentation and rollback.","requirements":["Equivalent semantics","Safe control mechanism","Measurement of control overhead","Rollback capability"],"constraints":["Control logic can alter execution paths","Some optimizations cannot be toggled cleanly"],"performance_implication":"The control mechanism must not be ignored when measuring small performance differences.","test_case":"Compare enabled and disabled states under equivalent workloads while measuring control overhead.","test_input":{"states":["disabled","enabled"]},"expected_test_result":"The performance effect of the optimization is separated from the overhead of the control mechanism where measurable.","testing_guidance":"Run repeated measurements and verify semantic equivalence.","verification_method":"Controlled feature-state performance experiment.","verification_guidance":"Record the exact configuration used for each result.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B65-R02","D56-B89-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B89-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B89","topic":"Performance Change Management","subtopic":"Rollback performance","concept":"Rollback can restore correctness or previous behavior while introducing a different performance profile.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should rollback performance be tested separately?","answer":"Because reverting an optimization can change cache state, resource configuration, dependency behavior, or workload distribution and therefore does not guarantee identical performance to the original baseline.","explanation":"Rollback is a state transition with its own operational behavior.","principle":"Rollback correctness and rollback performance are separate verification dimensions.","rule":"Validate both functional recovery and performance behavior after rollback when performance is operationally significant.","objective":"Ensure rollback is a complete performance-management mechanism rather than only a code-reversion action.","requirements":["Rollback procedure","Functional validation","Performance validation","State cleanup"],"edge_case":"Residual configuration or cached state can cause rollback behavior to differ from the original baseline.","performance_implication":"Rollback may temporarily or persistently alter latency, throughput, or resource usage.","test_case":"Execute optimization, rollback, and baseline comparison under controlled conditions.","test_input":{"states":["baseline","optimized","rolled_back"]},"expected_test_result":"Rollback restores required behavior and its performance characteristics are documented.","testing_guidance":"Check for residual state after rollback.","verification_method":"Three-state rollback performance experiment.","verification_guidance":"Do not assume rolled-back state is identical to historical baseline without evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B89-R01","D56-B89-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B89-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B89","topic":"Performance Change Management","subtopic":"Optimization lifecycle verification","concept":"Performance changes should have explicit evidence, applicability, rollback, and revalidation metadata.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a performance intervention durable engineering knowledge?","answer":"A durable record identifies the observed bottleneck, evidence, intervention, result, trade-offs, applicability conditions, rollback path, and future revalidation trigger.","explanation":"This transforms a one-time optimization into a reusable but conditional engineering lesson.","principle":"Performance knowledge should preserve both what worked and the conditions under which it worked.","rule":"Do not store an optimization as a context-free best practice when its evidence is workload- or environment-specific.","objective":"Create reusable and safely retrievable performance knowledge.","requirements":["Stable identity","Evidence classification","Applicability metadata","Rollback metadata","Revalidation trigger"],"test_case":"Validate performance lesson records for lifecycle metadata completeness.","test_input":{"required_fields":["observed_bottleneck","measurement","intervention","result","tradeoff","applicability","rollback","revalidation_trigger"]},"expected_test_result":"Complete records can be reused conditionally; incomplete records are marked with known limitations.","testing_guidance":"Do not infer missing results or operational experience.","verification_method":"Dataset quality-gate validation.","verification_guidance":"Preserve UNKNOWN values rather than filling them with assumptions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B85-R05","D56-B89-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B90-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B90","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Automated performance guardrails","concept":"Performance lessons become operationally durable when validated constraints can be converted into automated guardrails.","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a validated performance lesson become an engineering guardrail?","answer":"A lesson can become a guardrail when its metric, workload conditions, threshold policy, validation procedure, and failure response are sufficiently defined and reproducible.","explanation":"Not every lesson is suitable for automation; uncertain or highly environment-dependent observations may require advisory treatment instead.","principle":"Automate only performance knowledge whose evidence and decision criteria are sufficiently reliable.","rule":"Do not create blocking performance guardrails from unverified or poorly reproducible observations.","objective":"Translate validated performance knowledge into repeatable engineering controls.","requirements":["Validated metric","Known workload","Reliable measurement","Decision policy","Failure response"],"constraints":["Some metrics remain too noisy for blocking use","Guardrails can become stale"],"expected_behavior":"The guardrail detects defined performance violations under its documented applicability conditions.","performance_implication":"Automated guardrails can reduce regression escape risk when their evidence quality is adequate.","test_case":"Run the guardrail against unchanged baselines and controlled regressions.","test_input":{"scenarios":["stable_baseline","known_regression"]},"expected_test_result":"The guardrail behaves according to its documented detection policy.","testing_guidance":"Validate false-positive and detection behavior before making the guardrail blocking.","verification_method":"Automated guardrail validation suite.","verification_guidance":"Attach the underlying performance lesson and evidence conditions to the guardrail.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B86-R05","D56-B89-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B90-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B90","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Guardrail failure modes","concept":"A performance guardrail can become harmful when its baseline, workload, or threshold assumptions become stale.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an automated performance guardrail become unreliable over time?","answer":"Changes in workload, hardware, software versions, infrastructure, measurement methodology, or performance objectives can invalidate its original assumptions.","explanation":"Automation preserves a decision rule, not the truth of the assumptions behind that rule.","principle":"Automated performance controls require lifecycle management.","rule":"Revalidate guardrails when material workload, environment, implementation, or objective changes occur.","failure_mode":"Stale performance guardrail","cause":"Guardrail assumptions drift from current system conditions.","root_cause":"Guardrail is treated as permanently valid after initial validation.","solution":"Attach revalidation triggers and ownership metadata to automated performance controls.","alternative":"Use advisory mode when evidence quality or applicability is uncertain.","tradeoff":"More frequent validation increases maintenance cost but reduces stale-control risk.","performance_implication":"Stale guardrails can create false alarms or allow regressions to pass undetected.","test_case":"Change a documented guardrail assumption and verify whether the guardrail detects the applicability change.","test_input":{"changes":["workload","runtime_version","hardware","threshold_policy"]},"expected_test_result":"Material changes trigger review or revalidation rather than silently preserving the old assumption.","testing_guidance":"Test both detection and lifecycle metadata.","verification_method":"Guardrail lifecycle validation.","verification_guidance":"Record supersession or contradiction relationships instead of deleting historical evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B86-R01","D56-B89-R02","D56-B90-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B90-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B90","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Evidence-to-rule transformation","concept":"A performance observation should be generalized into a rule only after its applicability conditions and evidence boundaries are understood.","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a measured performance observation be transformed into reusable knowledge?","answer":"Preserve the original observation as evidence, identify its causal interpretation separately, record applicability conditions, and only then derive a conditional rule if supported.","explanation":"This prevents a single experiment from becoming an unsupported universal optimization rule.","principle":"Generalization must preserve evidence boundaries.","rule":"Separate observed result, interpretation, recommendation, and generalized lesson into distinct knowledge fields.","objective":"Convert performance evidence into reusable knowledge without losing epistemic status.","requirements":["Original evidence","Measurement conditions","Interpretation classification","Applicability conditions","Contradiction handling"],"constraints":["Generalization requires additional evidence when workloads differ materially"],"expected_behavior":"The resulting knowledge record preserves both the original observation and the limits of its generalization.","test_case":"Transform a workload-specific observation into a conditional lesson and validate that unsupported universal claims are absent.","test_input":{"observation":"optimization changed measured latency under defined workload"},"expected_test_result":"The resulting lesson remains conditional on the documented workload and evidence.","testing_guidance":"Never replace measured evidence with a stronger claim merely for retrieval convenience.","verification_method":"Knowledge transformation review and schema validation.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence status for every derived claim.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B70-R01","D56-B85-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B90-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B90","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Contradictory evidence","concept":"Contradictory performance observations should be preserved with context rather than silently merged into one rule.","knowledge_type":"CONFLICTED","question":"What should happen when two valid performance experiments produce different results?","answer":"Preserve both observations, compare their workload and environment conditions, and represent the relationship as conditional or conflicted until the difference is explained.","explanation":"Different results do not necessarily mean one experiment is invalid; they may reflect different workloads, versions, hardware, or bottlenecks.","principle":"Contradiction is evidence about context and should not be erased.","rule":"Do not collapse conflicting performance observations into a single universal claim without resolving the conditions causing the difference.","objective":"Maintain trustworthy performance knowledge under changing evidence.","requirements":["Stable record IDs","Experiment provenance","Condition comparison","Contradiction relationship"],"failure_mode":"Knowledge contradiction loss","cause":"Conflicting observations are deduplicated without context analysis.","root_cause":"Deduplication treats semantic disagreement as duplication.","solution":"Preserve distinct records and link them through contradiction, specialization, or applicability relationships.","alternative":"Supersede an older record only when evidence establishes the newer rule's scope and authority.","tradeoff":"More records increase graph complexity but preserve epistemic fidelity.","performance_implication":"Contextual contradictions can reveal workload-specific bottlenecks or regime changes.","test_case":"Compare two conflicting performance records across workload and environment metadata.","test_input":{"records":["observation_A","observation_B"]},"expected_test_result":"The dataset preserves both records and identifies the contextual dimensions associated with their differing outcomes.","testing_guidance":"Never fabricate a resolution when evidence is insufficient.","verification_method":"Contradiction-analysis validation.","verification_guidance":"Use UNKNOWN or CONFLICTED status when causal reconciliation is not established.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B89-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D56-B90-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"D56-B90","topic":"Performance Learning","subtopic":"Final lesson quality gate","concept":"A performance lesson is training-grade only when its evidence status, applicability, provenance, implementation context, and verification state are explicit.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What minimum properties should a durable performance lesson contain?","answer":"It should identify the bottleneck or performance question, measurement method, intervention, observed or unknown result, trade-offs, applicability conditions, evidence classification, provenance status, version/environment context, and verification state.","explanation":"These fields allow the knowledge system to distinguish facts, measurements, inferences, recommendations, unknowns, and conflicts without fabricating missing evidence.","principle":"Performance knowledge quality depends on epistemic precision as much as technical content.","rule":"Do not promote a performance lesson to durable reusable knowledge when material evidence status or applicability information is missing.","objective":"Ensure D56 records remain reliable for retrieval, reasoning, code generation, debugging, testing, and future learning.","requirements":["Stable record identity","Evidence classification","Measurement context","Applicability conditions","Version context","Verification state","Provenance status","Contradiction handling"],"constraints":["Not every lesson can be experimentally verified immediately","Some operational context may legitimately remain UNKNOWN"],"expected_behavior":"The knowledge system can distinguish verified evidence from inference and identify conditions under which a lesson should or should not be applied.","test_case":"Run the D56 final quality gate across structural schema, evidence classification, deduplication, contradiction handling, provenance status, and applicability metadata.","test_input":{"quality_dimensions":["schema","evidence","provenance","version","deduplication","contradiction","applicability","verification"]},"expected_test_result":"Records with missing or unsupported claims are retained only with explicit limitations and are not promoted to stronger evidence classes.","testing_guidance":"Validate structure and knowledge status independently; never infer execution or operational experience.","verification_method":"Dataset-level quality gate plus targeted record review.","verification_guidance":"Require every performance claim to remain MEASURED, ESTIMATED, INFERRED, or UNKNOWN according to available evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B85-R05","D56-B89-R05","D56-B90-R01","D56-B90-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}

{"record_id":"D56-B91-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"Automated Regression Detection","concept":"Turning validated performance lessons into explicit regression guardrails","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a performance lesson become an automated regression guardrail?","answer":"A performance lesson should become a guardrail only when its measured or otherwise validated invariant is explicitly defined, the measurement method is stable, acceptable variance is specified, and the guardrail can distinguish meaningful regression from measurement noise.","explanation":"A lesson is reusable engineering knowledge only when its observation can be operationalized into a repeatable verification condition. The guardrail should preserve the workload, environment assumptions, metric definition, and evidence basis of the original lesson.","principle":"Automated performance checks should encode validated invariants rather than vague expectations.","rule":"Do not create a hard performance threshold from an unmeasured or unstable claim.","objective":"Convert durable performance knowledge into repeatable regression detection.","constraints":["The metric must be defined","The workload must be reproducible","Measurement noise must be characterized","The threshold must have evidence"],"preconditions":["A performance lesson exists","The relevant metric is measurable","The measurement procedure is documented"],"expected_behavior":"A regression test evaluates a defined performance invariant and reports whether observed behavior remains within its validated acceptance criteria.","tradeoff":"Stricter thresholds detect smaller regressions but increase false alarms when measurement variance is significant.","edge_case":"A workload with naturally high variance may require distribution-based acceptance rather than a single threshold.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","performance_implication":"Guardrails reduce the time between performance regression introduction and detection.","test_case":"Run the same controlled workload against two implementations and evaluate the predefined guardrail.","test_input":"Stable workload, fixed environment assumptions, repeated metric measurements.","expected_test_result":"The guardrail identifies a deliberately introduced regression when the regression exceeds the validated acceptance condition.","testing_guidance":"Use repeated measurements and preserve the measurement protocol.","verification_method":"Automated performance regression test with documented acceptance criteria.","verification_guidance":"Verify both detection sensitivity and false-positive behavior before relying on the guardrail.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R05"],"derived_from":["D56-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B91-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"Threshold Selection","concept":"Separating engineering thresholds from arbitrary limits","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is an arbitrary performance threshold unsafe as a regression guardrail?","answer":"An arbitrary threshold can either miss meaningful regressions or fail healthy changes because it is not tied to observed workload behavior, measurement variance, or an explicit engineering requirement.","explanation":"Performance values depend on workload, hardware, software versions, concurrency, data shape, and measurement method. A threshold without those assumptions has weak interpretive value.","principle":"A performance threshold should represent a justified engineering boundary, not a convenient number.","rule":"Do not treat an arbitrary latency, throughput, memory, or CPU limit as a universal performance requirement.","failure_mode":"The test reports repeated false failures or allows regressions that remain below an unjustified limit.","cause":"Threshold selected without evidence or requirement analysis.","solution":"Derive acceptance criteria from requirements, historical validated measurements, or controlled experiments.","tradeoff":"Evidence-based thresholds require more setup and maintenance than arbitrary thresholds.","edge_case":"A contractual or explicitly documented service objective may legitimately define a threshold even without a historical baseline.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","performance_implication":"Poor thresholds reduce the reliability of performance regression detection.","test_case":"Compare an arbitrary threshold with a threshold derived from repeated baseline measurements.","expected_test_result":"The evidence-based threshold demonstrates a clearer relationship to workload variability and engineering requirements.","verification_method":"Review threshold provenance and evaluate false-positive and false-negative behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B91-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"Distribution-Based Validation","concept":"Using latency distributions rather than averages alone","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can percentile or distribution-based checks be preferable to average latency checks?","answer":"Distribution-based checks preserve information about tail behavior that an average can conceal, making them useful when rare slow requests materially affect user or system experience.","explanation":"Two workloads can have similar averages while having substantially different tail latency. A performance lesson concerning slow-path behavior therefore should not rely exclusively on arithmetic mean latency.","principle":"Performance validation should measure the portion of the distribution relevant to the engineering objective.","rule":"Use distribution-aware metrics when tail behavior is part of the requirement or failure mode.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","performance_implication":"Tail-aware validation can expose regressions that average-only checks overlook.","edge_case":"Very small sample sizes can make high percentiles unstable and should be interpreted cautiously.","test_case":"Run repeated requests containing both normal and intentionally slower cases and compare mean and percentile metrics.","expected_test_result":"A tail regression can be visible in percentile measurements even when the mean changes little.","testing_guidance":"Define the percentile, sample population, and minimum sample requirements before testing.","verification_method":"Compare distribution summaries across controlled baseline and candidate runs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B69-R02","D56-B91-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B91-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"False Regression Detection","concept":"Distinguishing software regressions from environmental variance","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should a performance regression system do when the environment changed between baseline and candidate measurements?","answer":"It should classify the result as potentially confounded rather than automatically attributing the difference to the software change.","explanation":"Hardware, operating-system state, dependency versions, workload data, background activity, thermal conditions, and resource contention can alter performance independently of the code under test.","principle":"Causal attribution requires control or explicit accounting for relevant environmental changes.","rule":"Do not label a performance difference as a software regression when material environmental variables are uncontrolled or changed.","failure_mode":"A false regression is attributed to a code change.","root_cause":"Baseline and candidate measurements were not comparable.","solution":"Record environment metadata and repeat the comparison under controlled or equivalently characterized conditions.","tradeoff":"Stricter environmental control increases test complexity and execution cost.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Repeat a performance comparison after restoring the baseline environment.","expected_test_result":"The classification changes from uncertain to attributable only when environmental confounders are sufficiently controlled.","verification_method":"Compare environment metadata and measurement conditions before assigning causality.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B70-R02","D56-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B91-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"Performance Guardrails","subtopic":"Change Gates","concept":"Using performance evidence as a controlled change gate","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should a performance change gate verify before blocking or approving a software change?","answer":"It should verify that the workload, measurement protocol, environment assumptions, metric, acceptance criteria, and evidence status are valid before interpreting a performance result as a release decision.","explanation":"A change gate is stronger than a raw benchmark because it combines measurement validity with an explicit engineering requirement.","principle":"A performance gate should evaluate evidence quality as well as measured behavior.","rule":"Do not block a release solely because a benchmark produced a value outside an unexplained threshold.","objective":"Prevent unreliable performance evidence from controlling engineering decisions.","requirements":["Defined workload","Stable measurement procedure","Documented acceptance criteria","Comparable environment","Traceable result"],"expected_behavior":"The gate distinguishes valid regression evidence from inconclusive or confounded measurements.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Feed the gate valid regression, valid non-regression, and confounded measurements.","expected_test_result":"Only sufficiently supported regression evidence triggers the configured blocking condition.","verification_method":"Decision-table testing of the gate's evidence states.","verification_guidance":"Verify that UNKNOWN or CONFLICTED evidence cannot silently become a verified regression.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01","D56-B91-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B92-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Performance Knowledge Maintenance","subtopic":"Lesson Lifecycle","concept":"Lifecycle management for performance lessons","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a performance lesson be maintained after it enters the knowledge fabric?","answer":"A performance lesson should retain its evidence, assumptions, validation status, applicability conditions, relationships, and revision history, and it should be revalidated when material dependencies or workloads change.","explanation":"Performance knowledge can become obsolete even when the original observation was correct because software, hardware, workload, architecture, and runtime behavior evolve.","principle":"Performance knowledge is versioned knowledge, not permanent truth.","rule":"Do not treat an old performance lesson as universally applicable after material system changes.","objective":"Keep performance lessons temporally and contextually accurate.","requirements":["Stable lesson ID","Evidence provenance","Applicability conditions","Version metadata","Revalidation policy"],"expected_behavior":"The knowledge fabric can determine whether a lesson is current, historical, superseded, or requires revalidation.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Change a dependency or runtime version referenced by a performance lesson and evaluate its status.","expected_test_result":"The system marks the lesson for review when the change could affect applicability.","verification_method":"Dependency-aware lifecycle validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B92-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Performance Knowledge Maintenance","subtopic":"Staleness","concept":"Detecting stale performance knowledge","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What makes a performance lesson stale?","answer":"A lesson becomes potentially stale when the implementation, runtime, hardware, workload, dependency versions, architecture, or measurement methodology changes in ways that can invalidate its assumptions or observed relationship.","explanation":"Staleness does not mean the historical observation was false. It means the observation may no longer describe the current system.","principle":"Historical validity and current applicability are separate properties.","rule":"Preserve historical evidence while separately tracking current applicability.","failure_mode":"Old performance guidance is applied to a changed system without revalidation.","cause":"No explicit applicability or freshness model.","solution":"Track affected versions and conditions and trigger revalidation when they change.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","performance_implication":"Staleness detection prevents obsolete lessons from silently becoming current engineering rules.","edge_case":"A fundamental algorithmic property may remain applicable across versions even when measured magnitude changes.","test_case":"Associate a lesson with a dependency version, then update the dependency metadata.","expected_test_result":"The lesson becomes reviewable without deleting its historical evidence.","verification_method":"Metadata and relationship validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B92-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Performance Knowledge Maintenance","subtopic":"Supersession","concept":"Superseding obsolete performance guidance","knowledge_type":"RELATIONSHIP","question":"How should a newer performance lesson replace an older one without destroying historical knowledge?","answer":"The newer lesson should explicitly supersede the older lesson while retaining the historical record, provenance, conditions, and reason for the change.","explanation":"Deletion loses useful historical context and can break relationships to earlier investigations or decisions.","principle":"Knowledge evolution should preserve lineage.","rule":"Use supersedes relationships instead of silently overwriting prior performance lessons.","objective":"Maintain an auditable evolution path for performance knowledge.","expected_behavior":"Retrieval can identify the current lesson while still exposing the superseded lesson when historical context is requested.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Create a revised lesson with materially changed applicability conditions and link it to the previous lesson.","expected_test_result":"The new lesson is retrievable as current while the old lesson remains traceable as historical.","verification_method":"Relationship and retrieval validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R01","D56-B92-R02"],"supersedes":["OLDER_PERFORMANCE_LESSON_IF_PRESENT"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B92-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Performance Knowledge Maintenance","subtopic":"Applicability","concept":"Context-bounded performance recommendations","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should performance recommendations include applicability conditions?","answer":"Because an optimization that helps one workload, architecture, runtime, or resource regime can be neutral or harmful under another.","explanation":"Performance behavior is conditional. Encoding those conditions prevents a local optimization from being promoted into a universal rule.","principle":"Every non-universal performance lesson should state where it applies.","rule":"Record workload and system conditions whenever they materially affect the lesson.","tradeoff":"More contextual metadata increases dataset size but improves retrieval precision and reduces misuse.","edge_case":"A broadly established complexity property may require fewer contextual constraints than a workload-specific optimization.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Retrieve the same lesson under matching and non-matching workload conditions.","expected_test_result":"The knowledge system identifies the lesson as applicable only when its conditions are satisfied or explicitly marked uncertain.","verification_method":"Condition-aware retrieval test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R01","D56-B92-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B92-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Performance Knowledge Maintenance","subtopic":"Knowledge Validation","concept":"Revalidating lessons after system evolution","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What is an appropriate validation strategy when a performance-sensitive component changes substantially?","answer":"Reproduce the relevant workload and measurement procedure, compare the new behavior against the lesson's original evidence and applicability conditions, and classify the lesson as confirmed, changed, superseded, or unresolved.","explanation":"Revalidation should test the knowledge claim itself rather than merely rerunning an old benchmark without checking whether its assumptions remain valid.","principle":"Knowledge validation follows the claim and its assumptions.","test_input":"Original lesson, current implementation, current workload, current environment metadata.","expected_test_result":"The lesson receives an explicit current validation status.","testing_guidance":"Preserve both original and new evidence.","verification_method":"Evidence lineage plus controlled performance comparison.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R01","D56-B92-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B93-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Performance Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Benchmark Validity","concept":"Representative workload construction","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What makes a performance workload useful for validating an operational lesson?","answer":"A useful workload exercises the behavior relevant to the lesson with representative inputs, realistic control flow, meaningful data distributions, and the concurrency or load conditions that matter to the claim.","explanation":"A benchmark can be technically repeatable yet irrelevant if it does not exercise the workload characteristic responsible for the lesson.","principle":"Benchmark relevance is determined by correspondence to the claim, not by benchmark complexity.","rule":"Do not generalize from a benchmark that does not exercise the behavior under investigation.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Compare a synthetic workload that omits the suspected bottleneck with one that exercises it.","expected_test_result":"Only the representative workload provides evidence directly relevant to the lesson.","verification_method":"Workload-to-claim coverage review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01","D56-B70-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B93-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Performance Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Benchmark Contamination","concept":"Separating benchmark setup cost from measured operation cost","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should benchmark setup and measurement costs be separated?","answer":"Setup work such as data generation, initialization, allocation, connection establishment, or cache preparation can distort the measured operation if it is unintentionally included in the timing interval.","explanation":"The measured interval must correspond to the performance claim. Otherwise the result represents a mixture of unrelated costs.","principle":"Measure the operation represented by the claim and account for setup separately.","rule":"Do not include unrepresentative setup work in an operation-level performance measurement unless setup cost is itself the subject of the lesson.","failure_mode":"The benchmark attributes initialization or preparation cost to the target operation.","cause":"Incorrect timing boundaries.","solution":"Define and document measurement boundaries and isolate setup phases.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Measure an operation with and without setup included in the timed region.","expected_test_result":"The two measurements differ when setup is material, demonstrating why the timing boundary matters.","verification_method":"Instrumentation boundary review and controlled comparison.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B93-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B93-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Performance Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Warmup Effects","concept":"Separating warm-up behavior from steady-state behavior","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should warm-up and steady-state performance sometimes be analyzed separately?","answer":"Initialization, caching, compilation, allocation patterns, connection establishment, and other transient effects can make early iterations behave differently from later iterations.","explanation":"A single aggregate measurement can hide the distinction between startup and steady-state behavior.","principle":"The measurement phase should match the operational phase being evaluated.","rule":"Do not combine transient warm-up behavior with steady-state measurements when they represent different engineering objectives.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","performance_implication":"Separating phases improves interpretation of startup and sustained performance.","edge_case":"If the workload consists mostly of short-lived executions, warm-up may itself be the primary performance objective.","test_case":"Record measurements by iteration or phase and inspect the transition from initialization to steady state.","expected_test_result":"Distinct phases are identifiable when warm-up materially affects performance.","verification_method":"Phase-aware measurement analysis.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B67-R05","D56-B79-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B93-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Performance Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Sample Independence","concept":"Avoiding correlated benchmark samples","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can correlated measurements weaken a performance conclusion?","answer":"If repeated measurements share state such as cache contents, warmed connections, reused data, synchronized timing, or other persistent effects, they may not represent independent observations of the intended workload.","explanation":"Correlation can make variability appear smaller than it actually is and can bias comparisons between implementations.","principle":"Repeated measurements should be interpreted according to the dependencies between observations.","rule":"Do not assume that a large iteration count automatically provides independent evidence.","failure_mode":"The benchmark reports excessive confidence in a small or correlated performance difference.","cause":"Persistent state or synchronized sampling was ignored.","solution":"Identify state dependencies and design repetitions that reflect the operational measurement objective.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Compare repeated measurements with persistent state against measurements with controlled state reset where appropriate.","expected_test_result":"Observed variance or central tendency can change when correlated state is removed.","verification_method":"State-dependency analysis of the benchmark.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B93-R01","D56-B93-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B93-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Performance Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Measurement Reproducibility","concept":"Reproducible performance experiment metadata","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What metadata should accompany a performance experiment so that its evidence can be interpreted later?","answer":"At minimum, preserve implementation identity, dependency and runtime versions, workload definition, input characteristics, environment assumptions, measurement method, metric definitions, repetitions, and result status.","explanation":"Without metadata, later engineers may be unable to determine whether a historical measurement applies to a current system or whether two measurements are comparable.","principle":"Performance evidence without experimental context has limited reuse value.","rule":"Do not store performance results without enough metadata to interpret their scope and comparability.","objective":"Make performance evidence reproducible and auditable.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Attempt to reproduce an experiment using only the stored record and verify whether essential context is available.","expected_test_result":"The record contains sufficient metadata to reconstruct or appropriately qualify the experiment.","verification_method":"Evidence completeness checklist.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01","D56-B92-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B94-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Performance Tradeoff Reasoning","subtopic":"Latency Versus Throughput","concept":"Separating latency optimization from throughput optimization","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should latency and throughput be treated as distinct performance objectives?","answer":"An intervention can improve throughput while increasing individual request latency, or reduce latency while lowering total processing capacity, depending on batching, concurrency, resource allocation, and workload behavior.","explanation":"The desired objective must be explicit before interpreting a performance change as an improvement.","principle":"Performance optimization is objective-dependent.","rule":"Do not classify a throughput increase as universally beneficial without checking latency and other relevant service objectives.","tradeoff":"Optimizing one metric can move another metric in the opposite direction.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Evaluate an intervention using both per-operation latency and completed-work rate.","expected_test_result":"The result identifies whether the intervention trades one objective against another.","verification_method":"Multi-metric comparison under the same workload.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B63-R02","D56-B56-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B94-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Performance Tradeoff Reasoning","subtopic":"Resource Tradeoffs","concept":"Evaluating performance improvements against resource consumption","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should performance improvements be evaluated together with resource consumption?","answer":"An optimization that reduces latency or increases throughput may consume substantially more CPU, memory, storage, network capacity, or operational complexity, changing its overall system value.","explanation":"Performance is not isolated from resource constraints. An optimization that shifts the bottleneck can simply move cost elsewhere.","principle":"Performance gains should be evaluated within the system resource budget.","rule":"Record the resources consumed by a performance intervention when they materially affect capacity or cost.","tradeoff":"Higher resource usage can buy better latency or throughput but reduce headroom.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","performance_implication":"Resource-aware evaluation reduces the risk of optimizing one subsystem while destabilizing another.","test_case":"Measure target performance and relevant resource utilization before and after an intervention.","expected_test_result":"The analysis records both target improvement and resource impact.","verification_method":"Joint performance-resource measurement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B57-R01","D56-B68-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B94-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Performance Tradeoff Reasoning","subtopic":"Memory Versus CPU","concept":"Recognizing memory-for-CPU tradeoffs","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What should be considered when an optimization uses additional memory to reduce computation?","answer":"The optimization should be evaluated for memory capacity, cache behavior, allocation and maintenance overhead, data freshness, concurrency effects, and failure behavior in addition to CPU savings.","explanation":"Caching, precomputation, memoization, indexing, and materialization can reduce repeated computation while increasing memory pressure and maintenance cost.","principle":"A resource substitution is beneficial only within the constraints of the complete system.","rule":"Do not evaluate a memory-for-CPU optimization using CPU measurements alone.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","edge_case":"The additional memory may increase cache pressure enough to negate the expected CPU benefit.","test_case":"Measure CPU, memory, and target operation performance under representative concurrency.","expected_test_result":"The system-level tradeoff is visible rather than hidden behind the CPU metric.","verification_method":"Resource and workload sensitivity analysis.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B94-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B94-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Performance Tradeoff Reasoning","subtopic":"Optimization Reversal","concept":"Reverting optimizations whose system-level cost exceeds their benefit","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"When should a performance optimization be reconsidered or reverted?","answer":"When controlled evidence shows that its benefit is not material for the target workload or is outweighed by resource consumption, complexity, reliability risk, maintenance burden, or negative effects on other objectives.","explanation":"Keeping an optimization solely because it once improved a benchmark creates optimization debt.","principle":"Performance changes should remain justified by current evidence and requirements.","rule":"Do not preserve an optimization indefinitely without evidence that its benefits remain relevant.","tradeoff":"Reverting complexity may sacrifice a local optimization but improve maintainability and system predictability.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Reevaluate a historical optimization under its current representative workload.","expected_test_result":"The decision reflects current evidence rather than historical assumption.","verification_method":"Current-versus-historical evidence review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R01","D56-B92-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B94-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Performance Tradeoff Reasoning","subtopic":"Objective Conflicts","concept":"Explicitly modeling conflicting performance objectives","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should conflicting performance objectives be represented in a knowledge record?","answer":"Record each objective separately, state the observed or inferred relationship between them, identify the workload conditions, and avoid collapsing the tradeoff into a single universal conclusion.","explanation":"Latency, throughput, resource usage, tail behavior, startup time, and cost can conflict. The knowledge record should preserve that structure.","principle":"Conflicting objectives should remain explicit in machine-readable knowledge.","rule":"Do not encode a multi-objective tradeoff as a single scalar lesson unless the aggregation rule is explicitly justified.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Represent an intervention affecting latency, throughput, and memory in one structured record.","expected_test_result":"Each objective and its relationship to the intervention remains independently retrievable.","verification_method":"Schema and retrieval validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B94-R01","D56-B94-R02","D56-B94-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B95-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Performance Debugging","subtopic":"Bottleneck Migration","concept":"Recognizing bottleneck movement after optimization","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should the system be profiled again after a successful optimization?","answer":"Removing one bottleneck can expose another previously masked bottleneck, so the dominant performance constraint can move to a different component or resource.","explanation":"Optimization changes the distribution of work and resource demand. The original bottleneck therefore should not be assumed to remain dominant.","principle":"Optimization is iterative because bottlenecks can migrate.","rule":"After a material optimization, remeasure the system before concluding that performance is solved.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","performance_implication":"Post-change profiling helps identify newly dominant constraints.","test_case":"Profile the same representative workload before and after a material bottleneck reduction.","expected_test_result":"The analysis identifies whether the dominant cost shifted.","verification_method":"Before-and-after profiling with comparable workload conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R01","D56-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B95-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Performance Debugging","subtopic":"Symptom Versus Cause","concept":"Separating performance symptoms from root causes","knowledge_type":"CAUSE","question":"Why should a measured slow component not automatically be treated as the root cause?","answer":"A slow component can itself be downstream of another constraint, such as queueing, contention, input amplification, synchronization, or upstream workload changes.","explanation":"The component with the largest observed time is not necessarily the component responsible for creating the excess work or delay.","principle":"Root-cause analysis must trace causal relationships rather than merely rank observed costs.","rule":"Do not equate the largest measured cost with the causal origin of the performance problem.","failure_mode":"Optimization targets a downstream symptom while the initiating constraint remains.","cause":"Insufficient causal analysis.","solution":"Trace work creation, dependencies, waits, resource constraints, and workload transformations.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Change an upstream workload parameter and observe downstream component cost.","expected_test_result":"The relationship reveals whether the downstream cost is caused or merely correlated.","verification_method":"Controlled causal experiment and dependency tracing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B64-R01","D56-B70-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B95-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Performance Debugging","subtopic":"Critical Path","concept":"Identifying work that actually constrains end-to-end completion","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the value of identifying the end-to-end critical path?","answer":"It identifies the sequence of dependent work whose delay directly constrains completion time, helping distinguish critical costs from parallel or non-blocking costs.","explanation":"Reducing non-critical work may improve resource usage without changing end-to-end latency if it does not constrain the completion path.","principle":"End-to-end performance depends strongly on critical dependencies, not merely total work.","rule":"Prioritize analysis of work that lies on the measured completion path when latency is the objective.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Trace dependent stages of a request and identify which stage delays completion when extended.","expected_test_result":"The end-to-end latency changes when critical-path work is delayed, while purely non-critical parallel work may not have the same effect.","verification_method":"Dependency-aware latency experiment.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B65-R03","D56-B67-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B95-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Performance Debugging","subtopic":"Instrumentation Distortion","concept":"Accounting for profiler and instrumentation overhead","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can performance instrumentation distort the behavior being measured?","answer":"Instrumentation consumes CPU, memory, synchronization, I/O, and timing resources and can therefore change scheduling and execution behavior.","explanation":"Detailed tracing can be necessary for diagnosis, but its overhead must be understood before treating instrumented measurements as representative of production behavior.","principle":"Measurement can perturb the system under measurement.","rule":"Do not assume heavily instrumented measurements represent uninstrumented performance without evaluating measurement overhead.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Compare a workload with and without the relevant instrumentation under equivalent conditions.","expected_test_result":"Any material measurement overhead becomes observable and can be accounted for.","verification_method":"Instrumentation overhead comparison.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B74-R01","D56-B74-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B95-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Performance Debugging","subtopic":"Hypothesis Testing","concept":"Using falsifiable performance hypotheses","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What makes a performance debugging hypothesis useful?","answer":"A useful hypothesis identifies a proposed causal mechanism and specifies an observation that would support or falsify it under a controlled experiment.","explanation":"Vague statements such as 'the system is slow' cannot guide efficient investigation. A falsifiable hypothesis defines what should change if the proposed cause is correct.","principle":"Performance debugging should be experiment-driven rather than assumption-driven.","rule":"Each major performance hypothesis should define an expected observable consequence.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Create a hypothesis linking a specific resource constraint to a measured latency component and alter only the proposed causal factor.","expected_test_result":"The observed response either supports or weakens the hypothesis.","verification_method":"Controlled hypothesis test with explicit expected outcomes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B70-R03","D56-B95-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B96-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Performance Safety","subtopic":"Optimization Risk","concept":"Ensuring performance changes preserve correctness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must performance optimizations be verified for functional correctness?","answer":"An optimization that changes computation, ordering, caching, concurrency, representation, or resource handling can improve measured performance while producing incorrect behavior.","explanation":"Performance is not a substitute for correctness. An invalid result cannot be accepted merely because it is faster.","principle":"Performance improvement is meaningful only within correctness constraints.","rule":"No performance result should be accepted as successful when correctness requirements are violated.","security_implication":"Incorrect caching, authorization checks, validation removal, or concurrency changes can create security defects while appearing faster.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Run functional and performance validation against the optimized implementation.","expected_test_result":"The implementation satisfies functional requirements while the performance claim is evaluated separately.","verification_method":"Combined correctness and performance test suite.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B47-R05","D56-B91-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B96-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Performance Safety","subtopic":"Security Tradeoffs","concept":"Preserving security controls during performance optimization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What is the correct approach when a security control contributes measurable performance cost?","answer":"The control should be optimized or redesigned only after its security requirement and protection boundary are understood, and any replacement should provide equivalent required protection before performance benefit is considered sufficient.","explanation":"Removing validation, authorization, integrity checks, encryption, isolation, or auditing solely to improve performance can create unacceptable security exposure.","principle":"Security requirements constrain acceptable performance optimizations.","rule":"Do not remove a security control merely because it appears on a performance profile.","security_implication":"Security-control removal can reduce protection even when latency or throughput improves.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Compare the original and optimized security-control paths for both performance and required security properties.","expected_test_result":"The optimized design retains the required protection properties.","verification_method":"Security property verification plus performance measurement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B47-R01","D56-B47-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B96-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Performance Safety","subtopic":"Data Integrity","concept":"Avoiding performance optimizations that weaken data integrity guarantees","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can performance optimization affect data integrity?","answer":"Changes to buffering, batching, asynchronous writes, caching, synchronization, or durability behavior can alter when and how data becomes persistent or consistent.","explanation":"An optimization can reduce latency by weakening durability or consistency guarantees unless those guarantees are explicitly preserved or intentionally changed.","principle":"Performance changes must preserve required integrity and durability semantics.","rule":"Do not classify reduced write latency as an improvement without checking the associated durability and consistency guarantees.","security_implication":"Unexpected integrity loss can become a reliability or security problem.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Compare optimized and baseline write paths under interruption or failure scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Required durability and consistency properties remain satisfied.","verification_method":"Failure-injection testing combined with performance measurement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B48-R05","D56-B96-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B96-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Performance Safety","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion","concept":"Preventing performance optimizations from creating resource exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an optimization create a new resource-exhaustion failure?","answer":"Increasing concurrency, buffering more data, retaining caches, prefetching aggressively, or reducing synchronization can raise resource consumption beyond safe capacity.","explanation":"A local performance improvement can shift pressure to memory, file descriptors, connections, queues, CPU, or other constrained resources.","principle":"Performance improvements must be evaluated under capacity boundaries and adverse workloads.","rule":"Test optimized resource usage at realistic and stress-level concurrency before treating the change as safe.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Exercise the optimized implementation at increasing concurrency while observing resource consumption and failure behavior.","expected_test_result":"Resource growth and saturation behavior remain within defined safety constraints.","verification_method":"Capacity and stress testing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B57-R01","D56-B68-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B96-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Performance Safety","subtopic":"Safe Optimization Gate","concept":"Performance acceptance requires correctness, security, and capacity checks","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What minimum conditions should a performance optimization satisfy before being considered acceptable?","answer":"It should demonstrate the intended performance effect under a relevant workload while preserving required correctness, security, reliability, and capacity constraints.","explanation":"A performance result is incomplete if it measures only the target metric and ignores system-level requirements.","principle":"Performance acceptance is a multi-dimensional verification problem.","rule":"Do not mark an optimization successful from a performance metric alone.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Evaluate an optimization across performance, correctness, security, failure behavior, and resource limits.","expected_test_result":"Acceptance occurs only when all mandatory requirements are satisfied.","verification_method":"Multi-gate validation matrix.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B96-R01","D56-B96-R02","D56-B96-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B97-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Performance Automation","subtopic":"Continuous Regression Testing","concept":"Continuous performance regression coverage","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should continuous performance regression automation preserve from the original lesson?","answer":"It should preserve the relevant workload, metric definition, measurement protocol, environment assumptions, acceptance criteria, evidence lineage, and failure classification.","explanation":"Automation should reproduce the reasoning behind the lesson rather than merely schedule an arbitrary benchmark.","principle":"Automated performance validation should preserve evidence semantics.","rule":"Do not automate a benchmark without recording what engineering claim it is intended to validate.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Trace a failed automated performance check back to its originating lesson and measurement definition.","expected_test_result":"The system can explain which claim failed and under what conditions.","verification_method":"Traceability and failure-diagnosis test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01","D56-B93-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B97-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Performance Automation","subtopic":"Flaky Benchmarks","concept":"Managing unstable performance tests","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should happen when a performance regression test produces inconsistent classifications across repeated runs?","answer":"The result should be classified as unstable or inconclusive until the sources of measurement variance are investigated and the acceptance method is improved.","explanation":"Automatically treating unstable measurements as regressions can create alert fatigue and erode trust in the test system.","principle":"Unreliable evidence should not be promoted to verified regression status.","rule":"Do not silently convert performance-test flakiness into a pass or fail result.","failure_mode":"The same implementation alternates between regression and non-regression classifications without a justified cause.","cause":"High measurement variance, unstable workload, environmental noise, or inadequate sample design.","solution":"Investigate variance, improve isolation, increase appropriate repetitions, or use a more suitable statistical decision rule.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Repeat the same benchmark under controlled conditions and classify result stability.","expected_test_result":"The system reports instability explicitly when classification remains inconsistent.","verification_method":"Repeated-run stability analysis.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R02","D56-B93-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B97-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Performance Automation","subtopic":"Failure Classification","concept":"Separating benchmark infrastructure failures from software regressions","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"Why must benchmark execution failures be separated from application performance regressions?","answer":"A benchmark can fail because the measurement environment, harness, data setup, dependency, or infrastructure is broken even when the application performance is unchanged.","explanation":"Treating infrastructure failure as a software regression produces incorrect engineering signals and can hide actual benchmark reliability problems.","principle":"Test execution status and performance result status are distinct dimensions.","rule":"Never classify an incomplete or invalid benchmark execution as a measured application regression.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Force a benchmark harness failure before application measurement and inspect the resulting classification.","expected_test_result":"The result is marked as infrastructure or execution failure rather than application regression.","verification_method":"Failure-injection test for benchmark infrastructure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R04","D56-B97-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B97-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Performance Automation","subtopic":"Baseline Management","concept":"Maintaining trustworthy performance baselines","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What makes a performance baseline trustworthy enough for regression comparison?","answer":"It should be generated under documented conditions, represent the intended workload, contain sufficient evidence for the selected acceptance method, and have an explicit version and provenance identity.","explanation":"A baseline is itself evidence. If its origin or conditions are unclear, subsequent comparisons inherit that uncertainty.","principle":"Regression detection quality cannot exceed baseline quality.","rule":"Do not use an unidentified or poorly characterized measurement as an authoritative baseline.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Inspect a baseline record for workload, environment, implementation, measurement, and provenance metadata.","expected_test_result":"Missing critical metadata prevents the baseline from being treated as fully authoritative.","verification_method":"Baseline completeness validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B93-R05","D56-B92-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B97-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Performance Automation","subtopic":"Automated Evidence Classification","concept":"Machine-readable classification of performance evidence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should automated performance results retain explicit evidence classifications?","answer":"Because a measured result, an inferred conclusion, an execution failure, an estimated effect, and an unknown result have different evidentiary meaning and should not be interchangeable.","explanation":"Explicit classification prevents downstream retrieval, reasoning, and code-generation systems from treating uncertain evidence as verified fact.","principle":"Evidence status is part of the knowledge, not incidental metadata.","rule":"Every automated performance result should preserve execution status, validation status, and performance-claim classification separately.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Feed measured, failed, inferred, and unknown records into downstream retrieval and verify that their statuses remain distinguishable.","expected_test_result":"Downstream consumers preserve the distinctions.","verification_method":"Schema and semantic classification validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01","D56-B97-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B98-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Performance Knowledge Integration","subtopic":"Retrieval Precision","concept":"Retrieving performance lessons by workload context","knowledge_type":"RETRIEVAL_RULE","question":"How should a knowledge system retrieve performance lessons without overgeneralizing them?","answer":"Retrieval should consider the relevant workload, system layer, metric, environment, implementation version, and applicability conditions rather than matching only generic optimization keywords.","explanation":"Context-aware retrieval reduces the chance that a valid lesson from a different workload is presented as directly applicable.","principle":"Performance knowledge retrieval should be condition-aware.","rule":"Rank contextual compatibility alongside semantic similarity.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Query for a performance issue using workload and metric constraints and inspect returned lessons.","expected_test_result":"Lessons matching the relevant conditions are distinguished from merely similar but incompatible lessons.","verification_method":"Context-aware retrieval evaluation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R04","D56-B93-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B98-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Performance Knowledge Integration","subtopic":"Contradictory Evidence","concept":"Preserving contradictory performance findings","knowledge_type":"CONFLICTED","question":"What should the knowledge fabric do when two performance lessons appear to contradict each other?","answer":"It should retain both findings, record their evidence and applicability conditions, and represent the contradiction explicitly until the differing conditions or evidence quality explain the apparent conflict.","explanation":"Contradictory performance results can be valid under different workloads, versions, architectures, or measurement methods.","principle":"Contradiction is information when conditions differ or evidence is incomplete.","rule":"Do not delete one performance finding merely because another finding differs.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Store two opposing performance observations with different workload conditions and query both.","expected_test_result":"Both remain available with explicit conditions and contradiction status.","verification_method":"Contradiction-aware retrieval and relationship validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R04","D56-B70-R01"],"contradicts":["CONTEXT_DEPENDENT_PERFORMANCE_FINDING"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B98-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Performance Knowledge Integration","subtopic":"Deduplication","concept":"Semantic deduplication of performance lessons","knowledge_type":"DATA_QUALITY_RULE","question":"What distinguishes a genuinely new performance lesson from a duplicate?","answer":"A new lesson should introduce a materially different causal mechanism, workload condition, intervention, tradeoff, failure mode, verification method, or evidence boundary; wording changes alone do not constitute new knowledge.","explanation":"Performance datasets can become artificially large through repeated restatements of the same optimization principle.","principle":"Knowledge density matters more than record count.","rule":"Deduplicate semantically equivalent performance lessons while preserving distinct applicability conditions.","objective":"Maintain a compact, high-information performance knowledge fabric.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Compare multiple records expressing the same bottleneck and intervention with no material difference in conditions.","expected_test_result":"Equivalent records are merged or linked rather than retained as independent lessons.","verification_method":"Semantic and relationship-aware deduplication.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B92-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B98-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Performance Knowledge Integration","subtopic":"Cross-Dataset Relationships","concept":"Linking performance lessons to implementation and testing knowledge","knowledge_type":"RELATIONSHIP","question":"How should D56 performance lessons connect to implementation and verification datasets?","answer":"Performance lessons should reference relevant implementation patterns, failure modes, tests, verification rules, and version constraints rather than duplicating those records.","explanation":"Relationships allow the knowledge fabric to combine performance reasoning with code-generation and verification knowledge while preserving dataset boundaries.","principle":"Cross-dataset relationships provide context without duplicating knowledge.","rule":"Prefer stable relationships such as requires, tests, verifies, refines, and contrasts_with over copying another dataset's content.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Resolve a performance lesson to its related implementation and verification records.","expected_test_result":"The graph provides the required context without duplicate standalone records.","verification_method":"Relationship integrity and graph traversal validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01","D56-B97-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B98-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Performance Knowledge Integration","subtopic":"Code Generation Constraints","concept":"Using performance lessons as constraints rather than automatic optimizations","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should an autonomous coding system use a performance lesson during code generation?","answer":"It should treat the lesson as contextual guidance or a constraint requiring applicability checks and verification, not as an unconditional instruction to apply an optimization.","explanation":"A performance optimization can be inappropriate under a different workload or can conflict with correctness, security, maintainability, or resource constraints.","principle":"Performance knowledge should guide reasoning before code transformation.","rule":"Do not automatically apply an optimization solely because a retrieved performance lesson exists.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Present an optimization lesson with both matching and non-matching workload conditions to a code-generation decision process.","expected_test_result":"The optimization is considered only when applicability conditions are satisfied.","verification_method":"Constraint-aware generation and verification test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R04","D56-B98-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B99-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Performance Learning Closure","subtopic":"Lesson Completeness","concept":"Complete representation of an operational performance lesson","knowledge_type":"SCHEMA_RULE","question":"What information is required for a complete D56 operational performance lesson?","answer":"A complete lesson should connect the observed bottleneck, measurement, intervention, result status, tradeoff, regression behavior, generalized lesson, applicability conditions, evidence classification, and verification method.","explanation":"The full chain preserves how an observation became reusable engineering knowledge without pretending that unverified outcomes are facts.","principle":"Operational performance knowledge should preserve observation-to-generalization lineage.","rule":"Do not mark a lesson complete when its causal or evidentiary chain is materially missing.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Validate a lesson containing every lifecycle element and compare it with a lesson missing evidence classification.","expected_test_result":"The incomplete lesson is identified as requiring additional evidence metadata.","verification_method":"Schema completeness and semantic completeness validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B70-R05","D56-B92-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B99-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Performance Learning Closure","subtopic":"Generalization Boundaries","concept":"Preventing overgeneralization from local performance evidence","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should a local performance observation not automatically become a universal engineering rule?","answer":"Because the observation may depend on workload shape, data size, architecture, runtime, hardware, concurrency, dependency versions, or measurement method.","explanation":"Generalization requires evidence that the lesson remains valid across the conditions for which it is claimed.","principle":"The scope of a performance lesson must not exceed its evidence.","rule":"State the strongest justified generalization and preserve unresolved applicability as uncertainty.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","failure_mode":"A workload-specific result becomes an unconditional optimization rule.","cause":"Evidence scope was broader than the actual experiment.","solution":"Record explicit applicability boundaries and confidence.","test_case":"Attempt to apply a workload-specific lesson outside its tested conditions.","expected_test_result":"The knowledge system flags applicability uncertainty rather than treating the lesson as universally valid.","verification_method":"Scope-aware retrieval and validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B92-R04","D56-B98-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B99-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Performance Learning Closure","subtopic":"Evidence Promotion","concept":"Promoting inferred performance knowledge only after validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When can an inferred performance lesson be promoted to a stronger evidence classification?","answer":"Only when additional evidence directly tests the relevant claim under documented conditions and the resulting evidence satisfies the dataset's validation criteria.","explanation":"Repeatedly restating an inference does not increase its evidentiary status. New evidence must address the uncertainty that caused the inference classification.","principle":"Evidence strength changes through evidence, not repetition.","rule":"Do not promote INFERRED or UNKNOWN performance claims to MEASURED or VERIFIED status without appropriate supporting evidence.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Provide an inferred lesson followed by a documented controlled experiment that directly tests the claim.","expected_test_result":"The classification changes only after the required validation evidence is present.","verification_method":"Evidence-lineage and promotion-rule validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B91-R01","D56-B97-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B99-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Performance Learning Closure","subtopic":"Negative Results","concept":"Preserving failed performance interventions as useful knowledge","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"Why should unsuccessful performance interventions be retained?","answer":"A failed intervention can reveal invalid assumptions, workload boundaries, hidden tradeoffs, or conditions under which an optimization does not work, preventing repeated investigation of the same false path.","explanation":"Negative results are valuable when their evidence and conditions are documented accurately.","principle":"Failed experiments can become reusable engineering knowledge.","rule":"Retain failed interventions when they provide distinct evidence about a performance hypothesis or boundary.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Store a failed optimization with its workload, measurement, failure reason, and applicability boundary.","expected_test_result":"Future retrieval can identify the failed approach and its conditions without treating it as a successful optimization.","verification_method":"Negative-result retrieval and classification test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B50-R05","D56-B95-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B99-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Performance Learning Closure","subtopic":"Lesson Quality Gate","concept":"Final quality gate for performance lessons","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be checked before a performance lesson is accepted into the locked D56 dataset?","answer":"The lesson should be checked for scope coverage, distinctness, evidence classification, applicability conditions, version awareness, provenance status, contradiction handling, truthful execution status, and verification completeness.","explanation":"A final quality gate prevents attractive but unsupported performance claims from becoming durable knowledge.","principle":"Dataset acceptance requires evidence integrity as well as structural validity.","rule":"Reject or mark incomplete any record that fabricates results, provenance, execution, benchmarks, or operational experience.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Run structural, semantic, evidence, deduplication, and relationship checks over the complete D56 record set.","expected_test_result":"Unsupported claims and duplicate or incomplete records are identified before locking.","verification_method":"Multi-stage dataset quality gate.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B98-R03","D56-B99-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B100-R01","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"D56 Completion","subtopic":"Coverage Validation","concept":"Final D56 scope coverage validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should final D56 scope coverage be validated before dataset locking?","answer":"Map every required performance-lesson category to records or explicit known gaps, verify that each category has distinct evidence-driven knowledge, and confirm that no required area was omitted merely because record-count targets were reached.","explanation":"D56 completion is determined by knowledge coverage and quality rather than by filling a numerical quota.","principle":"Coverage defines completion; record count does not define quality.","rule":"Do not lock D56 until the full defined scope has been reviewed for meaningful coverage and known gaps are explicitly recorded.","objective":"Ensure D56 is complete enough for downstream retrieval, reasoning, debugging, testing, and learning.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Run a scope-to-record coverage matrix over all D56 batches.","expected_test_result":"Every defined scope category is mapped to relevant records or an explicit unresolved gap.","verification_method":"Coverage matrix plus semantic review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B99-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B100-R02","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"D56 Completion","subtopic":"Deduplication Validation","concept":"Final semantic deduplication before locking","knowledge_type":"DATA_QUALITY_RULE","question":"What final deduplication checks should D56 pass before locking?","answer":"D56 should be checked for exact duplicates, near duplicates, semantic duplicates, repeated implementation patterns, duplicated lessons expressed through different wording, and unnecessary duplication across related datasets.","explanation":"Final deduplication protects retrieval quality and keeps the knowledge fabric information-dense.","principle":"Distinct records should represent distinct reusable knowledge.","rule":"Remove or relationally consolidate records that add no materially new knowledge.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Compare all D56 records using normalized concepts, causal mechanisms, conditions, interventions, and verification requirements.","expected_test_result":"Superficial variants are identified while genuinely distinct lessons remain separate.","verification_method":"Multi-level deduplication audit.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B98-R03","D56-B99-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B100-R03","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"D56 Completion","subtopic":"Truthfulness Validation","concept":"Final truthfulness audit for performance claims","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must the final D56 truthfulness audit verify?","answer":"It must verify that no record presents fabricated measurements, benchmarks, execution results, operational experience, provenance, verification, or universal conclusions as facts.","explanation":"D56 is specifically intended to preserve operational-style lessons without inventing operational history. Truthfulness therefore requires explicit separation of evidence from inference and test design.","principle":"Unsupported performance claims must remain explicitly uncertain or classified according to their actual evidence state.","rule":"Never promote generated examples or engineering reasoning into measured operational experience without evidence.","performance_claim_classification":"UNKNOWN","test_case":"Scan records for measured claims lacking evidence, execution claims lacking execution status, and provenance claims lacking sources.","expected_test_result":"Unsupported claims are downgraded, corrected, or marked as unresolved.","verification_method":"Evidence-status consistency audit.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B99-R03","D56-B99-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B100-R04","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"D56 Completion","subtopic":"Machine Readiness","concept":"Final machine-readability and relationship integrity audit","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should the final structural audit verify before D56 becomes available to the Autonomous Software Engineering Brain?","answer":"It should verify valid JSONL structure, stable IDs, required schema fields, valid enumerations, unique identifiers, intact relationships, consistent dataset and schema versions, explicit execution and validation states, and absence of malformed records.","explanation":"Machine-readable knowledge must be structurally reliable before downstream retrieval or reasoning systems consume it.","principle":"Structural correctness is a prerequisite for semantic reuse.","rule":"Do not integrate malformed or ambiguously typed records into the knowledge fabric.","test_case":"Parse every D56 record and validate schema, IDs, relationships, enumerations, and references.","expected_test_result":"Every record is parseable and all referenced relationships satisfy structural constraints or are explicitly marked as unresolved external relationships.","verification_method":"Automated schema, JSONL, identifier, enumeration, and relationship validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B98-R04","D56-B100-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D56-B100-R05","dataset_id":"D56","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"D56 Completion","subtopic":"Dataset Lock Readiness","concept":"Final lock criteria for D56","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What conditions should be satisfied before D56 is locked?","answer":"D56 should be locked only after scope coverage, structural validation, semantic validation, deduplication, evidence classification, version awareness, contradiction handling, relationship integrity, and known limitations have been reviewed and recorded.","explanation":"Locking freezes a validated dataset phase so downstream datasets can reference stable IDs and relationships without continual structural drift.","principle":"A dataset is locked only after its quality state is explicitly known.","rule":"Do not mark D56 locked merely because all planned batches have been generated.","objective":"Establish a stable, auditable baseline for downstream Knowledge Fabric integration.","requirements":["Scope coverage reviewed","All records structurally valid","Duplicates reviewed","Evidence classifications truthful","Execution status truthful","Relationships validated","Known gaps documented","Version metadata consistent"],"expected_behavior":"The dataset receives a lock-ready status only when all mandatory quality gates pass or explicitly documented exceptions remain.","performance_claim_classification":"INFERRED","test_case":"Run the complete D56 lock checklist against the finalized dataset.","expected_test_result":"The dataset is either LOCK_READY or remains NOT_LOCKED with explicit blocking findings.","verification_method":"Final dataset lock audit.","verification_guidance":"Record unresolved issues rather than silently treating them as resolved.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D56-B99-R05","D56-B100-R01","D56-B100-R02","D56-B100-R03","D56-B100-R04"]}

{"record_id":"D57-B01-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B01","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Issue Identification","concept":"Separating observed security symptoms from confirmed security issues","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should a security lesson distinguish an observed security symptom from a confirmed security issue?","answer":"A security lesson should record the observed signal separately from the confirmed security issue and should not treat an alert, anomaly, or suspicion as proof of compromise or vulnerability without supporting evidence.","explanation":"Security telemetry can indicate unusual behavior without establishing its cause. A useful lesson preserves the original observation, the evidence used for analysis, the resulting classification, and any remaining uncertainty.","principle":"Security conclusions must not exceed the evidence supporting them.","rule":"Do not convert an alert, anomaly, scan finding, or suspicious event directly into a confirmed security incident without validation.","objective":"Preserve accurate security reasoning from initial observation through final classification.","requirements":["Original security signal","Evidence supporting classification","Explicit uncertainty","Security issue classification"],"preconditions":["A security-relevant signal exists"],"expected_behavior":"The knowledge record distinguishes observed evidence from the security conclusion derived from that evidence.","failure_mode":"A suspected security issue is recorded as confirmed without sufficient evidence.","cause":"Evidence and conclusion were not represented separately.","solution":"Store observation, evidence, classification, and confidence as distinct fields.","edge_case":"A high-confidence automated detection may still require separate verification before being treated as a confirmed outcome.","security_implication":"Overstating evidence can cause unnecessary response actions, while understating evidence can delay containment.","performance_implication":"Additional verification may add processing cost but improves decision reliability.","test_case":"Create records for an alert with insufficient evidence and for a validated security finding.","test_input":"Alert-only record versus evidence-backed finding.","expected_test_result":"The alert-only case remains uncertain while the evidence-backed case can receive a stronger classification.","testing_guidance":"Verify that evidence status cannot be silently promoted during ingestion or retrieval.","verification_method":"Evidence-classification validation.","verification_guidance":"Inspect observation, evidence, conclusion, and confidence independently.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","version_range":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":[]}
{"record_id":"D57-B01-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B01","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Evidence Classification","concept":"Evidence strength in security lessons","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should security evidence strength be explicitly classified?","answer":"Security evidence should be classified because direct observations, correlated indicators, analyst inferences, generated hypotheses, and verified outcomes have different evidentiary value.","explanation":"A security knowledge system must prevent weak evidence from being consumed as established fact. Explicit evidence classification allows downstream reasoning to distinguish what was observed from what was inferred.","principle":"Evidence status is part of the security knowledge itself.","rule":"Every security lesson should preserve whether its conclusion is observed, corroborated, inferred, verified, conflicted, or unknown.","objective":"Prevent unsupported security conclusions from becoming durable knowledge.","constraints":["Evidence classification must be explicit","Inference must not be represented as fact","Conflicting evidence must remain visible"],"expected_behavior":"Downstream retrieval and reasoning preserve the distinction between evidence and interpretation.","failure_mode":"An inferred security conclusion is later retrieved as a verified fact.","cause":"Evidence classification was omitted or lost during dataset transformation.","solution":"Require explicit evidence and validation fields in the canonical schema.","security_implication":"Evidence confusion can lead to inappropriate containment, remediation, or risk decisions.","test_case":"Retrieve security records with VERIFIED, INFERRED, and UNKNOWN evidence states.","expected_test_result":"The three states remain distinguishable to downstream consumers.","testing_guidance":"Test serialization, ingestion, retrieval, and transformation pipelines for evidence-state preservation.","verification_method":"Schema and semantic evidence-state validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B01-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B01","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Issue Scope","concept":"Defining the affected security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What should a security lesson record about the scope of an issue?","answer":"It should identify the affected component, assets, identities, data, environment, trust boundary, relevant time window, and known affected population to the extent supported by evidence.","explanation":"Security remediation depends on knowing what was actually exposed or affected. Scope should remain evidence-bounded rather than being inferred from the existence of one affected component.","principle":"Security scope must be established independently from the existence of the initial finding.","rule":"Do not assume that a vulnerable or compromised component represents the entire system scope.","objective":"Create an evidence-bounded representation of security impact.","requirements":["Affected asset","Affected component","Relevant trust boundary","Known time window","Evidence-supported scope"],"edge_case":"The affected population may remain partially unknown during early investigation.","security_implication":"Under-scoping can leave affected assets unprotected; over-scoping can cause unnecessary disruption.","test_case":"Represent a finding affecting one component while the status of adjacent components remains unknown.","test_input":"Component A confirmed affected; Components B and C unverified.","expected_test_result":"Only Component A is confirmed affected and B/C remain explicitly unresolved.","verification_method":"Scope-boundary validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B01-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B01","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"False Security Conclusions","concept":"Avoiding premature attribution","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should a security lesson avoid premature attribution of an event to a specific actor, vulnerability, or root cause?","answer":"Because early indicators can have multiple explanations and attribution requires evidence beyond correlation or superficial similarity.","explanation":"A suspicious event may result from benign activity, configuration error, software defect, compromised credentials, or malicious action. The lesson should preserve uncertainty until the relevant evidence is established.","principle":"Security attribution requires evidence proportional to the claim.","rule":"Do not identify an actor, exploit path, or root cause solely from temporal correlation or a matching indicator.","failure_mode":"An unsupported attribution becomes embedded in the security knowledge base.","cause":"Correlation was treated as causation or attribution.","solution":"Record competing hypotheses and evidence supporting or weakening each hypothesis.","security_implication":"False attribution can direct response resources toward the wrong cause and obscure the actual security mechanism.","test_case":"Create multiple plausible explanations for the same security signal and evaluate classification.","expected_test_result":"The system retains competing hypotheses until evidence resolves them.","verification_method":"Hypothesis and evidence consistency review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R01","D57-B01-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B01-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B01","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Lesson Capture","concept":"Minimum evidence chain for a security lesson","knowledge_type":"SCHEMA_RULE","question":"What minimum evidence chain should a D57 security lesson preserve?","answer":"A D57 lesson should preserve the security issue, supporting evidence, analysis or root cause, remediation, verification status, residual risk, and generalized lesson, with explicit uncertainty wherever any stage remains unresolved.","explanation":"This chain prevents a remediation statement from being detached from the issue it addressed and prevents an unverified action from being recorded as a successful security outcome.","principle":"A security lesson should preserve the complete reasoning chain from issue to verified learning.","rule":"Do not record remediation as a completed security lesson without preserving what it was intended to address and how its effect was verified.","objective":"Make security lessons reusable for detection, diagnosis, remediation, verification, and future prevention.","requirements":["Security issue","Evidence","Root cause or unresolved cause","Remediation","Verification","Residual risk","Generalized lesson"],"expected_behavior":"Each lesson can be traced from issue identification through remediation and verification status.","security_implication":"Incomplete chains can cause repeated security failures or ineffective remediation.","test_case":"Validate a complete lesson against a lesson containing remediation but no verification state.","expected_test_result":"The incomplete lesson is flagged as not fully verified.","verification_method":"Security-lesson completeness validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B02-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B02","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Root Cause Analysis","concept":"Distinguishing immediate cause from systemic root cause","knowledge_type":"CAUSE","question":"Why should a security lesson distinguish the immediate cause from the systemic root cause?","answer":"The immediate cause explains how the security issue manifested, while the systemic root cause explains why the condition existed or remained possible. Both can be necessary for durable remediation.","explanation":"Fixing only the immediate trigger may remove one manifestation while leaving the underlying control weakness unchanged.","principle":"Effective security learning addresses both manifestation and enabling conditions.","rule":"Do not treat the first technical fault identified during investigation as the complete root cause without examining contributing control failures.","objective":"Capture causes at appropriate levels without collapsing them into one unsupported explanation.","failure_mode":"A local symptom is fixed while the systemic condition remains.","cause":"Root-cause analysis stopped at the first observable technical failure.","solution":"Separate immediate cause, contributing causes, systemic cause, and unresolved hypotheses.","edge_case":"Some incidents genuinely have a single well-supported cause; the model should allow that rather than forcing artificial causal layers.","security_implication":"Incomplete root-cause analysis increases recurrence risk.","test_case":"Represent an issue caused by an incorrect configuration that was enabled by an inadequate configuration-control process.","expected_test_result":"The immediate configuration cause and systemic process weakness remain distinct.","verification_method":"Causal-chain review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B02-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B02","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Root Cause Analysis","concept":"Control failure as a security root cause","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can a missing or ineffective security control be treated as a root-cause candidate?","answer":"A control can be a root-cause candidate when evidence shows that its absence, incorrect configuration, failure, or ineffective operation materially enabled the security condition.","explanation":"Security issues frequently involve control weaknesses rather than a single defective code statement. The lesson should identify the control and evidence connecting it to the issue.","principle":"Security root causes may exist at the control, process, architecture, implementation, or operational level.","rule":"Identify a control as causal only when evidence establishes its relationship to the security issue.","security_implication":"Control-focused lessons can address recurrence beyond the immediate technical symptom.","test_case":"Map a security issue to a specific missing control and record the evidence establishing the relationship.","expected_test_result":"The control is represented as a causal candidate with an explicit evidence status.","verification_method":"Control-to-issue causal mapping review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B02-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B02","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Root Cause Analysis","concept":"Avoiding root-cause monoculture","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is root-cause monoculture in security analysis?","answer":"It is the practice of forcing a security issue into a single root-cause explanation when multiple independent or interacting causes may have contributed.","explanation":"Security failures can emerge from combinations of technical, configuration, architectural, procedural, and human-system factors. A single-cause model can hide important remediation opportunities.","principle":"Security causality may be multi-factor and should be represented accordingly.","rule":"Do not force a single root cause when the evidence supports multiple contributing causes.","failure_mode":"One cause is recorded while additional enabling conditions are lost.","cause":"The analysis model permits only one causal explanation.","solution":"Support multiple causal relationships with evidence and confidence per relationship.","security_implication":"Missing contributing causes can leave exploitable paths unchanged.","test_case":"Represent a security issue involving an implementation defect and a compensating-control failure.","expected_test_result":"Both causes can be represented without requiring one to replace the other.","verification_method":"Multi-cause relationship validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B02-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B02-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B02","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Root Cause Verification","concept":"Testing root-cause hypotheses before remediation closure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should a proposed security root cause be tested where practical?","answer":"Testing the causal hypothesis helps distinguish a plausible explanation from a supported explanation and can reveal additional causes or incorrect assumptions.","explanation":"A remediation may appear appropriate even when the assumed root cause is wrong. Root-cause verification therefore provides an important link between investigation and remediation.","principle":"Root-cause confidence should be based on evidence, not explanatory convenience.","rule":"Do not mark a root cause as verified solely because the proposed explanation is plausible.","test_case":"Define a causal hypothesis and a controlled observation that would support or weaken it.","test_input":"Security issue plus proposed causal condition.","expected_test_result":"The hypothesis receives an evidence-based status rather than automatic confirmation.","testing_guidance":"Use safe validation methods appropriate to the security context and avoid introducing additional exposure.","verification_method":"Controlled causal validation or independent evidence corroboration.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B01-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B02-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B02","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Causal Uncertainty","concept":"Preserving unresolved root-cause uncertainty","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"How should D57 represent an unresolved security root cause?","answer":"It should explicitly record the unresolved state, known evidence, candidate causes, eliminated hypotheses where supported, and required follow-up rather than inventing a definitive root cause.","explanation":"Security investigations may end before every causal question is resolved. Preserving uncertainty is more useful than creating a false explanation that downstream systems may later treat as fact.","principle":"Unknown causality is preferable to fabricated certainty.","rule":"Use UNKNOWN or equivalent evidence classification when the root cause has not been established.","security_implication":"Explicit uncertainty prevents unsafe remediation or future detection logic from being based on unsupported assumptions.","test_case":"Create a security lesson with insufficient evidence to distinguish two plausible causes.","expected_test_result":"Both candidates remain represented and the root cause remains unresolved.","verification_method":"Uncertainty-state validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R02","D57-B02-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B03-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B03","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Remediation","concept":"Remediation must address the identified cause","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should determine the scope of a security remediation?","answer":"The remediation should address the confirmed security issue and its evidence-supported causal conditions while considering affected assets, dependencies, residual exposure, and required verification.","explanation":"A remediation that changes an unrelated symptom may reduce visible activity without eliminating the security condition.","principle":"Remediation should be causally connected to the security issue it is intended to resolve.","rule":"Do not classify an unrelated configuration or code change as remediation for a security issue without evidence connecting it to risk reduction.","objective":"Make remediation traceable to the security problem and its causal mechanism.","requirements":["Issue-to-remediation relationship","Affected scope","Security objective","Verification criteria","Residual-risk assessment"],"security_implication":"Causally weak remediation can leave the exploitable condition intact.","test_case":"Map a proposed remediation to a security issue and its identified cause.","expected_test_result":"The remediation has an explicit relationship to the issue and causal condition.","verification_method":"Remediation traceability review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B03-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B03","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Remediation","concept":"Temporary mitigation versus permanent remediation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should temporary mitigation be distinguished from permanent remediation?","answer":"A temporary mitigation reduces immediate exposure while a permanent remediation addresses the underlying condition. They can serve different objectives and should not share the same completion status.","explanation":"Blocking a pathway, disabling a feature, isolating an asset, or adding temporary monitoring may reduce risk without fixing the underlying defect or control weakness.","principle":"Risk reduction and root-cause removal are distinct outcomes.","rule":"Do not mark a temporary mitigation as permanent remediation unless evidence shows that the underlying issue has been resolved.","tradeoff":"Temporary mitigations can reduce immediate risk quickly but may introduce operational cost or residual exposure.","security_implication":"Confusing mitigation with remediation can create false closure.","test_case":"Record a temporary containment action followed by a planned permanent fix.","expected_test_result":"The mitigation and permanent remediation remain separately represented with independent verification states.","verification_method":"Remediation-state validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B03-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B03-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B03","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Remediation","concept":"Avoiding remediation side effects","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why must security remediation be checked for unintended side effects?","answer":"A remediation can reduce one security risk while introducing availability, integrity, compatibility, privacy, or operational risks elsewhere.","explanation":"Security changes operate within a larger system. Disabling a component, changing permissions, tightening validation, or altering network behavior can create new failure modes if dependencies are not considered.","principle":"Security remediation must be evaluated as a system change, not an isolated action.","rule":"Do not treat remediation as successful solely because the original security condition disappears.","failure_mode":"The original security issue is reduced while a new unacceptable risk is introduced.","cause":"Remediation validation considered only the target issue.","solution":"Verify the original security objective and relevant system safety properties after the change.","security_implication":"A security fix that creates a new exploitable or unsafe condition is not a complete security success.","performance_implication":"Security hardening may change resource or latency behavior and should be measured when material.","test_case":"Apply a security remediation in a representative test environment and evaluate security plus functional behavior.","expected_test_result":"The intended security property improves without violating required system constraints.","verification_method":"Security regression and system regression testing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B03-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B03-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B03","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Remediation","concept":"Least-change remediation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the value of limiting a security remediation to the smallest justified change?","answer":"A narrowly scoped remediation can reduce unintended side effects and simplify verification, rollback, and attribution while still addressing the identified security condition.","explanation":"The smallest change is not always the safest solution, but unnecessary change expands the number of behaviors that must be evaluated.","principle":"Remediation scope should be proportionate to the security problem and verification capability.","rule":"Avoid unrelated changes in a security remediation unless evidence shows they are required to address the issue.","tradeoff":"Broader redesign may be justified when the current architecture cannot safely eliminate the root cause, but it requires broader verification.","security_implication":"Unnecessary changes can create new attack surface or configuration errors.","test_case":"Compare a targeted remediation with a broad unrelated change for the same security issue.","expected_test_result":"The targeted change has a narrower verification surface when both satisfy the security objective.","verification_method":"Change-scope and verification-scope comparison.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B03-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B03-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B03","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Remediation Verification","concept":"Defining remediation success before implementation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should remediation success criteria be defined before or alongside the remediation?","answer":"Explicit success criteria make it possible to determine whether the security condition was actually reduced, whether required behavior remains intact, and whether residual risk is acceptable or unresolved.","explanation":"Without predefined criteria, teams can mistake implementation completion for security effectiveness.","principle":"Implementation completion and security effectiveness are different states.","rule":"Do not equate deploying a remediation with verifying its security outcome.","objective":"Separate action execution from security verification.","test_case":"Define security success criteria for a remediation and evaluate the post-change state against them.","expected_test_result":"The remediation can be classified as verified, partially verified, failed, or unresolved based on evidence.","verification_method":"Predefined security acceptance criteria plus post-change validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B03-R01","D57-B04-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B04-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B04","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Verification","concept":"Action executed is not equivalent to security outcome verified","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must remediation execution status be separated from security verification status?","answer":"An action can execute successfully while failing to address the intended security condition, affecting the wrong scope, or producing an unknown result.","explanation":"Execution answers whether the prescribed action ran. Verification answers whether the security property or risk condition changed as intended.","principle":"Action state and outcome state are separate dimensions.","rule":"Never mark a security lesson VERIFIED merely because the remediation action completed successfully.","objective":"Preserve truthful distinction between action execution and security outcome.","security_implication":"Confusing execution with verification creates false security closure.","test_case":"Represent a successfully executed remediation whose security effectiveness has not yet been tested.","expected_test_result":"Execution is marked successful while verification remains unresolved or NOT_VERIFIED.","verification_method":"State-separation validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B03-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B04-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B04","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Verification","concept":"Negative security verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a security verification test prove after remediation?","answer":"It should test that the security condition addressed by the remediation is no longer present or is reduced according to the defined acceptance criteria, while also checking relevant regression conditions.","explanation":"A remediation test should target the security property itself rather than merely confirm that the changed code or configuration exists.","principle":"Verification must test the security objective, not only the implementation artifact.","rule":"Do not use deployment success, configuration presence, or code existence as the sole proof of security effectiveness.","test_case":"Define a test that reproduces the security condition safely before remediation and checks the expected protected state afterward.","test_input":"Controlled security test case representing the relevant condition.","expected_test_result":"The protected state satisfies the predefined security acceptance criterion.","testing_guidance":"Use safe isolated test conditions and do not introduce unnecessary real-world exposure.","verification_method":"Security property test plus regression checks.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B04-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B04","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Verification","concept":"Verification scope must match remediation scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security verification cover the actual affected scope?","answer":"A remediation can work in one component, environment, or configuration while the same security condition remains elsewhere.","explanation":"Verification that is narrower than the remediation's claimed scope cannot establish that the entire intended security objective was achieved.","principle":"Verification claims must not exceed verification coverage.","rule":"Do not generalize a verified fix from one tested instance to untested instances without evidence supporting the generalization.","security_implication":"Partial verification can leave unprotected assets falsely classified as secure.","test_case":"Verify a remediation in one environment while another environment remains untested.","expected_test_result":"The verified scope is limited to the tested environment and the other environment remains unresolved.","verification_method":"Scope-aware verification audit.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R03","D57-B04-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B04-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B04","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Verification","concept":"Independent verification evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When is independent verification valuable for a security remediation?","answer":"Independent verification is valuable when the original implementer may be biased toward confirming success, when the change is high impact, or when the security consequence is difficult to infer from implementation evidence alone.","explanation":"Independent review or testing can provide a separate evidence path and reduce confirmation bias.","principle":"Higher-risk security conclusions benefit from stronger evidence independence.","rule":"Use independent verification when the security decision warrants evidence beyond implementation self-reporting.","security_implication":"Independent verification can detect incomplete fixes or incorrect assumptions before closure.","test_case":"Compare self-reported remediation completion with an independent security verification procedure.","expected_test_result":"The security status is based on verification evidence rather than self-report alone.","verification_method":"Independent review, test, or evidence corroboration.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R01","D57-B04-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B04-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B04","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Verification Failure","concept":"Recording failed or inconclusive security verification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should D57 represent a security verification that fails or cannot establish the expected result?","answer":"The lesson should retain the attempted verification, observed result, failure or uncertainty, and required follow-up rather than converting the result into successful verification.","explanation":"Failed verification is important security knowledge because it identifies an unresolved protection gap or an inadequate verification method.","principle":"Verification failure is a meaningful outcome, not missing data to be hidden.","rule":"Never mark a failed or inconclusive security verification as VERIFIED.","security_implication":"False verification can create a dangerous assumption that the security condition is resolved.","test_case":"Run a verification test that does not satisfy the acceptance criterion.","expected_test_result":"The lesson remains unresolved or failed and identifies the required next step.","verification_method":"Verification-state transition test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R01","D57-B04-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B05-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B05","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Residual Risk","concept":"Residual risk after remediation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must residual risk be recorded after security remediation?","answer":"Because eliminating one known security condition does not necessarily eliminate all related exposure, alternative attack paths, uncertainty, or future recurrence conditions.","explanation":"Security remediation should reduce a defined risk, but the remaining security state may still contain other weaknesses or unknowns.","principle":"Security closure does not imply zero residual risk.","rule":"Record residual risk explicitly rather than assuming that verified remediation means complete security.","objective":"Preserve what remains uncertain or exposed after remediation.","requirements":["Post-remediation security state","Remaining exposure","Unresolved assumptions","Follow-up requirements"],"security_implication":"Explicit residual risk prevents false confidence and supports prioritization of remaining security work.","test_case":"Record a verified remediation while an alternative untested attack path remains.","expected_test_result":"The remediation is verified for its intended condition while residual risk remains explicitly recorded.","verification_method":"Residual-risk review against the verified scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B05-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B05","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Residual Risk","concept":"Unknown residual risk","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"How should D57 represent residual risk that cannot currently be quantified or fully characterized?","answer":"It should be recorded as unknown or unresolved with the evidence supporting the known portion of the security state and the investigation required to reduce uncertainty.","explanation":"Unknown residual risk is different from zero residual risk. The distinction is essential for safe downstream decision-making.","principle":"Absence of evidence about remaining exposure is not evidence of absence of exposure.","rule":"Do not represent unmeasured residual risk as zero.","security_implication":"Treating unknown risk as zero can cause premature security closure.","test_case":"Complete remediation verification while visibility into an adjacent component remains incomplete.","expected_test_result":"The known remediation outcome is recorded while residual uncertainty remains explicit.","verification_method":"Residual-risk state validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R01","D57-B01-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B05-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B05","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Residual Risk","concept":"Compensating controls as residual-risk management","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can compensating controls be represented in a security lesson?","answer":"A compensating control should be recorded as a separate control that reduces residual exposure when the primary remediation cannot yet fully eliminate the underlying condition.","explanation":"Compensating controls can reduce risk while a permanent remediation is unavailable, but they should not be mistaken for removal of the original root cause.","principle":"Risk reduction through compensation is distinct from root-cause elimination.","rule":"Do not mark a compensating control as permanent remediation unless it actually satisfies the required security objective.","tradeoff":"Compensating controls may introduce monitoring, operational, performance, or maintenance costs.","security_implication":"A compensating control can reduce exposure while preserving explicit residual risk.","test_case":"Represent a temporary compensating control while the original defect remains unresolved.","expected_test_result":"The control is linked to residual-risk reduction while the underlying issue remains open.","verification_method":"Control-effectiveness and residual-risk review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B03-R02","D57-B05-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B05-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B05","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Risk Reassessment","concept":"Reassessing residual risk after system changes","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should residual security risk be reassessed after material system changes?","answer":"A change can remove, increase, or alter attack paths and control effectiveness, so the residual-risk state established before the change may no longer apply.","explanation":"Security risk is conditional on the system state. Architecture, dependencies, configuration, identities, and data flows can change the assumptions behind an earlier assessment.","principle":"Residual-risk knowledge is state-dependent.","rule":"Reassess material residual risks when relevant system or threat conditions change.","security_implication":"Stale residual-risk assessments can conceal newly introduced exposure.","test_case":"Change a dependency or trust boundary associated with a previously assessed residual risk and trigger reassessment.","expected_test_result":"The lesson is flagged for reassessment when its applicability conditions change.","verification_method":"Dependency-aware security-risk validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R01","D57-B02-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B05-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B05","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Risk Communication","concept":"Machine-readable residual-risk language","knowledge_type":"SCHEMA_RULE","question":"Why should residual risk use structured states instead of vague security language?","answer":"Structured states such as VERIFIED_REDUCED, PARTIALLY_REDUCED, UNKNOWN, UNRESOLVED, or ACCEPTED_WITH_EVIDENCE preserve the actual security conclusion more accurately than vague labels such as safe or fixed.","explanation":"Machine reasoning systems need explicit states to avoid treating ambiguous language as a definitive security conclusion.","principle":"Security state should be represented with explicit semantics.","rule":"Avoid ambiguous closure terms that do not identify evidence, scope, or remaining uncertainty.","security_implication":"Precise states reduce the chance of false security assumptions in downstream automation.","test_case":"Encode equivalent security outcomes using structured states and ambiguous prose labels.","expected_test_result":"Structured states preserve distinctions that vague labels lose.","verification_method":"Schema semantics and retrieval validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R01","D57-B05-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B06-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B06","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Generalization","concept":"Generalizing a security lesson beyond its original incident","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"When can a security lesson be generalized beyond the original security issue?","answer":"Only when the evidence and causal mechanism support applicability to other components, architectures, configurations, or workloads, and the boundaries of that generalization are explicitly recorded.","explanation":"A local security finding may expose a reusable control weakness, but the existence of one finding does not prove that every similar component has the same issue.","principle":"The scope of a generalized security lesson must not exceed its evidence.","rule":"State applicability boundaries when transferring a lesson from one security issue to other contexts.","objective":"Produce reusable security knowledge without overgeneralization.","edge_case":"A documented invariant or formal security property may support broader generalization than a single observational incident.","security_implication":"Overgeneralization can produce incorrect detections or unnecessary remediation.","test_case":"Attempt to apply a security lesson from one component to another with different architecture and controls.","expected_test_result":"The system identifies applicability as uncertain unless supporting evidence exists.","verification_method":"Context-aware security lesson validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R03","D57-B05-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B06-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B06","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Generalization","concept":"Security lesson applicability conditions","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What conditions should accompany a generalized security lesson?","answer":"The lesson should identify the security mechanism, affected system characteristics, assumptions, relevant versions, applicable trust boundaries, and evidence scope that justify the generalization.","explanation":"Applicability metadata allows retrieval systems to distinguish reusable principles from incident-specific observations.","principle":"Security lessons require explicit conditions when their validity is contextual.","rule":"Do not publish contextual security findings as unconditional rules.","requirements":["Applicability conditions","System context","Version scope","Evidence scope","Known exclusions"],"security_implication":"Context-aware security knowledge reduces inappropriate automated action.","test_case":"Retrieve a lesson under matching and non-matching applicability conditions.","expected_test_result":"The matching context receives stronger applicability while the non-matching context remains qualified.","verification_method":"Condition-aware retrieval validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B06-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B06-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B06","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Generalization","concept":"Negative security lessons","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"Why should failed security approaches be preserved as lessons?","answer":"A failed security approach can reveal invalid assumptions, ineffective controls, incomplete remediation, or unexpected tradeoffs and can prevent future repetition of the same mistake.","explanation":"Negative security evidence is useful when the failure conditions and evidence are accurately preserved.","principle":"Security learning includes unsuccessful approaches as well as successful remediation.","rule":"Retain a failed security approach when it adds distinct evidence about a security mechanism or boundary.","security_implication":"Negative lessons can prevent recurrence of ineffective security responses.","test_case":"Record a proposed control that fails to satisfy its security acceptance criterion.","expected_test_result":"The approach is preserved as failed or ineffective rather than transformed into a successful recommendation.","verification_method":"Outcome-classification validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B06-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B06","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Generalization","concept":"Security lesson boundaries","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is the risk of converting an incident-specific observation into a universal security rule?","answer":"The rule may generate false positives, inappropriate remediation, missed alternative causes, or incorrect assumptions when applied to systems with different controls or architecture.","explanation":"Security behavior is often conditional. A lesson should generalize only the mechanism that evidence supports.","principle":"Reusable security knowledge must preserve its boundary conditions.","rule":"Do not remove incident-specific assumptions when converting an observation into generalized knowledge.","failure_mode":"A local security pattern becomes a universal rule without evidence.","cause":"Generalization exceeded evidence scope.","solution":"Preserve conditions, exclusions, and evidence classification during generalization.","security_implication":"Overgeneralized rules can distort security detection and response decisions.","test_case":"Apply a generalized lesson to systems that lack the original causal conditions.","expected_test_result":"The lesson is marked not directly applicable or uncertain.","verification_method":"Applicability-boundary testing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B06-R01","D57-B06-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B06-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B06","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Generalization","concept":"Promotion from incident lesson to security principle","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should exist before an incident lesson is promoted into a reusable security principle?","answer":"The causal mechanism should be sufficiently understood, applicability conditions should be explicit, supporting evidence should be adequate, contradictory evidence should be addressed, and the generalized statement should remain within the evidence scope.","explanation":"Promotion changes the role of a record from a contextual lesson to reusable knowledge. That transition requires stronger reasoning than simply documenting the original incident.","principle":"Generalization is an evidence transformation and must be validated as such.","rule":"Do not promote a contextual incident lesson into a general security principle without checking its evidence and applicability boundaries.","test_case":"Evaluate an incident-specific lesson for promotion to a reusable principle.","expected_test_result":"Promotion occurs only when the generalization criteria are satisfied; otherwise the lesson remains contextual.","verification_method":"Generalization quality gate.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B06-R01","D57-B06-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B07-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B07","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Version Awareness","concept":"Version-bounded security lessons","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should security lessons record affected software and dependency versions when relevant?","answer":"Security behavior, vulnerabilities, defaults, mitigations, and control effectiveness can change between versions, so a lesson may not apply uniformly across all versions.","explanation":"Version metadata helps prevent historical security findings from being incorrectly applied to newer or different implementations.","principle":"Security knowledge should be version-aware when behavior or exposure is version-dependent.","rule":"Record relevant version ranges whenever the security issue or remediation depends on implementation or dependency version.","security_implication":"Incorrect version assumptions can cause missed vulnerabilities or unnecessary remediation.","test_case":"Associate a security lesson with an affected version range and retrieve it for an out-of-range version.","expected_test_result":"The system marks applicability as incompatible, uncertain, or requiring review rather than automatically applying the lesson.","verification_method":"Version-aware applicability validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B06-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B07-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B07","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Version Awareness","concept":"Security remediation regression across versions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a security remediation become ineffective after a version change?","answer":"A version change can alter interfaces, defaults, behavior, dependency relationships, configuration semantics, or attack surface in ways that invalidate the original remediation assumptions.","explanation":"A remediation is effective within the conditions under which it was verified. Material changes should trigger reassessment when they affect those conditions.","principle":"Remediation effectiveness is conditional on relevant system versions and assumptions.","rule":"Do not assume a previously verified security remediation remains effective after material version changes without appropriate evidence.","failure_mode":"A historical security fix is treated as current despite changed implementation behavior.","cause":"Version changes were not connected to security lesson applicability.","solution":"Track version dependencies and trigger revalidation when relevant versions change.","security_implication":"Stale remediation assumptions can leave a newly changed system exposed.","test_case":"Change a security-relevant dependency version and evaluate whether the existing lesson remains applicable.","expected_test_result":"The lesson becomes subject to revalidation when its assumptions may have changed.","verification_method":"Version-triggered security revalidation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B07-R01","D57-B05-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B07-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B07","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Version Awareness","concept":"Security control default changes","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should a security lesson handle a control whose default behavior changes across versions?","answer":"The lesson should distinguish behavior by version and avoid assuming that a configuration or mitigation required in one version has identical meaning in another.","explanation":"Defaults can affect security posture even when application code is unchanged. Version-specific behavior therefore belongs in the applicability model.","principle":"Security configuration semantics are part of version-aware knowledge.","rule":"Do not copy security configuration guidance across versions without checking the relevant default and semantic behavior.","security_implication":"Incorrect assumptions about defaults can create silent security gaps.","test_case":"Represent two versions with different security-control defaults and evaluate a shared lesson.","expected_test_result":"The knowledge system distinguishes their applicability and required configuration state.","verification_method":"Version-specific configuration validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B07-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B07-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B07","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Version Awareness","concept":"Historical security evidence","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why should historical security lessons remain available after a newer version changes the relevant condition?","answer":"Historical lessons preserve evidence about past failures, remediation decisions, and evolution of the security boundary, even when they are no longer directly applicable to the current version.","explanation":"Deleting historical security knowledge removes useful lineage and can cause repeated mistakes when similar conditions recur.","principle":"Historical security evidence and current applicability are separate properties.","rule":"Supersede or qualify obsolete lessons rather than silently deleting their historical record.","security_implication":"Historical evidence supports recurrence analysis and understanding of security evolution.","test_case":"Supersede an old version-specific lesson with a newer verified lesson.","expected_test_result":"The current lesson is preferred for current retrieval while the historical lesson remains traceable.","verification_method":"Supersession and retrieval validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B07-R01","D57-B07-R02"],"supersedes":[]}
{"record_id":"D57-B07-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B07","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Version Awareness","concept":"Version-aware verification requirements","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should security verification account for version differences?","answer":"Verification should execute or reason against the actual version-specific behavior relevant to the security claim rather than assuming that evidence from another version transfers automatically.","explanation":"A security property may depend on implementation details, defaults, or dependency behavior that differ across versions.","principle":"Verification scope must include relevant version identity.","test_input":"Security claim plus two supported software versions with potentially different behavior.","expected_test_result":"Verification status is established separately when version differences can affect the claim.","testing_guidance":"Record exact implementation and dependency versions used for verification.","verification_method":"Version-specific security test or documented compatibility analysis.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B07-R01","D57-B07-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B08-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B08","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Detection Learning","concept":"Using verified security lessons to improve future detection","knowledge_type":"LEARNING_RULE","question":"How should verified security lessons influence future detection logic?","answer":"A verified lesson can inform new detection hypotheses, indicators, relationships, or verification tests when the lesson's causal mechanism and applicability conditions support the change.","explanation":"Security learning should improve future detection without blindly converting every historical observation into a permanent rule.","principle":"Detection improvements should be derived from validated security knowledge and bounded by evidence.","rule":"Do not automatically turn every historical event characteristic into a detection rule.","security_implication":"Unvalidated detection rules can create false positives or miss the actual causal behavior.","test_case":"Convert a verified security mechanism into a candidate detection rule and validate it against benign and relevant security cases.","expected_test_result":"The detection rule is retained only when its evidence and applicability justify deployment.","verification_method":"Detection regression and evidence validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B06-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B08-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B08","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Detection Learning","concept":"Security lesson to prevention control","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can a verified security lesson be transformed into a preventive control?","answer":"Identify the causal condition, determine whether a preventive control can block or constrain it, define the control objective, implement the smallest justified control, and verify both security effectiveness and system impact.","explanation":"A lesson becomes preventive knowledge only after the causal mechanism and control objective are sufficiently understood.","principle":"Prevention should target the enabling condition rather than merely the historical symptom.","rule":"Do not derive a preventive control from an incident observation when the causal mechanism remains unresolved.","objective":"Convert verified security learning into repeatable prevention where justified.","security_implication":"Cause-focused prevention can reduce recurrence risk, while symptom-focused prevention may leave alternate paths open.","test_case":"Map a verified causal condition to a candidate preventive control and define its verification criteria.","expected_test_result":"The control has an explicit causal relationship and measurable security objective.","verification_method":"Control design and security-effectiveness review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B03-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B08-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B08","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Detection Learning","concept":"Avoiding overfitting security detections to one incident","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should future detection logic avoid encoding only the exact surface characteristics of one incident?","answer":"An incident-specific indicator may detect only one manifestation and can fail when the same security mechanism appears with different values, paths, identities, or representations.","explanation":"Reusable detection should capture the validated mechanism or relationship where evidence supports it, not merely memorize one historical instance.","principle":"Security detection should generalize the mechanism only to the extent justified by evidence.","rule":"Do not treat incident-specific identifiers as universal indicators of the underlying security condition.","security_implication":"Overfit detection can produce both missed variants and brittle rules.","test_case":"Create multiple manifestations of the same security mechanism and evaluate whether a candidate detection captures the common property.","expected_test_result":"The detection is evaluated on mechanism-level coverage rather than exact historical values alone.","verification_method":"Detection generalization and adversarial variation testing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R01","D57-B06-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B08-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B08","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Detection Learning","concept":"Security lesson feedback into verification","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security lessons influence future verification tests as well as detection?","answer":"A lesson identifies a known security condition or reasoning failure that can become a regression target, allowing future changes to be checked against previously learned security boundaries.","explanation":"Detection finds possible problems, while verification checks whether known security properties remain satisfied after changes.","principle":"Validated security learning should become durable regression knowledge when applicable.","rule":"When a lesson identifies a repeatable security property, consider encoding it as a future verification condition.","security_implication":"Security regression tests can reduce recurrence of previously observed failures.","test_case":"Convert a verified security property into a regression test and intentionally violate the property in a controlled test environment.","expected_test_result":"The regression test detects the deliberate violation.","verification_method":"Security regression-test validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R02","D57-B08-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B08-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B08","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Learning Boundaries","concept":"Evidence-gated security learning","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What prevents weak security observations from becoming autonomous learning signals?","answer":"Evidence classification, validation status, applicability conditions, contradiction handling, and explicit promotion criteria should gate whether a security observation can influence future automated decisions or controls.","explanation":"Autonomous learning systems can amplify mistakes if every observation is treated as trusted training data.","principle":"Security learning must be evidence-gated before influencing future behavior.","rule":"Do not promote unverified observations directly into authoritative security policy, detection logic, or automated action.","security_implication":"Evidence gating reduces the risk of feedback loops based on false or contaminated security knowledge.","test_case":"Submit verified, inferred, conflicted, and unknown security observations to a learning pipeline.","expected_test_result":"Only observations satisfying the configured promotion criteria influence authoritative learning outputs.","verification_method":"Learning-promotion gate validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R02","D57-B08-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B09-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B09","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Contradiction Handling","concept":"Preserving conflicting security findings","knowledge_type":"CONFLICTED","question":"How should D57 represent two security findings that appear to contradict each other?","answer":"Both findings should be retained with their evidence, scope, versions, assumptions, and validation status until the difference is explained or one finding is superseded by stronger evidence.","explanation":"Security findings may differ because of different environments, versions, threat conditions, configurations, or incomplete evidence.","principle":"Contradictory findings should remain explicit until evidence resolves the conflict.","rule":"Do not silently delete a security finding merely because a newer or different finding disagrees with it.","security_implication":"Hiding contradictory evidence can conceal important environmental or temporal conditions.","test_case":"Store two conflicting findings with different version or configuration conditions.","expected_test_result":"Both remain retrievable with explicit contradiction and applicability metadata.","verification_method":"Contradiction-aware graph and retrieval validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B07-R04"],"contradicts":[]}
{"record_id":"D57-B09-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B09","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Contradiction Handling","concept":"Resolving security contradictions through conditions","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can apparently contradictory security lessons be reconciled?","answer":"Compare their affected versions, configurations, architecture, threat assumptions, evidence quality, time periods, and verification methods to determine whether they actually apply to different conditions.","explanation":"Many apparent contradictions disappear when hidden context is made explicit.","principle":"Context analysis should precede contradiction resolution.","rule":"Do not force two security findings into agreement when their conditions differ materially.","objective":"Determine whether a contradiction is genuine or context-dependent.","test_case":"Compare two findings with different configurations and determine whether both can remain valid.","expected_test_result":"The system records context-dependent applicability when the findings are valid under different conditions.","verification_method":"Context and evidence comparison.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B09-R01","D57-B07-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B09-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B09","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Deduplication","concept":"Semantic deduplication of security lessons","knowledge_type":"DATA_QUALITY_RULE","question":"When are two security lessons duplicates rather than distinct records?","answer":"They are duplicates when they express materially the same security issue, causal mechanism, remediation, verification result, applicability conditions, and generalized lesson, even if wording or examples differ.","explanation":"Security knowledge quality decreases when repeated descriptions of the same lesson crowd out distinct causal, remediation, or verification knowledge.","principle":"Deduplication should operate on security meaning rather than textual similarity alone.","rule":"Do not preserve superficial wording variants as separate security lessons.","objective":"Maintain high information density and retrieval precision.","test_case":"Compare two records with different wording but identical security mechanism and applicability conditions.","expected_test_result":"They are identified as semantic duplicates or linked as equivalent representations.","verification_method":"Semantic deduplication review.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B09-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B09-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B09","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Supersession","concept":"Superseding outdated security lessons","knowledge_type":"RELATIONSHIP","question":"How should a newer security lesson replace an older one without losing historical evidence?","answer":"The newer lesson should explicitly supersede the older lesson when stronger evidence or changed conditions justify the update, while the historical record remains available for lineage and audit.","explanation":"Security knowledge evolves with versions, architecture, threats, and new evidence. Silent replacement destroys useful historical context.","principle":"Security knowledge evolution should preserve lineage.","rule":"Use explicit supersedes relationships instead of overwriting historical security lessons.","security_implication":"Historical lineage supports recurrence analysis and explains why security controls changed.","test_case":"Create a new verified lesson that changes the applicability of an older lesson.","expected_test_result":"The new lesson is preferred for current retrieval while the older lesson remains historically traceable.","verification_method":"Supersession relationship validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B07-R04"],"supersedes":[]}
{"record_id":"D57-B09-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B09","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Relationship Integrity","concept":"Security lesson relationship graph","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Which relationships are important for connecting D57 security lessons to the broader Knowledge Fabric?","answer":"Useful relationships include causes, fixes, mitigates, verifies, tests, contradicts, supersedes, refines, specializes, depends_on, derived_from, and related_to.","explanation":"Security lessons become more useful when the knowledge graph can connect an issue to its evidence, root cause, remediation, verification, residual risk, and generalized lesson without duplicating records from other datasets.","principle":"Relationships should carry security reasoning across dataset boundaries.","rule":"Prefer explicit stable relationships over copying external knowledge into D57.","objective":"Make D57 useful for retrieval, diagnosis, remediation reasoning, verification, and learning.","security_implication":"Broken or ambiguous relationships can cause downstream systems to miss required security context.","test_case":"Traverse a complete security lesson from issue to cause, remediation, verification, and generalized lesson.","expected_test_result":"Every required relationship resolves or is explicitly marked unresolved.","verification_method":"Graph integrity and traversal validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R05","D57-B03-R01","D57-B04-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B10-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B10","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Operational Truthfulness","concept":"Separating generated security examples from verified operational experience","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should D57 prevent generated examples from being mistaken for real security experience?","answer":"Generated examples should be explicitly marked illustrative or not executed, while operational experience should require evidence, provenance, and truthful execution and verification status.","explanation":"D57 is intended to capture security lessons from verified experience, so synthetic examples must never masquerade as historical operational outcomes.","principle":"Generated content and verified operational evidence are different knowledge classes.","rule":"Never describe a generated security scenario as an observed incident, measured outcome, executed remediation, or verified operational result without evidence.","objective":"Maintain truthful security-learning provenance.","security_implication":"Fabricated incident history can contaminate autonomous security reasoning and create false confidence.","test_case":"Submit an illustrative security scenario and a verified operational record to the dataset ingestion pipeline.","expected_test_result":"The illustrative record remains clearly non-operational while the verified record retains its evidence classification.","verification_method":"Provenance and execution-status consistency audit.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R02","D57-B08-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B10-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B10","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Operational Truthfulness","concept":"Execution status versus validation status","knowledge_type":"SCHEMA_RULE","question":"Why must D57 keep execution status separate from validation status?","answer":"Execution status describes whether an action or test actually ran, while validation status describes whether the resulting evidence supports the intended security conclusion.","explanation":"An executed action can fail, an executed test can produce inconclusive evidence, and a generated recommendation may never have been executed. Combining these states produces misleading knowledge.","principle":"Execution and validation represent different dimensions of security evidence.","rule":"Never infer VERIFIED from EXECUTED or EXECUTED_PASSED alone.","security_implication":"State confusion can cause autonomous systems to trust unverified security outcomes.","test_case":"Represent an executed remediation with a failed verification and an unexecuted proposed remediation.","expected_test_result":"Each state remains independently represented.","verification_method":"State-machine and schema validation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R01","D57-B10-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B10-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B10","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Provenance","concept":"Security lesson provenance requirements","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What provenance should a high-confidence D57 security lesson retain?","answer":"It should retain the source or evidence origin, relevant version or time context, validation method, and lineage connecting the lesson to the underlying security observation or evidence.","explanation":"Provenance allows downstream users and systems to determine why a security lesson exists and whether it remains applicable.","principle":"Security knowledge without traceable evidence has reduced auditability and reuse value.","rule":"Do not claim source-backed or operational provenance when no source or evidence has been attached.","requirements":["Evidence origin","Version or time context when relevant","Validation method","Lineage"],"security_implication":"Weak provenance can cause unsupported security knowledge to become authoritative.","test_case":"Validate records with attached provenance against records explicitly marked NOT_ATTACHED.","expected_test_result":"The records remain distinguishable by provenance status and confidence.","verification_method":"Provenance completeness and truthfulness audit.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R02","D57-B10-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B10-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B10","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Learning Integrity","concept":"Evidence-gated promotion into authoritative security knowledge","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should prevent a weak D57 lesson from becoming authoritative security knowledge?","answer":"Promotion should require sufficient evidence, validated security conclusions, explicit applicability, contradiction review, provenance status appropriate to the claim, and truthful execution and verification states.","explanation":"The Knowledge Fabric may later use D57 to influence detection, remediation, code generation, testing, and autonomous security decisions. Weak lessons therefore require explicit gates before promotion.","principle":"Security knowledge should become authoritative only through evidence-gated promotion.","rule":"Do not promote UNKNOWN, unsupported INFERENCE, fabricated experience, or unresolved contradiction into authoritative security policy or automated action.","security_implication":"Evidence-gated promotion reduces the risk of autonomous security behavior being driven by contaminated knowledge.","test_case":"Attempt to promote records with VERIFIED, INFERRED, UNKNOWN, and CONFLICTED states.","expected_test_result":"Only records satisfying promotion criteria become authoritative.","verification_method":"Security knowledge promotion gate test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R05","D57-B09-R01","D57-B10-R01"]}
{"record_id":"D57-B10-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B10","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Batch Quality Gate","concept":"D57 foundational batch quality gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What quality gate should D57 foundational security lessons pass before additional batches build on them?","answer":"The batch should pass structural validation, security-semantic validation, evidence classification, provenance honesty, version-awareness checks, relationship integrity, semantic deduplication, and explicit identification of unresolved security knowledge.","explanation":"D57 is an operational security-learning dataset and must not accumulate unsupported security claims merely to increase record count. Each foundational lesson must establish a trustworthy structure for later verified operational records.","principle":"Security dataset quality depends on evidence integrity, coverage, and reusable reasoning structure rather than record count.","rule":"Do not advance a D57 batch as complete when important foundational security-learning concepts remain uncovered or when records contain fabricated operational evidence.","objective":"Establish a reliable foundation for future verified security lessons.","requirements":["Structural validity","Security scope coverage","Evidence-state correctness","Provenance honesty","Version awareness","Relationship integrity","Deduplication","Known-gap recording"],"expected_behavior":"Only structurally valid, semantically distinct, evidence-honest records remain eligible for downstream integration.","security_implication":"A weak foundational batch can contaminate later autonomous security learning.","test_case":"Run the batch through schema, evidence, provenance, deduplication, relationship, and scope checks.","expected_test_result":"Unsupported claims, duplicates, malformed relationships, and missing required security-learning concepts are identified before integration.","verification_method":"Multi-stage D57 quality gate.","verification_guidance":"This seed batch is not a claim of verified operational experience; records without attached operational evidence must remain explicitly non-operational.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_ONLY","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R05","D57-B04-R01","D57-B09-R05","D57-B10-R01"]}

{"record_id":"D57-B11-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Threat Modeling Lessons","concept":"Security assumptions must be explicit and testable","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security assumptions be explicitly recorded in a security lesson?","answer":"An unstated security assumption can silently become a security dependency. A reusable lesson should identify assumptions about actors, assets, trust boundaries, capabilities, deployment conditions, and controls so later users can determine whether the lesson still applies.","explanation":"Security lessons are context-sensitive. Recording assumptions prevents a lesson derived under one threat model from being incorrectly generalized to systems with different trust boundaries or attacker capabilities.","principle":"A security lesson is reusable only when the assumptions that constrain its applicability are visible.","rule":"Record material security assumptions and make them testable where practical.","objective":"Prevent incorrect transfer of context-dependent security knowledge.","requirements":["Identify material assumptions.","State applicability conditions.","Avoid presenting assumptions as verified facts."],"constraints":["Some threat assumptions may remain uncertain.","Applicability can change as architecture changes."],"preconditions":["Relevant security context is available or explicitly marked unknown."],"input":{"assumptions":["trusted internal service","authenticated caller","protected secret store"],"status":"illustrative"},"output":{"lesson_scope":"bounded by explicit assumptions"},"expected_behavior":"Consumers can determine whether the lesson applies to their current threat model.","correct_example":"The lesson applies only when the service-to-service identity is cryptographically authenticated and authorization is evaluated against the requested resource.","invalid_example":"Internal services are trusted, so authorization is unnecessary.","failure_mode":"Hidden threat-model dependency","cause":"Security assumption omitted from the lesson","root_cause":"Context was treated as universal instead of an explicit precondition","solution":"Record the assumption and its security consequence","alternative":"Use a stricter threat model when assumptions cannot be established","tradeoff":"More explicit documentation increases maintenance effort but reduces misuse.","edge_case":"An assumption that was valid during initial deployment may become invalid after introducing a new integration.","security_implication":"Unrecorded assumptions can create exploitable trust gaps.","test_case":"Apply the lesson to an architecture where the previously trusted component is externally reachable.","test_input":{"architecture_change":"trusted_component_becomes_untrusted"},"expected_test_result":"The lesson is flagged for applicability review.","testing_guidance":"Test lesson applicability against materially different trust models.","verification_method":"Review assumptions against the current threat model and architecture.","verification_guidance":"Verify each security-critical assumption has an owner, evidence source, or explicit unknown status.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B01-R02","D57-B10-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B11-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Threat Modeling Lessons","concept":"Security boundary changes require lesson re-evaluation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should happen to a security lesson when a trust boundary changes?","answer":"The lesson should be re-evaluated against the new boundary before being treated as applicable. A boundary change can invalidate assumptions, alter attack paths, and change the effectiveness of existing controls.","explanation":"Security knowledge is dependent on system topology and authority relationships. Moving a component across a trust boundary or exposing it to a new actor can change the security conditions under which the original lesson was derived.","principle":"A material trust-boundary change invalidates unverified assumptions until they are re-established.","rule":"Trigger applicability review whenever an architectural change materially alters trust boundaries or actor capabilities.","objective":"Prevent stale security lessons from being applied to changed architectures.","requirements":["Detect material boundary changes.","Identify affected assumptions.","Re-verify lesson applicability."],"constraints":["Minor architectural changes may not affect the lesson.","The impact of a boundary change may require security analysis."],"preconditions":["The original lesson records its relevant security context."],"input":{"change":"internal component exposed through a new network boundary","original_lesson_scope":"internal trusted service"},"output":{"action":"re-evaluate applicability"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson is not automatically treated as valid after the boundary change.","correct_example":"Mark the lesson as requiring review because an external actor can now reach the previously internal component.","invalid_example":"The lesson remains valid because the source code did not change.","failure_mode":"Stale security knowledge","cause":"Architecture changed without knowledge review","root_cause":"Lesson applicability was coupled only to implementation state","solution":"Associate lessons with security-relevant architectural conditions","alternative":"Invalidate the lesson until a new threat assessment is completed","tradeoff":"Review adds process overhead but reduces stale-control risk.","edge_case":"A network boundary changes while application authorization logic remains unchanged.","security_implication":"Previously trusted inputs may become attacker-controlled.","test_case":"Compare lesson applicability before and after a trust-boundary transition.","test_input":{"before":"internal_only","after":"external_reachable"},"expected_test_result":"Applicability review is required.","testing_guidance":"Include architecture-change scenarios in security knowledge validation.","verification_method":"Trace affected trust boundaries and compare them with lesson preconditions.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit applicability status after material architecture changes.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B11-R01","D57-B10-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B11-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Threat Modeling Lessons","concept":"Security lessons should preserve attacker capability assumptions","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must attacker capabilities be preserved when recording a security lesson?","answer":"The same control can have different security implications depending on what an attacker can observe, influence, access, or execute. A lesson should therefore record material attacker capabilities rather than describing the threat only by a generic label.","explanation":"Terms such as attacker, malicious user, or external actor are often too broad for reliable reuse. Capability-specific descriptions support more accurate reasoning about applicability and control effectiveness.","principle":"Security reasoning should describe attacker capabilities, not only attacker identities.","rule":"Record material attacker capabilities whenever they influence the observed or inferred security behavior.","objective":"Improve precision when reusing security lessons across threat models.","requirements":["Describe relevant capabilities.","Separate known capabilities from assumptions.","Avoid unnecessary attacker-identifying labels."],"constraints":["Capability evidence may be incomplete.","Capabilities can change during an attack or investigation."],"preconditions":["The lesson has enough context to identify security-relevant capabilities."],"input":{"attacker_capabilities":["read_public_endpoint","submit_controlled_input"],"unknown_capabilities":["local_filesystem_access"]},"output":{"lesson":"bounded by observed and unknown capabilities"},"expected_behavior":"Unknown attacker capabilities remain explicitly unknown.","correct_example":"The lesson applies to an attacker able to control request parameters but does not assume filesystem access.","invalid_example":"The attacker could do anything.","failure_mode":"Threat-model overgeneralization","cause":"Attacker capabilities were described vaguely","root_cause":"Identity-based threat description replaced capability-based analysis","solution":"Represent capabilities individually with evidence status","alternative":"Use conservative capability assumptions when uncertainty is material","tradeoff":"Detailed capability modeling requires more evidence collection.","edge_case":"An attacker initially lacks a capability but obtains it through a separate compromise.","security_implication":"Underestimating capabilities can make a control appear stronger than it is.","test_case":"Evaluate whether a lesson changes when attacker capabilities are expanded.","test_input":{"capabilities_before":["controlled_input"],"capabilities_after":["controlled_input","credential_access"]},"expected_test_result":"Applicability and risk assumptions are re-evaluated.","testing_guidance":"Test lessons against capability expansion and reduction.","verification_method":"Compare recorded capability assumptions with available evidence.","verification_guidance":"Mark unsupported capabilities as assumptions or unknown rather than facts.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B03-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B11-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Threat Modeling Lessons","concept":"Security lessons should distinguish attacker opportunity from demonstrated exploitation","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"How should a security lesson distinguish exploitability from demonstrated exploitation?","answer":"A condition that could permit an attack should not be recorded as proof that an attack was successfully performed. The lesson should separately represent exposure, plausible exploitability, attempted exploitation, and verified successful exploitation.","explanation":"Conflating possibility with observed exploitation overstates evidence and can distort remediation priorities. A reusable security record should preserve the strongest claim actually supported by evidence.","principle":"Potential exploitability and demonstrated exploitation are different evidence states.","rule":"Never upgrade a potential attack path into an observed successful compromise without supporting evidence.","objective":"Maintain evidence-calibrated security knowledge.","requirements":["Represent exposure separately from exploitation evidence.","Record uncertainty explicitly.","Preserve evidence boundaries."],"constraints":["Exploitability assessment may itself require specialized analysis.","Absence of evidence is not automatically evidence of absence."],"preconditions":["Security evidence has been assessed for the relevant claim."],"input":{"condition":"authorization weakness permits a potential unauthorized access path","observed_success":"unknown"},"output":{"classification":"potential exploitability; successful exploitation unverified"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson avoids claiming a successful breach when none is verified.","correct_example":"The control permits an unauthorized access path under specified conditions; successful exploitation was not established.","invalid_example":"An attacker exploited the vulnerability successfully.","failure_mode":"Evidence inflation","cause":"Potential exploitability was treated as observed exploitation","root_cause":"Security claim states were not separated","solution":"Maintain distinct evidence fields for exposure, attempt, and success","alternative":"Use a conservative security-status taxonomy","tradeoff":"More granular evidence modeling increases schema complexity.","edge_case":"A test environment demonstrates exploitation while production exploitation remains unknown.","security_implication":"Confusing environments can produce false incident claims.","test_case":"Provide evidence for a lab exploit without production telemetry.","test_input":{"lab_result":"successful","production_result":"unknown"},"expected_test_result":"Production exploitation remains unknown.","testing_guidance":"Validate environment and evidence scope for every exploitation claim.","verification_method":"Trace each exploitation statement to environment-specific evidence.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit environment identifiers for exploitation results.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B02-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B11-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Threat Modeling Lessons","concept":"Security lesson scope should include environment boundaries","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should the environment be part of a security lesson's scope?","answer":"Security behavior can differ between development, test, staging, and production environments because identity sources, network exposure, secrets, configuration, monitoring, and permissions can differ. A lesson should therefore identify the environment to which its evidence applies.","explanation":"Without environment scope, a verified result from one environment can be incorrectly generalized to another. Environment-specific evidence should remain bounded unless equivalence has been established.","principle":"Security evidence is valid only within the environment and conditions it actually covers.","rule":"Record environment scope for security observations and do not transfer results across environments without establishing relevant equivalence.","objective":"Prevent environment-mismatch errors in security knowledge reuse.","requirements":["Identify environment scope.","Record material environment differences.","Do not infer production behavior from non-production evidence."],"constraints":["Environment equivalence can be difficult to establish.","Some security controls intentionally differ between environments."],"preconditions":["Evidence has an identifiable environment context."],"input":{"evidence_environment":"staging","target_environment":"production"},"output":{"transfer_status":"not established"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson remains environment-bounded until equivalence is verified.","correct_example":"A staging authorization test is recorded as staging evidence and does not establish production authorization behavior.","invalid_example":"Staging passed, therefore production is secure.","failure_mode":"Environment leakage","cause":"Evidence scope omitted","root_cause":"Security validation was treated as environment-independent","solution":"Attach environment metadata and transfer criteria to lessons","alternative":"Repeat verification in the target environment","tradeoff":"Additional validation requires resources but improves evidence integrity.","edge_case":"Production and staging use identical artifacts but different identity providers.","security_implication":"Different identity infrastructure can invalidate security conclusions.","test_case":"Compare a security result across environments with different authorization infrastructure.","test_input":{"staging_identity":"A","production_identity":"B"},"expected_test_result":"Cross-environment equivalence is not assumed.","testing_guidance":"Include environment metadata in security evidence tests.","verification_method":"Compare relevant security control dependencies between environments.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit equivalence evidence before generalizing results.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R03","D57-B05-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B12-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design Lessons","concept":"Security controls should fail closed when authorization state is unavailable","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security lesson follows when a required authorization decision cannot be determined?","answer":"For security-critical decisions, an unavailable or indeterminate authorization state should not silently become permission. The system should fail closed or enter an explicitly designed safe state appropriate to the operation.","explanation":"Treating an unknown security state as allow can convert availability or dependency failures into authorization bypasses. The exact safe behavior depends on the operation and architecture, but it must be deliberate and auditable.","principle":"Unknown authorization is not equivalent to authorization granted.","rule":"Do not grant security-sensitive authority solely because the authorization decision cannot be obtained.","objective":"Prevent fail-open authorization behavior.","requirements":["Represent authorization uncertainty explicitly.","Define unavailable-dependency behavior.","Audit security-relevant denial or fallback decisions."],"constraints":["Some systems may require carefully designed degraded operation.","Availability and safety requirements can affect the fallback design."],"preconditions":["The operation requires an authorization decision."],"input":{"authorization_service":"unavailable","requested_operation":"security_sensitive"},"output":{"authorization":"not_granted"},"expected_behavior":"The sensitive operation is denied or handled by a separately authorized degraded mode.","correct_example":"Authorization lookup fails, so the sensitive operation is rejected and the failure is recorded for investigation.","invalid_example":"Authorization lookup fails, so the request proceeds using default access.","failure_mode":"Fail-open authorization","cause":"Unknown decision treated as allow","root_cause":"Security state lacked an explicit indeterminate representation","solution":"Represent unavailable authorization separately and define fail-closed behavior","alternative":"Use a pre-authorized bounded degraded mode with explicit constraints","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior can reduce availability during dependency failure.","edge_case":"Emergency access is required during an authorization-provider outage.","security_implication":"Fallback authority can become an unintended privilege escalation path.","test_case":"Disable the authorization dependency and attempt a protected operation.","test_input":{"dependency_state":"unavailable"},"expected_test_result":"Protected operation is not silently authorized.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency failure states explicitly.","verification_method":"Inspect authorization decision paths and execute controlled failure tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every security-critical unknown state has a defined safe outcome.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B05-R02","D57-B06-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B12-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design Lessons","concept":"Security decisions should bind authority to the intended resource and action","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should a security lesson preserve the exact resource and action covered by an authorization decision?","answer":"An authorization decision is meaningful only in relation to the principal, action, resource, and relevant conditions it evaluates. Reusing a decision outside that context can create privilege confusion or unintended authority.","explanation":"A decision granting one operation on one resource should not implicitly authorize another operation or resource. Security lessons should therefore preserve the decision dimensions that made the authorization valid.","principle":"Authorization is contextual, not merely an identity property.","rule":"Bind authorization decisions to the intended principal, action, resource, and applicable conditions.","objective":"Prevent authority from being reused outside its security scope.","requirements":["Identify principal.","Identify action.","Identify resource.","Preserve relevant contextual conditions."],"constraints":["Some systems use policy abstractions that map multiple resources or actions.","The mapping must remain explicit."],"preconditions":["An authorization decision is required for an operation."],"input":{"principal":"user-or-service","action":"read","resource":"resource-A"},"output":{"authority_scope":"read resource-A only unless policy explicitly grants more"},"expected_behavior":"The authorization result does not silently transfer to unrelated actions or resources.","correct_example":"A read decision for resource A is not reused for a write operation on resource B.","invalid_example":"The user is authorized, so all operations are permitted.","failure_mode":"Authorization scope confusion","cause":"Decision context omitted","root_cause":"Authority was represented primarily as identity status","solution":"Model authorization as principal-action-resource-context","alternative":"Use a centralized policy abstraction with explicit resource/action bindings","tradeoff":"Fine-grained decisions can increase policy complexity.","edge_case":"Resource aliases resolve to the same underlying object.","security_implication":"Incorrect alias handling can expand authority unintentionally.","test_case":"Reuse an authorization result across a different action and resource.","test_input":{"original":{"action":"read","resource":"A"},"new":{"action":"write","resource":"B"}},"expected_test_result":"The original authorization does not grant the new operation unless policy explicitly permits it.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-action and cross-resource reuse.","verification_method":"Trace policy inputs through the complete authorization path.","verification_guidance":"Verify that cache keys and decision objects include all security-relevant dimensions.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B08-R01","D57-B08-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B12-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design Lessons","concept":"Security controls must protect against state confusion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What lesson applies when security-relevant state can be represented by multiple identifiers?","answer":"Security logic should use a canonical security identity for the decision being made and reject ambiguous or mismatched identifiers rather than guessing their equivalence.","explanation":"Confusion between identifiers representing different states, snapshots, versions, resources, or authorities can cause a security decision to be applied to the wrong object. The lesson is to make security identity explicit and validate consistency.","principle":"Ambiguous security state must not silently resolve to an arbitrary object.","rule":"Validate that all security-relevant identifiers refer to the same intended security state before permitting a protected operation.","objective":"Prevent state-confusion vulnerabilities.","requirements":["Define canonical identifiers.","Validate identifier consistency.","Reject ambiguous mappings."],"constraints":["Legacy systems may expose multiple identifiers.","Compatibility layers must preserve security semantics."],"preconditions":["Multiple identifiers can influence a security decision."],"input":{"identifier_a":"state-A","identifier_b":"snapshot-B","relationship":"unverified"},"output":{"decision":"reject_or_require_resolution"},"expected_behavior":"The system does not assume the identifiers are equivalent.","correct_example":"A protected action is rejected when the supplied state identifier does not match the authoritative snapshot identity.","invalid_example":"Use whichever identifier is available.","failure_mode":"Security state confusion","cause":"Identifier equivalence assumed","root_cause":"Security identity was not canonicalized","solution":"Define and validate canonical security identity relationships","alternative":"Require a trusted resolver to establish equivalence","tradeoff":"Strict identity validation can expose integration defects earlier.","edge_case":"Two identifiers are aliases by documented design but originate from different subsystems.","security_implication":"Incorrect aliasing can cause authorization or verification to target the wrong state.","test_case":"Supply mismatched identifiers to a security-sensitive operation.","test_input":{"id_1":"A","id_2":"B"},"expected_test_result":"Operation is rejected or explicitly resolved through a trusted mapping.","testing_guidance":"Include mismatched and stale identifier tests.","verification_method":"Trace identifier normalization and security decision inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify that canonical identity is enforced at the security boundary.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B03-R03","D57-B05-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B12-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design Lessons","concept":"Security validation must cover authorization context changes","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is validating a security decision only at request creation insufficient in some systems?","answer":"If authorization-relevant context can change before execution, a decision made earlier may no longer be valid. Security-sensitive operations should revalidate authority at the enforcement point when relevant state can change.","explanation":"Time, resource ownership, role status, revocation, policy state, or transaction context can change between decision and action. The lesson is to align verification with the actual enforcement point.","principle":"Security authority must remain valid for the state in which the protected action is executed.","rule":"Revalidate security-critical authority when relevant authorization context can change between decision and enforcement.","objective":"Reduce time-of-check to time-of-use authorization gaps.","requirements":["Identify mutable authorization context.","Define enforcement-point validation.","Handle revocation and state changes."],"constraints":["Repeated validation can add latency or dependency requirements.","Some immutable contexts permit earlier validation."],"preconditions":["Authorization state can change during the decision-to-action interval."],"input":{"initial_authorization":"granted","context_change":"authority_revoked_before_execution"},"output":{"execution":"denied"},"expected_behavior":"The protected action is blocked when authority is no longer valid at enforcement time.","correct_example":"A revoked role causes the final authorization check to reject a pending privileged operation.","invalid_example":"The operation proceeds because authorization was granted when the request was created.","failure_mode":"Stale authorization use","cause":"Authorization validated too early","root_cause":"Decision validity was assumed to persist despite mutable security context","solution":"Validate authority at or immediately before the security enforcement point","alternative":"Use short-lived signed authority with explicit validity conditions","tradeoff":"Stronger freshness can increase validation overhead.","edge_case":"Revocation propagation is asynchronous.","security_implication":"Propagation delay can create a temporary authorization window.","test_case":"Revoke authority after initial approval but before execution.","test_input":{"authorization":"granted_then_revoked","execution":"after_revocation"},"expected_test_result":"Protected execution is denied when policy requires current authority.","testing_guidance":"Test revocation races and stale authorization states.","verification_method":"Instrument decision and enforcement timestamps and security context.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the enforcement point evaluates the required current state.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B06-R03","D57-B08-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B12-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design Lessons","concept":"Security controls should make privileged transitions explicit","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What security lesson applies when a workflow transitions from ordinary authority to privileged authority?","answer":"The transition should be explicit, bounded, authenticated or otherwise authorized as required, and auditable. Privileged capability should not appear implicitly as a side effect of an ordinary workflow step.","explanation":"Implicit privilege transitions make it difficult to determine where authority changed and whether the change was justified. Explicit transitions make authorization, verification, and audit requirements easier to enforce.","principle":"Privilege changes are security-significant state transitions.","rule":"Represent privileged transitions explicitly and apply the required authorization and verification controls at the transition.","objective":"Prevent implicit privilege escalation.","requirements":["Identify privilege transitions.","Require appropriate authorization.","Record security-relevant transition evidence."],"constraints":["Some workflows naturally contain multiple authority levels.","Transition controls must reflect the risk of the privilege gained."],"preconditions":["The workflow can acquire additional authority."],"input":{"current_authority":"ordinary","requested_transition":"privileged"},"output":{"transition":"explicitly authorized or rejected"},"expected_behavior":"Privilege cannot be acquired merely by reaching an ordinary workflow branch.","correct_example":"A workflow requests and verifies elevated authority before invoking a privileged operation.","invalid_example":"Entering the administrative branch automatically grants administrative authority.","failure_mode":"Implicit privilege escalation","cause":"Privilege transition hidden in workflow control flow","root_cause":"Authority state was not modeled explicitly","solution":"Create an explicit privileged transition with authorization and audit controls","alternative":"Separate privileged operations into independently authorized interfaces","tradeoff":"Explicit transitions increase control points and implementation complexity.","edge_case":"A delegated operation temporarily inherits authority from another principal.","security_implication":"Implicit delegation can create unintended privilege inheritance.","test_case":"Attempt to reach privileged behavior without satisfying the explicit transition conditions.","test_input":{"transition_authorized":false},"expected_test_result":"Privileged operation is denied.","testing_guidance":"Test direct, indirect, delegated, and repeated privilege transitions.","verification_method":"Trace authority state changes through the workflow.","verification_guidance":"Verify every privilege increase has an explicit authorization boundary.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B08-R02","D57-B08-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B13-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Input and Boundary Handling","concept":"Security lessons should identify trust-boundary input assumptions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a security lesson record about data crossing a trust boundary?","answer":"It should identify the source trust level, expected representation, validation requirements, and security-sensitive interpretation of the data. Data crossing a trust boundary should not inherit trust merely from its transport path or caller context.","explanation":"Boundary crossings are common locations where assumptions about data integrity, identity, and semantics change. Recording these conditions makes lessons reusable and prevents accidental trust propagation.","principle":"Data trust must be established at the receiving security boundary.","rule":"Treat externally or differently trusted data as untrusted until the required validation and authorization conditions are satisfied.","objective":"Prevent unintended trust propagation across boundaries.","requirements":["Identify source trust level.","Define required validation.","Separate data integrity from authorization."],"constraints":["Validation requirements depend on the data's use.","Encoding correctness does not establish authorization."],"preconditions":["Data crosses a security-relevant boundary."],"input":{"source":"lower_trust","destination":"higher_trust","data":"security_sensitive"},"output":{"trust_status":"not automatically trusted"},"expected_behavior":"The receiving boundary evaluates the data according to its security requirements.","correct_example":"A service validates and authorizes a request after receiving it from another service instead of trusting the upstream service solely because it is internal.","invalid_example":"The request is trusted because it came from an internal network.","failure_mode":"Trust propagation","cause":"Transport or network location treated as proof of trust","root_cause":"Boundary semantics were not explicitly modeled","solution":"Define and enforce trust requirements at each receiving boundary","alternative":"Use authenticated service identity plus explicit authorization policy","tradeoff":"Boundary validation increases implementation and processing complexity.","edge_case":"A trusted intermediary sanitizes data but cannot establish authorization for the final resource.","security_implication":"Sanitization does not substitute for authorization.","test_case":"Send boundary-crossing input from a lower-trust source while preserving a valid transport channel.","test_input":{"transport":"authenticated","authorization":"absent"},"expected_test_result":"Security-sensitive operation is not authorized solely from transport authentication.","testing_guidance":"Separate transport, integrity, identity, and authorization tests.","verification_method":"Inspect security controls at each trust boundary.","verification_guidance":"Verify that receiving components establish the trust properties they require.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B11-R01","D57-B12-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B13-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Input and Boundary Handling","concept":"Validation should match the security interpretation of data","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should validation be based on how data is used rather than only on its input format?","answer":"A value can be syntactically valid while still being unsafe for its intended security-sensitive interpretation. Security validation should therefore consider semantics, authorization context, allowed ranges, and downstream use.","explanation":"Format validation can establish that a value conforms to a grammar but does not establish that it is authorized or safe for a particular operation. Security lessons should capture the distinction.","principle":"Syntactic validity is not equivalent to security validity.","rule":"Validate data according to the security properties required by its downstream use.","objective":"Prevent format-only validation from being mistaken for security validation.","requirements":["Validate syntax where required.","Validate semantic constraints.","Validate authorization separately."],"constraints":["Different consumers may require different security properties.","Overvalidation can reject legitimate input."],"preconditions":["Data is used in a security-sensitive operation."],"input":{"value":"valid_identifier","authorization":"not_checked"},"output":{"security_status":"insufficient_validation"},"expected_behavior":"The system does not treat syntactic validity as authorization or safety proof.","correct_example":"A syntactically valid resource identifier is additionally checked for ownership or access permission.","invalid_example":"The identifier matches the expected pattern, therefore access is allowed.","failure_mode":"Validation substitution","cause":"Format check treated as complete security validation","root_cause":"Security property was not mapped to the data's use","solution":"Define validation requirements from downstream security semantics","alternative":"Use typed and policy-aware boundary interfaces","tradeoff":"Semantic validation requires more context than simple parsing.","edge_case":"A value is safe for display but unsafe as a file path or query component.","security_implication":"Context-dependent interpretation can create injection or authorization weaknesses.","test_case":"Provide syntactically valid but unauthorized data.","test_input":{"syntax":"valid","authorization":"invalid"},"expected_test_result":"The operation is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Include valid-but-unauthorized and valid-but-dangerous semantic cases.","verification_method":"Map each security-sensitive input to the property being validated.","verification_guidance":"Verify that tests exercise semantic constraints rather than only parser acceptance.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B11-R03","D57-B13-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B13-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Input and Boundary Handling","concept":"Canonicalization must precede security-sensitive comparison","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when equivalent representations can bypass a security comparison?","answer":"When multiple representations can refer to the same security-relevant object or value, the system should establish a canonical representation before performing security-sensitive comparisons, or use a comparison mechanism explicitly defined for the representation.","explanation":"Different encodings, path forms, identifier aliases, or normalization states can cause a security check to evaluate a representation different from the one used for enforcement.","principle":"Security checks must operate on the representation whose semantics are actually enforced.","rule":"Canonicalize or otherwise normalize security-relevant representations before comparison when equivalent forms are possible.","objective":"Prevent representation inconsistencies from bypassing security checks.","requirements":["Identify equivalent representations.","Define canonicalization rules.","Ensure validation and enforcement use consistent representations."],"constraints":["Canonicalization must be safe and specification-defined.","Incorrect normalization can change intended semantics."],"preconditions":["Multiple representations can map to the same security object."],"input":{"representation_1":"variant-A","representation_2":"equivalent-variant-A"},"output":{"security_decision":"based_on_consistent_representation"},"expected_behavior":"Equivalent forms cannot produce inconsistent security outcomes unless intentionally defined.","correct_example":"A resource identifier is normalized according to the system's authoritative representation before authorization.","invalid_example":"Authorization compares raw strings while enforcement resolves aliases differently.","failure_mode":"Canonicalization mismatch","cause":"Security check and enforcement use different representations","root_cause":"Representation equivalence was not modeled","solution":"Use a shared canonical representation or resolver","alternative":"Reject noncanonical forms at the boundary","tradeoff":"Strict canonicalization can reduce compatibility with legacy representations.","edge_case":"Case sensitivity differs between validation and storage layers.","security_implication":"Representation mismatch can produce access-control bypasses.","test_case":"Submit semantically equivalent representations through separate validation and enforcement paths.","test_input":{"forms":["A","equivalent_A"]},"expected_test_result":"Both forms produce the intended security decision.","testing_guidance":"Test normalization, aliases, encoding, case, and path representation where relevant.","verification_method":"Trace the representation from input through authorization and enforcement.","verification_guidance":"Verify that security comparison and enforcement use compatible semantics.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B03-R03","D57-B05-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B13-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Input and Boundary Handling","subtopic":"Input and Boundary Handling","concept":"Security parsers should not silently reinterpret malformed input","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What security lesson applies when malformed input is silently converted into a different valid meaning?","answer":"Security-sensitive parsers should reject malformed or ambiguous input unless the reinterpretation is explicitly specified and security-safe. Silent recovery can cause validation and enforcement layers to disagree.","explanation":"Error recovery may be convenient for ordinary data processing but dangerous when malformed data can acquire a privileged or security-sensitive interpretation.","principle":"Ambiguous security input should not silently acquire a more permissive meaning.","rule":"Reject or explicitly handle malformed security-sensitive input rather than silently coercing it into an unintended valid form.","objective":"Prevent parser disagreement and security ambiguity.","requirements":["Define malformed-input behavior.","Keep validation and enforcement semantics aligned.","Avoid permissive silent coercion in security-sensitive paths."],"constraints":["Some protocols specify compatibility parsing.","Compatibility behavior must remain consistent with security requirements."],"preconditions":["Malformed input can reach a security-sensitive parser."],"input":{"value":"malformed_security_input"},"output":{"handling":"reject_or_explicitly_defined_safe_interpretation"},"expected_behavior":"Malformed security-sensitive input does not silently gain unintended authority.","correct_example":"The parser rejects an ambiguous security token instead of selecting a permissive default.","invalid_example":"The parser ignores the malformed field and continues with elevated defaults.","failure_mode":"Ambiguous parsing","cause":"Silent coercion or fallback","root_cause":"Security semantics were not defined for malformed input","solution":"Define explicit rejection or safe fallback semantics","alternative":"Use a strict parser for security boundaries and a separate compatibility parser elsewhere","tradeoff":"Strict parsing can reduce interoperability with malformed legacy inputs.","edge_case":"An upstream component intentionally sends a legacy representation.","security_implication":"Compatibility logic can become a bypass path if security semantics differ.","test_case":"Send malformed and ambiguous representations to the security boundary.","test_input":{"input":"ambiguous"},"expected_test_result":"The input is rejected or handled by an explicitly verified safe rule.","testing_guidance":"Include malformed, truncated, duplicated, and ambiguous fields where applicable.","verification_method":"Review parser error handling and compare it with security policy semantics.","verification_guidance":"Verify no malformed branch produces unintended privilege or trust.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B03-R02","D57-B13-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B13-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Input and Boundary Handling","concept":"Security boundary parsers should preserve security-relevant provenance","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security-sensitive data retain provenance across parsing and transformation?","answer":"When a value crosses multiple transformations, retaining its source and trust context helps determine whether later security decisions are based on trusted evidence, derived data, or untrusted input.","explanation":"Transformations can obscure where a value originated and whether its security properties were established. Provenance allows downstream decisions to distinguish validated attributes from merely propagated claims.","principle":"Derived data should not silently inherit stronger trust than its provenance supports.","rule":"Preserve material security provenance through transformations that influence authorization, integrity, or trust decisions.","objective":"Prevent trust escalation through data transformation.","requirements":["Track source where security-relevant.","Distinguish asserted from verified attributes.","Preserve transformation lineage."],"constraints":["Not every field requires full lineage.","Privacy and data-minimization requirements may limit retained provenance."],"preconditions":["Data transformation affects a security-relevant decision."],"input":{"source":"untrusted","transformation":"parsed_and_derived","security_claim":"not_verified"},"output":{"trust":"bounded_by_original_evidence"},"expected_behavior":"Transformation does not automatically elevate the security status of the underlying claim.","correct_example":"A parsed identity claim remains unverified until authenticated evidence establishes it.","invalid_example":"The claim becomes trusted because an internal component parsed it.","failure_mode":"Trust laundering","cause":"Transformation treated as validation","root_cause":"Provenance and trust state were disconnected","solution":"Carry provenance and evidence status with security-relevant derived data","alternative":"Re-establish trust independently at each security boundary","tradeoff":"Provenance tracking adds metadata and lifecycle complexity.","edge_case":"A trusted component derives a value from untrusted input and adds its own signature.","security_implication":"The signature proves origin of the derivative, not necessarily truth of the underlying input.","test_case":"Track an untrusted security claim through multiple transformations.","test_input":{"initial_trust":"untrusted","transformations":3},"expected_test_result":"The claim does not become verified solely through transformation.","testing_guidance":"Test provenance preservation across parsing, mapping, serialization, and service boundaries.","verification_method":"Trace source and evidence status through each transformation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that trust state changes only through defined security evidence or controls.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B02-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B14-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Security lessons must distinguish dependency presence from dependency trust","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security lesson applies when a project depends on external software components?","answer":"The presence of a dependency does not by itself establish that the dependency is trustworthy, secure, maintained, or appropriate for the required security boundary. Security lessons should preserve the dependency's evidence and trust assumptions separately.","explanation":"Dependency identity, version, provenance, maintenance status, configuration, and actual usage are distinct properties. A lesson should not treat any one property as proof of all others.","principle":"Dependency identity is not equivalent to dependency security assurance.","rule":"Evaluate security-relevant dependency properties separately and record unsupported trust claims as unknown.","objective":"Avoid implicit trust in software dependencies.","requirements":["Identify dependency and version.","Record relevant provenance evidence.","Assess security-relevant usage and configuration."],"constraints":["Complete dependency assurance may require external evidence.","A known component can still be insecurely configured or used."],"preconditions":["The lesson involves a software dependency."],"input":{"dependency":"external_component","identity":"known","security_status":"not_established"},"output":{"trust_status":"bounded_unknown"},"expected_behavior":"Dependency security is not inferred solely from its name or presence in a lockfile.","correct_example":"Record the exact dependency version and separately assess its provenance, known advisories, configuration, and use.","invalid_example":"The dependency is popular, therefore it is secure.","failure_mode":"Dependency trust assumption","cause":"Popularity or identity treated as security proof","root_cause":"Dependency assurance dimensions were collapsed","solution":"Represent dependency security properties independently","alternative":"Use an approved dependency policy with evidence requirements","tradeoff":"Dependency assessment increases maintenance effort.","edge_case":"A dependency is internally maintained but incorporates external components.","security_implication":"Transitive dependencies can introduce additional trust relationships.","test_case":"Provide a dependency with known identity but unknown provenance.","test_input":{"identity":"verified","provenance":"unknown"},"expected_test_result":"Trust remains bounded and requires further evidence.","testing_guidance":"Include direct and transitive dependency scenarios.","verification_method":"Compare dependency metadata with security-assurance requirements.","verification_guidance":"Verify that security claims reference evidence appropriate to the dependency property asserted.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B10-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B14-R02","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B14","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Transitive dependency security is part of the effective dependency surface","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security lessons account for transitive dependencies?","answer":"A direct dependency can execute, import, bundle, or otherwise rely on additional components. A security lesson should therefore consider the effective dependency graph rather than only the top-level declarations.","explanation":"Security exposure can originate below the direct dependency level. The exact depth and relevance depend on how components are resolved and used, but transitive relationships should not be ignored when they materially affect the software supply chain.","principle":"Security-relevant dependency exposure follows the effective dependency graph.","rule":"Include materially relevant transitive dependencies when assessing dependency-related security risk.","objective":"Prevent incomplete dependency security analysis.","requirements":["Resolve the effective dependency graph where feasible.","Identify security-relevant transitive components.","Track version relationships."],"constraints":["Some runtime or optional dependencies may not be active.","Dynamic loading can complicate static dependency enumeration."],"preconditions":["The software uses dependencies with transitive relationships."],"input":{"direct_dependency":"A","transitive_dependency":"B","usage":"runtime"},"output":{"security_scope":["A","B"]},"expected_behavior":"Security analysis includes B when B is materially part of the executed or distributed dependency surface.","correct_example":"A runtime library's transitive cryptographic component is included in the security review when it is actually used.","invalid_example":"Only direct dependencies matter for security.","failure_mode":"Incomplete dependency scope","cause":"Dependency graph truncated at direct dependencies","root_cause":"Security inventory model did not represent transitive exposure","solution":"Analyze the effective dependency graph","alternative":"Use runtime evidence to narrow the active dependency set","tradeoff":"Graph analysis can produce additional review volume.","edge_case":"A transitive dependency is installed but never loaded in the relevant execution path.","security_implication":"Static presence does not necessarily prove runtime exposure, so execution relevance must be distinguished.","test_case":"Compare direct-only and effective dependency graphs.","test_input":{"direct":["A"],"transitive":["B","C"]},"expected_test_result":"Material transitive components are included in the security scope.","testing_guidance":"Separate declared, resolved, packaged, and executed dependencies.","verification_method":"Inspect dependency resolution artifacts and relevant runtime paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify the security lesson states which dependency surface it covers.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["dependency_graph"],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B14-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B14-R03","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B14","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Dependency security lessons should preserve version scope","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why is dependency version scope important in a security lesson?","answer":"Security properties and vulnerabilities can differ across versions. A lesson should identify the versions for which its evidence applies rather than treating the dependency name as version-independent.","explanation":"Version changes can modify behavior, security fixes, defaults, transitive dependencies, or exposed interfaces. Therefore version scope is part of the evidence boundary.","principle":"A dependency security claim is version-scoped unless version independence is established.","rule":"Attach security-relevant dependency lessons to explicit version ranges or mark version applicability unknown.","objective":"Prevent outdated or misapplied dependency security knowledge.","requirements":["Record exact version or bounded version range.","Track security-relevant version transitions.","Reassess lessons after material upgrades."],"constraints":["Some fixes apply across multiple supported branches.","Version equivalence should be evidence-based."],"preconditions":["The lesson concerns a versioned dependency."],"input":{"dependency":"component-X","version":"v1","lesson_scope":"v1 only"},"output":{"applicability":"v1"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson is not automatically applied to v2 without evidence.","correct_example":"A lesson records that its verification covered version v1.4 and requires review after upgrading to v2.","invalid_example":"The component is secure because v1 was verified.","failure_mode":"Version scope leakage","cause":"Version omitted from security knowledge","root_cause":"Dependency identity was treated as immutable","solution":"Record version-aware applicability","alternative":"Tie lessons to semantic security properties rather than version names when independently established","tradeoff":"Version-aware knowledge requires lifecycle maintenance.","edge_case":"A patch version changes only non-security functionality.","security_implication":"Even apparently minor changes can alter transitive or security-relevant behavior, so assumptions should be checked according to evidence.","test_case":"Apply a v1 security lesson to v2 without new evidence.","test_input":{"verified_version":"v1","target_version":"v2"},"expected_test_result":"Applicability is marked unverified or requires review.","testing_guidance":"Test upgrade and downgrade transitions.","verification_method":"Compare dependency versions and security-relevant change information.","verification_guidance":"Verify version scope is present in retrieval metadata.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"VERSION_SCOPED","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B14-R01","D57-B14-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B14-R04","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B14","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Security lessons should distinguish vulnerable dependency from vulnerable usage","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should a security lesson distinguish a vulnerable dependency from insecure use of a dependency?","answer":"A dependency may contain a security issue independently of application usage, while an otherwise secure component can become dangerous through unsafe configuration or API usage. These are distinct causal conditions and should be recorded separately.","explanation":"Correct diagnosis requires identifying whether the issue arises from the component itself, its configuration, its invocation, its exposure, or an interaction among these factors.","principle":"Component vulnerability and application misuse are separate causal hypotheses.","rule":"Do not attribute a security issue solely to a dependency until the component behavior, version, configuration, and usage context have been distinguished.","objective":"Improve root-cause accuracy for dependency-related security lessons.","requirements":["Assess dependency behavior.","Assess application usage.","Assess configuration and exposure.","Preserve causal uncertainty when unresolved."],"constraints":["Multiple causes may coexist.","Root cause may require source or runtime evidence."],"preconditions":["A dependency-related security issue is being analyzed."],"input":{"dependency_state":"potentially_vulnerable","application_usage":"unknown"},"output":{"causal_status":"not_yet_attributed"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson preserves multiple causal hypotheses until evidence distinguishes them.","correct_example":"Record that an unsafe API usage contributed to exposure rather than asserting the dependency itself is defective without evidence.","invalid_example":"The library caused the vulnerability because it was present.","failure_mode":"Causal misattribution","cause":"Dependency presence treated as root cause","root_cause":"Component and usage conditions were not separated","solution":"Analyze component, version, configuration, and usage independently","alternative":"Use a causal hypothesis set with evidence levels","tradeoff":"More analysis is required before assigning responsibility.","edge_case":"Both a vulnerable dependency version and unsafe application usage contribute to the same exposure.","security_implication":"Fixing only one cause may leave the exposure intact.","test_case":"Compare safe and unsafe application usage of the same dependency version.","test_input":{"dependency_version":"same","usage":["safe","unsafe"]},"expected_test_result":"The lesson distinguishes usage-dependent and component-dependent behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled comparison where feasible.","verification_method":"Trace the causal chain from dependency behavior to security outcome.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for each causal assertion.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B03-R01","D57-B03-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B14-R05","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B14","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Dependency replacement is a security change requiring verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What security lesson applies when a dependency is replaced as remediation?","answer":"Replacing a dependency should be treated as a security-relevant change that requires verification of functionality, security properties, configuration, transitive dependencies, and residual exposure.","explanation":"A replacement can remove one risk while introducing different behavior, dependencies, configuration requirements, or compatibility assumptions. The remediation is therefore not proven merely because the package changed.","principle":"Security remediation through dependency replacement must be verified at the resulting system boundary.","rule":"Verify the post-replacement security state rather than treating replacement itself as proof of remediation.","objective":"Prevent false closure after dependency replacement.","requirements":["Verify the replacement identity and version.","Reassess effective dependency surface.","Repeat relevant security tests.","Assess residual risk."],"constraints":["Replacement components can have different semantics.","Security testing must reflect actual integration behavior."],"preconditions":["A dependency replacement is proposed or completed."],"input":{"before":"dependency-A","after":"dependency-B","security_issue":"dependency_related"},"output":{"verification_state":"required"},"expected_behavior":"The issue remains open or verification-pending until the replacement's security properties are established.","correct_example":"After replacement, rerun relevant security tests and inspect the resulting dependency graph.","invalid_example":"Dependency A was removed, therefore the security issue is fixed.","failure_mode":"Remediation closure without verification","cause":"Change treated as proof","root_cause":"Security outcome was inferred from implementation change","solution":"Define and execute post-change security acceptance criteria","alternative":"Use a verified approved replacement with still-required integration checks","tradeoff":"Verification consumes time but prevents incomplete remediation.","edge_case":"The replacement removes the original vulnerability but introduces a different insecure default.","security_implication":"Risk can migrate rather than disappear.","test_case":"Replace a dependency and compare security-relevant behavior before and after.","test_input":{"change":"A_to_B"},"expected_test_result":"Relevant security acceptance tests determine the resulting state.","testing_guidance":"Include direct behavior, configuration, transitive dependencies, and negative security tests.","verification_method":"Review resulting dependency graph and security test evidence.","verification_guidance":"Do not mark remediation verified until acceptance criteria are satisfied.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["dependency_graph","security_tests"],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B04-R05","D57-B05-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B15-R01","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B15","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Security Lessons","concept":"Cryptographic algorithm selection must be evidence-based and version-aware","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a security lesson record about cryptographic algorithm selection?","answer":"It should identify the cryptographic purpose, algorithm or primitive, relevant parameters, implementation context, and evidence supporting its suitability. Algorithm names alone are insufficient to establish secure use.","explanation":"Cryptographic security depends on purpose, parameters, implementation, key handling, protocol composition, and lifecycle. A lesson should avoid broad claims that detach the primitive from its actual use.","principle":"Cryptographic security is contextual rather than determined solely by algorithm name.","rule":"Record cryptographic purpose, parameters, implementation context, and evidence when deriving reusable security lessons.","objective":"Prevent oversimplified cryptographic security claims.","requirements":["Identify security purpose.","Record material parameters.","Record implementation context.","Distinguish established facts from recommendations."],"constraints":["Cryptographic suitability can depend on threat model and standards.","Some algorithms have context-specific acceptable uses."],"preconditions":["A lesson concerns cryptographic protection."],"input":{"purpose":"confidentiality","primitive":"example_primitive","parameters":"required_context"},"output":{"lesson_scope":"purpose_and_parameter_specific"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson does not declare a primitive universally secure or insecure without context.","correct_example":"The lesson states the purpose and parameter requirements for which the cryptographic construction is being evaluated.","invalid_example":"Algorithm X is secure for everything.","failure_mode":"Cryptographic overgeneralization","cause":"Primitive name treated as complete security description","root_cause":"Purpose and composition were omitted","solution":"Capture cryptographic context and parameters","alternative":"Reference an applicable cryptographic standard when provenance is available","tradeoff":"Detailed cryptographic records are more complex but more reusable.","edge_case":"A strong primitive is used with weak key management.","security_implication":"Weak surrounding controls can negate the intended protection.","test_case":"Evaluate identical primitive usage under different key-management conditions.","test_input":{"primitive":"same","key_management":["controlled","weak"]},"expected_test_result":"Security lesson distinguishes the contextual differences.","testing_guidance":"Test primitive, parameter, protocol, key, and lifecycle assumptions separately.","verification_method":"Review cryptographic configuration and implementation against the stated security purpose.","verification_guidance":"Require authoritative provenance before asserting standards compliance.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","version_range":"CONTEXT_SPECIFIC","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B09-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B15-R02","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B15","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Security Lessons","concept":"Cryptographic key lifecycle is part of the security lesson","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should cryptographic key lifecycle be included in security lessons?","answer":"Protection depends not only on cryptographic operations but also on key generation, storage, access, rotation, revocation, backup, destruction, and recovery. A lesson that records only the encryption operation is incomplete when key lifecycle affects the security property.","explanation":"A cryptographic primitive cannot compensate for uncontrolled key material. Reusable security knowledge should therefore preserve the key-management conditions necessary for the claimed protection.","principle":"The effective security of cryptography includes the lifecycle of the keys it depends on.","rule":"Include material key lifecycle controls in cryptographic security lessons.","objective":"Avoid treating cryptographic transformation as complete protection.","requirements":["Identify key lifecycle stages relevant to the threat model.","Record access controls.","Record rotation or revocation requirements where applicable."],"constraints":["Not every use case requires identical lifecycle controls.","Key-management architecture can vary substantially."],"preconditions":["Cryptographic keys are part of the protected system."],"input":{"crypto_operation":"encryption","key_storage":"security_sensitive"},"output":{"lesson_scope":"crypto_plus_key_lifecycle"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson identifies key lifecycle assumptions that affect security.","correct_example":"Encryption is documented together with the control governing who can retrieve the key and under which conditions.","invalid_example":"Data is secure because it is encrypted.","failure_mode":"Key-lifecycle omission","cause":"Cryptographic operation documented without key controls","root_cause":"Protection model ended at ciphertext creation","solution":"Include key lifecycle and access conditions","alternative":"Use a managed key service with documented security properties","tradeoff":"Centralized key management introduces service dependencies and operational complexity.","edge_case":"Backup systems retain old key material after primary key rotation.","security_implication":"Residual key copies may extend the compromise window.","test_case":"Review key lifecycle across active, rotated, backed-up, and revoked states.","test_input":{"states":["active","rotated","revoked","backup"]},"expected_test_result":"Security lesson accounts for relevant lifecycle states.","testing_guidance":"Test lifecycle transitions and access behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect key-management controls and lifecycle records.","verification_guidance":"Verify that rotation or revocation changes are reflected in enforcement behavior.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B09-R04","D57-B06-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B15-R03","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B15","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Security Lessons","concept":"Integrity protection and confidentiality protection are distinct security properties","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should security lessons distinguish confidentiality from integrity protection?","answer":"A mechanism that protects secrecy does not automatically establish that data has not been modified, and a mechanism that detects modification does not automatically conceal data. Lessons should identify the exact property supported by the control.","explanation":"Security claims become misleading when one cryptographic property is assumed to imply another. The lesson should map each control to the security property it actually establishes.","principle":"Security controls should be mapped to explicit security properties.","rule":"Do not infer integrity, authenticity, confidentiality, or freshness from a control that does not explicitly establish that property.","objective":"Prevent incorrect security guarantees.","requirements":["State the intended property.","Identify the mechanism providing it.","Identify properties that remain unestablished."],"constraints":["Some authenticated constructions provide multiple properties.","The claim must still match the actual construction and usage."],"preconditions":["A security lesson involves cryptographic or transport protection."],"input":{"control":"confidentiality_only","desired_property":"integrity"},"output":{"status":"property_not_established"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson identifies the missing property rather than assuming it.","correct_example":"Encryption is recorded as confidentiality protection, while integrity/authenticity is separately established by an appropriate mechanism when required.","invalid_example":"Encrypted data cannot be modified.","failure_mode":"Property conflation","cause":"One security property inferred from another","root_cause":"Control-to-property mapping was incomplete","solution":"Record explicit security properties and evidence","alternative":"Use a construction designed to provide the required combined properties","tradeoff":"Combined protections can simplify design but still require correct configuration and key management.","edge_case":"A lower layer provides integrity while an upper layer independently encrypts data.","security_implication":"Layer assumptions must be documented to avoid gaps or duplicated trust.","test_case":"Alter protected data and assess whether the claimed integrity property is actually enforced.","test_input":{"tampered":"true"},"expected_test_result":"The system rejects or detects tampering when integrity is part of the verified security property.","testing_guidance":"Test each claimed security property independently.","verification_method":"Map each property to its actual enforcement mechanism and evidence.","verification_guidance":"Do not treat confidentiality verification as integrity verification.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B05-R02","D57-B09-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B15-R04","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B15","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Security Lessons","concept":"Cryptographic verification must reject altered or context-invalid inputs","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a security lesson require from verification of cryptographic authentication or integrity data?","answer":"Verification should fail when the protected data, relevant context, or authentication material does not match the expected security binding. A successful verification result must not be reused for a different context unless that reuse is explicitly part of the security design.","explanation":"Cryptographic verification is meaningful only for the data and context that were actually authenticated. Context confusion can allow valid evidence to be applied to an unintended operation.","principle":"Cryptographic evidence is bound to the data and context it authenticates.","rule":"Reject verification when protected data or security-critical context differs from the authenticated context.","objective":"Prevent cryptographic context-confusion failures.","requirements":["Bind verification to protected data.","Bind material security context.","Reject mismatches."],"constraints":["Protocol designs differ in which context fields require binding.","The binding must match the intended security property."],"preconditions":["Cryptographic verification is used for a security decision."],"input":{"message":"M","context":"C1","verification_context":"C2"},"output":{"verification":"failed_if_C2_is_security_relevant_and_not_bound"},"expected_behavior":"A context mismatch cannot silently produce a valid security decision.","correct_example":"A signed operation is rejected when the authenticated resource identifier differs from the resource actually targeted.","invalid_example":"The signature is valid, so any resource may be acted upon.","failure_mode":"Cryptographic context confusion","cause":"Verification scope did not include relevant context","root_cause":"Authenticated message and authorized operation were not properly bound","solution":"Bind security-critical context into the authenticated data or verification process","alternative":"Use protocol-defined domain separation or explicit contextual binding","tradeoff":"Broader binding reduces reuse but may complicate interoperability.","edge_case":"The same signed object is intentionally valid across multiple contexts.","security_implication":"Intentional reuse must be explicitly modeled to avoid accidental authority expansion.","test_case":"Replay valid cryptographic evidence in a different security context.","test_input":{"original_context":"C1","replay_context":"C2"},"expected_test_result":"Verification or authorization rejects the replay when C2 is outside the authenticated scope.","testing_guidance":"Test context changes, replay, resource substitution, and action substitution.","verification_method":"Inspect the authenticated fields and enforcement binding.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all security-critical context required by the protocol is bound.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B12-R02","D57-B12-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B15-R05","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B15","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Security Lessons","concept":"Security lessons should record cryptographic uncertainty rather than invent compliance","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What should a security knowledge system do when cryptographic compliance or suitability cannot be verified?","answer":"It should mark the claim as unverified or unknown and preserve the missing evidence rather than inferring compliance from configuration names, algorithm labels, or generated code.","explanation":"Cryptographic correctness and compliance often depend on exact implementation, parameters, standards version, and operational controls. Without supporting evidence, the knowledge record must not promote an assumption to a verified claim.","principle":"Missing cryptographic evidence is an uncertainty state, not proof of compliance.","rule":"Never label a cryptographic implementation compliant or secure without evidence sufficient for that claim.","objective":"Prevent fabricated or overstated cryptographic assurance.","requirements":["Separate configuration from verification.","Record missing evidence.","Preserve uncertainty status."],"constraints":["Compliance requirements vary by jurisdiction, standard, and use case.","Generated code does not constitute verification."],"preconditions":["A cryptographic security claim is being evaluated without complete evidence."],"input":{"algorithm":"declared","compliance_evidence":"absent"},"output":{"status":"UNKNOWN"},"expected_behavior":"The record remains unverified.","correct_example":"Record algorithm and configuration details while marking compliance verification as unknown until authoritative evidence is available.","invalid_example":"The algorithm is standard, therefore the implementation is compliant.","failure_mode":"Fabricated cryptographic assurance","cause":"Configuration treated as compliance evidence","root_cause":"Verification state was collapsed into declaration state","solution":"Separate declared configuration, execution, testing, and compliance evidence","alternative":"Obtain authoritative assessment appropriate to the claim","tradeoff":"Unknown status can delay closure but preserves evidence integrity.","edge_case":"A library advertises compliance but the application's integration has not been assessed.","security_implication":"Library-level claims may not establish application-level compliance.","test_case":"Evaluate a declared compliant configuration without independent evidence.","test_input":{"declaration":"compliant","evidence":"absent"},"expected_test_result":"Validation status remains unverified.","testing_guidance":"Test that missing evidence cannot produce a verified compliance state.","verification_method":"Check provenance and verification evidence associated with the claim.","verification_guidance":"Require claim-specific evidence rather than labels or generated artifacts.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B05-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B16-R01","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B16","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Logging and Audit Security Lessons","concept":"Security logs must preserve enough context to support security verification","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should a security lesson capture about security-relevant logging?","answer":"Logs should preserve sufficient context to reconstruct the security-relevant decision or event without unnecessarily recording sensitive data. Material fields include time, actor or service identity where appropriate, action, target, decision, correlation context, and evidence status.","explanation":"A security event cannot reliably support investigation or verification if the log omits the information needed to distinguish actors, resources, actions, and outcomes. At the same time, logging excessive sensitive data can create a new exposure.","principle":"Security logging should maximize evidentiary usefulness while minimizing unnecessary sensitive-data exposure.","rule":"Record security-relevant decision context necessary for investigation and verification, subject to data-minimization requirements.","objective":"Make security evidence reconstructable without turning logs into a sensitive-data repository.","requirements":["Record material security context.","Protect log integrity and access.","Minimize sensitive values.","Preserve useful correlation identifiers."],"constraints":["Privacy and retention policies constrain logging.","Different events require different context."],"preconditions":["A security-relevant event or decision occurs."],"input":{"event":"authorization_decision","context":["actor","action","resource","decision"]},"output":{"log":"security_relevant_minimal_context"},"expected_behavior":"The log supports reconstruction of the security decision without unnecessarily storing secrets or sensitive payloads.","correct_example":"Log actor identifier, action, resource reference, decision, and correlation ID while excluding authentication secrets.","invalid_example":"Log the entire request including credentials for future investigation.","failure_mode":"Insufficient or excessive security logging","cause":"Logging design ignored evidentiary and data-minimization requirements","root_cause":"Security logging was treated as generic debugging output","solution":"Define event-specific security audit fields and sensitive-data exclusions","alternative":"Use protected event metadata plus separately controlled forensic data","tradeoff":"More context improves investigation but increases privacy and storage obligations.","edge_case":"The resource identifier itself is sensitive.","security_implication":"Even metadata can expose confidential information.","test_case":"Review security log fields for an authorization event.","test_input":{"fields":["actor","action","resource","decision","secret"]},"expected_test_result":"Required context is present while prohibited sensitive material is absent or protected.","testing_guidance":"Test both evidentiary sufficiency and sensitive-data leakage.","verification_method":"Compare logged fields against event-specific audit requirements.","verification_guidance":"Verify log access, integrity, retention, and minimization controls.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R05","D57-B09-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B16-R02","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B16","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Logging and Audit Security Lessons","concept":"Security logs should distinguish attempted action from successful outcome","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should security logs distinguish attempts from outcomes?","answer":"A request, authorization decision, execution attempt, and successful security-relevant outcome are separate states. Logging them as one event can create false incident or remediation conclusions.","explanation":"An attempted unauthorized operation may be denied, while an authorized operation may fail for unrelated reasons. Security lessons require accurate state transitions rather than assuming that an attempted action succeeded.","principle":"Security event stages must not be collapsed into a single outcome claim.","rule":"Represent attempt, authorization, execution, and verified outcome separately when they materially affect the security conclusion.","objective":"Prevent false security conclusions from ambiguous logs.","requirements":["Record relevant event stages.","Distinguish decision from outcome.","Preserve verification status."],"constraints":["Not every low-risk event requires the same granularity.","Outcome verification may be unavailable."],"preconditions":["A security-relevant action is attempted or executed."],"input":{"attempt":"true","authorization":"denied","execution":"not_performed"},"output":{"security_event":"denied_attempt"},"expected_behavior":"The record does not claim successful execution.","correct_example":"Record that a privileged operation was attempted and denied; do not report it as a successful privilege escalation.","invalid_example":"Privileged operation detected, therefore privilege escalation occurred.","failure_mode":"Event-state conflation","cause":"Attempt and outcome merged","root_cause":"Security event lifecycle lacked separate states","solution":"Represent decision, execution, and outcome independently","alternative":"Use a structured security event state machine","tradeoff":"More states require richer event schemas and processing.","edge_case":"Execution begins before final outcome telemetry is available.","security_implication":"Temporary unknown outcome must not be converted into success or failure without evidence.","test_case":"Generate a denied attempt and inspect the resulting security record.","test_input":{"attempt":"yes","authorization":"deny","execution":"no"},"expected_test_result":"Record shows denied attempt, not successful compromise.","testing_guidance":"Test denied, partially executed, failed, and successful states separately.","verification_method":"Compare event records with actual authorization and execution state.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence before marking an outcome verified.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B05-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B16-R03","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B16","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Logging and Audit Security Lessons","concept":"Security audit records require integrity and access protection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is protecting security audit data itself part of a security lesson?","answer":"Security records can influence investigations, authorization reviews, compliance decisions, and incident conclusions. If attackers or unauthorized users can alter or delete them, the evidence may become unreliable.","explanation":"Security logging is itself a security asset. Its protection requirements should reflect the consequences of tampering, disclosure, or destruction.","principle":"Security evidence must be protected according to its evidentiary value and sensitivity.","rule":"Apply integrity, access-control, and retention protections appropriate to security audit records.","objective":"Preserve trustworthiness and availability of security evidence.","requirements":["Restrict modification and deletion.","Protect sensitive audit data.","Maintain sufficient retention.","Detect integrity violations where required."],"constraints":["Retention requirements vary.","Immutable storage can introduce operational complexity."],"preconditions":["Security records are used as evidence or control inputs."],"input":{"audit_record":"security_event","risk":"tampering"},"output":{"protection":"integrity_and_access_control_required"},"expected_behavior":"Unauthorized modification or deletion is prevented or detectable according to requirements.","correct_example":"Security audit records are written to a protected store where ordinary application identities cannot alter historical entries.","invalid_example":"Anyone with application database access can edit audit history.","failure_mode":"Audit evidence tampering","cause":"Audit store treated as ordinary application data","root_cause":"Evidence protection requirements were not modeled","solution":"Separate or strengthen audit storage controls","alternative":"Use append-oriented or independently protected audit infrastructure","tradeoff":"Independent protection adds architecture and operational complexity.","edge_case":"Audit data must be deleted to satisfy a retention or privacy requirement.","security_implication":"Deletion policy must preserve required evidentiary guarantees while meeting lawful retention constraints.","test_case":"Attempt unauthorized modification of a security audit record.","test_input":{"actor":"unauthorized","operation":"modify"},"expected_test_result":"Modification is denied or reliably detected.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized read, write, delete, and administrative access.","verification_method":"Review access policies and perform controlled integrity tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that security records cannot be silently altered by ordinary application paths.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B09-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B16-R04","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B16","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Logging and Audit Security Lessons","concept":"Security logging failures should not silently erase security state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security lesson applies when security logging becomes unavailable?","answer":"The system should have an explicit policy for logging failure based on the risk of the protected operation. It should not silently assume that missing audit evidence is equivalent to no security event.","explanation":"Unavailable logs create an evidence gap. Whether the protected operation should continue depends on the security design, but the evidence loss must remain visible and controlled.","principle":"Missing security evidence is an uncertainty state.","rule":"Detect and explicitly handle security-audit failure rather than treating absent records as proof that nothing happened.","objective":"Prevent silent evidence loss.","requirements":["Detect audit pipeline failure.","Record or surface audit uncertainty.","Define operation-specific fallback behavior."],"constraints":["Some low-risk operations may continue during audit degradation.","High-impact operations may require stronger evidence guarantees."],"preconditions":["A security-relevant action depends on auditability."],"input":{"audit_service":"unavailable","operation":"security_sensitive"},"output":{"state":"audit_uncertain"},"expected_behavior":"The system follows an explicit degraded-security policy and does not silently erase the event.","correct_example":"A protected operation is blocked or flagged according to policy when required audit evidence cannot be recorded.","invalid_example":"Logging failed, so the event is ignored.","failure_mode":"Silent audit loss","cause":"Logging dependency failure treated as non-security-impacting","root_cause":"Auditability was not modeled as a security requirement","solution":"Define failure semantics for security audit infrastructure","alternative":"Use an independently protected local or durable audit path when appropriate","tradeoff":"Failing closed on audit failure can reduce availability.","edge_case":"The audit system is temporarily unavailable during a security incident.","security_implication":"The evidence gap can materially affect incident reconstruction.","test_case":"Simulate audit-store unavailability during a privileged action.","test_input":{"audit":"unavailable","action":"privileged"},"expected_test_result":"Explicit degraded-security behavior occurs and uncertainty is recorded.","testing_guidance":"Test audit outages at security-critical workflow stages.","verification_method":"Inspect audit failure handling and controlled outage behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that missing audit data produces an explicit uncertainty state.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B12-R01","D57-B16-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B16-R05","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B16","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Logging and Audit Security Lessons","concept":"Security lessons should identify logging blind spots","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a security lesson represent a known audit or telemetry blind spot?","answer":"The blind spot should be explicitly recorded with its affected event types, time or environment scope, missing evidence, and consequences for security conclusions. Unknown events should not be reconstructed as facts without supporting evidence.","explanation":"Security investigations often operate under incomplete visibility. Explicit blind-spot modeling prevents false confidence and helps prioritize future controls or evidence collection.","principle":"Visibility gaps are part of the security evidence state.","rule":"Record material audit blind spots explicitly and propagate their uncertainty into affected security conclusions.","objective":"Prevent overconfidence caused by incomplete security telemetry.","requirements":["Identify affected event classes.","Identify scope and duration when known.","Record what conclusions cannot be established."],"constraints":["Blind spots may be discovered only after the fact.","Exact duration may be unknown."],"preconditions":["Security telemetry is incomplete or unavailable."],"input":{"blind_spot":"missing_privileged_action_logs","scope":"unknown"},"output":{"evidence_state":"incomplete"},"expected_behavior":"Affected conclusions remain bounded by the visibility gap.","correct_example":"Record that privileged actions could not be fully reconstructed during the telemetry gap and mark successful or unsuccessful activity as unknown where evidence is absent.","invalid_example":"No privileged activity occurred because no log entry exists.","failure_mode":"False negative from telemetry gap","cause":"Absence of evidence treated as evidence of absence","root_cause":"Observability completeness was not modeled","solution":"Represent blind spots as explicit evidence limitations","alternative":"Corroborate with independent evidence sources","tradeoff":"Corroboration increases analysis effort.","edge_case":"An independent source covers only part of the blind-spot interval.","security_implication":"Partial corroboration cannot establish complete absence of activity.","test_case":"Query security conclusions for a period containing a known telemetry gap.","test_input":{"telemetry":"incomplete"},"expected_test_result":"Relevant conclusions carry an uncertainty marker.","testing_guidance":"Test evidence-completeness propagation.","verification_method":"Trace security conclusions back to the available evidence coverage.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unsupported absence claims are blocked.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R03","D57-B02-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B17-R01","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B17","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Configuration and Secrets","concept":"Security configuration defaults should be explicit and reviewable","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security lesson applies to configuration values that affect security behavior?","answer":"Security-relevant configuration should have explicit defaults, documented semantics, controlled modification, and verification appropriate to its impact. An undocumented or implicit default can become an unintended security policy.","explanation":"Configuration can alter authentication, authorization, network exposure, logging, cryptographic behavior, and other controls without changing application code. Security lessons should therefore treat configuration as part of the security implementation.","principle":"Security behavior includes security-relevant configuration, not only source code.","rule":"Make security-critical configuration explicit, bounded, reviewable, and testable.","objective":"Prevent accidental security policy through undocumented configuration.","requirements":["Identify security-critical settings.","Define safe defaults where applicable.","Validate configuration at startup or enforcement time.","Track configuration changes."],"constraints":["Defaults depend on deployment context.","Some environments intentionally require different values."],"preconditions":["Configuration influences a security control."],"input":{"setting":"security_control","configured":"implicit_default"},"output":{"status":"requires_explicit_policy"},"expected_behavior":"Security-relevant behavior is traceable to an explicit configuration or documented default.","correct_example":"A privileged feature is disabled unless an explicit configuration enables it under authorized conditions.","invalid_example":"The feature is enabled because an undocumented default happens to allow it.","failure_mode":"Configuration-induced exposure","cause":"Security behavior depended on implicit configuration","root_cause":"Configuration was not treated as part of the security boundary","solution":"Define and validate security-critical configuration","alternative":"Use immutable deployment configuration for high-impact controls","tradeoff":"Strict configuration validation can reduce deployment flexibility.","edge_case":"A missing configuration value has different defaults across versions.","security_implication":"Version-dependent defaults can silently change security posture.","test_case":"Remove a security-critical configuration value and observe system behavior.","test_input":{"setting":"missing"},"expected_test_result":"The system follows an explicitly defined safe policy or rejects startup/configuration.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, malformed, boundary, and version-specific configuration states.","verification_method":"Inspect configuration parsing and security-control initialization.","verification_guidance":"Verify configuration behavior against documented security requirements.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B04-R05","D57-B17-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B17-R02","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B17","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Configuration and Secrets","concept":"Security-sensitive configuration should be validated against policy rather than syntax alone","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is syntactic configuration validation insufficient for security-sensitive settings?","answer":"A configuration can be syntactically valid while violating security policy, using an unsafe combination, or selecting a value inconsistent with the deployment threat model. Security configuration requires semantic policy validation in addition to parsing.","explanation":"Configuration schemas can establish types and structure but not necessarily whether combinations of values satisfy security requirements. Lessons should preserve both parsing and policy validation.","principle":"Configuration correctness has both syntactic and security-semantic dimensions.","rule":"Validate security-critical configuration against explicit policy constraints after parsing.","objective":"Prevent valid syntax from being mistaken for secure configuration.","requirements":["Parse configuration safely.","Validate security semantics.","Reject prohibited combinations."],"constraints":["Policy differs by deployment and risk level.","Some combinations may be safe only under additional controls."],"preconditions":["Security behavior depends on multiple configuration values."],"input":{"config":{"auth":"enabled","authorization":"disabled"}},"output":{"policy_result":"reject_if_authorization_required"},"expected_behavior":"Configuration is rejected or flagged when it violates required security policy.","correct_example":"A service refuses startup when a mandatory authorization control is disabled in a protected deployment profile.","invalid_example":"Configuration is valid because all fields have correct types.","failure_mode":"Policy-invalid configuration","cause":"Schema validation used as security validation","root_cause":"Security constraints were not encoded or checked","solution":"Add semantic security-policy validation","alternative":"Use signed or centrally governed configuration profiles","tradeoff":"Policy validation can constrain legitimate deployment variation.","edge_case":"A control is disabled because another compensating control is formally active.","security_implication":"Exception handling must verify the compensating control rather than merely accept the configuration.","test_case":"Supply a type-valid configuration that violates a security invariant.","test_input":{"syntax":"valid","policy":"invalid"},"expected_test_result":"Configuration is rejected or explicitly flagged.","testing_guidance":"Test individual and cross-field security invariants.","verification_method":"Evaluate configuration against declared security policy.","verification_guidance":"Verify policy exceptions require explicit evidence and authorization.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B13-R02","D57-B17-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B17-R03","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Configuration and Secrets","concept":"Secrets should not be embedded in source or generated artifacts","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What security lesson applies when secrets are embedded in source code or generated artifacts?","answer":"Secrets should be supplied through an appropriately controlled secret-management mechanism rather than hard-coded into source, generated code, logs, or distributable artifacts. Existing exposure should trigger scoped remediation and rotation when warranted.","explanation":"Embedded secrets can propagate through version history, build artifacts, caches, logs, and copies. Removing the visible value does not necessarily invalidate previously exposed credentials.","principle":"Secret material must be controlled as sensitive authority, not treated as ordinary configuration text.","rule":"Do not hard-code operational secrets into source or generated artifacts; manage them through an appropriate protected mechanism.","objective":"Reduce secret exposure and uncontrolled credential propagation.","requirements":["Separate secret material from source.","Restrict access.","Avoid secret propagation into artifacts and logs.","Rotate exposed credentials when required."],"constraints":["Secret-management mechanisms vary by deployment.","Some development fixtures may use non-sensitive placeholders."],"preconditions":["A value grants or may grant security-sensitive authority."],"input":{"artifact":"source_code","secret":"sensitive_value"},"output":{"handling":"secret_externalized"},"expected_behavior":"Source and generated artifacts contain references or placeholders rather than live secret material.","correct_example":"Application configuration references a protected secret source without embedding the secret value in code.","invalid_example":"Store the production API credential directly in a source constant.","failure_mode":"Secret exposure through artifacts","cause":"Secret treated as static source data","root_cause":"Credential lifecycle was not separated from application code","solution":"Externalize secrets and rotate credentials when exposure occurred","alternative":"Use short-lived credentials where architecture supports them","tradeoff":"External secret management adds dependencies and operational controls.","edge_case":"A secret was committed and later deleted from the working tree.","security_implication":"Historical copies may still contain the secret; deletion alone does not prove remediation.","test_case":"Scan generated and source artifacts for known secret material or configured secret patterns.","test_input":{"artifact_set":"source_and_build_outputs"},"expected_test_result":"No live secret material is present where policy prohibits it.","testing_guidance":"Test source, generated files, build outputs, logs, caches, and history according to policy.","verification_method":"Use controlled secret-detection and repository/artifact inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify remediation includes exposure assessment and credential lifecycle action where required.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["secret_management"],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B09-R02","D57-B09-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B17-R04","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Configuration and Secrets","concept":"Security configuration changes should preserve change provenance","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security-relevant configuration changes have provenance?","answer":"Configuration changes can materially alter security posture even when source code is unchanged. Recording who or what changed the setting, when, why, and under which authorization helps distinguish intended security changes from unexplained drift.","explanation":"Security lessons need to connect observed behavior to the configuration state that produced it. Without provenance, a later investigation may not be able to determine whether a security change was deliberate or accidental.","principle":"Security posture depends on configuration state and its change history.","rule":"Maintain provenance for material security configuration changes.","objective":"Make security-state changes attributable and reproducible.","requirements":["Record change identity.","Record relevant time/version.","Record reason and authorization where applicable.","Preserve resulting configuration state."],"constraints":["Low-risk settings may not require identical audit depth.","Privacy and access controls apply to configuration metadata."],"preconditions":["A configuration change can affect a security control."],"input":{"change":"authorization_policy_update","provenance":"present"},"output":{"record":"attributable_security_change"},"expected_behavior":"The security-relevant configuration state can be traced to an authorized change or explicitly identified as unexplained.","correct_example":"A policy change references its approved change identifier and resulting configuration version.","invalid_example":"The security setting changed sometime during deployment; source code is unchanged.","failure_mode":"Configuration drift without provenance","cause":"Security configuration changes were not tracked","root_cause":"Configuration state was treated as ephemeral deployment detail","solution":"Record versioned provenance for material security configuration","alternative":"Use immutable configuration artifacts with deployment provenance","tradeoff":"Change tracking increases governance overhead.","edge_case":"A configuration is changed manually during emergency response.","security_implication":"Emergency changes require strong provenance because they may bypass normal review.","test_case":"Modify a security-critical configuration and inspect the resulting audit trail.","test_input":{"change_type":"manual_emergency"},"expected_test_result":"The change remains attributable and reviewable.","testing_guidance":"Test normal, automated, rollback, and emergency configuration changes.","verification_method":"Trace effective configuration to its source artifact and change record.","verification_guidance":"Verify unexplained security configuration drift is detectable.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B16-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B17-R05","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Configuration and Secrets","concept":"Security exceptions should be explicit, scoped, and time-bounded where appropriate","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a security lesson require when a security control is intentionally weakened or bypassed?","answer":"The exception should identify the affected control, scope, reason, compensating controls, authorization, evidence, and review or expiration conditions appropriate to the risk.","explanation":"Untracked exceptions tend to become permanent undocumented security behavior. A structured exception preserves the difference between an intentional deviation and an accidental control failure.","principle":"A security exception is a controlled deviation, not an implicit new default.","rule":"Record material security exceptions with explicit scope, justification, authorization, compensating controls, and review conditions.","objective":"Prevent temporary security deviations from becoming invisible permanent exposure.","requirements":["Identify control and scope.","Document reason.","Record authorization.","Define compensating controls where applicable.","Define review or expiration."],"constraints":["Some exceptions may require continuous rather than fixed expiration.","Emergency conditions can require accelerated review."],"preconditions":["A security control is intentionally not operating according to its baseline policy."],"input":{"control":"security_control_X","exception":"approved","scope":"bounded"},"output":{"exception_state":"explicit_and_reviewable"},"expected_behavior":"The exception is distinguishable from an unplanned security failure.","correct_example":"A temporary control exception names the affected deployment, reason, approving authority, compensating control, and review date.","invalid_example":"Disable the control for now and remember to restore it later.","failure_mode":"Permanent exception drift","cause":"Security exception lacked lifecycle governance","root_cause":"Deviation was not modeled as a security state","solution":"Create explicit exception records and review conditions","alternative":"Use a separate controlled deployment profile with documented security semantics","tradeoff":"Governance introduces process overhead.","edge_case":"The original exception owner leaves the project before review.","security_implication":"Ownership gaps can allow risky exceptions to persist.","test_case":"Create an exception without an owner or review condition.","test_input":{"owner":"missing","review":"missing"},"expected_test_result":"The exception is rejected or flagged as incomplete.","testing_guidance":"Test missing authorization, scope, owner, compensating control, and expiration fields.","verification_method":"Review exception records against security governance requirements.","verification_guidance":"Verify active exceptions are discoverable and periodically re-evaluated.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B06-R03","D57-B17-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B18-R01","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Error Handling","concept":"Security errors should reveal enough information for safe diagnosis without unnecessary sensitive detail","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should a security lesson capture about error messages?","answer":"Security-facing errors should provide the information required for safe handling and diagnosis while avoiding unnecessary secrets, internal security state, credentials, or sensitive data. Detailed diagnostic information should be exposed only through appropriately protected channels.","explanation":"Overly detailed errors can disclose information useful to attackers, while overly generic errors can prevent safe operations. The lesson is to separate user-facing security information from protected diagnostic evidence.","principle":"Security error disclosure should be purpose-specific and least revealing while remaining operationally useful.","rule":"Expose only the security-relevant error information needed by the recipient and keep sensitive diagnostics in protected channels.","objective":"Reduce information leakage without destroying diagnostic capability.","requirements":["Classify error recipients.","Minimize sensitive disclosure.","Protect detailed diagnostics.","Avoid secret material in errors."],"constraints":["Operational teams may require detailed diagnostics.","Protected channels must themselves be secured."],"preconditions":["An error occurs in a security-relevant workflow."],"input":{"error":"authorization_failure","recipient":"untrusted_client"},"output":{"message":"minimal_safe_error"},"expected_behavior":"The recipient receives enough information to handle the failure without unnecessary internal security detail.","correct_example":"Return a generic authorization failure while retaining detailed policy diagnostics in protected audit telemetry.","invalid_example":"Return the internal policy rule, secret token, stack trace, and protected resource metadata to the caller.","failure_mode":"Security information disclosure","cause":"Diagnostic detail exposed through an untrusted channel","root_cause":"Error and diagnostic audiences were not separated","solution":"Separate safe external errors from protected diagnostics","alternative":"Use structured error codes with authorized diagnostic lookup","tradeoff":"Less external detail can make troubleshooting harder without protected diagnostics.","edge_case":"The caller is an authenticated administrator but not authorized to view the protected resource.","security_implication":"Authentication alone does not establish entitlement to sensitive diagnostics.","test_case":"Trigger a protected operation failure as an unauthorized caller.","test_input":{"caller":"authenticated_but_unauthorized"},"expected_test_result":"Response does not expose prohibited sensitive details.","testing_guidance":"Test errors across caller roles and trust boundaries.","verification_method":"Inspect error serialization and protected diagnostic channels.","verification_guidance":"Verify sensitive fields are excluded or access-controlled.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B09-R02","D57-B13-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B18-R02","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Error Handling","concept":"Security failures should not be converted into successful default states","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should security-related exceptions not silently fall back to success or permissive behavior?","answer":"A security exception can represent uncertainty about authorization, validation, integrity, or policy state. Converting it to success can transform a control failure into an unauthorized operation.","explanation":"Error handling often focuses on availability, but security-critical failures require explicit semantics. A fallback is safe only when its security properties are deliberately defined and bounded.","principle":"Security control failure is not evidence that the protected operation is safe to continue.","rule":"Do not convert security-control exceptions into implicit allow, trusted, or successful states.","objective":"Prevent error-driven security bypasses.","requirements":["Classify security-control failures.","Define explicit fallback behavior.","Preserve uncertainty."],"constraints":["Availability requirements can motivate degraded operation.","Degraded operation must have explicit security boundaries."],"preconditions":["A security control throws or returns an indeterminate result."],"input":{"control":"authorization","result":"exception"},"output":{"operation":"deny_or_explicit_degraded_mode"},"expected_behavior":"The exception does not silently grant authority.","correct_example":"Authorization exception results in denial or a separately authorized degraded path.","invalid_example":"Authorization exception is caught and the request proceeds.","failure_mode":"Exception-driven fail-open","cause":"Generic exception handling bypassed security semantics","root_cause":"Security and availability failure states were conflated","solution":"Handle security failures explicitly before generic recovery logic","alternative":"Design bounded degraded modes with independent authorization","tradeoff":"Strict failure handling can reduce availability during dependency failures.","edge_case":"The security control is optional for a low-risk operation.","security_implication":"Optionality must be explicit rather than inferred from an exception.","test_case":"Force a security-control exception during a protected operation.","test_input":{"control_failure":"exception"},"expected_test_result":"No unintended authority is granted.","testing_guidance":"Test exceptions at authentication, authorization, validation, integrity, and policy boundaries.","verification_method":"Review exception paths and controlled fault injection.","verification_guidance":"Verify generic recovery handlers cannot bypass security enforcement.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B12-R01","D57-B16-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B18-R03","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Error Handling","concept":"Exception messages should not contain secret material","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security lesson applies to secrets that can appear in exceptions?","answer":"Secrets and sensitive authentication material should be excluded from exception messages, stack traces, serialized error objects, and diagnostic channels unless an explicitly controlled design requires otherwise.","explanation":"Exceptions frequently propagate farther than expected through logs, monitoring, support tooling, and client responses. Preventing secret propagation requires considering the complete error path rather than only the local exception statement.","principle":"Error propagation is a potential secret propagation path.","rule":"Sanitize security-sensitive exception content before it reaches logs, clients, telemetry, or persistent artifacts.","objective":"Prevent accidental credential and secret disclosure through error handling.","requirements":["Identify secret-bearing fields.","Sanitize exception serialization.","Control diagnostic destinations.","Test representative failure paths."],"constraints":["Sanitization must avoid destroying required diagnostics.","Secrets can be embedded indirectly in object representations."],"preconditions":["Exceptions can contain or derive from sensitive values."],"input":{"exception":{"message":"contains_sensitive_value"}},"output":{"external_message":"sanitized"},"expected_behavior":"Sensitive material is not propagated through unauthorized error channels.","correct_example":"An exception contains an internal identifier but the serialized external error omits secret values and protected context.","invalid_example":"Log the full request object in the exception handler, including credentials.","failure_mode":"Secret leakage through exceptions","cause":"Unfiltered exception serialization","root_cause":"Error objects were not treated as security-sensitive data carriers","solution":"Sanitize and constrain exception serialization","alternative":"Use structured error codes with separately protected diagnostic context","tradeoff":"Sanitization can make raw debugging harder.","edge_case":"A secret appears in a nested object or custom string representation.","security_implication":"Shallow filtering can miss indirect secret exposure.","test_case":"Trigger an exception containing nested sensitive data.","test_input":{"nested_secret":true},"expected_test_result":"No prohibited secret reaches external or untrusted diagnostic channels.","testing_guidance":"Test nested objects, formatted strings, stack traces, and logging adapters.","verification_method":"Inspect serialized error paths and run controlled secret-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify sanitization occurs before each security-sensitive output boundary.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B09-R02","D57-B17-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B18-R04","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Error Handling","concept":"Security error handling should preserve original failure semantics","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security error handling preserve the distinction between denial, invalid input, dependency failure, and unknown state?","answer":"These states have different security meanings and different remediation requirements. Collapsing them into a generic success or generic failure can hide whether a security control operated, failed, or lacked sufficient evidence.","explanation":"A security lesson should retain the causal and evidentiary meaning of the original failure. This improves investigation, verification, and learning without requiring disclosure of sensitive details to untrusted recipients.","principle":"Error normalization must not erase security semantics.","rule":"Normalize error presentation for consumers without collapsing materially different security states internally.","objective":"Preserve security reasoning while providing safe external errors.","requirements":["Define internal security error categories.","Map them to safe external representations.","Preserve evidence and cause metadata internally."],"constraints":["External clients may need a simplified error vocabulary.","Internal records must remain protected."],"preconditions":["Multiple security failure states exist."],"input":{"internal_errors":["denied","invalid","dependency_failure","unknown"]},"output":{"external_error":"safe_normalized","internal_state":"preserved"},"expected_behavior":"External messaging can be simplified while internal security state remains distinguishable.","correct_example":"Return a common protected-operation failure code externally while retaining whether the internal cause was denial or authorization dependency failure.","invalid_example":"Convert every security exception to success because the caller should not see details.","failure_mode":"Security-state erasure","cause":"Error normalization performed before security classification","root_cause":"Presentation and security semantics were coupled","solution":"Separate internal security state from external error presentation","alternative":"Use structured internal event types and safe public error codes","tradeoff":"Requires maintaining two representations.","edge_case":"A client legitimately needs to distinguish retryable dependency failure from permanent denial.","security_implication":"Exposing too much detail can leak policy state; exposing too little can cause unsafe retries.","test_case":"Map multiple internal security states to external responses.","test_input":{"states":["denied","dependency_failure","unknown"]},"expected_test_result":"Security distinctions remain available to trusted internal processing while external disclosure follows policy.","testing_guidance":"Test both internal classification and external redaction.","verification_method":"Trace errors from origin through classification, logging, and response generation.","verification_guidance":"Verify no transformation erases evidence needed for security analysis.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B18-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B18-R05","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Error Handling","concept":"Security error paths require the same authorization discipline as success paths","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security error handling itself be subject to authorization controls?","answer":"Error paths can expose protected diagnostics, resource existence, policy information, or sensitive identifiers. A request that is unauthorized for the success path may also be unauthorized for detailed error information.","explanation":"Security reviews often focus on normal execution and overlook failure branches. Error handlers can become an alternate information-disclosure path if they bypass access-control checks.","principle":"Failure paths remain within the security boundary.","rule":"Apply appropriate authorization and disclosure controls to security-relevant error responses and diagnostics.","objective":"Prevent error-path authorization bypasses.","requirements":["Identify protected diagnostic data.","Apply disclosure policy to error paths.","Test unauthorized callers against failure scenarios."],"constraints":["Operational diagnostics may require privileged access.","Generic errors may be appropriate for untrusted callers."],"preconditions":["Error behavior can reveal security-sensitive information."],"input":{"caller":"unauthorized","failure":"protected_resource_error"},"output":{"response":"disclosure_controlled"},"expected_behavior":"Unauthorized callers cannot use error behavior to obtain protected information.","correct_example":"An unauthorized caller receives a generic error while authorized diagnostic personnel can access protected details through a separate channel.","invalid_example":"The normal endpoint denies access but its error response reveals the protected resource's contents.","failure_mode":"Error-path information disclosure","cause":"Error handler bypassed disclosure authorization","root_cause":"Failure paths were excluded from security boundary analysis","solution":"Apply security review and authorization controls to error handling","alternative":"Use minimal external errors and protected diagnostics","tradeoff":"Restricted diagnostics can slow troubleshooting for unprivileged operators.","edge_case":"The error itself must disclose enough information to support a legitimate retry.","security_implication":"Disclosure should be bounded to the minimum necessary information.","test_case":"Trigger the same protected failure under different caller authorities.","test_input":{"callers":["unauthorized","authorized"]},"expected_test_result":"Disclosure differs according to explicit policy rather than error-path accident.","testing_guidance":"Test error branches with multiple identities and resource states.","verification_method":"Inspect authorization and disclosure checks in error handlers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that failure responses cannot reveal protected resource existence or contents beyond policy.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B08-R01","D57-B18-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B19-R01","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B19","dataset_version":"1.0.0","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Change Management","concept":"Security-sensitive code changes require security acceptance criteria","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should security changes have explicit acceptance criteria before closure?","answer":"A code or configuration change describes what was modified, not whether the intended security property was restored. Acceptance criteria translate the security objective into observable conditions that can be verified.","explanation":"Without explicit criteria, teams may close security work based on implementation activity rather than security outcome. Criteria should identify the affected control, negative behavior that must be blocked, positive behavior that must remain possible, and evidence required for verification.","principle":"Security remediation is complete only when its intended security properties have been verified.","rule":"Define security acceptance criteria before or during remediation design and verify them after implementation.","objective":"Prevent implementation-complete but security-unverified changes.","requirements":["State security objective.","Define positive and negative expected behavior.","Define required evidence.","Define residual-risk handling."],"constraints":["Some properties cannot be fully proven through tests alone.","Acceptance criteria must match the claim being made."],"preconditions":["A security change has a defined objective."],"input":{"change":"security_control_update","acceptance_criteria":"defined"},"output":{"closure_condition":"verification_required"},"expected_behavior":"The change remains verification-pending until security acceptance criteria are satisfied.","correct_example":"A remediation requires unauthorized access to be denied under specified conditions and provides test evidence for that behavior.","invalid_example":"The issue is closed because the patch was merged.","failure_mode":"Premature security closure","cause":"Implementation completion treated as security verification","root_cause":"No explicit security acceptance criteria","solution":"Define claim-specific acceptance criteria","alternative":"Use security verification templates for recurring control classes","tradeoff":"Criteria creation adds planning effort.","edge_case":"A security property is probabilistic or depends on an external service.","security_implication":"Acceptance criteria should include dependency assumptions and evidence limits.","test_case":"Attempt to close a security remediation without evidence for its acceptance criteria.","test_input":{"implementation":"complete","verification":"missing"},"expected_test_result":"Closure is blocked or marked unverified.","testing_guidance":"Test closure workflow with incomplete evidence.","verification_method":"Compare remediation status with acceptance-criteria evidence.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit verification state before security closure.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B04-R05","D57-B05-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B19-R02","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Change Management","concept":"Security changes should be evaluated for unintended control regression","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What lesson applies when a security fix changes behavior in adjacent controls?","answer":"A remediation should be evaluated for unintended weakening of controls that were not the original target. Security changes can alter control ordering, shared state, authorization context, error handling, or other security-sensitive behavior.","explanation":"Fixing one path can create a new exposure elsewhere. Security verification should therefore include relevant adjacent controls and regression conditions rather than testing only the original failure.","principle":"Security remediation can change the surrounding security system, not just the targeted defect.","rule":"Assess relevant adjacent security controls for regression after a material security change.","objective":"Prevent security fixes from introducing new weaknesses.","requirements":["Identify affected neighboring controls.","Define regression tests.","Compare security invariants before and after change."],"constraints":["Regression scope should be risk-based.","Complete formal proof may not be practical for every change."],"preconditions":["The remediation affects shared security code, state, policy, or infrastructure."],"input":{"fix":"authorization_change","adjacent_controls":["audit","delegation"]},"output":{"verification_scope":"expanded"},"expected_behavior":"Relevant adjacent controls are included in post-change verification.","correct_example":"An authorization fix is tested for its effect on delegated operations and audit records.","invalid_example":"Only the original unauthorized request test is rerun.","failure_mode":"Security regression after remediation","cause":"Verification scope matched only original defect","root_cause":"Shared security dependencies were ignored","solution":"Perform impact-based security regression analysis","alternative":"Use security invariants covering multiple dependent controls","tradeoff":"Broader regression testing increases verification effort.","edge_case":"The fix modifies a shared helper used by unrelated security controls.","security_implication":"Shared-code changes can have nonlocal security effects.","test_case":"Change a shared security helper and execute tests for dependent controls.","test_input":{"shared_component":"security_helper","dependents":["authorization","audit","delegation"]},"expected_test_result":"Relevant dependent controls remain within expected security behavior.","testing_guidance":"Build a dependency-aware security regression set.","verification_method":"Trace changed components to security consumers and compare relevant invariants.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for materially affected security controls.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B04-R03","D57-B05-R03"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B19-R03","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Change Management","concept":"Security changes should preserve rollback safety without restoring known exposure blindly","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What security lesson applies when rollback could restore a known security weakness?","answer":"Rollback planning must consider both change failure and the security state that rollback would restore. A technically successful rollback can be security-unsafe if it reintroduces the original exposure.","explanation":"Operational rollback is not automatically a safe security response. The rollback target, compensating controls, and residual risk must be evaluated before restoring a previous state.","principle":"Rollback correctness includes security state, not only deployment state.","rule":"Do not treat rollback as security-safe merely because the previous version was operationally stable.","objective":"Prevent remediation rollback from silently restoring known vulnerabilities.","requirements":["Identify security state of rollback target.","Define compensating controls if rollback is required.","Verify post-rollback security conditions."],"constraints":["Emergency availability needs may require temporary rollback.","Risk acceptance may be necessary for controlled rollback."],"preconditions":["A rollback target contains a previously identified security issue or uncertain security state."],"input":{"current_version":"remediated","rollback_target":"known_exposure"},"output":{"decision":"rollback_requires_security_assessment"},"expected_behavior":"Rollback considers the security implications of restoring the previous state.","correct_example":"If rollback is unavoidable, activate a documented compensating control and verify the resulting security boundary.","invalid_example":"Rollback immediately because the previous release was stable.","failure_mode":"Security regression through rollback","cause":"Operational rollback treated as security-neutral","root_cause":"Deployment state and security state were modeled separately without coordination","solution":"Include security posture in rollback criteria","alternative":"Use a forward fix or protected rollback with compensating controls","tradeoff":"Avoiding rollback can increase availability risk while rollback can increase security risk.","edge_case":"The current release has a new critical operational failure while the previous release has a known security defect.","security_implication":"Decision requires explicit risk and authority handling rather than automatic rollback.","test_case":"Simulate rollback to a version with a known security exposure.","test_input":{"rollback_target_security":"known_risk"},"expected_test_result":"Rollback triggers security assessment and appropriate controls rather than automatic restoration.","testing_guidance":"Test rollback paths for known security regressions.","verification_method":"Inspect rollback policy and verify post-rollback security state.","verification_guidance":"Record security risk and compensating controls for exceptional rollback.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"VERSION_SPECIFIC","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B06-R02","D57-B19-R02"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B19-R04","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Change Management","concept":"Security changes require controlled deployment identity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security verification identify the exact artifact being verified?","answer":"A security test result applies to the artifact, configuration, and environment actually tested. If deployment identity is ambiguous, verification evidence can be incorrectly attributed to another build or state.","explanation":"Security assurance depends on matching evidence to the exact implementation under consideration. Artifact identity can include version, source revision, configuration, and relevant dependency state.","principle":"Security evidence must be bound to the artifact and environment it verifies.","rule":"Record and verify the identity of the exact artifact and relevant configuration associated with security evidence.","objective":"Prevent evidence-to-artifact mismatches.","requirements":["Identify artifact version or immutable identity.","Identify relevant configuration.","Bind verification results to the tested state."],"constraints":["Dynamic environments may require multiple identity dimensions.","Some external dependencies can change independently."],"preconditions":["A security test or verification result is being recorded."],"input":{"artifact":"build-X","tested_artifact":"build-Y"},"output":{"verification_status":"mismatch"},"expected_behavior":"Evidence from build Y is not treated as verification of build X.","correct_example":"A security test result references the exact artifact digest or equivalent immutable identity that was tested.","invalid_example":"The security test passed yesterday, so the current build is verified.","failure_mode":"Verification artifact mismatch","cause":"Artifact identity omitted or changed","root_cause":"Verification evidence was not cryptographically or logically bound to implementation state","solution":"Bind security evidence to immutable artifact identity and configuration","alternative":"Re-run verification on the exact release candidate","tradeoff":"Strong artifact binding can require better build metadata and reproducibility.","edge_case":"Two builds have identical source but different dependency resolution.","security_implication":"Dependency differences can change security behavior.","test_case":"Attempt to apply a security verification result from one artifact to another artifact.","test_input":{"verified":"artifact_A","target":"artifact_B"},"expected_test_result":"Verification is rejected or marked non-transferable unless equivalence is established.","testing_guidance":"Test artifact, dependency, and configuration identity mismatches.","verification_method":"Compare recorded artifact identities with deployment and test metadata.","verification_guidance":"Require exact or justified equivalent identity before verification reuse.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"VERSION_AWARE","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B05-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B19-R05","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Change Management","concept":"Security deployment verification should cover configuration and dependency state","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is source revision alone insufficient to identify the security state of a deployment?","answer":"Security behavior can depend on configuration, dependencies, runtime policy, external identity systems, and deployment conditions. Verification should therefore bind the relevant state dimensions to the tested result.","explanation":"Two deployments with identical application source can differ in security posture if their dependency graph, configuration, secrets, policies, or external services differ. The lesson is to verify the security-relevant deployment state rather than source alone.","principle":"Security posture is a property of the effective deployed system state.","rule":"Include materially security-relevant configuration, dependencies, policies, and external security dependencies in deployment verification scope.","objective":"Prevent source-only security assurance.","requirements":["Identify effective security state.","Capture relevant dependency state.","Capture security-critical configuration and policy.","Verify external dependencies when material."],"constraints":["Not every environmental detail affects security.","Scope should be based on the security claim."],"preconditions":["Security behavior depends on more than source code."],"input":{"source_revision":"same","configuration":["different"],"dependencies":["different"]},"output":{"security_verification":"not_transferable_without_analysis"},"expected_behavior":"Security verification is not automatically reused across materially different deployment states.","correct_example":"A verified source revision requires renewed review when the production identity provider or security policy configuration differs.","invalid_example":"The source code is identical, therefore the deployment has identical security posture.","failure_mode":"Deployment-state mismatch","cause":"Source identity treated as complete security identity","root_cause":"Effective security state was under-modeled","solution":"Bind security verification to relevant deployment state","alternative":"Use immutable deployment bundles with controlled configuration inputs","tradeoff":"State capture increases metadata and deployment governance requirements.","edge_case":"A security dependency is external and changes without application deployment.","security_implication":"External change can invalidate previous security assumptions.","test_case":"Keep source constant while changing a security-critical external dependency.","test_input":{"source":"constant","external_security_dependency":"changed"},"expected_test_result":"Security verification is flagged for re-evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Test source-stable but security-state-changing scenarios.","verification_method":"Compare deployment security-state metadata with verification scope.","verification_guidance":"Require re-verification after material security dependency changes.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"VERSION_AWARE","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B11-R02","D57-B19-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B20-R01","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Generalization","concept":"Security lessons should generalize mechanisms and conditions rather than incident narratives","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should a security lesson be generalized without merely copying an incident narrative?","answer":"Extract the causal mechanism, security condition, control interaction, evidence boundary, and applicability conditions while retaining a relationship to the originating record. Do not generalize a unique narrative detail into a universal rule without evidence.","explanation":"A reusable lesson should preserve what is structurally useful while avoiding accidental overfitting to one system, incident, identifier, or deployment. Relationships can preserve lineage without duplicating the original evidence.","principle":"Generalization should preserve causal structure and applicability conditions while removing irrelevant incident-specific detail.","rule":"Generalize from the mechanism and conditions, not from incidental narrative details.","objective":"Create reusable security knowledge without overgeneralization.","requirements":["Identify mechanism.","Preserve material conditions.","Retain evidence scope.","Link to source knowledge."],"constraints":["Some lessons are inherently system-specific.","Generalization strength must match evidence."],"preconditions":["A source security observation or analysis exists, or the record is explicitly marked illustrative."],"input":{"source":"security_observation","mechanism":"control_failure_under_condition"},"output":{"generalized_lesson":"condition_and_mechanism_based"},"expected_behavior":"The reusable lesson remains bounded by evidence and does not invent universal applicability.","correct_example":"Generalize that authorization decisions can become stale when authority changes before enforcement, while retaining the original context as lineage.","invalid_example":"This exact architecture proves all authorization systems have the same failure.","failure_mode":"Overgeneralization","cause":"Incident-specific details promoted to universal rule","root_cause":"Evidence scope was lost during abstraction","solution":"Generalize mechanism plus conditions and preserve lineage","alternative":"Maintain multiple abstraction levels linked by relationships","tradeoff":"More contextual metadata reduces apparent simplicity but improves reuse accuracy.","edge_case":"A mechanism appears in several unrelated systems with different implementations.","security_implication":"Repeated evidence can increase confidence, but each observation still needs provenance.","test_case":"Generalize a system-specific security observation into a reusable rule.","test_input":{"source_scope":"single_system","target_scope":"general"},"expected_test_result":"The generalized rule preserves conditions and evidence limitations.","testing_guidance":"Review abstraction for unsupported universal claims.","verification_method":"Compare generalized lesson against its source evidence and applicability conditions.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence level for the generalized statement.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B10-R01","D57-B10-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B20-R02","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Generalization","concept":"Security lessons should separate control-specific rules from general security principles","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should reusable security knowledge distinguish a control-specific lesson from a general principle?","answer":"A control-specific lesson describes conditions for one mechanism or architecture, while a general principle describes a broader security property supported across contexts. Mixing the two can cause narrow evidence to be applied too broadly.","explanation":"Abstraction level determines retrieval and applicability. A lesson should identify whether it concerns a particular implementation pattern or a broader security concept.","principle":"The abstraction level of a security lesson is part of its meaning.","rule":"Label security knowledge according to its scope and do not promote implementation-specific evidence into general principles without supporting evidence.","objective":"Prevent abstraction-level errors in retrieval and reasoning.","requirements":["Identify abstraction level.","Preserve implementation context.","Require broader evidence for broader claims."],"constraints":["The same mechanism can support multiple abstraction levels.","General principles may still have conditions."],"preconditions":["A lesson is being transformed into reusable knowledge."],"input":{"lesson_type":"control_specific","claim":"bounded"},"output":{"abstraction":"control_specific"},"expected_behavior":"Retrieval systems can distinguish narrow implementation guidance from general security principles.","correct_example":"Record a lesson about a particular authorization cache separately from the general principle that stale authority should not authorize protected actions.","invalid_example":"A single cache implementation failure proves all caching is insecure.","failure_mode":"Abstraction-level leakage","cause":"Narrow lesson generalized without evidence","root_cause":"Knowledge type and scope were omitted","solution":"Tag abstraction level and maintain relationships between specific and general records","alternative":"Use hierarchical knowledge representations","tradeoff":"Additional metadata improves precision at the cost of schema complexity.","edge_case":"A control-specific lesson has independently recurring evidence across several implementations.","security_implication":"Repeated evidence may justify a broader principle but should not erase implementation-specific conditions.","test_case":"Retrieve lessons for a generic authorization query and an implementation-specific cache query.","test_input":{"queries":["authorization freshness","specific authorization cache"]},"expected_test_result":"Relevant abstraction levels remain distinguishable.","testing_guidance":"Test retrieval precision across general and implementation-specific queries.","verification_method":"Inspect knowledge-type, scope, and relationship metadata.","verification_guidance":"Verify general records are not created solely from one narrow example.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B08-R03","D57-B20-R01"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B20-R03","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Generalization","concept":"Security lessons should connect preventive, detective, and corrective controls","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should a security lesson identify preventive, detective, and corrective control relationships?","answer":"A single control may not prevent every failure. Linking prevention, detection, response, and correction shows how the security system behaves when a control fails and helps identify defense gaps.","explanation":"Security resilience often depends on layered controls. A reusable lesson is more useful when it records which control prevented the condition, which control detected it, and which control corrected or contained it, while keeping observed evidence separate from recommended design.","principle":"Security lessons should explain control interactions rather than treating one control as the entire defense.","rule":"Where materially relevant, link lessons to preventive, detective, containment, corrective, and verification controls.","objective":"Improve layered-security reasoning and identify missing control classes.","requirements":["Identify relevant control roles.","Distinguish observed controls from recommended controls.","Preserve evidence status for each relationship."],"constraints":["Not every lesson requires every control class.","Control classification can be context-dependent."],"preconditions":["The lesson concerns a security condition that can involve multiple controls."],"input":{"condition":"security_control_failure","controls":{"preventive":"present","detective":"present","corrective":"unknown"}},"output":{"control_model":"layered"},"expected_behavior":"The lesson exposes which security layers were established and which remain uncertain or missing.","correct_example":"A lesson records that prevention failed, detection identified the condition, and corrective action remains verification-pending.","invalid_example":"Detection means the system was secure because the attack was observed.","failure_mode":"Single-control reasoning","cause":"Only one security layer considered","root_cause":"Control interaction was not modeled","solution":"Represent layered control relationships","alternative":"Use a security-control graph linked to lessons","tradeoff":"Control graphs increase modeling complexity but improve gap analysis.","edge_case":"A detective control triggers a corrective action automatically.","security_implication":"Automation creates a new authorization and verification boundary that must also be evaluated.","test_case":"Disable preventive control while leaving detective control active.","test_input":{"preventive":"disabled","detective":"enabled"},"expected_test_result":"Detection behavior remains distinguishable from prevention success.","testing_guidance":"Test individual control failure and layered-control interactions.","verification_method":"Trace the security event across control layers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that detection or response is not incorrectly recorded as prevention success.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B04-R01","D57-B05-R01","D57-B07-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B20-R04","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Generalization","concept":"Security knowledge must support supersession without deleting historical lineage","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a security lesson be updated when later evidence changes its applicability?","answer":"Create or update a versioned successor relationship while retaining the historical record and its original evidence scope. The newer lesson should explain what changed and why the previous lesson is no longer sufficient or applicable.","explanation":"Deleting old security knowledge destroys provenance and can make historical decisions impossible to reconstruct. Supersession preserves lineage while directing current retrieval toward the newer understanding.","principle":"Security knowledge evolves through evidence while historical provenance remains preserved.","rule":"Use explicit versioning and supersession relationships when later evidence changes a security lesson.","objective":"Maintain current security knowledge without erasing historical evidence.","requirements":["Preserve historical record.","Create successor relationship.","Record reason for supersession.","Update applicability metadata."],"constraints":["Not every clarification requires supersession.","Conflicting evidence may require a conflicted state instead."],"preconditions":["New evidence materially changes a prior lesson's interpretation or applicability."],"input":{"old_lesson":"L1","new_evidence":"E2","impact":"material"},"output":{"relationship":"L2_supersedes_L1"},"expected_behavior":"Current retrieval favors the applicable successor while historical lineage remains available.","correct_example":"A new lesson narrows the applicability of an older lesson and links to it with an explicit supersedes or refines relationship.","invalid_example":"Delete the old lesson and replace its text without preserving provenance.","failure_mode":"Historical knowledge loss","cause":"Old lesson overwritten or deleted","root_cause":"Knowledge lifecycle lacked versioned lineage","solution":"Preserve records and represent supersession explicitly","alternative":"Mark old records deprecated while retaining them for historical queries","tradeoff":"Versioned history increases storage and retrieval complexity.","edge_case":"New evidence contradicts rather than supersedes the original because both conditions remain valid in different contexts.","security_implication":"False supersession can erase a valid security condition.","test_case":"Introduce evidence that changes applicability but does not invalidate historical correctness.","test_input":{"relationship":"contextual_refinement"},"expected_test_result":"The system uses refinement or scoped coexistence rather than inappropriate deletion.","testing_guidance":"Test supersession, refinement, contradiction, and contextual coexistence.","verification_method":"Inspect relationship graph and version metadata.","verification_guidance":"Verify historical records remain immutable or traceably preserved.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"VERSIONED","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R04","D57-B10-R04"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B20-R05","dataset_id":"D57","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Generalization","concept":"A security lesson should be promoted to reusable knowledge only when evidence and applicability are sufficient","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What criteria should govern promotion of a security lesson into reusable knowledge?","answer":"Promotion should require sufficient evidence for the claim, explicit scope and assumptions, a defined abstraction level, traceable provenance or clearly marked non-verified status, contradiction review, deduplication, and appropriate verification status. A generated lesson should not be promoted as operational experience without verified operational evidence.","explanation":"Knowledge promotion is a trust boundary. If weak observations or synthetic examples are promoted without status, later systems may treat them as facts and build decisions on unsupported assumptions.","principle":"Knowledge promotion must be evidence-gated and status-preserving.","rule":"Promote a security lesson only when its claim, scope, provenance, evidence level, contradictions, and verification state are explicitly represented and satisfy the dataset's promotion policy.","objective":"Prevent unsupported security knowledge from entering the trusted reusable knowledge layer.","requirements":["Claim must be clearly defined.","Evidence status must be explicit.","Scope and assumptions must be recorded.","Contradictions must be assessed.","Duplicates must be removed.","Operational experience must require actual verified evidence."],"constraints":["Promotion thresholds may vary by knowledge class.","Some useful heuristic knowledge may remain unverified by design."],"preconditions":["A candidate security lesson exists."],"input":{"candidate":{"claim":"security_lesson","provenance":"unknown","operational_observation":"absent"}},"output":{"promotion":"general_knowledge_only_if_status_preserved; operational_experience_not_promoted"},"expected_behavior":"The system refuses to represent an unsupported synthetic example as verified operational experience.","correct_example":"Store a well-established security principle as general knowledge with explicit provenance status, while marking an unexecuted project-specific scenario as illustrative.","invalid_example":"Promote a generated scenario as a verified security incident because it is plausible.","failure_mode":"Knowledge trust-boundary violation","cause":"Evidence status lost during promotion","root_cause":"Generation, observation, verification, and knowledge promotion were conflated","solution":"Implement evidence-gated promotion with immutable status transitions","alternative":"Use separate trust tiers for verified experience, general knowledge, inference, and illustrative examples","tradeoff":"Strict promotion reduces apparent knowledge volume but increases reliability.","edge_case":"A heuristic is highly useful but lacks direct operational verification.","security_implication":"Useful does not mean verified; the heuristic should remain clearly classified.","test_case":"Attempt to promote an unexecuted illustrative security scenario as verified experience.","test_input":{"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence":"not_operational"},"expected_test_result":"Promotion to verified operational experience is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Test promotion across VERIFIED_FACT, INFERENCE, RECOMMENDATION, UNKNOWN, CONFLICTED, and ILLUSTRATIVE states.","verification_method":"Validate promotion rules against evidence, provenance, execution, verification, contradiction, and deduplication metadata.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit state transitions and prohibit unsupported evidence upgrades.","language":"GENERAL","language_version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["evidence_model","provenance_model","verification_model","deduplication_model"],"execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B05-R01","D57-B10-R05"],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}

{"record_id":"D57-B21-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Input Security","concept":"Input validation lesson","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson should be captured when untrusted input reaches a security-sensitive operation?","answer":"Security-sensitive operations should validate input against an explicit expected contract before use; validation should be tied to the operation's actual security assumptions rather than relying on superficial filtering.","explanation":"A security lesson is stronger when it identifies which input property the security control depended on, what assumption failed, and how validation or safe handling prevents recurrence. Generic statements such as 'validate input' are insufficient without defining the relevant invariant.","principle":"Validate security-relevant input according to the invariant required by the consuming operation.","rule":"Do not treat syntactic validity as proof of security validity.","objective":"Prevent malformed, ambiguous, or attacker-controlled input from violating security assumptions.","requirements":["Identify the security-sensitive input boundary.","Define the expected input invariant.","Validate before security-sensitive use.","Preserve evidence showing what was validated."],"constraints":["Validation must match the consuming operation.","Validation alone does not establish complete security."],"preconditions":["An untrusted or partially trusted input crosses a security boundary."],"expected_behavior":"Inputs violating security-relevant invariants are rejected or handled safely before reaching the sensitive operation.","correct_example":"A parser accepts only explicitly permitted structured fields and rejects unexpected security-sensitive values before authorization logic consumes them.","invalid_example":"Accepting arbitrary input because it conforms to a broad syntactic format.","failure_mode":"Security control receives input outside its assumed domain.","cause":"Input contract is incomplete, ambiguous, or not enforced.","root_cause":"Security assumptions about input are not represented as enforceable validation invariants.","solution":"Define and enforce operation-specific security invariants at the appropriate trust boundary.","alternative":"Use a strongly constrained parser or typed representation when it provides equivalent invariant enforcement.","tradeoff":"Stricter validation can reject previously tolerated inputs and may require compatibility handling.","edge_case":"An input can be syntactically valid while still violating authorization, path, command, query, or resource-selection invariants.","security_implication":"Failure can create injection, authorization bypass, unsafe resource selection, or parser-confusion conditions depending on the operation.","test_case":"Submit valid-looking inputs that violate each security-relevant invariant.","test_input":"Boundary-value, ambiguous, unexpected-field, and attacker-controlled values.","expected_test_result":"Security-invalid inputs are rejected or safely normalized without bypassing the intended control.","testing_guidance":"Test both ordinary invalid inputs and adversarial representations of the same semantic value.","verification_method":"Inspect the validation contract and execute negative security tests where an executable implementation exists.","verification_guidance":"Verify that validation occurs before the security-sensitive operation and that rejected values cannot reach a bypass path.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B22-R01","D57-B27-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B21-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Parser Security","concept":"Parser differential handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What lesson should be captured when different components interpret the same security-sensitive input differently?","answer":"Security-critical data should have a single well-defined interpretation across components, or the system must explicitly control and test interpretation differences.","explanation":"Security failures can arise when validation, authorization, routing, and execution components disagree about the meaning of the same input. A lesson should preserve the semantic mismatch rather than merely documenting the malformed input.","principle":"Security decisions must operate on the same semantic representation that the protected operation ultimately uses.","rule":"Do not validate one interpretation of data and execute another interpretation.","objective":"Prevent parser differentials and inconsistent canonicalization from creating security-control bypasses.","requirements":["Identify all relevant parsers or interpreters.","Define the canonical representation.","Test equivalent and conflicting representations."],"constraints":["Canonicalization must not destroy information needed for legitimate processing.","Multiple formats may be required for compatibility."],"preconditions":["More than one component interprets security-sensitive data."],"expected_behavior":"Security validation and downstream execution agree on the meaning of accepted data.","failure_mode":"Validation approves one interpretation while a downstream component acts on another.","cause":"Different parsing, decoding, normalization, or canonicalization rules.","root_cause":"No enforced semantic contract exists between security validation and downstream consumers.","solution":"Establish a canonical representation and verify that all security-sensitive components use compatible semantics.","alternative":"Constrain the interface so only one parser handles the security-sensitive representation.","tradeoff":"Canonicalization can introduce compatibility complexity and must itself be security-reviewed.","edge_case":"Equivalent-looking encodings may become different values after decoding or normalization.","security_implication":"Parser differentials can undermine validation, authorization, routing, or policy enforcement.","test_case":"Provide semantically equivalent inputs encoded through different supported representations.","test_input":"Multiple encodings, normalization forms, delimiters, and parser-specific representations.","expected_test_result":"All security-sensitive representations receive consistent policy treatment.","testing_guidance":"Include differential tests comparing security-decision input with execution input.","verification_method":"Compare parser behavior and security decisions across all components handling the representation.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic equivalence assumptions with automated differential tests where feasible.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B21-R01","D57-B23-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B21-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Canonicalization","concept":"Canonicalization before security decision","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should canonicalization be treated as part of a security lesson?","answer":"Canonicalization belongs in the security lesson whenever security policy depends on equivalence between multiple representations of the same logical value.","explanation":"If policy evaluates a value before normalization but the protected operation consumes a normalized form, the security decision may not correspond to the actual resource or action. The lesson should identify the exact normalization boundary and its security invariant.","principle":"Security policy should evaluate the representation that defines the protected semantic value.","rule":"Canonicalize consistently before making decisions that depend on canonical identity.","objective":"Prevent representation ambiguity from bypassing security policy.","requirements":["Define canonical form.","Define which transformations are security-relevant.","Apply canonicalization consistently."],"constraints":["Canonicalization must be deterministic and appropriate to the data type.","Not every data type has a safe universal canonical form."],"preconditions":["Multiple representations can denote the same security-relevant value."],"expected_behavior":"Security decisions are made against a deterministic, policy-compatible representation.","failure_mode":"Two representations receive different security treatment despite representing the same protected value.","cause":"Security decision occurs at the wrong representation layer.","root_cause":"The system lacks a defined relationship between representation normalization and security policy.","solution":"Specify and enforce canonicalization semantics as part of the security contract.","alternative":"Reject non-canonical representations when compatibility permits.","tradeoff":"Rejecting variants can reduce ambiguity but may reduce interoperability.","edge_case":"Normalization rules can change across standards or library versions.","security_implication":"Inconsistent canonicalization can create authorization, identity, path, or policy bypasses.","test_case":"Test canonical and non-canonical representations of equivalent values.","test_input":"Equivalent representations before and after the required normalization.","expected_test_result":"Equivalent protected values receive equivalent security decisions.","testing_guidance":"Include regression tests for every security-relevant canonicalization rule.","verification_method":"Review normalization rules and compare policy decisions before and after canonicalization.","verification_guidance":"Verify that canonicalization is applied exactly once or that repeated application is idempotent where required.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B21-R02","D57-B22-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B21-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Boundary Enforcement","concept":"Security validation at trust boundaries","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security lessons identify the trust boundary where validation occurs?","answer":"A security control is meaningful only if untrusted data cannot bypass the boundary at which its security assumptions are established.","explanation":"Validation performed too early can become invalid after transformation, and validation performed too late may allow unsafe processing before the control acts. A reusable lesson should therefore capture the boundary, the protected operation, and the invariant connecting them.","principle":"Place security controls at the boundary where trust changes and verify that every path crossing the boundary is covered.","rule":"Do not assume that validation on one ingress path protects all paths to the same sensitive operation.","objective":"Prevent alternate ingress paths from bypassing security assumptions.","requirements":["Map trust boundaries.","Identify all paths to the protected operation.","Apply equivalent security invariants to every applicable path."],"constraints":["Some internal paths may have stronger preconditions but must be explicitly documented.","Duplicated controls can create inconsistent policy if not centrally defined."],"preconditions":["Data or authority crosses a trust boundary before a security-sensitive operation."],"expected_behavior":"Every path into the protected operation satisfies the required security contract.","failure_mode":"An alternate path reaches the sensitive operation without equivalent validation.","cause":"Incomplete attack-surface mapping or undocumented trust assumptions.","root_cause":"Security policy is attached to an ingress point rather than to the protected security invariant.","solution":"Define the invariant at the security boundary and verify all reachable paths against it.","alternative":"Centralize the sensitive operation behind a single policy-enforcing interface.","tradeoff":"Centralization can simplify policy enforcement but may introduce architectural coupling.","edge_case":"Internal services can become untrusted after compromise, configuration drift, or privilege changes.","security_implication":"Boundary bypass can convert a locally safe operation into a privilege or data-protection failure.","test_case":"Enumerate alternate paths and attempt to reach the protected operation through each.","expected_test_result":"Every reachable path enforces the required security invariant.","testing_guidance":"Include direct internal calls, alternate APIs, background jobs, administrative paths, and migration utilities where applicable.","verification_method":"Review call paths and security-control placement; execute bypass tests where implementation exists.","verification_guidance":"Verify coverage of all security-sensitive entry paths rather than only the primary interface.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B21-R01","D57-B24-R02","D57-B28-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B21-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Scope","concept":"Security issue boundary preservation","knowledge_type":"LESSON_PATTERN","question":"How should a security lesson preserve the scope of the original issue?","answer":"A security lesson should retain the affected asset, trust boundary, threat condition, security property, and applicable environment so that later users do not generalize a context-specific finding into an unsupported universal claim.","explanation":"Security knowledge loses precision when environmental assumptions disappear during abstraction. The reusable lesson should generalize the mechanism while retaining the conditions under which the lesson is valid.","principle":"Generalize the security mechanism, not the incident context beyond its evidence.","rule":"Every reusable security lesson should state its applicability conditions.","objective":"Prevent context loss and over-generalization during knowledge reuse.","requirements":["Preserve affected security property.","Preserve relevant environment assumptions.","Record applicability conditions.","Separate observed facts from generalized inference."],"constraints":["Some operational context may be unavailable.","Generalization should not claim evidence that was never observed."],"preconditions":["A security finding or pattern is being converted into reusable knowledge."],"expected_behavior":"Consumers can determine whether the lesson applies to their own security boundary and conditions.","failure_mode":"A context-specific lesson is reused outside its evidence-supported scope.","cause":"Context and applicability constraints were discarded during abstraction.","root_cause":"Knowledge representation does not preserve the conditions under which the security lesson was derived.","solution":"Store applicability, limitations, evidence classification, and generalized mechanism separately.","alternative":"Maintain a linked incident-specific record and a narrower reusable lesson record.","tradeoff":"Additional metadata increases storage and processing complexity but improves safe reuse.","edge_case":"A lesson may remain valid across architectures while its exact remediation does not.","security_implication":"Over-generalized security knowledge can cause inappropriate controls, missed threats, or false assurance.","test_case":"Attempt to retrieve and apply the lesson to environments with different trust boundaries.","expected_test_result":"The knowledge representation exposes whether the lesson's applicability conditions are satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Test retrieval with both matching and intentionally mismatched contexts.","verification_method":"Review scope metadata and applicability constraints.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the lesson does not state an observed outcome beyond the available evidence.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R01","D57-B30-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B22-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secrets","concept":"Secret exposure through diagnostic output","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when sensitive credentials or tokens can appear in diagnostic output?","answer":"Diagnostic output should exclude secrets by design, and security verification should test realistic error, debug, tracing, and exception paths rather than only normal logging paths.","explanation":"Secrets can reach diagnostic channels through automatic exception rendering, request dumps, debug payloads, tracing attributes, or developer-added fields. The lesson should address both the exposure path and the absence of a safe output invariant.","principle":"Sensitive values should not be emitted into channels whose confidentiality is not explicitly guaranteed.","rule":"Treat diagnostic output as a potential data-exposure boundary.","objective":"Prevent credentials, tokens, keys, and other sensitive values from being exposed through diagnostics.","requirements":["Identify sensitive fields.","Define redaction or exclusion policy.","Cover normal and exceptional diagnostic paths.","Verify emitted output."],"constraints":["Redaction can fail if sensitive data is transformed or embedded in larger values.","Some diagnostic systems automatically capture context."],"preconditions":["Application or tooling produces diagnostic output containing request, error, trace, or runtime context."],"expected_behavior":"Sensitive values are excluded, safely redacted, or otherwise protected in diagnostic output.","failure_mode":"A secret appears in a log, trace, error response, dump, or diagnostic artifact.","cause":"Sensitive data was included in diagnostic context without an effective protection policy.","root_cause":"Data-classification and diagnostic-output controls are not connected by an enforceable invariant.","solution":"Minimize diagnostic fields, centrally redact known sensitive data, and test exceptional paths for leakage.","alternative":"Use structured diagnostic events with explicit allowlisted fields.","tradeoff":"Allowlisting can reduce diagnostic detail and may require additional engineering effort.","edge_case":"Secrets can be encoded, concatenated, nested, or embedded inside serialized objects.","security_implication":"Exposure can enable unauthorized access if the affected secret remains usable.","test_case":"Trigger normal, validation-error, exception, timeout, and debug paths with synthetic secret markers.","test_input":"Unique non-production secret markers placed in sensitive fields.","expected_test_result":"No sensitive marker appears in diagnostic output outside an explicitly protected channel.","testing_guidance":"Use synthetic credentials rather than real secrets during testing.","verification_method":"Inspect diagnostic sinks and run automated leakage tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify both application-generated and automatically generated diagnostic content.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B09-R02","D57-B25-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B22-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secrets","concept":"Secret lifetime minimization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security lesson follows from retaining secrets longer than required?","answer":"Secrets should exist only for the minimum lifetime and scope required by the operation, because unnecessary retention increases the number of locations and times at which compromise can expose them.","explanation":"Secret lifetime is a security property separate from encryption or access control. A reusable lesson should distinguish storage protection from minimizing how long the secret remains available to application logic or intermediate systems.","principle":"Reduce secret exposure by minimizing lifetime, scope, and copies.","rule":"Do not retain sensitive credentials beyond the period required for the authorized operation.","objective":"Reduce the attack surface created by secret retention and propagation.","requirements":["Define required secret lifetime.","Avoid unnecessary copies.","Release or invalidate secret material when no longer required where practical.","Document unavoidable retention."],"constraints":["Some protocols require controlled credential reuse or rotation windows.","Runtime memory handling differs by language and platform."],"preconditions":["An application obtains or processes sensitive secret material."],"expected_behavior":"Secret material is available only where and when required by the authorized operation.","failure_mode":"Secrets persist unnecessarily in memory, temporary artifacts, caches, or configuration state.","cause":"Convenience-driven retention or unclear ownership of secret lifetime.","root_cause":"Secret lifecycle is not treated as an explicit security requirement.","solution":"Define lifecycle boundaries and minimize secret propagation and retention.","alternative":"Use short-lived credentials or scoped tokens where supported.","tradeoff":"Shorter lifetimes can increase refresh complexity and operational dependencies.","edge_case":"Crash dumps, debugging tools, temporary files, and process snapshots may retain data outside the intended application lifecycle.","security_implication":"Longer secret exposure windows increase potential impact after compromise.","test_case":"Inspect application behavior before, during, and after secret-consuming operations.","test_input":"Synthetic short-lived credential material.","expected_test_result":"No unnecessary persistence or propagation of the synthetic secret is observed within the tested scope.","testing_guidance":"Include crash, retry, caching, and temporary-artifact paths where applicable.","verification_method":"Review lifecycle design and inspect known persistence locations.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim memory erasure guarantees unless the runtime and implementation actually establish them.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B22-R01","D57-B22-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B22-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secrets","concept":"Secret storage protection versus access control","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why is encrypted secret storage not sufficient by itself as a security lesson?","answer":"Encryption at rest protects stored material under specified threat assumptions, but it does not by itself determine which identities or processes may retrieve or use the secret.","explanation":"A complete lesson must separate confidentiality protection of stored material from authorization over secret access. A system can protect storage cryptographically while still exposing secrets to an over-privileged component.","principle":"Protection of stored data and authorization to access data are separate security controls.","rule":"Do not treat encryption of secret storage as proof that secret access is appropriately restricted.","objective":"Prevent false assurance caused by conflating storage confidentiality with authorization.","requirements":["Define storage protection.","Define retrieval authorization.","Audit access where appropriate.","Test unauthorized retrieval."],"constraints":["Encryption key management introduces its own trust dependencies.","Access requirements vary by secret type and operation."],"preconditions":["Sensitive secrets are persisted or managed by a storage mechanism."],"expected_behavior":"Stored secrets receive appropriate confidentiality protection and retrieval is restricted by explicit authorization.","failure_mode":"Any sufficiently privileged application component can retrieve protected secrets without a necessary business or security justification.","cause":"Storage security was implemented without equivalent access-policy design.","root_cause":"Confidentiality at rest and authorization were modeled as one control rather than independent properties.","solution":"Evaluate encryption, key management, identity, authorization, and access auditing separately.","alternative":"Use an external secret-management system with scoped access policies when appropriate.","tradeoff":"Centralized secret management can improve control while adding dependency and availability considerations.","edge_case":"A service may legitimately need access to plaintext secrets at runtime while administrative users should not.","security_implication":"Over-broad retrieval rights can turn a storage compromise or application compromise into credential compromise.","test_case":"Attempt secret retrieval from authorized and unauthorized principals.","test_input":"Synthetic secret with multiple principal identities.","expected_test_result":"Only explicitly authorized principals can retrieve or use the secret as designed.","testing_guidance":"Test both direct retrieval and indirect access through dependent services.","verification_method":"Review storage encryption and access-control policies independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify that encryption keys and secret data do not share an uncontrolled trust boundary.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B06-R04","D57-B09-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B22-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secret Rotation","concept":"Rotation is incomplete without consumer verification","knowledge_type":"LESSON_PATTERN","question":"What should a security lesson capture when a secret is rotated?","answer":"Secret rotation should be treated as a lifecycle change requiring verification that authorized consumers use the new credential, unauthorized use of the old credential is prevented or appropriately bounded, and dependent systems remain within their intended security state.","explanation":"Changing a stored secret does not automatically prove that all consumers changed correctly or that the old credential is unusable. The lesson should capture the complete rotation invariant and evidence required to verify it.","principle":"Credential rotation is complete only when the intended post-rotation security state is established and verified.","rule":"Do not equate changing secret material with successful security remediation.","objective":"Prevent stale credentials, broken consumers, and false closure after secret rotation.","requirements":["Identify consumers.","Define expected old/new credential states.","Verify authorized consumer behavior.","Verify old credential invalidation where required."],"constraints":["Some systems support overlapping validity windows.","Revocation semantics vary by credential mechanism."],"preconditions":["A credential or secret requires replacement or rotation."],"expected_behavior":"Authorized consumers transition to the new credential and the old credential follows its defined revocation or expiry policy.","failure_mode":"Rotation completes administratively while an old credential remains usable or a consumer remains on stale credentials.","cause":"Rotation process verifies configuration change but not security state.","root_cause":"Acceptance criteria cover deployment of new secret material rather than end-to-end credential lifecycle behavior.","solution":"Define and verify post-rotation state across producers, consumers, authorization, revocation, and monitoring.","alternative":"Use short-lived credentials with automated renewal where supported.","tradeoff":"Automation reduces manual errors but increases dependency on lifecycle infrastructure.","edge_case":"A credential may be cached by a long-running process after configuration changes.","security_implication":"Stale credentials can prolong unauthorized access or create availability failures during rotation.","test_case":"Rotate a synthetic credential and test both new and old credential behavior.","test_input":"Old and replacement synthetic credentials across all intended consumers.","expected_test_result":"New credential works only where authorized and old credential follows the documented invalidation policy.","testing_guidance":"Include process restart, cache, retry, and failure-recovery paths.","verification_method":"Perform end-to-end post-rotation checks rather than checking configuration files alone.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence for each critical consumer and the old credential's effective status.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B09-R04","D57-B05-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B22-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Data Minimization","concept":"Security value of data minimization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should data minimization be recorded as a security lesson rather than only a privacy practice?","answer":"Data that is not collected, retained, or propagated cannot be exposed through many compromise paths, so minimizing unnecessary sensitive data can reduce the security impact and attack surface of failures.","explanation":"The lesson concerns reduction of security exposure, not a claim that minimization eliminates risk. The record should identify which unnecessary data element was removed and which security exposure it could otherwise create.","principle":"Avoid creating security exposure for sensitive data that the system does not need.","rule":"Collect, retain, and propagate sensitive data only when justified by a defined requirement.","objective":"Reduce the quantity and distribution of sensitive material available to attackers or compromised components.","requirements":["Classify sensitive data.","Identify business necessity.","Remove unnecessary collection and retention.","Verify dependent functionality remains correct."],"constraints":["Retention may be required for legal, operational, or analytical purposes.","Removing data can affect debugging or recovery."],"preconditions":["The system processes sensitive data beyond what is strictly required for the protected function."],"expected_behavior":"Only necessary sensitive data is collected, retained, and propagated.","failure_mode":"Sensitive information is unnecessarily duplicated or retained across components.","cause":"Data requirements were not minimized during design or later evolution.","root_cause":"Sensitive data quantity and propagation were not treated as explicit security constraints.","solution":"Review data flows and eliminate unnecessary sensitive fields, copies, and retention.","alternative":"Replace sensitive values with references, tokens, or derived non-sensitive representations where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Less retained data can reduce diagnostic or historical capability.","edge_case":"Derived data can remain sensitive even after direct identifiers are removed.","security_implication":"Reduced sensitive-data volume can reduce potential exposure and impact but does not remove access-control requirements.","test_case":"Compare data flows before and after removal of an unnecessary sensitive field.","test_input":"Synthetic sensitive field and equivalent non-sensitive representation.","expected_test_result":"The unnecessary sensitive field is absent from defined collection and downstream flows.","testing_guidance":"Trace storage, logs, messages, caches, backups, and diagnostic paths where relevant.","verification_method":"Perform data-flow review and targeted artifact inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that removal did not merely move the sensitive value into another representation.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B09-R05","D57-B22-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B23-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Use","concept":"Cryptographic control selection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson should be captured when a cryptographic mechanism is selected?","answer":"A cryptographic mechanism should be selected according to the security property, threat model, protocol context, and lifecycle requirements it must satisfy rather than by naming an algorithm alone.","explanation":"Security lessons should distinguish encryption, integrity, authentication, key establishment, password protection, signatures, and other properties. Choosing an algorithm without specifying the required security semantics does not establish that the design is secure.","principle":"Cryptography provides defined security properties only within an appropriate construction and threat model.","rule":"Do not treat the presence of a cryptographic primitive as proof of an appropriate security control.","objective":"Prevent cryptographic misuse caused by matching algorithms to requirements without considering construction and context.","requirements":["Define required security properties.","Define adversary and trust assumptions.","Specify key lifecycle requirements.","Use an appropriate protocol or construction."],"constraints":["Cryptographic choices are version- and library-dependent.","Correct implementation and key management remain necessary."],"preconditions":["A security requirement depends on cryptographic protection."],"expected_behavior":"The selected cryptographic construction provides the required property under documented assumptions.","failure_mode":"An algorithm is used even though its construction or properties do not match the security requirement.","cause":"Algorithm-centric selection without threat-model analysis.","root_cause":"Security requirements were not translated into cryptographic properties and lifecycle controls.","solution":"Map requirements to approved constructions and document assumptions and key handling.","alternative":"Use a maintained high-level cryptographic library or protocol rather than designing primitives directly.","tradeoff":"High-level abstractions may restrict unusual use cases but reduce misuse opportunities.","edge_case":"Encryption without authentication may provide confidentiality while failing integrity requirements.","security_implication":"Incorrect cryptographic construction can produce confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, or key-management failures.","test_case":"Review the security property provided by the selected construction against the stated requirement.","test_input":"Representative protected and tampered data.","expected_test_result":"The construction detects or prevents the security violation it is intended to address.","testing_guidance":"Include misuse and tampering cases, not only successful encryption/decryption.","verification_method":"Security design review plus implementation-specific cryptographic tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify algorithm, mode/construction, parameters, key handling, and library version independently.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B23-R02","D57-B26-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B23-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Keys","concept":"Key lifecycle security","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should cryptographic key lifecycle be part of a security lesson?","answer":"The security of a cryptographic system depends not only on its primitive but also on key generation, storage, access, rotation, revocation, backup, recovery, and destruction requirements.","explanation":"A key lifecycle lesson prevents the common mistake of treating encryption as a self-contained control. The relevant security state exists across the entire period in which a key can authorize or reveal protected information.","principle":"Cryptographic keys are security authorities or secrets whose lifecycle must be governed explicitly.","rule":"Document and verify key lifecycle controls as part of the cryptographic security boundary.","objective":"Reduce compromise risk arising from weak key lifecycle management.","requirements":["Define key ownership.","Define generation and storage controls.","Define rotation and revocation conditions.","Define backup and recovery requirements.","Define destruction or retirement semantics."],"constraints":["Availability requirements can constrain rotation or revocation timing.","Key-management architecture varies by platform and protocol."],"preconditions":["Cryptographic keys protect sensitive data or authorize cryptographic operations."],"expected_behavior":"Keys follow a defined lifecycle with controlled access and documented state transitions.","failure_mode":"Keys remain active, accessible, or recoverable outside their intended lifecycle.","cause":"Lifecycle requirements were omitted or treated as operational detail.","root_cause":"The security model focused on cryptographic operations without modeling key state and authority.","solution":"Represent key lifecycle states and transitions explicitly and verify each security-critical transition.","alternative":"Use a mature key-management service or library when its controls match the requirements.","tradeoff":"Managed systems add dependencies and integration complexity.","edge_case":"Backups can preserve retired keys longer than the active system expects.","security_implication":"Compromised or improperly retained keys can undermine otherwise strong cryptographic controls.","test_case":"Exercise key creation, use, rotation, revocation, recovery, and retirement scenarios in a controlled environment.","test_input":"Synthetic key material and controlled lifecycle events.","expected_test_result":"Each lifecycle transition produces the documented security state.","testing_guidance":"Include backup, restart, rollback, and recovery behavior where relevant.","verification_method":"Review lifecycle state model and test critical transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that retired or revoked keys cannot perform operations that the security model says they must no longer authorize.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B22-R04","D57-B23-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B23-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Verification","concept":"Cryptographic verification before trust","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What lesson applies when data authenticity or integrity depends on cryptographic verification?","answer":"Cryptographic verification must occur before the system treats the protected data or authority as trustworthy, and verification failure must lead to an explicitly defined safe outcome.","explanation":"A cryptographic signature, authentication tag, or equivalent mechanism is only useful if verification is actually enforced on the security decision path. Ignoring or weakening verification errors converts a security control into advisory metadata.","principle":"Security-relevant cryptographic verification is a gate, not an informational hint.","rule":"Never continue a security-sensitive operation as though verification succeeded when verification has failed or has not occurred.","objective":"Prevent unauthenticated or tampered data from crossing a trust boundary.","requirements":["Define the protected authenticity or integrity property.","Perform verification before trust-dependent processing.","Handle failure explicitly.","Test verification bypass attempts."],"constraints":["Verification can have performance and availability implications.","Some systems intentionally support unverified data but must keep it outside trusted paths."],"preconditions":["A security decision depends on cryptographic authenticity or integrity."],"expected_behavior":"Only successfully verified data enters the trusted security path.","failure_mode":"Invalid, missing, or failed verification is treated as success or ignored.","cause":"Verification result is not enforced by control flow.","root_cause":"Security policy does not make verification a mandatory precondition for the protected operation.","solution":"Make verification success an explicit precondition and fail closed for the protected operation.","alternative":"Quarantine unverified data for separate processing.","tradeoff":"Strict rejection can reduce availability when verification infrastructure fails.","edge_case":"A valid signature does not necessarily prove authorization for the requested operation.","security_implication":"Verification bypass can enable tampering, impersonation, or unauthorized data acceptance.","test_case":"Provide valid, invalid, missing, altered, and wrong-key verification inputs.","test_input":"Controlled signed and tampered artifacts.","expected_test_result":"Only artifacts satisfying the required verification policy reach the trusted operation.","testing_guidance":"Test error handling and fallback paths explicitly.","verification_method":"Inspect control flow and run negative verification tests.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that success of cryptographic verification is not conflated with authorization.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R02","D57-B23-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B23-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Failure Handling","concept":"Fail-safe cryptographic failure handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security lesson applies when cryptographic verification or decryption fails?","answer":"Failure handling should preserve the security boundary: failed cryptographic operations must not silently produce trusted output, bypass authorization, or trigger unsafe fallback behavior.","explanation":"Cryptographic failures can be caused by tampering, corruption, wrong keys, version mismatch, or system faults. The lesson should distinguish the technical failure from the security response and record whether the fallback maintains the intended trust state.","principle":"A cryptographic failure must not downgrade a protected operation into an implicitly trusted operation.","rule":"Do not silently bypass cryptographic controls after failure.","objective":"Prevent fallback paths from negating cryptographic protections.","requirements":["Define failure semantics.","Define safe fallback behavior.","Separate availability recovery from trust recovery.","Test failure paths."],"constraints":["Some applications require controlled availability fallback.","Fallback must not silently weaken security guarantees."],"preconditions":["A protected operation can encounter cryptographic failure."],"expected_behavior":"The system rejects, quarantines, or otherwise safely handles failed cryptographic processing according to policy.","failure_mode":"A cryptographic failure causes the system to continue with unverified or improperly protected data.","cause":"Availability-oriented fallback overrides the security invariant.","root_cause":"Failure semantics were not included in the security design.","solution":"Define explicit security-preserving failure states and test every fallback path.","alternative":"Use a separate degraded mode with reduced functionality but unchanged trust assumptions.","tradeoff":"Secure failure handling can reduce availability during key or cryptographic infrastructure failures.","edge_case":"Operational recovery may require access to encrypted backups while the normal key service is unavailable.","security_implication":"Unsafe fallback can turn transient cryptographic failures into persistent security bypasses.","test_case":"Inject cryptographic failures at each security-sensitive operation and inspect resulting control flow.","test_input":"Invalid ciphertext, invalid tag/signature, unavailable key, and wrong-key conditions.","expected_test_result":"No failed cryptographic operation produces trusted protected output.","testing_guidance":"Include timeout and dependency-unavailable conditions, not just invalid data.","verification_method":"Failure-path review plus controlled fault-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every fallback has an explicit security state and authorization requirement.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B23-R03","D57-B28-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B23-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Migration","concept":"Cryptographic migration verification","knowledge_type":"LESSON_PATTERN","question":"What should a security lesson capture when cryptographic algorithms or libraries are migrated?","answer":"A cryptographic migration should verify security properties, compatibility boundaries, key handling, persisted data behavior, and retirement of the old mechanism rather than verifying only that the application still runs.","explanation":"Cryptographic migration can change semantics, defaults, serialization, supported algorithms, or key compatibility. The lesson must therefore distinguish functional compatibility from preservation of the intended security properties.","principle":"Cryptographic migration is a security-state transition, not merely a dependency upgrade.","rule":"Verify security invariants across both the new implementation and the retirement of the old one.","objective":"Prevent migration changes from silently weakening or fragmenting cryptographic protection.","requirements":["Record old and new cryptographic assumptions.","Test protected-data compatibility where required.","Verify new security properties.","Define retirement of obsolete mechanisms."],"constraints":["Historical data may require legacy compatibility.","Legacy support can prolong exposure to weaker mechanisms."],"preconditions":["A cryptographic primitive, library, protocol, or configuration is being changed."],"expected_behavior":"The migrated system preserves required security properties and has an explicit policy for legacy data and mechanisms.","failure_mode":"Migration succeeds functionally while security properties, key handling, or legacy retirement remain incorrect.","cause":"Migration acceptance criteria focus on application functionality.","root_cause":"Cryptographic security invariants were not included in migration verification.","solution":"Create security-specific migration criteria covering properties, keys, persisted data, and retirement.","alternative":"Perform staged migration with explicit compatibility and deprecation phases.","tradeoff":"Staged migration can increase temporary complexity and dual-support exposure.","edge_case":"A compatibility mode can unintentionally keep a deprecated mechanism available indefinitely.","security_implication":"Incomplete migration can leave weaker cryptographic paths available to attackers.","test_case":"Exercise both migrated and legacy paths according to the documented transition policy.","test_input":"Representative protected data, keys, and protocol versions.","expected_test_result":"Only policy-approved mechanisms remain usable and required security properties hold.","testing_guidance":"Test downgrade and legacy-path activation explicitly.","verification_method":"Security-focused migration review and regression testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify configuration defaults, supported versions, key compatibility, and retirement conditions.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B23-R01","D57-B23-R02","D57-B29-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B24-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Boundaries","concept":"Authorization on indirect resource references","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What lesson applies when a user can select a resource indirectly through an identifier supplied to an operation?","answer":"Authorization must be evaluated against the resolved resource and requested action, not merely against possession of an identifier or successful resolution.","explanation":"An identifier can be syntactically valid and resolve to a real object while still referring to a resource outside the caller's authority. The reusable lesson is that object resolution and authorization are separate security steps.","principle":"Existence or resolvability of a resource does not establish authority over it.","rule":"Authorize the actual resolved resource and action before performing the protected operation.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized access through direct or indirect resource references.","requirements":["Resolve the requested resource safely.","Evaluate caller authority against the resolved resource.","Enforce authorization before sensitive action.","Test cross-boundary references."],"constraints":["Resource ownership and authorization can change dynamically.","Caching authorization decisions can create stale authority if not carefully bounded."],"preconditions":["A request selects a protected resource using an identifier or indirect reference."],"expected_behavior":"The operation proceeds only when the caller has current authority over the resolved resource and requested action.","failure_mode":"A caller accesses a resource because its identifier is valid even though the caller lacks authority.","cause":"Authorization is performed on identifier format or endpoint access instead of resource-level authority.","root_cause":"Resource resolution and authorization are incorrectly conflated.","solution":"Make resource-level authorization an explicit precondition of the protected operation.","alternative":"Expose only opaque references that still require server-side authorization.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained authorization can add policy complexity.","edge_case":"A resource can move between tenants or ownership domains while an identifier remains unchanged.","security_implication":"Incorrect resource authorization can create cross-tenant or horizontal privilege violations.","test_case":"Request valid identifiers for both authorized and unauthorized resources.","test_input":"Caller identity plus resource identifiers spanning multiple authorization domains.","expected_test_result":"Authorized resource access succeeds and unauthorized resource access is denied.","testing_guidance":"Include enumeration, alternate identifier forms, and stale ownership cases.","verification_method":"Inspect authorization placement and execute cross-resource access tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify authorization occurs after safe resource resolution but before protected data exposure or mutation.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R01","D57-B24-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B24-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Boundaries","concept":"Authorization on mutation operations","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security lessons distinguish read authorization from mutation authorization?","answer":"Permission to observe a resource does not inherently imply permission to modify, delete, execute, transfer, or otherwise change it.","explanation":"Authorization is action-specific. A reusable security lesson should capture the protected action and the exact authority required rather than documenting only that the user can access the resource.","principle":"Authorization should evaluate the requested action as well as the target resource and principal.","rule":"Do not infer mutation authority from read or discovery authority unless policy explicitly defines that relationship.","objective":"Prevent privilege expansion through action conflation.","requirements":["Identify resource.","Identify requested action.","Identify required authority.","Enforce action-specific policy."],"constraints":["Some policies intentionally bundle multiple actions.","Policy inheritance must be explicit."],"preconditions":["A protected resource supports multiple operations with different security impact."],"expected_behavior":"Each security-sensitive action is allowed only when the caller satisfies its explicit authorization policy.","failure_mode":"A caller with lower-risk access can invoke a higher-risk operation.","cause":"Authorization policy is resource-centric but not action-specific.","root_cause":"Security requirements do not model action granularity.","solution":"Represent authorization decisions as principal-resource-action relationships.","alternative":"Use role or capability models that explicitly enumerate permitted operations.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained permissions increase policy-management complexity.","edge_case":"A seemingly low-impact update may trigger a high-impact downstream action.","security_implication":"Action conflation can produce privilege escalation or unauthorized state changes.","test_case":"Exercise every protected action with principals having different permission sets.","test_input":"Read-only, update, delete, execute, and administrative authorization contexts.","expected_test_result":"Each operation follows its independently defined authorization policy.","testing_guidance":"Test indirect actions and side effects, not only endpoint names.","verification_method":"Review authorization matrix and run action-level negative tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that downstream side effects do not inherit broader authority accidentally.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R01","D57-B24-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B24-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Time-of-Check","concept":"Authorization freshness","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security lesson applies when authority can change between authorization and execution?","answer":"Where authorization can change during an operation, the security design must define whether the decision remains valid and must prevent stale authorization from silently authorizing a later sensitive action.","explanation":"An authorization check is evidence about authority at a particular point in time. If the protected operation occurs later or through another component, the lesson should capture the required freshness or transaction boundary.","principle":"Authorization validity is temporal when authority can change during the operation lifecycle.","rule":"Do not assume an earlier authorization decision remains valid after a security-relevant authority change unless the security model explicitly guarantees that validity.","objective":"Prevent stale authorization from enabling actions after privilege revocation or context changes.","requirements":["Define authorization decision lifetime.","Identify authority-changing events.","Recheck or bind authorization where required.","Test revocation races."],"constraints":["Rechecking can add latency and consistency requirements.","Some atomic operations naturally bind authorization and execution."],"preconditions":["Authorization state can change between policy evaluation and protected execution."],"expected_behavior":"The protected action is executed only under authority valid according to the defined transaction semantics.","failure_mode":"A revoked or changed authority remains effective for a later sensitive action without explicit policy justification.","cause":"Authorization decision is cached or detached from the protected operation.","root_cause":"Temporal validity of authorization was not modeled.","solution":"Bind authorization to the protected transaction or define and enforce an explicit decision lifetime.","alternative":"Use atomic policy-and-action enforcement where supported.","tradeoff":"Stronger freshness can increase coordination and system complexity.","edge_case":"Distributed systems may observe revocation at different times.","security_implication":"Stale authority can enable actions after privilege removal or security-context changes.","test_case":"Revoke or change authority between decision and execution.","test_input":"Controlled authorization state transition during a protected operation.","expected_test_result":"The system follows the documented freshness policy and does not exceed authorized temporal scope.","testing_guidance":"Include concurrency and distributed propagation delays where applicable.","verification_method":"Review authorization caching and transaction boundaries; execute controlled race tests.","verification_guidance":"Record the actual authority state used by the protected operation.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R03","D57-B24-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B24-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Delegation","concept":"Delegated authority scope","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What lesson should be captured when one component delegates authority to another?","answer":"Delegation should explicitly constrain the delegated principal, resources, actions, duration, and conditions rather than transferring the delegator's entire authority by default.","explanation":"Delegation changes the authority graph. A reusable lesson should identify the exact subset of authority intended to cross the boundary and ensure downstream actions cannot exceed it.","principle":"Delegated authority should be no broader than the authority required for the delegated task.","rule":"Do not propagate ambient or full authority when a narrower delegation can satisfy the operation.","objective":"Limit privilege expansion through service-to-service or component-to-component delegation.","requirements":["Define delegated scope.","Define duration or expiration where applicable.","Bind delegation to intended resources and actions.","Prevent unauthorized further delegation."],"constraints":["Some workflows require chained delegation.","Delegation metadata must remain trustworthy across boundaries."],"preconditions":["A principal or service acts using authority derived from another principal or service."],"expected_behavior":"The delegate can perform only the explicitly permitted operations within the delegated scope.","failure_mode":"Delegated component can exercise authority beyond the intended task.","cause":"Delegation passes broader credentials or authority than required.","root_cause":"Delegation was modeled as identity transfer rather than constrained authority transfer.","solution":"Use scoped delegation and explicitly enforce resource, action, and temporal limits.","alternative":"Use narrowly scoped capabilities or task-specific credentials.","tradeoff":"Narrow delegation may require more tokens, policy objects, or lifecycle management.","edge_case":"A delegated component may itself call another component and accidentally propagate broader authority.","security_implication":"Over-broad delegation can enable privilege escalation and confused-deputy behavior.","test_case":"Attempt actions both inside and outside the delegated scope.","test_input":"Delegated principal with intentionally narrow authority.","expected_test_result":"Only actions satisfying delegated constraints succeed.","testing_guidance":"Test chained delegation and attempted scope expansion.","verification_method":"Inspect authority propagation and execute scope-boundary tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that downstream components receive only the authority required for the task.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R04","D57-B24-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B24-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Auditability","concept":"Authorization decision explainability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What security lesson applies when authorization decisions cannot be reconstructed later?","answer":"Security-sensitive authorization should produce sufficient structured evidence to reconstruct which principal, resource, action, policy context, and decision state were involved, subject to data-minimization and confidentiality requirements.","explanation":"Auditability is not the same as logging every detail. The lesson should identify the minimum evidence needed to establish why a security decision was allowed or denied without creating a new sensitive-data exposure.","principle":"Security decisions should be sufficiently observable to support verification and investigation without excessive sensitive-data retention.","rule":"Record security-decision evidence at a level sufficient to reconstruct the decision under the applicable audit requirements.","objective":"Enable verification and investigation of authorization behavior.","requirements":["Identify decision subject.","Identify protected resource and action.","Record policy or decision context as appropriate.","Protect audit evidence itself."],"constraints":["Audit records may contain sensitive information.","Retention requirements vary by system and jurisdiction."],"preconditions":["Authorization decisions have material security impact."],"expected_behavior":"Authorized reviewers can reconstruct security-relevant decision context from protected evidence.","failure_mode":"A disputed authorization decision cannot be explained or verified because critical decision context was discarded.","cause":"Audit design focuses on event occurrence rather than decision reconstruction.","root_cause":"Authorization observability requirements were not modeled as a security property.","solution":"Define minimal decision evidence and protect it with appropriate access and retention controls.","alternative":"Use immutable or append-oriented audit infrastructure when justified.","tradeoff":"More detailed audit evidence increases storage and confidentiality requirements.","edge_case":"Logging the complete request can itself expose sensitive data.","security_implication":"Insufficient auditability can delay detection and make security remediation less reliable; excessive audit detail can create additional exposure.","test_case":"Perform an authorization decision and verify that the required decision evidence is reconstructable.","test_input":"Representative authorized and denied actions.","expected_test_result":"Required decision context is available without exposing unnecessary sensitive values.","testing_guidance":"Test both audit completeness and audit confidentiality.","verification_method":"Audit-schema review plus controlled decision reconstruction test.","verification_guidance":"Verify that audit evidence itself has access controls and integrity protections.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R05","D57-B25-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B25-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit Integrity","concept":"Audit evidence integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when audit records themselves can be modified without detection?","answer":"Security audit evidence requires integrity protection appropriate to its threat model; otherwise the record cannot reliably support later verification or investigation.","explanation":"Auditability depends on trust in the evidence. A lesson should therefore capture not only what is logged but also who or what can alter, delete, reorder, or suppress the records and how such changes are detected.","principle":"Security evidence is useful only to the extent that its integrity and provenance are trustworthy.","rule":"Protect security-critical audit evidence against unauthorized modification and deletion within its stated threat model.","objective":"Preserve trustworthy evidence for security verification and investigation.","requirements":["Define audit integrity requirements.","Restrict modification and deletion.","Protect ordering or sequence where material.","Monitor integrity failures."],"constraints":["No audit mechanism provides absolute tamper resistance under every threat model.","Availability and retention constraints affect architecture."],"preconditions":["Audit records are relied upon for security decisions, investigation, or compliance evidence."],"expected_behavior":"Unauthorized audit modification is prevented or detectable within the defined security model.","failure_mode":"Audit evidence can be altered without reliable detection.","cause":"Audit storage is treated as ordinary application data.","root_cause":"Evidence integrity requirements were not modeled separately from event generation.","solution":"Apply explicit integrity, access, retention, and monitoring controls to security evidence.","alternative":"Use append-oriented or independently protected evidence storage where justified.","tradeoff":"Independent protection increases architecture and operational complexity.","edge_case":"An attacker with control over the application may attempt to suppress events before they reach the audit store.","security_implication":"Compromised audit evidence can conceal security events and undermine incident reconstruction.","test_case":"Attempt unauthorized modification or deletion of controlled audit records.","test_input":"Synthetic audit events and controlled unauthorized write operations.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized changes are blocked or detected according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Test both stored-record tampering and event-suppression scenarios.","verification_method":"Review audit trust boundary and execute controlled integrity tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the audit mechanism's trust assumptions are explicitly documented.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B25-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B25-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit Completeness","concept":"Security event coverage","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a security lesson evaluate whether audit coverage is complete enough?","answer":"Audit coverage should be defined against security-relevant state transitions and decisions rather than against an arbitrary percentage of application events.","explanation":"A system can log many events while omitting the small number of transitions that matter most for security, such as authority changes or policy decisions. Coverage therefore requires a threat- and control-driven inventory.","principle":"Audit completeness is measured against security-relevant events and state transitions, not raw event volume.","rule":"Define auditable security events from the threat model and control requirements.","objective":"Ensure that critical security activity produces sufficient evidence.","requirements":["Inventory security-sensitive actions and state changes.","Map controls to required evidence.","Identify intentionally unaudited events and rationale.","Test critical event generation."],"constraints":["Logging everything can create unnecessary sensitive-data exposure and operational burden.","Some events are inherently difficult to observe."],"preconditions":["The system relies on audit evidence for security verification or investigation."],"expected_behavior":"Critical security decisions and state transitions generate the evidence required by the defined audit model.","failure_mode":"Large event volume masks missing evidence for critical security transitions.","cause":"Audit design is based on generic application logging rather than security requirements.","root_cause":"No security-specific audit coverage model exists.","solution":"Create a control-driven audit inventory and test it against protected operations.","alternative":"Use event schemas tied directly to security-control identifiers.","tradeoff":"Structured audit design requires upfront modeling and maintenance.","edge_case":"A security event may occur through multiple technical paths that produce different event shapes.","security_implication":"Missing critical events can prevent detection, reconstruction, and verification.","test_case":"Trigger each critical security event through each supported path.","test_input":"Authorized, denied, changed, revoked, and failed security transitions.","expected_test_result":"Required evidence is generated consistently across applicable paths.","testing_guidance":"Test alternate interfaces and asynchronous paths.","verification_method":"Map security controls to event coverage and execute representative event tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every critical security transition has a defined evidence source.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B25-R01","D57-B25-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B25-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit Failure","concept":"Audit pipeline failure as a security state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What lesson applies when the security audit pipeline itself fails?","answer":"Audit pipeline failure should be represented as a distinct security-relevant state with explicit handling because absence of evidence is not equivalent to evidence that no security event occurred.","explanation":"A failed audit sink, dropped event, inaccessible storage, or serialization failure can create an evidence gap. The lesson should avoid treating successful application execution as proof that security evidence was preserved.","principle":"Security observability has its own failure modes and must not be assumed healthy.","rule":"Do not silently treat audit-delivery failure as successful audit completion.","objective":"Prevent false assurance caused by missing security evidence.","requirements":["Detect audit delivery failures.","Define criticality of audit events.","Define fail-open or fail-closed behavior explicitly.","Preserve evidence gaps for later review."],"constraints":["Blocking on audit failure can affect availability.","Not all audit events require identical failure handling."],"preconditions":["Security-relevant events depend on an audit pipeline or external evidence sink."],"expected_behavior":"Audit failures are detected, represented, and handled according to explicit security policy.","failure_mode":"Application continues while security evidence is silently lost.","cause":"Audit pipeline health is not included in security state.","root_cause":"Observability availability was separated from security assurance without defining the dependency.","solution":"Model audit delivery as a security dependency and define event-specific failure policy.","alternative":"Buffer critical events locally until durable delivery is confirmed, subject to secure retention limits.","tradeoff":"Buffering introduces storage, replay, and confidentiality considerations.","edge_case":"A local buffer may itself become unavailable or compromised.","security_implication":"Evidence gaps can impair detection, verification, and incident reconstruction.","test_case":"Simulate audit sink unavailability and inspect application and evidence behavior.","test_input":"Controlled audit destination failure and recovery.","expected_test_result":"Failure is detected and the documented security policy is followed without silent evidence loss.","testing_guidance":"Test partial delivery, retry, buffering, restart, and recovery paths.","verification_method":"Fault-injection test of the audit pipeline plus state review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that missing evidence is explicitly distinguishable from absence of the underlying event.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B25-R01","D57-B25-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B25-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit Privacy","concept":"Audit-data minimization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What security lesson applies when audit records contain more sensitive data than necessary?","answer":"Audit evidence should preserve the security-relevant facts needed for reconstruction while minimizing unnecessary sensitive payloads.","explanation":"Audit systems can become secondary data stores containing credentials, personal data, request bodies, or internal secrets. The lesson is to preserve decision evidence without creating avoidable exposure.","principle":"Auditability and data minimization must be designed together.","rule":"Record the minimum sensitive information necessary to support the defined security evidence requirement.","objective":"Maintain audit usefulness without creating an unnecessary sensitive-data repository.","requirements":["Define audit purpose.","Identify minimum required fields.","Classify audit data.","Apply access and retention controls."],"constraints":["Some investigations require contextual evidence.","Redaction can remove information needed for forensic reconstruction."],"preconditions":["Security audit records contain potentially sensitive request or system context."],"expected_behavior":"Audit records contain sufficient security evidence while excluding unnecessary sensitive payloads.","failure_mode":"Audit records reproduce complete sensitive payloads even though only metadata was required.","cause":"Audit design prioritizes diagnostic completeness without data classification.","root_cause":"Audit evidence requirements lack a minimum-data specification.","solution":"Define security evidence fields separately from raw payload capture.","alternative":"Use references, hashes, structured identifiers, or controlled secure artifacts where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Reduced payload detail can make some investigations harder.","edge_case":"A supposedly non-sensitive field can become sensitive when combined with other fields.","security_implication":"Excessive audit data increases confidentiality impact if the audit repository is compromised.","test_case":"Review generated audit records for unnecessary sensitive fields.","test_input":"Synthetic requests containing sensitive and non-sensitive data.","expected_test_result":"Required security evidence remains while unnecessary sensitive payloads are absent or protected.","testing_guidance":"Test normal, denied, failed, and exception audit events.","verification_method":"Schema review and automated sensitive-field detection where available.","verification_guidance":"Verify both evidence sufficiency and minimization.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B22-R01","D57-B25-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B25-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit Retention","concept":"Retention-policy alignment","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What should a security lesson capture about retaining security evidence?","answer":"Security evidence should be retained for a defined purpose and period consistent with investigation, assurance, legal, and confidentiality requirements; indefinite retention should not be assumed to improve security.","explanation":"Retention has competing security properties: sufficient history supports reconstruction, while excessive retention increases exposure and management burden. The lesson should preserve the rationale and deletion or archival behavior.","principle":"Security evidence needs an explicit lifecycle just like other sensitive data.","rule":"Define retention, archival, access, and disposal requirements for security evidence.","objective":"Balance investigative usefulness with unnecessary long-term exposure.","requirements":["Define retention purpose.","Define retention period or trigger.","Protect archived evidence.","Define secure disposal or expiration."],"constraints":["Legal and regulatory requirements may constrain disposal.","Some evidence must remain immutable during an investigation or hold."],"preconditions":["Security evidence is stored beyond the immediate operation.","expected_behavior":"Evidence follows a documented lifecycle and remains protected throughout its required retention period.","failure_mode":"Evidence is deleted before it is useful or retained indefinitely without security justification.","cause":"Retention is treated as a storage setting rather than a security lifecycle.","root_cause":"Evidence lifecycle requirements are not explicitly modeled.","solution":"Define retention policy from investigation and security requirements and review it periodically.","alternative":"Use policy-driven archival with separate access controls.","tradeoff":"Longer retention improves historical visibility but increases exposure and storage requirements.","edge_case":"A legal or investigative hold can temporarily override normal deletion rules.","security_implication":"Poor retention can either destroy critical evidence or create unnecessary sensitive-data exposure.","test_case":"Verify retention behavior under normal expiration and controlled hold conditions.","test_input":"Synthetic evidence records with defined lifecycle metadata.","expected_test_result":"Records follow documented retention and disposal behavior while protected holds are respected.","testing_guidance":"Test expiration, archival, restoration, access, and disposal paths where applicable.","verification_method":"Review retention policy and execute lifecycle tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that retention exceptions are explicit and auditable.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B25-R01","D57-B25-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B26-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency Security","concept":"Dependency provenance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when software dependencies are introduced without trustworthy provenance?","answer":"Dependency selection should establish what artifact is being consumed, where it came from, which version is intended, and what integrity and trust assumptions apply before it becomes part of the security boundary.","explanation":"A package name alone does not establish artifact identity or provenance. The lesson should distinguish declared dependency intent from the actual artifact consumed.","principle":"Security-sensitive dependencies require trustworthy identity and provenance information.","rule":"Do not treat a dependency declaration as sufficient evidence of artifact integrity or provenance.","objective":"Reduce supply-chain uncertainty around third-party and transitive components.","requirements":["Identify exact dependency version or constraint.","Record source/provenance where supported.","Verify artifact integrity according to the ecosystem.","Track transitive dependencies."],"constraints":["Dependency ecosystems provide different provenance and verification capabilities.","Reproducibility can be limited by external infrastructure."],"preconditions":["The application depends on external or internally distributed software artifacts."],"expected_behavior":"The build consumes artifacts consistent with documented dependency and provenance requirements.","failure_mode":"An unintended, substituted, compromised, or ambiguous dependency artifact enters the build.","cause":"Dependency identity and provenance are not independently verified.","root_cause":"Supply-chain trust assumptions are implicit rather than represented in the build process.","solution":"Record exact dependency identity and apply available integrity/provenance controls.","alternative":"Use an internally controlled artifact repository or reproducible build process where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Stronger provenance controls increase build and repository management requirements.","edge_case":"A direct dependency may pull a changed or compromised transitive dependency.","security_implication":"A compromised dependency can introduce vulnerabilities or malicious behavior into trusted application code.","test_case":"Compare resolved dependency graph and artifact metadata against the intended dependency specification.","test_input":"Pinned dependency graph and controlled artifact metadata.","expected_test_result":"Resolved artifacts match documented identity and integrity requirements.","testing_guidance":"Include transitive dependencies and clean-environment resolution.","verification_method":"Inspect lock/resolution metadata and artifact verification results.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim provenance verification when the ecosystem or available evidence does not provide it.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B26-R02","D57-B29-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B26-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency Security","concept":"Transitive dependency visibility","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security lessons include transitive dependencies?","answer":"A component can inherit security behavior from dependencies that were not directly selected, so security review limited to direct dependencies can miss material parts of the executable dependency graph.","explanation":"The lesson is about graph completeness rather than requiring every dependency to receive identical treatment. The security-relevant question is whether all components that enter the trusted execution environment are visible and governed appropriately.","principle":"The effective software supply chain is the resolved dependency graph, not only the direct declarations.","rule":"Evaluate security-relevant transitive dependencies according to the project's dependency policy.","objective":"Prevent hidden dependency paths from escaping security review.","requirements":["Resolve dependency graph.","Identify transitive components.","Track versions and relevant metadata.","Define treatment of unreviewed or unexpected dependencies."],"constraints":["Some ecosystems resolve dependencies dynamically.","Optional and platform-specific dependencies may differ by environment."],"preconditions":["The project uses dependencies that can introduce additional components transitively."],"expected_behavior":"The security inventory reflects the dependency set actually used by the target build or environment.","failure_mode":"A transitive component enters production without being represented in security inventory or policy.","cause":"Dependency review stops at direct declarations.","root_cause":"Security inventory is modeled as a flat declaration list rather than a resolved graph.","solution":"Generate and review the resolved dependency graph for supported environments.","alternative":"Use automated dependency graph and policy tooling.","tradeoff":"Graph management increases metadata and policy complexity.","edge_case":"Platform-specific resolution can produce different graphs from the same top-level declaration.","security_implication":"Unknown transitive dependencies can introduce unreviewed vulnerabilities or malicious functionality.","test_case":"Resolve dependencies in a clean environment and compare the graph with the security inventory.","test_input":"Declared dependency set plus target platform/environment.","expected_test_result":"All resolved security-relevant components are represented or explicitly exempted.","testing_guidance":"Run for each materially different supported environment.","verification_method":"Dependency graph comparison and policy validation.","verification_guidance":"Record the environment and resolution inputs used to produce the graph.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B26-R01","D57-B26-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B26-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency Updates","concept":"Security update verification","knowledge_type":"LESSON_PATTERN","question":"What lesson should be captured when a dependency is updated for a security reason?","answer":"A security dependency update should verify the intended artifact and version, confirm that the vulnerable path is no longer present or is appropriately mitigated, and test for regressions in security-sensitive behavior.","explanation":"Updating a version is an implementation action, not evidence that the vulnerability condition has been eliminated. The lesson should connect the dependency change to the actual security property being restored.","principle":"Security remediation is complete when the relevant vulnerable condition is addressed and verified, not merely when a version number changes.","rule":"Link dependency security updates to explicit acceptance criteria and verification evidence.","objective":"Prevent false closure after dependency upgrades.","requirements":["Identify security-relevant dependency condition.","Verify intended version/artifact.","Verify affected behavior or exposure condition.","Run security regression tests."],"constraints":["A dependency may contain multiple security-relevant components or optional features.","Version updates can introduce unrelated compatibility changes."],"preconditions":["A dependency update is intended to remediate a security concern."],"expected_behavior":"The dependency update produces the intended security state and passes relevant regression checks.","failure_mode":"Version is changed but the vulnerable path remains reachable or the wrong artifact is consumed.","cause":"Remediation verifies declaration rather than security effect.","root_cause":"Acceptance criteria are version-centric instead of security-property-centric.","solution":"Define security-specific verification criteria tied to the vulnerability mechanism.","alternative":"Use a supported backport or compensating control when an immediate full update is not feasible.","tradeoff":"Temporary compensating controls may increase complexity and residual risk.","edge_case":"A package update may fix one vulnerable path while another affected component remains exposed.","security_implication":"False remediation closure can leave a known security condition active.","test_case":"Reproduce the security-relevant condition in a controlled test environment before and after the update where feasible.","test_input":"Controlled vulnerable-path input and updated dependency graph.","expected_test_result":"The affected security condition is no longer reproduced under the defined test.","testing_guidance":"Do not claim universal vulnerability elimination solely from version comparison.","verification_method":"Combine artifact verification with targeted security regression testing.","verification_guidance":"Record exact dependency resolution and test evidence.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R05","D57-B26-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B26-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency Configuration","concept":"Secure dependency defaults","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should security lessons inspect dependency configuration and defaults separately from dependency version?","answer":"A secure library version can still be used insecurely through unsafe configuration, disabled protections, permissive modes, or incompatible defaults.","explanation":"Security posture is determined by the combination of dependency behavior and application configuration. A lesson should therefore preserve configuration assumptions and verify that required protections are actually enabled.","principle":"Dependency security depends on both artifact behavior and its configured security-relevant semantics.","rule":"Verify security-critical dependency configuration explicitly rather than assuming safe defaults.","objective":"Prevent configuration misuse from negating dependency security controls.","requirements":["Identify security-sensitive settings.","Document required values or modes.","Test both intended and unsafe configurations where practical.","Track version-dependent default changes."],"constraints":["Defaults can change between versions.","Configuration may be environment-specific."],"preconditions":["A dependency exposes security-relevant configuration options."],"expected_behavior":"Required security protections are explicitly configured or verified to be enabled according to the supported version.","failure_mode":"A secure component operates in a permissive or unsafe configuration.","cause":"Configuration assumptions are undocumented or implicit.","root_cause":"Dependency security review considers the artifact but not its runtime configuration.","solution":"Treat security-relevant dependency settings as explicit security requirements.","alternative":"Use configuration validation that rejects unsupported security modes.","tradeoff":"Strict configuration validation can reduce compatibility with legacy deployments.","edge_case":"A version upgrade can change a default and silently alter the effective security posture.","security_implication":"Misconfiguration can disable or weaken protections without changing application source code.","test_case":"Inspect effective runtime configuration for security-sensitive dependency settings.","test_input":"Supported deployment configurations and intentionally unsafe settings.","expected_test_result":"Unsafe security configurations are rejected or clearly detected according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Test configuration loading, environment overrides, and version upgrades.","verification_method":"Configuration review plus runtime inspection where possible.","verification_guidance":"Record effective configuration separately from intended configuration.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B23-R05","D57-B26-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B26-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency Removal","concept":"Removing unnecessary security-sensitive dependencies","knowledge_type":"LESSON_PATTERN","question":"What security lesson follows when a dependency is no longer required?","answer":"Removing an unnecessary dependency can reduce attack surface, but the lesson should verify that no required behavior, indirect dependency, build path, or security control still relies on it.","explanation":"Dependency removal is not automatically a security improvement if the component is still needed through another path or if removal causes a security control to disappear. The security lesson therefore requires dependency-graph and behavior verification.","principle":"Reduce unnecessary trusted components only after verifying the security properties they provide or influence.","rule":"Do not remove a security-relevant dependency solely because it appears unused at the source level.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary attack surface without silently removing required security behavior.","requirements":["Identify dependency usage.","Check transitive relationships.","Identify security functionality provided by the dependency.","Verify build and runtime behavior after removal."],"constraints":["Static source analysis may miss dynamic loading.","A dependency may provide security behavior indirectly."],"preconditions":["A dependency is a candidate for removal and may influence security posture."],"expected_behavior":"The removed component is not required by supported execution paths and no intended security control is lost.","failure_mode":"Removal eliminates hidden security behavior or causes a fallback to a less secure implementation.","cause":"Removal decision relies only on direct source references.","root_cause":"Dependency role is not understood at the architecture and runtime levels.","solution":"Trace dependency relationships and security responsibilities before removal.","alternative":"Keep the dependency but constrain or update it when removal is unsafe.","tradeoff":"Keeping an unnecessary component preserves attack surface; removing it may increase implementation burden.","edge_case":"A build plugin, native library, or runtime loader can introduce dependency usage outside obvious source imports.","security_implication":"Unnecessary dependencies increase attack surface, while unsafe removal can weaken controls.","test_case":"Remove the candidate dependency in a controlled branch and verify all required security behavior.","test_input":"Clean build and supported runtime scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Required security controls and application behavior remain intact without the removed dependency.","testing_guidance":"Include packaging, startup, plugin-loading, and platform-specific paths where applicable.","verification_method":"Dependency graph review plus build/runtime security regression testing.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that removal did not silently activate an alternative implementation with weaker security properties.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B26-R02","D57-B29-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B27-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Error Handling","concept":"Security-safe error semantics","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when error responses reveal more information than the caller needs?","answer":"Error handling should disclose enough information for legitimate behavior and debugging while avoiding unnecessary security-sensitive internal details to untrusted callers.","explanation":"Different audiences need different error detail. A reusable lesson should distinguish internal diagnostic evidence from externally visible response semantics rather than simply recommending generic error suppression.","principle":"Error information should be appropriate to the trust level of its recipient.","rule":"Do not expose internal security-sensitive state merely because it is available to the error handler.","objective":"Reduce information disclosure through externally visible error behavior.","requirements":["Classify error information.","Define caller-visible response contract.","Keep sensitive diagnostics in protected channels.","Test error paths."],"constraints":["Overly generic errors can harm usability or legitimate debugging.","Internal diagnostics still require confidentiality controls."],"preconditions":["An application returns or emits error information across trust boundaries."],"expected_behavior":"Untrusted callers receive bounded error information while protected diagnostic channels retain necessary evidence.","failure_mode":"External error output reveals secrets, internal topology, authorization state, or implementation details unnecessarily.","cause":"Internal diagnostic content is reused as external response content.","root_cause":"Error audiences and security disclosure requirements are not separated.","solution":"Define distinct external error contracts and internal diagnostic records.","alternative":"Use stable error codes with protected diagnostic correlation identifiers.","tradeoff":"Stable codes require supporting documentation and correlation mechanisms.","edge_case":"Differences in timing or response status can still reveal information even when message text is generic.","security_implication":"Error disclosure can aid reconnaissance, data extraction, or authorization probing.","test_case":"Trigger representative error conditions from authorized and unauthorized callers.","test_input":"Invalid identifiers, denied operations, malformed inputs, and internal failure simulations.","expected_test_result":"External responses stay within the defined disclosure boundary while internal evidence remains protected.","testing_guidance":"Test message, status, headers, structured fields, and observable side channels where relevant.","verification_method":"Error-contract review plus negative disclosure tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that internal exception details cannot escape through alternate response paths.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B22-R01","D57-B27-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B27-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Failure Handling","concept":"Fail-closed versus fail-safe semantics","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should a security lesson decide whether a failure must deny an operation or permit degraded behavior?","answer":"The lesson should derive failure behavior from the security invariant and availability requirement rather than applying a universal 'fail closed' rule to every component.","explanation":"Some security decisions must deny access when their enforcement dependency fails, while other systems can safely enter a restricted degraded mode. The critical distinction is whether the fallback preserves the required security property.","principle":"Failure behavior should preserve the security invariant appropriate to the operation.","rule":"Define explicit security semantics for dependency and control failures.","objective":"Prevent unsafe or unnecessarily unavailable failure handling.","requirements":["Identify security invariant.","Identify failure condition.","Define safe degraded state if one exists.","Test both failure and recovery."],"constraints":["Fail-closed can create availability impact.","Fail-open can violate security properties."],"preconditions":["A security control depends on a component that can fail or become unavailable."],"expected_behavior":"The system enters the policy-defined security state when the control dependency fails.","failure_mode":"Failure behavior either bypasses protection or causes unnecessary broad denial because semantics were never defined.","cause":"Failure policy is chosen as a generic pattern rather than derived from security requirements.","root_cause":"The security state machine does not model dependency failure explicitly.","solution":"Define per-control failure semantics and test them under controlled fault conditions.","alternative":"Use a restricted degraded mode that preserves critical security properties.","tradeoff":"Degraded modes add complexity and must be carefully bounded.","edge_case":"Different operations using the same dependency may require different failure semantics.","security_implication":"Incorrect failure semantics can produce either unauthorized access or avoidable denial of legitimate operations.","test_case":"Disable the security dependency and execute protected and non-protected operations.","test_input":"Controlled dependency failure.","expected_test_result":"Each operation follows its explicitly documented security failure policy.","testing_guidance":"Test partial dependency failure, timeout, stale state, and recovery.","verification_method":"Security state-machine review plus fault-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that fallback behavior is not broader than the documented security policy.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B23-R04","D57-B25-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B27-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Exception Handling","concept":"Exception boundary and trust preservation","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What security lesson applies when exceptions cross trust boundaries without controlled translation?","answer":"Exceptions crossing a trust boundary should be converted into a bounded, policy-defined representation so internal implementation details do not become externally controllable security behavior.","explanation":"Raw exception propagation can expose information and can also couple external behavior to internal failure states. The lesson should capture both information disclosure and control-flow consequences.","principle":"Trust boundaries should define explicit error semantics independent of internal exception structure.","rule":"Do not expose raw internal exceptions as a security-sensitive external contract.","objective":"Prevent exception leakage and uncontrolled coupling across trust boundaries.","requirements":["Define exception-to-response mapping.","Protect diagnostic detail.","Test unexpected exceptions.","Ensure security decisions do not depend on raw exception text."],"constraints":["Some exceptions contain useful debugging information that must remain internally available.","Mapping must preserve legitimate protocol semantics."],"preconditions":["Internal exceptions can cross an external or lower-trust boundary."],"expected_behavior":"External behavior is determined by a stable security-aware error contract.","failure_mode":"Raw exception details or categories influence external security behavior unexpectedly.","cause":"Internal exception handling is reused as protocol behavior.","root_cause":"Error translation is absent or placed after the trust boundary.","solution":"Translate internal failures into explicit bounded external semantics.","alternative":"Use typed error objects with a controlled serialization layer.","tradeoff":"Typed translation increases code and schema maintenance.","edge_case":"Third-party libraries may introduce unexpected exception types or messages.","security_implication":"Raw exception exposure can leak sensitive details and enable reconnaissance or behavioral probing.","test_case":"Raise expected and unexpected internal exceptions and inspect external behavior.","test_input":"Synthetic exceptions with sensitive and implementation-specific messages.","expected_test_result":"External output remains within the defined disclosure and security semantics.","testing_guidance":"Include third-party and malformed exception scenarios.","verification_method":"Review exception boundaries and execute disclosure/control-flow tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that exception text is never used as an authorization or policy signal.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B27-R01","D57-B27-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B27-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security State","concept":"Unknown security state","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should an unknown security state be represented explicitly in a security lesson?","answer":"When the system cannot establish whether a security condition is satisfied, representing the state as unknown prevents missing evidence from being incorrectly treated as a secure or insecure fact.","explanation":"Security systems frequently encounter unavailable sensors, incomplete evidence, failed checks, or ambiguous states. The lesson is that uncertainty must remain explicit rather than being collapsed into a convenient boolean.","principle":"Unknown security state is distinct from both secure and insecure state.","rule":"Do not infer successful security verification from absence of evidence.","objective":"Prevent false assurance caused by uncertainty collapse.","requirements":["Represent unknown or indeterminate state.","Record why the state is unknown.","Define policy for security-sensitive actions under uncertainty.","Provide recovery or evidence-acquisition paths."],"constraints":["Some operations cannot remain indefinitely pending.","Different controls may have different acceptable uncertainty policies."],"preconditions":["A security property cannot currently be established with available evidence."],"expected_behavior":"The system preserves uncertainty and applies the defined policy for unknown security state.","failure_mode":"Missing evidence is interpreted as success or failure without justification.","cause":"Boolean-only security state representation.","root_cause":"The knowledge or control model lacks an explicit uncertainty state.","solution":"Represent unknown state separately and define action policy for it.","alternative":"Use confidence-bounded state with explicit thresholds where justified.","tradeoff":"Explicit uncertainty increases state complexity but improves reasoning correctness.","edge_case":"A stale successful result may look like current evidence even though freshness has expired.","security_implication":"False certainty can allow unsafe actions or trigger inappropriate remediation.","test_case":"Remove or invalidate required evidence and inspect security state.","test_input":"Missing, stale, contradictory, and unavailable security evidence.","expected_test_result":"State becomes unknown or otherwise follows the defined uncertainty policy rather than silently remaining secure.","testing_guidance":"Test evidence freshness and dependency failures.","verification_method":"State-model review plus controlled evidence-loss tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unknown is distinguishable in storage, retrieval, policy, and audit output.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B05-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B27-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Recovery","concept":"Recovery-path security equivalence","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when recovery or emergency paths bypass normal controls?","answer":"Recovery paths must have explicit security semantics and should not automatically inherit weaker controls merely because they exist for operational recovery.","explanation":"Emergency credentials, restore procedures, migration utilities, maintenance endpoints, and break-glass workflows can become alternate security boundaries. The lesson should capture their authority, activation conditions, evidence requirements, and expiry.","principle":"Recovery is part of the security model, not an exception to it.","rule":"Treat emergency and recovery paths as first-class security-sensitive operations.","objective":"Prevent recovery mechanisms from becoming permanent security bypasses.","requirements":["Define recovery authority.","Define activation conditions.","Record and protect recovery actions.","Define expiration or restoration to normal state."],"constraints":["Recovery paths may require broader authority than ordinary operations.","Availability objectives can require controlled emergency access."],"preconditions":["The system has a recovery, emergency, administrative, or break-glass mechanism."],"expected_behavior":"Recovery mechanisms operate within explicit authority and return to the normal security state after use.","failure_mode":"Emergency access remains active, bypasses audit, or permits broader actions than intended.","cause":"Recovery design is treated as operational convenience rather than a security boundary.","root_cause":"Emergency state is absent from the security model.","solution":"Model recovery as a separate security state with explicit authorization, audit, and exit conditions.","alternative":"Use narrowly scoped time-limited recovery credentials.","tradeoff":"Strict recovery controls can slow emergency response.","edge_case":"Normal authorization infrastructure may itself be unavailable during recovery.","security_implication":"Poorly governed recovery paths can become high-privilege persistence mechanisms.","test_case":"Exercise recovery activation, protected action, audit, expiration, and restoration.","test_input":"Controlled break-glass scenario using non-production credentials.","expected_test_result":"Recovery actions follow defined authority and the system returns to normal security state as specified.","testing_guidance":"Test both successful recovery and failed or abandoned recovery flows.","verification_method":"Recovery security review and controlled lifecycle test.","verification_guidance":"Verify that emergency authority cannot silently persist beyond its intended scope.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B24-R04","D57-B28-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B28-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Boundaries","concept":"Network location is not authorization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security lesson applies when access is permitted primarily because a request originates from a trusted network location?","answer":"Network location can be one contextual signal but should not substitute for identity, authorization, and explicit security policy when the protected resource requires them.","explanation":"Network boundaries can change without changing the identity or intent of a caller, and trusted networks can contain compromised systems. The lesson should preserve the distinction between transport reachability and application authorization.","principle":"Network reachability and authorization are different security properties.","rule":"Do not infer sufficient application authority solely from network origin unless the security model explicitly defines and protects that trust assumption.","objective":"Prevent network-location assumptions from becoming unauthorized access paths.","requirements":["Define network trust assumptions.","Define identity and authorization requirements.","Test access from different network contexts.","Review internal and administrative paths."],"constraints":["Some systems intentionally use network segmentation as a security control.","Network controls remain useful but do not necessarily provide application-level identity."],"preconditions":["A security decision depends partly or primarily on network location."],"expected_behavior":"Network controls complement rather than silently replace required identity and authorization controls.","failure_mode":"A reachable internal service accepts sensitive actions without validating the required authority.","cause":"Network location is treated as equivalent to trusted identity.","root_cause":"Network and application trust models are conflated.","solution":"Separate reachability controls from authorization policy and explicitly document any network-based trust assumptions.","alternative":"Use network segmentation as defense in depth around application-level controls.","tradeoff":"Additional application-level checks can increase implementation and operational complexity.","edge_case":"A compromised host inside the trusted network can originate apparently trusted traffic.","security_implication":"Network-only trust can enable lateral movement or unauthorized internal access.","test_case":"Attempt protected operations from both trusted-network and untrusted-network contexts using authorized and unauthorized identities.","test_input":"Controlled identities and network locations.","expected_test_result":"Access follows the documented authorization policy rather than network origin alone.","testing_guidance":"Include internal service-to-service and administrative paths.","verification_method":"Review trust-boundary model and execute network-origin bypass tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify which security properties are actually provided by network controls.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B21-R04","D57-B24-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B28-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Trust","concept":"Service-to-service trust validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What lesson applies when an internal service trusts another service without validating its security context?","answer":"Service-to-service communication should establish the identity and authorization context required for the requested operation rather than relying solely on the fact that traffic came from an expected service path.","explanation":"Internal architecture does not automatically define a complete trust relationship. The lesson should identify which service is acting, on whose authority, for which resource, and with what scope.","principle":"Service identity and delegated authority should be explicit across service boundaries.","rule":"Do not treat internal network reachability as sufficient evidence of service authorization.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized internal service calls and privilege propagation.","requirements":["Authenticate service identity where required.","Define delegated authority.","Authorize requested action.","Protect service credentials."],"constraints":["Service meshes and platform identity mechanisms vary.","Not every internal call needs identical authentication mechanisms, but trust assumptions must be explicit."],"preconditions":["Multiple services communicate across security boundaries."],"expected_behavior":"The receiving service evaluates the caller's identity and authority according to the defined trust model.","failure_mode":"Any reachable internal service can invoke privileged functionality.","cause":"Internal topology is treated as a complete authorization boundary.","root_cause":"Service trust relationships are implicit rather than policy-defined.","solution":"Model service identity, authorization, and delegation explicitly.","alternative":"Use platform-provided workload identity with narrowly scoped permissions.","tradeoff":"Explicit service authorization adds policy and credential lifecycle management.","edge_case":"A compromised intermediary may forward requests while preserving an apparently valid service identity.","security_implication":"Weak service-to-service trust can enable lateral privilege escalation.","test_case":"Attempt privileged operations from services with different identities and scopes.","test_input":"Authorized and unauthorized service identities.","expected_test_result":"Only policy-authorized service actions succeed.","testing_guidance":"Test direct and chained service calls.","verification_method":"Review service trust graph and execute identity/scope tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that forwarded requests preserve trustworthy caller context rather than relying on unverified headers or metadata.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B24-R04","D57-B28-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B28-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Recovery","concept":"Administrative recovery interface exposure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What lesson should be captured when an administrative or recovery interface is exposed more broadly than intended?","answer":"Administrative and recovery interfaces require explicit exposure boundaries, authentication, authorization, and lifecycle controls because their privileges often exceed ordinary application functions.","explanation":"An interface can be logically protected while still being reachable through an unintended network path. The lesson should connect exposure, authority, and the protected operation rather than recording only the network configuration change.","principle":"High-privilege interfaces deserve an explicitly modeled exposure boundary.","rule":"Do not expose administrative or recovery operations through a broader trust boundary than the security design permits.","objective":"Reduce attack surface around privileged operations.","requirements":["Define intended exposure boundary.","Require appropriate identity and authorization.","Restrict unnecessary network reachability.","Audit privileged actions."],"constraints":["Emergency accessibility may be required.","Network restrictions alone may not satisfy application authorization requirements."],"preconditions":["Privileged administrative or recovery functionality exists."],"expected_behavior":"Privileged interfaces are reachable only through intended security boundaries and enforce required authorization.","failure_mode":"A privileged endpoint becomes reachable from a broader network or lower-trust principal.","cause":"Exposure configuration changes without corresponding security review.","root_cause":"Interface exposure is not linked to privilege classification.","solution":"Classify privileged interfaces and enforce exposure and authorization policy as a combined control.","alternative":"Place administrative functions behind a dedicated management boundary.","tradeoff":"Dedicated management boundaries add infrastructure and operational complexity.","edge_case":"Temporary emergency exposure may outlive the emergency condition.","security_implication":"Unexpected exposure can make high-impact privileged operations reachable to unauthorized actors.","test_case":"Enumerate reachable privileged interfaces from each supported network zone.","test_input":"Controlled network-zone access tests.","expected_test_result":"Only intended zones can reach the interface and authorization remains enforced.","testing_guidance":"Test temporary and recovery exposure configurations.","verification_method":"Network exposure review plus privileged-operation access testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both reachability and application-level authorization.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B27-R05","D57-B28-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B28-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Security Failure","concept":"Dependency outage security semantics","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security lesson applies when a security-relevant network dependency becomes unreachable?","answer":"The system should transition to an explicitly defined security state when a network dependency fails rather than silently bypassing the dependency or assuming its last successful result remains valid indefinitely.","explanation":"Network failures can affect identity verification, policy retrieval, key services, revocation, threat intelligence, or audit delivery. The lesson should identify freshness and fallback semantics for each dependency.","principle":"Dependency unavailability can invalidate security assumptions and must be modeled explicitly.","rule":"Define security behavior for unavailable or stale network-dependent security controls.","objective":"Prevent network failures from becoming security bypasses or undefined trust states.","requirements":["Identify network-dependent security controls.","Define evidence freshness.","Define failure behavior.","Test recovery and stale-state handling."],"constraints":["Some security controls can safely use bounded cached state.","Availability requirements may require degraded operation."],"preconditions":["A security decision depends on a remote or network-reachable control.","expected_behavior":"The system follows explicit policy when the dependency is unavailable or its evidence becomes stale.","failure_mode":"The system continues sensitive operations using stale or absent security evidence without policy justification.","cause":"Availability handling ignores security-state freshness.","root_cause":"Network dependency state is not represented in the security model.","solution":"Model dependency health and evidence freshness as security state.","alternative":"Use bounded, integrity-protected cached policy where the threat model permits it.","tradeoff":"Caching improves resilience but can extend stale authority or stale security knowledge.","edge_case":"A dependency may be reachable but return stale or invalid data.","security_implication":"Undefined network failure semantics can create authorization, verification, or detection gaps.","test_case":"Simulate dependency timeout, unreachable state, stale response, and recovery.","test_input":"Controlled dependency failures and stale timestamps.","expected_test_result":"Security behavior follows documented freshness and fallback policy.","testing_guidance":"Test partial network failure and asymmetric connectivity.","verification_method":"Security state-model review plus fault-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that network reachability is not confused with correctness or freshness of security evidence.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B27-R02","D57-B27-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B28-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Segmentation","concept":"Segmentation as defense in depth","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security lesson should be recorded about network segmentation?","answer":"Network segmentation can constrain reachable attack paths and limit blast radius, but it should be treated as one layer of defense rather than proof that systems inside a segment are mutually trusted.","explanation":"Segmentation changes reachability, not necessarily application authority or component integrity. A reusable lesson should preserve the exact threat reduction it provides and its remaining assumptions.","principle":"Segmentation reduces reachable paths but does not eliminate the need for appropriate endpoint and application controls.","rule":"Do not infer full trust from shared network segment membership.","objective":"Use segmentation to constrain exposure without creating unsupported trust assumptions.","requirements":["Define segmentation objective.","Identify allowed flows.","Review privileged and sensitive flows.","Test boundary enforcement."],"constraints":["Segmentation can be bypassed by misconfiguration or compromised infrastructure.","Operational requirements may require controlled cross-segment communication."],"preconditions":["Network zones are used as part of the security architecture."],"expected_behavior":"Only intended network flows cross defined segment boundaries, while application-level authorization remains appropriate.","failure_mode":"Segment membership becomes an implicit authorization mechanism.","cause":"Segmentation purpose is not clearly distinguished from identity and authorization.","root_cause":"Network reachability and trust are modeled as equivalent properties.","solution":"Document segmentation as a bounded control and maintain independent authorization where required.","alternative":"Combine segmentation with service identity and policy enforcement.","tradeoff":"Layered controls increase complexity but can reduce single-control dependency.","edge_case":"A compromised host can attack peers within the same segment unless additional controls exist.","security_implication":"Over-trusting a segment can enable lateral movement after one component is compromised.","test_case":"Attempt permitted and forbidden cross-segment flows and inspect application authorization independently.","test_input":"Controlled network flow matrix and authorized/unauthorized identities.","expected_test_result":"Network segmentation enforces intended reachability without replacing required application authorization.","testing_guidance":"Test configuration drift and newly introduced flows.","verification_method":"Flow-policy review and boundary testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the documented security benefit matches what the network control actually enforces.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B28-R01","D57-B28-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B29-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Build Security","concept":"Build artifact integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when the artifact built for deployment cannot be confidently linked to its source and build inputs?","answer":"Security-sensitive builds should preserve enough provenance and integrity evidence to establish which source, dependencies, configuration, and build process produced the artifact.","explanation":"A deployment artifact is part of the trusted software supply chain. The lesson should distinguish source intent from the actual artifact produced and should avoid claiming reproducibility unless it is demonstrated.","principle":"Deployment trust depends on knowing what was actually built and from which inputs.","rule":"Maintain verifiable linkage between security-relevant source/build inputs and deployment artifacts where required by the threat model.","objective":"Reduce uncertainty about artifact identity and build integrity.","requirements":["Identify source revision.","Identify dependency inputs.","Identify build configuration.","Protect artifact identity and integrity evidence."],"constraints":["Build environments can contain nondeterminism.","Complete reproducibility may not always be feasible."],"preconditions":["Software artifacts are built and deployed through a build pipeline."],"expected_behavior":"The deployment artifact has sufficient provenance to establish its intended build inputs within the defined security model.","failure_mode":"An artifact is deployed without trustworthy evidence of what produced it.","cause":"Build provenance is not captured or protected.","root_cause":"Artifact identity is separated from source and build-state evidence.","solution":"Capture and protect build provenance and artifact identity metadata.","alternative":"Use reproducible or hermetic build techniques where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Stronger build isolation and reproducibility can increase build complexity.","edge_case":"A build may include generated files or environment-dependent inputs that are not obvious from source revision alone.","security_implication":"Untrusted or substituted build artifacts can introduce malicious or vulnerable code despite trusted source.","test_case":"Compare artifact metadata with expected source and dependency inputs in a controlled build.","test_input":"Known source revision, dependency graph, and build configuration.","expected_test_result":"Artifact provenance matches the documented build inputs within the defined verification model.","testing_guidance":"Include clean-environment builds and artifact integrity checks where supported.","verification_method":"Build provenance inspection and artifact verification.","verification_guidance":"Do not equate source-control history with proof of the exact deployed artifact.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B26-R01","D57-B29-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B29-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Build Security","concept":"Build-time versus runtime security controls","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should a security lesson distinguish build-time controls from runtime controls?","answer":"A build-time security check can establish properties about source or artifacts at one stage, but it does not automatically establish the runtime security state of the deployed system.","explanation":"Build scanning, dependency checks, static analysis, and artifact validation provide evidence about particular stages. Runtime configuration, identity, environment, external dependencies, and dynamic behavior require separate verification.","principle":"Security evidence is scoped to the system state and stage in which it was produced.","rule":"Do not use build-time evidence as unqualified proof of runtime security.","objective":"Prevent stage-confusion in security assurance.","requirements":["Identify evidence stage.","Define what property each control establishes.","Perform runtime verification for runtime-dependent properties.","Preserve evidence relationships."],"constraints":["Some controls provide useful predictive evidence but cannot prove runtime state.","Runtime verification can be environment-specific."],"preconditions":["Security assurance uses controls at multiple lifecycle stages."],"expected_behavior":"Security claims identify the stage and scope of the evidence supporting them.","failure_mode":"A successful build check is interpreted as proof that the deployed system is secure.","cause":"Evidence scope is omitted from security reporting.","root_cause":"Security assurance model does not distinguish lifecycle stages.","solution":"Attach stage and applicability metadata to security evidence and require complementary verification.","alternative":"Use end-to-end deployment verification for properties that depend on runtime state.","tradeoff":"More verification stages increase effort and execution requirements.","edge_case":"A runtime configuration can differ materially from build assumptions.","security_implication":"Stage confusion can leave deployment-specific vulnerabilities or misconfigurations undetected.","test_case":"Change a runtime security configuration without changing the build artifact and inspect whether build evidence detects it.","test_input":"Same artifact under differing controlled runtime configurations.","expected_test_result":"Runtime verification identifies security-state differences that build evidence cannot establish.","testing_guidance":"Use this as a boundary test between build and deployment assurance.","verification_method":"Lifecycle evidence mapping and controlled runtime configuration test.","verification_guidance":"Record exactly which claim each security check can support.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B29-R01","D57-B30-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B29-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Build Security","concept":"Security-sensitive build configuration","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What lesson applies when security behavior depends on build configuration?","answer":"Security-relevant build flags, feature selection, compiler options, generated code, and packaging decisions should be treated as security inputs and verified for the target artifact.","explanation":"Source code alone may not determine the resulting security behavior. The lesson should capture configuration that changes security controls and distinguish intended configuration from effective build state.","principle":"Security properties can depend on build-time configuration as well as source code.","rule":"Track and verify security-sensitive build configuration for supported artifact variants.","objective":"Prevent insecure artifact variants caused by unintended build configuration.","requirements":["Identify security-sensitive build inputs.","Record effective configuration.","Test supported variants.","Prevent unsupported insecure combinations where feasible."],"constraints":["Multiple platforms and build profiles can create many variants.","Some build systems generate configuration dynamically."],"preconditions":["Build configuration can alter security-relevant code or behavior."],"expected_behavior":"Supported artifacts are built with documented security-relevant configuration.","failure_mode":"An artifact variant disables or changes a security control unexpectedly.","cause":"Build configuration is treated as non-security metadata.","root_cause":"Security requirements are not connected to build configuration constraints.","solution":"Model security-sensitive build settings as explicit inputs and validate them during artifact creation.","alternative":"Restrict build profiles to approved configurations.","tradeoff":"Restricting variants can reduce flexibility.","edge_case":"A release profile may differ from the test profile in security-relevant ways.","security_implication":"An insecure build variant can bypass protections even when source and tests are correct under another configuration.","test_case":"Build each supported security-sensitive configuration and compare required control behavior.","test_input":"Approved and intentionally altered build configurations.","expected_test_result":"Unsupported security configurations are rejected or detected, and approved variants satisfy required controls.","testing_guidance":"Include release-like configurations rather than relying solely on development builds.","verification_method":"Build configuration validation plus security regression testing.","verification_guidance":"Record effective configuration with artifact provenance.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B26-R04","D57-B29-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B29-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Artifact Verification","concept":"Artifact identity verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should deployment verify artifact identity independently of source review?","answer":"Source review establishes what was intended, while artifact verification establishes what is actually being deployed; both are needed when artifact substitution is within the threat model.","explanation":"A trusted source repository does not automatically prove that a deployment system selected the intended artifact. The lesson should preserve the distinction between intended source state and deployed artifact identity.","principle":"Deployment should verify the identity and integrity of the artifact it actually executes.","rule":"Do not infer deployed artifact identity solely from source revision or build pipeline metadata.","objective":"Prevent deployment of unintended or substituted artifacts.","requirements":["Define artifact identity.","Verify integrity according to the threat model.","Bind deployment reference to the verified artifact.","Record deployment evidence."],"constraints":["Verification mechanisms differ across deployment environments.","Artifact identity does not prove runtime configuration correctness."],"preconditions":["Deployment consumes a built artifact whose identity matters to security."],"expected_behavior":"The deployed artifact matches the artifact approved by the defined release process.","failure_mode":"Deployment executes an artifact that differs from the reviewed or approved artifact.","cause":"Deployment references mutable or ambiguous artifact identifiers.","root_cause":"Artifact identity is not a first-class deployment security property.","solution":"Use immutable or verifiable artifact identity and bind deployment to it.","alternative":"Use signed release metadata where appropriate to the trust model.","tradeoff":"Immutable release processes can reduce operational flexibility.","edge_case":"A tag or mutable reference can resolve to different content over time.","security_implication":"Artifact substitution can introduce malicious or vulnerable code after security review.","test_case":"Attempt deployment with a different artifact behind the same logical release reference.","test_input":"Controlled artifact variants with distinct identities.","expected_test_result":"The deployment verification detects the mismatch and refuses or flags it according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Test mutable references and registry/cache substitution scenarios where applicable.","verification_method":"Artifact identity verification during deployment.","verification_guidance":"Verify the exact artifact actually executed, not merely the requested reference.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B29-R01","D57-B29-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B29-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Release Security","concept":"Security approval versus deployment execution","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security lesson applies when an artifact has passed security review but deployment occurs later under different conditions?","answer":"Security approval is evidence about a defined artifact and context at a particular point in time; deployment should verify that the approved security conditions still hold before execution.","explanation":"Security state can change through artifact replacement, configuration drift, dependency changes, expired credentials, revoked approvals, or environmental differences. The lesson should preserve approval scope and freshness.","principle":"Approval is scoped evidence, not an everlasting security property.","rule":"Verify artifact identity, approval scope, and relevant security conditions at deployment time.","objective":"Prevent stale security approval from authorizing a materially different deployment.","requirements":["Bind approval to artifact identity.","Define approval validity conditions.","Verify deployment context.","Record final deployment evidence."],"constraints":["Reverification depth should match risk and change scope.","Some low-risk changes may not require identical approval workflows."],"preconditions":["Security approval occurs separately from deployment execution."],"expected_behavior":"Deployment proceeds only when the approved artifact and required security conditions remain valid.","failure_mode":"A different or materially changed artifact is deployed under an older approval.","cause":"Approval identity or freshness is not enforced.","root_cause":"Approval is modeled as a generic status rather than scoped authorization evidence.","solution":"Bind approvals to immutable artifact identity and explicit validity conditions.","alternative":"Use risk-based reapproval triggers for material changes.","tradeoff":"More frequent reapproval increases release friction.","edge_case":"A runtime environment can change without artifact changes.","security_implication":"Stale approval can authorize deployment of an artifact or environment outside the reviewed security state.","test_case":"Change artifact identity or a defined security condition after approval and attempt deployment.","test_input":"Controlled approval record plus altered artifact/context.","expected_test_result":"Deployment detects the mismatch and requires the defined additional verification or approval.","testing_guidance":"Test approval expiration and material-change conditions.","verification_method":"Approval binding review plus controlled deployment authorization test.","verification_guidance":"Verify that approval evidence is not interpreted more broadly than its recorded scope.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R04","D57-B29-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B30-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Lesson Generalization","concept":"Generalization boundary for security lessons","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When can a security lesson be generalized from a specific finding?","answer":"A security lesson can be generalized when the underlying mechanism, security property, applicability conditions, and evidence support transfer beyond the original context; the exact remediation should not be generalized automatically.","explanation":"Generalization should abstract the causal security mechanism while preserving conditions and uncertainty. A single context-specific observation does not automatically establish a universal rule.","principle":"Generalize mechanisms with evidence; preserve conditions and uncertainty.","rule":"Do not promote a context-specific security observation to a universal claim without sufficient supporting evidence.","objective":"Build reusable security knowledge without introducing unsupported generalizations.","requirements":["Identify underlying mechanism.","Identify applicability conditions.","Separate observation from inference.","Record evidence level and limitations."],"constraints":["Evidence thresholds depend on the intended use of the knowledge.","Some lessons may remain deliberately context-specific."],"preconditions":["A specific security finding is being transformed into reusable knowledge."],"expected_behavior":"The resulting lesson states only what the evidence and mechanism justify.","failure_mode":"A narrow observation is converted into an unconditional security rule.","cause":"Context is removed during abstraction.","root_cause":"Knowledge promotion lacks explicit generalization criteria.","solution":"Use a promotion process that requires mechanism, evidence, applicability, and limitation metadata.","alternative":"Keep a linked context-specific lesson and a separate conditional generalized pattern.","tradeoff":"Conditional knowledge is more precise but may be harder to retrieve or apply automatically.","edge_case":"A widely established principle may support generalization even when one local implementation provides only illustrative evidence.","security_implication":"Over-generalized lessons can cause incorrect controls or false assurance.","test_case":"Attempt to apply the lesson to contexts with materially different assumptions.","test_input":"Original context plus controlled variant contexts.","expected_test_result":"The lesson identifies whether the variant satisfies its applicability conditions.","testing_guidance":"Include positive, negative, and uncertain applicability cases.","verification_method":"Review promotion criteria and test retrieval/application against varied contexts.","verification_guidance":"Ensure evidence classification remains attached to the generalized lesson.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R01","D57-B30-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B30-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Knowledge Classification","concept":"Control-specific lesson versus universal rule","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should the Brain distinguish a control-specific security lesson from a universal security principle?","answer":"A control-specific lesson describes what was learned about a particular mechanism or environment, while a universal principle represents broader knowledge supported independently of that single implementation.","explanation":"Both can be valuable, but they have different reuse scopes and evidence requirements. The dataset should preserve the distinction so retrieval does not treat local implementation knowledge as universal truth.","principle":"Knowledge scope must match evidence scope.","rule":"Never promote implementation-specific security behavior to a universal rule solely because it appears successful in one context.","objective":"Prevent scope inflation in the security knowledge base.","requirements":["Classify knowledge scope.","Record supporting evidence.","Record applicability conditions.","Link local lessons to broader principles where appropriate."],"constraints":["Universal claims can still have assumptions and exceptions.","Implementation-specific lessons may be more actionable than broad principles."],"preconditions":["Security knowledge is being classified for future retrieval or reasoning."],"expected_behavior":"The Brain can distinguish local evidence from broadly supported security knowledge.","failure_mode":"A local implementation lesson is retrieved and applied as though it were a universal security law.","cause":"Knowledge scope is not encoded.","root_cause":"Classification model does not distinguish implementation context from generalized principle.","solution":"Add explicit scope and evidence-classification metadata and preserve relationships between local and generalized records.","alternative":"Use separate namespaces or retrieval filters for experience, inference, and established principles.","tradeoff":"More metadata and retrieval logic are required.","edge_case":"A local lesson may independently agree with a well-established principle but should not be used as its sole evidence.","security_implication":"Scope confusion can cause inappropriate security decisions.","test_case":"Retrieve knowledge for both matching and mismatching implementation contexts.","test_input":"Local lesson plus generalized principle with different scope metadata.","expected_test_result":"Retrieval and reasoning preserve the distinction between local and universal knowledge.","testing_guidance":"Test conflict and evidence-priority behavior.","verification_method":"Schema review and retrieval classification tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that evidence class remains visible to downstream reasoning.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B30-R01","D57-B30-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B30-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Preventive Controls","concept":"Lesson-to-control linkage","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should a reusable security lesson connect to preventive and detective controls?","answer":"A reusable lesson should identify the security mechanism it describes and link that mechanism to applicable preventive, detective, and verification controls without duplicating the controls' full definitions.","explanation":"The lesson dataset is not a replacement for dedicated control or detection datasets. Relationships should allow the Brain to move from lesson to control and from control evidence back to the lesson.","principle":"Security lessons become more useful when they preserve traceable relationships to controls and verification evidence.","rule":"Link lessons to relevant controls rather than copying control definitions into every lesson record.","objective":"Enable retrieval, reasoning, prevention, detection, and verification from a shared knowledge graph.","requirements":["Identify lesson mechanism.","Identify relevant control types.","Link to control records.","Link verification evidence where available."],"constraints":["Referenced control IDs must exist before the relationship can be considered resolved.","A lesson may map to multiple controls."],"preconditions":["Related security control knowledge exists or can be represented separately."],"expected_behavior":"The Brain can traverse from a lesson to applicable controls and verification knowledge without content duplication.","failure_mode":"Lessons contain duplicated control content that diverges from authoritative control records.","cause":"Knowledge graph relationships are replaced with text duplication.","root_cause":"Dataset boundaries are not respected.","solution":"Store concise lesson knowledge and explicit stable relationships to authoritative control records.","alternative":"Use derived retrieval views that combine lesson and control records at query time.","tradeoff":"Graph traversal or join logic becomes necessary.","edge_case":"A lesson may reveal that an existing control is insufficient, requiring a new or modified control relationship.","security_implication":"Traceable control linkage improves the ability to convert lessons into preventive or detective improvements.","test_case":"Traverse a lesson relationship to a control and back to verification evidence.","test_input":"Known lesson, control, and verification record IDs.","expected_test_result":"Relationships resolve correctly without duplicated authoritative content.","testing_guidance":"Validate referential integrity and detect orphaned relationship IDs.","verification_method":"Graph integrity validation and retrieval traversal test.","verification_guidance":"Ensure relationship semantics distinguish related, implements, tests, and verifies rather than treating all links as equivalent.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B30-R02","D57-B30-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B30-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Contradiction Handling","concept":"Contradictory security lessons","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"How should the Brain handle two security lessons that appear to contradict each other?","answer":"The Brain should preserve both records, identify the exact proposition and applicability conditions in conflict, classify the contradiction, and avoid silently merging them into a single rule.","explanation":"Security advice can differ because threat models, architectures, versions, evidence quality, or assumptions differ. Contradiction handling should therefore compare propositions and contexts rather than resolving disagreements by textual similarity.","principle":"Contradiction is knowledge to model, not noise to erase.","rule":"Do not overwrite or silently merge security lessons with incompatible claims.","objective":"Preserve uncertainty and context when security knowledge conflicts.","requirements":["Identify conflicting proposition.","Compare scope and assumptions.","Classify evidence strength.","Record resolution or unresolved status."],"constraints":["Some apparent contradictions disappear after scope analysis.","No resolution should be fabricated when evidence is insufficient."],"preconditions":["Two or more records make materially incompatible security claims."],"expected_behavior":"The knowledge graph preserves the conflict and exposes the conditions under which each claim applies.","failure_mode":"One lesson is silently discarded or the conflict is hidden from retrieval.","cause":"Deduplication treats semantic disagreement as duplicate content.","root_cause":"Contradiction and duplication are not modeled as distinct relations.","solution":"Use explicit contradiction relationships and evidence-aware resolution status.","alternative":"Create a scoped synthesis record only when the evidence supports it.","tradeoff":"Conflict-aware retrieval is more complex than simple nearest-neighbor matching.","edge_case":"A newer version may legitimately supersede an older security recommendation without making the older record historically false.","security_implication":"Hidden contradictions can cause unsafe security decisions.","test_case":"Insert two scoped lessons with conflicting recommendations and evaluate retrieval behavior.","test_input":"Conflicting records with different versions, threat models, or evidence levels.","expected_test_result":"Both records remain traceable and the contradiction is visible with applicable scope metadata.","testing_guidance":"Test resolved and unresolved contradiction cases.","verification_method":"Graph validation plus contradiction-retrieval test.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence before marking a contradiction resolved or superseded.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R04","D57-B30-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B30-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Knowledge Promotion","concept":"Promotion of security lessons into reusable knowledge","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What criteria should be satisfied before a security lesson is promoted into reusable Brain knowledge?","answer":"Promotion should require a clearly defined mechanism, bounded applicability, adequate evidence classification, preserved provenance, validation or verification where applicable, and no unresolved contradiction that would make the generalized claim unsafe to reuse.","explanation":"A lesson is not automatically reusable knowledge merely because it is plausible or useful. Promotion should preserve the distinction between observed experience, validated pattern, inference, and recommendation.","principle":"Knowledge promotion is an evidence and scope decision, not a popularity or frequency decision.","rule":"Promote a security lesson only when its claim, scope, evidence status, provenance, and contradictions are explicitly represented.","objective":"Maintain a trustworthy security knowledge substrate for future reasoning.","requirements":["Define claim precisely.","Classify knowledge type and evidence.","Record provenance.","Record applicability and limitations.","Validate implementation claims when applicable.","Resolve or explicitly preserve contradictions."],"constraints":["Some valuable lessons may remain provisional until more evidence exists.","Promotion thresholds can differ by risk and intended use."],"preconditions":["A security lesson candidate is being considered for reusable knowledge.","expected_behavior":"Promoted knowledge remains traceable to evidence and clearly communicates uncertainty and applicability.","failure_mode":"Plausible or anecdotal security advice is promoted as verified reusable fact.","cause":"Promotion criteria prioritize availability or count over evidence quality.","root_cause":"Knowledge lifecycle lacks an explicit evidence gate.","solution":"Implement a promotion gate that checks evidence, provenance, scope, validation, contradiction status, and deduplication.","alternative":"Keep provisional lessons in a lower-trust knowledge tier until promotion criteria are met.","tradeoff":"Strict promotion reduces immediate knowledge volume but improves reliability.","edge_case":"A highly established security principle may have strong external provenance even when no local operational observation exists.","security_implication":"Weak promotion controls can contaminate downstream security reasoning and automated decisions.","test_case":"Submit lesson candidates with varying evidence, provenance, contradiction, and validation states to the promotion gate.","test_input":"Verified fact, inference, illustrative pattern, conflicted lesson, and provenance-missing candidates.","expected_test_result":"Only candidates satisfying the defined promotion criteria enter the reusable high-trust knowledge tier.","testing_guidance":"Test each rejection reason independently and verify stable classification after promotion.","verification_method":"Automated schema/policy validation plus human review where required by governance.","verification_guidance":"Verify that promotion never changes an inference into a verified fact without new evidence.","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R05","D57-B30-R01","D57-B30-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}

{"record_id":"D57-B31-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Session Security","concept":"Session invalidation after privilege or authentication-state changes","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when a user's authentication or privilege state changes?","answer":"Previously issued sessions or tokens may retain authority unless the system explicitly invalidates, re-evaluates, or otherwise constrains them according to the security model.","explanation":"Changing the current account state does not automatically change the authority already embedded in previously issued credentials. Security design must define how revocation, privilege reduction, password changes, account disablement, and other security-state transitions affect existing sessions.","principle":"Security state changes must have an explicit effect on previously issued authority.","rule":"Define and verify session or token behavior for every security-sensitive account-state transition.","objective":"Prevent stale authentication state from preserving authority after a security boundary changes.","requirements":["Define revocation semantics","Identify credential lifetime","Handle privilege reductions explicitly","Test existing sessions after security-state changes"],"constraints":["Some stateless tokens cannot be instantly revoked without additional state or short lifetimes","Existing connections may have independent authorization semantics"],"failure_mode":"A disabled or downgraded account continues using an already issued session.","cause":"Session validity is evaluated only from token validity or issuance time.","root_cause":"The security model does not bind active authority to current account security state.","solution":"Introduce explicit revocation, short-lived credentials, state-aware authorization checks, or another documented mechanism appropriate to the threat model.","alternative":"Allow existing sessions to remain valid until expiry while applying narrowly scoped authorization checks.","tradeoff":"Immediate revocation improves containment but can increase state management and operational complexity.","edge_case":"A privilege reduction occurs while a long-lived session is actively performing a multi-step operation.","security_implication":"Stale sessions can create unauthorized access after account compromise or privilege reduction.","test_case":"Invalidate or reduce account authority while an existing session remains active.","test_input":{"initial_state":"authenticated_session_exists","state_change":"account_privilege_reduced"},"expected_test_result":"Subsequent security-sensitive authorization follows the documented post-change policy rather than blindly trusting the original session state.","testing_guidance":"Test existing sessions, refresh flows, concurrent sessions, and long-lived connections after each relevant security-state transition.","verification_method":"Review authorization behavior and execute revocation/state-transition tests against active credentials.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R02","D57-B08-R03","D57-B08-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B31-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Session Security","concept":"Session fixation prevention","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson follows from session fixation risks?","answer":"A session identifier must not remain attacker-controlled or unchanged across an authentication or privilege-establishing transition when that would allow an attacker to predict or reuse the authenticated session.","explanation":"Authentication changes the security meaning of a session. The transition should establish a fresh, unpredictable session context rather than upgrading an identifier that could have been obtained before authentication.","principle":"Do not promote an untrusted session identifier into a higher-trust authenticated session.","rule":"Regenerate or otherwise replace session identifiers at authentication and other documented privilege-boundary transitions.","objective":"Prevent pre-authentication session state from becoming authenticated authority.","requirements":["Unpredictable session identifiers","Explicit authentication transition handling","Session replacement or equivalent isolation","Verification of pre-authentication identifiers"],"constraints":["Applications may use different session architectures","Stateless authentication can implement the boundary differently"],"failure_mode":"An attacker supplies or learns a pre-authentication session identifier and the victim later authenticates within the same session context.","cause":"Authentication upgrades the existing session without establishing a fresh authenticated identifier.","root_cause":"The authentication transition lacks a trust-boundary reset.","solution":"Regenerate the session identifier or establish a fresh authenticated security context at the privilege transition.","alternative":"Use an architecture where authentication tokens are independently issued and cannot be attacker-selected.","tradeoff":"Session regeneration can require careful migration of legitimate pre-authentication state.","edge_case":"A shopping cart or other benign pre-authentication state must survive authentication without preserving the original session identifier.","security_implication":"Failure can allow session hijacking without requiring the attacker to obtain the user's password.","test_case":"Set a pre-authentication session identifier, authenticate, and determine whether authenticated authority is associated with a new security identifier.","test_input":{"pre_auth_session":"attacker_selected_or_known","transition":"authenticate"},"expected_test_result":"The authenticated session does not rely on an attacker-controlled pre-authentication identifier.","testing_guidance":"Test login, privilege elevation, federated authentication callbacks, passwordless authentication, and account linking.","verification_method":"Inspect session-transition behavior and perform controlled session-identifier reuse tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B31-R01","D57-B08-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B31-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Session Security","concept":"Session binding to security context","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why should sensitive sessions be bound to an appropriate security context?","answer":"Binding session use to relevant security context can reduce the value of stolen or replayed credentials, but the binding mechanism must be chosen carefully because network attributes and device characteristics can change legitimately.","explanation":"A session may need additional context such as authentication strength, device registration, or transaction-specific proof. Overly rigid binding can reject legitimate users, while insufficient binding can leave high-value sessions vulnerable to replay.","principle":"Use additional session context when it materially reduces a defined threat and remains reliable enough for the intended users.","rule":"Do not treat a changing environmental attribute as a universally reliable identity proof.","objective":"Reduce credential replay while preserving legitimate session continuity.","requirements":["Explicit threat model","Defined context signals","Fallback and recovery behavior","Verification against false acceptance and false rejection"],"constraints":["Context signals can be unstable or spoofable","Privacy and usability requirements may constrain collection"],"failure_mode":"A stolen session remains fully usable because no additional security context is checked.","cause":"Session design assumes possession of the session credential is sufficient for every sensitive action.","root_cause":"Threat-specific session assurance requirements were not defined.","solution":"Apply risk-appropriate additional authentication or transaction authorization for sensitive operations.","alternative":"Use short-lived sessions and step-up authentication instead of persistent environmental binding.","tradeoff":"Stronger contextual controls may increase user friction and operational complexity.","edge_case":"A legitimate user changes networks or devices during an active session.","security_implication":"Improper session binding can either permit replay or create denial-of-service-like access failures for legitimate users.","test_case":"Evaluate session behavior when relevant context changes and when a credential is replayed from a different context.","expected_test_result":"The system follows a documented risk-based policy rather than relying on an unverified contextual assumption.","testing_guidance":"Separate security assurance from convenience signals and test both legitimate context changes and unauthorized replay.","verification_method":"Review threat model, security policy, and controlled session-replay tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B31-R01","D57-B31-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B31-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Session Security","concept":"Logout semantics and credential revocation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"What should a security lesson record about logout distinguish?","answer":"User-interface logout, local session deletion, server-side session invalidation, refresh-token revocation, and termination of related credentials are separate security mechanisms and should not be treated as equivalent.","explanation":"A logout control may remove a browser-side identifier while a server-side credential remains valid elsewhere. The actual security effect depends on the authentication architecture and documented revocation behavior.","principle":"Record the concrete credential invalidation effect rather than treating the word logout as proof of revocation.","rule":"Verify every credential type that should cease to authorize after logout.","objective":"Prevent residual authorization after a security-sensitive logout action.","requirements":["Inventory credential types","Define logout scope","Define revocation behavior","Test credentials issued before logout"],"constraints":["Some credentials may intentionally remain valid across logout events","Distributed systems may require propagation time"],"failure_mode":"A user logs out but another active credential remains authorized.","cause":"Logout invalidates only the local presentation layer.","root_cause":"Credential lifecycle was not modeled as a complete security state transition.","solution":"Define and implement revocation semantics for applicable sessions, refresh credentials, and delegated authorities.","alternative":"Use short-lived access credentials combined with revocable refresh credentials.","tradeoff":"Broader revocation can improve containment while requiring more state and coordination.","edge_case":"Logout from one device is expected to leave sessions on other devices active.","security_implication":"Ambiguous logout semantics can cause false assumptions about whether access has actually ended.","test_case":"Log out from one client and test each relevant credential from the same and different clients.","expected_test_result":"Observed credential behavior matches the documented logout policy.","testing_guidance":"Document per-credential semantics instead of using a single boolean called logged_out.","verification_method":"Execute multi-session logout tests and inspect server-side credential state where applicable.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B31-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B31-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Session Security","concept":"Sensitive action reauthentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"When should a security lesson consider reauthentication or step-up authorization?","answer":"High-impact actions may require fresh authentication or transaction-specific authorization when compromise of an existing session would otherwise permit unacceptable consequences.","explanation":"An authenticated session provides ongoing authority, but not necessarily sufficient assurance for every operation. Security requirements should distinguish ordinary session use from actions whose impact warrants stronger proof.","principle":"Authentication assurance should be proportional to the consequence of the protected action.","rule":"Define high-impact operations and require an appropriate stronger assurance mechanism where the threat model warrants it.","objective":"Limit the impact of stolen or unattended authenticated sessions.","requirements":["Action classification","Defined assurance requirements","Fresh authentication or transaction authorization mechanism","Audit evidence"],"constraints":["Step-up controls increase friction","The mechanism must resist replay and confused-deputy behavior"],"failure_mode":"A stolen but valid session can immediately perform irreversible or high-impact operations.","cause":"All authenticated actions inherit identical session assurance.","root_cause":"Authorization policy does not distinguish action impact or authentication freshness.","solution":"Introduce risk-appropriate step-up authentication or transaction authorization for defined sensitive operations.","alternative":"Use narrowly scoped credentials or explicit approval workflows for high-impact actions.","tradeoff":"Higher assurance improves containment but adds latency and user interaction.","edge_case":"A background automation needs to perform a sensitive operation without an interactive user.","security_implication":"Interactive reauthentication alone cannot solve machine-to-machine authorization; delegated authority must be modeled separately.","test_case":"Attempt a protected high-impact operation using only an aged but otherwise valid session.","expected_test_result":"The system either requires the documented stronger assurance or denies the operation.","testing_guidance":"Test stale sessions, credential replay, automation identities, and approval workflows separately.","verification_method":"Review action classification and execute authorization tests with varying authentication age and assurance levels.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R01","D57-B08-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B32-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Policy Evaluation","concept":"Deny-by-default authorization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What lesson applies when an authorization policy does not explicitly define a case?","answer":"Security-sensitive authorization should fail closed when no applicable allow decision exists, unless an explicitly documented policy requires another behavior.","explanation":"Implicit permission created by missing policy coverage can turn new resources, actions, or identities into unintended authority. A default-deny model makes authorization expansion explicit.","principle":"Absence of an authorization grant should not silently become authorization.","rule":"Require an explicit applicable allow decision for protected operations.","objective":"Prevent policy gaps from creating unintended access.","requirements":["Explicit authorization policy","Defined default behavior","Deny handling","Tests for unmatched policy cases"],"constraints":["Some systems require explicit public resources or actions","Policy engines must distinguish public access from missing policy coverage"],"failure_mode":"A new endpoint or resource becomes accessible because no deny rule matches it.","cause":"The authorization engine defaults to allow or treats missing policy as success.","root_cause":"Policy completeness and default behavior were not explicitly specified.","solution":"Implement documented default-deny behavior for protected resources and actions.","alternative":"Use explicit public-access declarations while maintaining fail-closed behavior for protected namespaces.","tradeoff":"Default deny can expose missing configuration as availability or usability issues during deployment.","edge_case":"A newly introduced endpoint is intentionally public.","security_implication":"Default deny requires deliberate classification but reduces accidental privilege expansion.","test_case":"Request an unrecognized protected action with valid authentication but no matching authorization rule.","expected_test_result":"The action is denied and the decision is auditable.","testing_guidance":"Include unknown resources, unknown actions, malformed policy references, and policy-engine errors.","verification_method":"Inspect policy-engine default behavior and execute unmatched-policy tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R05","D57-B06-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B32-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Policy Evaluation","concept":"Policy decision versus policy enforcement","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should authorization policy decisions be distinguished from their enforcement?","answer":"A correct policy decision does not guarantee security if the enforcement point applies it incorrectly, incompletely, or to a different resource than intended.","explanation":"Authorization can be evaluated centrally while enforcement occurs in application, service, database, gateway, or workflow layers. Security lessons must therefore verify both the decision and the actual protected operation.","principle":"Security authorization is effective only when the decision is correctly enforced at the protected boundary.","rule":"Test the complete path from subject and resource evaluation through actual operation enforcement.","objective":"Prevent false assurance caused by validating policy logic without validating enforcement.","requirements":["Defined enforcement points","Decision traceability","End-to-end authorization tests","Failure handling"],"constraints":["Distributed enforcement can create consistency problems","Different services may interpret policy context differently"],"failure_mode":"The policy engine returns deny but the protected operation still succeeds.","cause":"The enforcement layer ignores, misreads, or bypasses the policy result.","root_cause":"Policy evaluation and enforcement were treated as a single unverified component.","solution":"Make enforcement explicit, fail closed on missing or invalid decisions, and test the end-to-end path.","alternative":"Centralize enforcement where architecture permits without assuming centralization eliminates verification needs.","tradeoff":"More centralized enforcement can reduce duplication but may introduce dependency and availability considerations.","edge_case":"A downstream service receives a request directly without passing through the upstream policy gateway.","security_implication":"Policy bypass can create a complete authorization failure despite apparently correct policy definitions.","test_case":"Attempt the same protected action through every reachable enforcement path.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized paths cannot bypass the intended authorization decision.","testing_guidance":"Test direct service access, alternate protocols, internal callers, retries, and asynchronous workers.","verification_method":"Trace policy decisions to actual protected operations and execute bypass-path tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R01","D57-B08-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B32-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Policy Evaluation","concept":"Authorization context integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What lesson applies when authorization depends on request context?","answer":"Security-critical authorization attributes must come from trustworthy sources or be cryptographically or structurally protected against unauthorized modification.","explanation":"Authorization may depend on tenant, subject, role, resource owner, authentication assurance, or transaction context. If an untrusted caller can modify those attributes before authorization, the policy may make a correct decision over false inputs.","principle":"Authorization correctness depends on the integrity of every security-relevant input to the decision.","rule":"Do not trust caller-controlled security context without establishing its provenance and integrity.","objective":"Prevent authorization decisions based on forged security attributes.","requirements":["Identify security-relevant context fields","Define authoritative sources","Protect context integrity","Verify propagation across boundaries"],"constraints":["Distributed systems may transform request context","Trusted intermediaries must have explicitly defined authority"],"failure_mode":"A caller changes a tenant or subject identifier and receives authorization for another principal.","cause":"The service accepts security context directly from an untrusted request.","root_cause":"Authority-bearing context was not separated from ordinary application data.","solution":"Derive security context from authenticated identity and trusted server-side state or protect it with an appropriate integrity mechanism.","alternative":"Use a trusted authorization service that resolves the context from authoritative identity and resource data.","tradeoff":"Authoritative lookups can increase coupling and processing overhead.","edge_case":"A gateway adds identity headers that downstream services assume are trustworthy even when directly reachable.","security_implication":"Forged context can cause cross-user or cross-tenant authorization failures.","test_case":"Modify security-relevant request attributes while keeping the authenticated identity unchanged.","expected_test_result":"The protected service does not treat caller-controlled modifications as authoritative.","testing_guidance":"Test direct access, proxy removal, header duplication, conflicting identity fields, and context transformation.","verification_method":"Trace the origin and integrity of every authorization input across service boundaries.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R02","D57-B08-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B32-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Policy Evaluation","concept":"Authorization decision caching","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What security lesson applies when authorization decisions are cached?","answer":"A cached authorization decision must have explicit validity, invalidation, and context-binding rules so that stale permissions do not survive security-state changes.","explanation":"Caching can preserve an earlier allow decision after role removal, resource ownership changes, policy updates, or tenant changes. The security consequence depends on what inputs the decision represents and how long they remain valid.","principle":"Authorization caches are security state and require security-specific lifecycle controls.","rule":"Bind authorization-cache entries to all security-relevant context and define invalidation or expiration semantics.","objective":"Prevent stale authorization from extending access beyond intended policy state.","requirements":["Cache-key completeness","Defined TTL or invalidation","Policy-change handling","Context isolation"],"constraints":["Immediate invalidation may be difficult across distributed systems","Longer cache lifetimes can increase stale-decision exposure"],"failure_mode":"A user retains access after their role or resource authorization is revoked.","cause":"The old allow decision remains cached.","root_cause":"Authorization caching was treated only as a performance optimization.","solution":"Define security-aware cache keys, bounded validity, and revocation or policy-change invalidation mechanisms.","alternative":"Avoid caching high-risk authorization decisions and evaluate them against authoritative state.","tradeoff":"Stronger freshness can increase authorization evaluation cost.","edge_case":"A cache key contains the user identity but omits tenant, resource, policy version, or authentication assurance.","security_implication":"Incomplete cache context can cause cross-resource or cross-tenant authorization leakage.","test_case":"Change authorization state while an allow decision is cached and retry the protected operation.","expected_test_result":"Access behavior follows the documented freshness and revocation policy.","testing_guidance":"Test policy changes, role changes, ownership changes, tenant changes, cache expiration, and cache failures.","verification_method":"Inspect cache-key construction and execute authorization-state transition tests before and after cache invalidation.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B31-R01","D57-B32-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B32-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Authorization Policy Evaluation","concept":"Authorization consistency across synchronous and asynchronous paths","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"Why must asynchronous workers apply authorization rules independently?","answer":"A background worker should not inherit unverified authority merely because an initiating request was authorized; the worker's own authority, task scope, and resource permissions must be explicitly defined.","explanation":"Asynchronous processing separates request-time authorization from execution-time behavior. A queued task can outlive the original user, resource state, or authorization grant.","principle":"Delegated work requires explicit authority semantics rather than implicit inheritance of the initiator's privileges.","rule":"Record and enforce the authorized scope and actor semantics of asynchronous work.","objective":"Prevent queued or delayed tasks from executing with stale or excessive authority.","requirements":["Task identity","Authorized scope","Execution-time policy","Revocation semantics"],"constraints":["Tasks may need to complete after the initiating session ends","Some workflows intentionally preserve authority for a bounded period"],"failure_mode":"A queued task executes after the initiator loses permission to modify the resource.","cause":"The worker trusts authorization captured only at enqueue time.","root_cause":"Authorization lifecycle was not modeled across asynchronous execution.","solution":"Define task-scoped authority, expiration, resource-state checks, and execution-time authorization where required.","alternative":"Bind tasks to a dedicated service identity with narrowly scoped permissions and explicit approval semantics.","tradeoff":"Execution-time checks improve security freshness but may cause legitimate queued tasks to fail after policy changes.","edge_case":"A task must continue after the user's account is disabled because it represents an already-approved business operation.","security_implication":"Ambiguous delegation semantics can create privilege persistence or unauthorized post-revocation actions.","test_case":"Authorize a task, revoke or alter the initiating authority, then allow the task to execute.","expected_test_result":"Execution follows the documented delegation and revocation policy.","testing_guidance":"Test queue delays, retries, duplicate delivery, task ownership changes, and revoked permissions.","verification_method":"Inspect task authorization metadata and execute delayed-task authorization scenarios.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R05","D57-B31-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B33-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Server-side tenant boundary enforcement","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What is the core security lesson for multi-tenant data access?","answer":"Tenant isolation must be enforced by trusted server-side controls rather than by tenant identifiers supplied by the client.","explanation":"A client may request a tenant identifier, but the system must derive or validate the caller's authorized tenant scope independently. Client-provided tenant metadata is input, not proof of authority.","principle":"Tenant identity and tenant authorization are distinct from tenant selection supplied by a caller.","rule":"Derive effective tenant scope from trusted identity and authorization state before accessing tenant-scoped resources.","objective":"Prevent cross-tenant data access caused by caller-controlled tenant selection.","requirements":["Trusted identity","Tenant authorization mapping","Server-side resource scoping","Cross-tenant tests"],"constraints":["Users may legitimately belong to multiple tenants","Administrative identities may have intentionally broader scope"],"failure_mode":"Changing a tenant identifier in a request returns another tenant's resource.","cause":"The server trusts the supplied tenant identifier without validating effective scope.","root_cause":"Tenant selection was incorrectly treated as authorization.","solution":"Resolve authorized tenant scope server-side and enforce it at every relevant data-access boundary.","alternative":"Use resource identifiers that encode or resolve tenant ownership independently of caller input.","tradeoff":"Explicit tenant checks can increase implementation complexity across repositories and services.","edge_case":"A platform administrator legitimately has cross-tenant access.","security_implication":"A single tenant-boundary failure can expose multiple customers rather than a single user's data.","test_case":"Authenticate under tenant A and request a known resource belonging to tenant B.","expected_test_result":"The request is denied unless the authenticated principal has explicitly authorized cross-tenant scope.","testing_guidance":"Test every API, background job, export path, cache, search endpoint, and administrative workflow.","verification_method":"Trace tenant scope from identity through authorization and storage access and execute cross-tenant negative tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B08-R02","D57-B32-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B33-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant isolation in shared caches","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why must shared caches include tenant security context when required?","answer":"A shared cache can leak one tenant's data to another if cache keys or authorization checks do not distinguish security boundaries.","explanation":"Caching changes the path by which data is retrieved. Even when the primary database query is correctly tenant-scoped, a cache lookup can bypass that isolation if the cache key or stored object does not encode the necessary context.","principle":"Every alternate data-access path must preserve the same security boundary as the authoritative store.","rule":"Include all security-relevant scope in cache identity or perform an independent authorization check before returning cached data.","objective":"Prevent cross-tenant data leakage through secondary storage paths.","requirements":["Security-aware cache keys","Tenant-aware invalidation","Authorization checks where required","Cross-tenant cache tests"],"constraints":["Not every cache object should necessarily contain tenant identifiers if an equivalent isolation boundary exists","Global shared objects may intentionally be tenant-independent"],"failure_mode":"Tenant B receives a cached response generated for tenant A.","cause":"The cache key identifies only the resource identifier and omits tenant scope.","root_cause":"Cache design was separated from the application's authorization model.","solution":"Bind cached objects to the effective security scope or enforce authorization before cache return.","alternative":"Use physically or logically isolated caches for tenant-sensitive data.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation can increase memory use and cache fragmentation.","edge_case":"A resource identifier is globally unique and tenant-independent.","security_implication":"Cache leakage can bypass otherwise correct database-level tenant isolation.","test_case":"Populate a cache under tenant A and request the same cache key under tenant B.","expected_test_result":"Tenant B cannot receive tenant A's tenant-scoped content.","testing_guidance":"Test cache hits, misses, invalidation, negative caching, serialization, and shared application instances.","verification_method":"Inspect cache identity and execute cross-tenant hit/miss scenarios.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B33-R01","D57-B32-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B33-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant isolation in search and indexing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What security lesson applies to shared search indexes?","answer":"Search filtering must be treated as part of authorization when indexed records contain tenant-scoped or access-controlled data.","explanation":"A database may enforce tenant isolation while a search index contains documents from multiple tenants. If queries omit security filters or the index's authorization model is incomplete, search can become an alternate disclosure path.","principle":"Indexes and derived data stores inherit the security requirements of the source data.","rule":"Apply authoritative access constraints to search results before disclosure.","objective":"Prevent derived-data paths from bypassing primary storage authorization.","requirements":["Security-aware indexing model","Tenant/access filters","Authorization validation","Search-specific negative tests"],"constraints":["Search engines may have different consistency and authorization semantics than the primary database","Some indexes intentionally contain public information"],"failure_mode":"A search query returns records outside the caller's authorized tenant or resource scope.","cause":"The index query lacks equivalent authorization constraints.","root_cause":"Derived data was not included in the security-boundary inventory.","solution":"Apply tenant and access controls within the search authorization model or filter results using authoritative policy before disclosure.","alternative":"Maintain separately isolated indexes for strongly isolated security domains.","tradeoff":"Additional authorization filtering can affect query complexity and result consistency.","edge_case":"A document changes from private to public or vice versa while an index is stale.","security_implication":"Search can disclose data even when the primary database would deny direct access.","test_case":"Index records from multiple tenants and query them using identities with different scopes.","expected_test_result":"Only authorized records are returned.","testing_guidance":"Test stale indexes, deleted records, access changes, autocomplete, snippets, aggregations, and faceting.","verification_method":"Trace authorization from source record through indexing and query-result generation.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B33-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B33-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant isolation in asynchronous jobs","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What security lesson applies when background jobs process tenant-scoped data?","answer":"Background jobs must carry explicit, integrity-protected tenant scope and must not infer scope from mutable global state or the worker's default identity.","explanation":"Workers often process tasks outside the original request context. If tenant context is missing, mutable, or implicitly inherited, a job can read or modify another tenant's data.","principle":"Asynchronous execution must preserve explicit security scope across the trust boundary.","rule":"Bind each tenant-scoped job to an authoritative tenant context and validate it before resource access.","objective":"Prevent cross-tenant execution caused by lost or corrupted asynchronous context.","requirements":["Explicit task scope","Integrity of task metadata","Tenant-aware authorization","Isolation tests"],"constraints":["Workers may process tasks for multiple tenants","Retries and dead-letter queues must preserve or safely reconstruct security context"],"failure_mode":"A worker executes a tenant A task using tenant B's current context.","cause":"Worker state is reused between tasks without resetting security scope.","root_cause":"Tenant context was treated as ambient process state.","solution":"Make tenant scope explicit in the task and derive effective authority from trusted task metadata and authorization policy.","alternative":"Use isolated worker pools for high-assurance tenant separation.","tradeoff":"Explicit context handling is more robust but requires careful propagation and lifecycle management.","edge_case":"A worker crashes after receiving a task and the task is retried by a different worker.","security_implication":"Context confusion can create cross-tenant reads or writes during retries.","test_case":"Process consecutive jobs for different tenants on the same worker and inspect effective security scope.","expected_test_result":"Each job uses only its own authorized tenant context.","testing_guidance":"Test retries, concurrent jobs, worker reuse, task deserialization, and dead-letter processing.","verification_method":"Trace tenant context lifecycle from enqueue through final resource access.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B32-R05","D57-B33-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B33-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Cross-tenant verification as a release criterion","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"Why should cross-tenant negative tests be release criteria for multi-tenant systems?","answer":"Tenant isolation is a boundary property that requires explicit negative verification; successful same-tenant tests do not demonstrate that cross-tenant access is impossible.","explanation":"Functional tests commonly verify that authorized users can access their own records. Security verification must additionally prove that equivalent identifiers, queries, exports, searches, and alternate paths do not expose other tenants' data.","principle":"Isolation requires evidence of denied cross-boundary access, not only successful in-boundary behavior.","rule":"Include representative cross-tenant negative tests in security verification for every relevant access path.","objective":"Detect regressions that weaken tenant isolation.","requirements":["Known multi-tenant fixtures","Cross-tenant identities","Representative access paths","Automated negative assertions"],"constraints":["Test data must avoid exposing real customer information","Coverage must account for derived and asynchronous paths"],"failure_mode":"A release passes functional tests but introduces cross-tenant access through a new endpoint.","cause":"Testing covers positive authorization but not boundary violations.","root_cause":"Tenant isolation was not represented as an explicit security invariant.","solution":"Define tenant isolation invariants and make negative tests part of release verification.","alternative":"Use formal policy checks supplemented by targeted integration tests.","tradeoff":"Broader isolation tests increase test maintenance but provide stronger regression detection.","edge_case":"An administrator is intentionally authorized across tenants and could invalidate naive negative assertions.","security_implication":"Incorrect test identities can create false confidence or false failures.","test_case":"Attempt equivalent resource access across two tenant identities through every supported access path.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized cross-tenant access is consistently denied.","testing_guidance":"Separate ordinary tenant identities from explicitly authorized cross-tenant administrative identities.","verification_method":"Run automated cross-tenant authorization and data-isolation regression suites.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B33-R01","D57-B33-R02","D57-B33-R03","D57-B33-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B34-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"File and Resource Boundaries","concept":"Path traversal prevention","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What is the core lesson for file paths derived from untrusted input?","answer":"A file path supplied or influenced by an untrusted party must not be allowed to escape its intended filesystem boundary.","explanation":"String-based prefix checks are insufficient because path normalization, alternate separators, symbolic links, encoding, and filesystem semantics can change the actual target. Security validation must correspond to the resolved resource boundary.","principle":"Authorize the effective resource, not merely the textual path representation.","rule":"Resolve and constrain untrusted paths against an authoritative allowed root using platform-appropriate filesystem semantics.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized filesystem access through path manipulation.","requirements":["Defined allowed root","Canonical or resolved path evaluation","Symlink policy","Negative path tests"],"constraints":["Filesystem semantics differ by operating system","Some applications intentionally permit controlled links or mounts"],"failure_mode":"An attacker-controlled path resolves outside the intended directory.","cause":"Validation checks only the input string or uses an unsafe prefix comparison.","root_cause":"Resource authorization was performed on representation rather than effective filesystem target.","solution":"Resolve the path using trusted filesystem operations and verify that the effective target remains within the authorized boundary.","alternative":"Use opaque resource identifiers mapped server-side to approved filesystem objects.","tradeoff":"Secure path resolution can be more complex than direct path concatenation.","edge_case":"A path initially resolves inside the root but a symbolic link points outside it.","security_implication":"Path traversal can expose, overwrite, or delete unauthorized files.","test_case":"Supply traversal components, alternate path representations, and links that target outside the permitted root.","expected_test_result":"Every unauthorized effective target is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Test normalization, symbolic links, absolute paths, platform-specific separators, encoded input, and race-sensitive cases.","verification_method":"Inspect resource-resolution logic and execute filesystem-boundary negative tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B21-R02","D57-B33-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B34-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"File and Resource Boundaries","concept":"Archive extraction boundary enforcement","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when extracting untrusted archives?","answer":"Every archive entry must be checked so its effective extraction target remains within the intended destination and does not exploit path traversal or unintended filesystem links.","explanation":"Archive formats can contain paths that appear relative but resolve outside the extraction directory. Archive extraction therefore creates a resource-boundary problem similar to direct filesystem path handling.","principle":"Archive extraction must enforce the destination boundary per entry before materializing files.","rule":"Validate each archive member's effective target and apply explicit policies for links, special files, and duplicate paths.","objective":"Prevent archive contents from writing outside the authorized extraction area.","requirements":["Per-entry path validation","Link handling policy","Special-file policy","Extraction tests"],"constraints":["Archive formats differ","Some legitimate archives contain links or unusual metadata"],"failure_mode":"An archive entry writes to a location outside the intended extraction directory.","cause":"The extractor trusts archive member names or link metadata.","root_cause":"Archive content was treated as trusted filesystem structure.","solution":"Apply destination-boundary validation before extraction and reject unsupported or unsafe entry types.","alternative":"Extract into an isolated temporary environment and move only validated outputs into the final destination.","tradeoff":"Isolation can add storage and processing requirements but reduces direct filesystem impact.","edge_case":"A malicious archive contains duplicate names where an earlier safe entry is replaced by a later unsafe entry.","security_implication":"Archive extraction can become arbitrary file write or overwrite behavior.","test_case":"Provide archive entries containing traversal paths, absolute paths, links, and duplicate names.","expected_test_result":"Unsafe entries are rejected without writing outside the allowed destination.","testing_guidance":"Test all supported archive formats and entry types rather than only ordinary files.","verification_method":"Inspect extraction policy and run adversarial archive fixtures in an isolated test environment.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B34-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B34-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"File and Resource Boundaries","concept":"Temporary-file security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies to temporary files containing sensitive data?","answer":"Temporary files should have controlled permissions, predictable ownership semantics, bounded lifetime, and safe creation mechanisms that prevent unauthorized access or name collisions.","explanation":"Temporary artifacts may contain credentials, personal data, source code, or intermediate security-sensitive information. Weak creation semantics or excessive lifetime can turn temporary storage into an unintended exposure path.","principle":"Temporary storage is still a security-relevant data store.","rule":"Create sensitive temporary files using secure exclusive creation and enforce appropriate permissions and cleanup semantics.","objective":"Reduce exposure through temporary filesystem artifacts.","requirements":["Controlled permissions","Exclusive creation where needed","Defined cleanup","Sensitive-data classification"],"constraints":["Cleanup may fail after crashes","Some operating systems provide different temporary-file primitives"],"failure_mode":"Another process accesses or replaces a temporary sensitive file.","cause":"Predictable names, permissive permissions, or unsafe creation semantics.","root_cause":"Temporary files were treated as disposable rather than security-sensitive resources.","solution":"Use secure operating-system primitives, restrictive permissions, and bounded retention.","alternative":"Use memory or controlled application-managed storage when appropriate.","tradeoff":"Stronger temporary-file handling can complicate interoperability with external tools.","edge_case":"A crash leaves a sensitive temporary file behind.","security_implication":"Residual temporary files can expose sensitive information after the primary operation ends.","test_case":"Inspect temporary-file permissions, ownership, name collision behavior, and cleanup after normal and abnormal termination.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized processes cannot access or replace protected temporary artifacts, and retention follows policy.","testing_guidance":"Test crashes, concurrent creation, cleanup failure, process termination, and filesystem permission differences.","verification_method":"Inspect file-creation primitives and execute permission, collision, and lifecycle tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B09-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B34-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"File and Resource Boundaries","concept":"Symlink and time-of-check-to-time-of-use risks","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can checking a filesystem path before using it still be insufficient?","answer":"A path or filesystem object can change between validation and use, so a security check performed on one state may not describe the resource actually operated on.","explanation":"Filesystem operations can race with other processes. Symbolic links, renames, mounts, or permission changes can alter the effective object between a check and a later operation.","principle":"Security-sensitive resource validation must account for state changes between check and use.","rule":"Prefer atomic or descriptor-based filesystem operations where supported instead of separate check-then-use sequences.","objective":"Reduce race conditions that bypass filesystem security checks.","requirements":["Threat model for concurrent modification","Atomic or race-resistant operations","Controlled test environment","Filesystem-specific verification"],"constraints":["Available primitives vary by platform and filesystem","Not every race can be eliminated purely at application level"],"failure_mode":"A validated safe path is replaced or redirected before the privileged operation executes.","cause":"Separate validation and use operations create a race window.","root_cause":"The security control assumes filesystem state remains unchanged between operations.","solution":"Use race-resistant operating-system primitives and minimize the interval between security decision and resource use.","alternative":"Perform sensitive operations inside an isolated filesystem namespace or controlled service.","tradeoff":"Race-resistant APIs can be more platform-specific and complex.","edge_case":"An application performs validation in one process and the actual operation in another.","security_implication":"A path validation control can provide false assurance if the resource can change before use.","test_case":"Attempt controlled concurrent path replacement between validation and operation.","expected_test_result":"The protected operation does not act on an unauthorized resource.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency fixtures where possible and test symbolic-link replacement and rename races.","verification_method":"Review check/use sequences and verify use of atomic or descriptor-based primitives where applicable.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B34-R01","D57-B34-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B34-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"File and Resource Boundaries","concept":"Resource ownership across lifecycle boundaries","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What security lesson applies when ownership of a file or resource changes between components?","answer":"Security authority should remain explicit when a resource moves between processes, services, workers, or lifecycle stages; physical possession does not by itself establish authorization.","explanation":"A temporary artifact, uploaded file, generated report, or queue payload may move across components. Each transition can create an opportunity for unauthorized access if ownership metadata is missing or trusted without verification.","principle":"Resource transfer must preserve or deliberately redefine authorization semantics.","rule":"Model ownership and access scope as explicit security metadata and verify them at each trust boundary.","objective":"Prevent privilege changes caused by resource movement between components.","requirements":["Resource ownership model","Transfer semantics","Boundary authorization","Lifecycle tests"],"constraints":["Some resources intentionally become shared or public","Ownership may change as part of a workflow"],"failure_mode":"A downstream component treats possession of an artifact as permission to access or modify it.","cause":"Resource ownership is inferred from transport or storage location.","root_cause":"Lifecycle transitions lack explicit authorization semantics.","solution":"Attach authoritative resource identity and scope to the lifecycle and verify authorization at each sensitive transition.","alternative":"Use capability-based references with narrowly scoped rights.","tradeoff":"Explicit ownership metadata adds design and validation complexity.","edge_case":"A resource is intentionally transferred from one tenant or user to another.","security_implication":"Implicit ownership can create unauthorized disclosure or modification after workflow transitions.","test_case":"Transfer a resource across components and attempt access using both previous and new principals.","expected_test_result":"Access follows the documented transfer policy rather than physical possession.","testing_guidance":"Test uploads, generated artifacts, queues, temporary storage, exports, and cleanup workflows.","verification_method":"Trace ownership state across every lifecycle transition and execute unauthorized-transfer tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B33-R04","D57-B09-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B35-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Dependency provenance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when software depends on external packages?","answer":"Security assurance for a dependency includes knowing which artifact was selected, where it came from, how it was resolved, and whether the project has a mechanism for responding to compromised or vulnerable dependencies.","explanation":"A package name alone does not fully identify the code incorporated into a build. Dependency provenance connects declared requirements, resolved versions, artifacts, and build inputs.","principle":"Software supply-chain security requires traceability from declared dependency to consumed artifact.","rule":"Maintain machine-readable dependency resolution and provenance appropriate to the build environment.","objective":"Make dependency composition auditable and support controlled response to supply-chain issues.","requirements":["Dependency manifest","Resolved dependency graph","Artifact identity","Build traceability"],"constraints":["Package ecosystems differ in metadata and provenance capabilities","Transitive dependencies can change the effective dependency graph"],"failure_mode":"A project cannot determine which exact dependency artifact entered a released build.","cause":"Only broad version requirements were retained.","root_cause":"Dependency provenance was not treated as part of the software artifact identity.","solution":"Record resolved versions and artifact identity and connect them to reproducible build metadata where feasible.","alternative":"Use controlled internal mirrors or curated dependency sets.","tradeoff":"More provenance data increases build and release metadata requirements.","edge_case":"A dependency is retrieved from multiple sources with identical version labels but different artifact content.","security_implication":"Ambiguous provenance makes compromise investigation and remediation harder.","test_case":"Build the same dependency specification under controlled conditions and compare resolved artifacts and metadata.","expected_test_result":"The consumed dependency set is identifiable and traceable according to the defined provenance policy.","testing_guidance":"Test direct and transitive dependencies and dependency-source changes.","verification_method":"Inspect lockfiles, build manifests, artifact metadata, and dependency resolution records.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B02-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B35-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Transitive dependency exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should security lessons include transitive dependencies?","answer":"A project can execute or package code from transitive dependencies even when those dependencies were not declared directly by the application.","explanation":"Dependency graphs extend beyond top-level packages. A vulnerability or compromise in a transitive component can therefore affect the resulting software even when developers never imported that component directly.","principle":"The effective software supply chain is the resolved dependency graph, not only the direct dependency list.","rule":"Assess and track transitive dependencies as part of dependency security management.","objective":"Prevent blind spots caused by limiting security review to direct dependencies.","requirements":["Resolved dependency graph","Transitive dependency visibility","Vulnerability response process","Removal or upgrade capability"],"constraints":["Large graphs can produce substantial maintenance work","Not every reported vulnerability is exploitable in every use context"],"failure_mode":"A vulnerable transitive package remains embedded because it was absent from the direct dependency inventory.","cause":"Security inventory tracks only direct dependencies.","root_cause":"The dependency graph was modeled incompletely.","solution":"Generate and retain the full resolved dependency graph and assess affected transitive components.","alternative":"Minimize dependency depth and vendor critical components where justified.","tradeoff":"Dependency reduction can increase maintenance burden and reduce reuse.","edge_case":"A vulnerable transitive dependency is present but unreachable in the deployed feature set.","security_implication":"Inventory alone does not establish exploitability; affected-code reachability and usage context still require analysis.","test_case":"Introduce a known vulnerable transitive component and verify that dependency analysis identifies it.","expected_test_result":"The component appears in the resolved dependency inventory and enters the defined security assessment process.","testing_guidance":"Separate detection of presence from determination of exploitability.","verification_method":"Compare direct manifests against the complete resolved dependency graph.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B35-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B35-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Dependency pinning versus secure freshness","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson should distinguish dependency pinning from dependency freshness?","answer":"Pinning improves reproducibility and reduces uncontrolled version drift, while remaining on an old pinned version can preserve known vulnerabilities; secure dependency management therefore requires both controlled selection and an update process.","explanation":"A fixed version is not inherently secure. Security requires a mechanism that identifies when the selected version should change and verifies the resulting update.","principle":"Reproducibility and security freshness are complementary controls, not substitutes.","rule":"Pin or otherwise constrain dependency resolution while maintaining a documented vulnerability and update process.","objective":"Avoid both uncontrolled dependency changes and indefinite retention of insecure versions.","requirements":["Controlled dependency resolution","Version review process","Security advisory monitoring","Verification after updates"],"constraints":["Update cadence depends on project risk and compatibility","Some updates can introduce breaking changes"],"failure_mode":"A dependency remains pinned indefinitely despite a relevant security correction.","cause":"Reproducibility is treated as a reason to prohibit updates.","root_cause":"Dependency lifecycle lacks security-driven review criteria.","solution":"Maintain controlled versions with explicit security-update procedures and regression verification.","alternative":"Use bounded version ranges combined with lockfile generation and continuous review.","tradeoff":"More frequent updates can increase compatibility testing while reducing exposure to known issues.","edge_case":"A security update changes behavior relied upon by the application.","security_implication":"Security patching without compatibility verification can create new defects, while avoiding the patch preserves the original exposure.","test_case":"Mark a pinned dependency as requiring a security update and verify the project's update workflow.","expected_test_result":"The dependency enters an explicit review and controlled update path.","testing_guidance":"Test security updates separately from routine dependency updates.","verification_method":"Inspect dependency policy and trace a security-driven version change through build and regression verification.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B35-R01","D57-B35-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B35-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Build artifact integrity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"What should be verified about software artifacts before deployment?","answer":"The deployment process should establish that the artifact being deployed is the intended artifact produced by the authorized build process and has not been substituted or corrupted in transit.","explanation":"Source review and build tests do not by themselves prove that the deployed bytes correspond to the reviewed and authorized build output. Artifact identity and integrity connect build-time assurance to deployment-time reality.","principle":"Security verification must cover the artifact boundary between build and deployment.","rule":"Use an appropriate artifact identity and integrity mechanism and verify it at deployment according to the trust model.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized artifact substitution between build and execution.","requirements":["Artifact identity","Authorized build source","Integrity verification","Deployment verification evidence"],"constraints":["Verification mechanisms vary by deployment environment","A valid signature does not by itself prove the artifact is safe or correctly configured"],"failure_mode":"A deployment system runs an artifact different from the one produced or approved by the intended build process.","cause":"Deployment does not verify artifact identity or integrity.","root_cause":"Build assurance and deployment assurance are disconnected.","solution":"Bind artifact identity to the build output and verify integrity and authorization at deployment.","alternative":"Use a trusted artifact repository with controlled promotion between environments.","tradeoff":"Additional verification adds release-process complexity but strengthens supply-chain traceability.","edge_case":"The same version label is reused for different artifact bytes.","security_implication":"Version labels alone are insufficient to establish artifact identity.","test_case":"Attempt to deploy a modified artifact under the expected version identifier.","expected_test_result":"Deployment rejects the artifact when its identity or integrity does not satisfy policy.","testing_guidance":"Test artifact replacement, repository compromise scenarios, metadata mismatch, and rollback artifacts.","verification_method":"Compare deployment-time artifact identity against the authorized build record.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B35-R01","D57-B05-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B35-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Security","concept":"Security response to dependency compromise","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"What information should a security lesson preserve when a dependency may be compromised?","answer":"Preserve the affected dependency identity, versions, artifact provenance, affected builds or releases, exposure analysis, containment actions, verification evidence, and residual uncertainty.","explanation":"A dependency compromise is not resolved merely by upgrading a package. The organization needs to determine where the affected artifact was consumed and whether the application actually used the impacted functionality.","principle":"Supply-chain response requires traceability from dependency identity to deployed artifacts and affected behavior.","rule":"Correlate dependency inventories with build and deployment records before declaring exposure resolved.","objective":"Make dependency compromise response evidence-driven and auditable.","requirements":["Dependency inventory","Build-to-artifact mapping","Deployment inventory","Remediation verification","Residual-risk record"],"constraints":["Historical provenance may be incomplete","Compromise scope may remain uncertain when build records are insufficient"],"failure_mode":"A team upgrades the dependency but cannot establish which previously deployed artifacts contained the affected component.","cause":"Dependency-to-artifact traceability was missing.","root_cause":"Supply-chain provenance was not retained across the release lifecycle.","solution":"Maintain dependency, build, artifact, and deployment relationships and use them during incident analysis.","alternative":"Rebuild and redeploy all potentially affected artifacts when precise historical scope cannot be established and policy permits.","tradeoff":"Broad rebuilds can increase operational effort but may reduce uncertainty when historical evidence is incomplete.","edge_case":"A vulnerable dependency was present but never reachable by deployed code.","security_implication":"Presence, exploitability, and observed compromise are separate claims and must not be conflated.","test_case":"Trace a dependency identifier from resolved graph through build and deployment records.","expected_test_result":"Potentially affected artifacts can be identified or the missing provenance is explicitly recorded as uncertainty.","testing_guidance":"Record what is known, inferred, and unknown rather than assuming remediation proves historical safety.","verification_method":"Reconcile dependency inventory, artifact metadata, deployment records, and remediation evidence.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B02-R05","D57-B35-R01","D57-B35-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B36-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Configuration","concept":"Secure defaults","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why are secure defaults an important security lesson?","answer":"Systems are safer when their default configuration minimizes unnecessary exposure and requires deliberate action to enable higher-risk capabilities.","explanation":"Optional security controls that default to disabled can be forgotten during deployment. Secure defaults reduce reliance on perfect configuration knowledge and make the safer state the ordinary state.","principle":"The default configuration should provide the minimum exposure compatible with intended operation.","rule":"Disable unnecessary privileged, administrative, debug, and externally exposed capabilities by default.","objective":"Reduce security exposure caused by configuration omission.","requirements":["Defined baseline configuration","Explicit enablement for exceptional capabilities","Configuration verification"],"constraints":["Some development or integration environments may require less restrictive settings","Defaults must remain usable for the supported deployment model"],"failure_mode":"A new deployment exposes a sensitive capability because it was enabled by default.","cause":"The default configuration assumes operators will disable the capability manually.","root_cause":"Security-sensitive options were designed around configuration perfection rather than safe failure.","solution":"Adopt secure defaults and require explicit configuration to enable exceptional capabilities.","alternative":"Use deployment-specific policy validation to reject insecure configurations.","tradeoff":"More restrictive defaults can require additional setup for legitimate advanced use cases.","edge_case":"A capability is required for a supported product mode but dangerous in another mode.","security_implication":"Mode-dependent defaults can create inconsistent security boundaries if mode selection is not authenticated or validated.","test_case":"Deploy with no optional security configuration and inspect externally reachable and privileged capabilities.","expected_test_result":"Only capabilities explicitly intended by the default security baseline are enabled.","testing_guidance":"Test fresh installations, upgrades, reset-to-default behavior, and omitted configuration values.","verification_method":"Compare effective runtime configuration with the documented secure baseline.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R03","D57-B06-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B36-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Configuration","concept":"Fail-closed configuration parsing","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when a security-critical configuration value is invalid or missing?","answer":"For security-critical controls, invalid or ambiguous configuration should generally prevent the protected operation rather than silently selecting a permissive fallback.","explanation":"Configuration parsing is part of the security boundary when values determine authentication, authorization, network exposure, encryption, or other controls. Silent fallback can convert configuration errors into security exposure.","principle":"Security-critical configuration errors should not silently weaken the security policy.","rule":"Define explicit failure behavior for missing, malformed, conflicting, or unsupported security configuration.","objective":"Prevent configuration mistakes from creating unintended security states.","requirements":["Configuration schema","Validation rules","Explicit failure policy","Startup or runtime verification"],"constraints":["Some availability-critical systems require carefully designed degraded modes","Not every configuration field is security-critical"],"failure_mode":"A malformed security setting causes the application to use a permissive default.","cause":"Parser treats invalid input as equivalent to omission.","root_cause":"Security significance of configuration values was not modeled.","solution":"Classify security-critical settings and reject invalid or ambiguous values where safe degradation would weaken security.","alternative":"Use a pre-deployment configuration validator that blocks unsafe configurations.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior can reduce availability when operators make configuration mistakes.","edge_case":"A security feature depends on an external policy service that is temporarily unavailable.","security_implication":"Fallback behavior can become an implicit bypass if it is not explicitly governed.","test_case":"Remove, corrupt, or conflict security-critical configuration values.","expected_test_result":"The system follows the documented secure failure behavior and does not silently weaken protection.","testing_guidance":"Test missing values, malformed values, conflicting settings, unknown fields, and external configuration failures.","verification_method":"Inspect configuration validation and execute invalid-configuration tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R02","D57-B06-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B36-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Configuration","concept":"Configuration drift as a security risk","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why should configuration drift be treated as a security lesson?","answer":"A system can become insecure after deployment when runtime configuration diverges from the approved security baseline.","explanation":"Security verification performed only at initial deployment does not establish that configuration remains unchanged. Manual changes, environment differences, emergency settings, and partial rollouts can introduce drift.","principle":"Security configuration is a continuously changing control state, not a one-time deployment property.","rule":"Detect and assess security-relevant configuration changes against an authoritative baseline.","objective":"Identify security exposure introduced after initial verification.","requirements":["Versioned baseline","Configuration inventory","Drift detection","Change attribution"],"constraints":["Some configuration is intentionally dynamic","Detection frequency should match risk and change rate"],"failure_mode":"A security control is disabled manually and remains disabled without detection.","cause":"Runtime configuration is not compared with an approved baseline.","root_cause":"Configuration security was treated as static deployment metadata.","solution":"Track effective security configuration and detect unauthorized or unexplained changes.","alternative":"Enforce immutable infrastructure or policy-controlled configuration where feasible.","tradeoff":"Continuous drift detection adds operational telemetry and reconciliation requirements.","edge_case":"An emergency security response temporarily changes a configuration and requires later restoration.","security_implication":"Emergency changes can become permanent exposure if their lifecycle is not tracked.","test_case":"Modify a protected configuration outside the approved change mechanism.","expected_test_result":"The change is detected, attributed, or blocked according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Test manual changes, partial deployments, restarts, environment overrides, and rollback behavior.","verification_method":"Compare effective configuration against the versioned security baseline and inspect change provenance.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B36-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B36-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Configuration","concept":"Debug and diagnostic mode exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies to debug or diagnostic modes in deployed systems?","answer":"Diagnostic modes can change error handling, logging, endpoints, data exposure, or administrative behavior and therefore must be explicitly classified and controlled in production environments.","explanation":"Debug functionality often provides information or capabilities that are useful during development but inappropriate for untrusted environments. The security impact depends on the exact behavior changed by the mode.","principle":"A configuration mode is a security-relevant behavior change when it alters trust boundaries or exposed information.","rule":"Require explicit production-safe configuration and verification of diagnostic features.","objective":"Prevent development-oriented diagnostics from becoming production exposure.","requirements":["Mode classification","Production configuration baseline","Diagnostic endpoint inventory","Negative tests"],"constraints":["Operational troubleshooting may require temporary diagnostics","Emergency enablement needs controlled authorization and expiry"],"failure_mode":"A production system exposes verbose errors or administrative diagnostics because a development flag remains enabled.","cause":"Debug mode is enabled by default or inherited from deployment configuration.","root_cause":"Diagnostic features were not included in the production security baseline.","solution":"Disable unsafe diagnostics by default and govern temporary enablement through controlled change procedures.","alternative":"Expose narrowly scoped diagnostics through authenticated administrative channels.","tradeoff":"Restricted diagnostics can make troubleshooting slower but reduce information disclosure.","edge_case":"A temporary diagnostic capability must remain active during incident investigation.","security_implication":"Temporary diagnostic exposure can outlive the incident if not automatically expired or reverted.","test_case":"Deploy with production configuration and inspect diagnostic endpoints, errors, logs, and administrative behaviors.","expected_test_result":"Unsafe development diagnostics are unavailable or appropriately protected.","testing_guidance":"Test both default deployment and emergency diagnostic activation/deactivation.","verification_method":"Review effective configuration and execute production-mode security checks.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B36-R01","D57-B36-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B36-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Configuration","concept":"Configuration provenance and change attribution","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"Why should security-relevant configuration changes have provenance?","answer":"A security configuration change should be attributable to an authorized source, change event, and effective version so that its legitimacy and security impact can be evaluated.","explanation":"Knowing that a setting changed is less useful if the system cannot establish who or what changed it, why it changed, and which effective configuration was active during a security event.","principle":"Security configuration should be both observable and attributable.","rule":"Record sufficient provenance for security-relevant configuration changes to reconstruct effective security state.","objective":"Support investigation, rollback, accountability, and lesson extraction from configuration changes.","requirements":["Change identity","Actor or automation identity","Before/after state or equivalent diff","Effective timestamp","Authorization context"],"constraints":["Sensitive configuration values should not be exposed in audit records","Some secrets require metadata-only provenance"],"failure_mode":"A security control changed but investigators cannot determine when, why, or through which authorized mechanism.","cause":"Configuration changes are not tracked with provenance.","root_cause":"Configuration was treated as untracked mutable state.","solution":"Record security-relevant configuration provenance while protecting secret values.","alternative":"Use immutable configuration artifacts with deployment-level provenance.","tradeoff":"Detailed provenance increases storage and governance requirements.","edge_case":"A configuration contains secrets that cannot safely be stored in plaintext audit logs.","security_implication":"Insufficient provenance impairs reconstruction of security state while excessive logging can itself expose secrets.","test_case":"Change a security-relevant setting through an approved mechanism and verify that its provenance is recorded without exposing secret material.","expected_test_result":"The change is attributable and reconstructable while sensitive values remain protected.","testing_guidance":"Test automated changes, manual changes, failed changes, rollbacks, and emergency changes.","verification_method":"Inspect configuration audit records and reconcile them with effective runtime state.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B36-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B37-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit and Security Telemetry","concept":"Security event semantics","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What makes a security audit event useful for later investigation?","answer":"A useful security event records enough structured context to distinguish who or what acted, what was attempted, which resource or policy was involved, what decision occurred, and when the event occurred.","explanation":"A large volume of unstructured logs does not necessarily provide evidence for reconstructing security decisions. Event semantics should be designed around questions investigators must answer.","principle":"Security telemetry should capture decision-relevant facts rather than merely application activity.","rule":"Define security events around actors, actions, targets, decisions, outcomes, and relevant context.","objective":"Enable reliable reconstruction of security-sensitive behavior.","requirements":["Stable event taxonomy","Actor identity","Action and target","Decision or outcome","Time semantics"],"constraints":["Do not log unnecessary sensitive data","Distributed systems require consistent event identifiers or correlation mechanisms"],"failure_mode":"An audit trail shows that a request occurred but cannot establish what resource was accessed or whether authorization succeeded.","cause":"Events capture generic request activity without security semantics.","root_cause":"Audit logging was designed for debugging rather than security evidence.","solution":"Define structured security event schemas tied to security decisions and outcomes.","alternative":"Enrich generic application logs with a separate security audit stream.","tradeoff":"Richer telemetry increases storage and governance requirements.","edge_case":"An authorization decision is made in one service and enforced in another.","security_implication":"Incomplete event semantics can prevent reliable incident reconstruction.","test_case":"Perform a sensitive operation across multiple components and reconstruct the security decision from telemetry.","expected_test_result":"The relevant actor, action, target, decision, and outcome can be distinguished without unsupported inference.","testing_guidance":"Test successful, denied, failed, retried, and partially completed security actions.","verification_method":"Review event schema against required investigation questions and perform controlled reconstruction tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R05","D57-B07-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B37-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit and Security Telemetry","concept":"Audit log integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is audit-log integrity separate from audit-log completeness?","answer":"A complete log is not reliable evidence if unauthorized parties can alter, delete, reorder, or selectively suppress its records.","explanation":"Security telemetry has evidentiary value only to the extent that its integrity and retention properties match the investigation requirements. Integrity controls may include restricted write access, append-oriented storage, cryptographic mechanisms, replication, or external collection.","principle":"Security evidence must be protected against unauthorized modification appropriate to its threat model.","rule":"Define and verify integrity, access, retention, and tamper-detection properties for security audit records.","objective":"Preserve trustworthy evidence for security analysis and accountability.","requirements":["Restricted write access","Integrity protection","Retention policy","Failure monitoring"],"constraints":["No single integrity mechanism is sufficient for every threat model","Logs may contain sensitive information requiring separate access controls"],"failure_mode":"An attacker who compromises an application can silently modify its local security logs.","cause":"Application and audit records share the same trust boundary and write authority.","root_cause":"Audit evidence was not modeled as a separate security asset.","solution":"Separate collection or protection of audit records from the component generating them and monitor integrity failures.","alternative":"Use append-only or externally collected audit storage where appropriate.","tradeoff":"External collection adds infrastructure and operational dependencies.","edge_case":"Telemetry transport is unavailable during an incident.","security_implication":"Failure to define evidence-preservation behavior can create gaps exactly when evidence is most needed.","test_case":"Attempt unauthorized modification or deletion of audit records under a controlled test account.","expected_test_result":"The modification is prevented, detected, or preserved according to the defined audit-integrity policy.","testing_guidance":"Test local compromise assumptions, transport failure, retention expiration, and clock inconsistencies.","verification_method":"Review audit storage permissions and integrity controls and execute controlled tamper tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R01","D57-B37-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B37-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit and Security Telemetry","concept":"Security telemetry failure handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What security lesson applies when security telemetry collection fails?","answer":"The system should have an explicit policy for whether protected actions continue, degrade, queue, or fail when required security telemetry cannot be recorded.","explanation":"Telemetry can be operationally important or evidentiary, but treating every logging failure as equivalent can either weaken security or unnecessarily stop critical services. The required behavior depends on the control's purpose and threat model.","principle":"Telemetry availability requirements must be derived from the security function the telemetry supports.","rule":"Classify security telemetry as required for authorization, detection, audit, or observability and define failure behavior for each class.","objective":"Avoid accidental security degradation when telemetry infrastructure fails.","requirements":["Telemetry classification","Failure policy","Detection of collection gaps","Recovery and reconciliation"],"constraints":["Fail-closed logging can affect availability","Some non-security diagnostics can safely be dropped"],"failure_mode":"A security control continues silently after required evidence collection becomes unavailable.","cause":"Logging is treated as optional infrastructure despite being part of the security control.","root_cause":"Telemetry dependency semantics were undefined.","solution":"Define critical telemetry dependencies and explicit behavior for collection failure.","alternative":"Buffer security events locally when safe and reconcile them after recovery.","tradeoff":"Buffering requires secure local storage and bounded capacity.","edge_case":"A critical operation must continue during a temporary telemetry outage.","security_implication":"Unrecorded high-impact actions can create blind spots and weaken accountability.","test_case":"Disable required telemetry collection and attempt protected actions.","expected_test_result":"The system follows the documented failure policy and records the telemetry gap itself where possible.","testing_guidance":"Test transport failure, storage exhaustion, process restart, and recovery reconciliation.","verification_method":"Inspect dependency policy and execute telemetry-failure scenarios against protected operations.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B37-R01","D57-B37-R02","D57-B06-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B37-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit and Security Telemetry","concept":"Detection coverage versus alert volume","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why is alert count insufficient as a security detection lesson?","answer":"The number of alerts does not directly measure whether important attack paths or security failures are detectable.","explanation":"A system can generate many low-value alerts while missing a critical event, or generate few alerts because its detection strategy is selective. Security learning should evaluate coverage of defined threats and the evidence needed to support investigation.","principle":"Detection quality is about meaningful coverage and actionable evidence, not raw event or alert volume.","rule":"Evaluate detection against defined threat scenarios, required signals, and investigation needs.","objective":"Avoid optimizing security monitoring for activity volume instead of meaningful detection capability.","requirements":["Threat coverage model","Detection conditions","Evidence requirements","Validation scenarios"],"constraints":["Complete detection coverage is generally not achievable","Detection rules may intentionally trade sensitivity for noise control"],"failure_mode":"A monitoring system is declared effective because it generates many alerts despite missing a defined security scenario.","cause":"Alert volume is used as the primary security metric.","root_cause":"Detection objectives were not connected to explicit threat coverage.","solution":"Measure detection against scenario coverage, evidence quality, and documented limitations.","alternative":"Use multiple complementary measures including detection gaps and investigation completeness.","tradeoff":"Coverage-oriented measurement requires more scenario modeling and validation.","edge_case":"A detection rule is intentionally silent for low-risk activity to reduce noise.","security_implication":"Low alert volume may represent either good selectivity or a detection gap; context is required.","test_case":"Inject controlled security scenarios and determine whether required evidence and detections occur.","expected_test_result":"Detection performance is assessed against the predefined scenario rather than alert count alone.","testing_guidance":"Include known-positive, known-negative, ambiguous, and telemetry-missing scenarios.","verification_method":"Map detections to threat scenarios and execute controlled detection validation tests.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B07-R05","D57-B37-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B37-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Audit and Security Telemetry","concept":"Security metric interpretation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"What should a security lesson record before interpreting a security metric?","answer":"The metric's definition, population, time window, collection completeness, denominator, and known blind spots should be established before drawing conclusions from it.","explanation":"Metrics such as incident counts, detection rates, remediation counts, or authentication failures can be misleading when their population or collection quality changes. A change in measurement can look like a change in security reality.","principle":"A security metric is evidence about a defined measurement process, not a direct measurement of the entire security state.","rule":"Attach scope and measurement-quality metadata to security metrics before using them for learning or decisions.","objective":"Prevent false security conclusions caused by incomplete or changing measurement.","requirements":["Metric definition","Population and time window","Collection completeness","Known limitations"],"constraints":["Some security states cannot be directly measured","Historical comparisons may become invalid after instrumentation changes"],"failure_mode":"A reduction in alerts is interpreted as reduced security risk without checking whether telemetry coverage changed.","cause":"Metric context is omitted from interpretation.","root_cause":"Measurement process and security state are treated as identical.","solution":"Record metric semantics, data quality, and instrumentation changes alongside the measurement.","alternative":"Use multiple independent indicators and scenario validation.","tradeoff":"More contextual metadata makes reporting more complex but improves interpretability.","edge_case":"A detection system is intentionally reconfigured to reduce duplicate alerts.","security_implication":"A metric improvement can reflect instrumentation changes rather than an actual reduction in security events.","test_case":"Change telemetry coverage while keeping simulated security activity constant and compare reported metrics.","expected_test_result":"The system identifies the measurement change rather than treating the metric difference as proof of improved security.","testing_guidance":"Record collection gaps and instrumentation changes as first-class metadata.","verification_method":"Audit metric definitions and reconcile metric changes with telemetry configuration changes.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R03","D57-B37-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B38-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Key Lifecycle","concept":"Key lifecycle management","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies to cryptographic keys?","answer":"Cryptographic keys require lifecycle controls covering generation, activation, use, rotation or replacement, revocation where applicable, archival, and destruction according to their purpose and threat model.","explanation":"A cryptographic algorithm can be correctly implemented while the overall system remains insecure because keys are generated, stored, used, or retired incorrectly. Key lifecycle is therefore part of cryptographic security rather than an administrative afterthought.","principle":"Cryptographic security depends on both algorithmic correctness and key lifecycle control.","rule":"Define key lifecycle states and authorized transitions for every security-relevant key class.","objective":"Prevent long-lived, exposed, misused, or improperly retired keys from weakening cryptographic protections.","requirements":["Key inventory","Lifecycle states","Access controls","Rotation or replacement policy","Retirement semantics"],"constraints":["Different key types have different lifecycle requirements","Rotation may require compatibility or re-encryption planning"],"failure_mode":"A key remains active after its intended security lifetime or role has ended.","cause":"No explicit key retirement process exists.","root_cause":"Keys are treated as configuration values rather than security assets.","solution":"Model keys as managed security assets with explicit lifecycle states and access policies.","alternative":"Use a managed key service with lifecycle controls appropriate to the threat model.","tradeoff":"Centralized key management can improve control but introduces service dependencies.","edge_case":"A key must remain available temporarily to decrypt historical data while no longer being used for new encryption.","security_implication":"Retirement must distinguish cessation of new use from required historical decryption.","test_case":"Transition a key through each defined lifecycle state and verify allowed and prohibited operations.","expected_test_result":"Operations match the documented lifecycle policy for the key state.","testing_guidance":"Test rotation, retirement, recovery, access revocation, and historical-data handling.","verification_method":"Inspect key lifecycle policy and execute controlled state-transition tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B09-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B38-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Key Lifecycle","concept":"Key separation and purpose binding","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should cryptographic keys have explicit purposes?","answer":"Using distinct keys or cryptographic contexts for different security purposes can limit the consequences of key compromise and reduce accidental cross-purpose use.","explanation":"A key used for one security role should not automatically be treated as suitable for unrelated roles. Purpose separation makes authority and compromise scope easier to reason about.","principle":"Cryptographic authority should be scoped to the intended purpose and context.","rule":"Define key purpose and prohibit unintended cross-purpose use.","objective":"Limit blast radius and reduce misuse of cryptographic material.","requirements":["Key purpose metadata","Usage restrictions","Context binding","Lifecycle policy"],"constraints":["Some cryptographic systems intentionally derive multiple subkeys from a root key","Separation may be logical rather than physical when using secure derivation mechanisms"],"failure_mode":"The same key material is reused across unrelated security functions.","cause":"Key management treats all cryptographic keys as interchangeable secrets.","root_cause":"Cryptographic authority scope was not explicitly modeled.","solution":"Use purpose-specific keys or authenticated derivation with explicit domain separation.","alternative":"Use a key-management service that exposes purpose-scoped cryptographic operations without exporting raw keys.","tradeoff":"More key classes increase lifecycle management complexity.","edge_case":"Legacy data requires continued access using a historical key that was previously used for multiple purposes.","security_implication":"Historical cross-purpose use can expand compromise impact and complicate migration.","test_case":"Attempt to use a purpose-restricted key for an unrelated cryptographic operation.","expected_test_result":"The operation is rejected or structurally prevented.","testing_guidance":"Test key identifiers, algorithm parameters, context labels, and service-level authorization.","verification_method":"Inspect key-purpose metadata and execute prohibited-use tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B38-R01","D57-B24-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B38-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Key Lifecycle","concept":"Nonce and IV lifecycle","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies to nonces and initialization vectors?","answer":"Nonce or IV requirements are algorithm- and mode-specific; where uniqueness is required, reuse under the same key can undermine the security guarantees of the construction.","explanation":"It is incorrect to treat all IVs as requiring the same generation rule. Security depends on the exact cryptographic construction and its documented nonce requirements.","principle":"Nonce and IV handling must follow the security requirements of the selected cryptographic construction.","rule":"Document and enforce nonce uniqueness or unpredictability requirements according to the algorithm and mode in use.","objective":"Prevent cryptographic misuse caused by incorrect nonce or IV lifecycle assumptions.","requirements":["Algorithm identification","Nonce/IV requirement","Generation policy","Reuse prevention"],"constraints":["Requirements differ between encryption modes and algorithms","Distributed generation can complicate uniqueness guarantees"],"failure_mode":"The same nonce is reused with the same key where the construction requires uniqueness.","cause":"Nonce generation is random without adequate uniqueness guarantees or state is incorrectly reused.","root_cause":"Cryptographic parameters were handled without construction-specific security requirements.","solution":"Use the construction's recommended nonce-generation mechanism and enforce its uniqueness or randomness requirement.","alternative":"Use an authenticated encryption API that manages nonce generation and misuse-resistant behavior where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Automatic nonce management can reduce application errors but requires correct API semantics.","edge_case":"Multiple processes generate nonces independently for the same key.","security_implication":"Independent generators can create collisions even when each local generator appears correct.","test_case":"Verify nonce generation across repeated and concurrent operations under the same key.","expected_test_result":"Nonce behavior satisfies the selected construction's documented security requirement.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, concurrency, state loss, and distributed generation scenarios.","verification_method":"Review cryptographic API usage and execute uniqueness or construction-specific nonce tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B24-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B38-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Key Lifecycle","concept":"Cryptographic verification before trust","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should be verified before accepting cryptographically protected data as trusted?","answer":"The system must verify the applicable authenticity or integrity protection using the correct key, algorithm, context, and validation rules before treating protected data as trusted.","explanation":"The presence of a signature, MAC, or encrypted container is not itself proof that verification succeeded or that the protection applies to the intended context.","principle":"Cryptographic protection is meaningful only after successful verification under the correct security context.","rule":"Perform and explicitly check the required cryptographic verification before security-sensitive processing or authorization.","objective":"Prevent applications from trusting data merely because cryptographic metadata is present.","requirements":["Correct verification key","Algorithm and context binding","Explicit verification result handling","Failure-path tests"],"constraints":["Some protocols allow partial processing before verification but must not trust protected claims prematurely","Key selection itself can be security-sensitive"],"failure_mode":"An application processes identity or authorization claims before confirming the signature or MAC.","cause":"Verification is performed late or its result is not enforced.","root_cause":"Cryptographic verification was treated as metadata processing instead of a trust transition.","solution":"Make successful verification a prerequisite for trusting security-relevant protected claims.","alternative":"Use protocol libraries that expose only verified claims after successful validation.","tradeoff":"Strict verification can reject malformed or partially compatible legacy data.","edge_case":"An invalid signature is detected after application logic has already acted on the payload.","security_implication":"Late verification can convert an integrity control into ineffective post-processing validation.","test_case":"Provide an invalidly authenticated security-sensitive message and trace processing order.","expected_test_result":"No protected security claim is trusted before successful verification.","testing_guidance":"Test invalid signatures, wrong keys, wrong contexts, expired credentials, and algorithm mismatches.","verification_method":"Inspect control flow and execute negative cryptographic verification tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B38-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B38-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Cryptographic Key Lifecycle","concept":"Cryptographic migration and algorithm agility","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"Why should cryptographic systems plan for algorithm and key migration?","answer":"Cryptographic controls have finite compatibility and security lifetimes, so systems should support controlled migration without losing access to protected data or silently weakening verification.","explanation":"Migration can involve new algorithms, key sizes, libraries, protocols, or policy requirements. A system that cannot transition safely may be forced to retain obsolete protections or perform unsafe emergency changes.","principle":"Cryptographic security includes the ability to change cryptographic dependencies safely.","rule":"Design cryptographic storage and protocols with an explicit migration path for keys and algorithms.","objective":"Prevent obsolete cryptographic controls from becoming permanent because migration is impractical.","requirements":["Algorithm metadata","Key versioning","Migration strategy","Backward-compatibility policy","Verification after migration"],"constraints":["Historical data may require legacy decryption","Protocol interoperability may limit immediate removal"],"failure_mode":"A deprecated algorithm remains enabled indefinitely because protected data cannot be migrated safely.","cause":"Cryptographic lifecycle was designed without migration support.","root_cause":"Algorithm choice was treated as permanent configuration.","solution":"Version cryptographic formats and define staged migration and retirement procedures.","alternative":"Use envelope encryption or abstraction layers that permit key and algorithm replacement without changing application semantics.","tradeoff":"Migration support increases design complexity and metadata requirements.","edge_case":"A legacy algorithm is required only to read old records but must not be used for new records.","security_implication":"Legacy-read and new-write policies must be separated to prevent accidental continued use of obsolete protection.","test_case":"Migrate newly created data to a new cryptographic policy while retaining controlled access to legacy data.","expected_test_result":"New data uses the current policy and legacy access follows the documented restricted policy.","testing_guidance":"Test partial migration, rollback, legacy reads, key rotation, and mixed-version datasets.","verification_method":"Inspect cryptographic version metadata and execute migration and policy-enforcement tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B38-R01","D57-B38-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B39-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Distributed Security","concept":"Race conditions in authorization-sensitive operations","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when authorization and state mutation occur separately?","answer":"A resource can change between authorization and mutation, allowing a previously valid decision to become invalid before the protected operation completes.","explanation":"Security checks that depend on mutable state must account for concurrency. An authorization decision about ownership, balance, status, or policy may become stale before the final write.","principle":"Security decisions over mutable state require atomicity or explicit consistency guarantees.","rule":"Where required by the threat model, combine authorization-relevant state validation with the protected state transition atomically.","objective":"Prevent time-of-check-to-time-of-use authorization races.","requirements":["Identify mutable security state","Atomic transaction or equivalent synchronization","Concurrency tests","Conflict handling"],"constraints":["Strong serialization can reduce concurrency","Distributed transactions may be unavailable or expensive"],"failure_mode":"A user authorized to modify a resource loses authorization before the write executes, but the write still succeeds.","cause":"Authorization and mutation are separate operations over mutable state.","root_cause":"Security validity was assumed to persist across a race window.","solution":"Use transactional conditional updates, version checks, locks, or equivalent mechanisms appropriate to the consistency model.","alternative":"Re-evaluate authorization immediately before mutation and reject stale versions.","tradeoff":"Stronger consistency can increase contention or operational complexity.","edge_case":"The resource is deleted or transferred between the authorization check and mutation.","security_implication":"Race conditions can bypass otherwise correct authorization logic.","test_case":"Concurrent operations modify authorization-relevant resource state between authorization and mutation.","expected_test_result":"The stale operation is rejected or handled according to the documented consistency policy.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency tests and include retries and duplicate submissions.","verification_method":"Review transaction boundaries and execute concurrent state-transition tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B34-R04","D57-B08-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B39-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Distributed Security","concept":"Replay protection for security-sensitive operations","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What lesson applies when a security-sensitive request can be repeated?","answer":"Operations whose repeated execution could cause unauthorized or harmful effects require explicit replay, duplication, or idempotency semantics appropriate to the operation.","explanation":"A valid request can remain dangerous when replayed after its intended context has changed. Authentication or message integrity alone does not necessarily prove that a request is fresh or has not already been consumed.","principle":"Authenticity and freshness are separate security properties.","rule":"Define and enforce replay semantics for security-sensitive requests.","objective":"Prevent valid credentials or messages from being reused outside their intended transaction context.","requirements":["Request identity or nonce where required","Freshness policy","Duplicate detection","State transition rules"],"constraints":["Not every request requires replay protection","Distributed systems complicate global uniqueness and freshness"],"failure_mode":"The same authorized operation can be submitted repeatedly when only one execution was intended.","cause":"The protocol verifies authenticity but not freshness or duplicate state.","root_cause":"Replay was not included in the threat model for the operation.","solution":"Use transaction identifiers, expiration, sequence numbers, idempotency controls, or another mechanism suited to the protocol.","alternative":"Make the underlying operation naturally idempotent where security and business semantics permit.","tradeoff":"Replay protection introduces state or metadata requirements.","edge_case":"A legitimate client retries because it did not receive the original response.","security_implication":"Controls must distinguish legitimate retries from malicious replay without allowing duplicate harmful effects.","test_case":"Submit the same valid security-sensitive request multiple times.","expected_test_result":"The system follows the documented duplicate/replay policy and does not perform unintended repeated effects.","testing_guidance":"Test network retries, delayed messages, duplicate queue delivery, and reordered requests.","verification_method":"Inspect freshness/idempotency controls and execute duplicate-message tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B31-R05","D57-B38-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B39-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Distributed Security","concept":"Distributed authorization consistency","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What security lesson applies when authorization state is replicated across services?","answer":"The system must define how authorization changes propagate and what operations are allowed while replicas are stale.","explanation":"Distributed replication can produce temporary disagreement between components about roles, ownership, policy, or revocation. Security architecture must explicitly define the acceptable consistency window for each protected operation.","principle":"Security consistency requirements must be specified independently of general data consistency assumptions.","rule":"Classify authorization state by required freshness and use authoritative checks for operations that cannot tolerate stale security state.","objective":"Prevent replication lag from extending revoked or unauthorized access.","requirements":["Authority source","Consistency policy","Freshness requirement","Revocation behavior"],"constraints":["Strong consistency can increase latency or reduce availability","Some low-risk authorization decisions may tolerate bounded staleness"],"failure_mode":"A revoked permission remains effective in a lagging service.","cause":"Authorization replica is used without a defined security freshness bound.","root_cause":"General replication semantics were assumed to be sufficient for security decisions.","solution":"Define security freshness requirements and use authoritative or strongly consistent checks where necessary.","alternative":"Use short-lived authorization grants whose lifetime bounds stale-state exposure.","tradeoff":"Fresh authorization checks can add latency and dependency on the authority service.","edge_case":"The authorization service is unavailable during a high-impact operation.","security_implication":"Fallback behavior determines whether availability or stale-authority risk dominates and must be explicitly governed.","test_case":"Revoke authorization and invoke the protected operation against a deliberately stale replica.","expected_test_result":"The system follows the documented security consistency policy.","testing_guidance":"Test revocation propagation, failover, replica lag, cache interaction, and service outages.","verification_method":"Review authorization consistency requirements and execute controlled stale-state tests.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B31-R01","D57-B32-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B39-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Distributed Security","concept":"Idempotent security remediation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"Why should automated security remediation be designed with idempotency where possible?","answer":"A remediation action may be retried after uncertain execution, so repeating it should either safely produce the same intended state or be explicitly detected and handled.","explanation":"Distributed systems can lose responses after an action succeeds. If a security remediation blindly retries, it may cause destructive or conflicting effects instead of converging on the intended security state.","principle":"Security remediation should converge safely despite duplicate delivery or uncertain completion where architecture permits.","rule":"Give remediation actions explicit idempotency semantics or require verification before retrying non-idempotent actions.","objective":"Prevent remediation retries from creating additional security damage.","requirements":["Action identity","Target state definition","Duplicate handling","Post-action verification"],"constraints":["Some actions are inherently non-idempotent","Verification may itself require reliable telemetry"],"failure_mode":"A remediation is executed twice because the first result was not observed.","cause":"The action lacks an identity or safe retry policy.","root_cause":"Execution and outcome verification were not modeled separately.","solution":"Use idempotent state transitions, unique action identifiers, or verify current state before retrying.","alternative":"Require explicit approval for repeated non-idempotent remediation.","tradeoff":"State verification can add delay but reduces duplicate destructive actions.","edge_case":"The first action succeeded but the verification channel failed.","security_implication":"The system must distinguish unknown outcome from known failure before deciding to repeat a high-impact action.","test_case":"Execute a remediation while suppressing the completion response, then invoke the retry logic.","expected_test_result":"The system avoids unintended duplicate impact or safely recognizes the existing target state.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, network failure, duplicate messages, process restart, and partial completion.","verification_method":"Inspect action identity and outcome-state handling and execute uncertain-outcome retry tests.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R01","D57-B07-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B39-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Distributed Security","concept":"Security state versus execution state","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why should security systems distinguish intended action, execution, and resulting state?","answer":"A command being issued or executed does not establish that the intended security state was actually achieved.","explanation":"Distributed systems can partially execute, fail, race with other changes, or produce outcomes different from the requested target state. Security lessons should therefore preserve the distinction between decision, execution, and verified outcome.","principle":"Security control effectiveness is established by verified resulting state, not by command issuance alone.","rule":"Represent decision, execution status, observed outcome, and verification status as separate states.","objective":"Prevent false claims that a security remediation or control succeeded merely because execution was attempted.","requirements":["Distinct state model","Outcome observation","Verification step","Failure and unknown states"],"constraints":["Some outcomes cannot be observed immediately","Verification mechanisms can themselves fail"],"failure_mode":"A security system marks a control successful immediately after issuing the command.","cause":"Execution status is conflated with verified outcome.","root_cause":"The security state model lacks explicit outcome semantics.","solution":"Separate requested action, execution result, observed state, and verification status.","alternative":"Use asynchronous reconciliation that verifies target state after execution.","tradeoff":"Additional verification increases system complexity and processing but improves correctness.","edge_case":"The action returned success but the target system subsequently reverted the change.","security_implication":"Execution-only evidence can create persistent false assurance about protection.","test_case":"Issue a remediation command, simulate successful transport but unsuccessful resulting state, and inspect recorded status.","expected_test_result":"Execution success does not automatically become verified security-state success.","testing_guidance":"Test partial execution, delayed outcomes, rollback, external interference, and verification failure.","verification_method":"Inspect the state machine and execute scenarios where execution and outcome diverge.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R01","D57-B05-R05","D57-B07-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B40-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Change Management","concept":"Risk-based security review of changes","knowledge_type":"RULE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"What security lesson applies when deciding which software changes require security review?","answer":"Security review depth should be determined by the change's potential effect on assets, trust boundaries, authority, data exposure, and security controls rather than by change size alone.","explanation":"A one-line change can alter authorization or input handling, while a large refactor may leave the security boundary unchanged. Review scope should therefore be linked to security impact.","principle":"Security review should follow security-relevant change impact, not source-code volume.","rule":"Classify changes according to affected security boundaries and apply proportionate review and verification.","objective":"Ensure security-significant changes receive appropriate scrutiny.","requirements":["Security-impact classification","Defined review criteria","Required verification level","Change traceability"],"constraints":["Impact classification itself can be imperfect","High-risk changes may require independent review"],"failure_mode":"A security-sensitive one-line authorization change receives only ordinary code review because the diff is small.","cause":"Review effort is estimated from change size rather than security impact.","root_cause":"Security impact was not a first-class change attribute.","solution":"Add security-impact classification to the change process and map classifications to review and verification requirements.","alternative":"Require predefined security ownership for protected components.","tradeoff":"More review for security-sensitive changes can increase delivery time.","edge_case":"A large refactor changes no intended security semantics but accidentally changes control flow.","security_implication":"Both explicit security changes and accidental security effects require verification.","test_case":"Submit small and large changes with different security impacts and compare required review paths.","expected_test_result":"Review requirements correspond to security impact rather than line count.","testing_guidance":"Test authorization, trust-boundary, parser, cryptographic, tenant, and deployment changes as security-sensitive categories.","verification_method":"Inspect change-classification policy and review representative changes against it.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R03","D57-B05-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B40-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Change Management","concept":"Threat-model synchronization after architecture changes","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"When should a security lesson require threat-model review?","answer":"A threat model should be revisited when a change materially alters assets, trust boundaries, actors, data flows, authority, attack surfaces, or assumptions that the existing model depends on.","explanation":"Security tests can remain green while an architecture change creates a new attack path outside the original threat model. Threat-model maintenance therefore complements implementation-level testing.","principle":"Security reasoning must remain synchronized with the system architecture it describes.","rule":"Trigger threat-model review from defined security-impacting architectural changes rather than from arbitrary release frequency alone.","objective":"Prevent architectural security assumptions from becoming stale.","requirements":["Threat-model scope","Change-impact triggers","Updated assumptions","Review evidence"],"constraints":["Not every implementation change requires a full threat-model rewrite","Threat models can contain uncertainty and assumptions"],"failure_mode":"A new trust boundary is introduced without updating the security analysis.","cause":"Threat-model review is disconnected from architectural change.","root_cause":"Security assumptions are treated as static documentation.","solution":"Define change triggers that require threat-model review and record updated assumptions and residual uncertainty.","alternative":"Use lightweight threat-model deltas for bounded changes.","tradeoff":"Frequent review increases maintenance work but reduces stale-model risk.","edge_case":"A third-party service is introduced with no intended change to application functionality.","security_implication":"A new trust relationship can materially change the threat model even if user-facing behavior remains similar.","test_case":"Introduce a new external service boundary and verify that the threat-model process is triggered.","expected_test_result":"Affected trust relationships and assumptions are reviewed before the change is considered security-complete.","testing_guidance":"Include data-flow changes, identity-provider changes, new privileged services, and storage changes.","verification_method":"Compare architecture change records with threat-model review triggers.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B01-R02","D57-B40-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B40-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Change Management","concept":"Security regression tests as persistent controls","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"Why should security regression tests remain after a security defect is fixed?","answer":"A regression test converts a previously identified security failure into an explicit check that can detect recurrence, provided the test actually exercises the relevant security boundary.","explanation":"A fix can be accidentally removed during later refactoring. Preserving a focused negative test retains the lesson as executable knowledge rather than leaving it only in historical documentation.","principle":"Important security lessons should become durable verification controls when they can be expressed as tests.","rule":"Retain representative regression tests for security defects whose recurrence would be material.","objective":"Prevent known security failures from silently returning.","requirements":["Reproducible test case","Clear security invariant","Negative assertion","Maintenance ownership"],"constraints":["A single regression test cannot establish complete security","Tests must remain aligned with the actual threat and control"],"failure_mode":"A previously fixed authorization bypass returns after refactoring because no regression test covers the boundary.","cause":"The fix was documented but not encoded as a persistent verification check.","root_cause":"Lessons were not promoted into the testing system.","solution":"Add and maintain focused security regression tests linked to the relevant lesson and control.","alternative":"Use broader security suites when they demonstrably cover the same invariant.","tradeoff":"More tests increase maintenance cost but preserve institutional security knowledge.","edge_case":"The implementation changes so the original test no longer reaches the same security boundary.","security_implication":"A stale test can create false confidence even while remaining green.","test_case":"Reintroduce the class of defect in a controlled test branch and confirm the regression test fails.","expected_test_result":"The regression control detects the prohibited security behavior.","testing_guidance":"Periodically review whether security regression tests still exercise the intended invariant.","verification_method":"Trace each regression test to the security invariant it is intended to protect.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B05-R03","D57-B07-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B40-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Change Management","concept":"Rollback limitations after security exposure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why does rollback not necessarily undo a security exposure?","answer":"Reverting software can restore an earlier code state, but it cannot automatically erase data already disclosed, credentials already exposed, external side effects, or copies created while the vulnerable state was active.","explanation":"Security remediation must distinguish software-state restoration from recovery of affected security assets and external consequences. A rollback may itself reintroduce the original vulnerability.","principle":"Rollback changes system state; it does not automatically reverse every security consequence of prior execution.","rule":"Evaluate credential rotation, data exposure, external side effects, and vulnerability reintroduction separately from software rollback.","objective":"Prevent false closure after reverting a security-sensitive change.","requirements":["Exposure assessment","Rollback analysis","Credential and data remediation","Post-rollback verification"],"constraints":["Historical exposure may be uncertain","Rollback availability and safety vary by system"],"failure_mode":"A vulnerable release is rolled back and the incident is considered resolved without addressing exposed credentials or data.","cause":"Code state restoration is treated as complete remediation.","root_cause":"Security consequences were modeled as equivalent to application version state.","solution":"Pair rollback with exposure-specific remediation and verify the resulting security state.","alternative":"Forward-fix the vulnerability while preserving a safe deployment state.","tradeoff":"Broader remediation can take longer but addresses consequences rollback cannot reverse.","edge_case":"Rollback returns the system to a version containing the original vulnerability.","security_implication":"Rollback can reduce one immediate effect while restoring another security weakness.","test_case":"Model a vulnerable release that exposed a credential and perform rollback without rotating the credential.","expected_test_result":"The remediation process identifies the unresolved credential exposure and does not mark the security issue fully closed.","testing_guidance":"Test rollback with data disclosure, secret exposure, external notifications, and persistent attacker-created state.","verification_method":"Compare software rollback status with independent exposure and residual-risk checks.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B04-R01","D57-B06-R01","D57-B40-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B40-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Change Management","concept":"Security lesson promotion into engineering controls","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"RECOMMENDATION","question":"When should a security lesson become a reusable engineering control?","answer":"A lesson should be promoted when its evidence is sufficient, its scope is understood, its generalized claim is justified, and an owner and verification mechanism can maintain the resulting control.","explanation":"Not every security observation should become a universal rule. Promotion requires separating observed facts from inference and ensuring that the resulting control does not overgeneralize beyond the evidence.","principle":"Security knowledge becomes durable engineering guidance through evidence, scoped generalization, ownership, and verification.","rule":"Promote a lesson only after evidence review, scope definition, contradiction assessment, control mapping, and verification planning.","objective":"Convert useful security learning into durable controls without encoding unsupported conclusions.","requirements":["Evidence assessment","Scope and applicability","Contradiction review","Control mapping","Verification method","Ownership"],"constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete","A lesson may remain local to one system or threat model"],"failure_mode":"A single incident produces an overly broad security rule that creates unnecessary restrictions or misses the actual causal factor.","cause":"Observation is generalized without scope analysis.","root_cause":"Evidence, inference, and recommendation are not represented separately.","solution":"Store observed facts, inference, applicability, uncertainty, and resulting control as separate knowledge elements.","alternative":"Keep the lesson as a scoped case rather than promoting it to a generalized rule.","tradeoff":"Conservative promotion reduces overgeneralization but may delay reusable knowledge.","edge_case":"Later evidence contradicts or narrows the original lesson.","security_implication":"Promoted security knowledge requires versioning and supersession so stale guidance does not persist as authoritative.","test_case":"Attempt to promote a lesson with incomplete evidence and compare it with one having corroborated evidence and a defined verification method.","expected_test_result":"Promotion status reflects evidence quality and scope rather than record existence alone.","testing_guidance":"Track lesson lifecycle from observation to inference, review, control mapping, verification, and possible supersession.","verification_method":"Audit the promotion workflow for evidence, applicability, contradiction, ownership, and verification fields.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B02-R02","D57-B03-R05","D57-B10-R01","D57-B10-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}

{"record_id":"D57-B41-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Update Systems","concept":"Update authenticity is distinct from update availability","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must a security update system verify update authenticity rather than merely retrieve an available update?","answer":"Availability confirms that an update can be obtained; authenticity provides evidence that the update originated from an authorized source and has not been altered. A secure update process must establish authenticity before installation.","explanation":"A reachable update endpoint does not by itself establish trust in the artifact. Authenticity controls such as signature verification or an equivalent trusted integrity mechanism reduce the risk of installing an attacker-controlled artifact.","principle":"Never equate successful retrieval with trusted software provenance.","rule":"Verify update authenticity before execution or installation.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized software artifacts from entering the trusted execution environment.","requirements":["Trusted verification material must be available.","The artifact must be checked before installation.","Verification failure must prevent installation."],"constraints":["Trust anchors themselves require lifecycle management.","Authenticity verification does not prove the artifact is vulnerability-free."],"failure_mode":"An untrusted or modified update is accepted because retrieval succeeded.","cause":"Update workflow treats transport success as sufficient trust evidence.","root_cause":"Missing separation between artifact acquisition and artifact authorization.","solution":"Introduce explicit pre-installation authenticity verification with fail-closed behavior.","alternative":"Use a trusted package-management mechanism that performs equivalent authenticity verification.","tradeoff":"Additional verification introduces processing and operational complexity but establishes a stronger trust boundary.","edge_case":"A validly signed artifact can still contain an undesired or vulnerable change; signature validity must not be treated as a complete security assessment.","security_implication":"Compromised update infrastructure can become a high-impact supply-chain attack path if authenticity is not independently established.","test_case":"Provide an artifact with an invalid or untrusted signature and attempt installation.","test_input":{"artifact":"tampered-or-untrusted-update"},"expected_test_result":"Installation is rejected before the artifact becomes executable or installed.","testing_guidance":"Test valid, invalid, expired or revoked trust material where supported, and altered artifacts.","verification_method":"Inspect update-control logic and execute negative verification tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B25-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B41-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Update Systems","concept":"Rollback integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should rollback artifacts receive security validation rather than being trusted merely because they are older versions?","answer":"An older artifact is not automatically trustworthy. Rollback artifacts must retain provenance and authenticity guarantees because an attacker may target the rollback path or replace stored recovery artifacts.","explanation":"Security controls frequently focus on forward updates while treating rollback packages as trusted history. This creates an alternative execution path that can bypass newer controls.","principle":"Every executable recovery path must satisfy the same applicable trust requirements as the primary deployment path.","rule":"Validate rollback artifacts before activation.","objective":"Prevent rollback mechanisms from becoming a security bypass.","requirements":["Rollback artifacts require identifiable provenance.","Integrity or authenticity must be checked.","Activation must be governed by an authorized transition."],"failure_mode":"A system installs an unverified rollback artifact because rollback is considered inherently trusted.","cause":"Rollback path receives weaker security controls than normal deployment.","root_cause":"Security model excludes recovery transitions from the trusted deployment lifecycle.","solution":"Apply artifact verification and authorization to rollback operations.","alternative":"Use immutable, pre-verified recovery images managed through the same trust framework.","tradeoff":"Stronger rollback controls can increase recovery time but reduce recovery-path attack exposure.","edge_case":"A security fix may make rollback to an older version intentionally prohibited.","security_implication":"Attackers may deliberately trigger or exploit rollback to reintroduce removed vulnerabilities.","test_case":"Attempt rollback using a modified or unauthorized recovery artifact.","test_input":{"rollback_artifact":"altered_previous_release"},"expected_test_result":"Rollback is rejected and the failed transition is auditable.","testing_guidance":"Test both normal rollback and tampered, unauthorized, or policy-prohibited rollback paths.","verification_method":"Verify that rollback invokes equivalent authenticity, authorization, and audit controls.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R01","D57-B5-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B41-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Update Systems","concept":"Downgrade resistance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson follows from allowing unauthorized downgrade to a vulnerable version?","answer":"Version transitions can themselves be security-sensitive actions. Where the threat model requires it, systems should prevent unauthorized downgrades or require explicit authorization for transitions to versions with known security weaknesses.","explanation":"An attacker may exploit downgrade capability to bypass a security fix. The security property is therefore not only artifact authenticity but also whether the requested version transition is permitted.","principle":"Treat security-relevant version transitions as authorized state changes.","rule":"Do not assume every authentic version is an acceptable deployment target.","objective":"Prevent version-transition mechanisms from bypassing security controls.","failure_mode":"A valid but vulnerable older release is installed without adequate authorization.","cause":"Update policy validates authenticity but ignores security state and transition policy.","root_cause":"Version authorization is modeled independently from security policy.","solution":"Evaluate downgrade requests against supported-version policy and known security constraints.","alternative":"Permit emergency downgrade only through explicit privileged approval with compensating controls.","tradeoff":"Downgrade restrictions can complicate legitimate recovery scenarios.","edge_case":"A newer release may introduce a critical compatibility defect requiring emergency rollback.","security_implication":"Unrestricted downgrade can reopen previously remediated vulnerabilities.","test_case":"Request a downgrade to a version prohibited by security policy.","test_input":{"target_version":"security-prohibited-version"},"expected_test_result":"The transition is denied unless an explicitly authorized exception path exists.","testing_guidance":"Test allowed rollback, prohibited downgrade, and authorized exception behavior separately.","verification_method":"Review transition policy and perform negative downgrade tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B41-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Update Systems","concept":"Update failure containment","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should a secure update system do when artifact verification fails?","answer":"It should fail closed for the affected installation path, preserve sufficient evidence for investigation, and avoid silently substituting an unverified artifact.","explanation":"Continuing after a trust-verification failure converts an explicit security signal into an implicit trust decision.","principle":"Security verification failures must not silently degrade into acceptance.","rule":"Reject unverifiable artifacts and record the security-relevant failure.","objective":"Prevent verification failures from becoming bypass conditions.","failure_mode":"Verification failure causes fallback to an unverified source.","cause":"Availability-oriented fallback logic overrides security policy.","root_cause":"Security failure semantics are not explicitly defined for fallback behavior.","solution":"Separate availability fallback from security authorization and make unauthorized fallback impossible.","alternative":"Provide multiple independently trusted sources, each subject to the same verification requirement.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior may reduce availability during trust-infrastructure outages.","edge_case":"The verification service itself may be unavailable while a cached trust decision remains valid under an explicit policy.","security_implication":"Silent fallback can transform a defensive control into an attack bypass.","test_case":"Make the primary verification path unavailable and provide an unverified fallback artifact.","expected_test_result":"The artifact is not installed unless the fallback satisfies the same trust requirements.","verification_method":"Inject verification failure and observe transition behavior.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B41-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Update Systems","concept":"Update trust-anchor lifecycle","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies to long-lived update trust anchors?","answer":"Trust anchors require controlled provisioning, rotation, revocation, backup, and recovery procedures; treating them as permanent configuration can create a durable single point of compromise.","explanation":"An update-verification mechanism depends on trusted verification material. Its security therefore includes the lifecycle of that material, not only the signature-checking algorithm.","principle":"The trust infrastructure supporting a security control is itself part of the security boundary.","rule":"Define lifecycle controls for update trust anchors.","objective":"Prevent stale or compromised trust material from undermining artifact verification.","requirements":["Document trust-anchor ownership.","Define rotation and revocation procedures.","Protect recovery material.","Test lifecycle transitions."],"failure_mode":"A compromised or obsolete trust anchor remains authoritative indefinitely.","cause":"No defined lifecycle for verification trust material.","root_cause":"Trust infrastructure is treated as static rather than security-sensitive state.","solution":"Implement controlled trust-anchor lifecycle management with auditable transitions.","alternative":"Use a hierarchical trust model with independently managed recovery paths.","tradeoff":"Lifecycle management adds governance and operational complexity.","edge_case":"Emergency revocation may need to work when normal update infrastructure is unavailable.","security_implication":"Compromise of long-lived trust material can affect many future update decisions.","test_case":"Simulate trust-anchor rotation and attempt verification using the retired trust material.","expected_test_result":"The retired trust material is rejected according to the defined lifecycle policy.","verification_method":"Review lifecycle controls and test rotation, retirement, and recovery paths.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B42-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Integrity and Recovery","concept":"Integrity verification after recovery","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should restored data or systems be integrity-checked after recovery?","answer":"Recovery proves that restoration procedures ran; it does not by itself prove that the restored state is complete, authentic, or free from unauthorized modification.","explanation":"Backups and recovery copies can be stale, corrupted, incomplete, or compromised. Security verification must therefore evaluate the recovered state separately from the restoration operation.","principle":"Recovery execution and recovered-state integrity are separate properties.","rule":"Perform explicit integrity verification after restoration when integrity is security-relevant.","objective":"Detect unauthorized or incomplete recovered state before normal trust is restored.","failure_mode":"A restored system is declared secure because the restore operation completed successfully.","cause":"Recovery completion is treated as equivalent to integrity validation.","root_cause":"Operational success and security state validation are conflated.","solution":"Define post-recovery integrity checks and acceptance criteria.","alternative":"Use immutable recovery artifacts with independent integrity metadata plus post-restore verification.","tradeoff":"Additional validation increases recovery duration.","edge_case":"Some runtime state is intentionally regenerated rather than restored and must be validated differently.","security_implication":"Compromised recovery material can reintroduce persistence or corrupted security configuration.","test_case":"Restore from a deliberately altered recovery artifact.","expected_test_result":"Integrity validation detects the alteration before the restored state is accepted.","testing_guidance":"Test corrupted, incomplete, stale, and unauthorized recovery states.","verification_method":"Compare recovered state against independently trusted integrity evidence.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B5-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B42-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Integrity and Recovery","concept":"Recovery-point trust","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should be learned when the newest recovery point is not necessarily the safest recovery point?","answer":"Recovery-point selection should consider security state and evidence, not only recency. A newer recovery point may contain unwanted modifications or compromised state.","explanation":"Recency optimizes data loss characteristics, while security requires confidence in the state being restored. These are related but distinct selection criteria.","principle":"Recovery-point recency and recovery-point trustworthiness are separate dimensions.","rule":"Evaluate recovery points against defined security acceptance criteria.","objective":"Avoid restoring a recent but compromised state when a more trustworthy recovery point is available.","failure_mode":"The newest backup is always selected despite evidence that it may contain compromise.","cause":"Recovery policy optimizes solely for recency.","root_cause":"Security state is omitted from recovery-point selection.","solution":"Include integrity, provenance, and compromise indicators in recovery-point evaluation.","alternative":"Maintain independently verified recovery checkpoints.","tradeoff":"Older trusted recovery points may increase recoverable data loss.","edge_case":"Compromise timing may be uncertain, requiring multiple candidate recovery points to be evaluated.","security_implication":"Automatic selection of the newest point can preserve attacker persistence.","test_case":"Provide two recovery points where the newer point fails a security acceptance criterion.","expected_test_result":"The system does not automatically select the newer point as trusted.","verification_method":"Review recovery selection policy and test competing recovery-point states.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B42-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B42-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Integrity and Recovery","concept":"Recovery configuration security","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must security configuration be included in recovery validation?","answer":"Restoring application data without restoring or reconstructing the intended security configuration can produce a system that operates but has incorrect access, network, logging, or policy controls.","explanation":"Security state often exists outside primary business data. Recovery validation must account for the controls required to keep the recovered system within its intended trust boundaries.","principle":"A recovered workload is not secure merely because its business data is available.","rule":"Define security configuration as part of the recoverable security state.","objective":"Prevent recovery from producing functionally available but improperly protected systems.","failure_mode":"Recovered service starts with permissive or incomplete security controls.","cause":"Recovery plan models application data but omits security configuration dependencies.","root_cause":"The recovery state model is incomplete.","solution":"Explicitly model and validate security-critical configuration dependencies during recovery.","alternative":"Rebuild security configuration from version-controlled policy artifacts before activation.","tradeoff":"Broader recovery validation requires additional dependency mapping.","edge_case":"Some security configuration must be generated dynamically after restoration.","security_implication":"Configuration gaps can create privilege escalation, exposure, or monitoring blind spots immediately after recovery.","test_case":"Restore application state while intentionally omitting a required security control.","expected_test_result":"Recovery acceptance fails until the required control is restored or safely reconstructed.","verification_method":"Use a recovery checklist derived from the security dependency graph.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B42-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B42-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Integrity and Recovery","concept":"Recovery testing versus recovery evidence","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is a documented recovery procedure insufficient evidence that secure recovery works?","answer":"Documentation describes intended behavior; a tested recovery exercise provides evidence about actual behavior under the tested conditions. Neither alone proves correctness for every possible failure state.","explanation":"Security recovery depends on dependencies, ordering, trust material, permissions, and environmental assumptions that documentation may omit.","principle":"A procedure is not equivalent to demonstrated recovery behavior.","rule":"Test critical recovery paths and preserve evidence of the tested conditions and results.","objective":"Increase confidence that recovery controls operate as designed.","failure_mode":"Teams assume recovery is secure because a procedure exists.","cause":"Documentation is treated as validation evidence.","root_cause":"No separation between prescribed process and demonstrated behavior.","solution":"Pair documented recovery procedures with repeatable recovery tests and evidence capture.","alternative":"Use controlled recovery simulations where full restoration is impractical.","tradeoff":"Testing consumes resources and may require isolated environments.","edge_case":"A test environment may not reproduce production trust dependencies.","security_implication":"Untested recovery assumptions can become hidden security failure points during an incident.","test_case":"Execute the recovery procedure in a controlled environment and verify security acceptance criteria.","expected_test_result":"Required security controls are demonstrated and test evidence identifies environmental limitations.","verification_method":"Review recovery-test artifacts, scope, assumptions, and observed results.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B42-R01","D57-B5-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B42-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Integrity and Recovery","concept":"Recovery lesson expiration","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why should recovery-related security lessons be reviewed after major architecture changes?","answer":"A recovery lesson derived from one architecture may become invalid when dependencies, trust boundaries, storage mechanisms, or recovery procedures change.","explanation":"Security knowledge is context-dependent. A previously valid recovery control may no longer address the same failure mode after structural changes.","principle":"Security lessons require context and lifecycle management.","rule":"Revalidate recovery lessons when material architecture or recovery dependencies change.","objective":"Prevent obsolete recovery assumptions from remaining authoritative.","failure_mode":"An old recovery lesson is reused after its underlying architecture has changed.","cause":"Knowledge records lack dependency or applicability metadata.","root_cause":"Lessons are treated as timeless statements rather than context-bound knowledge.","solution":"Attach applicability conditions and review triggers to recovery lessons.","alternative":"Automatically flag lessons linked to changed architectural components.","tradeoff":"Lifecycle management increases knowledge-maintenance effort.","edge_case":"A general principle may remain valid even when its original implementation context changes.","security_implication":"Stale lessons can create false confidence in recovery security.","test_case":"Change a documented recovery dependency and check whether linked lessons are flagged for review.","expected_test_result":"Materially affected lessons are identified for revalidation.","verification_method":"Inspect lesson dependency metadata and perform a controlled architecture-change review.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B43-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy decision versus policy enforcement","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is a policy engine decision insufficient evidence that a security policy was enforced?","answer":"A policy decision determines what should be allowed or denied; enforcement requires the protected operation to actually honor that decision at the relevant execution boundary.","explanation":"A correctly evaluating policy engine can still be bypassed if callers ignore results, enforcement occurs in the wrong layer, or an alternate execution path lacks the check.","principle":"Authorization policy evaluation and enforcement are separate security properties.","rule":"Verify that every protected operation is bound to the applicable policy decision.","objective":"Prevent policy bypass through unenforced or incorrectly enforced decisions.","failure_mode":"A deny decision is generated but the protected action still executes.","cause":"Decision and enforcement components are not securely coupled.","root_cause":"The architecture assumes policy evaluation implies enforcement.","solution":"Bind protected actions to mandatory enforcement points and verify denial behavior.","alternative":"Use centralized enforcement at a trusted boundary where practical.","tradeoff":"Centralized enforcement can simplify guarantees but may reduce flexibility or introduce dependency concentration.","edge_case":"Emergency or administrative paths may intentionally use separate policy rules and must remain explicitly governed.","security_implication":"A policy engine without reliable enforcement provides weaker protection than its apparent policy coverage suggests.","test_case":"Return a deny decision and attempt the protected operation through every supported execution path.","expected_test_result":"The operation is denied on all paths governed by the policy.","verification_method":"Perform negative authorization tests against primary and alternate execution paths.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B8-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B43-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Default-deny coverage","knowledge_type":"RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What lesson applies when a newly introduced operation has no explicit security policy?","answer":"A security-sensitive operation should not become implicitly allowed merely because no matching policy rule exists; the system should define explicit default behavior appropriate to the trust boundary, commonly deny for protected actions.","explanation":"Policy gaps are inevitable as systems evolve. A permissive interpretation of an unmatched rule can turn an unrecognized operation into an unintended capability.","principle":"Unknown authorization state should not silently become authorization.","rule":"Define and test explicit behavior for unmatched security policy cases.","objective":"Prevent policy omissions from creating implicit privileges.","failure_mode":"New operation executes because the policy engine has no matching rule.","cause":"Unmatched policy semantics default to allow.","root_cause":"Security policy model does not explicitly define unknown-state handling.","solution":"Define fail-closed behavior for protected operations where the threat model requires it.","alternative":"Use explicit policy registration as a deployment requirement for new protected operations.","tradeoff":"Strict defaults can temporarily block legitimate new functionality until policy is defined.","edge_case":"Public or intentionally unauthenticated operations may legitimately have explicit allow-by-design rules.","security_implication":"Policy omissions can become privilege-escalation or exposure paths.","test_case":"Invoke an operation with no matching policy rule.","expected_test_result":"The system follows its documented safe default and does not gain unintended privilege.","verification_method":"Test unmatched-policy cases and inspect policy semantics.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B43-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy precedence ambiguity","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can conflicting security policies create a security defect even when each individual rule appears correct?","answer":"Conflicting rules require deterministic precedence or conflict-resolution semantics. Without them, different components or evaluation paths may produce inconsistent authorization decisions.","explanation":"Security properties depend on the final decision, not merely the correctness of individual policy fragments.","principle":"Policy composition is part of the security model.","rule":"Define deterministic and testable semantics for policy conflicts.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent security decisions caused by policy composition.","failure_mode":"One enforcement path allows an action while another denies it.","cause":"Policy precedence is implicit or implemented differently across components.","root_cause":"The composed policy model lacks explicit conflict semantics.","solution":"Define precedence, deny/allow interaction, inheritance, and override behavior explicitly.","alternative":"Reduce policy composition through centralized policy ownership.","tradeoff":"Explicit conflict semantics increase model complexity but reduce ambiguity.","edge_case":"Emergency overrides may intentionally supersede ordinary rules and require stronger authorization.","security_implication":"Inconsistent policy interpretation can create exploitable bypass paths.","test_case":"Create two applicable policies with conflicting outcomes.","expected_test_result":"All enforcement points produce the documented deterministic result.","verification_method":"Run policy conflict tests and compare decisions across enforcement points.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B43-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy decision freshness","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can a previously valid security decision become unsafe when authority or context changes?","answer":"Authorization decisions may depend on time-sensitive identity, resource, risk, session, or environmental state. Reusing a stale decision can authorize an operation after the conditions that justified it have changed.","explanation":"Caching policy decisions can improve efficiency but changes the security semantics of the decision's validity period.","principle":"The lifetime of an authorization decision must match the lifetime of the assumptions supporting it.","rule":"Define explicit freshness requirements for cached security decisions.","objective":"Prevent stale authorization from surviving material security-state changes.","failure_mode":"A revoked or changed authority remains effective because an old decision is reused.","cause":"Decision cache lifetime exceeds the relevant authority or context lifetime.","root_cause":"Freshness requirements were not modeled as part of authorization.","solution":"Invalidate or constrain cached decisions according to security-relevant state changes.","alternative":"Evaluate high-impact authorization at action time.","tradeoff":"More frequent evaluation increases latency or policy-system load.","edge_case":"Low-risk operations may legitimately use bounded cached decisions under an explicit threat model.","security_implication":"Stale decisions can bypass revocation and dynamic policy controls.","test_case":"Change authorization state while a cached allow decision remains active.","expected_test_result":"The protected operation follows the defined freshness policy and does not exceed permitted authority.","verification_method":"Test revocation and context-change behavior against decision-cache semantics.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B8-R03","D57-B8-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B43-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy change verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should be verified after changing a security policy?","answer":"Verification should cover intended behavior, unintended access changes, conflict behavior, and protected high-impact paths rather than only confirming that the policy file or rule was updated.","explanation":"A syntactically valid policy change can alter effective authorization in unexpected ways through inheritance, precedence, defaults, or dependencies.","principle":"Security policy changes must be verified at the effective enforcement boundary.","rule":"Test both intended allows and security-critical denies after material policy changes.","objective":"Detect authorization regressions introduced by policy modifications.","test_case":"Modify a policy and evaluate affected positive and negative authorization scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Expected decisions are produced and previously protected denial conditions remain enforced.","testing_guidance":"Include regression cases, conflict cases, unmatched cases, and privileged operations.","verification_method":"Compare effective decisions against versioned acceptance criteria.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B5-R03","D57-B43-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B44-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Isolation Boundaries","concept":"Isolation must be enforced at every reachable boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is isolation not guaranteed merely because the primary interface separates two security domains?","answer":"An isolation boundary is only effective if alternate interfaces, shared resources, management paths, and indirect communication channels also enforce the intended separation.","explanation":"Security domains can interact through APIs, shared storage, caches, queues, logs, metadata, administrative paths, or other channels that bypass the obvious interface.","principle":"Isolation is a system property, not a property of one interface.","rule":"Enumerate and verify all reachable crossing paths between security domains.","objective":"Prevent indirect boundary bypass.","failure_mode":"The primary interface enforces isolation while an alternate path permits cross-domain access.","cause":"Threat analysis focuses only on the intended communication path.","root_cause":"The security boundary is modeled too narrowly.","solution":"Map all communication and shared-resource paths and apply boundary controls consistently.","alternative":"Reduce shared infrastructure between security domains.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation can increase resource duplication and architectural complexity.","edge_case":"Operational telemetry may require controlled cross-domain transfer and must be explicitly mediated.","security_implication":"Hidden channels can invalidate otherwise strong isolation controls.","test_case":"Attempt cross-domain access through primary and alternate interfaces.","expected_test_result":"Only explicitly authorized crossing paths succeed.","verification_method":"Perform boundary enumeration followed by negative tests across identified channels.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B27-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B44-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Isolation Boundaries","concept":"Shared resource contamination","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when isolated workloads share caches or reusable state?","answer":"Shared state can become an unintended information or authorization channel unless entries are explicitly partitioned, validated, and invalidated according to the relevant security boundary.","explanation":"A cache or shared state store may preserve data beyond the request or identity context that created it.","principle":"Reusable state must preserve the security context of the data it contains.","rule":"Partition or validate shared state across security boundaries.","objective":"Prevent information leakage or unauthorized reuse through shared state.","failure_mode":"One security principal receives data generated under another principal's context.","cause":"Shared state key does not encode or enforce the required security partition.","root_cause":"Security context was omitted from state-isolation design.","solution":"Use explicit security-domain partitioning and verify cross-context retrieval behavior.","alternative":"Avoid shared state for security-sensitive data where isolation cannot be reliably enforced.","tradeoff":"Partitioning can reduce reuse efficiency and increase storage requirements.","edge_case":"Public immutable data may legitimately be shared across domains.","security_implication":"Shared-state leakage can expose confidential data or create privilege bypasses.","test_case":"Populate shared state under one security context and retrieve it under another.","expected_test_result":"The second context cannot retrieve data outside its authorized partition.","verification_method":"Perform cross-context state-isolation tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B44-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B44-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Isolation Boundaries","concept":"Administrative-plane isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must management interfaces be included when evaluating workload isolation?","answer":"Administrative interfaces often possess elevated authority and can cross otherwise protected workload boundaries. They therefore require explicit authentication, authorization, auditing, and isolation analysis.","explanation":"A workload can appear isolated from user traffic while management APIs, debugging endpoints, orchestration interfaces, or maintenance channels retain broad authority.","principle":"Administrative paths are part of the attack surface of every isolation boundary they can control.","rule":"Include management-plane interfaces in isolation threat models and verification.","objective":"Prevent privileged control paths from becoming unreviewed boundary bypasses.","failure_mode":"A management endpoint permits cross-domain action without equivalent security controls.","cause":"Isolation review covers only application-facing interfaces.","root_cause":"Management-plane authority is excluded from the boundary model.","solution":"Model administrative paths explicitly and apply least privilege, strong authentication, authorization, and auditing.","alternative":"Separate management infrastructure physically or logically where justified by the threat model.","tradeoff":"Management isolation can increase operational complexity.","edge_case":"Emergency maintenance paths may require exceptional authority and stronger auditing.","security_implication":"Compromise of an administrative path can defeat multiple isolation controls simultaneously.","test_case":"Attempt unauthorized cross-domain administration through management interfaces.","expected_test_result":"The operation is denied and the attempt is auditable.","verification_method":"Inventory management interfaces and test their boundary permissions.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B44-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B44-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Isolation Boundaries","concept":"Boundary metadata integrity","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can incorrect security-domain metadata undermine otherwise strong isolation controls?","answer":"If access decisions rely on mutable or attacker-influenced metadata identifying a tenant, security zone, classification, or trust domain, manipulating that metadata can redirect an operation into an unauthorized context.","explanation":"Boundary enforcement depends on trustworthy identity and context attributes. Security labels cannot provide isolation if their integrity is not protected.","principle":"Security context used for boundary decisions must itself be trustworthy.","rule":"Do not trust security-domain metadata solely because it is supplied by an untrusted caller.","objective":"Prevent context-confusion attacks against isolation controls.","failure_mode":"A caller changes a domain identifier and gains access to another domain.","cause":"Boundary metadata is accepted from an untrusted source without authoritative binding.","root_cause":"Security context provenance is not enforced.","solution":"Derive or verify security-domain context from trusted authorization state.","alternative":"Bind resource ownership to server-side identifiers rather than caller-provided labels.","tradeoff":"Authoritative context derivation may require additional state lookup.","edge_case":"Delegated systems may receive externally asserted claims that require cryptographic or trusted validation.","security_implication":"Context manipulation can convert a data-isolation check into an attacker-controlled selector.","test_case":"Alter the caller-provided security-domain identifier while retaining the same authenticated principal.","expected_test_result":"The operation remains bound to the authoritative security context and does not cross the boundary.","verification_method":"Test tampered context attributes and verify authoritative binding.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B44-R01","D57-B8-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B44-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Isolation Boundaries","concept":"Isolation verification through negative testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What is the central verification lesson for isolation controls?","answer":"Isolation should be verified by attempting prohibited cross-boundary interactions, not only by demonstrating permitted interactions within each domain.","explanation":"Positive tests can show that workloads function correctly without proving that forbidden communication is blocked.","principle":"Security boundaries require adversarial negative testing.","rule":"Include explicit cross-boundary denial tests in isolation verification.","objective":"Provide evidence that prohibited boundary crossings are actually blocked.","test_case":"Attempt representative unauthorized reads, writes, control actions, and metadata queries across the boundary.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized cross-boundary operations are denied consistently across supported interfaces.","testing_guidance":"Include direct, indirect, administrative, shared-state, and error-path access where applicable.","verification_method":"Execute a boundary-specific abuse-case test suite and preserve the tested topology and authorization context.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B44-R01","D57-B5-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B45-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Race Conditions","concept":"Time-of-check versus time-of-use","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can a security check become invalid before the protected operation executes?","answer":"If security-relevant state can change between validation and use, an attacker or concurrent process may alter the state after the check and before the operation.","explanation":"A check performed at one point in time does not necessarily constrain a later operation unless the state transition is protected by an appropriate atomicity or concurrency mechanism.","principle":"Security checks must remain valid for the operation they authorize.","rule":"Protect security-sensitive check-and-use sequences against relevant concurrent state changes.","objective":"Prevent race conditions from invalidating authorization or integrity checks.","failure_mode":"A resource passes validation and is replaced or modified before use.","cause":"Validation and protected operation are separated by an exploitable state transition.","root_cause":"Security invariant is not maintained across the full check-to-use interval.","solution":"Use atomic operations, locking, immutable references, transactional semantics, or equivalent mechanisms appropriate to the threat model.","alternative":"Revalidate the security property immediately before the sensitive operation when atomicity is not practical.","tradeoff":"Stronger atomicity can increase contention or implementation complexity.","edge_case":"Filesystem and distributed-state operations may require platform-specific atomic primitives.","security_implication":"TOCTOU conditions can bypass access or integrity checks.","test_case":"Introduce a concurrent modification between validation and use.","expected_test_result":"The protected operation detects or prevents the invalidated state.","verification_method":"Perform controlled race-condition tests around security-sensitive transitions.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B28-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B45-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Race Conditions","concept":"Check-then-act authorization race","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when authorization is checked separately from a state-changing action?","answer":"A separate authorization check can become stale before the state-changing operation. Security-sensitive actions should preserve the authorization invariant through the action or revalidate under an appropriate synchronization mechanism.","explanation":"The risk is not that authorization was absent, but that the authorization decision ceased to represent the state being modified.","principle":"Authorization validity must cover the state transition it permits.","rule":"Couple authorization and security-sensitive state changes sufficiently to prevent exploitable races.","objective":"Prevent stale authorization from authorizing a changed resource state.","failure_mode":"A resource is authorized, its security state changes, and the previously authorized action still executes.","cause":"Authorization and mutation are independently scheduled.","root_cause":"No concurrency invariant binds the decision to the mutation.","solution":"Use transactionally coupled authorization and mutation or equivalent revalidation.","alternative":"Serialize high-impact state transitions through a trusted control point.","tradeoff":"Serialization may reduce concurrency.","edge_case":"Distributed systems may require optimistic concurrency with version or state validation.","security_implication":"Race conditions can bypass otherwise correct authorization logic.","test_case":"Change the resource authorization state between permission evaluation and mutation.","expected_test_result":"The mutation is rejected or safely reconciled according to the security policy.","verification_method":"Use concurrency tests with controlled state transitions.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B45-R01","D57-B43-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B45-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Race Conditions","concept":"Replay of valid security decisions","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can replaying a previously valid security decision become a security problem?","answer":"A decision that was valid for one request, state, or transaction may not remain valid when reused outside its original context. Security decisions should carry sufficient context, lifetime, or transaction binding to prevent unauthorized replay.","explanation":"Decision reuse can detach an authorization result from the conditions under which it was produced.","principle":"Security decisions should be bound to the context and lifetime for which they are valid.","rule":"Prevent reuse of security decisions outside their authorized context.","objective":"Reduce replay of stale or context-mismatched authorization.","failure_mode":"A valid authorization artifact is reused for a different resource, action, or time period.","cause":"Decision lacks binding to the original security context.","root_cause":"Authorization result is treated as a generic capability rather than a context-bound decision.","solution":"Bind decisions to subject, action, resource, relevant state, and validity interval as appropriate.","alternative":"Re-evaluate authorization at each high-impact action boundary.","tradeoff":"Stronger binding can reduce decision reuse and increase evaluation overhead.","edge_case":"Delegated capabilities may intentionally be reusable but require explicit scope and lifetime constraints.","security_implication":"Replay can convert a legitimate authorization into unauthorized access.","test_case":"Reuse an authorization artifact against a different resource or after its validity period.","expected_test_result":"The system rejects the replay.","verification_method":"Test context mismatch, expiration, and duplicate-use conditions.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B45-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Race Conditions","concept":"Distributed consistency and security state","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"What security lesson applies when multiple components hold different views of authorization state?","answer":"A distributed system can temporarily expose inconsistent security state. High-impact authorization should account for the consistency guarantees required by the security property rather than assuming eventual convergence is always sufficient.","explanation":"Eventual consistency may be acceptable for some informational data but unsafe for decisions where a stale authorization state could cause material harm.","principle":"Security consistency requirements must be derived from the protected invariant.","rule":"Match authorization-state consistency to the consequence of stale decisions.","objective":"Prevent distributed state lag from creating exploitable authorization windows.","failure_mode":"One component continues accepting revoked authority because its state has not converged.","cause":"Security decision relies on eventually consistent state without an acceptable staleness bound.","root_cause":"Consistency requirements were not derived from the security consequence.","solution":"Define explicit freshness and consistency requirements for security-critical state.","alternative":"Centralize high-impact authorization decisions or use strongly consistent state where justified.","tradeoff":"Stronger consistency can increase latency, availability constraints, or infrastructure complexity.","edge_case":"Low-risk operations may tolerate bounded staleness under an explicit risk model.","security_implication":"Replication lag can create temporary privilege windows.","test_case":"Revoke authority in one authoritative component and immediately exercise the action through another component.","expected_test_result":"Behavior matches the documented security consistency policy.","verification_method":"Measure and verify authorization behavior across replication boundaries.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B45-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Concurrency and Race Conditions","concept":"Concurrency security regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should security regression tests include concurrent execution?","answer":"Sequential tests may miss race conditions that only occur when security checks, state changes, and resource access overlap in time.","explanation":"Security invariants can fail only under specific interleavings. Regression suites should therefore include concurrency cases for security-sensitive state transitions.","principle":"Security correctness must account for relevant execution interleavings.","rule":"Include controlled concurrency tests for security-sensitive check-and-use operations.","objective":"Detect race-dependent security regressions.","test_case":"Run concurrent authorization, revocation, resource mutation, and access operations according to the threat model.","expected_test_result":"Security invariants remain satisfied across tested interleavings.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic synchronization points where possible so failures are reproducible.","verification_method":"Repeat controlled race tests and preserve the exact synchronization scenario used.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B45-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B46-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Auditability and Evidence","concept":"Security event semantics","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must security logs describe what actually happened rather than merely record that a function was called?","answer":"Security investigations require meaningful event semantics such as actor, action, target, outcome, relevant context, and time. A generic function-call log may not establish whether the protected action succeeded or failed.","explanation":"Audit evidence is useful only when its meaning is sufficiently precise to reconstruct security-relevant events.","principle":"Audit records should represent security-relevant outcomes and context, not merely implementation activity.","rule":"Define explicit semantics for security audit events.","objective":"Make security evidence interpretable and suitable for investigation and verification.","failure_mode":"Logs show an authorization check occurred but cannot establish whether access was granted or data was changed.","cause":"Logging is coupled to code execution rather than security semantics.","root_cause":"Audit requirements were not defined at the security-event level.","solution":"Model security events with explicit actor, action, target, decision, outcome, and contextual fields as applicable.","alternative":"Use structured security-event schemas shared across enforcement components.","tradeoff":"Richer audit records require more storage and careful data-protection controls.","edge_case":"Some security actions may have asynchronous outcomes requiring correlated event records.","security_implication":"Ambiguous audit evidence can delay detection and prevent reliable reconstruction of security failures.","test_case":"Perform allowed and denied security actions and inspect whether logs distinguish their outcomes.","expected_test_result":"Audit records unambiguously represent the relevant security events.","verification_method":"Review event schemas and test representative security operations.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B2-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B46-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Auditability and Evidence","concept":"Audit evidence integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must security audit records themselves be protected against unauthorized modification?","answer":"If security evidence can be altered without detection, investigators cannot reliably distinguish original events from later manipulation.","explanation":"Audit data is part of the evidence chain for security decisions and incident reconstruction. Its integrity and access controls therefore require explicit treatment.","principle":"Security evidence requires its own integrity and access-control model.","rule":"Protect audit records against unauthorized modification and define detection or retention controls appropriate to their purpose.","objective":"Preserve trustworthy security evidence.","failure_mode":"An attacker modifies or deletes records that could reveal security activity.","cause":"Audit storage has weaker protection than the systems it monitors.","root_cause":"Evidence integrity was not included in the audit threat model.","solution":"Use access-controlled, tamper-evident, append-oriented, or independently protected audit storage as appropriate.","alternative":"Export critical audit events to a separately governed security system.","tradeoff":"Independent protection adds infrastructure and data-management requirements.","edge_case":"Even protected logs may have collection gaps and should not be treated as complete evidence without qualification.","security_implication":"Compromised audit evidence can conceal unauthorized activity and distort root-cause analysis.","test_case":"Attempt unauthorized modification or deletion of an audit record.","expected_test_result":"The modification is prevented or detectable according to the defined evidence-integrity model.","verification_method":"Test audit storage permissions and tamper-detection mechanisms.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B2-R01","D57-B46-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B46-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Auditability and Evidence","concept":"Audit completeness versus audit volume","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why does generating more security logs not necessarily improve security evidence?","answer":"Security evidence quality depends on coverage, semantics, integrity, correlation, and usability rather than raw event volume. Excessive low-value records can obscure important events.","explanation":"A large event stream can still omit the security events needed to establish causality or outcome.","principle":"Audit coverage and evidentiary quality matter more than raw log volume.","rule":"Define audit requirements around security-relevant events and investigative questions.","objective":"Capture sufficient evidence without treating event count as a security metric.","failure_mode":"Critical security actions are not recorded clearly despite high log volume.","cause":"Logging strategy optimizes quantity rather than evidentiary coverage.","root_cause":"No explicit mapping exists between security questions and required audit events.","solution":"Map security controls and investigative requirements to structured audit events.","alternative":"Use risk-based audit coverage with explicit exclusions and documented assumptions.","tradeoff":"Targeted logging can reduce volume but requires stronger design discipline.","edge_case":"High-volume telemetry may be useful for anomaly analysis but does not replace authoritative audit events.","security_implication":"Missing high-value evidence can impair detection and post-event reconstruction.","test_case":"Map critical security operations to required audit records and identify missing coverage.","expected_test_result":"Each defined critical operation has an explicit evidence path or documented limitation.","verification_method":"Perform an audit-coverage review against security requirements.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B46-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B46-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Auditability and Evidence","concept":"Correlation integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when events from multiple systems cannot be reliably correlated?","answer":"Without trustworthy correlation identifiers and consistent temporal or contextual metadata, independently valid events may not be sufficient to reconstruct the sequence of a security action.","explanation":"Distributed security analysis frequently depends on joining identity, request, resource, decision, and outcome events across components.","principle":"Security event correlation must preserve the relationship between related actions.","rule":"Use stable correlation context appropriate to the event lifecycle and protect it from attacker-controlled ambiguity.","objective":"Enable reliable reconstruction of distributed security events.","failure_mode":"Investigation cannot establish whether a denial, execution, and resulting state change belong to the same operation.","cause":"Events lack consistent or trustworthy correlation information.","root_cause":"Distributed audit model does not define event relationships.","solution":"Define correlation identifiers and contextual fields for security-relevant workflows.","alternative":"Use transaction or trace identifiers where their security semantics are appropriate.","tradeoff":"Additional metadata increases storage and processing requirements.","edge_case":"Correlation identifiers can themselves contain sensitive information and require data-protection controls.","security_implication":"Broken correlation can conceal attack chains or produce incorrect conclusions.","test_case":"Execute a multi-component security-sensitive operation and trace all resulting audit events.","expected_test_result":"Related events can be reliably associated without relying solely on attacker-controlled fields.","verification_method":"Perform end-to-end event-correlation tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B46-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B46-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Auditability and Evidence","concept":"Audit gaps as explicit uncertainty","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How should missing security evidence be represented in a lesson record?","answer":"A missing audit record should be represented as an evidence gap or uncertainty rather than silently inferred as evidence that an event did not occur.","explanation":"Absence of a record can result from logging failure, collection loss, retention limits, filtering, or actual absence. These possibilities must not be collapsed into a single conclusion without supporting evidence.","principle":"Absence of evidence is not automatically evidence of absence.","rule":"Represent audit gaps explicitly and preserve alternative explanations until evidence resolves them.","objective":"Prevent incomplete telemetry from becoming false security conclusions.","failure_mode":"An investigation concludes that no event occurred solely because no corresponding log exists.","cause":"Logging completeness is assumed without verification.","root_cause":"Evidence absence is incorrectly treated as event absence.","solution":"Record the gap, assess logging coverage, and distinguish unknown from disproven.","alternative":"Use independent evidence sources to corroborate the event state.","tradeoff":"Explicit uncertainty can make conclusions less definitive but improves evidentiary integrity.","edge_case":"A cryptographically complete audit stream with verified coverage may provide stronger evidence that an event was absent during the covered interval.","security_implication":"False certainty from logging gaps can lead to incorrect containment or remediation decisions.","test_case":"Remove or suppress a required audit event and evaluate lesson generation.","expected_test_result":"The resulting knowledge identifies an evidence gap rather than asserting that the event did not occur.","verification_method":"Inspect evidence-state handling for missing and incomplete telemetry.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B2-R02","D57-B2-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B47-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Control Coverage","concept":"Preventive control coverage","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should a security lesson identify which attack stage or failure condition a preventive control actually addresses?","answer":"A control may prevent one condition without covering adjacent stages or alternative paths. Explicit coverage mapping prevents a narrow control from being interpreted as complete protection.","explanation":"Security defenses are generally scoped to particular assets, actions, assumptions, or attack conditions.","principle":"Every security control has a defined coverage boundary.","rule":"Document the protected condition and known exclusions for material security controls.","objective":"Prevent overgeneralization of control effectiveness.","failure_mode":"A control designed for one attack path is assumed to prevent all variants of the threat.","cause":"Coverage boundaries are undocumented.","root_cause":"Control semantics are expressed as broad security claims instead of explicit protected conditions.","solution":"Record control objective, coverage, assumptions, exclusions, and verification method.","alternative":"Compose multiple complementary controls with explicit coverage mapping.","tradeoff":"Detailed coverage modeling requires additional maintenance.","edge_case":"A control may reduce risk without preventing the attack completely.","security_implication":"Overstated coverage can leave unrecognized attack paths untreated.","test_case":"Map a security control against multiple attack paths and identify unsupported paths.","expected_test_result":"Covered and uncovered conditions are explicitly distinguished.","verification_method":"Review control-to-threat coverage mapping.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B47-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Control Coverage","concept":"Defense in depth without duplicated semantics","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What is the useful security lesson from using multiple controls for one important threat?","answer":"Independent or differently situated controls can reduce dependence on a single failure point, but merely duplicating the same assumption does not necessarily create meaningful defense in depth.","explanation":"Two controls that depend on the same identity source, configuration, or vulnerable component may fail together.","principle":"Defense in depth depends on meaningful independence of failure modes, not control count.","rule":"Evaluate shared dependencies when claiming layered protection.","objective":"Distinguish genuinely complementary controls from redundant appearances of protection.","failure_mode":"Multiple controls fail simultaneously because they share the same compromised dependency.","cause":"Layering was measured by number of controls rather than independence.","root_cause":"Common-mode failure was omitted from security architecture analysis.","solution":"Map shared dependencies and diversify critical trust assumptions where justified.","alternative":"Use a single stronger control when duplicated layers add no independent protection.","tradeoff":"Independence can increase complexity and operational cost.","edge_case":"Some common dependencies are unavoidable and should instead be treated as explicit concentration risks.","security_implication":"Apparent defense in depth may provide little additional resistance to a common-mode compromise.","test_case":"Identify a shared dependency between two supposedly independent security controls and simulate its failure.","expected_test_result":"The analysis reveals the resulting common-mode security exposure.","verification_method":"Perform dependency and failure-mode analysis across layered controls.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B47-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B47-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Control Coverage","concept":"Control dependency failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should a security lesson capture when a control fails because a dependency fails?","answer":"The lesson should distinguish the control's local behavior from the dependency failure and identify whether the control fails safely, fails open, or becomes unavailable.","explanation":"A control can be correctly implemented while still depending on an external service, key, policy source, clock, network, or data feed. The dependency failure behavior is therefore part of the control's security semantics.","principle":"Security-control resilience includes defined behavior under dependency failure.","rule":"Document and test security behavior when critical dependencies are unavailable or corrupted.","objective":"Prevent dependency failures from becoming undocumented security bypasses.","failure_mode":"A security control becomes permissive when its dependency is unavailable.","cause":"Fallback behavior prioritizes availability without explicit security semantics.","root_cause":"Dependency failure was omitted from the control's threat and failure model.","solution":"Define fail-closed, fail-safe, or bounded fallback behavior according to the protected property.","alternative":"Remove the dependency from high-impact authorization paths where feasible.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior can reduce availability during dependency outages.","edge_case":"Some low-risk operations may legitimately continue under bounded degraded security behavior.","security_implication":"Dependency failure can become an indirect attack vector against the control.","test_case":"Disable a critical security dependency while attempting a protected action.","expected_test_result":"The system follows documented secure failure behavior.","verification_method":"Execute dependency-failure tests and inspect both authorization and audit outcomes.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B47-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Control Coverage","concept":"Control bypass path discovery","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What is a useful method for extracting a security lesson from a control bypass?","answer":"Identify the protected invariant, enumerate every path capable of changing the protected state, determine which path lacked enforcement, and update the control model and verification coverage accordingly.","explanation":"Fixing only the observed bypass can leave equivalent alternate paths exposed. The lesson should therefore generalize around the invariant and path class rather than the single endpoint.","principle":"Learn from the violated security invariant, not only the specific bypass location.","rule":"After a bypass, search for structurally equivalent enforcement gaps.","objective":"Convert a localized bypass into broader preventive knowledge without overgeneralizing.","requirements":["Identify the violated invariant.","Enumerate alternative paths.","Determine control coverage.","Add regression verification."],"test_case":"Given a bypass in one interface, identify and test other interfaces capable of performing the same protected action.","expected_test_result":"Equivalent paths are either protected or explicitly documented as out of scope.","verification_method":"Perform attack-surface equivalence analysis after remediation.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R01","D57-B47-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B47-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Control Coverage","concept":"Control removal as a security change","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should removing a security control trigger security analysis even when application functionality remains unchanged?","answer":"Removing a control changes the system's security properties even if its functional behavior appears unchanged. Dependencies, attack paths, residual risk, and compensating controls must therefore be reassessed.","explanation":"Security controls are part of the system's effective architecture, not merely optional implementation details.","principle":"Security architecture changes include removal or weakening of controls.","rule":"Treat material control removal as a security-impacting change.","objective":"Prevent unnoticed expansion of the attack surface or residual risk.","failure_mode":"A control is removed for maintenance or simplification without reassessing the protected property.","cause":"Change review considers functional impact but not security-control coverage.","root_cause":"Security controls are excluded from architectural dependency analysis.","solution":"Require security-impact analysis and verification for material control removal.","alternative":"Replace the control with a documented equivalent before removal.","tradeoff":"Additional review can slow apparently simple changes.","edge_case":"A truly redundant control may be removable if independent evidence demonstrates equivalent remaining coverage.","security_implication":"Control removal can silently create gaps in preventive or detective defenses.","test_case":"Remove a material control and execute its former security acceptance tests.","expected_test_result":"The change is blocked, compensated, or explicitly risk-accepted according to policy.","verification_method":"Review change-control evidence and security regression results.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B47-R01","D57-B5-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B48-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design and Change","concept":"Threat model drift","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can a previously adequate threat model become incomplete without any explicit security defect in the original design?","answer":"Architecture, dependencies, assets, interfaces, attacker capabilities, and deployment environments change over time. A threat model can therefore become stale even when the original assumptions were reasonable.","explanation":"Security assurance is conditional on assumptions. Changes can invalidate those assumptions without modifying the original control code.","principle":"Threat models require lifecycle maintenance.","rule":"Review security assumptions after material changes to assets, trust boundaries, dependencies, or attacker capabilities.","objective":"Detect security gaps created by environmental or architectural change.","failure_mode":"Existing controls continue to be trusted after their underlying assumptions become invalid.","cause":"Threat model is not linked to change triggers.","root_cause":"Security assumptions lack lifecycle ownership.","solution":"Associate material architecture changes with threat-model review requirements.","alternative":"Maintain machine-readable security assumptions linked to affected components.","tradeoff":"Continuous review adds governance effort.","edge_case":"A change may be operationally large but security-neutral, while a small boundary change may be security-critical.","security_implication":"Threat-model drift can create unrecognized attack paths.","test_case":"Modify a trust boundary or external dependency and inspect whether relevant threat assumptions are re-evaluated.","expected_test_result":"Affected assumptions are identified and either revalidated or updated.","verification_method":"Trace architecture changes to threat-model review records.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B48-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design and Change","concept":"Security assumption inventory","knowledge_type":"KNOWLEDGE_MODEL","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why should important security assumptions be represented explicitly in a security knowledge system?","answer":"Explicit assumptions allow the system to determine when evidence, architecture, or version changes may invalidate a security conclusion.","explanation":"A lesson without its assumptions can be incorrectly reused in a different context. Capturing assumptions supports applicability analysis and contradiction detection.","principle":"Security knowledge should preserve the conditions under which a conclusion is valid.","rule":"Record material security assumptions separately from conclusions.","objective":"Prevent context-free reuse of security lessons.","requirements":["Identify assumptions that materially affect the conclusion.","Link assumptions to affected controls or components.","Define review triggers where possible."],"failure_mode":"A security lesson is applied to a system that violates an unstated original assumption.","cause":"Knowledge record contains conclusion but not applicability conditions.","root_cause":"Security knowledge model does not preserve assumptions.","solution":"Add explicit assumption and applicability metadata to security lessons.","alternative":"Represent lessons as conditional rules with machine-readable predicates.","tradeoff":"More metadata improves precision but increases dataset complexity.","edge_case":"Some foundational principles have minimal contextual assumptions and can be represented more generally.","security_implication":"Hidden assumptions can produce false security confidence.","test_case":"Apply a lesson to a deliberately changed context and verify whether applicability metadata detects the mismatch.","expected_test_result":"The knowledge system flags the lesson for review or identifies it as conditionally applicable.","verification_method":"Evaluate lesson retrieval against changed assumptions.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B48-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B48-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design and Change","concept":"Security regression from feature interaction","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can two individually acceptable security features create a new security problem when combined?","answer":"Feature interactions can create emergent behavior that neither component's isolated security analysis covered. Combined authorization, data-flow, state, and fallback behavior must therefore be evaluated for material interactions.","explanation":"Security properties can depend on composition. A feature that is safe in isolation may expose a new path when combined with another feature.","principle":"Security assurance must account for relevant composition effects.","rule":"Analyze material security-sensitive feature interactions rather than validating components only in isolation.","objective":"Detect emergent security failures caused by composition.","failure_mode":"Two individually tested controls interact to create an unintended bypass.","cause":"Testing and threat analysis cover components independently but not their composition.","root_cause":"Security model lacks interaction analysis.","solution":"Add interaction-focused threat analysis and regression tests for security-sensitive combinations.","alternative":"Restrict combinations when their security semantics cannot be reliably composed.","tradeoff":"Interaction testing grows with feature combinations and requires risk-based selection.","edge_case":"Combinatorial explosion makes exhaustive testing impractical; high-impact combinations require prioritization.","security_implication":"Composition failures can bypass controls that appear correct individually.","test_case":"Enable two security-sensitive features together and exercise their combined state transitions.","expected_test_result":"Security invariants remain satisfied or documented incompatibility prevents unsafe composition.","verification_method":"Perform pairwise or risk-based interaction testing.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B47-R02","D57-B5-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B48-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design and Change","concept":"Security invariant preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What is a useful way to verify security after a substantial architectural change?","answer":"Identify the security invariants that must remain true, map each invariant to affected components and controls, and verify them under the new architecture.","explanation":"Component-level regression alone may miss changes in trust boundaries or control composition. Invariants provide a stable security target across implementation changes.","principle":"Security verification should preserve properties, not merely implementation structure.","rule":"Re-evaluate affected security invariants after material architectural changes.","objective":"Maintain security properties across implementation evolution.","test_case":"For each affected invariant, execute positive and negative tests against the changed architecture.","expected_test_result":"Each invariant has current evidence or an explicitly documented unresolved gap.","testing_guidance":"Do not treat unchanged code as evidence that its security assumptions remain unchanged.","verification_method":"Perform invariant-to-control traceability review plus targeted regression tests.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B48-R01","D57-B47-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B48-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Design and Change","concept":"Security exception accumulation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why can individually justified security exceptions become a systemic security problem?","answer":"Exceptions can accumulate until the combined system no longer resembles the security model under which each exception was approved.","explanation":"Each exception may be bounded, but interactions, forgotten expiration dates, ownership changes, and increasing scope can create aggregate exposure.","principle":"Security exceptions require lifecycle and aggregate-risk management.","rule":"Track scope, owner, justification, compensating controls, and expiry for material security exceptions.","objective":"Prevent temporary exceptions from becoming unmanaged permanent security state.","failure_mode":"Multiple expired or overlapping exceptions remain active without review.","cause":"Exception records lack expiration or ownership enforcement.","root_cause":"Exceptions are treated as one-time approvals rather than governed security state.","solution":"Implement explicit exception lifecycle controls and periodic aggregate review.","alternative":"Eliminate recurring exceptions through architectural remediation.","tradeoff":"Exception governance adds administrative overhead.","edge_case":"Emergency exceptions may need very short lifetimes and rapid revocation mechanisms.","security_implication":"Exception accumulation can erode defense-in-depth and least-privilege guarantees.","test_case":"Create an expired security exception and attempt the associated operation.","expected_test_result":"The exception no longer authorizes the operation after its defined lifetime.","verification_method":"Review exception lifecycle metadata and execute expiry tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B6-R03","D57-B48-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B49-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Human and Process Controls","concept":"Security approval is not technical enforcement","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is a human approval record insufficient evidence that a security-sensitive action was technically authorized?","answer":"An approval documents an authorization decision by a person or process, while technical enforcement determines whether the system actually constrained execution to that decision.","explanation":"Approval workflows can be bypassed, misapplied, replayed, or disconnected from the action they were intended to authorize.","principle":"Governance approval and technical enforcement are complementary but distinct controls.","rule":"Bind approvals to specific actions, scope, identity, and validity conditions, then enforce them technically.","objective":"Prevent approval records from becoming unenforced authorization claims.","failure_mode":"A valid approval exists but the approved scope does not match the executed action.","cause":"Approval is recorded independently from action enforcement.","root_cause":"Governance and execution models are not cryptographically or logically bound where required.","solution":"Bind approval state to the exact protected action and enforce the binding at execution time.","alternative":"Use policy-driven authorization with human approval as an additional condition for high-impact actions.","tradeoff":"Strong binding can reduce workflow flexibility.","edge_case":"Emergency actions may use special approval semantics requiring enhanced auditing.","security_implication":"Unbound approvals can permit actions outside the authorized scope.","test_case":"Modify action scope after approval and attempt execution.","expected_test_result":"Execution is rejected because the approval no longer matches the requested action.","verification_method":"Test approval scope, identity, expiration, and action-binding semantics.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B49-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Human and Process Controls","concept":"Separation of duties as a security control","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson does separation of duties provide for high-impact actions?","answer":"Separating incompatible authorities can reduce the chance that one compromised or mistaken identity can independently complete a high-impact operation.","explanation":"Separation of duties is a governance control that limits concentration of authority. Its effectiveness depends on clearly defined incompatible roles and technically enforced boundaries where required.","principle":"Critical authority can be reduced by dividing control across independent roles or conditions.","rule":"Use separation of duties where the threat model identifies concentrated authority as material risk.","objective":"Reduce single-actor control over high-impact security operations.","failure_mode":"One identity can request, approve, and execute a sensitive operation without independent constraint.","cause":"Authority is concentrated in one role or credential.","root_cause":"Security governance did not model conflicting responsibilities.","solution":"Separate request, approval, and execution authority according to risk.","alternative":"Use automated policy gates or independent control services where human separation is impractical.","tradeoff":"Separation can increase operational latency and coordination cost.","edge_case":"Small teams may require alternative controls when organizational separation is impossible.","security_implication":"Compromise of one privileged identity has greater impact when duties are concentrated.","test_case":"Attempt to complete a protected operation using only one role when two independent authorities are required.","expected_test_result":"The operation cannot complete without satisfying the required independent conditions.","verification_method":"Review role constraints and test unauthorized role combinations.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B49-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B49-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Human and Process Controls","concept":"Approval context integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must security approvals preserve the context in which they were granted?","answer":"An approval without trustworthy context may be reused for a different resource, environment, version, scope, or risk condition than the approver evaluated.","explanation":"Security decisions are conditional on the information available at decision time. Changing that context can invalidate the original approval.","principle":"A security approval is meaningful only within its authorized context.","rule":"Bind material approvals to relevant subject, action, target, scope, version, and validity conditions.","objective":"Prevent context substitution in security approvals.","failure_mode":"An approval for one environment or resource is reused for another.","cause":"Approval record contains insufficient contextual binding.","root_cause":"Approval model treats authorization as an unscoped boolean.","solution":"Use explicit approval scope and contextual binding.","alternative":"Require fresh approval whenever defined security-relevant context changes.","tradeoff":"Context binding may increase approval frequency.","edge_case":"Stable, low-risk actions may use reusable approvals under tightly bounded policies.","security_implication":"Context substitution can turn legitimate approval into unauthorized execution.","test_case":"Change a security-relevant approval attribute after approval and attempt execution.","expected_test_result":"Execution is rejected or requires reapproval according to policy.","verification_method":"Test each material approval-binding attribute independently.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B49-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B49-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Human and Process Controls","concept":"Human-review evidence quality","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should security approval records capture decision rationale and relevant evidence rather than only an approval flag?","answer":"A binary approval does not preserve what was reviewed, which assumptions were accepted, or what evidence supported the decision. Structured rationale improves later verification and lesson extraction.","explanation":"Security governance benefits from reconstructable decision context, especially for high-impact or exceptional actions.","principle":"Security decisions should preserve sufficient evidence to explain their authorization context.","rule":"Record material decision inputs, scope, authority, rationale, and relevant evidence references where policy requires.","objective":"Make security governance decisions auditable and reusable as learning evidence.","failure_mode":"A later reviewer cannot determine why a sensitive action was approved.","cause":"Approval system records only the final boolean decision.","root_cause":"Decision provenance was not modeled.","solution":"Capture structured decision metadata and evidence references.","alternative":"Link approval records to immutable review artifacts.","tradeoff":"Richer approval records increase storage and privacy considerations.","edge_case":"Sensitive rationale may itself require restricted access.","security_implication":"Weak decision evidence can prevent effective post-event analysis and accountability.","test_case":"Review a historical approval and determine whether its scope, authority, and evidence can be reconstructed.","expected_test_result":"Required governance context is available to authorized reviewers.","verification_method":"Audit approval-record completeness against defined security requirements.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B2-R01","D57-B49-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B49-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Human and Process Controls","concept":"Security control bypass by emergency procedure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when emergency procedures bypass ordinary security controls?","answer":"Emergency procedures can be necessary, but bypasses should have explicit scope, authority, duration, compensating controls, and post-action review rather than becoming undocumented permanent alternatives.","explanation":"Emergency paths often receive less testing and may have broader authority. Their security semantics must therefore be deliberately governed.","principle":"Emergency capability is still part of the security architecture.","rule":"Govern emergency security bypasses with explicit authorization, time bounds, auditing, and recovery of normal controls.","objective":"Limit security exposure introduced by emergency operations.","failure_mode":"An emergency bypass remains active after the emergency or becomes a routine alternative path.","cause":"Emergency state lacks expiry and lifecycle enforcement.","root_cause":"Emergency procedures are treated as exceptional documentation rather than governed system state.","solution":"Implement explicit emergency-state controls and mandatory post-event review.","alternative":"Design emergency operations to preserve ordinary security controls while increasing authorized capability.","tradeoff":"Strong emergency governance can slow urgent response.","edge_case":"Life-safety or service-continuity conditions may require temporary deviations under predefined policy.","security_implication":"Emergency paths can become persistent privileged attack surfaces.","test_case":"Activate an emergency procedure and allow its defined validity period to expire.","expected_test_result":"Emergency authority is revoked or transitions back to ordinary controls as specified.","verification_method":"Test activation, authorization, expiry, audit, and recovery of emergency state.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B48-R05","D57-B49-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B50-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Lesson Operationalization","concept":"Lesson-to-control traceability","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How should a reusable security lesson be connected to preventive or detective controls?","answer":"The lesson should identify the security property or failure condition it addresses and link that condition to concrete controls, tests, monitoring, or governance mechanisms.","explanation":"A lesson that cannot influence a control, test, decision, or review process remains descriptive rather than operationally useful.","principle":"Security learning should be traceable to mechanisms that can change future system behavior or verification.","rule":"Link reusable lessons to affected controls and verification artifacts without claiming that the lesson itself proves control effectiveness.","objective":"Convert security knowledge into actionable engineering and assurance relationships.","requirements":["Identify the lesson's security condition.","Map relevant controls.","Map verification methods.","Preserve evidence status."],"failure_mode":"A lesson is stored but never connected to engineering controls or tests.","cause":"Knowledge management is separated from security-control lifecycle.","root_cause":"No traceability model connects lessons to preventive or detective mechanisms.","solution":"Create explicit lesson-to-control and lesson-to-test relationships.","alternative":"Use a security knowledge graph linking lessons, controls, requirements, and verification artifacts.","tradeoff":"Traceability maintenance increases metadata workload.","edge_case":"Some lessons may be architectural principles rather than directly implementable controls.","security_implication":"Unconnected lessons have limited value for preventing recurrence.","test_case":"Retrieve a lesson and verify that relevant control and verification relationships are available.","expected_test_result":"The knowledge record identifies applicable controls and evidence limitations.","verification_method":"Perform graph-traceability validation from lesson to control and test.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B50-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Lesson Operationalization","concept":"Lesson promotion criteria","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What evidence should be considered before promoting a security lesson into a reusable rule?","answer":"Promotion should consider evidence quality, scope, reproducibility, causal support, applicability conditions, contradictions, and whether the proposed rule overgeneralizes the underlying observation.","explanation":"A single event or weak inference can be useful for investigation without being sufficient to establish a broad engineering rule.","principle":"Knowledge promotion requires stronger evidence than knowledge capture.","rule":"Do not promote a context-specific or weakly supported observation into a universal security rule without sufficient evidence.","objective":"Prevent the security knowledge base from turning uncertain observations into false facts.","requirements":["Assess evidence quality.","Assess scope and assumptions.","Check contradictions.","Separate observation from inference.","Define confidence and applicability."],"failure_mode":"A single incident-derived observation becomes a universal security rule.","cause":"Knowledge promotion ignores evidence scope and uncertainty.","root_cause":"No distinction exists between captured observations and reusable verified knowledge.","solution":"Use explicit promotion states such as observation, inference, candidate lesson, and reusable rule.","alternative":"Require independent corroboration or repeatable test evidence for higher-confidence promotion.","tradeoff":"Stricter promotion reduces false generalization but slows knowledge growth.","edge_case":"A foundational security principle may have strong external evidence even without local operational observations.","security_implication":"False security rules can cause inappropriate controls or hide important exceptions.","test_case":"Submit a single context-specific observation for rule promotion.","expected_test_result":"The system preserves it as bounded knowledge rather than automatically promoting it to a universal rule.","verification_method":"Inspect knowledge-promotion state transitions and evidence thresholds.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B50-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Lesson Operationalization","concept":"Lesson contradiction handling","knowledge_type":"CONFLICT_RESOLUTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How should a security knowledge system handle two lessons that appear to conflict?","answer":"It should preserve both claims with their evidence and applicability conditions, determine whether they truly address the same context, and mark the relationship as compatible, conditional, contradicted, or superseded only when evidence supports that relationship.","explanation":"Apparent contradictions may result from different threat models, versions, architectures, assumptions, or evidence quality.","principle":"Contradiction resolution requires context and provenance rather than deletion of inconvenient knowledge.","rule":"Do not silently overwrite conflicting security lessons.","objective":"Preserve uncertainty and context while preventing contradictory rules from being applied indiscriminately.","failure_mode":"A newer lesson deletes an older lesson even though the two apply to different contexts.","cause":"Knowledge store treats duplicate-looking claims as mutually exclusive.","root_cause":"Context and applicability are not represented in contradiction handling.","solution":"Store explicit contradiction metadata, applicability conditions, evidence strength, and supersession relationships.","alternative":"Partition lessons by version, architecture, or threat model when their scopes differ.","tradeoff":"Richer contradiction modeling increases retrieval complexity.","edge_case":"A later authoritative correction may genuinely supersede an earlier claim and should retain the historical relationship.","security_implication":"Incorrect contradiction resolution can cause the system to apply an unsafe rule in the wrong context.","test_case":"Insert two context-dependent security lessons with opposing conclusions.","expected_test_result":"The system preserves both and distinguishes their applicability rather than silently choosing one.","verification_method":"Run semantic contradiction and applicability tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B10-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B50-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Lesson Operationalization","concept":"Lesson regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should important security lessons become regression tests when they describe preventable technical behavior?","answer":"A regression test converts a lesson into repeatable evidence that a known failure condition remains prevented. The test does not prove universal security, but it preserves a specific learned invariant.","explanation":"Lessons can decay as code and architecture change. Regression tests provide a repeatable mechanism for detecting recurrence of the modeled failure.","principle":"A technically actionable lesson should be connected to repeatable verification where practical.","rule":"Translate preventable technical failure lessons into regression tests with explicit scope and expected behavior.","objective":"Prevent recurrence of known security failure modes.","test_case":"Execute the security regression test associated with a previously identified failure condition.","expected_test_result":"The previously prevented failure condition remains rejected or otherwise controlled.","testing_guidance":"Preserve the original failure condition, security invariant, environment assumptions, and expected result.","verification_method":"Run the test under supported versions and review whether its assumptions remain valid.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B47-R04","D57-B48-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B50-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Lesson Operationalization","concept":"Security lesson closure","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"When can a security lesson be considered operationally closed?","answer":"A lesson should be considered closed only when the intended corrective or preventive mechanism is implemented, the relevant security property has been verified within defined scope, residual risk is recorded, ownership is established, and evidence is preserved.","explanation":"Implementing a change alone does not establish that the security issue is resolved. Closure requires a complete chain from lesson to remediation, verification, and residual-risk understanding.","principle":"Security closure requires evidence of outcome, not merely completion of an action item.","rule":"Separate remediation completion from security-lesson closure.","objective":"Prevent administrative completion from being mistaken for verified security improvement.","requirements":["Corrective action implemented or otherwise addressed.","Security behavior verified.","Residual risk documented.","Evidence preserved.","Applicability and ownership recorded."],"failure_mode":"A ticket is closed after code change without verifying the security property.","cause":"Process equates implementation completion with security resolution.","root_cause":"Closure criteria do not distinguish action execution from outcome verification.","solution":"Define explicit security closure criteria and require verification evidence.","alternative":"Use staged states such as ACTION_COMPLETED, VERIFICATION_PENDING, VERIFIED, and RESIDUAL_RISK_ACCEPTED.","tradeoff":"Stronger closure criteria increase process effort but improve evidence quality.","edge_case":"Some risks cannot be eliminated and require documented acceptance rather than claiming full resolution.","security_implication":"Premature closure allows unresolved vulnerabilities or control gaps to disappear from active tracking.","test_case":"Attempt to close a security lesson with remediation recorded but verification evidence absent.","expected_test_result":"Closure remains incomplete until required verification and residual-risk conditions are satisfied.","verification_method":"Audit the lesson lifecycle state machine against closure criteria.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B5-R01","D57-B6-R01","D57-B50-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}

{"record_id":"D57-B51-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Trust Boundaries","concept":"Network location is not identity","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should network location not be treated as proof of identity or authorization?","answer":"An address, subnet, interface, or network segment identifies a network location or routing context, not necessarily the authenticated identity or authority of the requester.","explanation":"Network topology can change, traffic can be proxied, and systems within a trusted segment can be compromised. Security decisions requiring identity should use an authoritative identity mechanism rather than location alone.","principle":"Network reachability and identity are separate security properties.","rule":"Do not grant sensitive authority solely because a request originates from an expected network location.","objective":"Prevent network-position assumptions from becoming authorization bypasses.","requirements":["Identity must be independently established where required.","Network location may be treated as contextual evidence only.","Authorization must evaluate the protected action and subject."],"failure_mode":"A sensitive endpoint trusts any request from an internal network.","cause":"Network position is treated as equivalent to authenticated authority.","root_cause":"Trust boundary is defined solely by topology.","solution":"Use authenticated identity and explicit authorization at the protected operation.","alternative":"Use network segmentation as a compensating layer rather than the sole authorization mechanism.","tradeoff":"Identity enforcement adds authentication and policy-management complexity.","edge_case":"Some low-risk infrastructure protocols may intentionally rely on network-level controls under a documented threat model.","security_implication":"Compromise of one internal host can otherwise become a path to privileged operations.","test_case":"Send a protected request from an unauthorized identity located inside the expected network segment.","expected_test_result":"The request is denied despite its network location.","verification_method":"Test authorization using identical network origin with different authenticated identities.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B8-R05","D57-B44-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B51-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Trust Boundaries","concept":"Network segmentation is not absolute isolation","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should network segmentation not automatically be described as complete security isolation?","answer":"Segmentation reduces reachable communication paths but does not guarantee that all possible management, routing, shared-service, or permitted communication paths are incapable of crossing the intended boundary.","explanation":"A segment boundary is effective only to the extent that its enforcement points and permitted flows preserve the intended security property.","principle":"Segmentation is a control with defined coverage, not an unconditional isolation guarantee.","rule":"Document permitted crossings and verify that they cannot be abused to bypass the intended boundary.","objective":"Prevent overstatement of network segmentation effectiveness.","failure_mode":"A segmented environment is assumed secure despite an overlooked allowed path.","cause":"Segmentation is treated as equivalent to complete isolation.","root_cause":"Boundary coverage and exceptions are not explicitly modeled.","solution":"Map flows, enforcement points, management paths, and exceptions.","alternative":"Use stronger isolation mechanisms where network segmentation alone does not satisfy the threat model.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation can reduce interoperability.","edge_case":"A formally enforced one-way communication path may provide stronger properties than ordinary segmentation.","security_implication":"Unmodeled allowed flows can undermine the intended trust boundary.","test_case":"Enumerate all permitted flows between two supposedly separated zones.","expected_test_result":"Every permitted path has an identified purpose and applicable security control.","verification_method":"Perform network-boundary rule review and negative connectivity testing.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B51-R01","D57-B44-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B51-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Trust Boundaries","concept":"Egress control as security boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can unrestricted outbound connectivity create security risk even when inbound access is restricted?","answer":"Outbound connectivity can enable unauthorized data transfer, command-and-control communication, dependency retrieval, or interaction with untrusted services.","explanation":"A system can be protected from direct inbound access while still possessing substantial ability to initiate external communication.","principle":"Outbound authority is also part of a system's security boundary.","rule":"Define and review outbound communication requirements for sensitive systems.","objective":"Limit unauthorized external communication and data movement.","failure_mode":"A compromised internal component establishes unrestricted outbound communication.","cause":"Egress traffic is treated as inherently trusted.","root_cause":"Network threat model focuses only on inbound exposure.","solution":"Apply risk-based egress controls and monitor exceptions.","alternative":"Use controlled outbound proxies or allowlists for high-sensitivity environments.","tradeoff":"Strict egress controls can interfere with legitimate dependencies.","edge_case":"Dynamic external services may require controlled discovery rather than static allowlists.","security_implication":"Unrestricted egress can enable exfiltration or external command channels.","test_case":"Attempt outbound communication to an unauthorized destination.","expected_test_result":"The communication is blocked or otherwise governed by the defined policy.","verification_method":"Test representative allowed and prohibited egress destinations.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B51-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B51-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Trust Boundaries","concept":"Network rule exception lifecycle","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies to permanent network-rule exceptions?","answer":"Network exceptions should have an identified purpose, owner, scope, and review or expiration condition because obsolete exceptions can preserve unintended communication paths.","explanation":"Connectivity requirements evolve. A rule that was once necessary can become an unnecessary security exposure after an architecture or dependency changes.","principle":"Network exceptions are security state and require lifecycle management.","rule":"Track material network exceptions with ownership and review conditions.","objective":"Prevent obsolete network paths from becoming permanent exposure.","failure_mode":"A historical firewall exception remains active after its original dependency disappears.","cause":"Exception has no lifecycle owner or expiration mechanism.","root_cause":"Network policy is treated as static configuration rather than governed security state.","solution":"Attach lifecycle metadata and periodic review to material exceptions.","alternative":"Use automatically expiring rules where operationally appropriate.","tradeoff":"Review and expiration controls can create maintenance overhead.","edge_case":"Critical long-lived communication may legitimately have no fixed expiration but still requires periodic review.","security_implication":"Stale exceptions can bypass intended segmentation.","test_case":"Mark a network exception as expired and attempt the associated communication.","expected_test_result":"The expired path is blocked or requires renewed authorization.","verification_method":"Inspect exception metadata and test expiry behavior.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B48-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B51-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Network Trust Boundaries","concept":"Network control verification from actual paths","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should network security controls be tested from actual reachable paths rather than only reviewing configuration?","answer":"Configuration inspection can show intended rules, but path testing provides evidence about effective connectivity, routing, intermediary behavior, and unexpected alternate paths.","explanation":"Effective network behavior can differ from an isolated configuration view because of routing, proxies, inherited rules, tunnels, or alternate interfaces.","principle":"Effective network security must be verified at the reachable-path level.","rule":"Combine configuration analysis with representative connectivity testing.","objective":"Detect differences between intended and effective network boundaries.","test_case":"Attempt representative permitted and prohibited connections through relevant network paths.","expected_test_result":"Observed connectivity matches the documented security policy.","testing_guidance":"Include alternate interfaces and intermediary paths where relevant.","verification_method":"Compare observed network behavior with the authorized flow model.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B51-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B52-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Name Resolution Security","concept":"Name resolution is security-sensitive","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can name resolution become a security concern rather than merely a connectivity function?","answer":"Applications often use resolved names to select destinations, services, or security contexts. Manipulated or unexpected resolution can redirect security-sensitive communication.","explanation":"A trusted application can still communicate with an unintended destination if name resolution is compromised or ambiguously interpreted.","principle":"Destination selection is part of the security decision when identity or trust depends on the destination.","rule":"Treat security-sensitive name resolution as part of the trust chain.","objective":"Prevent destination substitution through manipulated resolution.","failure_mode":"A security-sensitive client connects to an attacker-controlled destination because resolution was altered.","cause":"Destination trust is inferred solely from an unverified resolution result.","root_cause":"Name resolution is excluded from the security trust model.","solution":"Bind destination identity to an independently validated security property where required.","alternative":"Use authenticated service discovery or certificate-based endpoint identity.","tradeoff":"Additional destination validation increases implementation complexity.","edge_case":"Dynamic service discovery requires controlled identity mapping rather than fixed addresses.","security_implication":"Resolution compromise can redirect credentials, data, or privileged requests.","test_case":"Provide an unexpected resolution result for a security-sensitive service.","expected_test_result":"The client detects the destination mismatch or otherwise prevents unauthorized trust.","verification_method":"Test destination identity validation independently of name resolution.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B51-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B52-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Name Resolution Security","concept":"Resolution integrity versus destination authorization","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why does trusted name resolution not by itself prove that a destination is authorized?","answer":"A trustworthy resolver can provide the answer it was configured or authorized to provide, but the resulting destination may still be outside the application's intended security scope.","explanation":"Resolution integrity and authorization of the resolved destination are different properties.","principle":"Correct resolution does not automatically imply acceptable destination.","rule":"Validate destination authorization when destination identity is security-critical.","objective":"Prevent trusted resolution from being mistaken for complete endpoint authorization.","failure_mode":"A validly resolved endpoint is accepted despite being outside the permitted service identity or scope.","cause":"Resolver trust is treated as equivalent to endpoint trust.","root_cause":"Resolution and destination authorization are conflated.","solution":"Define endpoint authorization independently where required.","alternative":"Use service identities that directly bind the resolved endpoint to the intended logical service.","tradeoff":"Independent validation adds policy and identity-management complexity.","edge_case":"Public, non-sensitive services may not require additional endpoint authorization.","security_implication":"An attacker controlling legitimate resolution data may still redirect sensitive traffic.","test_case":"Resolve a valid but unauthorized endpoint for a protected service.","expected_test_result":"The client rejects the endpoint or refuses the sensitive operation.","verification_method":"Test endpoint authorization independently from resolver integrity.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B52-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B52-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Name Resolution Security","concept":"Resolution caching security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies to cached name-resolution results?","answer":"Cached resolution can remain valid beyond the security conditions under which the original destination was trusted. Cache lifetime and invalidation must therefore match the relevant security assumptions.","explanation":"Caching improves availability and efficiency but can preserve stale destination information after service or trust changes.","principle":"Cached security-relevant state has a validity lifetime.","rule":"Define security-aware freshness requirements for cached destination information.","objective":"Prevent stale resolution from directing sensitive operations to an obsolete destination.","failure_mode":"A client continues using an outdated destination after a security-relevant change.","cause":"Cache lifetime is longer than the required security validity period.","root_cause":"Freshness requirements were not considered in destination trust.","solution":"Apply appropriate cache lifetime, invalidation, and destination identity validation.","alternative":"Perform fresh resolution for high-impact operations where necessary.","tradeoff":"Shorter caching can increase dependency on resolution infrastructure.","edge_case":"Long-lived immutable service identities may safely tolerate longer cache periods under an explicit model.","security_implication":"Stale resolution can extend exposure to compromised or retired destinations.","test_case":"Change the authoritative destination and exercise the client while a cached result remains available.","expected_test_result":"Behavior follows the documented security freshness policy.","verification_method":"Test cache expiration and destination-change handling.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B52-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Name Resolution Security","concept":"Ambiguous destination representation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can different representations of the same destination create security inconsistencies?","answer":"If different components normalize or interpret names differently, one component may authorize a destination that another component interprets differently.","explanation":"Security decisions become unreliable when canonicalization and comparison semantics differ across components.","principle":"Security-sensitive destination identity requires consistent representation and comparison semantics.","rule":"Define canonical destination representation before security-sensitive comparison.","objective":"Prevent parser or representation differences from creating destination-validation bypasses.","failure_mode":"Validation accepts one representation while the connection layer interprets another.","cause":"Components use inconsistent normalization rules.","root_cause":"Destination identity semantics are not centrally defined.","solution":"Use a consistent canonical representation and verify the value at the enforcement boundary.","alternative":"Avoid complex textual identity comparisons by using cryptographically bound service identities.","tradeoff":"Canonicalization logic can introduce implementation complexity.","edge_case":"Internationalized or encoded names may require carefully defined normalization rules.","security_implication":"Representation confusion can redirect security-sensitive traffic.","test_case":"Provide equivalent-looking but differently encoded destination representations.","expected_test_result":"All security-relevant components resolve and compare the destination consistently.","verification_method":"Use parser-differential and canonicalization tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B21-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B52-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Name Resolution Security","concept":"Resolution failure semantics","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should happen when a security-sensitive destination cannot be resolved or validated?","answer":"The system should follow an explicitly defined security failure policy rather than silently substituting an untrusted destination or weakening validation.","explanation":"Availability pressure can create unsafe fallback behavior when destination trust cannot be established.","principle":"Unknown destination state must not silently become trusted destination state.","rule":"Define fail-closed or explicitly bounded fallback behavior for security-sensitive resolution failures.","objective":"Prevent resolution failures from becoming trust bypasses.","failure_mode":"Resolution failure causes fallback to an unverified endpoint.","cause":"Availability fallback overrides destination security policy.","root_cause":"Resolution failure semantics were not included in the threat model.","solution":"Define secure failure behavior and test fallback paths.","alternative":"Use independently trusted service discovery mechanisms.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior may reduce availability.","edge_case":"Preconfigured emergency destinations may be acceptable if they have equivalent trust validation.","security_implication":"Fallback destinations can become attacker-controlled communication channels.","test_case":"Disable trusted resolution and provide an unauthorized fallback destination.","expected_test_result":"The sensitive operation does not proceed to the unauthorized destination.","verification_method":"Test resolution outage and fallback behavior.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B51-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B53-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Transport Security","concept":"Encryption in transit versus endpoint authentication","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why does encrypted transport not automatically prove that the application is communicating with the intended endpoint?","answer":"Encryption protects confidentiality and integrity of the protected channel, while endpoint authentication establishes who or what is on the other end. The two properties are related but distinct.","explanation":"A secure channel to the wrong authenticated identity can still be a security failure for an application that requires a specific destination.","principle":"Channel protection and endpoint identity must both satisfy the application's security requirements.","rule":"Verify the endpoint identity required by the threat model before trusting sensitive communication.","objective":"Prevent secure communication with an unauthorized endpoint.","failure_mode":"An application accepts any encrypted connection without validating the expected service identity.","cause":"Encryption is treated as complete endpoint trust.","root_cause":"Transport confidentiality and endpoint authentication are conflated.","solution":"Validate endpoint identity according to the protocol and application security requirements.","alternative":"Use service identity mechanisms that bind channel establishment to the intended logical service.","tradeoff":"Strict endpoint identity can complicate service migration and dynamic infrastructure.","edge_case":"Internal service meshes may use dynamically issued identities rather than static host names.","security_implication":"Attackers can potentially receive protected traffic if endpoint identity is not validated.","test_case":"Present an encrypted connection using an unauthorized endpoint identity.","expected_test_result":"The client rejects the connection for security-sensitive operations.","verification_method":"Test endpoint identity validation independently from channel encryption.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B52-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B53-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Transport Security","concept":"Certificate validation failure handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is ignoring certificate or endpoint-identity validation failure a security defect?","answer":"Ignoring validation failure removes an intended trust-boundary check and can allow communication with an unauthorized endpoint.","explanation":"A validation error is security-relevant evidence. Continuing as though validation succeeded changes the security semantics of the connection.","principle":"Security validation failures must not be converted into silent success.","rule":"Do not bypass endpoint-validation failures for sensitive communication.","objective":"Preserve the trust guarantees provided by endpoint authentication.","failure_mode":"The client continues after endpoint identity validation fails.","cause":"Compatibility or availability logic suppresses security errors.","root_cause":"Validation failure handling is not security-governed.","solution":"Fail the security-sensitive operation when required endpoint validation fails.","alternative":"Provide a separately governed diagnostic mode that cannot be used for protected production operations.","tradeoff":"Strict validation can expose deployment or configuration defects earlier.","edge_case":"Local development environments may intentionally use non-production trust material under explicit isolation controls.","security_implication":"Validation bypass can enable interception or unauthorized endpoint substitution.","test_case":"Trigger endpoint-validation failure and attempt the protected operation.","expected_test_result":"The protected operation does not proceed under invalid trust conditions.","verification_method":"Inspect error-handling paths and perform negative trust-validation tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B53-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Transport Security","concept":"Certificate expiration and lifecycle","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is certificate lifecycle management part of security rather than only availability management?","answer":"Expired, revoked, misissued, or otherwise invalid certificates can alter endpoint trust decisions. Lifecycle management therefore directly affects authentication and trust.","explanation":"A certificate represents an identity assertion within a trust framework. Its validity conditions must remain aligned with the security policy.","principle":"Credential lifecycle is part of the security control that uses the credential.","rule":"Monitor and govern certificate issuance, validity, renewal, revocation, and replacement.","objective":"Prevent stale or invalid identity credentials from undermining endpoint trust.","failure_mode":"An expired or revoked credential remains accepted or emergency workarounds disable validation.","cause":"Lifecycle monitoring and replacement processes are inadequate.","root_cause":"Credential lifecycle is treated as an operational afterthought.","solution":"Define lifecycle ownership, renewal, revocation, and failure handling.","alternative":"Automate credential renewal within a controlled trust framework.","tradeoff":"Automation requires secure provisioning and monitoring.","edge_case":"Offline systems may require manually managed long-lived credentials under stronger compensating controls.","security_implication":"Lifecycle failures can cause either unauthorized trust or pressure to disable security validation.","test_case":"Present an expired or revoked credential to a security-sensitive client.","expected_test_result":"The credential is rejected according to the trust policy.","verification_method":"Test expiration, revocation, replacement, and failure scenarios.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B53-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Transport Security","concept":"Protocol version policy","knowledge_type":"RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should supported transport protocol versions be governed explicitly?","answer":"Older or unsupported protocol versions may lack required security properties or may expose known weaknesses. The acceptable protocol set should therefore be defined according to the system's security requirements and supported implementations.","explanation":"Protocol negotiation can select behavior that is technically interoperable but outside the intended security policy.","principle":"Interoperability is not equivalent to acceptable security.","rule":"Define and verify the permitted protocol versions and security properties.","objective":"Prevent downgrade to unsupported or insufficiently secure protocol behavior.","failure_mode":"A client negotiates an obsolete protocol because compatibility is prioritized without policy constraints.","cause":"Protocol compatibility is broader than security policy.","root_cause":"Supported-version policy is not explicit.","solution":"Configure an explicit acceptable protocol range and test negotiation behavior.","alternative":"Use a protocol stack with centrally managed security policy.","tradeoff":"Restricting versions can reduce compatibility with legacy systems.","edge_case":"Legacy interoperability may require isolated compensating controls.","security_implication":"Protocol downgrade can weaken confidentiality, integrity, or authentication guarantees.","test_case":"Offer a prohibited older protocol version during negotiation.","expected_test_result":"The connection is rejected or negotiated only within the permitted security range.","verification_method":"Perform protocol-version negotiation tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B53-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Transport Security","concept":"Transport security scope","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should security analysis continue after an encrypted transport channel is established?","answer":"Transport protection does not automatically secure application-layer authorization, data handling, endpoint behavior, or downstream communication after termination.","explanation":"A protected channel is one security layer. The application and its downstream dependencies can still mishandle authenticated data or authorize an unsafe action.","principle":"Transport security is necessary for some threats but is not a complete application security model.","rule":"Evaluate security properties above and beyond transport protection.","objective":"Prevent transport encryption from being treated as complete security assurance.","failure_mode":"Application trusts all requests because transport encryption succeeded.","cause":"Transport state is used as a substitute for application authorization.","root_cause":"Security layers are conflated.","solution":"Keep transport authentication, application identity, authorization, and data validation as distinct controls.","alternative":"Use an integrated identity framework while preserving explicit security semantics for each layer.","tradeoff":"Multiple security layers require careful composition.","edge_case":"A strongly authenticated channel may provide identity context, but authorization still depends on application policy.","security_implication":"Compromised or overprivileged clients can misuse an otherwise secure channel.","test_case":"Send an authenticated but unauthorized application action over a valid encrypted channel.","expected_test_result":"Transport succeeds but the application action is denied.","verification_method":"Test authorization independently of transport establishment.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B8-R05","D57-B53-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B54-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Endpoint Runtime Security","concept":"Runtime privilege reduction","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should an application process run with only the privileges it requires?","answer":"Reducing runtime privileges limits the authority available if the process is compromised or misused.","explanation":"A vulnerability in application logic can become more damaging when the process possesses unnecessary operating-system or platform privileges.","principle":"Runtime authority should be constrained to the minimum required for intended behavior.","rule":"Run processes with the least privileges compatible with their required operations.","objective":"Limit the impact of application compromise.","failure_mode":"A compromised application can perform unrelated privileged operations.","cause":"Runtime identity has broader authority than required.","root_cause":"Privilege scope was not derived from actual application requirements.","solution":"Identify required capabilities and remove unnecessary privileges.","alternative":"Use sandboxing or separate privileged helper components for exceptional operations.","tradeoff":"Privilege reduction may require architectural changes.","edge_case":"Some services need dynamic capabilities that must be explicitly modeled.","security_implication":"Excess runtime privilege increases blast radius after compromise.","test_case":"Attempt a non-required privileged operation from the application runtime identity.","expected_test_result":"The operation is denied by the underlying security boundary.","verification_method":"Inspect runtime privileges and execute representative unauthorized operations.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B4-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B54-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Endpoint Runtime Security","concept":"Privilege separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can separating privileged functionality into a smaller component improve security?","answer":"A smaller privileged component can reduce the amount of code and functionality exposed to high authority, limiting the portion of the system that must operate with elevated privilege.","explanation":"Privilege separation does not remove vulnerabilities but can reduce the authority available to vulnerable lower-privileged components.","principle":"Sensitive authority should be concentrated only where necessary and guarded by explicit interfaces.","rule":"Isolate high-privilege operations behind narrowly scoped interfaces when practical.","objective":"Reduce the attack surface associated with elevated authority.","failure_mode":"A large application runs entirely with high privilege because one operation requires it.","cause":"Privilege requirement is propagated to the whole process.","root_cause":"Security authority is not separated from ordinary application logic.","solution":"Move privileged operations behind a restricted component or capability boundary.","alternative":"Use operating-system capabilities or sandbox mechanisms where they provide equivalent separation.","tradeoff":"Separation introduces IPC, interface, and operational complexity.","edge_case":"The privileged component itself becomes security-critical and requires focused hardening.","security_implication":"Reduced privileged code scope can limit compromise impact.","test_case":"Attempt to invoke privileged operations outside the authorized interface.","expected_test_result":"Only explicitly permitted operations reach the privileged component.","verification_method":"Review privilege boundaries and test unauthorized interface use.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B54-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B54-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Endpoint Runtime Security","concept":"Debug capability exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why are debugging and diagnostic interfaces security-sensitive in production environments?","answer":"Debug interfaces can expose internal state, execution controls, memory, credentials, or privileged operations beyond the normal application interface.","explanation":"Diagnostic functionality is often designed for trusted developers and may not receive the same authorization model as production functionality.","principle":"Diagnostic capability is part of the production attack surface when enabled.","rule":"Disable or strongly protect unnecessary debugging interfaces in security-sensitive deployments.","objective":"Prevent diagnostic capabilities from bypassing normal security controls.","failure_mode":"A production debug interface exposes privileged state or execution controls.","cause":"Development functionality remains enabled after deployment.","root_cause":"Deployment security model does not distinguish diagnostic authority from ordinary application authority.","solution":"Remove unnecessary diagnostics or enforce strong authentication and authorization.","alternative":"Expose diagnostics only through isolated administrative channels.","tradeoff":"Removing diagnostics can make troubleshooting harder.","edge_case":"Operational diagnostics may be required but should expose only the minimum necessary information.","security_implication":"Debug exposure can provide direct access to sensitive internals or privileged actions.","test_case":"Enumerate diagnostic interfaces in a production-like configuration and attempt unauthorized access.","expected_test_result":"Unnecessary interfaces are unavailable and protected interfaces enforce intended authorization.","verification_method":"Perform deployment configuration review and negative diagnostic-interface tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B26-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B54-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Endpoint Runtime Security","concept":"Runtime hardening drift","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"INFERENCE","question":"Why should runtime security configuration be continuously compared with the intended baseline?","answer":"Runtime environments can drift through manual changes, package updates, configuration changes, or deployment tooling. A previously hardened baseline may therefore no longer describe the actual security state.","explanation":"Security assurance depends on the effective runtime configuration, not only the configuration originally deployed.","principle":"Runtime security posture requires state-aware verification.","rule":"Detect material deviations from the intended security baseline.","objective":"Identify security-control drift before it becomes an unnoticed exposure.","failure_mode":"A privileged or insecure runtime setting remains enabled after deployment changes.","cause":"No effective-state comparison exists.","root_cause":"Security baseline is treated as a deployment artifact rather than an ongoing invariant.","solution":"Compare effective runtime state against a versioned security baseline and investigate deviations.","alternative":"Use immutable infrastructure where appropriate to reduce configuration drift.","tradeoff":"Continuous drift detection requires telemetry and baseline maintenance.","edge_case":"Some runtime settings are intentionally dynamic and require policy-defined acceptable ranges.","security_implication":"Drift can silently weaken hardening controls.","test_case":"Introduce an unauthorized runtime configuration change and run the posture check.","expected_test_result":"The deviation is detected and classified according to policy.","verification_method":"Compare effective runtime state with the approved security baseline.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B26-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B54-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Endpoint Runtime Security","concept":"Security-relevant runtime capability inventory","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should a runtime capability inventory capture?","answer":"It should identify security-relevant privileges, exposed interfaces, mounted resources, service identities, administrative capabilities, and other authorities that materially affect compromise impact.","explanation":"Knowing the process name or software version is insufficient to determine the authority available after compromise.","principle":"Security posture includes effective runtime authority, not just software identity.","rule":"Inventory security-relevant runtime capabilities and compare them with required capabilities.","objective":"Detect unnecessary authority and hidden attack surface.","test_case":"Generate a runtime capability inventory and compare it with the documented application requirements.","expected_test_result":"Unnecessary or undocumented capabilities are identified for review.","verification_method":"Perform capability-to-requirement traceability analysis.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B54-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B55-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Container and Sandbox Security","concept":"Container boundary limitations","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should a container boundary not automatically be treated as a complete security boundary?","answer":"Container isolation depends on the host kernel, runtime configuration, privileges, namespaces, capabilities, and other shared infrastructure. The boundary therefore has specific assumptions and limitations.","explanation":"A container can reduce attack surface and provide isolation but does not eliminate risks associated with shared host components or privileged configuration.","principle":"Isolation strength depends on the actual enforcement mechanism and its trusted computing base.","rule":"Model container isolation assumptions explicitly rather than treating containers as universally equivalent to separate hosts.","objective":"Prevent overstatement of container security guarantees.","failure_mode":"A compromised container is assumed unable to affect shared host resources without verifying the actual isolation configuration.","cause":"Container abstraction is treated as an absolute security boundary.","root_cause":"Underlying runtime and host dependencies are omitted from the threat model.","solution":"Assess privileges, capabilities, host dependencies, runtime configuration, and escape-relevant attack surfaces.","alternative":"Use stronger isolation mechanisms when the threat model requires them.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation can increase infrastructure cost and operational complexity.","edge_case":"Highly privileged containers may intentionally require host-level capabilities and therefore have a substantially different threat model.","security_implication":"Weak isolation assumptions can magnify the impact of workload compromise.","test_case":"Review a container's effective privileges and shared resources against the intended isolation model.","expected_test_result":"All security-relevant boundary assumptions are explicitly identified.","verification_method":"Perform runtime isolation configuration review and boundary testing.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B44-R01","D57-B54-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B55-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Container and Sandbox Security","concept":"Host resource exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should host-mounted resources be treated as part of the container's security boundary?","answer":"A mounted host resource can expose sensitive data, control interfaces, or write capabilities that extend the container's authority beyond its apparent application scope.","explanation":"The effective authority of a workload includes resources made available through its environment, not merely the permissions of the application process itself.","principle":"Mounted resources are security capabilities.","rule":"Review each host or external resource exposed to an isolated workload.","objective":"Prevent unintended privilege through resource exposure.","failure_mode":"A workload can modify a host-sensitive resource through an unnecessary mount.","cause":"Resource exposure is configured for convenience without security analysis.","root_cause":"Mounts are treated as deployment details rather than authority.","solution":"Minimize exposed resources and apply explicit access controls.","alternative":"Expose purpose-specific interfaces instead of broad host resources.","tradeoff":"Narrow interfaces can require additional engineering.","edge_case":"Read-only access can reduce but does not eliminate information-disclosure risk.","security_implication":"Resource exposure can undermine otherwise strong workload isolation.","test_case":"Enumerate exposed resources and attempt unauthorized access or modification.","expected_test_result":"Only required operations succeed.","verification_method":"Compare resource exposure with documented workload requirements.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B55-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B55-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Container and Sandbox Security","concept":"Privileged workload classification","knowledge_type":"RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should privileged workloads receive different security treatment from ordinary isolated workloads?","answer":"Elevated capabilities can weaken isolation guarantees and expand the workload's authority over shared infrastructure. They therefore require explicit classification and stronger review.","explanation":"Calling a workload isolated while granting it broad host-level capabilities creates a mismatch between stated and effective security boundaries.","principle":"Security classification must reflect effective privilege rather than deployment labels.","rule":"Treat elevated-capability workloads as higher-authority security subjects.","objective":"Prevent incorrect security assumptions caused by deployment abstractions.","failure_mode":"A privileged workload is governed by ordinary workload isolation assumptions.","cause":"Privilege configuration is not included in security classification.","root_cause":"Security model uses workload type rather than effective authority.","solution":"Derive security classification from actual capabilities and shared-resource access.","alternative":"Remove unnecessary elevated capabilities.","tradeoff":"Reduced privileges may require application redesign.","edge_case":"Some infrastructure workloads legitimately require broad capabilities but need compensating controls.","security_implication":"Misclassification can cause severe boundary assumptions to fail.","test_case":"Compare security classification before and after adding an elevated capability.","expected_test_result":"The workload's security classification and review requirements change appropriately.","verification_method":"Validate classification against effective capability configuration.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B55-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B55-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Container and Sandbox Security","concept":"Image identity versus runtime identity","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why does a trusted container image not prove that the running workload has the intended security state?","answer":"Runtime configuration, mounted resources, privileges, environment values, and external dependencies can materially change the effective security state after the image is selected.","explanation":"Artifact provenance establishes information about the artifact; runtime authorization establishes how that artifact is allowed to operate.","principle":"Artifact trust and runtime security are separate assurance dimensions.","rule":"Verify runtime security properties in addition to image provenance.","objective":"Prevent trusted artifacts from receiving unintended runtime authority.","failure_mode":"A verified image is assumed secure despite insecure runtime configuration.","cause":"Artifact verification is treated as complete deployment assurance.","root_cause":"Build-time and runtime security properties are conflated.","solution":"Verify both artifact provenance and effective runtime configuration.","alternative":"Use policy-enforced deployment specifications with runtime admission checks.","tradeoff":"Runtime verification adds deployment complexity.","edge_case":"Immutable environments reduce configuration drift but still require correct initial policy.","security_implication":"A trusted artifact can still be deployed with excessive authority.","test_case":"Deploy a trusted image with an unauthorized runtime capability.","expected_test_result":"Deployment is rejected or the capability is removed according to policy.","verification_method":"Compare artifact identity and runtime security policy separately.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B41-R01","D57-B54-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B55-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Container and Sandbox Security","concept":"Isolation regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should be tested when container or sandbox configuration changes?","answer":"Tests should verify that prohibited access to host resources, peer workloads, privileged interfaces, and sensitive mounted resources remains blocked.","explanation":"Small runtime configuration changes can alter isolation semantics without changing application code.","principle":"Isolation configuration is security-sensitive code-like state and requires regression verification.","rule":"Run boundary-focused tests after material isolation changes.","objective":"Detect unintended expansion of workload authority.","test_case":"Modify workload capabilities or resource exposure and execute boundary denial tests.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized boundary crossings remain blocked.","testing_guidance":"Test both direct access and indirect paths exposed by the changed configuration.","verification_method":"Compare effective isolation properties against the approved baseline.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B55-R01","D57-B44-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B56-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Memory and Native Boundaries","concept":"Memory safety as a security property","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can memory-safety defects become security issues?","answer":"Memory-safety failures can cause unintended memory access, corruption, control-flow manipulation, or exposure of sensitive data, depending on the language, runtime, architecture, and defect.","explanation":"The security impact depends on exploitability and environmental conditions; a memory defect should not automatically be described as an exploitable vulnerability without supporting evidence.","principle":"Memory correctness and security assurance are related but should not be conflated.","rule":"Treat security impact of memory-safety defects as an evidence-based assessment.","objective":"Capture both the technical defect and the supported security implications without overclaiming exploitability.","failure_mode":"A memory defect is either dismissed as non-security-relevant or declared exploitable without evidence.","cause":"Technical defect classification and security impact assessment are conflated.","root_cause":"No separate evidence model for defect behavior and exploitability.","solution":"Record defect behavior, affected boundary, exploit preconditions, and evidence separately.","alternative":"Use memory-safe abstractions or language guarantees where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Migration or stronger memory-safety mechanisms can introduce development and interoperability costs.","edge_case":"A memory error may be contained by a runtime and have different security implications across environments.","security_implication":"Certain memory defects can compromise confidentiality, integrity, or control flow.","test_case":"Trigger the identified memory-safety failure under controlled conditions.","expected_test_result":"The observed behavior matches the documented defect classification without assuming unsupported exploitability.","verification_method":"Combine static analysis, targeted tests, and evidence-based impact assessment.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B3-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B56-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Memory and Native Boundaries","concept":"Unsafe native interface boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should transitions between memory-safe and unsafe/native components receive explicit security analysis?","answer":"The boundary can introduce assumptions about memory ownership, lifetimes, sizes, encoding, and error handling that are not automatically enforced across the interface.","explanation":"Security properties can be lost at language or runtime boundaries when one side assumes guarantees that the other side does not provide.","principle":"Cross-runtime and cross-language boundaries require explicit contract validation.","rule":"Specify and test ownership, length, lifetime, encoding, and error contracts at unsafe interfaces.","objective":"Prevent boundary assumptions from creating security defects.","failure_mode":"Native code receives invalid size or lifetime information from a higher-level component.","cause":"Interface contract is informal or incomplete.","root_cause":"Security assumptions at the boundary are not represented in the interface contract.","solution":"Define explicit contracts and validation at the boundary.","alternative":"Reduce unsafe boundary surface through safer wrappers or memory-safe abstractions.","tradeoff":"Wrappers can add development and runtime complexity.","edge_case":"Performance-sensitive code may require direct native access but still needs explicit safety contracts.","security_implication":"Boundary defects can undermine security guarantees of the higher-level component.","test_case":"Provide invalid length, lifetime, or ownership conditions at the native boundary.","expected_test_result":"The boundary rejects or safely handles invalid conditions.","verification_method":"Perform boundary-focused negative testing and static analysis where available.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B56-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B56-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Memory and Native Boundaries","concept":"Integer and size validation at memory boundaries","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should size calculations be validated before memory-sensitive operations?","answer":"Incorrect size calculations can cause under-allocation, over-read, overflow, truncation, or inconsistent interpretation between components.","explanation":"Security-sensitive memory operations require consistent assumptions about lengths and numeric ranges.","principle":"Security-relevant size values must satisfy explicit range and representation invariants.","rule":"Validate size calculations and conversions before using them for allocation or memory access.","objective":"Prevent numeric inconsistencies from becoming memory-safety failures.","failure_mode":"A large or wrapped size value causes an unsafe allocation or access.","cause":"Numeric conversion or arithmetic is not constrained to safe ranges.","root_cause":"Boundary contract omits numeric invariants.","solution":"Use checked arithmetic and explicit range validation.","alternative":"Use language or library abstractions that encode safe size operations.","tradeoff":"Additional validation can add complexity but reduces undefined behavior risk.","edge_case":"Different platforms may have different integer widths and overflow semantics.","security_implication":"Size errors can contribute to memory corruption or data exposure.","test_case":"Provide boundary, overflow, and conversion values to the size-handling logic.","expected_test_result":"Invalid values are rejected before unsafe memory operations occur.","verification_method":"Combine boundary-value tests with static or dynamic analysis where appropriate.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B21-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B56-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Memory and Native Boundaries","concept":"Use-after-release security implications","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should lifetime violations be treated as potentially security-relevant?","answer":"Accessing data after its intended lifetime can expose stale data, corrupt unrelated state, or create behavior that violates memory safety guarantees.","explanation":"The actual security consequence depends on the runtime and surrounding implementation. The lesson should distinguish observed lifetime failure from demonstrated exploitability.","principle":"Object lifetime is part of the security contract when memory safety affects protected state.","rule":"Track ownership and lifetime explicitly across security-sensitive boundaries.","objective":"Prevent stale or invalid references from affecting security-sensitive operations.","failure_mode":"A component accesses memory after ownership or lifetime has ended.","cause":"Ownership contract is violated.","root_cause":"Lifetime semantics are not enforced or verified.","solution":"Use ownership-safe abstractions, explicit lifetime management, or runtime checks where appropriate.","alternative":"Refactor the boundary to transfer ownership explicitly.","tradeoff":"Stricter ownership models can require substantial interface changes.","edge_case":"Managed runtimes may transform the failure mode but do not eliminate all lifetime-related security issues.","security_implication":"Lifetime violations can expose or corrupt protected state.","test_case":"Exercise object lifecycle transitions around the affected operation.","expected_test_result":"Invalid post-lifetime access is prevented or detected.","verification_method":"Use memory-safety tooling and targeted lifecycle tests where applicable.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B56-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B56-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Memory and Native Boundaries","concept":"Security verification of unsafe code","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should unsafe or native code receive additional verification attention?","answer":"Unsafe code often operates outside stronger language-level safety guarantees and therefore requires explicit review and testing of memory, ownership, bounds, error, and interface assumptions.","explanation":"The purpose is not to claim that unsafe code is automatically vulnerable, but to identify where automatic guarantees are weaker or absent.","principle":"Verification effort should reflect the strength of available safety guarantees.","rule":"Apply targeted security review and testing to security-sensitive unsafe boundaries.","objective":"Reduce assurance gaps where language or runtime guarantees are weaker.","test_case":"Review unsafe operations and execute boundary and invalid-input tests.","expected_test_result":"Relevant safety invariants have explicit evidence or documented limitations.","verification_method":"Combine code review, static analysis, targeted tests, and runtime analysis as appropriate.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B56-R02","D57-B56-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B57-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","concept":"Client trust limitation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should security-critical authorization not depend solely on client-side checks?","answer":"Client-side code executes in an environment that may be modified, bypassed, or replaced by an untrusted user. Security-critical decisions therefore require enforcement in a trusted server or authoritative execution boundary.","explanation":"Client-side validation improves usability and can reduce accidental misuse but should not be the sole security control for protected operations.","principle":"Untrusted clients cannot be the final authority for their own authorization.","rule":"Enforce security-critical authorization at a trusted boundary.","objective":"Prevent client manipulation from bypassing server-side security controls.","failure_mode":"A client hides or disables an unauthorized action but the backend accepts a manually constructed request.","cause":"Authorization is enforced only in the client interface.","root_cause":"Presentation-layer controls are mistaken for authoritative enforcement.","solution":"Perform authorization independently at the protected service boundary.","alternative":"Use client-side checks only as defense-in-depth and usability controls.","tradeoff":"Server-side enforcement requires additional backend policy logic.","edge_case":"Offline applications may require different trust assumptions and should explicitly define their security model.","security_implication":"Client manipulation can otherwise bypass sensitive controls.","test_case":"Invoke a protected operation without using the official client interface.","expected_test_result":"The backend independently rejects unauthorized requests.","verification_method":"Test direct API or protocol access outside the intended client.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B43-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B57-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","concept":"Client-stored authorization state","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should locally stored role or privilege information not be treated as authoritative authorization state?","answer":"Local state can be modified or replayed by the client. Authoritative privilege must come from a trusted security context.","explanation":"Client-side role information can be useful for interface behavior but does not establish that the backend should permit the corresponding action.","principle":"Display state and authorization state are different security properties.","rule":"Treat client-stored privilege information as untrusted input unless cryptographically or otherwise authoritatively bound.","objective":"Prevent local state manipulation from escalating privilege.","failure_mode":"A client modifies a stored role value and receives privileged behavior.","cause":"Backend trusts client-provided privilege state.","root_cause":"Authorization authority is delegated to an untrusted client.","solution":"Derive authorization from trusted server-side identity and policy state.","alternative":"Use signed claims with explicit validation and bounded scope where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Authoritative state may require additional communication or token management.","edge_case":"Offline clients may use bounded capabilities but require a distinct trust model.","security_implication":"Manipulated local state can become a privilege-escalation path.","test_case":"Alter client-side privilege state and request the corresponding protected operation.","expected_test_result":"The backend ignores unauthorized local state and enforces authoritative policy.","verification_method":"Tamper client state and test server authorization independently.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B57-R01","D57-B8-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B57-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","concept":"Sensitive data exposure in client state","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should sensitive data not be placed in client-side storage merely because the interface needs it temporarily?","answer":"Client environments may expose stored data to users, extensions, malware, debugging tools, backups, or other local mechanisms. Sensitive data should therefore be minimized and protected according to the client threat model.","explanation":"Client-side storage changes the data's exposure surface. Data necessary for a session is not automatically safe to persist locally.","principle":"Data availability to the client creates a client-side exposure boundary.","rule":"Minimize and appropriately protect sensitive data stored or cached on clients.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary exposure of sensitive information.","failure_mode":"Sensitive server data remains in persistent client storage after the session ends.","cause":"Convenience-oriented caching lacks data lifecycle controls.","root_cause":"Client storage is not included in the data threat model.","solution":"Minimize local persistence and define expiration, deletion, and protection controls.","alternative":"Keep sensitive data server-side and retrieve only what is necessary.","tradeoff":"Reduced local storage may increase network dependency.","edge_case":"Some applications require offline operation and therefore need explicit local-data security architecture.","security_implication":"Compromise or loss of the client can expose persistent sensitive information.","test_case":"Inspect client storage after sensitive operations and after logout or session expiration.","expected_test_result":"Sensitive data is absent or protected according to the documented policy.","verification_method":"Perform client-storage inspection and lifecycle tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B9-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B57-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","concept":"Client security boundary disclosure","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson follows when an application exposes internal implementation details to an untrusted client?","answer":"Information exposed to the client should be assumed observable and potentially modifiable. Internal trust decisions should therefore not depend on the secrecy or integrity of client-visible implementation details.","explanation":"Obscuring client logic may reduce casual discovery but does not create a trustworthy security boundary.","principle":"Client-visible logic and metadata are not reliable secrets or authoritative controls.","rule":"Keep security-critical secrets and authority decisions outside untrusted client control.","objective":"Prevent client reverse engineering or manipulation from undermining security.","failure_mode":"A secret or security decision is embedded in client code and treated as protected.","cause":"Client environment is incorrectly considered trusted.","root_cause":"Trust boundary is placed on the wrong side of the client interface.","solution":"Move secrets and authoritative security decisions to trusted infrastructure.","alternative":"Use short-lived, scoped credentials when client participation is unavoidable.","tradeoff":"Moving authority server-side can increase service dependency.","edge_case":"Public client identifiers may be safe to expose when explicitly designed as non-secret values.","security_implication":"Client compromise can expose embedded secrets or bypass local security logic.","test_case":"Inspect client artifacts for security-critical secrets or authority decisions.","expected_test_result":"No protected secret or authoritative authorization mechanism relies solely on client secrecy.","verification_method":"Review client artifacts and test direct manipulation of exposed state.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B57-R01","D57-B9-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B57-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","concept":"Client security regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should security regression testing do when client functionality changes?","answer":"It should verify that client changes do not introduce new privileged operations, sensitive data persistence, bypassable authorization assumptions, or unsafe trust decisions.","explanation":"A UI or client feature can change the security boundary even when backend code is unchanged.","principle":"Client changes can have security consequences through new data flows and authority assumptions.","rule":"Include security analysis for client-side changes that affect data, identity, authorization, or trust boundaries.","objective":"Detect security regressions introduced outside backend enforcement code.","test_case":"Modify a client feature that accesses sensitive data or triggers protected actions and execute security regression tests.","expected_test_result":"Backend authorization remains authoritative and sensitive client-state behavior remains within policy.","verification_method":"Review changed data flows and test direct interaction outside the intended UI path.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B57-R01","D57-B57-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B58-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Browser and Web Security","concept":"Origin separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why is origin separation important for browser-based security?","answer":"Browser security policies use origin boundaries to restrict how content from different security contexts can interact. Applications must not assume that visually similar or related sites share the same trust authority.","explanation":"Browser-enforced origin controls are part of the client security model and can be weakened by unsafe cross-origin configuration.","principle":"Web application trust must explicitly account for origin boundaries.","rule":"Define and validate permitted cross-origin interactions for security-sensitive resources.","objective":"Prevent unintended cross-origin access to protected data or actions.","failure_mode":"A web application permits broader cross-origin access than its threat model requires.","cause":"Origin policy is configured for convenience without explicit security scope.","root_cause":"Cross-origin trust is not modeled as an authorization boundary.","solution":"Restrict cross-origin access to explicitly required origins and operations.","alternative":"Avoid cross-origin exposure for resources that do not require it.","tradeoff":"Strict origin controls can complicate integrations.","edge_case":"Public resources may legitimately allow broad cross-origin reads under explicit policy.","security_implication":"Misconfigured origin controls can expose protected browser-accessible resources.","test_case":"Attempt cross-origin access from an unauthorized origin.","expected_test_result":"The browser or application policy prevents unauthorized access.","verification_method":"Test allowed and prohibited origin combinations.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B27-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B58-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Browser and Web Security","concept":"Cross-origin trust scope","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can broad cross-origin permissions create security risk?","answer":"A broad permission can allow many origins to interact with a resource, including origins that were not intended to receive the data or perform the associated actions.","explanation":"Cross-origin permissions should correspond to actual integration requirements rather than broad convenience defaults.","principle":"Cross-origin authorization should be scoped to required origins and capabilities.","rule":"Use the narrowest practical cross-origin policy for sensitive resources.","objective":"Reduce unintended browser-mediated data and action exposure.","failure_mode":"An application permits arbitrary external origins to interact with a sensitive endpoint.","cause":"Cross-origin policy uses broad wildcard-like behavior without considering credential or data sensitivity.","root_cause":"Integration scope is not mapped to security scope.","solution":"Restrict permitted origins and capabilities according to the integration model.","alternative":"Expose a dedicated public endpoint with intentionally limited data and actions.","tradeoff":"Narrow policies may require additional integration configuration.","edge_case":"Public read-only resources can have intentionally broad origin access.","security_implication":"Broad permissions can convert an external website into a privileged interaction surface.","test_case":"Attempt sensitive interaction from an origin outside the approved integration set.","expected_test_result":"The interaction is denied or does not expose protected data.","verification_method":"Test origin allowlist boundaries and credentialed requests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B58-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B58-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Browser and Web Security","concept":"Browser credential attachment","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must applications carefully distinguish browser-originated requests that carry ambient credentials from requests explicitly authorized by the application?","answer":"Browsers can automatically attach credentials under certain conditions. If a server relies only on credential presence and not on request intent or applicable anti-forgery controls, another origin may induce a privileged request.","explanation":"The security lesson is to model how credentials are attached and what prevents an unauthorized origin from causing a state-changing operation.","principle":"Credential presence does not by itself establish user intent.","rule":"Protect security-sensitive state changes against unauthorized cross-origin request initiation according to the application's authentication model.","objective":"Prevent unintended authenticated actions initiated from an untrusted origin.","failure_mode":"A user is authenticated, another origin induces a state-changing request, and the server accepts it without sufficient request-origin or intent validation.","cause":"Authentication state is treated as proof of request intent.","root_cause":"Authentication and request authorization semantics are conflated.","solution":"Use appropriate anti-forgery and request-integrity mechanisms consistent with the authentication architecture.","alternative":"Use explicit non-ambient authorization mechanisms for sensitive actions.","tradeoff":"Additional request validation increases implementation and testing requirements.","edge_case":"APIs using non-ambient credentials may have a different threat model but still require explicit authorization.","security_implication":"Cross-origin request induction can cause unauthorized state changes under an authenticated session.","test_case":"Attempt a protected state-changing operation from an unauthorized origin while a victim authentication context exists.","expected_test_result":"The protected operation is rejected unless the request satisfies the defined anti-forgery requirements.","verification_method":"Perform cross-origin state-change tests under the actual authentication model.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B58-R01","D57-B57-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B58-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Browser and Web Security","concept":"Security headers as layered controls","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should browser security headers be treated as layered controls rather than complete application security?","answer":"Security headers can constrain browser behavior and reduce certain attack classes, but they do not replace server-side authorization, input handling, secure state management, or correct application logic.","explanation":"A browser control can reduce the exploitability of a condition while the underlying application flaw remains present.","principle":"Client-enforced controls complement rather than replace authoritative server-side security.","rule":"Record the specific threat addressed by each browser security control and retain independent server-side defenses.","objective":"Prevent defense-in-depth controls from being mistaken for complete remediation.","failure_mode":"A server-side authorization defect is considered resolved because a browser policy reduces one exploitation path.","cause":"Mitigation scope is broader than actual control coverage.","root_cause":"Control effectiveness is not mapped to the underlying security property.","solution":"Document control coverage and retain authoritative backend enforcement.","alternative":"Combine browser controls with server-side preventive and detective controls.","tradeoff":"Layered controls require more comprehensive verification.","edge_case":"Some client-only threats can legitimately be addressed primarily through browser security mechanisms.","security_implication":"Overreliance on client controls leaves non-browser clients and direct requests exposed.","test_case":"Attempt the protected operation using a non-browser client after enabling browser security controls.","expected_test_result":"Server-side security controls independently enforce the protected property.","verification_method":"Test protected functionality outside the browser security context.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B57-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B58-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Browser and Web Security","concept":"Browser security regression verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What should be verified when browser-facing security policy changes?","answer":"Verification should cover intended origins, credential behavior, protected resource access, state-changing requests, and behavior across supported client contexts.","explanation":"Browser security policies can change effective data exposure without changing backend business logic.","principle":"Browser-facing policy changes require behavior-level security verification.","rule":"Test both allowed and prohibited browser interactions after material policy changes.","objective":"Detect unintended expansion of browser security trust.","test_case":"Evaluate protected resource access from approved and unapproved origins and with relevant credential states.","expected_test_result":"Observed browser behavior matches the documented security policy.","verification_method":"Execute cross-origin, credential, and state-change tests against the affected resources.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B58-R01","D57-B58-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B59-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Decommissioning","concept":"Decommissioning is a security state transition","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should decommissioning be treated as a security operation rather than only an infrastructure cleanup task?","answer":"Decommissioning changes identity, access, data availability, credentials, network reachability, and trust relationships. Incomplete removal can leave residual security exposure.","explanation":"A system can be functionally retired while credentials, copies, integrations, or management paths remain active.","principle":"Retirement must remove or govern the security authority and data associated with the retired component.","rule":"Define security acceptance criteria for decommissioning.","objective":"Prevent retired assets from retaining unauthorized access or sensitive data.","failure_mode":"A retired service account remains valid after the associated system is removed.","cause":"Decommissioning focuses on compute removal but not identity and access state.","root_cause":"Security lifecycle ends later than infrastructure lifecycle without explicit governance.","solution":"Coordinate decommissioning across identity, network, data, credentials, monitoring, and management systems.","alternative":"Use automated lifecycle coupling between resource retirement and security-state cleanup.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive retirement procedures require cross-system coordination.","edge_case":"Some records or credentials may require controlled retention for regulatory or recovery purposes.","security_implication":"Residual identities and integrations can become abandoned attack surfaces.","test_case":"Decommission a test resource and verify all defined security dependencies transition to the expected retired state.","expected_test_result":"No unauthorized active access path remains within the defined scope.","verification_method":"Run a decommissioning checklist against authoritative security state.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B8-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B59-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Decommissioning","concept":"Credential retirement","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should credentials be explicitly retired when a component is decommissioned?","answer":"Credentials can remain usable after the component that originally required them has been removed. If they are not revoked or otherwise rendered unusable, they can become orphaned access paths.","explanation":"Credential ownership and system lifecycle are distinct unless explicitly coupled.","principle":"Retiring a resource does not automatically retire every credential associated with it.","rule":"Identify and revoke or reassign credentials during decommissioning according to policy.","objective":"Eliminate abandoned authentication and authorization paths.","failure_mode":"A former service credential remains valid after its workload is retired.","cause":"Credential inventory is not linked to resource lifecycle.","root_cause":"Identity lifecycle and infrastructure lifecycle are managed independently.","solution":"Trace credentials to owners and lifecycle states and process them during retirement.","alternative":"Use short-lived credentials that naturally expire when no longer needed.","tradeoff":"Short-lived credentials require stronger issuance and renewal infrastructure.","edge_case":"Shared credentials may have multiple dependencies and require careful ownership analysis before revocation.","security_implication":"Orphaned credentials can provide unauthorized access without an active legitimate owner.","test_case":"Retire a resource and attempt authentication using its former credential.","expected_test_result":"The credential is no longer accepted unless an explicitly documented legitimate dependency remains.","verification_method":"Perform credential inventory and post-retirement authentication tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B59-R01","D57-B9-R04"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B59-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Decommissioning","concept":"Residual integration removal","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can a decommissioned system remain a security risk through external integrations?","answer":"Other systems may continue sending data, accepting callbacks, retaining trust relationships, or routing requests to an address associated with the retired component.","explanation":"Decommissioning must consider both the retired component and systems that still believe it exists.","principle":"Security lifecycle closure includes dependent systems and trust relationships.","rule":"Identify and remove or update material integrations during decommissioning.","objective":"Prevent stale integrations from creating unauthorized or confusing security paths.","failure_mode":"A retired endpoint remains trusted by another service.","cause":"Dependency inventory is incomplete.","root_cause":"Decommissioning scope is limited to the target system.","solution":"Perform dependency-aware retirement and validate remaining trust relationships.","alternative":"Use service discovery and lifecycle-aware identity mechanisms that automatically reflect retirement.","tradeoff":"Dependency-aware retirement requires broader system visibility.","edge_case":"Historical integrations may remain intentionally for audit or migration and require explicit restricted handling.","security_implication":"Stale trust relationships can expose data or create impersonation opportunities.","test_case":"Retire a service and inspect systems that previously trusted or communicated with it.","expected_test_result":"Unneeded trust relationships and communication paths are removed or explicitly governed.","verification_method":"Perform dependency graph review and post-retirement connectivity tests.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B59-R01"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B59-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Decommissioning","concept":"Data retention during retirement","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What security lesson applies when data must be retained after a system is decommissioned?","answer":"Retention should preserve required records without leaving unnecessary operational access to the retired system or its former identities.","explanation":"Retention and continued operational accessibility are different requirements. Data may need to remain available for a defined purpose without preserving the original attack surface.","principle":"Retain required information without retaining unnecessary operational authority.","rule":"Separate archival retention from active system access during decommissioning.","objective":"Reduce attack surface while preserving legitimately required records.","failure_mode":"A retired production system remains online solely because historical data must be retained.","cause":"Retention requirement is implemented as continued operational availability.","root_cause":"Data lifecycle and service lifecycle are not separated.","solution":"Move required records into controlled archival storage and retire unnecessary services and credentials.","alternative":"Use isolated read-only archival environments where required.","tradeoff":"Separate archival systems require additional storage and retrieval processes.","edge_case":"Some records may require controlled replay or forensic preservation and need specialized handling.","security_implication":"Keeping obsolete systems active extends their attack surface and trust relationships.","test_case":"Move retained records to archival storage and verify the retired system is no longer required for access.","expected_test_result":"Required records remain accessible through the approved retention mechanism while unnecessary operational paths are closed.","verification_method":"Review retention architecture and post-retirement access paths.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B59-R01","D57-B9-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B59-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Secure Decommissioning","concept":"Decommissioning verification evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What evidence is required to support secure decommissioning?","answer":"Evidence should show that relevant resources, identities, credentials, integrations, sensitive data, and management paths reached their intended retired states within the defined scope.","explanation":"A deletion command or infrastructure status alone may not establish that external security dependencies were also removed.","principle":"Decommissioning closure requires evidence across the complete lifecycle boundary.","rule":"Define explicit retirement acceptance criteria and preserve verification evidence.","objective":"Prevent incomplete retirement from being reported as complete.","test_case":"Execute the retirement workflow and verify each security lifecycle dependency.","expected_test_result":"Every defined retirement criterion has a recorded outcome or an explicit unresolved exception.","verification_method":"Perform post-decommission security-state reconciliation.","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B59-R01","D57-B50-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B60-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Quality","concept":"Security claim scope","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why must the scope of a security claim match the scope of its evidence?","answer":"Evidence supports only the conditions, versions, environments, configurations, and behaviors actually covered by the evidence. Extending the claim beyond that scope is an unsupported generalization.","explanation":"A successful test or review can establish a bounded property without proving universal security.","principle":"Security assurance is scoped evidence, not an unrestricted property.","rule":"Record the environment, version, configuration, threat assumptions, and tested behavior associated with security evidence.","objective":"Prevent security claims from exceeding their evidence.","failure_mode":"A narrow test result is reported as proof that the entire system is secure.","cause":"Evidence scope is omitted from the security claim.","root_cause":"Assurance model lacks explicit scope metadata.","solution":"Bind claims to evidence scope and applicability conditions.","alternative":"Use conditional security assertions with machine-readable predicates.","tradeoff":"Scoped claims are more precise but require richer metadata.","edge_case":"A broadly established external security principle may have wider scope than one local test result.","security_implication":"Overbroad claims can conceal untested attack surfaces.","test_case":"Attempt to promote a test result from one configuration to all configurations.","expected_test_result":"The knowledge system flags the unsupported scope expansion.","verification_method":"Compare claim scope against evidence metadata.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B2-R02","D57-B50-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B60-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Quality","concept":"Security evidence freshness","knowledge_type":"LESSON","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can old security evidence become insufficient even when it was originally valid?","answer":"Software versions, architecture, dependencies, configurations, threat capabilities, and operational assumptions can change, making previous evidence no longer representative of the current system.","explanation":"Evidence has an applicability period determined by the assumptions under which it was produced.","principle":"Security evidence has validity conditions and may require revalidation after material change.","rule":"Identify change triggers that invalidate or weaken security evidence.","objective":"Prevent stale assurance from being treated as current verification.","failure_mode":"Historical security testing is used as current evidence after major system changes.","cause":"Evidence records have no applicability or revalidation metadata.","root_cause":"Security evidence is treated as timeless.","solution":"Attach version, configuration, architecture, and review conditions to evidence records.","alternative":"Automate revalidation triggers based on material dependency changes.","tradeoff":"More frequent verification increases engineering effort.","edge_case":"Foundational design properties may remain valid across versions if the relevant invariant and implementation assumptions are unchanged.","security_implication":"Stale evidence can create false confidence in current security posture.","test_case":"Change a security-relevant dependency and evaluate whether linked evidence is marked for revalidation.","expected_test_result":"Affected evidence is identified as requiring review.","verification_method":"Trace security evidence to changed components and assumptions.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B48-R01","D57-B48-R02"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B60-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Quality","concept":"Security evidence independence","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why can multiple security checks fail to provide independent assurance when they share the same evidence source?","answer":"If several controls or reviews depend on the same potentially flawed source, their agreement does not establish independent confirmation.","explanation":"Correlated evidence can produce apparent corroboration while preserving the same underlying blind spot.","principle":"Assurance strength depends partly on independence of evidence and failure modes.","rule":"Identify shared evidence dependencies when assessing corroboration.","objective":"Avoid overstating confidence from correlated security evidence.","failure_mode":"Multiple security components all rely on the same incorrect identity or telemetry source and therefore reach the same wrong conclusion.","cause":"Evidence sources are correlated but treated as independent.","root_cause":"Evidence-dependency relationships are not represented.","solution":"Map evidence dependencies and seek independent corroboration where material.","alternative":"Use one authoritative source with explicit trust assumptions rather than pretending duplicated dependent checks are independent.","tradeoff":"Independent evidence can increase cost and complexity.","edge_case":"Some evidence must necessarily share infrastructure; the shared dependency should be treated as an explicit common-mode risk.","security_implication":"Common-mode evidence failure can defeat multiple security controls simultaneously.","test_case":"Simulate failure of a shared evidence source and observe dependent security decisions.","expected_test_result":"The common dependency and resulting assurance limitation become visible.","verification_method":"Perform evidence-dependency and common-mode analysis.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B47-R02","D57-B2-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B60-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Quality","concept":"Security uncertainty preservation","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why should a security knowledge system preserve unknown states instead of forcing every lesson into true or false?","answer":"Security evidence is often incomplete, contradictory, delayed, or context-dependent. Explicit unknown states prevent unsupported certainty from entering security decisions.","explanation":"A missing observation, incomplete telemetry, or unresolved contradiction does not justify selecting a definite security conclusion.","principle":"Uncertainty is information and should be represented explicitly.","rule":"Use explicit unknown, unresolved, or conflicted states when evidence is insufficient.","objective":"Prevent false certainty from contaminating security reasoning.","failure_mode":"An evidence gap is automatically interpreted as a safe state.","cause":"Knowledge model requires binary conclusions.","root_cause":"Uncertainty is not represented as a first-class state.","solution":"Add explicit uncertainty, confidence, evidence, and contradiction metadata.","alternative":"Use probabilistic or confidence-aware reasoning where appropriate, while preserving the underlying evidence state.","tradeoff":"Uncertainty-aware reasoning is more complex than binary classification.","edge_case":"A formally verified invariant may justify a stronger conclusion within its defined scope.","security_implication":"False certainty can cause unsafe authorization, remediation, or risk decisions.","test_case":"Provide incomplete and contradictory security evidence to the knowledge system.","expected_test_result":"The system preserves uncertainty or conflict rather than inventing a definitive conclusion.","verification_method":"Test knowledge-state transitions using incomplete and contradictory evidence.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B2-R02","D57-B2-R04","D57-B50-R03"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}
{"record_id":"D57-B60-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Security Lessons","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Quality","concept":"Security lesson lifecycle closure","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What complete lifecycle should a high-quality reusable security lesson follow?","answer":"A reusable lesson should preserve the original evidence, identify the security issue and causal reasoning, define scope and assumptions, connect remediation or controls, record verification status, capture residual uncertainty, and remain subject to later review when its assumptions change.","explanation":"Security learning is strongest when the chain from evidence to conclusion to control to verification remains traceable rather than storing only a final recommendation.","principle":"Security knowledge should preserve provenance, reasoning, applicability, verification, and lifecycle state.","rule":"Do not finalize reusable security knowledge without preserving its evidence status and applicability conditions.","objective":"Create durable, auditable, non-overclaiming security knowledge.","requirements":["Evidence provenance.","Issue and causal model.","Scope and assumptions.","Control or remediation relationship.","Verification status.","Residual uncertainty.","Lifecycle review conditions."],"failure_mode":"A final lesson survives while its evidence, assumptions, and verification state are lost.","cause":"Knowledge extraction stores only the conclusion.","root_cause":"Lesson representation lacks full evidence-to-outcome traceability.","solution":"Represent the complete lesson lifecycle as linked machine-readable records.","alternative":"Use a security knowledge graph with explicit provenance and verification relationships.","tradeoff":"Richer records require more storage and lifecycle management.","edge_case":"Some generic principles may not have local operational evidence and must remain explicitly classified as general knowledge rather than experience.","security_implication":"Loss of provenance and verification state can cause unsupported lessons to be treated as operationally proven facts.","test_case":"Create a lesson from incomplete evidence and inspect whether evidence, scope, assumptions, controls, and verification states remain linked.","expected_test_result":"The lesson remains traceable and does not acquire unsupported verification status.","verification_method":"Perform end-to-end lesson lifecycle validation from evidence ingestion through reusable knowledge publication.","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"GENERAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","observed_result":"UNKNOWN — no operational observation supplied","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[],"related_records":["D57-B50-R01","D57-B50-R02","D57-B50-R03","D57-B50-R05"],"created_at":"2026-09-22","updated_at":"2026-09-22"}

{"record_id":"D57-B61-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Concurrency Security","subtopic":"TOCTOU","concept":"Time-of-check-to-time-of-use race","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a security check become invalid before the protected operation executes?","answer":"Because another actor or concurrent operation can change the relevant resource between the security check and its use.","explanation":"A check performed on mutable state is not necessarily authoritative at the later point of use. Security-sensitive operations should minimize the check/use gap or use atomic mechanisms that bind authorization and operation to the same state transition.","principle":"A security decision must remain valid for the operation it authorizes.","rule":"Do not rely on a separately checked mutable condition when the protected operation can observe changed state.","objective":"Prevent race conditions from invalidating security decisions.","constraints":["Shared or mutable state may change concurrently.","The check and protected operation may execute in different synchronization contexts."],"failure_mode":"Authorization or safety condition passes during validation but is false when the resource is actually used.","cause":"Concurrent state mutation between validation and use.","root_cause":"Security decision is separated from the state transition it is intended to protect.","solution":"Use atomic operations, transactions, locks, version checks, compare-and-swap semantics, or equivalent mechanisms that bind validation to use.","edge_case":"A resource can be replaced while retaining the same logical identifier.","security_implication":"TOCTOU races can produce unauthorized access, privilege escalation, or unsafe resource manipulation.","test_case":"Change the protected resource after validation but before the operation and verify that the operation is rejected or atomically applies to the authorized state.","test_input":"Concurrent mutation scheduled between authorization check and resource use.","expected_test_result":"The protected operation cannot consume unauthorized or changed state.","testing_guidance":"Introduce deterministic synchronization points in tests rather than relying only on probabilistic race reproduction.","verification_method":"Inspect atomicity guarantees and execute controlled concurrent tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the authorization decision and protected state transition cannot be separated by an exploitable race.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B61-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Concurrency Security","subtopic":"Locking","concept":"Security-critical synchronization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What lesson applies when security state depends on concurrent updates?","answer":"The synchronization mechanism must protect the security invariant rather than merely individual data structures.","explanation":"Locking one variable does not guarantee that a multi-variable authorization or ownership invariant remains consistent. All state participating in the invariant must be updated under an appropriate atomicity model.","principle":"Synchronize the invariant, not just the field.","rule":"Identify the complete security invariant and ensure every state transition that can affect it participates in the same synchronization or transactional boundary.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent concurrent state from bypassing security controls.","constraints":["Security invariants may span multiple objects.","Different code paths may mutate related state."],"failure_mode":"Each individual operation appears synchronized while the combined security invariant can still be violated.","cause":"Incomplete synchronization boundary.","root_cause":"The implementation defines synchronization around storage operations rather than around the security property.","solution":"Define the invariant explicitly and enforce atomic updates across all relevant state.","alternative":"Use transactional storage or serialized state transitions where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Broader synchronization can reduce concurrency and increase contention.","security_implication":"Broken invariants can result in duplicate authority, unauthorized ownership, inconsistent policy state, or resource misuse.","test_case":"Run competing updates against all state components participating in a security invariant.","expected_test_result":"No execution interleaving produces an invalid security state.","testing_guidance":"Use stress and deterministic concurrency tests with invariant assertions after every committed transition.","verification_method":"Review synchronization boundaries against the formal security invariant and test concurrent transitions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B61-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Concurrency Security","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Security-relevant check-and-update atomicity","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a security-sensitive quota or ownership check be implemented under concurrency?","answer":"The check and the state update should occur as one atomic transaction or equivalent indivisible operation.","explanation":"Separate read and write operations allow competing requests to observe the same pre-update state and both pass the security condition.","principle":"Security-sensitive authorization conditions that depend on mutable state should be evaluated and committed atomically.","rule":"Never implement a security-critical check-then-update sequence as independent unsynchronized operations when concurrent requests can race.","objective":"Prevent concurrent requests from collectively violating a limit or ownership invariant.","requirements":["Atomic transaction or equivalent concurrency primitive.","Explicit invariant defining the maximum permitted state."],"failure_mode":"Multiple requests independently pass the same limit check and collectively exceed the permitted state.","cause":"Non-atomic check and update.","root_cause":"Concurrency model does not bind the security decision to the state mutation.","solution":"Use a transaction, atomic compare-and-update, serialized queue, or equivalent mechanism.","security_implication":"Attackers may exploit concurrency to exceed quotas, duplicate operations, or obtain conflicting ownership.","performance_implication":"Serialization can reduce throughput and should be scoped to the minimum necessary state.","test_case":"Submit simultaneous requests that collectively exceed the allowed limit.","expected_test_result":"Only operations consistent with the invariant are committed.","verification_method":"Inspect transaction boundaries and run deterministic concurrent tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B61-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Concurrency Security","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Security state during cancellation","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must cancellation and interruption be considered in security-sensitive operations?","answer":"An interrupted operation can leave partially updated security state unless cleanup and rollback semantics are explicitly defined.","explanation":"Cancellation may occur between state mutations, resource acquisition, or authorization-related transitions. Security invariants must survive cancellation just as they must survive ordinary failures.","principle":"Unexpected termination must not leave security state in an invalid intermediate condition.","rule":"Design cancellation paths as explicit failure paths with rollback or safe intermediate states.","objective":"Preserve security invariants during interrupted execution.","failure_mode":"Operation is cancelled after one security-relevant mutation but before a dependent mutation.","cause":"Cancellation not treated as a transactional failure.","root_cause":"Security state transitions assume normal completion.","solution":"Use transactions, compensating actions, structured cleanup, or state machines that make intermediate states safe.","edge_case":"Process cancellation occurs immediately after privilege or ownership state is modified.","security_implication":"Partial state can create orphaned privileges, inconsistent ownership, or bypassable controls.","test_case":"Inject cancellation at each security-sensitive transition boundary.","expected_test_result":"No cancellation point leaves an unauthorized or unsafe committed state.","verification_method":"Perform fault-injection tests at transition boundaries and inspect resulting state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B61-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Concurrency Security","subtopic":"Duplicate execution","concept":"Concurrent duplicate security-sensitive actions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security lesson applies when the same action can be submitted concurrently?","answer":"Authorization alone does not prevent duplicate execution; the operation may require idempotency or atomic deduplication semantics.","explanation":"Two independently authorized requests can still produce an unsafe combined result if the action is not designed for concurrency.","principle":"Authorization establishes permission for an action but does not guarantee safe multiplicity of execution.","rule":"Define whether security-sensitive operations may execute more than once and enforce that property explicitly.","objective":"Prevent duplicate execution from violating security or resource invariants.","failure_mode":"Two valid requests execute the same sensitive operation when only one execution was intended.","cause":"No atomic uniqueness or idempotency constraint.","root_cause":"Permission semantics were incorrectly treated as execution uniqueness semantics.","solution":"Use transactional uniqueness, idempotency keys, sequence numbers, or equivalent controls.","security_implication":"Duplicate execution can cause repeated transfers, repeated privilege changes, duplicated credentials, or repeated destructive actions.","test_case":"Submit identical authorized requests concurrently.","expected_test_result":"The system enforces the documented multiplicity rule.","verification_method":"Review uniqueness/idempotency guarantees and execute concurrent duplicate tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B62-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"DNS and Name Resolution Security","subtopic":"Trust","concept":"DNS result as untrusted routing input","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should a DNS response not automatically be treated as proof that a destination is trusted?","answer":"Name resolution determines an address but does not by itself establish that the resulting destination is authorized for the intended security-sensitive operation.","explanation":"Resolution can vary by environment, time, resolver, configuration, or attacker influence. Security policy should explicitly define what destination identity is trusted.","principle":"Routing information and authorization evidence are different security properties.","rule":"Do not equate successful name resolution with destination authorization.","objective":"Prevent mutable or attacker-influenced resolution data from silently becoming a trust decision.","failure_mode":"An application connects to a resolved address that was not intended by the security policy.","cause":"DNS result used directly as an authorization decision.","root_cause":"Network routing metadata was treated as authoritative identity.","solution":"Bind destination authorization to authenticated identity, explicit allowlists, validated service identity, or another appropriate security mechanism.","security_implication":"Incorrect resolution assumptions can enable access to unintended infrastructure or sensitive endpoints.","test_case":"Return an unexpected but syntactically valid resolution result and verify the security policy response.","expected_test_result":"The application does not authorize an unintended destination merely because resolution succeeded.","verification_method":"Review destination trust logic and test alternate resolution results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B62-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"DNS and Name Resolution Security","subtopic":"Race conditions","concept":"Resolution-time versus connection-time destination change","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security problem occurs when a hostname is resolved once but the actual connection can use a different destination later?","answer":"A validation decision based on the first resolution may not describe the destination ultimately contacted.","explanation":"Hostname resolution and network connection are separate operations. If the address can change between validation and use, a security check based solely on the earlier result may become stale.","principle":"Security validation must correspond to the destination actually used.","rule":"Do not assume a previously observed address remains the address used by a later network operation.","objective":"Prevent resolution/use races from bypassing destination restrictions.","failure_mode":"A trusted initial destination is replaced by an untrusted destination before connection.","cause":"Time gap between resolution validation and actual connection.","root_cause":"Destination identity was checked outside the authoritative connection boundary.","solution":"Use connection-time destination validation, controlled resolution, authenticated endpoint identity, or another mechanism appropriate to the threat model.","edge_case":"Multiple addresses are returned and the client retries another address after the first connection fails.","security_implication":"Security filters based only on an initial resolution can be bypassed through destination changes or alternate addresses.","test_case":"Change resolution results between validation and connection attempts.","expected_test_result":"Every actual destination is subjected to the required security policy.","verification_method":"Trace resolution, validation, connection, and retry behavior as one security flow.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B62-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"DNS and Name Resolution Security","subtopic":"Environment assumptions","concept":"Split-horizon DNS trust assumptions","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should systems document assumptions about internal and external DNS views?","answer":"The same hostname can resolve differently in different network contexts, so hostname equality does not necessarily imply destination equality.","explanation":"Internal, external, development, testing, and production environments may intentionally provide different DNS answers. Security controls that rely on one environment's resolution behavior can fail when deployed elsewhere.","principle":"Security assumptions about name resolution must be environment-specific when resolution views differ.","rule":"Document and verify the expected resolver context and destination identity for security-sensitive names.","objective":"Prevent environment-specific DNS behavior from violating security boundaries.","failure_mode":"A hostname trusted in one environment resolves to a different security domain in another.","cause":"Undocumented split-horizon or environment-specific DNS behavior.","root_cause":"Security policy assumed hostname identity was globally stable.","solution":"Define environment-specific trust boundaries and verify the actual authenticated destination identity.","edge_case":"A test environment resolves an internal service while production resolves a public service under the same hostname.","security_implication":"Unexpected resolution can redirect sensitive operations across trust boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Test security behavior under each supported DNS view rather than only the developer environment.","verification_method":"Compare configured resolution context with the security policy and verify destination identity.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B62-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"DNS and Name Resolution Security","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Security implications of DNS caching","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What lesson applies when DNS caching is used in security-sensitive routing?","answer":"Caching improves efficiency but can extend the lifetime of a previously valid or invalid resolution decision.","explanation":"Security behavior may depend on how long cached results remain authoritative for the application. Cache lifetime should be considered when destinations, authorization, or incident response can change.","principle":"Caching changes the temporal properties of security decisions.","rule":"Treat cache lifetime and invalidation behavior as part of the security model when DNS results influence sensitive operations.","objective":"Prevent stale resolution state from undermining current security policy.","tradeoff":"Shorter caching can improve responsiveness to security changes but may increase resolution traffic and latency.","edge_case":"A destination is revoked but clients continue using a previously cached address.","security_implication":"Stale routing information can prolong access to a destination that is no longer trusted.","testing_guidance":"Test security behavior before, during, and after cache expiration or invalidation events.","verification_method":"Inspect cache semantics and verify that security policy remains valid for the permitted cache lifetime.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B62-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B62","topic":"DNS and Name Resolution Security","subtopic":"Negative responses","concept":"Negative DNS response handling","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can negative DNS responses matter to security logic?","answer":"A cached or manipulated absence of a DNS record can alter fallback behavior and potentially redirect a security-sensitive operation.","explanation":"Applications sometimes interpret resolution failure as permission to use defaults, alternate hosts, cached addresses, or other fallback paths. The security properties of those fallback paths must be explicit.","principle":"Resolution failure is an input condition, not automatically a safe outcome.","rule":"Define secure behavior for both successful and unsuccessful name resolution.","objective":"Prevent DNS failure from activating an unintended trust path.","failure_mode":"A failed lookup causes the application to use an insecure or unintended fallback destination.","cause":"Unspecified security semantics for resolution failure.","root_cause":"Error handling was designed for availability rather than security invariants.","solution":"Define explicit failure behavior and ensure fallback destinations receive equivalent security validation.","security_implication":"DNS failures can become indirect routing or authorization bypass conditions.","test_case":"Simulate positive, negative, timeout, and malformed resolution responses.","expected_test_result":"Each response follows an explicitly defined security policy.","verification_method":"Review error-handling branches and test all supported DNS failure modes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B63-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Abuse Resistance","subtopic":"Rate limiting","concept":"Security purpose of rate limits","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should a security lesson say about rate limiting?","answer":"Rate limits are controls against excessive activity, not substitutes for authentication, authorization, or input validation.","explanation":"A request can be fully authenticated and authorized while still being dangerous when repeated at excessive frequency. Conversely, a rate limit cannot prove that an individual request is legitimate.","principle":"Abuse-resistance controls complement, rather than replace, core security controls.","rule":"Assign each security control a defined threat it mitigates and do not treat rate limiting as universal authorization.","objective":"Prevent control-purpose confusion.","security_implication":"Misclassifying rate limits as authorization can leave unauthorized access paths unprotected.","testing_guidance":"Test authorized, unauthorized, burst, sustained, and distributed request patterns separately.","verification_method":"Map rate-limit behavior to its documented threat model and verify that independent authorization remains enforced.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B63-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Abuse Resistance","subtopic":"Identity dimensions","concept":"Rate-limit key selection","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why is the identity used for rate limiting a security design decision?","answer":"Different keys such as account, credential, source network, device, resource, or operation can detect different abuse patterns and can each be evaded or shared differently.","explanation":"A single rate-limit dimension may be ineffective against distributed activity or unfairly constrain shared infrastructure. The selected key should correspond to the threat being mitigated.","principle":"Rate-limit dimensions should match the abuse model.","rule":"Document why each rate-limit key is used and avoid assuming one identifier represents one actor.","objective":"Make abuse controls resistant to obvious identity-sharing and distribution patterns.","failure_mode":"Attackers distribute activity across identities or infrastructure that the rate limiter treats independently.","cause":"Rate-limit key does not represent the relevant abuse unit.","root_cause":"Threat model was not mapped to rate-limit identity semantics.","solution":"Use appropriately scoped and possibly combined dimensions while considering legitimate shared usage.","tradeoff":"More dimensions improve detection of some distributed abuse but increase state, complexity, and risk of false restriction.","security_implication":"Weak key selection can allow abusive activity to bypass limits.","test_case":"Distribute equivalent activity across the identifiers used by the limiter.","expected_test_result":"Behavior matches the documented threat model and policy.","verification_method":"Review rate-limit key semantics and perform distributed abuse simulations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B63-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Abuse Resistance","subtopic":"Distributed enforcement","concept":"Distributed rate-limit consistency","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What can happen when rate limits are enforced independently by multiple nodes without a suitable shared or coordinated model?","answer":"Aggregate activity can exceed the intended limit because each node observes only part of the traffic.","explanation":"A local limiter may be correct for its own state while the global security policy is violated across nodes.","principle":"Distributed enforcement must match the scope of the security invariant.","rule":"If a limit is global, its enforcement mechanism must provide semantics appropriate to global enforcement.","objective":"Prevent horizontal scaling from unintentionally weakening abuse controls.","failure_mode":"Requests distributed across nodes collectively exceed a supposedly global limit.","cause":"Per-node state used for a global policy.","root_cause":"Deployment topology was not included in the security model.","solution":"Use coordinated state, partition-aware limits, or explicitly define the limit as node-local.","security_implication":"Attackers can exploit distribution to amplify activity beyond intended controls.","performance_implication":"Global coordination can add latency, state management, and availability dependencies.","test_case":"Distribute traffic across all enforcement nodes while maintaining a constant aggregate rate.","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches the documented scope of the limit.","verification_method":"Test enforcement across the actual supported topology or a representative model.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B63-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Abuse Resistance","subtopic":"Cost asymmetry","concept":"Resource-cost abuse","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security testing consider operations where attacker cost is much lower than defender cost?","answer":"A request can be individually valid while imposing disproportionate computation, storage, network, or downstream cost on the system.","explanation":"Abuse resistance must consider resource amplification, not only request frequency.","principle":"Security controls should account for attacker-to-defender cost asymmetry.","rule":"Identify operations whose processing, storage, or downstream effects can greatly exceed the cost of initiating them.","objective":"Prevent economically or computationally asymmetric abuse.","failure_mode":"Low-cost inputs trigger expensive processing or durable resource consumption.","cause":"Resource cost was not included in the threat model.","root_cause":"Security analysis focused on correctness and authorization without modeling resource amplification.","solution":"Apply bounded work, quotas, admission controls, staged processing, or cost-aware limits as appropriate.","security_implication":"Attackers may exhaust resources without exceeding ordinary request-count limits.","performance_implication":"Defensive work should itself remain bounded.","test_case":"Measure resource consumption across low-cost and high-cost inputs under the same request limits.","expected_test_result":"The system maintains documented resource safety bounds.","verification_method":"Perform threat-model-based resource amplification analysis and controlled load testing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B63-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Abuse Resistance","subtopic":"Bypass handling","concept":"Rate-limit failure semantics","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security lesson applies when the rate-limit service becomes unavailable?","answer":"The fallback behavior must be explicitly threat-modeled; silently disabling a required abuse control can change the system's security posture.","explanation":"Fail-open may preserve availability but remove an important protection, while fail-closed may protect the limit but affect legitimate availability. The correct behavior depends on the protected operation and threat model.","principle":"Security-control failure is itself a security state transition.","rule":"Define and verify the security behavior when the rate-limit dependency is unavailable or inconsistent.","objective":"Prevent accidental weakening of abuse protection during dependency failures.","tradeoff":"Fail-open and fail-closed behavior have different availability and security consequences.","security_implication":"An unplanned fail-open path can remove abuse resistance exactly when defensive infrastructure is degraded.","test_case":"Simulate limiter timeout, unavailable state, stale state, and malformed responses.","expected_test_result":"The system follows the explicitly documented security policy for each failure mode.","verification_method":"Inspect fallback branches and execute dependency-failure tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B64-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Server-Side Request Security","subtopic":"SSRF","concept":"User-controlled outbound destination","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is a user-controlled URL a security boundary even when the application itself is trusted?","answer":"The server becomes the network principal making the request, potentially giving user-controlled input access to destinations unavailable to the user directly.","explanation":"Outbound requests inherit the server's network reachability and credentials. Therefore destination selection is security-sensitive input.","principle":"Server-side network reachability must not be implicitly delegated through untrusted destination input.","rule":"Treat externally supplied URLs, hosts, ports, and redirects as security-sensitive and validate them against an explicit outbound policy.","objective":"Prevent unintended server-side network access.","failure_mode":"An attacker causes the server to connect to an internal, privileged, or otherwise restricted destination.","cause":"Untrusted destination accepted without appropriate network-policy enforcement.","root_cause":"Application treated outbound request construction as ordinary data handling rather than authority delegation.","solution":"Use an explicit outbound destination policy, safe URL parsing, controlled network egress, and redirect-aware validation.","security_implication":"SSRF can expose internal services, metadata endpoints, administrative interfaces, or network trust relationships.","testing_guidance":"Test public, private, loopback, link-local, redirected, encoded, and alternate-address destinations according to the environment's threat model.","verification_method":"Review URL parsing and egress policy together and execute destination-policy tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B64-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Server-Side Request Security","subtopic":"Redirects","concept":"Redirect revalidation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must redirects be included in outbound destination security checks?","answer":"The final destination may differ from the initially validated destination.","explanation":"A URL that is safe at the first hop can redirect to a destination outside the allowed security policy. Validation performed only before the first request does not necessarily constrain later hops.","principle":"Every security-sensitive destination transition must remain within policy.","rule":"Apply destination policy to each redirect target or disable redirects when that better matches the threat model.","objective":"Prevent redirect-based outbound-policy bypass.","failure_mode":"Trusted URL redirects to a restricted destination and the client follows it automatically.","cause":"Only the initial destination was validated.","root_cause":"Security validation was bound to the request origin rather than the complete request chain.","solution":"Disable automatic redirects or validate each redirect destination against the same policy.","security_implication":"Redirects can turn an apparently safe request into access to an unintended network location.","test_case":"Return a redirect from an allowed endpoint to a disallowed endpoint.","expected_test_result":"The client blocks the redirect or applies the documented policy to the new destination.","verification_method":"Inspect redirect handling and test multi-hop redirect chains.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B64-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Server-Side Request Security","subtopic":"URL parsing","concept":"Parser consistency","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What security lesson applies when different URL parsers interpret the same destination differently?","answer":"Security validation and network execution must use compatible parsing semantics; otherwise one component may approve a destination another component interprets differently.","explanation":"Differences in normalization, hostname parsing, ports, encoding, user-information fields, or address representation can create security-policy gaps.","principle":"Security policy must be defined over the same canonical representation used for execution.","rule":"Avoid validating one interpretation of a URL and executing another interpretation.","objective":"Prevent parser differential from bypassing outbound security policy.","failure_mode":"The validator identifies an allowed destination while the network client connects elsewhere according to its parsing rules.","cause":"Inconsistent URL parsing or normalization.","root_cause":"Security policy and execution components do not share equivalent destination semantics.","solution":"Use a well-defined parsing model, canonical representation, and consistent enforcement before connection.","security_implication":"Parser discrepancies can enable destination-policy bypasses.","testing_guidance":"Test ambiguous, encoded, unusual, and boundary URL forms across both validation and execution components.","verification_method":"Compare parser outputs and verify that the final connection target matches the security decision.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B64-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Server-Side Request Security","subtopic":"Credential exposure","concept":"Ambient credentials on outbound requests","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should outbound requests not automatically inherit all server credentials?","answer":"A request redirected or routed to an unintended destination can expose credentials that were never intended for that destination.","explanation":"Credential attachment expands the consequences of destination-policy failure. Credentials should be scoped to explicitly trusted destinations and required operations.","principle":"Credential use must be bound to destination trust and least privilege.","rule":"Do not attach sensitive credentials to outbound requests unless the destination and purpose satisfy an explicit trust policy.","objective":"Limit the impact of outbound destination mistakes.","security_implication":"Destination confusion combined with ambient credentials can turn network access into credential disclosure or privilege escalation.","test_case":"Attempt outbound requests to allowed and disallowed destinations with credential-bearing and credential-free modes.","expected_test_result":"Credentials are sent only where explicitly authorized.","verification_method":"Inspect request construction and capture test traffic in a controlled environment.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B64-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Server-Side Request Security","subtopic":"Network-layer enforcement","concept":"Application validation plus egress restriction","knowledge_type":"DEFENSE_IN_DEPTH","question":"Why should SSRF defenses not depend exclusively on application-layer URL validation?","answer":"Application parsing can contain defects or be bypassed by unexpected network behavior, while network-layer egress controls provide an independent boundary.","explanation":"A layered design reduces reliance on one parser, policy implementation, or application code path. Network restrictions should still match legitimate application requirements.","principle":"Sensitive outbound access benefits from independent enforcement layers.","rule":"Combine application destination policy with appropriate network egress restrictions when the environment permits.","objective":"Limit the consequences of application-layer validation failure.","tradeoff":"Network restrictions can complicate legitimate integrations and must be designed around required connectivity.","security_implication":"Independent egress restrictions can prevent application flaws from reaching sensitive network zones.","verification_method":"Verify both application policy and network egress policy and test attempted access through each layer.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B65-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Transaction boundaries","concept":"Security invariant across database transactions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must transaction boundaries reflect security invariants?","answer":"A transaction that commits only part of a security-sensitive state transition can leave authorization, ownership, or audit state inconsistent.","explanation":"Database correctness and security correctness are related but not identical. The transaction should encompass the state changes required to preserve the security invariant.","principle":"Security invariants must survive partial failure and concurrent transactions.","rule":"Define transaction boundaries around the complete security-relevant state transition.","objective":"Prevent partial commits from producing invalid security states.","failure_mode":"Primary state changes commit while the associated authorization or ownership update fails.","cause":"Transaction boundary excludes security-relevant state.","root_cause":"Database transaction design followed data convenience rather than the security invariant.","solution":"Use an atomic transaction or explicit compensating state machine appropriate to the storage architecture.","security_implication":"Partial state can create unauthorized access, orphaned ownership, or inconsistent policy enforcement.","test_case":"Inject failure between each security-relevant database mutation.","expected_test_result":"The system either commits the complete invariant-preserving transition or safely rolls back.","verification_method":"Review transaction scope and perform fault-injection tests at commit boundaries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B65-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Object authorization","concept":"Authorization before object mutation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What lesson applies when database identifiers are directly supplied by a caller?","answer":"The identifier identifies an object but does not establish that the caller is authorized to read or mutate it.","explanation":"Database lookup and authorization are separate concerns. Security enforcement must bind the requested object to the caller's current authority.","principle":"Object identity is not object authorization.","rule":"Perform authorization using trusted server-side context before security-sensitive object access or mutation.","objective":"Prevent identifier-based cross-object access.","failure_mode":"A caller changes an identifier and receives or modifies another object's data.","cause":"Database lookup performed without object-level authorization.","root_cause":"Identifier validation was incorrectly treated as authorization.","solution":"Apply object-level authorization as part of the access path and verify ownership or delegated permissions.","security_implication":"Missing object authorization can produce horizontal privilege escalation or data exposure.","test_case":"Use an authorized identity with identifiers belonging to another security principal.","expected_test_result":"The unauthorized object operation is denied.","verification_method":"Review data-access functions and test cross-object access systematically.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B65-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Soft deletion","concept":"Security semantics of soft-deleted records","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must soft deletion be included in security analysis?","answer":"A logically deleted object may remain physically present and accessible through alternate queries, indexes, exports, caches, or administrative paths.","explanation":"Changing a record's status is not necessarily equivalent to removing all access paths. Security policy must define what deleted means for every supported interface.","principle":"Logical deletion is a state transition whose security semantics must be explicit.","rule":"Ensure every access path enforces the security meaning of the deleted state.","objective":"Prevent deleted or revoked objects from remaining unintentionally accessible.","failure_mode":"Normal application views hide deleted records while another query path still exposes them.","cause":"Deletion semantics enforced only in selected code paths.","root_cause":"Logical state was not treated as a universal security constraint.","solution":"Centralize or consistently enforce deletion-state authorization across queries, APIs, exports, caches, and administrative paths.","security_implication":"Revoked or deleted data may remain accessible after the intended security transition.","testing_guidance":"Test direct queries, APIs, background jobs, exports, and administrative interfaces against deleted state.","verification_method":"Trace all supported access paths and verify consistent enforcement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B65-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Database error handling","concept":"Security-safe database error exposure","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should database errors be separated from externally visible error messages?","answer":"Raw database errors can expose schema details, query structure, identifiers, configuration, or other information useful to an attacker.","explanation":"Diagnostic detail can remain available to controlled internal logging while external responses expose only information required by the application contract.","principle":"Operational diagnostics and external error disclosure have different security requirements.","rule":"Map internal database failures to controlled external responses and protect detailed diagnostics.","objective":"Reduce unintended information disclosure without losing diagnostic capability.","security_implication":"Detailed database errors can aid reconnaissance and reveal sensitive implementation information.","testing_guidance":"Trigger representative database failures and inspect both external responses and protected diagnostic logs.","verification_method":"Review exception mapping and verify that sensitive database details are not exposed through untrusted interfaces.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B65-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Constraints","concept":"Database constraints as security invariants","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security invariants be enforced by the database where appropriate instead of relying only on application code?","answer":"Database constraints can provide an independent enforcement point that remains active across multiple application paths.","explanation":"Application-level checks can be missed by new code, administrative tools, background jobs, or alternate interfaces. Appropriate database constraints can prevent invalid states regardless of the caller path.","principle":"Critical invariants should be enforced as close as practical to the authoritative state.","rule":"Use database constraints for invariants that can be expressed reliably at the storage layer.","objective":"Reduce the number of application paths capable of creating invalid security state.","tradeoff":"Some complex policy decisions cannot be expressed solely through database constraints and require application or service-layer enforcement.","security_implication":"Independent storage constraints can reduce bypass opportunities.","verification_method":"Compare documented invariants against actual database constraints and test alternate write paths.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B66-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B66","topic":"Memory and Resource Safety","subtopic":"Sensitive memory","concept":"Sensitive-data lifetime in memory","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why does logical completion of a security-sensitive operation not necessarily mean sensitive data has left memory?","answer":"Copies can remain in buffers, temporary objects, caches, logs, serialization structures, or allocator-managed memory after logical use ends.","explanation":"High-level language memory management often makes exact lifetime difficult to control. Security design should minimize unnecessary copies and avoid assuming that ordinary object release guarantees immediate physical erasure.","principle":"Data lifetime should be treated as broader than variable scope when sensitive information is involved.","rule":"Minimize sensitive copies and use appropriate platform-supported handling where memory lifetime matters.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary exposure of sensitive data in process memory.","security_implication":"Residual sensitive data may increase exposure after logical use has ended.","tradeoff":"Aggressive memory handling can be difficult or ineffective in managed runtimes and should not be represented as guaranteed erasure without evidence.","verification_method":"Review data flow and memory-handling guarantees for the specific runtime rather than assuming universal erasure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B66-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B66","topic":"Memory and Resource Safety","subtopic":"Integer safety","concept":"Integer overflow and security boundaries","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can integer conversion become a security issue?","answer":"An overflow, truncation, sign conversion, or incompatible integer width can cause a security-sensitive size, offset, limit, or index to be interpreted differently from the validated value.","explanation":"Validation performed before conversion may no longer constrain the representation used by a lower-level operation.","principle":"Security validation must cover the representation actually consumed by the operation.","rule":"Validate numeric ranges after all security-relevant conversions and before use in allocation, indexing, sizing, or boundary calculations.","objective":"Prevent numeric representation changes from bypassing security constraints.","failure_mode":"A validated large value is converted into a smaller or differently interpreted value and produces an unsafe operation.","cause":"Unsafe numeric conversion.","root_cause":"Validation and execution used different numeric domains.","solution":"Use bounded numeric types where available and explicitly validate conversion ranges.","security_implication":"Numeric errors can cause memory corruption, out-of-bounds access, truncation, resource exhaustion, or authorization boundary errors.","test_case":"Test minimum, maximum, overflow, underflow, signedness, and conversion-boundary values.","expected_test_result":"Values outside the supported representation are rejected safely.","verification_method":"Review every security-sensitive numeric conversion and execute boundary tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B66-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B66","topic":"Memory and Resource Safety","subtopic":"Resource exhaustion","concept":"Bounded resource consumption","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security lessons include memory, CPU, file descriptor, and queue bounds?","answer":"An input can be syntactically valid and authorized while still causing unbounded resource consumption.","explanation":"Security failures are not limited to confidentiality and authorization. Resource exhaustion can violate availability and can also cascade into unsafe fallback behavior.","principle":"Resource bounds are part of the security contract for operations exposed to untrusted input.","rule":"Define and enforce explicit resource limits for security-sensitive untrusted operations.","objective":"Prevent unbounded attacker-controlled resource consumption.","failure_mode":"A valid request causes unlimited or unexpectedly large allocation or processing.","cause":"No effective resource bound.","root_cause":"Security analysis considered functional correctness but omitted resource behavior.","solution":"Apply bounded input, work quotas, memory limits, queue limits, timeouts, or admission controls as appropriate.","security_implication":"Resource exhaustion can cause denial of service or trigger secondary security failures.","testing_guidance":"Test maximum supported values and adversarially large inputs under concurrent load.","verification_method":"Verify that resource consumption remains within documented limits under worst-case supported input.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B66-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B66","topic":"Memory and Resource Safety","subtopic":"Cleanup","concept":"Security resource cleanup","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive resource cleanup be verified rather than assumed from normal execution?","answer":"Exceptions, cancellation, process termination, or alternate control flow can bypass normal cleanup paths.","explanation":"Resources such as temporary files, handles, locks, network connections, and sensitive buffers may require explicit lifecycle handling.","principle":"Cleanup is a security property when retained resources carry authority, secrets, or sensitive data.","rule":"Define cleanup semantics for normal completion and failure paths and test both.","objective":"Prevent stale security-sensitive resources from remaining usable or exposed.","failure_mode":"An exceptional path leaves a sensitive resource accessible after the operation ends.","cause":"Cleanup implemented only on the normal path.","root_cause":"Resource lifecycle was not modeled as a security invariant.","solution":"Use structured resource-management mechanisms and explicit failure-path tests.","security_implication":"Stale credentials, handles, files, locks, or temporary artifacts can extend access beyond intended lifetime.","verification_method":"Inject failures at each resource-acquisition stage and verify cleanup or safe containment.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B66-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B66","topic":"Memory and Resource Safety","subtopic":"Boundary checks","concept":"Length versus content validation","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should security checks validate both declared length and actual available data?","answer":"A declared length can be inconsistent with the actual buffer, stream, message, or file contents.","explanation":"Trusting metadata about size without verifying the underlying representation can cause truncation, over-read, allocation errors, or parser inconsistencies.","principle":"Security decisions must be based on authoritative representation rather than attacker-controlled metadata alone.","rule":"Validate that declared lengths are internally consistent with the actual data source before processing.","objective":"Prevent length metadata from creating unsafe parsing or allocation behavior.","failure_mode":"The parser trusts a declared size that exceeds available data or causes excessive allocation.","cause":"Length metadata accepted without consistency validation.","root_cause":"Metadata was treated as authoritative without binding it to the underlying representation.","solution":"Validate declared lengths against actual available data and enforce maximum supported sizes.","security_implication":"Inconsistent lengths can cause denial of service, parser confusion, or memory-safety failures.","test_case":"Supply mismatched length metadata and actual payload sizes.","expected_test_result":"The input is rejected or safely handled without unsafe allocation or access.","verification_method":"Test boundary and mismatch conditions at every parser or protocol layer.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B67-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Plugin and Extension Security","subtopic":"Privilege inheritance","concept":"Extension privilege inheritance","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is installing a plugin or extension a security decision?","answer":"Extension code generally executes with some portion of the host application's privileges and can therefore expand the effective trusted computing base.","explanation":"The security impact depends on what capabilities the host exposes to extensions and whether extensions are isolated, reviewed, signed, or otherwise trusted.","principle":"An extension's effective authority is determined by the capabilities available through its execution context.","rule":"Define and minimize extension capabilities explicitly rather than assuming extensions are harmless add-ons.","objective":"Control privilege expansion caused by dynamic extensions.","security_implication":"A compromised or malicious extension can misuse host capabilities.","verification_method":"Inventory extension capabilities and verify that access is restricted to documented requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B67-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Plugin and Extension Security","subtopic":"Capability design","concept":"Capability-scoped plugin interfaces","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a host reduce the security impact of plugins?","answer":"Expose narrowly scoped capabilities instead of giving plugins unrestricted access to host internals.","explanation":"A capability-oriented interface limits what extension code can request and makes privilege boundaries explicit.","principle":"Least privilege should apply to extension interfaces as well as users and services.","rule":"Expose only the minimum operations and data required by each extension capability.","objective":"Reduce the blast radius of compromised or defective extensions.","requirements":["Explicit capability inventory.","Host-enforced capability checks.","Minimal data and operation exposure."],"security_implication":"Capability scoping can limit extension access even when extension code is not fully trusted.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained capability design increases interface and lifecycle complexity.","test_case":"Attempt an extension operation outside its declared capability set.","expected_test_result":"The host denies the operation regardless of plugin intent.","verification_method":"Review host-side capability enforcement and test unauthorized plugin calls.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B67-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Plugin and Extension Security","subtopic":"Isolation","concept":"Extension isolation boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"When is plugin isolation particularly important?","answer":"Isolation becomes especially important when extension code is not fully trusted, has complex dependencies, or can access sensitive host resources.","explanation":"Running code in a separate process, restricted runtime, sandbox, or other boundary can reduce the consequences of extension compromise, although the strength of the boundary must be verified for the specific platform.","principle":"Untrusted or semi-trusted code should not automatically share the full authority of the host process.","rule":"Choose an isolation boundary appropriate to the extension threat model and required capabilities.","objective":"Limit compromise propagation from extensions to the host.","security_implication":"Weak or assumed isolation can create a false security boundary.","verification_method":"Document the isolation mechanism, reachable resources, escape assumptions, and tested guarantees.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B67-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Plugin and Extension Security","subtopic":"Dynamic loading","concept":"Module loading trust","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should dynamic module loading be treated as a security boundary?","answer":"Loading a module causes executable code from an external artifact or location to enter the trusted execution context.","explanation":"The security decision includes artifact identity, provenance, integrity, compatibility, and the authority granted after loading.","principle":"Code loading is an authority transition, not merely a file operation.","rule":"Apply explicit trust and integrity checks before dynamically loading executable components.","objective":"Prevent untrusted executable content from entering privileged execution contexts.","failure_mode":"A loader accepts a module solely because it is present at an expected path or has a compatible name.","cause":"Module identity and integrity not independently verified.","root_cause":"Code loading was treated as ordinary configuration or file discovery.","solution":"Use controlled loading paths, integrity/authenticity mechanisms, and explicit extension policy.","security_implication":"Compromised modules can execute with host privileges.","verification_method":"Review loading paths and test altered or unauthorized modules.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B67-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Plugin and Extension Security","subtopic":"Failure containment","concept":"Plugin failure containment","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should plugin failure be prevented from weakening host security controls?","answer":"A plugin exception, timeout, or crash must not cause the host to bypass authorization, validation, or isolation requirements.","explanation":"Extension failure can occur in unexpected places and may trigger fallback logic. Security invariants must remain enforced independently of plugin success.","principle":"Extension failure must preserve host security invariants.","rule":"Treat plugin failure as an untrusted failure condition and preserve mandatory host-side controls.","objective":"Prevent extension failures from becoming security bypasses.","failure_mode":"Plugin failure activates a fallback path that skips a required security check.","cause":"Security control depended on successful plugin execution.","root_cause":"Mandatory security enforcement was delegated to optional extension logic.","solution":"Keep mandatory controls in trusted host enforcement paths and define safe failure behavior.","security_implication":"Optional components should not control whether mandatory security checks occur.","test_case":"Force plugin timeout, exception, malformed response, and process termination.","expected_test_result":"Security controls remain active and the host enters a documented safe state.","verification_method":"Trace failure paths and execute fault-injection tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B68-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Randomness and Unpredictability","subtopic":"Security randomness","concept":"Cryptographic randomness versus ordinary randomness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must security-sensitive random values use an appropriate cryptographic randomness source?","answer":"General-purpose pseudo-random utilities may be predictable or unsuitable for secrets, tokens, reset links, nonces, or other security-sensitive values.","explanation":"Randomness requirements depend on the security property. Unpredictability is distinct from statistical variation or convenience randomness.","principle":"Use a randomness mechanism whose guarantees match the security property required.","rule":"Do not substitute ordinary simulation or convenience randomness for a cryptographically appropriate source in security-sensitive contexts.","objective":"Prevent predictable security values.","security_implication":"Predictable values can enable token guessing, secret recovery, replay, or cryptographic failure.","testing_guidance":"Verify the documented randomness API and runtime guarantees rather than attempting to infer cryptographic quality from a small output sample.","verification_method":"Inspect the selected randomness primitive against authoritative platform documentation and usage requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B68-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Randomness and Unpredictability","subtopic":"Seed handling","concept":"Predictable seed security failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can predictable seeding undermine otherwise random-looking security values?","answer":"If an attacker can predict or reconstruct the seed, outputs from a deterministic generator may become predictable.","explanation":"A generator's security depends on the guarantees of its seeding and state management, not only on the appearance of generated values.","principle":"Security randomness requires protected unpredictability across initialization and state.","rule":"Do not derive security-sensitive generator state from predictable attacker-controlled values unless the algorithm explicitly provides the required security guarantees.","objective":"Prevent reconstruction of security-sensitive random sequences.","failure_mode":"An attacker observes or predicts initialization inputs and reconstructs future outputs.","cause":"Predictable or insufficiently protected seed material.","root_cause":"Randomness quality was evaluated only at the output API rather than the complete generator lifecycle.","solution":"Use an operating-system or platform cryptographic randomness facility designed for security use.","security_implication":"Predictable generator state can compromise tokens, identifiers, keys, or nonces.","verification_method":"Review seed source, generator state handling, and platform guarantees.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B68-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Randomness and Unpredictability","subtopic":"Token generation","concept":"Security token uniqueness and unpredictability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified for security-sensitive generated tokens?","answer":"The implementation should satisfy the token's documented requirements for unpredictability, sufficient security strength, lifetime, and context binding where applicable.","explanation":"Uniqueness alone does not establish unpredictability. A token can be unique yet guessable.","principle":"Security token assurance requires more than collision avoidance.","rule":"Verify the generation mechanism and token lifecycle against the intended threat model rather than checking only for duplicate outputs.","objective":"Prevent weak token-generation implementations from being accepted as secure.","security_implication":"Guessable tokens can permit unauthorized access even when collisions are rare.","testing_guidance":"Tests can validate API usage, length, lifecycle, and rejection behavior, but output sampling alone cannot prove cryptographic unpredictability.","verification_method":"Review the approved cryptographic primitive and its documented security properties.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B68-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Randomness and Unpredictability","subtopic":"Context separation","concept":"Independent randomness contexts","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security-sensitive random values be generated with explicit purpose and context?","answer":"Different security objects may have different lifetime, secrecy, uniqueness, and misuse requirements, so treating all random values as interchangeable can create unsafe reuse.","explanation":"A value used as a session token has different semantics from a nonce, identifier, sampling value, or test fixture.","principle":"Randomness purpose is part of the security contract.","rule":"Document the purpose, lifecycle, uniqueness requirements, and security properties of each security-sensitive random value.","objective":"Prevent semantic reuse of randomness mechanisms or outputs across incompatible contexts.","security_implication":"Cross-context reuse can create correlation, prediction, replay, or cryptographic misuse risks.","verification_method":"Inventory security-sensitive random values and verify each has an explicit purpose and lifecycle.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B68-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Randomness and Unpredictability","subtopic":"Testing limitations","concept":"Limits of statistical randomness testing","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can statistical tests not by themselves prove that a random generator is cryptographically secure?","answer":"Statistical tests can identify some distributional defects but generally cannot establish resistance to prediction or compromise of generator state.","explanation":"Cryptographic security depends on construction, entropy assumptions, state protection, seeding, implementation, and threat model rather than output statistics alone.","principle":"Statistical appearance is not equivalent to cryptographic unpredictability.","rule":"Use authoritative cryptographic primitives and implementation guarantees instead of treating statistical output tests as proof of security.","objective":"Prevent false assurance from inadequate randomness validation.","security_implication":"A predictable generator may pass many statistical tests while remaining unsuitable for security use.","verification_method":"Verify the primitive, API, entropy source, and documented security properties.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B69-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Build and Artifact Security","subtopic":"Reproducibility","concept":"Reproducible build security","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is build reproducibility relevant to security lessons?","answer":"Reproducibility makes it easier to establish that the reviewed source and declared dependencies correspond to the produced artifact.","explanation":"When identical inputs cannot reliably produce equivalent outputs, unexpected artifact changes are harder to distinguish from legitimate build variation.","principle":"Artifact provenance is stronger when build inputs and outputs are reproducible or independently verifiable.","rule":"Define which build properties must be reproducible and verify them within the supported build environment.","objective":"Improve confidence in the relationship between reviewed source and released artifacts.","security_implication":"Unexplained build variation can make unauthorized artifact modification harder to detect.","tradeoff":"Perfect reproducibility may be difficult across environments and must be scoped to realistic build guarantees.","verification_method":"Perform controlled repeated builds and compare documented reproducibility properties.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B69-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Build and Artifact Security","subtopic":"Build-time versus runtime dependencies","concept":"Build environment trust","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should build tools and build-time dependencies be included in supply-chain security analysis?","answer":"A build component can modify the resulting artifact even if it is not present in the deployed runtime.","explanation":"Security review focused only on runtime dependencies can miss privileged build-time code that controls compilation, packaging, code generation, signing, or artifact assembly.","principle":"The trusted computing base includes components capable of influencing security-relevant artifacts.","rule":"Inventory and assess security-sensitive build tools, generators, compilers, packaging steps, and signing components.","objective":"Prevent hidden build-stage trust dependencies.","security_implication":"Compromised build tooling can produce malicious artifacts without appearing as a runtime dependency.","verification_method":"Map the complete build pipeline and identify components capable of modifying release artifacts.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B69-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Build and Artifact Security","subtopic":"Artifact provenance","concept":"Source-to-artifact traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What security lesson applies when an artifact cannot be traced back to its intended source revision?","answer":"The artifact's relationship to the reviewed source and dependency set cannot be established reliably.","explanation":"A version label alone may not prove which source, configuration, dependency graph, and build process produced an artifact.","principle":"Release identity should include sufficient provenance to reconstruct or verify its origin.","rule":"Maintain traceable relationships between source revision, dependency inputs, build process, and released artifact.","objective":"Make release provenance independently inspectable.","security_implication":"Weak provenance makes unauthorized or unexpected artifact substitution harder to detect.","verification_method":"Verify that a release can be mapped to its declared source and build inputs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B69-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Build and Artifact Security","subtopic":"Generated code","concept":"Generated source trust","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must generated code be included in security review boundaries?","answer":"Generated code executes as part of the resulting software even though developers may not edit it manually.","explanation":"Generators, templates, schemas, code-generation plugins, and generated artifacts can introduce security-relevant behavior or change between versions.","principle":"Security responsibility follows executable effect, not whether code was manually authored.","rule":"Track generated-code inputs, generator versions, and security review requirements according to their impact on the produced artifact.","objective":"Prevent generated components from becoming an unreviewed security boundary.","security_implication":"A compromised or changed generator can introduce vulnerabilities into otherwise reviewed output.","verification_method":"Compare generator inputs and generated outputs across controlled builds and review security-sensitive generated sections.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B69-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Build and Artifact Security","subtopic":"Signing boundary","concept":"Signing does not establish semantic correctness","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What security lesson applies to a correctly signed but vulnerable artifact?","answer":"A valid signature can establish authenticity or integrity relative to the signing authority, but it does not by itself prove that the artifact is secure or semantically correct.","explanation":"Signing and security assurance answer different questions. A compromised signing process or an intentionally signed vulnerable release can remain authentic according to the signature model.","principle":"Artifact authenticity is distinct from artifact security assurance.","rule":"Do not treat successful signature verification as proof of security correctness.","objective":"Prevent signing status from being misinterpreted as comprehensive security verification.","security_implication":"A trusted signer can unintentionally or maliciously distribute insecure software.","verification_method":"Verify signature authenticity separately from source review, security testing, policy compliance, and artifact provenance.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B70-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Decommissioning Security","subtopic":"Identity revocation","concept":"Revoke authority before decommissioning","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should identities and credentials be addressed before or during component decommissioning?","answer":"Deleting or disabling a component does not necessarily revoke credentials, tokens, service identities, or alternate access paths associated with it.","explanation":"A decommissioned component may leave behind credentials that remain valid elsewhere. Security closure therefore requires authority revocation as an explicit step.","principle":"Decommissioning is incomplete while associated authority remains usable.","rule":"Inventory and revoke applicable identities, credentials, tokens, certificates, and access grants as part of decommissioning.","objective":"Prevent abandoned components from retaining usable authority.","security_implication":"Stale credentials can provide unauthorized access after the original component has been removed.","verification_method":"Verify credential and identity state independently from component deletion status.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B70-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Decommissioning Security","subtopic":"Reachability","concept":"Residual access paths","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is deleting the primary component insufficient evidence that access has been removed?","answer":"Alternate endpoints, replicas, caches, administrative interfaces, automation, or direct infrastructure paths may remain reachable.","explanation":"Decommissioning must evaluate the complete access surface rather than the status of one named component.","principle":"Security removal concerns reachable authority and data, not merely resource existence.","rule":"Identify and verify removal or restriction of all supported access paths during decommissioning.","objective":"Prevent residual infrastructure from preserving unintended access.","failure_mode":"Primary service is marked decommissioned while an alternate path still exposes its resources.","cause":"Decommissioning scope limited to the primary component.","root_cause":"Resource lifecycle was modeled without an access-surface inventory.","solution":"Perform access-path discovery and verify each relevant path is removed, revoked, or intentionally retained under documented controls.","security_implication":"Residual access paths can undermine decommissioning claims.","verification_method":"Test documented endpoints, credentials, service discovery, automation paths, and administrative interfaces.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B70-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Decommissioning Security","subtopic":"Backups and retained artifacts","concept":"Decommissioning does not erase retained copies","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must backups and retained artifacts be included in decommissioning security analysis?","answer":"Retained backups, snapshots, exports, logs, and archives can continue to contain sensitive data or security-relevant credentials after the primary system is removed.","explanation":"The security lifecycle of retained copies may differ from the lifecycle of the active resource. Access, retention, restoration, and deletion policies must therefore be considered separately.","principle":"Primary-resource deletion and retained-copy security are separate lifecycle events.","rule":"Track retained copies and verify that their access and retention controls remain appropriate after decommissioning.","objective":"Prevent decommissioning from creating a false assumption that all associated data has disappeared.","security_implication":"Retained copies may become forgotten security assets with weaker controls.","verification_method":"Inventory documented retention locations and verify their access controls and lifecycle status.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B70-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Decommissioning Security","subtopic":"Verification","concept":"Decommissioning verification evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What distinguishes a decommissioning record from verified decommissioning?","answer":"A status record says the process was marked complete; verified decommissioning requires evidence that the defined security closure conditions were actually satisfied.","explanation":"Administrative state and technical state can diverge. Verification should test the security properties defined by the decommissioning plan.","principle":"Lifecycle status is not proof of security state.","rule":"Define objective closure criteria and collect evidence for each security-relevant condition before declaring decommissioning verified.","objective":"Prevent administrative completion from being confused with technical security verification.","requirements":["Explicit closure criteria.","Evidence for identity revocation.","Evidence for access-path closure.","Evidence for retained-resource handling where applicable."],"security_implication":"Unverified decommissioning can leave hidden authority or exposure behind.","verification_method":"Check each closure criterion independently and retain reproducible evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B70-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Decommissioning Security","subtopic":"Ownership transfer","concept":"Security ownership during retirement","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must security ownership remain explicit during decommissioning?","answer":"A component can become security-orphaned when the original owner stops operating it before all credentials, data, dependencies, and residual risks are closed.","explanation":"Responsibility should remain assigned until security closure criteria are satisfied. Otherwise unresolved residual access and retained assets can fall between organizational boundaries.","principle":"Security responsibility must persist through the final lifecycle state.","rule":"Assign an accountable owner for every decommissioning step and retain ownership until closure verification is complete.","objective":"Prevent security gaps caused by ownership termination before technical closure.","failure_mode":"The original team considers a system retired while another team assumes it has already been secured or removed.","cause":"Ownership ended before security closure was verified.","root_cause":"Lifecycle governance treated retirement as an administrative event rather than a security transition.","solution":"Use explicit ownership transfer or closure responsibility with defined acceptance criteria.","security_implication":"Unowned assets and credentials can remain exposed without active monitoring or remediation.","verification_method":"Verify ownership assignment, closure criteria, and final acceptance evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Time Security","subtopic":"Clock trust","concept":"System time as security input","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should system time be treated as security-sensitive input?","answer":"Security decisions such as token expiry, certificate validity, replay windows, scheduling, and authorization may depend on a clock that can be inaccurate, adjusted, or unavailable.","explanation":"Wall-clock time is an environmental input rather than an inherently trusted fact. Security logic must define acceptable clock uncertainty and failure behavior.","principle":"Time-dependent security decisions require explicit clock assumptions.","rule":"Document which clock is authoritative, its acceptable skew, and the behavior when time cannot be trusted.","objective":"Prevent incorrect security decisions caused by clock assumptions.","failure_mode":"A clock adjustment causes an expired credential to become accepted or a valid credential to be rejected.","cause":"Security decision depends on an unbounded or poorly understood clock assumption.","root_cause":"Time was treated as universally authoritative without defining synchronization and failure semantics.","solution":"Use appropriate trusted time sources, bounded skew, monotonic timing for elapsed-duration logic, and explicit failure policies.","security_implication":"Incorrect time can enable replay, extend authorization, invalidate signatures, or disrupt security controls.","testing_guidance":"Test clock skew, backward jumps, forward jumps, unavailable time, and boundary timestamps.","verification_method":"Review time dependencies and execute controlled clock-manipulation tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Time Security","subtopic":"Monotonic clocks","concept":"Elapsed-time measurement","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should elapsed-duration logic generally use a monotonic clock rather than wall-clock time?","answer":"A monotonic clock is designed to measure elapsed duration without being affected by ordinary wall-clock corrections.","explanation":"Wall-clock time can move forward or backward because of synchronization, manual adjustment, virtualization, or other environmental changes. Duration checks can therefore become incorrect if they depend on wall-clock timestamps.","principle":"Use a clock appropriate to the temporal property being measured.","rule":"Use monotonic timing for timeouts, deadlines, rate windows, and elapsed-duration calculations when the platform provides it.","objective":"Prevent clock adjustments from invalidating duration-based security controls.","security_implication":"Incorrect elapsed-time calculations can weaken lockouts, replay windows, expiration handling, or timeout protections.","test_case":"Adjust wall-clock time during an active timeout and verify that elapsed-duration behavior remains correct.","expected_test_result":"The timeout follows the monotonic elapsed-time contract.","verification_method":"Inspect clock selection for every security-sensitive duration calculation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Time Security","subtopic":"Expiry boundaries","concept":"Expiration boundary consistency","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must expiration boundaries be explicitly defined?","answer":"Ambiguous inclusive or exclusive time comparisons can create inconsistent acceptance of credentials, tokens, sessions, or certificates at the exact expiration boundary.","explanation":"Different components may interpret the same timestamp differently, especially when precision or timezone conversion differs.","principle":"Expiration semantics must be precise and consistent across producers and consumers.","rule":"Define whether expiration is inclusive or exclusive and normalize the time representation before comparison.","objective":"Prevent boundary inconsistencies from creating authorization gaps.","failure_mode":"One component considers a credential expired while another still accepts it.","cause":"Different comparison or timestamp semantics.","root_cause":"Expiration contract was underspecified.","solution":"Define precise expiration semantics and test exact boundary values.","security_implication":"Boundary inconsistency can create temporary unauthorized access or denial of legitimate access.","testing_guidance":"Test timestamps immediately before, exactly at, and immediately after expiration.","verification_method":"Compare producer and consumer expiration rules and execute boundary tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B71-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Time Security","subtopic":"Replay windows","concept":"Time-based replay protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security lesson applies when timestamps are used to prevent replay?","answer":"A timestamp alone does not prevent replay unless the accepted time window, uniqueness, context, and replay-detection semantics are explicitly defined.","explanation":"An attacker may reuse a valid message multiple times while it remains inside an accepted time window unless another mechanism detects duplication.","principle":"Freshness requires more than temporal validity when repeated use is dangerous.","rule":"Combine timestamp validation with appropriate nonce, sequence, state, or replay-detection mechanisms when the threat model requires uniqueness.","objective":"Prevent valid messages from being maliciously reused.","security_implication":"Timestamp-only controls can permit repeated execution during the accepted window.","test_case":"Replay the same valid message multiple times within the permitted timestamp window.","expected_test_result":"Behavior matches the documented replay policy and prevents unauthorized repeated execution where required.","verification_method":"Review freshness semantics and execute replay tests across the complete validity window.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B71-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B71","topic":"Time Security","subtopic":"Time synchronization failure","concept":"Security behavior under clock desynchronization","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should clock synchronization failure be modeled explicitly?","answer":"A security system may otherwise silently enter an inconsistent state where different components disagree about validity, freshness, or authorization time.","explanation":"Distributed systems can experience clock drift or temporary synchronization failures. Security controls should define whether to reject, degrade, or continue under bounded uncertainty.","principle":"Loss of time confidence is a security state, not merely an operational inconvenience.","rule":"Define maximum tolerated clock uncertainty and the resulting security behavior.","objective":"Prevent clock disagreement from silently weakening security controls.","security_implication":"Unsynchronized clocks can cause inconsistent token validation, replay protection, certificate checks, and policy expiration.","verification_method":"Simulate clock divergence across participating components and verify the documented behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Unicode and Text Security","subtopic":"Normalization","concept":"Unicode normalization before security comparison","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can Unicode normalization matter to security decisions?","answer":"Visually or semantically equivalent text can have different underlying representations, causing validation, comparison, or authorization logic to disagree.","explanation":"Security-sensitive identifiers, filenames, usernames, domains, and policy values can be affected when components normalize text differently.","principle":"Security comparisons require a defined representation and normalization policy.","rule":"Define and consistently apply the required Unicode normalization and comparison semantics before security-sensitive decisions.","objective":"Prevent representation differences from creating security-policy inconsistencies.","failure_mode":"A validator accepts one representation while another component interprets it as a different identifier.","cause":"Inconsistent Unicode normalization or comparison.","root_cause":"Text identity semantics were not defined across the processing pipeline.","solution":"Establish a canonical representation where appropriate and use consistent comparison rules.","security_implication":"Unicode differences can contribute to identifier confusion, authorization bypasses, or security-policy mismatches.","testing_guidance":"Test canonically equivalent and visually similar representations through every security boundary.","verification_method":"Trace normalization from input through authorization and storage.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Unicode and Text Security","subtopic":"Confusable characters","concept":"Visual identifier confusion","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should security-sensitive interfaces account for visually confusable characters?","answer":"Different identifiers can appear visually similar while representing different underlying values.","explanation":"Human review can fail when identifiers contain confusable Unicode characters. Security-sensitive workflows should reduce reliance on visual inspection alone.","principle":"Human-readable similarity is not equivalent to identifier equality.","rule":"Use canonical identifiers, explicit identity metadata, or controlled character sets where visual confusion would create security risk.","objective":"Reduce human and system confusion between distinct identifiers.","security_implication":"Confusable identifiers can support impersonation, deceptive naming, or operator mistakes.","testing_guidance":"Include representative confusable-character cases in review and UI security testing where relevant.","verification_method":"Verify identifier comparison semantics independently of rendered appearance.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Unicode and Text Security","subtopic":"Case handling","concept":"Case-folding security semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should case-insensitive security comparisons use explicit semantics?","answer":"Case conversion is language and representation dependent, and naive transformations can cause two components to disagree about identifier equality.","explanation":"Security-sensitive identifiers should not rely on arbitrary locale-sensitive transformations or inconsistent case handling.","principle":"Case-insensitive identity requires a defined comparison contract.","rule":"Use an explicit, documented case comparison strategy appropriate to the identifier domain.","objective":"Prevent case-handling differences from creating security inconsistencies.","security_implication":"Inconsistent case handling can cause duplicate identities, authorization mismatches, or policy bypasses.","verification_method":"Test mixed-case and locale-sensitive inputs across validation, storage, lookup, and authorization layers.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B72-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B72","topic":"Unicode and Text Security","subtopic":"Length semantics","concept":"Unicode length versus byte length","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can character count and byte count produce different security outcomes?","answer":"A string's number of characters is not necessarily equal to its encoded byte length, so validating one measure does not automatically bound the other.","explanation":"Resource limits, protocol fields, database columns, and buffer boundaries may operate on different representations.","principle":"Security limits must be expressed in the representation actually consumed by the constrained operation.","rule":"Define whether limits apply to characters, code points, grapheme units, encoded bytes, or another representation and validate accordingly.","objective":"Prevent size checks from becoming ineffective after encoding.","security_implication":"Mismatched size semantics can cause truncation, allocation errors, protocol confusion, or denial of service.","testing_guidance":"Test multilingual and multi-byte inputs at all documented length boundaries.","verification_method":"Trace validation through encoding and downstream storage or transmission.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"ILLUSTRATIVE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

