{"record_id":"D57-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Audit Record Security","subtopic":"Tamper evidence","concept":"Integrity of security audit records","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must security audit records have integrity protections appropriate to their threat model?","answer":"An audit record cannot reliably support investigation or accountability if unauthorized actors can silently modify or delete it.","explanation":"Audit evidence has security value only when its provenance, ordering, retention, and integrity are sufficiently trustworthy for the claims made from it.","principle":"Evidence quality includes protection against unauthorized alteration.","rule":"Define and enforce integrity, access, retention, and provenance controls for security-relevant audit records.","objective":"Preserve the evidentiary value of security logs.","security_implication":"Tampered audit records can conceal actions, fabricate chronology, or undermine incident reconstruction.","verification_method":"Test access controls, append semantics, integrity mechanisms, retention behavior, and administrative paths.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B73-R02","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Audit Record Security","subtopic":"Clock and ordering","concept":"Event ordering evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should audit analysis avoid assuming that local timestamps alone establish event order?","answer":"Distributed systems can produce clock skew, delayed delivery, batching, retries, and asynchronous processing that make timestamps alone insufficient for exact causal ordering.","explanation":"Reliable reconstruction may require sequence identifiers, causal relationships, ingestion metadata, or other provenance depending on the system.","principle":"Observed timestamp order and causal order are different properties.","rule":"Preserve enough event metadata to distinguish occurrence time, observation time, and processing time when those distinctions matter.","objective":"Prevent incorrect security conclusions from misleading event chronology.","security_implication":"Incorrect ordering can lead to false root-cause analysis or missed attack sequences.","verification_method":"Compare event-generation and ingestion timelines under delayed and reordered delivery.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B73-R03","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Audit Record Security","subtopic":"Sensitive content","concept":"Audit logging data minimization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security logs avoid recording unnecessary secrets and sensitive payloads?","answer":"Audit records are additional copies of information and can become a secondary exposure surface.","explanation":"Logging sensitive data can expand retention, access, backup, and disclosure risks without improving the security signal.","principle":"Security observability should maximize useful evidence while minimizing unnecessary sensitive content.","rule":"Log security-relevant metadata and outcomes without unnecessarily copying secrets or sensitive payloads.","objective":"Preserve investigative value while reducing log-induced exposure.","security_implication":"Logs containing credentials, tokens, or sensitive data can become high-value attack targets.","testing_guidance":"Inspect logs generated by authentication, authorization, error, and debug paths for accidental secret exposure.","verification_method":"Perform structured log-content review and secret-pattern testing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

{"record_id":"D57-B73-R04","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B73","topic":"Audit Record Security","subtopic":"Deletion and retention","concept":"Audit retention as security evidence lifecycle","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should audit retention periods be explicitly justified?","answer":"Keeping records too briefly can destroy needed evidence, while keeping them unnecessarily long expands exposure and compliance risk.","explanation":"Retention is both an investigative and security-control decision. It should correspond to the system's threat model, legal requirements, operational purpose, and access controls.","principle":"Audit retention should be purpose-bound rather than indefinite by default.","rule":"Define retention requirements and verify that records are protected throughout their lifecycle.","objective":"Balance evidentiary availability against unnecessary data exposure.","tradeoff":"Longer retention improves historical visibility but increases storage, access, and compromise exposure.","verification_method":"Review retention policy, deletion behavior, access controls, and backup lifecycle together.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

