{"record_id":"D57-B78-R05","dataset_id":"D57","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Security Boundary Parsing","subtopic":"Canonical representation","concept":"Single canonical security representation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is canonical representation useful as a security control?","answer":"It is useful when multiple equivalent representations can otherwise produce inconsistent security decisions.","explanation":"Canonicalization should be defined narrowly and deterministically because incorrect normalization can change semantics or create new ambiguity.","principle":"A security policy should operate on one well-defined representation where representation equivalence matters.","rule":"Canonicalize exactly the transformations required by the security model and validate the resulting representation before use.","objective":"Reduce semantic ambiguity without introducing unsafe transformations.","tradeoff":"Canonicalization adds processing and specification complexity and can itself become a security-sensitive parser.","verification_method":"Define canonicalization rules and test equivalence classes, malformed inputs, and parser consistency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED","provenance":[]}

