":"B76","topic":"Logging and Diagnostics","subtopic":"Sampling","concept":"Diagnostic sampling boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Where should diagnostic sampling be applied?","answer":"Sampling should be applied at a boundary where the system can preserve the diagnostic information required for the operational objective while controlling volume.","explanation":"Sampling too early can remove information needed for aggregation or diagnosis; sampling too late may already incur excessive processing and storage costs. The correct boundary depends on the diagnostic signal and its consumers.","principle":"Sampling should preserve the information required by the diagnostic purpose while controlling cost.","rule":"Define sampling policy based on signal semantics, downstream analysis, and resource constraints.","objective":"Reduce diagnostic volume without destroying required observability information.","tradeoff":"More aggressive sampling reduces cost but can reduce visibility into rare events.","test_case":"Compare unsampled and sampled diagnostic streams for representative workloads and rare-event scenarios.","testing_guidance":"Verify that sampling preserves required fields and does not systematically remove important event classes.","verification_method":"Trace where sampling occurs and evaluate its effect on the intended diagnostic objective.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B76-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B76","topic":"Logging and Diagnostics","subtopic":"Correlation","concept":"Diagnostic correlation fields","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should related diagnostic events carry correlation information?","answer":"Correlation identifiers allow separate events belonging to the same operation, request, workflow, or causal context to be associated without relying solely on timestamps or message text.","explanation":"Distributed or asynchronous execution can produce interleaved events. Explicit correlation fields preserve relationships that would otherwise be difficult or ambiguous to reconstruct.","principle":"Relationships between diagnostic events should be represented explicitly when reconstruction matters.","rule":"Propagate stable correlation context across components participating in the same traceable operation.","objective":"Enable reliable reconstruction of related execution events.","testing_guidance":"Execute a multi-stage operation and verify that its diagnostic events can be grouped using correlation data.","verification_method":"Trace correlation fields across the complete operation boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B77-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Data Integrity","subtopic":"Checksums and Hashes","concept":"Checksum versus cryptographic hash purpose","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why must checksum and cryptographic hash purposes be distinguished?","answer":"Checksums and cryptographic hashes serve different integrity and security objectives; selecting one requires understanding whether accidental corruption detection or stronger adversarial-resistance properties are required.","explanation":"A lightweight checksum may be suitable for detecting common transmission or storage errors, while a cryptographic hash provides stronger properties for many integrity and identification uses. Neither should be treated as interchangeable solely because both produce derived values.","principle":"Integrity mechanisms must be selected according to the threat and correctness model.","rule":"Do not claim security properties for an integrity mechanism that it does not provide.","objective":"Prevent incorrect integrity assumptions.","security_implication":"Using a mechanism without the required security properties can leave integrity claims unsupported.","testing_guidance":"Verify that the selected mechanism satisfies the documented integrity requirement rather than merely producing repeatable output.","verification_method":"Compare the mechanism's documented properties with the required threat and integrity model.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B77-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Data Integrity","subtopic":"Uniqueness","concept":"Uniqueness constraint","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should uniqueness requirements be enforced at the data integrity boundary?","answer":"A uniqueness constraint prevents multiple records from representing the same value when uniqueness is part of the data model.","explanation":"Application-level prechecks can race when multiple writers operate concurrently. Where supported, enforcing uniqueness at the authoritative persistence boundary provides a stronger integrity guarantee.","principle":"Data invariants should be enforced at the boundary capable of atomically protecting them.","rule":"Enforce uniqueness where the authoritative state is committed, not only through prior application checks.","objective":"Prevent duplicate authoritative state when uniqueness is required.","failure_mode":"Two concurrent operations both pass a precheck and create conflicting records.","cause":"Uniqueness was checked outside the authoritative atomic boundary.","solution":"Use an authoritative uniqueness constraint and handle conflicts explicitly.","test_case":"Attempt concurrent creation of records with the same unique value.","testing_guidance":"Verify that at most one operation can establish the authoritative unique value according to the data contract.","verification_method":"Inspect persistence constraints and exercise concurrent conflict cases.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B77-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Data Integrity","subtopic":"References","concept":"Referential integrity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is the purpose of referential integrity?","answer":"Referential integrity ensures that references between related data objects satisfy the relationships defined by the data model.","explanation":"A reference to a missing or invalid target can create orphaned or semantically inconsistent state. The enforcement mechanism should match lifecycle requirements for creation, update, and deletion.","principle":"Relationships represented as references require explicit integrity rules.","rule":"Define what makes a reference valid and enforce that relationship at the authoritative boundary where practical.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent relationships between related data.","edge_case":"The referenced object is intentionally unavailable during a staged or eventual-consistency workflow.","testing_guidance":"Test creation, update, deletion, and failure paths for both valid and invalid references.","verification_method":"Inspect the data model and verify that reference constraints match the intended lifecycle semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B77-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Data Integrity","subtopic":"Versioning","concept":"Monotonic version or sequence semantics","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When are monotonic versions useful for data integrity?","answer":"Monotonic versions provide an ordering signal that can help detect stale updates, regressions, or unexpected state movement when the data model requires ordered progression.","explanation":"A version value is useful only if its ordering semantics are defined. It should not automatically be interpreted as a timestamp, globally unique identifier, or proof that an update succeeded.","principle":"A version field must have an explicit ordering contract and limited interpretation.","rule":"Define whether versions are per-object, global, contiguous, or merely monotonic before using them for integrity checks.","objective":"Detect invalid state progression without overclaiming what a version represents.","edge_case":"A valid concurrent system may allow skipped version numbers while preserving monotonic progression.","testing_guidance":"Test stale writes, repeated writes, concurrent updates, and version gaps according to the declared contract.","verification_method":"Inspect version-generation and update rules against the documented ordering semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B77-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B77","topic":"Data Integrity","subtopic":"Durable Ordering","concept":"Durable intent before dependent state transition","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can durable intent need to precede a dependent state transition?","answer":"When recovery requires knowledge of an intended operation, recording durable intent before the dependent state change can provide a basis for recovery or reconciliation after interruption.","explanation":"This is a general durability pattern rather than a requirement for every system. The exact mechanism may be a journal, log, transaction record, or other durable structure appropriate to the architecture.","principle":"Recovery depends on preserving enough durable information to reconstruct or reconcile incomplete transitions.","rule":"When recovery requires operation intent, persist the required intent before making dependent state changes that could otherwise become ambiguous.","objective":"Improve recoverability after interruption between related state transitions.","constraints":["Durability guarantees depend on the actual storage mechanism and commit semantics.","Recording intent does not by itself guarantee successful completion."],"failure_mode":"A process stops after a state change but before the system can determine what operation was intended or whether completion occurred.","cause":"Required recovery information was not durably recorded.","solution":"Define and persist the minimum recovery information required by the state transition contract.","testing_guidance":"Interrupt execution at transition boundaries and verify that recovery can distinguish completed, incomplete, and unknown states.","verification_method":"Inspect the recovery model and trace durable records across each critical transition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B78-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Scheduling and Time Patterns","subtopic":"Clock Semantics","concept":"Monotonic versus wall-clock time","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should monotonic time be used instead of wall-clock time?","answer":"Monotonic time should be used for measuring elapsed durations because wall-clock time can change due to clock adjustments, synchronization, or administrative changes.","explanation":"Wall-clock time is appropriate for calendar timestamps and human-facing dates. Elapsed-time calculations require a clock whose progression is suitable for duration measurement.","principle":"Use a time source whose semantics match the operation being performed.","rule":"Use monotonic time for elapsed-duration and timeout calculations when the platform provides it.","objective":"Prevent clock adjustments from corrupting duration measurements.","edge_case":"A system needs both an elapsed deadline and a human-readable event timestamp; these are separate requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test duration logic under simulated or controlled clock changes where the environment permits.","verification_method":"Inspect time-source selection for every duration and timestamp calculation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B78-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Scheduling and Time Patterns","subtopic":"Deadlines","concept":"Deadline propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should an operation propagate a deadline instead of independently creating unlimited downstream timeouts?","answer":"A propagated deadline preserves the original end-to-end time budget and prevents downstream operations from extending execution beyond the caller's remaining allowance.","explanation":"Independent timeouts can accumulate and cause the overall operation to exceed its intended latency or cancellation boundary. A deadline represents the remaining absolute time budget for the operation context.","principle":"Time budgets should follow the operation context across dependent work.","rule":"Propagate a shared deadline or equivalent remaining-time budget to downstream operations when end-to-end timing matters.","objective":"Keep composed operations within their declared temporal boundary.","failure_mode":"Downstream stages continue running after the overall operation should have expired.","cause":"Each stage used an independent timeout without regard to remaining budget.","solution":"Pass the operation deadline through the call chain and derive local timeouts from it.","testing_guidance":"Test near-expired deadlines and verify downstream work does not extend beyond the parent operation's contract.","verification_method":"Trace deadline propagation through all relevant call boundaries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B78-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Scheduling and Time Patterns","subtopic":"Clock Skew","concept":"Clock skew tolerance","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should distributed systems define clock-skew tolerance when comparing timestamps?","answer":"Different machines or services may observe slightly different wall-clock times, so timestamp comparisons can become incorrect if the system assumes perfect clock agreement.","explanation":"The required tolerance depends on the environment and the semantic purpose of the comparison. Critical ordering should not rely on wall-clock timestamps alone when clock synchronization uncertainty is material.","principle":"Timestamp comparisons require an explicit assumption about clock accuracy and synchronization.","rule":"Define clock-skew assumptions for distributed timestamp comparisons and avoid treating timestamps as perfect global ordering unless the system provides that guarantee.","objective":"Prevent incorrect temporal conclusions caused by clock differences.","edge_case":"Events generated on different hosts have timestamps that overlap or appear out of order despite valid execution ordering.","testing_guidance":"Test comparisons around the documented skew boundary.","verification_method":"Inspect distributed time assumptions and identify where timestamp ordering is relied upon.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B78-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Scheduling and Time Patterns","subtopic":"Intervals","concept":"Explicit interval semantics","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should time intervals define endpoint inclusion explicitly?","answer":"An interval such as start-inclusive/end-exclusive avoids ambiguity about whether boundary timestamps belong to one interval, another interval, or both.","explanation":"Scheduling, pagination, retention, and event-window logic can produce boundary duplication or omission when interval semantics are implicit. The contract should specify endpoint behavior.","principle":"Temporal boundaries are part of the data contract.","rule":"Define interval inclusion and exclusion semantics explicitly, preferably consistently across related APIs.","objective":"Prevent gaps and overlaps caused by ambiguous time boundaries.","edge_case":"Two adjacent intervals use different endpoint conventions and both claim ownership of the shared boundary.","testing_guidance":"Test values exactly at the start, end, and immediately around each boundary.","verification_method":"Inspect interval definitions and verify boundary tests match the declared convention.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B78-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B78","topic":"Scheduling and Time Patterns","subtopic":"Time Zones","concept":"Time-zone normalization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should systems normalize time-zone handling at defined boundaries?","answer":"Explicit time-zone normalization prevents different components from interpreting the same calendar value under incompatible local-time assumptions.","explanation":"Calendar dates and human-facing schedules have time-zone semantics, while many internal operations can use a canonical time representation. The boundary should preserve enough information to reconstruct the intended local meaning.","principle":"Time-zone semantics must be explicit rather than inferred from process or machine locale.","rule":"Define the canonical representation and explicitly preserve the time-zone information required by the business or technical contract.","objective":"Prevent time-zone ambiguity and environment-dependent behavior.","edge_case":"A local time occurs during a daylight-saving transition where a local clock time may be ambiguous or nonexistent.","testing_guidance":"Test multiple time zones and daylight-saving boundary cases when applicable.","verification_method":"Inspect parsing, storage, conversion, and display boundaries for explicit time-zone semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B79-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Filesystem Patterns","subtopic":"Atomic Replacement","concept":"Atomic file replacement","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can atomic replacement be preferable to modifying a published file in place?","answer":"Atomic replacement can prevent readers from observing a partially written file when the filesystem and implementation provide the required atomicity semantics.","explanation":"A common pattern is to write a complete new file and then replace the published path in one filesystem operation with the required atomicity guarantees. Durability and crash consistency are separate concerns.","principle":"Publication of a complete artifact should be separated from construction when partial visibility is unacceptable.","rule":"Use an atomic publication mechanism when readers must never observe partially written content and the filesystem contract supports it.","constraints":["Atomicity guarantees vary by filesystem and operation.","Atomic replacement does not automatically guarantee durable persistence after power loss."],"failure_mode":"A reader observes truncated or partially updated content.","cause":"The published file was modified in place while readers could access it.","solution":"Construct the new artifact separately and atomically publish it when supported.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent readers during replacement and separately test crash or durability requirements.","verification_method":"Verify the filesystem operation's documented atomicity and the publication sequence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B79-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Filesystem Patterns","subtopic":"Path Safety","concept":"Path normalization and traversal safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must filesystem paths be validated against the intended root or resource boundary?","answer":"A path can syntactically appear valid while resolving outside the intended directory through traversal, symbolic links, or other filesystem semantics.","explanation":"Security-sensitive file operations should establish the intended path boundary using appropriate canonicalization and filesystem-aware checks. Simple string prefix checks are not sufficient to prove containment.","principle":"Filesystem authorization depends on resolved resource identity, not merely path text.","rule":"Validate that a resolved target remains within the authorized filesystem boundary before performing sensitive operations.","objective":"Prevent unintended access outside the authorized filesystem scope.","security_implication":"Incorrect path validation can expose files or allow modifications outside the intended resource boundary.","test_case":"Test normal paths, parent traversal representations, symbolic-link cases where applicable, and boundary-adjacent paths.","testing_guidance":"Use filesystem-aware containment checks appropriate to the platform and threat model.","verification_method":"Trace path resolution and compare the resolved target with the authorized root.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B79-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Filesystem Patterns","subtopic":"Temporary Files","concept":"Temporary-file lifecycle","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should temporary files have an explicit lifecycle?","answer":"Temporary files can consume storage and may expose intermediate data if they are not created, protected, and removed according to a defined lifecycle.","explanation":"A temporary file should have clear ownership, naming, permissions, cleanup behavior, and failure handling. Cleanup should account for exceptions and process interruption where the platform permits recovery.","principle":"Temporary storage is still a resource and must have explicit lifecycle management.","rule":"Define creation, ownership, protection, cleanup, and failure semantics for temporary files.","objective":"Prevent storage leaks and unintended persistence of intermediate data.","security_implication":"Temporary files may contain sensitive intermediate data and therefore require appropriate access controls and cleanup.","testing_guidance":"Test normal completion, exceptions, cancellation, and interrupted process conditions where recoverable.","verification_method":"Trace the temporary-file lifecycle from creation through final cleanup or documented retention.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B79-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Filesystem Patterns","subtopic":"Durability","concept":"Filesystem durability semantics","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should filesystem durability be distinguished from successful write completion?","answer":"A write operation completing successfully does not necessarily mean the data is durable against every class of system or hardware failure.","explanation":"Application-visible write completion, filesystem persistence, storage-device behavior, and crash recovery can provide different guarantees. Systems requiring durability must define the required failure model and use mechanisms that provide the corresponding guarantee.","principle":"Successful API completion and durable persistence are distinct claims.","rule":"Do not equate a successful write call with durability unless the relevant storage contract establishes that guarantee.","objective":"Prevent false durability assumptions in persistent data handling.","failure_mode":"Data believed to be durable is lost after a crash or power failure.","cause":"The implementation relied on write completion without establishing the required durability semantics.","solution":"Define the durability requirement and use the platform's documented mechanisms appropriate to that requirement.","testing_guidance":"Where feasible, test recovery under the relevant failure model rather than inferring durability from successful writes.","verification_method":"Inspect the storage contract and persistence sequence for explicit durability guarantees.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B79-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B79","topic":"Filesystem Patterns","subtopic":"File Locking","concept":"File-lock limitation awareness","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should file locking be treated as a platform-dependent coordination mechanism rather than a universal correctness guarantee?","answer":"Lock behavior, scope, failure semantics, and enforcement can vary across operating systems, filesystems, processes, and access methods.","explanation":"A file lock can coordinate cooperating participants under a defined environment, but it does not automatically protect against every actor or filesystem behavior. Correctness depends on the documented lock model and all relevant writers respecting it.","principle":"A coordination mechanism is only as strong as its scope and enforcement model.","rule":"Document the lock semantics and ensure all relevant participants use a compatible coordination mechanism.","constraints":["Lock semantics vary by platform and filesystem.","Locks may not protect against actors that ignore or bypass them."],"failure_mode":"Concurrent writers corrupt or overwrite data despite an assumed lock.","cause":"The lock's actual scope or enforcement differed from the assumed model.","solution":"Use a coordination mechanism whose guarantees match the concurrency and deployment model.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent access across the supported operating environments and writer types.","verification_method":"Inspect the platform-specific locking contract and verify that all writers participate in the same protocol.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B80-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Process and Execution Boundaries","subtopic":"Argument Handling","concept":"Process argument separation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should subprocess arguments be passed as structured arguments rather than constructed as shell command text when shell interpretation is unnecessary?","answer":"Structured argument passing keeps argument boundaries explicit and avoids unintended interpretation by a command shell.","explanation":"When a subprocess API accepts an argument sequence directly, values can remain data rather than becoming part of an interpreted command string. Shell execution may be required for specific functionality, but it creates additional parsing semantics that must be intentionally handled.","principle":"Data should remain data at process boundaries unless command interpretation is explicitly required.","rule":"Prefer direct structured argument passing when shell features are not required.","objective":"Reduce ambiguity and unintended interpretation at process invocation boundaries.","security_implication":"Avoiding unnecessary shell interpretation can reduce command-injection risk from untrusted data.","test_case":"Pass arguments containing whitespace and shell metacharacters through the structured interface and verify they remain literal argument values.","testing_guidance":"Test argument boundaries independently rather than only testing simple alphanumeric values.","verification_method":"Inspect the process invocation API and verify whether shell interpretation is enabled or required.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B80-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Process and Execution Boundaries","subtopic":"Exit Status","concept":"Process exit-status semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should process exit status be interpreted according to an explicit contract?","answer":"A process exit status communicates termination information but its numeric meaning is defined by the invoked program and execution environment; treating every nonzero value identically can lose useful semantics.","explanation":"Callers should know whether a status represents success, a recoverable condition, invalid input, interruption, or another documented outcome. If only success/failure matters, the boundary should still explicitly define that simplification.","principle":"Process completion status is part of the external execution contract.","rule":"Interpret exit statuses according to the invoked program's documented contract rather than assuming universal numeric meanings.","objective":"Prevent incorrect process outcome classification.","failure_mode":"The caller retries, ignores, or escalates a process result incorrectly.","cause":"Exit status semantics were assumed rather than defined.","solution":"Document the status mapping and convert it into the application's explicit outcome model.","testing_guidance":"Test documented success and failure statuses, including interruption or boundary conditions when supported.","verification_method":"Compare status interpretation with the invoked program's documented exit contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B80-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Process and Execution Boundaries","subtopic":"Output Streams","concept":"Standard output and error separation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should stdout and stderr remain semantically distinct at a process boundary?","answer":"Separating normal program output from diagnostic or error output allows consumers to process results without confusing them with operational diagnostics.","explanation":"Merging streams can be appropriate for a specific capture requirement, but the distinction should be preserved when downstream logic needs to parse machine-readable output or classify diagnostics independently.","principle":"Process output channels should retain their documented semantics unless an explicit contract intentionally combines them.","rule":"Keep standard output and standard error separate when their meanings differ to the consumer.","objective":"Prevent diagnostic text from corrupting machine-readable output or outcome interpretation.","failure_mode":"A parser receives diagnostic content as if it were successful program output.","cause":"Output streams were merged without accounting for their semantic roles.","solution":"Capture and process the streams independently unless the contract explicitly requires combination.","testing_guidance":"Generate both normal output and diagnostics and verify each reaches the expected consumer path.","verification_method":"Inspect stream configuration and validate downstream parsing against the declared channel semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B80-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Process and Execution Boundaries","subtopic":"Timeout and Termination","concept":"Process termination escalation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why may process termination require an escalation sequence instead of a single termination action?","answer":"A process may ignore, delay, or fail to respond to a requested termination, so the execution contract may require staged handling from graceful termination through stronger enforcement.","explanation":"The exact mechanism depends on the operating system and process model. The important design principle is to define what happens when the initial termination request does not produce an exit within the allowed deadline.","principle":"Termination is an outcome contract, not merely an API call.","rule":"Define timeout behavior, graceful termination, escalation, and final outcome classification for managed processes when required.","objective":"Prevent indefinitely running child processes from violating execution boundaries.","constraints":["Escalation mechanisms are platform-dependent.","Forced termination can prevent cleanup and may leave external state incomplete."],"failure_mode":"A child process remains active after the parent operation's deadline expires.","cause":"The execution boundary relied on a single termination request without handling non-compliance.","solution":"Define a bounded termination sequence and explicitly classify unresolved termination.","testing_guidance":"Test normal completion, timeout, graceful termination failure, and escalation paths.","verification_method":"Trace the process lifecycle from launch through timeout and final termination classification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B80-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B80","topic":"Process and Execution Boundaries","subtopic":"Environment Isolation","concept":"Process environment isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should a spawned process receive only the environment it requires?","answer":"Restricting inherited environment state reduces accidental dependency on unrelated variables and limits exposure of environment-held configuration or secrets.","explanation":"Process environments can influence program behavior through paths, locale, configuration, and credentials. Passing an explicit environment is useful when deterministic behavior or isolation is important.","principle":"Execution behavior should depend only on explicitly permitted environmental inputs.","rule":"Define which environment variables a child process requires and avoid unnecessary inheritance when isolation matters.","objective":"Improve reproducibility and reduce unintended configuration or secret exposure.","security_implication":"Unnecessary inherited environment values can expose sensitive configuration to child processes that do not require it.","performance_implication":"Explicit environment construction can also reduce hidden configuration dependencies that complicate diagnosis.","test_case":"Run the child process with the minimal required environment and verify expected behavior, then test absence of unrelated variables.","testing_guidance":"Test required variables, missing variables, conflicting variables, and environment-sensitive behavior.","verification_method":"Compare the child's actual environment with the documented environment contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}

{"record_id":"D58-B81-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B81","topic":"Dependency Injection and Testability","subtopic":"Dependency Boundaries","concept":"Explicit dependency injection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should externally variable dependencies be injected rather than constructed deep inside business logic?","answer":"Explicit dependency injection makes dependencies visible, replaceable, and controllable at the composition boundary.","explanation":"When business logic constructs databases, clocks, network clients, or filesystem adapters internally, testing and substitution become harder. Injection separates policy from mechanism and exposes required capabilities.","principle":"Components should receive replaceable external capabilities rather than hiding their construction inside core logic.","rule":"Inject dependencies whose implementation, lifecycle, environment, or behavior must vary independently from the component's core logic.","objective":"Improve testability, modularity, and dependency control.","constraints":["Not every simple value requires an injection abstraction.","Injection should not create unnecessary indirection for stable pure logic."],"tradeoff":"Dependency injection can increase configuration complexity and object-wiring overhead.","test_case":"Replace an external dependency with a deterministic test implementation and verify the component's core behavior independently.","testing_guidance":"Test business behavior with controlled dependency implementations and separately test the real adapters.","verification_method":"Inspect component construction and identify externally variable capabilities that are hidden rather than injected.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B81-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B81","topic":"Dependency Injection and Testability","subtopic":"Pure Logic","concept":"Pure core with impure boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why is it useful to keep core decision logic separate from external side effects?","answer":"Separating pure or deterministic decision logic from I/O and other side effects makes reasoning, testing, replay, and verification easier.","explanation":"A core function that transforms explicit inputs into outputs can be tested independently from databases, networks, clocks, and process state. Side effects remain necessary at boundaries but do not need to dominate the core logic.","principle":"Keep deterministic reasoning separate from environmental effects whenever the domain permits.","rule":"Move external effects to explicit boundaries and keep core transformations independent of them where practical.","objective":"Reduce hidden state and improve testability and reproducibility.","constraints":["Some domains inherently require stateful behavior.","The separation must preserve required transactional and consistency semantics."],"test_case":"Run core logic repeatedly with identical inputs and controlled dependencies and compare outputs.","testing_guidance":"Test core logic without real external services, then separately verify boundary adapters.","verification_method":"Trace core functions for hidden I/O, global state, time, randomness, or environment dependencies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B81-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B81","topic":"Dependency Injection and Testability","subtopic":"Dependency Scope","concept":"Dependency lifetime alignment","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should dependency lifetime match the lifetime of the state it owns?","answer":"Aligning dependency lifetime with owned state reduces stale state, premature disposal, unintended sharing, and resource leakage.","explanation":"A dependency that owns request-scoped state should not unintentionally outlive that request, while expensive shared resources may legitimately have a broader lifetime. The correct scope follows ownership and concurrency semantics.","principle":"Dependency lifetime should follow the lifetime and sharing requirements of the state and resources it manages.","rule":"Choose dependency scope according to ownership, concurrency, resource lifetime, and isolation requirements.","objective":"Prevent lifecycle mismatches and accidental state sharing.","failure_mode":"A dependency retains state beyond its intended scope or is destroyed while consumers still require it.","cause":"Dependency lifetime was chosen independently of the state lifecycle.","solution":"Define dependency ownership and scope explicitly at the composition boundary.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated operations, concurrent operations, disposal, and scope transitions.","verification_method":"Map dependency ownership and lifetime against the lifecycle of the data and resources it manages.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B81-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B81","topic":"Dependency Injection and Testability","subtopic":"Test Doubles","concept":"Behavioral test doubles","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What makes a test double useful for dependency-boundary testing?","answer":"A useful test double reproduces the dependency behavior relevant to the component's contract rather than merely returning arbitrary values.","explanation":"A fake or stub that does not model relevant success, failure, timing, or state semantics can produce misleading tests. The double should remain intentionally narrower than the real dependency while preserving the behavior under test.","principle":"Test doubles should model the contractually relevant behavior, not accidentally create a different system.","rule":"Design test doubles around dependency behavior that materially affects the component under test.","objective":"Increase test validity while keeping external dependencies controlled.","failure_mode":"Tests pass against an unrealistic double but fail against the actual dependency.","cause":"The double omitted behavior that was relevant to the component contract.","solution":"Identify and model the dependency behaviors that affect the tested scenario.","testing_guidance":"Include representative success and failure behaviors and periodically compare the double's assumptions with the real dependency contract.","verification_method":"Compare the double's modeled behavior with the documented dependency contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B81-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B81","topic":"Dependency Injection and Testability","subtopic":"Hidden Dependencies","concept":"Ambient dependency detection","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why are hidden ambient dependencies problematic?","answer":"Implicit dependencies on global state, environment variables, current time, process state, or singleton objects make behavior harder to reason about and reproduce.","explanation":"A component can appear to have a small interface while depending on external state that is invisible to callers. Such dependencies complicate testing and can create environment-specific failures.","principle":"Behaviorally relevant dependencies should be visible in the component contract or controlled execution context.","rule":"Identify and explicitly control ambient state that can materially change component behavior.","objective":"Reduce hidden sources of nondeterminism and configuration coupling.","failure_mode":"The same input produces different behavior in different environments without an explicit input difference.","cause":"Behavior depends on uncontrolled ambient state.","solution":"Inject or explicitly capture relevant environmental dependencies.","testing_guidance":"Run tests with controlled environment, time, randomness, and global state where those factors affect behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect code for implicit access to global or process-wide state that affects results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B82-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B82","topic":"Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Compatibility","concept":"Schema evolution compatibility boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should schema evolution define compatibility expectations explicitly?","answer":"Consumers may depend on field names, types, presence, defaults, and semantics, so changing a schema without an explicit compatibility contract can break existing integrations.","explanation":"Compatibility is multidimensional. A change can be syntactically compatible while changing semantics, or syntactically incompatible while being safely migrated through a versioned boundary.","principle":"A schema is an interface whose evolution requires explicit compatibility rules.","rule":"Classify schema changes by their effect on existing producers and consumers before deployment.","objective":"Prevent accidental contract breakage during data-model evolution.","constraints":["Compatibility depends on actual consumer behavior and contract guarantees.","A default value does not automatically make a semantic change compatible."],"testing_guidance":"Test existing representative payloads against the new schema and test consumers against changed fields.","verification_method":"Compare old and new schema contracts and classify each changed field by compatibility impact.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B82-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B82","topic":"Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Defaults","concept":"Explicit default semantics","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should schema defaults be explicitly defined?","answer":"A default determines how missing data is interpreted and therefore changes the meaning of records that do not contain the field.","explanation":"A default can be useful for additive evolution, but it must represent a valid semantic value for historical and newly produced records. Treating absence as equivalent to an arbitrary value can corrupt meaning.","principle":"A schema default is a semantic rule, not merely a convenience for parsing.","rule":"Define a default only when absence has a documented and valid interpretation.","objective":"Prevent missing fields from acquiring unintended meaning.","edge_case":"Historical records may predate the field and require a different interpretation from newly created records.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, explicit-default, null, and valid non-default values separately when the schema distinguishes them.","verification_method":"Inspect the schema contract and confirm the default has an explicit semantic justification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B82-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B82","topic":"Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Field Removal","concept":"Deprecation before schema removal","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why should externally consumed fields usually be deprecated before removal?","answer":"Deprecation provides consumers with a transition period to migrate before the field disappears and prevents an immediate contract break.","explanation":"Removal is safer when consumers can identify the replacement, migrate, and verify compatibility before the old field is removed. The required deprecation period depends on the ecosystem and contract.","principle":"Externally consumed contract elements should have a controlled retirement path.","rule":"Provide a migration path and deprecation period before removing a field that consumers may rely upon.","objective":"Reduce uncontrolled breakage during schema evolution.","failure_mode":"A consumer fails immediately after a producer removes a previously available field.","cause":"The field was removed without an established migration path.","solution":"Deprecate, document replacement semantics, measure or verify consumer migration where possible, then remove according to the contract.","testing_guidance":"Test consumers using both the deprecated and replacement fields during the transition period.","verification_method":"Inspect the deprecation notice, migration path, compatibility tests, and removal criteria.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B82-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B82","topic":"Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Unknown Fields","concept":"Preservation of unknown fields","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can preserving unknown schema fields be useful?","answer":"Preserving unknown fields can allow intermediaries to forward newer data without destroying information they do not yet understand.","explanation":"This pattern is useful for pass-through or forward-compatible systems, but only when the contract permits preservation and the fields cannot introduce unsafe or invalid semantics at the intermediary.","principle":"A component that does not understand a field should not automatically destroy it when the contract requires forward information preservation.","rule":"Preserve unknown fields only when the schema contract explicitly supports safe pass-through semantics.","objective":"Improve forward compatibility without silently changing data meaning.","constraints":["Unknown fields must not be interpreted as trusted or validated merely because they were preserved.","Preservation may increase storage or forwarding complexity."],"testing_guidance":"Send records containing future fields through older components and verify that supported pass-through semantics are maintained.","verification_method":"Inspect the schema contract for unknown-field handling and test round-trip preservation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B82-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B82","topic":"Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Migration","concept":"Data migration idempotence","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should repeatable data migrations be designed to be idempotent when practical?","answer":"Idempotent migrations can be retried after interruption without applying the same transformation repeatedly or producing progressively corrupted state.","explanation":"Migration execution can fail after partial progress. An idempotent design, checkpointed migration, or transactional strategy allows recovery without assuming the migration completed atomically.","principle":"Migration recovery should tolerate interruption without compounding state changes.","rule":"Design migrations so repeated execution either produces the same target state or safely detects already-completed work.","objective":"Improve migration recoverability and operational safety.","failure_mode":"A retried migration applies a transformation twice and corrupts or changes data unexpectedly.","cause":"Migration progress and repeat behavior were not defined.","solution":"Use idempotent transformations, checkpoints, transactional boundaries, or explicit completion markers as appropriate.","testing_guidance":"Interrupt migrations at multiple stages and rerun them from the resulting state.","verification_method":"Execute the migration logic conceptually or in a controlled environment twice against the same intermediate state and verify stable target semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B83-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B83","topic":"Numerical and Precision Patterns","subtopic":"Representation","concept":"Precision-aware representation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should numeric representation be chosen according to required precision semantics?","answer":"Different numeric representations provide different precision, range, rounding, and reproducibility properties, so selecting one solely for convenience can produce incorrect results.","explanation":"Floating-point, fixed-point, decimal, integer, and arbitrary-precision representations have different semantics. The correct choice depends on domain requirements rather than on a universal preference.","principle":"Numeric representation is part of the domain contract.","rule":"Select numeric representation according to required range, precision, rounding, and interoperability semantics.","objective":"Prevent representation-induced correctness errors.","constraints":["Higher precision can increase computational or storage cost.","Representation choice does not remove the need for explicit rounding policy."],"edge_case":"Values near representational boundaries may behave differently from ordinary values.","testing_guidance":"Test boundary values, rounding cases, and values requiring exact representation according to the domain contract.","verification_method":"Compare representation semantics with documented numeric requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B83-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B83","topic":"Numerical and Precision Patterns","subtopic":"Rounding","concept":"Explicit rounding policy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should rounding behavior be explicit?","answer":"Different rounding rules can produce different results, especially when repeated calculations or boundary values are involved.","explanation":"Rounding can occur during storage, display, aggregation, conversion, or intermediate computation. An implicit or inconsistent policy can cause discrepancies between components.","principle":"Rounding is a semantic operation and should be governed by an explicit policy.","rule":"Define when rounding occurs, the precision used, and the rounding mode when exact results matter.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent numeric outcomes across components.","failure_mode":"Two components calculate different results from equivalent values because they round at different stages or use different rules.","cause":"Rounding semantics were implicit.","solution":"Define and centralize the applicable rounding policy.","testing_guidance":"Test half-boundary values, repeated rounding, negative values where relevant, and cross-component calculations.","verification_method":"Trace numeric transformations and identify every point where rounding occurs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B83-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B83","topic":"Numerical and Precision Patterns","subtopic":"Overflow","concept":"Overflow and range handling","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should numeric range limits be explicitly handled?","answer":"Values outside the supported numeric range can overflow, saturate, wrap, raise errors, or lose precision depending on the representation and operation.","explanation":"Assuming that all numeric inputs fit the chosen representation can create silent corruption or runtime failures. The correct behavior must follow the domain and language contract.","principle":"Numeric boundaries are part of input and computation correctness.","rule":"Define behavior for values near and beyond supported numeric limits.","objective":"Prevent silent numerical corruption and undefined application behavior.","failure_mode":"A value exceeds supported range and produces an incorrect result without an explicit failure.","cause":"Range assumptions were implicit or unchecked.","solution":"Validate ranges or use a representation whose semantics satisfy the required domain.","testing_guidance":"Test minimum, maximum, just-inside, and just-outside supported ranges.","verification_method":"Compare domain limits with the representation's documented range and operation semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B83-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B83","topic":"Numerical and Precision Patterns","subtopic":"Comparison","concept":"Tolerance-based numeric comparison","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is tolerance-based comparison appropriate for numeric values?","answer":"Tolerance-based comparison is appropriate when representation or computation introduces bounded differences that the domain considers equivalent.","explanation":"Exact equality may be inappropriate for some approximate numeric calculations, but tolerance must be derived from the domain and error model. Arbitrary tolerances can hide genuine defects.","principle":"Approximate equality requires an explicit error tolerance justified by the computation or domain.","rule":"Use tolerance-based comparison only when approximate equivalence is part of the contract and define the tolerance semantics explicitly.","objective":"Distinguish meaningful numerical differences from expected representation error.","constraints":["Tolerance must not replace exact comparison where exact semantics are required.","Relative and absolute tolerances have different behavior and may both be needed."],"testing_guidance":"Test values inside, exactly at, and outside the declared tolerance.","verification_method":"Inspect the comparison policy and identify its relationship to expected numerical error.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B83-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B83","topic":"Numerical and Precision Patterns","subtopic":"Unit Semantics","concept":"Unit-aware values","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should physical or domain units be explicit in numerical interfaces?","answer":"Explicit units prevent values with identical numeric types but different meanings from being combined incorrectly.","explanation":"A number such as 10 can represent seconds, meters, dollars, or another quantity. Unit ambiguity becomes dangerous when values cross component boundaries or are converted between systems.","principle":"A numeric value is incomplete when its unit is part of its meaning but absent from the contract.","rule":"Represent or explicitly document units at interfaces where unit confusion can affect correctness.","objective":"Prevent dimension and unit mismatch errors.","failure_mode":"Values with different units are combined or converted as though they were equivalent.","cause":"Unit semantics were implicit.","solution":"Use unit-aware types, explicit unit metadata, or strongly documented conversion boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Test incompatible units and conversion boundaries explicitly.","verification_method":"Inspect interfaces for unit declarations and trace every conversion between representations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B84-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B84","topic":"Collections and Iteration","subtopic":"Mutation","concept":"Mutation during iteration","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can mutating a collection during iteration be unsafe?","answer":"Mutation can invalidate iteration assumptions, skip elements, process elements multiple times, or trigger runtime-specific errors depending on the collection semantics.","explanation":"The exact behavior depends on the language and collection type. Safe approaches include iterating over a stable snapshot, using collection-specific mutation operations, or constructing a new collection.","principle":"Iteration semantics must remain valid while the collection changes.","rule":"Do not mutate a collection during iteration unless the collection contract explicitly defines safe mutation behavior.","objective":"Prevent skipped, duplicated, or invalid iteration behavior.","failure_mode":"Elements are unexpectedly omitted or processed more than once.","cause":"The collection was structurally modified while an iterator depended on its prior structure.","solution":"Use an explicitly supported mutation pattern or iterate over a stable representation.","testing_guidance":"Test removal, insertion, and replacement during iteration where relevant to the collection type.","verification_method":"Inspect collection documentation and control flow for structural mutation during iteration.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B84-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B84","topic":"Collections and Iteration","subtopic":"Iterator Consumption","concept":"Single-pass iterator semantics","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should single-pass iterators be distinguished from reusable collections?","answer":"A single-pass iterator may be consumed as it is traversed, so subsequent consumers can observe no remaining elements.","explanation":"Treating an iterator as though it were a reusable collection can cause missing data, especially when multiple functions share or sequentially consume the same iterator.","principle":"Consumption semantics are part of an iterable interface contract.","rule":"Document whether a returned iterable is reusable or single-pass when consumers may depend on repeated traversal.","objective":"Prevent accidental data loss caused by unexpected iterator consumption.","edge_case":"A generator is passed to multiple functions that each assume they can iterate over all elements.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated traversal and multiple consumers explicitly.","verification_method":"Inspect the returned object's iteration and consumption semantics before designing downstream processing.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B84-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B84","topic":"Collections and Iteration","subtopic":"Aliasing","concept":"Shared collection alias control","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should APIs define whether returned collections are owned or shared?","answer":"Consumers may mutate a returned collection, unintentionally changing internal state if the collection is shared rather than copied.","explanation":"An API can return an owned mutable collection, an immutable view, a defensive copy, or another explicitly defined representation. The choice affects performance and encapsulation.","principle":"Collection ownership and mutability are part of the API contract.","rule":"Define whether callers may mutate returned collections and whether mutations affect internal state.","objective":"Prevent unintended state changes through collection aliasing.","tradeoff":"Defensive copying improves isolation but can increase memory and processing cost.","test_case":"Modify a returned collection and verify behavior matches the documented ownership contract.","testing_guidance":"Test both mutation attempts and subsequent reads of the originating component.","verification_method":"Inspect collection-returning APIs for explicit ownership and mutability semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B84-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B84","topic":"Collections and Iteration","subtopic":"Ordering","concept":"Collection ordering contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should collection ordering be documented when consumers can observe it?","answer":"Consumers may accidentally or intentionally depend on iteration order, so unspecified ordering can create unstable behavior when implementations or internal representations change.","explanation":"If ordering is semantically meaningful, it should be part of the contract. If ordering is intentionally unspecified, consumers should not infer meaning from observed implementation behavior.","principle":"Observable ordering should be either explicitly guaranteed or explicitly treated as unspecified.","rule":"Do not let incidental collection ordering become an undocumented API contract.","objective":"Prevent hidden dependencies on implementation-specific ordering.","testing_guidance":"Test expected ordering only when the contract guarantees it; otherwise test order-independent semantics.","verification_method":"Inspect API documentation and implementation behavior for explicit ordering guarantees.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B84-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B84","topic":"Collections and Iteration","subtopic":"Empty Collections","concept":"Empty-input semantics","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should empty collections have explicit behavior?","answer":"Empty input is often a valid boundary case and should produce a defined result rather than relying on incidental behavior or an exception that consumers did not expect.","explanation":"Operations such as aggregation, filtering, mapping, searching, and batching have different mathematically or operationally meaningful results for empty input. The contract must define the intended behavior.","principle":"Empty input is part of the normal domain boundary unless explicitly prohibited.","rule":"Define the result, error, or no-op semantics for empty collections.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent boundary behavior across collection-processing components.","edge_case":"An aggregation has no elements and therefore has no naturally inferred identity value.","testing_guidance":"Include empty input in unit and integration tests whenever collections are accepted.","verification_method":"Inspect the operation's mathematical or business contract for an explicit empty-input case.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B85-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B85","topic":"Protocol and Message Handling","subtopic":"Message Framing","concept":"Explicit message framing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why must message boundaries be explicit in stream-based communication?","answer":"A byte or character stream does not inherently identify where one logical message ends and another begins, so the protocol must define framing semantics.","explanation":"Length prefixes, delimiters, fixed-size frames, or another explicit mechanism can establish boundaries. Without framing, consumers can concatenate or split messages incorrectly.","principle":"Logical message boundaries must be represented by the protocol rather than inferred from arbitrary read operations.","rule":"Define and validate message framing before interpreting stream content as complete messages.","objective":"Prevent partial, concatenated, or ambiguous message interpretation.","failure_mode":"A receiver treats a partial message as complete or combines multiple messages incorrectly.","cause":"Message framing was implicit or tied to transport read boundaries.","solution":"Use an explicit framing contract and maintain incomplete data until a complete frame is available.","testing_guidance":"Test partial reads, multiple messages in one read, and message boundaries at maximum supported sizes.","verification_method":"Inspect the protocol specification and receiver state machine for explicit framing behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B85-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B85","topic":"Protocol and Message Handling","subtopic":"Message Validation","concept":"Validate before semantic processing","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should protocol messages be structurally validated before semantic processing?","answer":"Structural validation establishes that the message can be safely interpreted before business logic relies on its fields or relationships.","explanation":"Processing malformed structures inside deeper business logic mixes protocol concerns with domain logic and can produce confusing failures. Validation should establish the minimum structural contract first.","principle":"Semantic interpretation depends on structural validity.","rule":"Validate required structure, types, sizes, and framing before applying domain-specific processing.","objective":"Contain malformed input at the earliest appropriate boundary.","failure_mode":"Malformed data reaches business logic and causes unrelated errors or incorrect interpretation.","cause":"Structural validation was delayed or omitted.","solution":"Introduce an explicit protocol validation boundary before semantic processing.","testing_guidance":"Test missing fields, wrong types, invalid lengths, and malformed framing.","verification_method":"Trace message flow and confirm structural validation precedes domain interpretation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B85-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B85","topic":"Protocol and Message Handling","subtopic":"Message Identity","concept":"Explicit message identity","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an explicit message identifier useful?","answer":"A message identifier is useful when the system needs to correlate, deduplicate, audit, or reason about an individual message independently of its transport connection.","explanation":"Message identity should have defined uniqueness and lifetime semantics. It should not automatically be treated as proof of delivery or successful processing.","principle":"Identifiers establish reference identity, not completion semantics.","rule":"Define identifier scope, uniqueness, and lifecycle before using message IDs for correlation or deduplication.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous message tracking and incorrect delivery assumptions.","constraints":["Identifier uniqueness may be local, scoped, or global depending on the protocol.","An identifier alone does not prove that processing succeeded."],"testing_guidance":"Test duplicate identifiers, repeated delivery, and identifiers across independent scopes.","verification_method":"Inspect the message contract for identifier uniqueness and lifecycle guarantees.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B85-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B85","topic":"Protocol and Message Handling","subtopic":"Unknown Message Types","concept":"Unknown-message handling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should protocols define behavior for unknown message types?","answer":"As protocols evolve, older consumers may receive message types they do not understand, so the protocol must define whether they reject, ignore, preserve, or safely forward them.","explanation":"Undefined unknown-type behavior can cause crashes, silent data loss, or incompatible upgrades. The appropriate behavior depends on whether messages are optional, mandatory, ordered, or security-sensitive.","principle":"Protocol evolution requires explicit handling of messages outside the current implementation's known type set.","rule":"Define safe behavior for unknown message types and distinguish ignorable from mandatory messages where applicable.","objective":"Improve protocol evolution and failure predictability.","edge_case":"An unknown message contains a required state transition that cannot safely be ignored.","testing_guidance":"Send unknown message types and verify the documented behavior without corrupting protocol state.","verification_method":"Inspect protocol versioning rules and state-machine handling for unknown types.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B85-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B85","topic":"Protocol and Message Handling","subtopic":"Backwards Compatibility","concept":"Compatibility envelope","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a protocol compatibility envelope simplify version evolution?","answer":"An explicit envelope can carry version, message type, metadata, and payload boundaries separately, allowing consumers to reason about compatibility before interpreting payload details.","explanation":"Separating transport-level metadata from payload semantics creates a stable boundary for version negotiation or dispatch. It does not eliminate the need to define compatibility rules for payload schemas.","principle":"Stable protocol metadata can isolate version and dispatch concerns from evolving payload structures.","rule":"Use an explicit compatibility boundary when protocol evolution requires version-aware dispatch or negotiation.","objective":"Make protocol evolution and message dispatch explicit.","constraints":["The envelope itself becomes a compatibility contract and must therefore evolve carefully.","Version information does not automatically establish semantic compatibility."],"testing_guidance":"Test older and newer consumers against supported message versions and unsupported versions.","verification_method":"Inspect protocol layering and verify that version handling occurs before incompatible payload interpretation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B86-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B86","topic":"Search and Indexing Patterns","subtopic":"Search Semantics","concept":"Search semantics contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should search behavior define matching semantics explicitly?","answer":"Users and downstream systems need to know whether matching is exact, prefix-based, token-based, case-sensitive, normalized, ranked, or otherwise transformed.","explanation":"A search interface without explicit semantics can produce results that are technically valid but surprising or inconsistent across versions and implementations.","principle":"Search results are meaningful only relative to a defined matching and ranking contract.","rule":"Document the normalization, matching, filtering, ordering, and ranking semantics that affect search results.","objective":"Make search behavior predictable and testable.","constraints":["Search semantics can differ between indexes and data types.","Ranking behavior may be implementation-dependent unless explicitly contracted."],"testing_guidance":"Test exact matches, partial matches, case variations, normalization cases, and no-match inputs according to the contract.","verification_method":"Compare search implementation and tests with the documented matching semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B86-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B86","topic":"Search and Indexing Patterns","subtopic":"Index Freshness","concept":"Index freshness semantics","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should an index declare its freshness semantics?","answer":"A search index may lag behind authoritative state, so consumers need to know whether results are strongly current, eventually consistent, or potentially stale.","explanation":"Search systems often optimize retrieval separately from authoritative storage. Without freshness semantics, users may incorrectly interpret absence or outdated results as proof about current state.","principle":"Derived indexes require an explicit relationship to authoritative state freshness.","rule":"Define expected index lag and communicate when search results may differ from current authoritative data.","objective":"Prevent incorrect decisions based on stale derived information.","failure_mode":"A consumer interprets a missing search result as proof that the authoritative record does not exist.","cause":"Index freshness semantics were undefined.","solution":"Document freshness guarantees and, when necessary, expose synchronization state or authoritative lookup paths.","testing_guidance":"Test searches during known indexing delays and verify the documented behavior.","verification_method":"Trace update propagation from authoritative state to the index and identify the declared consistency boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B86-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B86","topic":"Search and Indexing Patterns","subtopic":"Pagination","concept":"Stable pagination ordering","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should paginated search results use a stable ordering key?","answer":"Stable ordering prevents records from moving unpredictably between pages when the same query is evaluated repeatedly.","explanation":"Offset-based pagination can produce duplicates or omissions when the underlying dataset changes between requests. A deterministic ordering key, often combined with a cursor, can provide stronger traversal semantics.","principle":"Pagination requires an ordering model that remains meaningful across page boundaries.","rule":"Define deterministic ordering and cursor or snapshot semantics when consistent pagination matters.","objective":"Prevent page duplication and omission caused by unstable ordering.","edge_case":"Records are inserted or removed between page requests.","testing_guidance":"Test pagination while the underlying dataset changes and verify behavior against the declared consistency model.","verification_method":"Inspect ordering keys, cursor semantics, and mutation behavior during multi-page retrieval.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B86-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B86","topic":"Search and Indexing Patterns","subtopic":"Index Rebuild","concept":"Rebuildable derived indexes","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should derived indexes be rebuildable from authoritative data when practical?","answer":"Rebuildability reduces dependence on potentially corrupted or outdated derived state and provides a recovery path after index loss or incompatible index changes.","explanation":"An index should generally be treated as derived state when the authoritative source can reconstruct it. The rebuild process itself must have defined completeness and consistency semantics.","principle":"Derived state is safer when it can be regenerated from authoritative state.","rule":"Design important derived indexes with a documented reconstruction path when architecture permits.","objective":"Improve recoverability and reduce permanent dependence on derived artifacts.","failure_mode":"Index corruption requires manual reconstruction because no authoritative rebuild path exists.","cause":"Derived state became operationally authoritative without a reconstruction mechanism.","solution":"Define deterministic index generation from authoritative data and verify rebuild completeness.","testing_guidance":"Delete or invalidate a test index and rebuild it from authoritative data, then compare supported query semantics.","verification_method":"Trace the index source-of-truth relationship and verify that reconstruction does not depend on the corrupted artifact.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B86-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B86","topic":"Search and Indexing Patterns","subtopic":"Query Limits","concept":"Bounded search queries","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should search queries have explicit result and resource limits?","answer":"Unbounded search can consume excessive CPU, memory, storage, or network resources and produce responses that are difficult for consumers to process.","explanation":"Limits should reflect legitimate workload requirements and may apply to result count, query complexity, execution time, or scanned data. Rejection or continuation behavior should be explicit.","principle":"Search resource consumption should be bounded by an explicit contract.","rule":"Define and enforce appropriate query and result limits where search cost can grow without bound.","objective":"Prevent pathological queries from destabilizing the search component.","performance_implication":"Bounded queries improve predictability under variable workloads.","testing_guidance":"Test minimum, normal, maximum, and over-limit queries and verify documented failure or truncation semantics.","verification_method":"Inspect query controls and compare them with expected workload and resource constraints.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B87-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B87","topic":"Serialization and Encoding","subtopic":"Character Encoding","concept":"Explicit character encoding","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should character encoding be explicit at text boundaries?","answer":"The same byte sequence can represent different text under different encodings, so implicit encoding assumptions can corrupt data or cause decoding failures.","explanation":"Encoding should be part of the interface contract when bytes and text cross boundaries. UTF-8 is widely used, but the correct choice depends on the external protocol and compatibility requirements.","principle":"Byte-to-text interpretation requires an explicit encoding contract.","rule":"Declare and consistently apply character encoding at byte/text boundaries.","objective":"Prevent encoding-dependent corruption and interoperability failures.","edge_case":"A legacy system emits a non-default encoding that cannot be inferred safely from the content.","testing_guidance":"Test representative non-ASCII characters and malformed byte sequences where applicable.","verification_method":"Inspect all byte/text conversion boundaries for explicit encoding selection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B87-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B87","topic":"Serialization and Encoding","subtopic":"Round Trip","concept":"Round-trip preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does round-trip preservation verify in serialization?","answer":"Round-trip testing checks whether serializing and then deserializing supported values preserves the properties that the data contract requires to remain equivalent.","explanation":"Exact byte equality is not always required; semantic equality may be the actual contract. Conversely, some formats require canonical byte representation. The test must match the intended preservation semantics.","principle":"Serialization correctness is defined by the properties the contract requires to survive transformation.","rule":"Test serialize-deserialize round trips against explicit semantic or representational preservation requirements.","objective":"Detect data loss or unintended transformation during serialization.","edge_case":"Multiple serialized representations are semantically equivalent but not byte-identical.","testing_guidance":"Include ordinary values, boundary values, optional fields, special characters, and supported version variants.","verification_method":"Compare round-trip results using the contract's defined equivalence relation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B87-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B87","topic":"Serialization and Encoding","subtopic":"Canonical Forms","concept":"Canonical serialization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is canonical serialization useful?","answer":"Canonical serialization is useful when equivalent logical data must produce one deterministic representation for comparison, hashing, signing, caching, or reproducible artifacts.","explanation":"Canonicalization requires explicit rules for ordering, representation, optional fields, numeric forms, and encoding. It should not be assumed merely because a serializer produces repeatable output in current implementations.","principle":"Deterministic representation requires a defined canonicalization contract.","rule":"Define canonical serialization rules when byte-level determinism has semantic value.","objective":"Make serialized representation reproducible and comparable where required.","constraints":["Canonicalization can restrict implementation choices.","Deterministic output does not by itself prove semantic correctness."],"testing_guidance":"Serialize semantically equivalent inputs and verify they produce the same canonical representation when the contract requires it.","verification_method":"Inspect canonicalization rules and compare outputs across equivalent input representations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B87-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B87","topic":"Serialization and Encoding","subtopic":"Malformed Input","concept":"Malformed serialization rejection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should malformed serialized input be rejected or isolated before semantic processing?","answer":"Malformed input can violate parser assumptions, produce ambiguous interpretations, or consume excessive resources if processed without structural limits.","explanation":"A serialization boundary should establish syntax, size, and structural validity before passing data to deeper application logic. Error handling should preserve enough context for diagnosis without exposing sensitive content.","principle":"Parsing untrusted serialized data requires explicit structural and resource boundaries.","rule":"Validate serialization structure and resource limits before semantic interpretation.","objective":"Contain malformed input and reduce parser-induced failure propagation.","security_implication":"Strict parsing and resource limits can reduce exposure to parser abuse and resource exhaustion.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed syntax, unexpected types, truncated input, oversized structures, and invalid nesting where applicable.","verification_method":"Inspect parser configuration and boundary validation against the serialization contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B87-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B87","topic":"Serialization and Encoding","subtopic":"Schema Mismatch","concept":"Explicit schema mismatch handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should schema mismatches be classified separately from generic parsing failures?","answer":"A message can be syntactically valid while incompatible with the expected schema, requiring different diagnosis and recovery behavior from malformed syntax.","explanation":"Separating syntax errors from schema incompatibility preserves causal information and allows systems to distinguish invalid bytes from valid data produced by a different contract version.","principle":"Failure classification should preserve the distinction between malformed representation and incompatible meaning.","rule":"Classify schema mismatch separately from syntax or transport failure when the distinction affects recovery or diagnosis.","objective":"Improve failure diagnosis and migration handling.","failure_mode":"A valid but incompatible message is reported as generic malformed data.","cause":"Parser and schema validation failures were collapsed into one category.","solution":"Maintain separate structural parsing and schema compatibility validation stages.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed representations and valid representations from incompatible schema versions separately.","verification_method":"Trace the parsing pipeline and verify distinct failure classifications.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B88-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B88","topic":"Resource Scheduling","subtopic":"Fairness","concept":"Work scheduling fairness","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should schedulers define fairness semantics when multiple work sources compete for capacity?","answer":"Without a fairness policy, one workload can consume disproportionate capacity and delay other eligible work indefinitely.","explanation":"Fairness can mean equal shares, weighted shares, bounded waiting, priority ordering, or another domain-specific policy. The correct model depends on requirements and should be explicit.","principle":"Resource allocation among competing workloads requires an explicit fairness policy when starvation or imbalance matters.","rule":"Define scheduling priority and fairness behavior rather than relying on incidental queue order.","objective":"Prevent unintended starvation and make resource allocation predictable.","tradeoff":"Strict fairness can reduce throughput or responsiveness for high-priority work.","edge_case":"A continuously arriving high-priority workload can prevent lower-priority work from executing.","testing_guidance":"Test competing workloads under sustained load and verify behavior against the declared fairness model.","verification_method":"Inspect scheduler policy and analyze waiting behavior for each workload class.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B88-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B88","topic":"Resource Scheduling","subtopic":"Priority","concept":"Priority inversion awareness","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is priority inversion?","answer":"Priority inversion occurs when a higher-priority task is indirectly blocked by lower-priority work holding or controlling a resource it needs, potentially delaying the higher-priority task beyond expectations.","explanation":"The exact manifestation depends on the scheduling and synchronization model. Systems with priority-sensitive workloads should identify shared-resource interactions that can produce unexpected priority blocking.","principle":"Priority ordering does not eliminate blocking caused by shared resources.","rule":"Analyze resource dependencies when priority differences affect latency or scheduling guarantees.","objective":"Prevent hidden resource dependencies from violating priority expectations.","failure_mode":"A high-priority operation waits unexpectedly for lower-priority work.","cause":"A lower-priority holder controlled a resource required by the higher-priority operation.","solution":"Use a synchronization or scheduling strategy appropriate to the documented priority and latency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Exercise competing priority levels under controlled resource contention.","verification_method":"Construct a dependency graph of prioritized tasks and shared resources.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B88-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B88","topic":"Resource Scheduling","subtopic":"Admission Control","concept":"Admission control before overload","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can admission control be preferable to accepting unlimited work?","answer":"Admission control limits new work when available capacity is insufficient, preventing overload from causing uncontrolled queue growth and widespread latency degradation.","explanation":"Rejecting, delaying, or redirecting work can be preferable to accepting all requests and allowing resource exhaustion. The policy must define which work is admitted and what happens to rejected work.","principle":"A system should protect existing work and critical resources when demand exceeds safe capacity.","rule":"Define admission criteria and overload behavior for workloads whose demand can exceed available capacity.","objective":"Keep resource usage within an operationally safe envelope.","tradeoff":"Admission control improves stability but may increase rejected or deferred work.","testing_guidance":"Increase demand beyond capacity and verify that the system follows the documented admission policy.","verification_method":"Inspect capacity thresholds and admission decisions under overload conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B88-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B88","topic":"Resource Scheduling","subtopic":"Queue Aging","concept":"Queue aging visibility","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should queue age be observable when work can wait for an unpredictable period?","answer":"Queue age reveals waiting time that execution-time metrics alone cannot show and can expose starvation, overload, or scheduling imbalance.","explanation":"A task may execute quickly after spending a long time waiting. Monitoring only processing duration can therefore hide end-to-end latency problems.","principle":"Waiting time is part of workload latency when queued work contributes to the user or system objective.","rule":"Track or otherwise make waiting duration observable when queue delay materially affects correctness or service objectives.","objective":"Distinguish processing delay from scheduling delay.","performance_implication":"Queue age can reveal saturation before average execution time changes significantly.","testing_guidance":"Test workloads with controlled queue delays and verify that waiting time remains observable.","verification_method":"Trace work from admission through execution and identify whether queue delay is measurable.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B88-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B88","topic":"Resource Scheduling","subtopic":"Work Cancellation","concept":"Queued-work cancellation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should queued work be cancellable when its result is no longer useful?","answer":"Cancelling obsolete queued work releases scheduling capacity and prevents resources from being consumed for results that no longer have a valid consumer or deadline.","explanation":"Cancellation should be propagated consistently and must account for whether work has started and whether side effects have already occurred. Cancellation is not automatically equivalent to rollback.","principle":"Work without a valid objective should not consume resources indefinitely.","rule":"Define cancellation semantics for queued and executing work when obsolete results can be identified.","objective":"Reduce wasted capacity and stale-result processing.","constraints":["Cancellation after side effects may require compensation rather than simple termination.","Cancellation semantics must be explicit for each work stage."],"testing_guidance":"Cancel work before execution, during execution, and near completion and verify documented outcomes.","verification_method":"Trace cancellation propagation from the originating request through scheduler and worker boundaries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B89-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B89","topic":"Feature Flag and Rollout Patterns","subtopic":"Flag Semantics","concept":"Feature flag ownership","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should feature flags have explicit ownership and lifecycle semantics?","answer":"A feature flag without an owner or retirement condition can become permanent hidden configuration and increase behavioral complexity over time.","explanation":"Flags should identify their purpose, scope, responsible owner, expected lifetime, and removal condition. The exact metadata depends on system governance requirements.","principle":"Temporary behavior controls should have an explicit lifecycle rather than becoming undocumented permanent state.","rule":"Assign ownership and lifecycle criteria to feature flags that materially affect behavior.","objective":"Prevent configuration debt and unclear behavior control.","failure_mode":"An obsolete flag remains active indefinitely and future code must preserve unnecessary branches.","cause":"No removal owner or lifecycle condition existed.","solution":"Track flag ownership, intended duration, and retirement criteria.","testing_guidance":"Test both flag states and verify that obsolete flags can be removed without hidden dependencies.","verification_method":"Inspect feature-flag metadata, references, and retirement process.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B89-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B89","topic":"Feature Flag and Rollout Patterns","subtopic":"Rollout Safety","concept":"Progressive rollout","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can progressive rollout reduce change exposure?","answer":"Progressive rollout limits the initial population exposed to a behavior change, allowing detection of unexpected effects before broader activation.","explanation":"The rollout mechanism must provide reliable population targeting and an effective rollback or disable path when the change is not acceptable. Progressive rollout does not guarantee safety by itself.","principle":"Limiting exposure can reduce the scope of an unexpected change while evidence is collected.","rule":"Use staged exposure when the architecture and operational objective benefit from controlled rollout.","objective":"Reduce the initial impact scope of behavior changes.","constraints":["The rollout mechanism itself must be reliable.","Small exposure does not eliminate risk to affected users or systems."],"testing_guidance":"Verify targeting, activation, deactivation, and monitoring behavior before broad rollout.","verification_method":"Inspect rollout controls and trace which population receives each behavior state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B89-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B89","topic":"Feature Flag and Rollout Patterns","subtopic":"Flag Combinations","concept":"Feature flag interaction control","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can combinations of multiple feature flags create hidden behavior states?","answer":"Independent flags can multiply possible configurations, including combinations that were never explicitly designed or tested.","explanation":"The number of possible states grows as flags interact. Critical combinations should be modeled explicitly, constrained, or replaced by a higher-level state representation when appropriate.","principle":"Configuration state-space grows through interaction, not merely through individual option count.","rule":"Identify and control materially relevant feature-flag combinations.","objective":"Prevent untested configuration combinations from producing invalid behavior.","failure_mode":"Two individually valid flags create an invalid combined state.","cause":"Interactions were not modeled or constrained.","solution":"Define compatible combinations, validate configurations, or consolidate related flags.","testing_guidance":"Test supported combinations and explicitly reject unsupported combinations where possible.","verification_method":"Build a dependency or compatibility matrix for interacting flags.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B89-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B89","topic":"Feature Flag and Rollout Patterns","subtopic":"Evaluation Context","concept":"Feature flag evaluation consistency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should feature-flag evaluation context be consistent across related operations?","answer":"If related components evaluate a flag using different identity, environment, version, or targeting context, one logical operation can observe conflicting behavior.","explanation":"Evaluation context should be explicit when a flag controls behavior across multiple components. Consistency is especially important when the operation requires a coherent state rather than independent component decisions.","principle":"A behavior decision should use a consistent context across the scope in which that behavior must remain coherent.","rule":"Define the evaluation context and propagation semantics for flags that affect multi-component operations.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent feature behavior within one logical operation.","edge_case":"A flag changes while a long-running operation is in progress.","testing_guidance":"Test context propagation and flag changes during multi-stage operations.","verification_method":"Trace flag evaluation inputs across all participating components.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B89-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B89","topic":"Feature Flag and Rollout Patterns","subtopic":"Rollback","concept":"Flag rollback versus state rollback","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why is disabling a feature flag not necessarily equivalent to rolling back state?","answer":"A flag can stop future behavior while data, external side effects, or already-completed operations remain changed.","explanation":"Behavior rollback and state rollback are separate concepts. A feature flag can provide a forward control boundary but cannot automatically reverse effects already committed elsewhere.","principle":"Stopping a behavior path does not imply reversal of its historical effects.","rule":"Define separately what disabling a feature controls and how affected state is recovered or reconciled.","objective":"Prevent false assumptions about rollback completeness.","failure_mode":"A team disables a feature and assumes the system has returned to its previous state.","cause":"Behavior control was confused with state restoration.","solution":"Document flag effects, irreversible actions, and any required compensating recovery procedure.","testing_guidance":"Test flag disablement after state-changing operations and verify which effects remain.","verification_method":"Trace both behavioral activation and persistent side effects of the feature.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B90-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B90","topic":"Recovery and Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Recovery Classification","concept":"Recoverable versus irrecoverable failure distinction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should recovery logic distinguish recoverable failures from irrecoverable failures?","answer":"Different failure classes require different actions, and blindly retrying or repairing an irrecoverable condition can worsen state or waste resources.","explanation":"Recoverability depends on the failure cause, current state, operation semantics, and available recovery mechanisms. A failure should not be classified as recoverable merely because a retry is technically possible.","principle":"Recovery action should follow failure semantics rather than a generic retry policy.","rule":"Classify failures by whether safe recovery is possible before selecting a recovery action.","objective":"Prevent inappropriate recovery behavior.","failure_mode":"A non-recoverable failure is repeatedly retried or partially repaired without a valid recovery path.","cause":"Failure classification was absent or overly broad.","solution":"Define failure categories and map each to an explicit recovery policy.","testing_guidance":"Exercise representative transient, permanent, invalid-state, and unknown failure conditions.","verification_method":"Inspect failure taxonomy and recovery mapping for each relevant failure class.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B90-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B90","topic":"Recovery and Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Repair Boundaries","concept":"Bounded automated repair","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should automated repair have explicit scope and termination conditions?","answer":"Unbounded repair can repeatedly modify state without establishing whether the underlying invariant has actually been restored.","explanation":"Automated repair should define the conditions it is allowed to change, the evidence required to attempt repair, the maximum scope or attempts, and the verification needed afterward.","principle":"Automated repair must be constrained by authority, evidence, and verified recovery criteria.","rule":"Define repair scope, attempt limits, preconditions, and post-repair verification before automating state correction.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled repair loops and unintended state changes.","constraints":["Repair authority must match the affected resource and risk.","Successful execution of a repair action does not prove recovery."],"failure_mode":"A repair process repeatedly modifies state without restoring the required invariant.","cause":"No bounded repair scope or verified stopping condition existed.","solution":"Use explicit repair boundaries and stop when the recovery criterion is satisfied or the repair budget is exhausted.","testing_guidance":"Test successful repair, failed repair, repeated failure, and already-correct state.","verification_method":"Inspect repair preconditions, limits, and postcondition verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B90-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B90","topic":"Recovery and Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Checkpointing","concept":"Recovery checkpoint semantics","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes a recovery checkpoint useful?","answer":"A useful checkpoint represents a state from which processing can resume without violating the operation's consistency and correctness requirements.","explanation":"A checkpoint must define what work is included, what remains pending, and whether the checkpoint itself is durable enough for the intended recovery model. Merely recording progress does not prove prior work completed successfully.","principle":"Recovery checkpoints must represent a well-defined state boundary rather than an approximate progress indicator.","rule":"Define checkpoint contents, durability, validity, and resume semantics explicitly.","objective":"Enable controlled recovery without duplicating or losing work.","edge_case":"A checkpoint is recorded after partial side effects but before all dependent work has completed.","testing_guidance":"Interrupt processing around checkpoint boundaries and verify resume behavior.","verification_method":"Trace state included in the checkpoint and compare it with the recovery algorithm's assumptions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B90-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B90","topic":"Recovery and Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Unknown State","concept":"Explicit unknown-state handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should recovery systems represent unknown state explicitly?","answer":"After interruption, the system may be unable to determine whether an external operation completed, so forcing the situation into success or failure can produce incorrect recovery actions.","explanation":"Unknown state can occur when a process loses connectivity after submitting an operation but before receiving confirmation. The correct response may require reconciliation, status inquiry, or human review rather than blind repetition.","principle":"Uncertainty about outcome is a distinct state that must not be collapsed into success or failure without evidence.","rule":"Represent unknown outcome explicitly when the system cannot establish whether an external effect occurred.","objective":"Prevent duplicate or contradictory recovery actions caused by false certainty.","failure_mode":"An uncertain operation is retried as though it definitely failed, potentially duplicating its external effect.","cause":"Unknown outcome was collapsed into failure.","solution":"Preserve unknown state and use reconciliation or authoritative status evidence before selecting the next action.","security_implication":"Unknown state can require stricter authorization before consequential corrective actions.","testing_guidance":"Simulate loss of response after submission and verify that the system records unknown outcome rather than falsely declaring failure.","verification_method":"Trace outcome classification after communication interruption and verify evidence requirements for state resolution.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"} {"record_id":"D58-B90-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B90","topic":"Recovery and Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Recovery Verification","concept":"Post-recovery invariant verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must recovery verify the resulting state instead of only verifying that the repair action completed?","answer":"An action can execute successfully while failing to restore the intended system invariant.","explanation":"Recovery should distinguish action execution from outcome verification. The relevant invariant, consistency condition, or expected state must be checked after recovery according to the system contract.","principle":"Recovery is complete only when the required postcondition is established, not merely when a repair command returns successfully.","rule":"Verify the target invariant or recovery condition after repair before declaring recovery complete.","objective":"Prevent false recovery claims based solely on action execution.","failure_mode":"A repair operation returns successfully but the system remains inconsistent.","cause":"Execution success was treated as proof of recovery.","solution":"Define and verify explicit post-recovery invariants.","testing_guidance":"Test cases where the repair action succeeds but the desired state remains incorrect.","verification_method":"Compare post-recovery state against the authoritative recovery invariant or contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}

{"record_id":"D58-B91-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"State Ownership and Encapsulation","subtopic":"Ownership Transfer","concept":"Explicit ownership transfer","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should ownership of mutable state be transferred between components?","answer":"Define an explicit transfer boundary that states which component becomes responsible for mutation, lifetime, and cleanup after transfer.","explanation":"Implicit ownership creates competing mutation authority and unclear lifecycle responsibility. An explicit transfer contract makes authority observable and reduces aliasing-related defects.","principle":"Every mutable resource should have an identifiable current owner or an explicitly defined shared-ownership policy.","rule":"Do not transfer mutable state without defining post-transfer mutation authority and lifecycle responsibility.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous ownership and uncontrolled mutation.","constraints":["The transfer contract must be understandable at the API boundary.","Shared ownership requires explicit synchronization or immutability semantics."],"preconditions":["The state has mutable or lifecycle-sensitive behavior."],"expected_behavior":"After transfer, the previous owner no longer performs unauthorized mutation or cleanup.","failure_mode":"Multiple components continue treating the same mutable state as exclusively owned.","cause":"Ownership was implicit or undocumented.","root_cause":"No authoritative ownership model exists.","solution":"Encode ownership transfer in the interface and enforce it through API or type boundaries where possible.","edge_case":"A transfer may fail midway; the contract must define whether ownership remains with the source or moves only after successful completion.","security_implication":"Ambiguous ownership can permit unauthorized mutation of protected state.","test_case":"Transfer a mutable resource from component A to B and verify A can no longer mutate it through the supported interface.","test_input":"Ownership transfer followed by mutation attempts from both components.","expected_test_result":"Only the designated owner can perform authorized mutation.","testing_guidance":"Test successful transfer, failed transfer, repeated transfer, and stale references.","verification_method":"Review ownership transitions against the component contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every ownership transition has one defined authority.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B63-002","D58-B65-002","D58-B81-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B91-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"State Ownership and Encapsulation","subtopic":"Representation Hiding","concept":"Representation hiding","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should internal state representation be hidden behind an abstraction?","answer":"Hide representation details so callers depend on stable behavior rather than storage choices that may change.","explanation":"Exposing internal representation creates coupling between consumers and implementation details. Representation hiding permits internal changes without requiring unrelated consumers to change.","principle":"Public contracts should expose required behavior and invariants rather than unnecessary internal representation.","rule":"Do not expose mutable internal structures when callers can satisfy their requirements through controlled operations.","objective":"Reduce coupling and preserve implementation freedom.","constraints":["The abstraction must still expose sufficient behavior for legitimate consumers.","Hiding representation must not obscure necessary performance or consistency semantics."],"expected_behavior":"Internal storage can change while the externally documented behavior remains compatible.","failure_mode":"Consumers mutate or depend directly on internal structures.","cause":"Representation was exposed as part of the public interface.","root_cause":"Implementation details were incorrectly treated as contract.","solution":"Expose behavior-oriented methods or immutable views instead of mutable internals.","tradeoff":"Additional abstraction can introduce API surface and indirection.","edge_case":"Performance-sensitive consumers may require a documented bulk-access mechanism rather than repeated individual operations.","security_implication":"Hiding sensitive representation can reduce accidental exposure of protected state.","test_case":"Replace an internal collection representation while keeping public behavior unchanged.","expected_test_result":"Existing contract-level tests continue to pass without consumer changes.","testing_guidance":"Test observable behavior rather than internal storage layout.","verification_method":"Compare public contract dependencies with implementation details.","verification_guidance":"Flag consumers that require knowledge of internal representation without an explicit contract.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B50-001","D58-B48-001","D58-B63-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B91-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"State Ownership and Encapsulation","subtopic":"Mutation Boundaries","concept":"Invariant-preserving mutation boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Where should mutations that must preserve related state consistency occur?","answer":"Place related mutations behind a boundary that can validate and preserve the required consistency conditions atomically from the caller's perspective.","explanation":"Allowing callers to update correlated fields independently exposes intermediate invalid states and distributes invariant enforcement across consumers.","principle":"The component responsible for an invariant should control the mutations that can violate it.","rule":"Do not expose independent mutation operations when valid state requires coordinated updates.","objective":"Centralize consistency enforcement.","constraints":["The boundary must contain enough context to validate the invariant.","External side effects should not be falsely represented as atomic if they are not."],"expected_behavior":"Each supported mutation leaves the component in a state satisfying its documented consistency rules.","failure_mode":"One field changes while its dependent state remains stale or inconsistent.","cause":"Related state was mutated through separate uncontrolled operations.","root_cause":"The invariant boundary was placed below the state that required coordinated mutation.","solution":"Introduce a domain operation that performs validation and coordinated state change.","edge_case":"A multi-resource invariant may require transaction or compensation semantics rather than a single in-memory mutation boundary.","test_case":"Attempt a mutation that would violate a cross-field invariant.","expected_test_result":"The operation is rejected or produces a valid replacement state.","testing_guidance":"Test valid transitions, invalid transitions, and partial-failure paths.","verification_method":"Check every mutation path against the invariant definition.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unsupported direct mutation paths are unavailable or explicitly documented.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B42-001","D58-B19-003","D58-B43-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B91-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"State Ownership and Encapsulation","subtopic":"Capabilities","concept":"Capability-oriented API","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can an API limit callers to only the operations they are authorized or intended to perform?","answer":"Expose narrowly scoped capabilities representing permitted operations instead of handing callers unrestricted access to the underlying object or resource.","explanation":"A capability can encode authority through the interface itself, reducing accidental access and making dependencies more explicit.","principle":"Grant the minimum operational surface required for a task.","rule":"Prefer operation-specific interfaces when a consumer does not require full resource authority.","objective":"Reduce accidental coupling and unnecessary authority.","constraints":["The capability must not provide hidden escalation paths.","The capability boundary must remain understandable to callers."],"expected_behavior":"Consumers can perform required operations but cannot directly invoke unrelated operations through the provided interface.","failure_mode":"A consumer receives broad access and begins depending on unrelated behavior.","cause":"The full implementation object was exposed instead of a narrow capability.","root_cause":"Authority and dependency boundaries were not modeled separately.","solution":"Define a capability interface containing only required operations.","security_implication":"Narrow capabilities can reduce the impact of accidental or unauthorized operations.","test_case":"Provide a read-only capability to a component and attempt a mutation.","expected_test_result":"The mutation operation is unavailable through the supported capability.","testing_guidance":"Test both required operations and prohibited interface operations.","verification_method":"Inspect capability interfaces against consumer requirements.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no unnecessary authority is exposed.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B73-001","D58-B81-001","D58-B17-002"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B91-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B91","topic":"State Ownership and Encapsulation","subtopic":"Aggregate Consistency","concept":"Aggregate consistency boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"When should several related objects be controlled through one consistency boundary?","answer":"When their validity depends on coordinated changes, place those objects behind an aggregate boundary that owns the relevant consistency rules.","explanation":"Distributed mutation of tightly coupled objects makes consistency rules difficult to enforce and verify.","principle":"Consistency should be owned by the smallest boundary that can enforce all required relationships.","rule":"Do not distribute enforcement of one invariant across unrelated components when a single consistency owner is feasible.","objective":"Make consistency responsibility explicit.","constraints":["The aggregate boundary should not become an indiscriminate container for unrelated behavior.","Cross-boundary consistency may require explicit transactional or eventual-consistency semantics."],"expected_behavior":"Operations affecting the aggregate preserve its documented consistency rules.","failure_mode":"Individual objects remain locally valid while their combined state is invalid.","cause":"No component owns the cross-object invariant.","root_cause":"The consistency boundary was incorrectly decomposed.","solution":"Define an explicit aggregate operation or transaction boundary.","tradeoff":"Larger boundaries can reduce independent scalability or concurrency.","edge_case":"Eventually consistent distributed systems may intentionally permit temporary cross-boundary inconsistency.","testing_guidance":"Test coordinated updates and invalid cross-object combinations.","verification_method":"Map each invariant to the smallest component capable of enforcing it.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every aggregate invariant has one identifiable owner.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B43-001","D58-B63-001","D58-B19-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B92-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Value Semantics","subtopic":"Equality","concept":"Equality and hashing consistency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What relationship must exist between equality and hashing when objects are used in hash-based collections?","answer":"Objects considered equal must produce the same hash value for the lifetime relevant to collection membership.","explanation":"Hash-based collections use hashes to locate candidate entries before equality comparison. Equal objects with inconsistent hashes can become unreachable or duplicated in logical terms.","principle":"Equality semantics and hash semantics must agree wherever hashing participates in lookup.","rule":"If a == b is true, hash(a) must equal hash(b).","objective":"Preserve correctness of hash-based lookup and membership operations.","constraints":["The hash must remain stable while the object participates in a hash-based collection unless the collection explicitly supports mutation.","Equality must itself have a defined semantic contract."],"expected_behavior":"Equal values are located consistently in hash-based structures.","failure_mode":"An equal key cannot be found after insertion or produces inconsistent membership behavior.","cause":"Hash depends on fields inconsistent with equality or mutable fields changed after insertion.","root_cause":"Equality and hashing contracts were designed independently.","solution":"Define both from the same immutable logical identity or value components.","edge_case":"Identity-based objects may intentionally use identity equality and identity hashing.","test_case":"Create two logically equal values and compare equality and hash results.","expected_test_result":"Equality is true and hashes are equal.","testing_guidance":"Test equal, unequal, collision, and post-insertion mutation scenarios.","verification_method":"Review equality and hashing definitions together.","verification_guidance":"Verify the equality-to-hash implication explicitly.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B83-001","D58-B84-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B92-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Value Semantics","subtopic":"Comparison","concept":"Comparison consistency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should ordering or comparison operations be consistent with equality where the domain requires it?","answer":"A comparison contract should define whether equivalence under ordering means semantic equality or a distinct notion of equivalence, and collection behavior must follow that contract.","explanation":"Inconsistent comparison and equality semantics can cause sorted structures to treat distinct values as duplicates or equal values as distinct.","principle":"Comparison semantics must be explicit wherever ordering affects identity or collection membership.","rule":"Do not assume comparison returning equivalence has the same meaning as equality unless the contract states that it does.","objective":"Prevent collection and ordering anomalies.","constraints":["Some domains intentionally use ordering equivalence different from object equality.","Consumers must know which equivalence relation an ordered collection uses."],"expected_behavior":"Sorting and ordered lookup follow the documented comparison semantics.","failure_mode":"Values unexpectedly disappear, duplicate, or become unfindable in ordered collections.","cause":"Consumers assumed incompatible equality and comparison contracts.","root_cause":"Equivalence semantics were unspecified.","solution":"Document and test the relationship between equality and comparison.","edge_case":"Case-insensitive comparison may treat strings as equivalent for ordering while preserving case-sensitive equality.","test_case":"Insert values that compare equivalent but are not equal into an ordered structure.","expected_test_result":"Behavior matches the documented collection contract.","testing_guidance":"Test comparison equivalence separately from equality.","verification_method":"Review comparison laws and collection assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Verify transitivity, consistency, and documented equivalence semantics where required.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B83-001","D58-B84-004"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B92-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Value Semantics","subtopic":"Value Objects","concept":"Immutable value object","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an immutable value object useful?","answer":"Use an immutable value object when a concept is defined by its value and should remain stable after construction.","explanation":"Immutability prevents unexpected changes after validation and makes values easier to share, cache, compare, and reason about.","principle":"Represent stable domain values as immutable objects when identity is not the primary semantic.","rule":"Validate the complete value at construction or creation and prevent unsupported mutation afterward.","objective":"Make value semantics explicit and reduce temporal coupling.","constraints":["All fields contributing to value semantics must be protected from mutable aliasing.","Updates require creation of a new valid value."],"expected_behavior":"A created value remains semantically stable until replaced.","failure_mode":"A value changes after validation and invalidates assumptions made by consumers.","cause":"Mutable representation escaped the creation boundary.","root_cause":"Value and entity semantics were conflated.","solution":"Use immutable representation and explicit replacement operations.","tradeoff":"Replacement can create additional allocations or copying costs.","edge_case":"Large values may require structural sharing rather than full copying.","test_case":"Construct a value, retain a reference, and attempt unsupported mutation.","expected_test_result":"The original value remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Test immutability, equality, validation, and aliasing behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect all mutation paths and nested mutable members.","verification_guidance":"Verify that externally reachable state cannot mutate the value unexpectedly.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B65-001","D58-B91-002","D58-B63-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B92-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Value Semantics","subtopic":"Identity","concept":"Identity versus value distinction","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why must identity and value semantics be distinguished?","answer":"Identity represents continuity of a particular entity, while value semantics represent equivalence based on attributes; choosing the wrong model causes incorrect equality, caching, persistence, and update behavior.","explanation":"Two records can have identical attributes but represent different entities, while the same entity can change attributes over time.","principle":"Equality semantics should reflect the domain meaning of the concept rather than its storage representation.","rule":"Define whether a concept is identified by stable identity, value, or an explicit combination before implementing equality or persistence behavior.","objective":"Prevent semantic confusion between entities and values.","constraints":["Identity requires a stable identification mechanism.","Value equality requires clearly defined value components."],"expected_behavior":"Equality and lifecycle operations follow the selected semantic model.","failure_mode":"Distinct entities collapse into one logical value or equivalent values are treated as unrelated entities.","cause":"Identity and value semantics were not explicitly modeled.","root_cause":"Domain meaning was inferred from representation instead of specified.","solution":"Document identity and value semantics at the model boundary.","edge_case":"A value object may contain an identifier-like field without making the entire object an entity.","test_case":"Compare two objects with identical attributes but different identities.","expected_test_result":"Behavior matches the declared identity/value model.","testing_guidance":"Test equality, persistence, update, caching, and serialization semantics together.","verification_method":"Trace equality and identity decisions to domain requirements.","verification_guidance":"Verify that storage keys are not automatically treated as domain identity.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B92-001","D58-B64-001","D58-B67-002"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B92-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B92","topic":"Value Semantics","subtopic":"Defensive Construction","concept":"Validated construction boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why validate a value before exposing it as a usable domain object?","answer":"Construct or admit an object only after required structural and semantic conditions are satisfied, so downstream code can rely on the object's declared invariants.","explanation":"Repeated validation across consumers is error-prone and permits partially valid objects to circulate.","principle":"A component should not expose a state that violates its own construction contract.","rule":"Perform mandatory validation before publishing an object to consumers.","objective":"Move validity enforcement to the earliest reliable boundary.","constraints":["Validation must cover all conditions the object promises.","Conditions dependent on external mutable state may require later validation as well."],"expected_behavior":"Published objects satisfy their construction contract.","failure_mode":"Invalid objects reach downstream logic and cause failures far from their origin.","cause":"Construction accepted invalid state.","root_cause":"The validity boundary was placed too late.","solution":"Validate before publication and distinguish structural validation from contextual validation.","edge_case":"An object may intentionally represent an incomplete draft; its contract must state that incomplete state is valid.","test_case":"Attempt to construct an object with an invalid required field.","expected_test_result":"Construction rejects the invalid state or returns an explicit validation result.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, malformed, boundary, and semantically invalid values.","verification_method":"Map constructor or factory checks to the object contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no supported creation path bypasses required validation.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B72-001","D58-B63-001","D58-B66-004"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B93-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Control Flow and Side Effects","subtopic":"Command Query Separation","concept":"Command-query separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the value of separating commands from queries?","answer":"Commands change state while queries return information without changing externally observable state; separating these roles makes behavior easier to reason about, test, and compose.","explanation":"A method that both retrieves information and performs hidden mutation creates temporal and reasoning dependencies.","principle":"An operation should have an explicit primary role as state-changing command or observational query.","rule":"Avoid hidden mutation in operations presented as queries unless the contract explicitly documents it.","objective":"Make side effects predictable.","constraints":["Some systems intentionally use caching or lazy initialization as observable implementation behavior; such behavior must be documented.","A command may return a result describing its performed mutation."],"expected_behavior":"Calling a query does not unexpectedly alter domain state.","failure_mode":"Repeated reads produce state changes or alter later command behavior.","cause":"Mutation was hidden inside an observational API.","root_cause":"Side-effect semantics were not explicit.","solution":"Separate mutation and observation or document unavoidable side effects.","edge_case":"A read may update internal metrics; distinguish operational instrumentation from domain mutation.","test_case":"Call a query repeatedly and compare relevant domain state before and after.","expected_test_result":"No unsupported domain mutation occurs.","testing_guidance":"Use state snapshots to detect hidden domain changes.","verification_method":"Inspect query implementations for externally relevant mutation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that documented side effects match observed behavior.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B91-002","D58-B19-003","D58-B76-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B93-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Control Flow and Side Effects","subtopic":"Temporal Coupling","concept":"Temporal coupling detection","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is temporal coupling?","answer":"Temporal coupling occurs when one operation must happen before another even though the dependency is not represented explicitly in the interface or data flow.","explanation":"Hidden ordering requirements create fragile callers and failures when execution order changes.","principle":"Required sequencing should be represented explicitly wherever practical.","rule":"Do not require undocumented call ordering for correctness.","objective":"Reduce hidden execution-order dependencies.","constraints":["Some workflows inherently require sequencing; those sequences should be modeled as workflow or state transitions.","External protocols may impose ordering that cannot be removed."],"expected_behavior":"Required order is visible through state, API structure, workflow, or explicit preconditions.","failure_mode":"A valid operation fails because a caller omitted an undocumented earlier step.","cause":"Initialization or sequencing dependency was implicit.","root_cause":"Temporal dependency was not represented in the contract.","solution":"Combine dependent operations, encode lifecycle state, or expose explicit preconditions.","edge_case":"A resource may require open-before-read semantics; the lifecycle should make that requirement explicit.","test_case":"Invoke an operation before its prerequisite and inspect the contract-defined behavior.","expected_test_result":"The system either rejects the call with an explicit precondition failure or prevents invalid sequencing through the API.","testing_guidance":"Test valid and invalid orderings.","verification_method":"Trace call-order dependencies through callers and implementations.","verification_guidance":"Identify operations whose correctness depends on prior calls not expressed in their contract.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B19-003","D58-B44-001","D58-B81-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B93-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Control Flow and Side Effects","subtopic":"Side Effect Boundary","concept":"Explicit side-effect boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should systems isolate operations that perform external side effects?","answer":"Concentrate external side effects at explicit boundaries so core decision logic can be reasoned about independently from external systems.","explanation":"Separating side effects from computation reduces hidden dependencies and makes deterministic testing easier.","principle":"Keep pure or deterministic reasoning separate from effectful execution where practical.","rule":"Do not allow external I/O to become an implicit dependency of logic that can otherwise be computed independently.","objective":"Improve testability, determinism, and failure isolation.","constraints":["Some computations inherently depend on external state.","The boundary must carry enough information to execute required effects correctly."],"expected_behavior":"Core logic produces explicit effect requests or consumes explicit external results.","failure_mode":"Core logic becomes difficult to test because it directly performs uncontrolled external operations.","cause":"Effectful operations were mixed with decision logic.","root_cause":"No explicit effect boundary was established.","solution":"Separate decision computation from effect execution and inject effect capabilities at the boundary.","tradeoff":"Additional data structures or orchestration may be required.","test_case":"Run core logic using deterministic effect results without contacting an external system.","expected_test_result":"Decision behavior can be tested independently.","testing_guidance":"Test core decisions with controlled effect inputs and separately test effect adapters.","verification_method":"Map all external I/O from the core decision path.","verification_guidance":"Verify that side effects occur only through documented boundaries.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B81-002","D58-B50-004","D58-B40-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B93-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Control Flow and Side Effects","subtopic":"Policy and Mechanism","concept":"Policy-mechanism separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why separate policy decisions from execution mechanisms?","answer":"Policy defines what should happen under specified conditions, while mechanism defines how the decision is carried out; separating them allows either to evolve independently.","explanation":"Embedding policy directly into low-level mechanisms makes policy changes require widespread implementation changes.","principle":"Decision rules should be represented independently from the mechanism that executes them when the domain requires policy changeability.","rule":"Do not hard-code mutable business or operational policy into reusable mechanisms without an explicit policy boundary.","objective":"Reduce policy-driven change propagation.","constraints":["The policy interface must expose sufficient context for correct decisions.","Not every tiny rule warrants a separate abstraction."],"expected_behavior":"Changing policy does not require rewriting unrelated execution machinery.","failure_mode":"A policy change requires edits across multiple low-level components.","cause":"Policy was embedded in mechanism code.","root_cause":"Decision and execution responsibilities were coupled.","solution":"Represent policy as explicit rules, strategies, or decision inputs and keep mechanism generic.","tradeoff":"Additional abstraction can be unnecessary for stable, local policy.","test_case":"Replace a policy implementation while retaining the same execution mechanism.","expected_test_result":"Execution machinery remains unchanged and honors the new policy.","testing_guidance":"Test policy decisions independently from mechanism execution.","verification_method":"Trace policy conditions through execution code.","verification_guidance":"Verify that reusable mechanisms do not encode unrelated policy assumptions.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B67-001","D58-B48-003","D58-B81-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B93-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B93","topic":"Control Flow and Side Effects","subtopic":"Explicit Preconditions","concept":"Precondition-preserving API sequencing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should APIs communicate required preconditions without relying on caller knowledge?","answer":"Represent important preconditions through explicit state, types, operations, or documented validation rather than relying only on informal calling conventions.","explanation":"Hidden preconditions create failures that appear unrelated to the caller's actual mistake.","principle":"Correct API use should be discoverable and enforceable where practical.","rule":"Make important state-dependent preconditions visible at the API boundary.","objective":"Reduce invalid call sequences and improve diagnosability.","constraints":["Some dynamic conditions cannot be encoded statically.","Runtime validation remains necessary for external or mutable conditions."],"expected_behavior":"Invalid operation attempts are rejected clearly or made unavailable through the supported interface.","failure_mode":"Callers unknowingly violate an undocumented prerequisite.","cause":"Precondition existed only in implementation assumptions.","root_cause":"The contract did not represent required state.","solution":"Expose explicit lifecycle state, validate preconditions, or provide a higher-level operation that performs required setup.","edge_case":"Preconditions depending on time or external resources must be checked at execution time.","test_case":"Call an operation with each required prerequisite absent.","expected_test_result":"A clear contract-level failure occurs rather than an unrelated internal error.","testing_guidance":"Test each precondition independently and in combination.","verification_method":"Compare documented preconditions with implementation guards.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all mandatory preconditions are represented at the boundary.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B71-001","D58-B93-002","D58-B19-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B94-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Modularity and Coupling","subtopic":"Dependency Direction","concept":"Stable dependency direction","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should dependencies be directed to preserve modularity?","answer":"Dependencies should point toward intentionally stable abstractions rather than forcing stable components to depend on volatile implementation details.","explanation":"When stable code depends directly on frequently changing details, changes propagate unnecessarily across the system.","principle":"Dependency direction should minimize coupling from stable policy or domain logic to volatile mechanisms.","rule":"Do not allow core abstractions to depend directly on replaceable infrastructure when an explicit boundary can isolate the dependency.","objective":"Reduce change propagation.","constraints":["The actual stability of components must be assessed rather than assumed from naming.","Abstractions should be justified by real dependency variation."],"expected_behavior":"Changes in volatile mechanisms have limited impact on stable consumers.","failure_mode":"Infrastructure changes require widespread modifications to core logic.","cause":"Dependency direction follows implementation convenience rather than stability.","root_cause":"Architectural dependency boundaries were not evaluated.","solution":"Invert the dependency through an interface, adapter, or explicit port where justified.","tradeoff":"Extra abstractions can increase complexity when volatility is not real.","test_case":"Replace a volatile infrastructure implementation without changing core logic.","expected_test_result":"Core logic remains unchanged and continues through the defined boundary.","testing_guidance":"Test implementations behind the same contract independently.","verification_method":"Construct a dependency graph and inspect direction across architectural boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stable components do not unnecessarily depend on volatile details.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B17-001","D58-B48-002","D58-B81-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B94-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Modularity and Coupling","subtopic":"Cohesion","concept":"Responsibility cohesion","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does high responsibility cohesion mean in a software component?","answer":"A cohesive component groups behavior that changes for closely related reasons and supports a focused responsibility.","explanation":"Unrelated responsibilities create broad change surfaces and make component behavior difficult to understand and test.","principle":"A component should have a focused set of closely related responsibilities.","rule":"Separate responsibilities when they have materially different reasons to change or distinct invariants.","objective":"Reduce unrelated change coupling.","constraints":["Splitting every behavior into a separate component can create excessive fragmentation.","Responsibilities that share strong invariants may legitimately belong together."],"expected_behavior":"Changes to one responsibility have limited impact on unrelated behavior.","failure_mode":"A component accumulates unrelated operations and becomes difficult to modify safely.","cause":"Responsibilities were grouped by convenience rather than semantic cohesion.","root_cause":"No responsibility boundary was established.","solution":"Identify reasons for change and move unrelated behavior behind appropriate boundaries.","tradeoff":"More components introduce additional interfaces and coordination.","test_case":"Identify independent change scenarios affecting one component.","expected_test_result":"Unrelated responsibilities can be changed without modifying the component unnecessarily.","testing_guidance":"Review tests and change history for unrelated behavior clusters.","verification_method":"Map component operations to responsibilities and reasons for change.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the component's contract has a coherent purpose.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B48-001","D58-B62-002"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B94-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Modularity and Coupling","subtopic":"Coupling Measurement","concept":"Change coupling detection","knowledge_type":"HEURISTIC","question":"How can excessive coupling be detected during maintenance?","answer":"Look for changes that repeatedly require coordinated edits across components that should otherwise evolve independently.","explanation":"Frequent synchronized modifications can reveal hidden dependencies even when static dependency graphs appear simple.","principle":"Observed change patterns can expose semantic coupling not visible from structural references alone.","rule":"Treat repeated coordinated changes as evidence requiring investigation, not automatic proof that components must be merged or split.","objective":"Detect hidden coupling using maintenance evidence.","constraints":["Change history may reflect temporary projects or team organization rather than architecture.","The heuristic should be combined with dependency and responsibility analysis."],"expected_behavior":"Repeated coordinated changes trigger architectural investigation.","failure_mode":"Hidden coupling remains because structural references alone appear acceptable.","cause":"Only static dependencies were considered.","root_cause":"Semantic change relationships were ignored.","solution":"Analyze change sets alongside dependency and responsibility boundaries.","edge_case":"A planned cross-cutting feature can legitimately change many components once without indicating persistent coupling.","testing_guidance":"Review historical change sets for recurring co-change relationships.","verification_method":"Compare repeated co-change patterns with declared architecture.","verification_guidance":"Classify coupling as structural, semantic, operational, or temporary before changing architecture.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"MEDIUM","related_records":["D58-B62-002","D58-B70-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B94-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Modularity and Coupling","subtopic":"Abstraction Boundaries","concept":"Abstraction leakage","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is abstraction leakage?","answer":"Abstraction leakage occurs when consumers must understand or depend on internal details that the abstraction was intended to hide.","explanation":"An abstraction is weakened when its users must know implementation-specific behavior to use it correctly.","principle":"A useful abstraction should hide details that consumers do not need to reason about.","rule":"Investigate consumers that require undocumented implementation knowledge as evidence of a leaking abstraction.","objective":"Preserve meaningful boundaries.","failure_mode":"Consumers contain special cases for internal behavior.","cause":"The abstraction does not represent required semantics adequately or exposes incompatible assumptions.","root_cause":"Boundary design omitted important behavior or failed to isolate implementation details.","solution":"Strengthen the contract, move required behavior into the abstraction, or remove the abstraction if it does not provide useful separation.","edge_case":"Performance characteristics may intentionally be part of a contract and therefore are not necessarily leakage.","test_case":"Implement a consumer using only documented abstraction behavior and compare required knowledge with actual dependencies.","expected_test_result":"No undocumented implementation details are required for correct use.","testing_guidance":"Search consumers for implementation-specific conditionals and casts.","verification_method":"Review contract completeness against consumer assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Distinguish genuine leakage from legitimate documented semantics.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B91-002","D58-B48-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B94-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B94","topic":"Modularity and Coupling","subtopic":"Layer Boundaries","concept":"Layer boundary enforcement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should architectural layer boundaries be enforceable rather than merely documented?","answer":"Enforceable boundaries prevent accidental dependencies from becoming permanent architecture and make violations detectable during development or verification.","explanation":"Documentation alone does not prevent a convenient shortcut from introducing a forbidden dependency.","principle":"Important architectural constraints should have machine-checkable or reviewable enforcement mechanisms where practical.","rule":"Encode critical dependency-direction or layer constraints in tooling, tests, module structure, or verification checks when feasible.","objective":"Prevent architectural drift.","constraints":["Not every architectural preference can be mechanically enforced.","Rules must reflect the actual intended architecture."],"expected_behavior":"Forbidden dependencies are detected before they become accepted design.","failure_mode":"Repeated shortcuts gradually collapse architectural boundaries.","cause":"Architecture rules were descriptive only.","root_cause":"No enforcement mechanism existed for important structural constraints.","solution":"Add dependency checks, module boundaries, architectural tests, or review gates.","test_case":"Introduce a deliberately forbidden cross-layer dependency.","expected_test_result":"The applicable architectural check detects the violation.","testing_guidance":"Include both valid and intentionally invalid dependency cases.","verification_method":"Run architecture constraint checks against the dependency graph.","verification_guidance":"Verify that enforcement covers the critical boundaries and does not silently omit modules.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B48-004","D58-B62-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B95-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Extension and Plugin Design","subtopic":"Extension Boundary","concept":"Explicit extension point","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should a system expose an extension point?","answer":"Expose an extension point when supported variability is expected and a stable contract can isolate extension implementations from the core.","explanation":"Explicit extension boundaries prevent plugins from depending on internal implementation details and allow controlled evolution.","principle":"Variability should be isolated behind an explicit contract rather than through informal internal hooks.","rule":"Do not expose extension mechanisms without defining lifecycle, error, compatibility, and authority semantics.","objective":"Make extensibility predictable and maintainable.","constraints":["An extension point should solve real expected variability.","The core must retain control over critical invariants and security boundaries."],"expected_behavior":"Extensions interact only through the supported contract.","failure_mode":"Extensions depend on internal details and break during unrelated core changes.","cause":"Extension capability was provided without a stable contract.","root_cause":"Extensibility was implemented as incidental access to internals.","solution":"Define an explicit extension interface and lifecycle contract.","edge_case":"A private internal hook may be acceptable when no external compatibility promise exists, but it should not be represented as a stable extension point.","security_implication":"Extensions can become privileged code; authority must be explicitly bounded.","test_case":"Implement an extension using only the published extension contract.","expected_test_result":"The extension works without accessing core internals.","testing_guidance":"Test extension loading, failure, compatibility, and lifecycle behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect extension dependencies against the published contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify that extensions cannot bypass core invariants through unsupported access.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B50-005","D58-B73-001","D58-B17-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B95-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Extension and Plugin Design","subtopic":"Plugin Failure","concept":"Plugin failure isolation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should a host system contain failures from optional extensions?","answer":"Define an extension boundary where plugin failures are classified and prevented from corrupting unrelated host state or violating host invariants.","explanation":"Optional extensions should not convert their local failure into an uncontrolled failure of the host system.","principle":"Optional components should fail within the smallest boundary that can preserve system correctness.","rule":"Do not allow extension exceptions, resource exhaustion, or invalid output to bypass host validation and containment.","objective":"Protect core functionality from optional extension failures.","constraints":["Some extensions are essential for specific workflows and cannot always be isolated completely.","Containment must preserve honest failure semantics rather than silently discarding critical errors."],"expected_behavior":"An extension failure is reported and contained according to the extension contract.","failure_mode":"A plugin failure leaves shared host state inconsistent.","cause":"Extension output or execution was trusted without a containment boundary.","root_cause":"Plugin and host failure domains were not separated.","solution":"Validate extension outputs, isolate resources, and define explicit failure behavior.","test_case":"Make an extension fail during execution and inspect host state.","expected_test_result":"Host invariants remain valid and the extension failure is surfaced according to contract.","testing_guidance":"Test exceptions, invalid outputs, timeouts, and resource exhaustion.","verification_method":"Trace extension execution through host state mutation paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that plugin failure cannot bypass host consistency checks.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B17-003","D58-B14-004","D58-B90-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B95-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Extension and Plugin Design","subtopic":"Compatibility","concept":"Extension contract stability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must remain stable for independently developed extensions to remain compatible?","answer":"The host must preserve the documented extension contract or provide explicit compatibility and migration mechanisms for changes.","explanation":"Extensions often evolve on different schedules from the host, so undocumented changes can cause failures without synchronized releases.","principle":"Independent evolution requires explicit compatibility boundaries.","rule":"Treat the extension contract as a versioned interface rather than an incidental collection of callable internals.","objective":"Support controlled independent evolution.","constraints":["Compatibility guarantees must define scope and duration.","Breaking changes require a migration or version transition strategy."],"expected_behavior":"Compatible extensions continue to operate across supported host versions.","failure_mode":"A host implementation change breaks extensions despite no documented contract change.","cause":"Extensions depended on unstable internal behavior.","root_cause":"The compatibility boundary was not clearly defined.","solution":"Version and document the extension interface and test supported compatibility combinations.","testing_guidance":"Test extensions against supported host contract versions.","verification_method":"Compare host changes with declared extension contract guarantees.","verification_guidance":"Verify that compatibility claims are scoped to explicit versions and behaviors.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B68-003","D58-B95-001","D58-B82-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B95-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Extension and Plugin Design","subtopic":"Discovery","concept":"Explicit capability discovery","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should optional capabilities be discoverable rather than assumed?","answer":"A consumer should determine whether an optional capability is supported before invoking it when implementations can legitimately differ.","explanation":"Assuming optional functionality exists converts normal capability variation into runtime failure.","principle":"Optional behavior should be represented as optional in the contract.","rule":"Do not invoke an optional capability without an explicit support or negotiation mechanism.","objective":"Handle heterogeneous implementations safely.","constraints":["Capability discovery itself must have stable semantics.","Required capabilities should fail clearly rather than being treated as optional."],"expected_behavior":"Consumers adapt based on explicitly reported capability support.","failure_mode":"A consumer assumes unsupported functionality and fails unexpectedly.","cause":"Capability variation was not represented in the interface.","root_cause":"Optional and mandatory behavior were conflated.","solution":"Expose capability metadata, negotiation, or a typed optional operation.","edge_case":"A capability may become unavailable dynamically; discovery may need to be repeated or tied to a validity interval.","test_case":"Run the same consumer against implementations with and without the optional capability.","expected_test_result":"The consumer selects supported behavior without an unsupported invocation.","testing_guidance":"Test supported, unsupported, and capability-change scenarios.","verification_method":"Compare consumer assumptions with the provider's declared capabilities.","verification_guidance":"Verify that mandatory capabilities are distinguished from optional capabilities.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B85-004","D58-B68-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B95-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B95","topic":"Extension and Plugin Design","subtopic":"Extension Lifecycle","concept":"Extension lifecycle contract","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What should an extension lifecycle contract define?","answer":"It should define discovery or loading, initialization, operation, shutdown, failure behavior, resource ownership, and compatibility expectations.","explanation":"An extension is not only an interface of callable functions; lifecycle assumptions determine when dependencies are valid and when resources must be released.","principle":"Lifecycle semantics are part of an extension contract.","rule":"Do not define an extension interface without specifying lifecycle assumptions that affect correctness.","objective":"Prevent lifecycle-related extension failures.","constraints":["Lifecycle stages should be limited to behavior actually required by the host.","Resource ownership must be explicit at every lifecycle transition."],"expected_behavior":"Host and extension agree on when resources become available and when responsibility ends.","failure_mode":"An extension uses resources before initialization or after shutdown.","cause":"Lifecycle was implicit.","root_cause":"Only operation signatures were documented.","solution":"Define lifecycle states and allowed operations for each state.","test_case":"Invoke extension operations before initialization and after shutdown.","expected_test_result":"Invalid lifecycle operations are rejected clearly.","testing_guidance":"Test all supported lifecycle transitions and failure transitions.","verification_method":"Model lifecycle states and permitted operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every externally callable extension operation has a defined lifecycle state.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B14-001","D58-B44-001","D58-B95-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B96-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Maintainability and Technical Debt","subtopic":"Complexity Control","concept":"Complexity budget","knowledge_type":"HEURISTIC","question":"What is a complexity budget?","answer":"A complexity budget is an explicit limit or review threshold for structural complexity within a component, module, or change.","explanation":"Complexity tends to increase the reasoning and verification burden of future changes. A budget makes that cost visible before complexity becomes entrenched.","principle":"Complexity should be treated as a constrained engineering resource rather than an unlimited byproduct.","rule":"Investigate changes that materially increase complexity without a corresponding requirement or documented tradeoff.","objective":"Control long-term maintenance cost.","constraints":["Complexity metrics are indicators, not universal quality measurements.","A numerical threshold must be calibrated to the system and context."],"expected_behavior":"Significant complexity increases trigger explicit review or mitigation.","failure_mode":"Complexity grows incrementally until changes become difficult to reason about.","cause":"No visibility or review boundary existed for complexity growth.","root_cause":"Complexity was treated as free.","solution":"Track relevant complexity indicators and require justification for significant increases.","tradeoff":"Strict limits can discourage necessary sophisticated implementations.","edge_case":"A temporary increase may be justified during migration if a removal plan exists.","test_case":"Introduce a change that substantially increases branching or dependency complexity.","expected_test_result":"The change is identified for review according to the project's complexity policy.","testing_guidance":"Use metrics as review signals rather than proof of defect.","verification_method":"Compare complexity before and after a change.","verification_guidance":"Verify that significant increases have explicit rationale and, where applicable, removal plans.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"MEDIUM","related_records":["D58-B62-002","D58-B74-001","D58-B70-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B96-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Maintainability and Technical Debt","subtopic":"Dead Code","concept":"Dead code removal","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should confirmed dead code be removed rather than merely ignored?","answer":"Removing confirmed dead code reduces maintenance surface, ambiguity, and the number of locations that future engineers or automated systems must reason about.","explanation":"Unused code can still appear authoritative, accumulate stale dependencies, and increase the apparent behavior space of a system.","principle":"Code that is demonstrably unnecessary should not remain part of the maintained behavior surface without a reason.","rule":"Remove code only after confirming that it is not required through supported dynamic or external entry points.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary maintenance and reasoning burden.","constraints":["Dynamic loading, reflection, configuration, generated calls, and external integrations can make dead-code detection uncertain.","Removal should preserve required public compatibility where promised."],"expected_behavior":"Confirmed unreachable or obsolete implementation is removed and relevant references are updated.","failure_mode":"Obsolete code remains and later receives incorrect fixes or creates dependency confusion.","cause":"Removal was deferred without evidence.","root_cause":"No explicit dead-code confirmation and cleanup process existed.","solution":"Establish evidence for unreachability or obsolescence before removal.","edge_case":"Generated or plugin-discovered code may appear unused statically while remaining operationally required.","testing_guidance":"Run relevant tests and reference analysis after removal.","verification_method":"Combine static references, runtime entry-point analysis, configuration inspection, and contract review.","verification_guidance":"Do not equate zero static references with universal proof of deadness.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B62-001","D58-B70-005","D58-B69-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B96-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Maintainability and Technical Debt","subtopic":"Duplication","concept":"Semantic duplication detection","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is semantic duplication more important than exact textual duplication?","answer":"Two implementations can perform the same conceptual role while using different syntax, structures, or names, so textual deduplication alone can miss duplicated behavior and maintenance responsibility.","explanation":"Semantic duplicates create multiple sources of truth even when code is not textually similar.","principle":"Deduplication should consider behavior and responsibility, not only syntax.","rule":"Investigate independently maintained implementations that satisfy the same conceptual contract.","objective":"Reduce conflicting sources of behavior.","constraints":["Similar behavior can be intentionally specialized and should not be merged solely because outputs resemble each other.","Deduplication requires understanding contracts and constraints."],"expected_behavior":"Equivalent responsibilities have a deliberate canonical implementation or clearly documented specialization.","failure_mode":"One implementation is fixed while a semantically duplicate implementation remains inconsistent.","cause":"Only textual duplication was considered.","root_cause":"Duplication analysis ignored semantic responsibility.","solution":"Compare contracts, inputs, outputs, invariants, and change responsibilities before consolidating.","edge_case":"Platform-specific implementations may legitimately duplicate semantics because their mechanisms differ.","test_case":"Identify two implementations with different code but the same documented responsibility.","expected_test_result":"They are classified as canonical, specialized, or duplicate based on contract analysis.","testing_guidance":"Use semantic comparison and behavior tests rather than text similarity alone.","verification_method":"Compare responsibility, contract, and behavior of candidate implementations.","verification_guidance":"Preserve intentional specialization and document why consolidation is inappropriate.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B70-005","D58-B62-002"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B96-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Maintainability and Technical Debt","subtopic":"Simplification","concept":"Simplification before abstraction","knowledge_type":"HEURISTIC","question":"Why can simplification be preferable to adding an abstraction?","answer":"Removing unnecessary variation or duplicated paths can reduce complexity directly, whereas adding an abstraction may preserve the underlying complexity while introducing another layer.","explanation":"Abstraction is useful when it captures meaningful variation or isolates a real boundary, but it is not inherently a substitute for simplification.","principle":"Prefer removing unnecessary complexity before introducing mechanisms to manage it.","rule":"Before adding an abstraction, determine whether the underlying variation or duplication can be eliminated safely.","objective":"Avoid abstraction-driven complexity growth.","constraints":["Some complexity represents genuine domain requirements and cannot be removed.","Simplification must preserve required behavior."],"expected_behavior":"New abstractions are introduced only when they solve a demonstrated boundary or variability problem.","failure_mode":"A small problem becomes a hierarchy of unnecessary abstractions.","cause":"Abstraction was used as the first response to complexity.","root_cause":"The source of complexity was not analyzed.","solution":"Attempt safe simplification first, then introduce an abstraction if a real reusable boundary remains.","tradeoff":"Aggressive simplification can remove useful flexibility if variability is real.","test_case":"Compare a direct simplified implementation with an abstraction-heavy alternative against the actual requirements.","expected_test_result":"The chosen design satisfies requirements with no unnecessary complexity.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate behavior, changeability, and required variation rather than abstraction count.","verification_method":"Trace each abstraction to a concrete requirement or boundary.","verification_guidance":"Flag abstractions with no independent responsibility or variation.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B48-001","D58-B62-002","D58-B94-002"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B96-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B96","topic":"Maintainability and Technical Debt","subtopic":"Technical Debt","concept":"Explicit technical-debt representation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should technical debt be represented so that it remains actionable?","answer":"Record technical debt as a concrete problem with affected scope, reason, impact, evidence, and intended remediation rather than as an informal label.","explanation":"A vague debt label does not tell future engineers what is wrong or what evidence justifies remediation.","principle":"Technical debt should preserve enough context to support a future engineering decision.","rule":"Do not record debt without identifying the affected behavior or structure and the reason it matters.","objective":"Make deferred engineering work traceable and actionable.","constraints":["Not every imperfection is technical debt.","Debt records should distinguish known risk from speculative future inconvenience."],"expected_behavior":"Debt records identify a concrete engineering concern and its relevant evidence.","failure_mode":"Debt accumulates as vague notes that cannot be prioritized or safely resolved.","cause":"Debt was recorded without scope or rationale.","root_cause":"No structured debt representation existed.","solution":"Record debt with scope, evidence, impact, constraints, and remediation criteria.","edge_case":"A deliberate tradeoff may not be debt if no future correction is expected; record the decision and rationale instead.","testing_guidance":"Verify that debt remediation includes relevant regression or architecture tests.","verification_method":"Inspect debt records for concrete scope and evidence.","verification_guidance":"Reject records that contain only subjective severity without supporting context.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-005","D58-B70-001","D58-B62-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B97-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Advanced Testing Patterns","subtopic":"Property-Based Testing","concept":"Property-based testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What does property-based testing add beyond fixed example tests?","answer":"It checks general properties across many generated inputs rather than validating only a small set of manually selected examples.","explanation":"Example tests demonstrate behavior for specific cases. Property-based tests can explore broader input spaces when a stable invariant or relation can be expressed.","principle":"When behavior has generalizable properties, test the property across diverse valid and invalid inputs.","rule":"Define the property independently from the implementation and generate inputs within the intended domain.","objective":"Increase coverage of input-space behavior without enumerating every example.","constraints":["Generated inputs must satisfy meaningful domain constraints.","A property test is only as useful as its oracle.","Random generation alone does not establish correctness."],"expected_behavior":"Inputs satisfying the generator constraints are checked against the declared property.","failure_mode":"A test passes because the generated inputs are narrow or the property is equivalent to the implementation.","cause":"Weak generators or weak oracles.","root_cause":"The test did not independently represent expected behavior.","solution":"Design domain-aware generators and independent properties.","edge_case":"Rare boundary cases may require explicit targeted examples even with property-based generation.","test_case":"Generate valid values and verify a documented round-trip or algebraic property.","expected_test_result":"All generated cases satisfy the property or produce a minimized counterexample.","testing_guidance":"Combine property-based tests with targeted boundary and regression tests.","verification_method":"Review generator assumptions and oracle independence.","verification_guidance":"Verify that properties are derived from requirements rather than implementation structure.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-003","D58-B64-002","D58-B70-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B97-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Advanced Testing Patterns","subtopic":"Metamorphic Testing","concept":"Metamorphic testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"When is metamorphic testing useful?","answer":"It is useful when a direct expected output is difficult to compute but relationships between outputs under controlled input transformations are known.","explanation":"A metamorphic relation can test consistency without requiring a complete oracle for every input.","principle":"Test known relationships between executions when exact expected outputs are unavailable or expensive to derive.","rule":"Define an input transformation and the expected relation between the original and transformed outputs independently of the implementation.","objective":"Test systems with difficult or incomplete direct oracles.","constraints":["The metamorphic relation must be valid for the selected input domain.","A relation that is only approximately valid requires explicit tolerance semantics."],"expected_behavior":"The output relation holds for valid transformed inputs.","failure_mode":"The test reports false failures because the transformation does not preserve the required relation.","cause":"Incorrect metamorphic relation or domain assumptions.","root_cause":"The relation was not established from system semantics.","solution":"Define and validate metamorphic relations from documented behavior.","edge_case":"Some transformations alter outputs legitimately and require a relation other than equality.","test_case":"Apply a semantics-preserving input transformation and compare the required output relationship.","expected_test_result":"The documented metamorphic relation holds.","testing_guidance":"Include transformations that target different failure modes rather than arbitrary input changes.","verification_method":"Trace the relation to a specification or mathematical property.","verification_guidance":"Verify the relation independently of the implementation.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B97-001","D58-B20-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B97-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Advanced Testing Patterns","subtopic":"Differential Testing","concept":"Differential testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What is differential testing?","answer":"Differential testing compares outputs or behavior from two or more independently implemented or versioned systems under equivalent inputs to identify discrepancies.","explanation":"The comparison target acts as a reference relation rather than requiring an absolute oracle for every case.","principle":"Independent implementations or versions can expose behavioral divergence when their intended semantics should agree.","rule":"Use differential comparison only where the compared systems have a justified behavioral equivalence scope.","objective":"Detect regressions or implementation discrepancies.","constraints":["Differences do not automatically identify which implementation is correct.","The comparison scope must account for intentional version or platform differences."],"expected_behavior":"Unexpected differences are identified and classified for investigation.","failure_mode":"A valid behavioral difference is incorrectly treated as a defect.","cause":"Equivalence scope was undefined.","root_cause":"Comparison was performed without an explicit compatibility contract.","solution":"Define the comparison domain and classify expected differences before treating discrepancies as failures.","edge_case":"Floating-point, ordering, timing, or platform-specific differences may require normalized comparison.","test_case":"Run equivalent inputs through two implementations with a documented equivalence contract.","expected_test_result":"Unexpected deviations are detected without treating declared differences as failures.","testing_guidance":"Normalize only differences explicitly allowed by the contract.","verification_method":"Review the differential relation and discrepancy classification.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer correctness solely from agreement between implementations.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-003","D58-B68-002","D58-B97-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B97-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Advanced Testing Patterns","subtopic":"Mutation Testing","concept":"Mutation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What does mutation testing evaluate?","answer":"Mutation testing evaluates whether a test suite can detect deliberately introduced small changes to implementation behavior.","explanation":"A test suite may have high execution coverage while failing to detect important behavioral defects. Mutants provide a mechanism for assessing test sensitivity.","principle":"Tests should detect meaningful deviations from required behavior, not merely execute code.","rule":"Interpret surviving mutants as signals requiring investigation rather than as direct proof of inadequate tests in every case.","objective":"Assess whether tests are sensitive to relevant implementation faults.","constraints":["Some mutants may be semantically equivalent to the original implementation.","Mutation operators must represent plausible defect classes.","Mutation scores are not universal correctness measures."],"expected_behavior":"Relevant behavioral mutations are detected by the test suite.","failure_mode":"Important mutants survive without a documented reason.","cause":"Tests exercise code but do not assert meaningful behavior.","root_cause":"Test oracle weakness.","solution":"Strengthen assertions or add tests for the affected behavioral property.","edge_case":"A surviving mutant may be equivalent or outside the intended contract.","test_case":"Introduce a mutation that changes a documented branch condition and run the relevant tests.","expected_test_result":"A test detects the behavioral change.","testing_guidance":"Classify surviving mutants before adding tests.","verification_method":"Map surviving mutants to contract requirements and test assertions.","verification_guidance":"Avoid optimizing only for a numerical mutation score.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-003","D58-B70-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B97-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B97","topic":"Advanced Testing Patterns","subtopic":"Oracle Independence","concept":"Independent test oracle","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should a test oracle be independent from the implementation under test?","answer":"If the expected result is derived using the same flawed logic as the implementation, the test can reproduce the same defect and falsely pass.","explanation":"Tests become stronger when expected behavior is derived from requirements, mathematical relations, trusted references, or simpler independent logic.","principle":"The mechanism used to judge correctness should not merely replicate the implementation being tested.","rule":"Avoid constructing expected results by calling the same algorithm or shared defective helper used by the system under test.","objective":"Reduce correlated test and implementation defects.","constraints":["Some complex systems require partial oracle reuse; such dependencies should be explicit.","Reference implementations also require evidence of correctness within their intended scope."],"expected_behavior":"Test failures reflect meaningful deviation from an independently defined expectation.","failure_mode":"A defective implementation and test produce the same incorrect result.","cause":"The test oracle duplicated implementation logic.","root_cause":"Oracle independence was not considered.","solution":"Derive expected behavior from an independent specification, relation, simpler algorithm, or trusted fixture.","edge_case":"A trusted external oracle may have different version or semantic assumptions.","test_case":"Compare a production algorithm against independently computed expected results.","expected_test_result":"Known defect cases are detected.","testing_guidance":"Review test helpers for duplicated business logic.","verification_method":"Trace expected-value generation separately from implementation execution.","verification_guidance":"Identify shared dependencies between system-under-test and oracle.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"TEST_DESIGN","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-003","D58-B97-003","D58-B70-004"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B98-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Advanced Verification","subtopic":"Proof Obligations","concept":"Explicit proof obligation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is a proof obligation in software verification?","answer":"A proof obligation is a specific condition that must be demonstrated for a claimed property, transition, or implementation relationship to hold.","explanation":"Breaking broad correctness claims into explicit obligations makes verification scope concrete and prevents unsupported conclusions.","principle":"Every nontrivial correctness claim should have identifiable evidence or proof obligations appropriate to its assurance level.","rule":"Do not claim a property is verified without identifying what conditions were checked to support that claim.","objective":"Make verification claims precise and auditable.","constraints":["Not every software property is practically amenable to formal proof.","Testing and analysis can provide evidence without constituting mathematical proof."],"expected_behavior":"Verification records identify the property, assumptions, obligations, and evidence used.","failure_mode":"A broad verification label is applied without identifying what was actually checked.","cause":"Verification scope was not decomposed.","root_cause":"The claim exceeded its supporting evidence.","solution":"Decompose the claim into explicit obligations and map each to an appropriate verification method.","edge_case":"A proof may depend on assumptions that themselves require separate verification.","test_case":"Take a state-transition correctness claim and enumerate its precondition, transition, and postcondition obligations.","expected_test_result":"Each obligation has an identified verification method or explicit unresolved status.","testing_guidance":"Test assumptions separately where they can fail operationally.","verification_method":"Review claim-to-obligation-to-evidence traceability.","verification_guidance":"Mark unproven assumptions explicitly rather than treating them as verified.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B42-001","D58-B70-001","D58-B19-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B98-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Advanced Verification","subtopic":"Refinement","concept":"Refinement relationship","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does it mean for an implementation to refine a specification?","answer":"An implementation refines a specification when its permitted behavior remains within the behavior allowed by the specification while satisfying required guarantees.","explanation":"Refinement connects an abstract contract to a concrete implementation without treating implementation details as new unrestricted behavior.","principle":"Implementation correctness can be evaluated by whether concrete behavior satisfies the permitted abstract behavior.","rule":"Do not claim refinement without defining the abstraction mapping and allowed behavior relation.","objective":"Connect specifications to implementations systematically.","constraints":["The abstraction relation must be explicit enough to evaluate.","Specifications that permit nondeterministic behavior require appropriate refinement semantics."],"expected_behavior":"Concrete executions satisfy the constraints and guarantees of the abstract specification.","failure_mode":"Implementation introduces behavior that violates the specification or omits required behavior.","cause":"Specification-to-implementation relation was not checked.","root_cause":"The implementation was evaluated only through local tests or syntax correctness.","solution":"Define an abstraction mapping and verify relevant refinement obligations.","edge_case":"An implementation may intentionally strengthen behavior beyond the specification if that strengthening remains compatible.","test_case":"Compare an implementation transition against the corresponding specification transition.","expected_test_result":"The concrete transition satisfies the permitted abstract relation.","testing_guidance":"Use tests to exercise refinement-critical behaviors but do not treat test coverage as complete proof.","verification_method":"Formal or structured contract comparison where appropriate.","verification_guidance":"Record assumptions and abstraction mappings explicitly.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-004","D58-B42-002","D58-B70-004"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B98-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Advanced Verification","subtopic":"Counterexamples","concept":"Counterexample-guided investigation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a counterexample from a verification or property check be used?","answer":"Treat the counterexample as a concrete witness of a violated condition, minimize or reproduce it, classify the violated assumption, and use the result to refine the implementation, specification, or test.","explanation":"A counterexample is evidence about a specific failure path, not merely a generic error message.","principle":"Verification failures should produce actionable evidence that can update the model or implementation.","rule":"Do not discard a counterexample without determining whether it exposes a real defect, invalid assumption, or modeling error.","objective":"Turn verification failures into useful engineering evidence.","constraints":["The counterexample may arise from an inaccurate model or environment assumption.","Reduction must preserve the property violation."],"expected_behavior":"A verification failure results in a classified and traceable finding.","failure_mode":"The same verification failure recurs because the witness was ignored or misclassified.","cause":"Counterexample lacked ownership or follow-up analysis.","root_cause":"Verification output was not integrated into the debugging workflow.","solution":"Capture, reproduce, minimize, classify, and link the counterexample to corrective action.","edge_case":"A counterexample may reveal an intentional behavior missing from the specification rather than an implementation defect.","test_case":"Introduce a known violating input and follow it through the counterexample workflow.","expected_test_result":"The violation is reproduced and classified correctly.","testing_guidance":"Preserve the original witness while creating minimized regression cases.","verification_method":"Trace counterexample from verifier output to root cause and resolution.","verification_guidance":"Retain the distinction between model error, specification error, and implementation error.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B11-001","D58-B11-002","D58-B70-004"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B98-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Advanced Verification","subtopic":"Assumption Verification","concept":"Assumption closure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is assumption closure in verification?","answer":"Assumption closure means that assumptions required to establish a verification claim are themselves either verified, explicitly trusted, or bounded as unresolved.","explanation":"A proof or verification result can be misleading if it depends on unverified assumptions that are treated as facts.","principle":"Verification claims inherit the validity requirements of their assumptions.","rule":"Every critical assumption supporting a verification claim must have an explicit status.","objective":"Prevent unsupported verification conclusions.","constraints":["Some external assumptions cannot be fully verified within the system boundary.","Trusted environmental assumptions must be scoped rather than silently universalized."],"expected_behavior":"Verification records identify all material assumptions and their status.","failure_mode":"A property is marked verified even though a critical prerequisite assumption is false or unknown.","cause":"Assumptions were omitted from the verification record.","root_cause":"The claim boundary was larger than the evidence boundary.","solution":"Record assumptions and verify or constrain them where possible.","edge_case":"An assumption may be accepted as an external contract but must remain attributed to that contract.","test_case":"Remove or violate a critical assumption and observe whether the verification claim still holds.","expected_test_result":"The claim is correctly marked invalid, conditional, or unresolved.","testing_guidance":"Include negative tests for material assumptions where practical.","verification_method":"Perform assumption-to-claim dependency analysis.","verification_guidance":"Do not collapse TRUSTED, VERIFIED, and UNKNOWN into one status.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-004","D58-B70-001","D58-B70-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B98-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B98","topic":"Advanced Verification","subtopic":"Verification Scope","concept":"Claim-scope alignment","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should verification scope align with a correctness claim?","answer":"The verified property, input domain, environment, version, and assumptions must be no broader than the evidence actually established.","explanation":"A test or proof for one configuration cannot automatically establish correctness for every environment, version, or input class.","principle":"Verification claims must not exceed their evidence boundary.","rule":"State the exact scope of a verification result and avoid universalizing a bounded result.","objective":"Prevent overclaiming verification.","constraints":["Scope dimensions may include version, platform, configuration, input domain, concurrency model, and dependencies.","Broader claims require broader evidence."],"expected_behavior":"Verification results clearly state what was and was not established.","failure_mode":"A limited successful test is reported as universal correctness.","cause":"Evidence scope was omitted from the claim.","root_cause":"Verification status and evidence scope were conflated.","solution":"Record verification scope explicitly and preserve unresolved dimensions.","edge_case":"A formal proof may establish broader behavior than tests, but only within its formal model and assumptions.","test_case":"Run verification for one supported configuration and inspect the resulting claim.","expected_test_result":"The claim is limited to the verified configuration and conditions.","testing_guidance":"Store environment and version metadata with execution evidence.","verification_method":"Compare claim dimensions against evidence dimensions.","verification_guidance":"Reject claims whose scope exceeds their supporting evidence.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-005","D58-B69-005","D58-B70-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B99-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Engineering Documentation","subtopic":"Decision Records","concept":"Decision rationale preservation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should significant engineering decisions preserve rationale?","answer":"Record the problem, alternatives considered, constraints, chosen approach, and evidence so future maintainers can distinguish intentional design from accidental behavior.","explanation":"Without rationale, future engineers may repeat rejected approaches or remove constraints whose purpose is no longer obvious.","principle":"Important design decisions should preserve enough context to reconstruct why the decision was made.","rule":"Do not record only the chosen implementation when the choice involved meaningful alternatives or tradeoffs.","objective":"Preserve decision context across time and personnel changes.","constraints":["Records should remain concise and factual.","Rationale should distinguish evidence from assumptions and preferences."],"expected_behavior":"Future changes can evaluate whether the original constraints still apply.","failure_mode":"A deliberate design is mistaken for accidental complexity.","cause":"Decision rationale was lost.","root_cause":"Only implementation details were preserved.","solution":"Record decision context and link it to relevant requirements or evidence.","edge_case":"Routine implementation choices may not require formal decision records.","test_case":"Review a nontrivial architectural decision and determine whether its rationale can be reconstructed.","expected_test_result":"The decision's constraints, alternatives, and reasoning are traceable.","testing_guidance":"Check decision records during significant architecture changes.","verification_method":"Trace implementation constraints to documented decisions.","verification_guidance":"Mark assumptions and unresolved alternatives explicitly.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-005","D58-B62-005","D58-B70-001"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B99-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Engineering Documentation","subtopic":"Contract Documentation","concept":"Behavioral contract completeness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes an API contract behaviorally complete enough for independent consumers?","answer":"It should define relevant inputs, outputs, errors, side effects, preconditions, postconditions, compatibility semantics, and important boundary conditions rather than only signatures.","explanation":"A signature describes structure but not necessarily the behavior consumers depend on.","principle":"A contract should describe observable semantics required for correct independent use.","rule":"Do not treat type or signature compatibility as sufficient evidence of behavioral compatibility.","objective":"Make interfaces usable without implementation knowledge.","constraints":["Only behavior material to consumers needs to be documented.","Contracts should not promise incidental implementation details."],"expected_behavior":"Independent consumers can determine correct usage and expected outcomes from the contract.","failure_mode":"Consumers infer behavior from implementation or trial and error.","cause":"Contract documented syntax but omitted semantics.","root_cause":"Behavioral requirements were not represented at the interface boundary.","solution":"Document observable behavior and compatibility conditions.","test_case":"Ask an independent implementer to use the contract without source-code access.","expected_test_result":"Required behavior can be implemented without undocumented assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Derive contract tests from documented behavior.","verification_method":"Compare consumer assumptions with explicit contract statements.","verification_guidance":"Identify undocumented behavior that consumers currently rely upon.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B16-001","D58-B68-002","D58-B20-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B99-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Engineering Documentation","subtopic":"Executable Documentation","concept":"Executable contract example","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can examples provide stronger documentation than prose alone?","answer":"Examples can make exact input, output, sequencing, and boundary behavior concrete, especially when they are linked to executable tests or validation.","explanation":"Prose can be interpreted differently, while executable examples can detect drift between documentation and implementation.","principle":"Use executable examples when exact behavioral semantics are important and stable enough to encode.","rule":"Do not label an example as verified behavior unless its execution or verification status is actually known.","objective":"Reduce documentation ambiguity and drift.","constraints":["Examples must be maintained as behavior changes.","An executable example proves only what it actually exercises."],"expected_behavior":"Examples remain consistent with the documented contract.","failure_mode":"Documentation examples become stale and teach unsupported behavior.","cause":"Examples were not linked to validation.","root_cause":"Documentation and verification were treated as unrelated artifacts.","solution":"Convert critical examples into executable contract tests where appropriate.","edge_case":"Conceptual examples may intentionally be illustrative and need not execute.","test_case":"Run a documented example as a contract test against the implementation.","expected_test_result":"The example behavior matches the documented contract.","testing_guidance":"Clearly classify illustrative versus executed examples.","verification_method":"Compare example content with execution or validation metadata.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer execution merely because code is syntactically plausible.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-002","D58-B20-003","D58-B70-004"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B99-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Engineering Documentation","subtopic":"Unknowns","concept":"Explicit unknown representation","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"Why should unknown engineering facts be represented explicitly?","answer":"Explicitly representing unknowns prevents assumptions from silently becoming facts and allows unresolved questions to remain visible to reasoning and decision processes.","explanation":"Systems that collapse unknown into false, true, default, or guessed values can make downstream decisions appear more certain than their evidence supports.","principle":"Unknown information is a valid knowledge state and should remain distinguishable from known false or known true information.","rule":"Do not fill an unresolved field with a fabricated or unsupported value merely to complete a record.","objective":"Preserve epistemic correctness.","constraints":["The consumer must understand unknown semantics.","Some operational workflows require explicit fallback behavior when information is unknown."],"expected_behavior":"Unknown values propagate or trigger defined fallback behavior without being silently converted into facts.","failure_mode":"An assumption is stored as factual data and later used for automated reasoning.","cause":"Unknown state was not representable.","root_cause":"The data model forced premature certainty.","solution":"Add explicit unknown status and define downstream handling.","edge_case":"A default may be acceptable operationally if it is explicitly marked as a default rather than factual knowledge.","test_case":"Remove required evidence and inspect how the knowledge system represents the missing fact.","expected_test_result":"The value becomes UNKNOWN or an equivalent explicit unresolved state.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, conflicting, and stale evidence separately.","verification_method":"Inspect state transitions from known to unknown and back to known.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unknown is not silently coerced into a concrete value.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-001","D58-B70-001","D58-B70-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B99-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B99","topic":"Engineering Documentation","subtopic":"Negative Knowledge","concept":"Known non-applicability","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why record when a rule or capability does not apply?","answer":"Explicit non-applicability prevents future reasoning systems or engineers from repeatedly considering an invalid option as if it were unresolved.","explanation":"Absence of a record can mean unknown, forgotten, or not applicable. These states have different meanings and should not be conflated.","principle":"Negative knowledge should be represented when non-applicability materially affects decisions.","rule":"Distinguish NOT_APPLICABLE from UNKNOWN when the distinction changes future reasoning.","objective":"Improve knowledge completeness and reduce repeated invalid exploration.","constraints":["Non-applicability must be supported by scope or evidence.","Do not use negative knowledge to assert unsupported universal exclusions."],"expected_behavior":"Consumers can distinguish unsupported, unknown, and explicitly inapplicable options.","failure_mode":"A previously rejected option is repeatedly reconsidered because its exclusion was not recorded.","cause":"Only positive knowledge was retained.","root_cause":"The knowledge model lacked negative or scope-exclusion semantics.","solution":"Record the applicable scope and explicit non-applicability rationale where material.","edge_case":"A capability may be inapplicable only under one version or environment and applicable elsewhere.","test_case":"Query the knowledge base for an option explicitly excluded for a known scope.","expected_test_result":"The system returns the non-applicability state and its scope rather than treating it as unknown.","testing_guidance":"Test scope-specific exclusions and later applicability changes.","verification_method":"Check that exclusion records contain scope and supporting rationale.","verification_guidance":"Avoid turning local non-applicability into universal prohibition.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-005","D58-B70-003","D58-B67-002"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}{"record_id":"D58-B100-001","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"Engineering Synthesis","subtopic":"Evidence-Driven Implementation","concept":"Evidence-to-implementation traceability","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should an engineering implementation be connected to the evidence supporting it?","answer":"Maintain traceability from requirements or observed constraints through design decisions and implementation elements to tests or verification evidence.","explanation":"Traceability allows an autonomous engineering system to determine why an implementation exists, what it satisfies, and what evidence supports its behavior.","principle":"Implementation claims should be connected to explicit requirements, decisions, and verification evidence.","rule":"Do not treat an implementation as justified merely because it compiles or resembles a known pattern.","objective":"Enable evidence-driven code generation and maintenance.","constraints":["Traceability granularity should be practical and aligned with meaningful engineering decisions.","One implementation may satisfy multiple requirements."],"expected_behavior":"A reasoning system can trace an implementation element to its intended requirement and supporting evidence.","failure_mode":"Code exists without a clear basis or verification relationship.","cause":"Implementation and knowledge artifacts evolved independently.","root_cause":"No traceability model connected engineering artifacts.","solution":"Create stable identifiers and relationships between requirements, decisions, implementation, tests, and verification.","test_case":"Select a nontrivial implementation element and trace it through requirement and verification records.","expected_test_result":"The relationship chain is complete enough to explain intended behavior and evidence scope.","testing_guidance":"Check for orphan implementations and orphan requirements.","verification_method":"Graph traversal across requirement, decision, implementation, test, and verification relationships.","verification_guidance":"Flag missing links without assuming the implementation is incorrect.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-004","D58-B70-004","D58-B62-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B100-002","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"Engineering Synthesis","subtopic":"Uncertainty Propagation","concept":"Uncertainty propagation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should uncertainty propagate through engineering reasoning?","answer":"When a conclusion depends materially on uncertain inputs or assumptions, the conclusion should retain that uncertainty rather than being represented as fully certain knowledge.","explanation":"Collapsing uncertainty at intermediate steps can make downstream automated decisions more confident than the evidence supports.","principle":"Knowledge systems should preserve material uncertainty across dependent reasoning steps.","rule":"Do not convert an uncertain premise into a verified conclusion without additional evidence.","objective":"Prevent false certainty in autonomous engineering decisions.","constraints":["Uncertainty representation must be explicit enough for downstream consumers.","Not every internal uncertainty requires propagation if it has been safely eliminated by valid reasoning."],"expected_behavior":"Dependent conclusions retain appropriate uncertainty or acquire independent supporting evidence.","failure_mode":"A low-confidence assumption becomes a high-confidence engineering fact through repeated transformations.","cause":"Confidence or evidence status was discarded during reasoning.","root_cause":"The reasoning pipeline did not preserve epistemic metadata.","solution":"Propagate evidence classification, confidence, scope, and assumptions through derived records.","edge_case":"A conclusion can become certain relative to an assumption even if the assumption remains uncertain globally; both scopes should be recorded.","test_case":"Derive a conclusion from an explicitly unknown input and inspect the resulting knowledge status.","expected_test_result":"The conclusion is not marked independently verified without new evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test chains containing unknown, conflicted, and weak evidence states.","verification_method":"Trace epistemic metadata through derivation relationships.","verification_guidance":"Verify that derived claims do not silently upgrade evidence status.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-001","D58-B70-001","D58-B70-003"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B100-003","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"Engineering Synthesis","subtopic":"Contradiction Handling","concept":"Contradiction-preserving reasoning","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should an engineering knowledge system handle conflicting evidence?","answer":"Preserve the conflicting claims with their scopes, versions, provenance, and evidence status instead of silently selecting one as universally correct.","explanation":"Conflicts may result from version differences, environment differences, measurement variation, incomplete evidence, or genuine disagreement.","principle":"Contradiction is information that requires resolution or scoping, not automatic deletion.","rule":"Do not overwrite a conflicting claim merely because one value is newer or more convenient without establishing the applicable scope.","objective":"Prevent loss of important contradictory evidence.","constraints":["Conflict resolution may legitimately select a scoped canonical claim after evidence analysis.","Contradictions should distinguish semantic disagreement from different applicability conditions."],"expected_behavior":"Conflicting records remain traceable until resolved or scoped.","failure_mode":"One claim silently replaces another and downstream reasoning loses the conflict context.","cause":"Knowledge ingestion treated conflict as duplication.","root_cause":"Contradiction state was not modeled separately from deduplication.","solution":"Store both claims, compare scope and provenance, and create an explicit resolution relationship when justified.","edge_case":"Two claims may both be correct under different versions or environments.","test_case":"Insert two incompatible claims with different version scopes and query the knowledge system.","expected_test_result":"Both claims remain available with their scopes and conflict relationship.","testing_guidance":"Test genuine contradictions separately from version-specific differences.","verification_method":"Inspect contradiction graph and resolution metadata.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence before marking a conflict resolved.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B70-003","D58-B68-002","D58-B20-005"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B100-004","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"Engineering Synthesis","subtopic":"Autonomous Code Generation","concept":"Generation-verification separation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must generated code be separated from verified code status?","answer":"Generated code represents a proposed implementation artifact, while verification requires independent evidence that the artifact satisfies defined requirements under a stated scope.","explanation":"Syntactically plausible or generated code can still contain semantic defects, unsupported assumptions, dependency errors, or untested behavior.","principle":"Creation of an artifact and establishment of its correctness are separate states.","rule":"Never mark generated code as verified solely because it was produced successfully or resembles a known implementation pattern.","objective":"Prevent autonomous systems from confusing generation with correctness.","constraints":["Verification scope must specify what was actually checked.","Execution success alone does not prove all required properties."],"expected_behavior":"Generated, executed, tested, and verified states remain independently represented.","failure_mode":"An autonomous system reuses unverified generated code as established knowledge.","cause":"Artifact lifecycle states were collapsed.","root_cause":"Generation and verification were modeled as one status.","solution":"Track generation, execution, testing, and verification independently with explicit evidence relationships.","test_case":"Generate code without executing it and inspect its knowledge status.","expected_test_result":"The artifact remains generated or illustrative and is not marked verified.","testing_guidance":"Test status transitions independently: generated, executed, tested, passed, verified.","verification_method":"Audit artifact state transitions against evidence records.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence before each stronger status transition.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B20-002","D58-B20-003","D58-B70-004"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"},{"record_id":"D58-B100-005","dataset_id":"D58","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"KB-1.0","batch_id":"B100","topic":"Engineering Synthesis","subtopic":"Knowledge Lifecycle","concept":"Knowledge lifecycle closure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What constitutes a closed engineering knowledge lifecycle?","answer":"A knowledge item should move through appropriate stages from creation or ingestion through validation, use, feedback, correction, versioning, and retirement without losing provenance or relationships.","explanation":"A knowledge base that only stores initial facts cannot reliably adapt when implementations, dependencies, requirements, or evidence change.","principle":"Engineering knowledge must remain traceable and revisable throughout its lifecycle.","rule":"Do not treat ingestion as the final state of knowledge; preserve evidence, validation state, version context, corrections, and supersession relationships.","objective":"Support continuously improving engineering reasoning without corrupting historical evidence.","constraints":["Historical records should remain available when required for reproducibility.","Retirement must not erase evidence needed to understand previous decisions."],"expected_behavior":"Knowledge can be created, validated, corrected, superseded, and retrieved with its historical relationships intact.","failure_mode":"Stale or incorrect knowledge remains indistinguishable from current validated knowledge.","cause":"Knowledge lifecycle metadata was incomplete.","root_cause":"Storage focused on content rather than evidence and state transitions.","solution":"Track provenance, version, validation status, contradictions, corrections, supersession, and retirement explicitly.","edge_case":"A historical fact may remain valid even after a newer implementation supersedes an older implementation.","test_case":"Create a knowledge record, supersede it with a newer scoped record, and retrieve both historically and currently.","expected_test_result":"Current retrieval prefers applicable knowledge while historical provenance remains recoverable.","testing_guidance":"Test creation, validation, correction, contradiction, supersession, and retirement paths.","verification_method":"Audit lifecycle transitions and relationship integrity.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no lifecycle transition silently destroys provenance or stable identifiers.","language":"GENERAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VERIFIED","evidence_level":"GENERAL_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","evidence_classification":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D58-B70-001","D58-B70-003","D58-B70-005","D58-B69-002"],"provenance":[],"provenance_status":"NOT_ATTACHED"}

